window.ATLAS_PROFILES_EXT = {"socrates":{"life":["Socrates lived in Athens in the 5th century BCE, when the city was at the height of its power. Athens was a democracy in which ordinary male citizens voted on laws and sat on juries. It was also a city of new ideas: travelling teachers called sophists charged fees to teach young men how to argue and win. For much of his adult life Athens was fighting the long Peloponnesian War against Sparta, which it finally lost in 404 BCE.","Socrates was born c. 470 BCE, probably the son of a stonemason and a midwife. He served as a heavy-armed soldier in several battles. He wrote nothing. Instead he spent his days in public places questioning anyone who would talk to him about courage, justice and how to live. According to Plato, a friend asked the oracle at Delphi whether anyone was wiser than Socrates and was told no. Socrates took this as a puzzle and a mission. In 399 BCE he was tried for impiety and corrupting the young, found guilty, and executed by drinking hemlock.","Almost everything we know about Socrates comes from others, mainly his student Plato, the soldier and writer Xenophon, and the comic playwright Aristophanes, who mocked him on stage. Their portraits differ, so scholars debate which ideas were really his. All agree that he was poor, physically tough and famous for an odd, unhandsome face. He did not charge for conversation. When the ruling group known as the Thirty ordered him to help arrest an innocent man, he refused and went home, putting his life at risk."],"timeline":[[-470,"Born in Athens, c. 470 BCE"],[-432,"Serves as a soldier at the siege of Potidaea, c. 432 BCE"],[-423,"Mocked as a muddle-headed thinker in Aristophanes' comedy The Clouds"],[-406,"Serving on the Athenian council, opposes an illegal mass trial of generals"],[-404,"Refuses an order from the Thirty to arrest Leon of Salamis"],[-399,"Tried for impiety and corrupting the young; executed in Athens"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Wisdom starts with knowing your ignorance","body":"Socrates found that people who claimed to be experts on justice or courage could not explain what those things were. He concluded he was wiser only in one way: he did not think he knew what he did not know. Admitting you are confused is the first step to learning anything."},{"head":"The Socratic method","body":"Socrates would ask someone to define an idea, then ask more questions until the definition ran into trouble. If someone says courage is standing firm in battle, he might ask about a soldier who wisely retreats. The aim is not to humiliate but to test beliefs and replace weak ones with better ones."},{"head":"Care for your soul","body":"Socrates urged people to care less about money, fame and their bodies, and more about the state of their soul, meaning their character. He held that doing wrong harms the wrongdoer more than the victim. A cheat may get rich, but he has damaged the most important thing he has."},{"head":"No one does wrong on purpose","body":"Socrates seems to have believed that virtue is a kind of knowledge. If you truly understood what was good for you, you would do it. People do bad things because they are mistaken about what is good, so the cure for wrongdoing is better understanding."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Socrates said: I know that I know nothing.","truth":"That exact line does not appear in Plato. In the Apology Socrates says he does not think he knows what he does not know, which is a claim about the limits of his knowledge of the most important matters, not a claim that he knows nothing at all."},"startHere":"Read Plato's Apology, Socrates' speech at his trial. It is short, dramatic, and shows his mission, his method and his calm in the face of death in one sitting.","critics":"Aristotle objected that Socrates' view that no one knowingly does wrong ignores weakness of will: people often know perfectly well what is best and still do something else.","legacy":"Socrates became the model of the philosopher who follows an argument wherever it leads, even at personal cost. Nearly every later Greek school, from Plato's Academy to the Cynics and Stoics, claimed him as an inspiration.","quote":{"text":"The unexamined life is not worth living.","src":"Plato, Apology 38a","tr":"Benjamin Jowett"}},"plato":{"life":["Plato was born in Athens c. 428 BCE into a wealthy and politically powerful family. He grew up during the Peloponnesian War between Athens and Sparta, which Athens lost. Afterwards the city lurched from a brutal oligarchy (rule by a small group), which included some of Plato's own relatives, back to democracy. That restored democracy then put his teacher Socrates to death. These events left Plato deeply distrustful of politics as usually practised.","As a young man Plato followed Socrates. After Socrates was executed in 399 BCE, Plato left Athens for a time and probably travelled. Around 387 BCE he founded the Academy, a school in a grove outside Athens, which lasted for centuries. He made several trips to Syracuse in Sicily, hoping to guide its rulers towards wise government, but the visits ended badly. He wrote for about fifty years and died in Athens c. 348 BCE.","Plato wrote dialogues, not treatises. These are conversations, usually with Socrates as the main speaker, so Plato himself never speaks directly. This makes it hard to say exactly what Plato believed, and his views seem to change between early, middle and late works. He was also a fine literary artist: his dialogues use jokes, myths and vivid scenes. All his known dialogues have survived, which is rare for an ancient author."],"timeline":[[-428,"Born in Athens, c. 428 BCE"],[-399,"His teacher Socrates is tried and executed"],[-387,"Founds the Academy in Athens, c. 387 BCE"],[-375,"Writes the Republic, c. 375 BCE"],[-367,"Second visit to Syracuse, to advise the young ruler Dionysius II"],[-348,"Dies in Athens, c. 348 BCE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The Forms","body":"Plato argued that behind the changing things we see are perfect, unchanging Forms, such as Beauty itself or Justice itself. Beautiful things are beautiful because they share in the Form of Beauty. Think of every circle you have drawn: none is perfect, yet you understand what a perfect circle is."},{"head":"The allegory of the cave","body":"Plato pictures prisoners chained in a cave, watching shadows on a wall and taking them for reality. One escapes, sees the sunlit world, and returns to tell the others, who do not believe him. Education, for Plato, is turning the whole person away from shadows and towards what is real."},{"head":"A soul with three parts","body":"In the Republic the soul has reason, spirit (pride and anger) and appetite (desires for food, sex, money). A just person is one in whom reason rules, with spirit as its ally and appetite kept in check. When you skip a party to study, reason is winning."},{"head":"Philosophers should rule","body":"Plato argued that a city will only be just if those who understand the good are in charge. He distrusted democracy because, in his view, it hands power to whoever can flatter the crowd. Many readers find this idea elitist, and he was criticised for it even in ancient times."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Platonic love, for Plato, simply meant a friendship with no romance.","truth":"In the Symposium love is an intense desire that can begin with attraction to a beautiful body and climb towards love of Beauty itself. The modern sense of 'platonic love' as non-romantic friendship developed much later, in the Renaissance and after."},"startHere":"Start with the allegory of the cave at the beginning of Book 7 of the Republic. It takes ten minutes to read and opens up his views on reality, knowledge and education.","critics":"Aristotle argued that Forms existing separately from things explain nothing and lead to an endless regress, the so-called Third Man argument, a problem Plato himself raised in his dialogue Parmenides.","legacy":"Plato's dialogues set the agenda for Western philosophy, raising questions about knowledge, justice, love and reality that are still argued over. The philosopher A. N. Whitehead remarked that European philosophy is a series of footnotes to Plato.","quote":null},"aristotle":{"life":["Aristotle was born in 384 BCE in Stagira, a small town in northern Greece. His father was a doctor to the king of Macedon, a kingdom on the edge of the Greek world that was growing powerful. During his life Macedon, under Philip II and then Alexander the Great, came to dominate the Greek city-states. Aristotle lived much of his life in Athens, but as a foreign resident, not a citizen.","At about seventeen he joined Plato's Academy in Athens and stayed for some twenty years, until Plato died in 347 BCE. He then spent time in Asia Minor and on the island of Lesbos, where he studied animals and sea life closely. Around 343 BCE Philip of Macedon hired him to tutor the young Alexander. In 335 BCE he returned to Athens and founded his own school, the Lyceum. When Alexander died in 323 BCE, anti-Macedonian feeling rose and Aristotle left for Chalcis, where he died in 322 BCE.","Aristotle tried to study almost everything: logic, physics, biology, ethics, politics, poetry and more. He collected facts carefully before theorising, and his school gathered the constitutions of over 150 Greek cities. The polished dialogues he wrote for the public are lost. What survives are mostly lecture notes and working texts, which explains why his writing is often dense and compressed."],"timeline":[[-384,"Born in Stagira, northern Greece"],[-367,"Joins Plato's Academy in Athens"],[-347,"Plato dies; Aristotle leaves Athens for Asia Minor and Lesbos"],[-343,"Becomes tutor to the young Alexander of Macedon, c. 343 BCE"],[-335,"Founds the Lyceum in Athens"],[-323,"Alexander dies; Aristotle leaves Athens for Chalcis"],[-322,"Dies in Chalcis"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Happiness as flourishing","body":"Aristotle called the goal of life eudaimonia, often translated as happiness but closer to flourishing or living well. It is not a passing feeling but a whole life spent using our abilities well, especially reason. A good life is judged over its whole length, not by a single good day."},{"head":"Virtue as a mean","body":"Each good character trait sits between two bad extremes. Courage lies between cowardice and recklessness; generosity between stinginess and wastefulness. The right point depends on the situation, and we find it through practice, just as you become a good guitarist by playing the guitar."},{"head":"The four causes","body":"To understand anything, Aristotle said, ask four questions: what is it made of, what form or structure does it have, what made it, and what is it for. For a table: wood, its design, the carpenter, and eating at. He thought nature too has purposes, such as an acorn growing towards being an oak."},{"head":"Logic","body":"Aristotle created the first system of formal logic, built around the syllogism: if all humans are mortal and Socrates is human, then Socrates is mortal. He studied which forms of argument are valid regardless of their subject. His logic remained the standard in Europe and the Islamic world for about two thousand years."},{"head":"Humans are political animals","body":"Aristotle held that humans naturally live in communities and can only fully flourish as citizens taking part in shared life. Someone who needs no community at all, he said, must be either a beast or a god."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Aristotle's 'golden mean' means always choosing a moderate, middle-of-the-road response.","truth":"The mean is relative to the situation. Sometimes the right response is strong anger or great boldness; virtue means feeling and acting as the situation truly calls for, not always halfway."},"startHere":"Read Books 1 and 2 of the Nicomachean Ethics. They set out his idea of the good life and of virtue as a habit, and are clearer than most of his other writing.","critics":"From the 17th century, thinkers such as Francis Bacon and Galileo rejected his physics and his idea that nature acts for purposes, and modern biology explains apparent purpose through evolution instead. His defence of 'natural slavery' in the Politics is also widely condemned.","legacy":"Aristotle shaped logic, science and ethics for two millennia, through Islamic philosophers like Avicenna and Averroes and Christian ones like Aquinas. Today 'virtue ethics', which focuses on character rather than rules, is a major approach built on his work.","quote":{"text":"For one swallow does not make a summer, nor does one day; and so too one day, or a short time, does not make a man blessed and happy.","src":"Nicomachean Ethics, Book 1, chapter 7","tr":"W. D. Ross"}},"confucius":{"life":["Confucius, whose name was Kong Qiu, lived in the small state of Lu in what is now Shandong province, China, traditionally from 551 to 479 BCE. This was the Spring and Autumn period. The Zhou kings still reigned in name, but real power had passed to rival states and to powerful families within them. Confucius saw his age as one of moral decline and looked back to the early Zhou rulers as a model of good government.","Confucius came from a family of low-ranking nobility and was poor in his youth. He held minor posts in Lu and, according to tradition, later a high office. Around 497 BCE he left Lu and spent many years travelling between states, hoping a ruler would put his ideas into practice. None did. He returned to Lu c. 484 BCE and spent his last years teaching. He was remembered above all as a teacher who accepted students regardless of wealth.","Confucius wrote little or nothing that survives as his own. The Analects, a collection of his sayings and conversations, was put together by his students and their followers over several generations after his death. In it he appears as modest, humorous, fond of music, quick to praise his students and honest about his own failings. Many later stories about him are legends."],"timeline":[[-551,"Born in the state of Lu, traditionally 551 BCE"],[-501,"Begins serving in office in Lu, traditionally c. 501 BCE"],[-497,"Leaves Lu and begins years of travel between states, c. 497 BCE"],[-484,"Returns to Lu and devotes himself to teaching, c. 484 BCE"],[-479,"Dies in Lu, traditionally 479 BCE"],[-136,"Han dynasty makes Confucian classics the basis of official learning, c. 136 BCE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ren: humaneness","body":"Ren is the central virtue in the Analects: caring for others and treating them well. Confucius summed up part of it as: do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself. It starts close to home, with family, and widens outwards."},{"head":"Li: ritual and good manners","body":"Li covers ceremonies, customs and everyday courtesy, like greeting elders properly or mourning a parent. Confucius thought these forms train our feelings and hold society together. A sincere bow shows respect; doing it again and again also builds respect."},{"head":"The junzi, or exemplary person","body":"The junzi is someone of good character, not just high birth. Such a person is loyal, honest and keeps learning, and cares about what is right rather than what pays. Confucius held that anyone could work towards this through study and self-reflection."},{"head":"Leading by example","body":"Confucius argued that rulers should govern through virtue, not harsh punishment. If you lead people with laws and penalties, they will dodge punishment but feel no shame; lead them with virtue and ritual, and they will correct themselves. A good leader is like the wind; the people bend like grass."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Confucius taught blind obedience to parents and rulers.","truth":"The Analects says a child should gently remonstrate with parents who are in the wrong, and that ministers should tell rulers the truth. Obedience is owed to those who act rightly, and rulers must earn loyalty by being virtuous."},"startHere":"Read Books 1 and 2 of the Analects. They are short sayings that introduce learning, family respect and good government, and you can dip in a few lines at a time.","critics":"The philosopher Mozi and his followers objected that Confucian love is partial, favouring one's own family, and argued for impartial care for everyone; they also attacked costly rituals and long mourning as wasteful.","legacy":"Confucian ideas shaped government, education and family life in China, Korea, Japan and Vietnam for over two thousand years, including the civil service examinations. Debates about Confucian values remain part of East Asian public life today.","quote":{"text":"When you know a thing, to hold that you know it; and when you do not know a thing, to allow that you do not know it; this is knowledge.","src":"Analects, Book 2, chapter 17","tr":"James Legge"}},"laozi":{"life":["Laozi means 'Old Master'. According to tradition he was an older contemporary of Confucius in the 6th century BCE, living in the late Zhou period, when China was divided into competing states. That age soon gave way to the Warring States period, an era of constant war in which many thinkers offered rival plans for bringing order. The Daodejing, the book linked to his name, speaks to that world of ambition and violence.","The earliest biography of Laozi was written by the historian Sima Qian around 100 BCE, several centuries later, and even he was unsure of the facts. He reports that Laozi was an archivist at the Zhou court who, seeing the dynasty decline, left for the west. At a mountain pass the gatekeeper asked him to write down his teachings, and he produced a short book in two parts. This is a later story, not established history.","Most scholars today think the Daodejing was not written by one person at one time. It seems to have grown and been edited over a long period. Ancient copies support this: bamboo slips found at Guodian in 1993, from a tomb of c. 300 BCE, contain only part of the text, in a different order. Whether a real person called Laozi stands behind it is unknown."],"timeline":[[-500,"Traditional time of Laozi's life, as an older contemporary of Confucius, c. 6th century BCE"],[-300,"Earliest surviving partial copy of the text placed in a tomb at Guodian, c. 300 BCE"],[-168,"Two silk copies of the Daodejing buried in a tomb at Mawangdui"],[-94,"Sima Qian completes his history containing the first biography of Laozi, c. 94 BCE"],[1973,"The Mawangdui silk manuscripts are discovered"],[1993,"The Guodian bamboo slips are discovered"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The Dao that cannot be named","body":"Dao means 'way'. In the Daodejing it is the source and pattern of everything, but it cannot be fully captured in words. The book opens by saying that a Dao that can be spoken of is not the constant Dao. Words carve the world into pieces; the Dao is the whole."},{"head":"Wu wei: acting without forcing","body":"Wu wei, often translated as non-action, means acting without strain or interference. Think of a skilled swimmer moving with the current rather than thrashing against it. A good ruler, on this view, governs so lightly that the people say things happened by themselves."},{"head":"The strength of softness","body":"Water is the softest thing, yet it wears away rock. The Daodejing praises softness, humility and yielding over hardness and aggression. Trees that cannot bend snap in a storm; grass survives."},{"head":"Opposites depend on each other","body":"We only know beauty because of ugliness, and long because of short. The text suggests that pushing hard for one side of a pair tends to produce its opposite. Grasping for success can bring failure; the person who does not show off is noticed."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Laozi taught people to be lazy and do nothing.","truth":"Wu wei does not mean idleness. It means acting in a natural, unforced way that fits the situation, without needless meddling or showing off."},"startHere":"Read chapters 1, 8, 11 and 17 of the Daodejing. Each is only a few lines long and together they introduce the Dao, water, emptiness and light-handed rule.","critics":"Confucian critics objected that withdrawing from learning, ritual and active government would leave society without moral guidance; modern readers also note that the text is so brief and poetic it can be read to support almost any view.","legacy":"The Daodejing is one of the most translated books in the world. It became a founding text of Daoist philosophy and religion and shaped Chinese art, medicine and Chan (Zen) Buddhism.","quote":null},"buddha":{"life":["Siddhartha Gautama lived in north-eastern India, in the region of the Ganges plain, probably in the 5th century BCE; his exact dates are disputed. It was a time of growing towns, trade and new kingdoms. Many wandering ascetics, people who gave up ordinary life to seek spiritual truth, challenged the authority of the Brahmin priests and their sacrificial religion. The Buddha was one of these wandering teachers.","According to the Buddhist tradition, he was born at Lumbini, in today's Nepal, into the Shakya clan, and grew up in comfort. At about 29 he left home to seek an end to suffering. He tried extreme fasting, found it useless, and adopted a middle way. Sitting under a tree at Bodh Gaya, he reached awakening, which is what 'Buddha' (awakened one) means. He then taught for about 45 years and died at Kushinagar at around 80.","The Buddha wrote nothing. His teachings were memorised and passed on orally by his monks and nuns, and written down only centuries later, so details of his life come wrapped in legend. The early texts present him as a calm, practical teacher who adapted his words to his listeners, refused to answer questions he thought pointless, and founded orders of monks and, later, nuns."],"timeline":[[-480,"Born at Lumbini, c. 480 BCE (older tradition says c. 563 BCE)"],[-451,"Leaves home to seek liberation, traditionally at about 29"],[-445,"Reaches awakening at Bodh Gaya and gives his first sermon near Varanasi, c. 445 BCE"],[-400,"Dies at Kushinagar, c. 400 BCE (older tradition says c. 483 BCE)"],[-249,"Emperor Ashoka puts up a pillar marking Lumbini as his birthplace, c. 249 BCE"],[-29,"Pali teachings written down in Sri Lanka, traditionally c. 1st century BCE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The Four Noble Truths","body":"Life involves dukkha, often translated as suffering but meaning unsatisfactoriness. Dukkha arises from craving. It can end. And there is a path to end it. The structure is like a doctor's: symptom, cause, cure and treatment."},{"head":"The Middle Way","body":"The Buddha rejected both a life of indulgence and a life of harsh self-denial. The path between them, the Noble Eightfold Path, includes right view, right speech, right action, right livelihood, and training of attention through meditation."},{"head":"Not-self","body":"The Buddha taught that there is no permanent, unchanging self behind our body, feelings and thoughts. What we call 'I' is a changing stream of processes. Clinging to a fixed self, he held, is a root of suffering."},{"head":"Everything is impermanent","body":"All conditioned things arise and pass away. Feelings, possessions, relationships and bodies all change. Suffering comes when we cling to things as if they could stay the same; seeing their impermanence clearly loosens that grip."},{"head":"Dependent arising","body":"Things arise in dependence on conditions: when this exists, that comes to be. Nothing exists entirely on its own. Craving, for example, depends on contact and feeling, so changing the conditions changes what follows."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The Buddha was the fat, laughing figure often seen in statues.","truth":"That figure is Budai, a Chinese Buddhist monk from around the 10th century CE who became a folk symbol of contentment. Early images of Siddhartha Gautama show a slim figure."},"startHere":"Read the short sermon known as Setting in Motion the Wheel of Dharma (Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta), traditionally his first teaching, which sets out the Middle Way and the Four Noble Truths in a few pages.","critics":"Hindu philosophers of the Nyaya school and others objected to the not-self teaching: if there is no lasting self, who remembers the past, who is responsible for actions, and who is reborn?","legacy":"Buddhism spread across Asia, shaping the cultures of Sri Lanka, Tibet, China, Japan and South-East Asia, and is followed by hundreds of millions today. Its ideas about attention and the self influence modern psychology and mindfulness practice.","quote":null},"thales":{"life":["Thales lived in Miletus, a rich Greek trading city on the coast of what is now western Turkey, in the late 7th and 6th centuries BCE. Miletus had links with Egypt, Babylon and the kingdom of Lydia, so ideas from older civilisations flowed through it. Near the end of his life the Persian Empire was expanding into the region, conquering Lydia c. 546 BCE.","Thales was later listed among the Seven Sages of Greece, a group of wise men famous for practical wisdom. Herodotus reports that he predicted a solar eclipse, usually dated to 585 BCE, and that he advised the Ionian Greeks to unite under a shared council. Aristotle, writing some two centuries later, named him the founder of natural philosophy, the attempt to explain nature in terms of nature itself.","Thales left no writings that survive, and possibly wrote none. We know him only through later authors, mainly Herodotus and Aristotle, and many stories about him are anecdotes. In one, told by Plato, he fell into a well while gazing at the stars. In another, told by Aristotle, he predicted a good olive harvest, rented all the oil presses and made a fortune, to show philosophers could get rich if they wanted."],"timeline":[[-624,"Born in Miletus, c. 624 BCE"],[-585,"Reported by Herodotus to have predicted a solar eclipse"],[-547,"Advises the Ionian Greeks to form a single council, according to Herodotus, c. 547 BCE"],[-546,"Dies, c. 546 BCE"],[-350,"Aristotle names him the first natural philosopher, c. 350 BCE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Explaining nature by nature","body":"Instead of saying storms or earthquakes come from angry gods, Thales looked for natural explanations. For example, he is reported to have said the earth floats on water, and that earthquakes happen when the water shakes. The answer was wrong, but the kind of answer was new."},{"head":"Everything comes from water","body":"Aristotle reports that Thales held water to be the basic source of all things. He may have noticed that living things need moisture and that seeds are moist. What matters is the bold idea that one simple stuff might underlie the variety of the world."},{"head":"All things are full of gods","body":"Aristotle also reports that Thales thought a magnet has a soul because it moves iron. This suggests he saw life and movement spread through nature, rather than located only in a few divine beings on Mount Olympus."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Thales is famous because he proved that everything really is made of water.","truth":"He is remembered not for being right but for asking a new kind of question: what is the basic stuff of the world, answered with reasons and observation rather than myth."},"startHere":"Read Aristotle's Metaphysics, Book 1, chapter 3, where Aristotle reports Thales' view and his possible reasons. It is a single page and shows how later Greeks saw where philosophy began.","critics":"Anaximander, by tradition his pupil, proposed an unlimited stuff (the apeiron) instead of water; later ancient reports suggest the reason was that one element such as water could not give rise to its opposite, fire, though this may be a reconstruction rather than his own words.","legacy":"Thales is traditionally counted as the first Greek philosopher. The habit he started, looking for natural causes and arguing about them, grew into both philosophy and science.","quote":null},"pythagoras":{"life":["Pythagoras was born on the Greek island of Samos c. 570 BCE. Samos was a busy sea power ruled for part of his life by the tyrant Polycrates. Greek colonies had spread across the Mediterranean, including southern Italy, which the Greeks called Great Greece. Religious movements promising life after death were also spreading in this period, and Greek thinkers in Ionia had begun to explain nature without myth.","Around 530 BCE Pythagoras moved to Croton in southern Italy, perhaps to escape Polycrates' rule. There he founded a community of followers who shared a strict way of life and gained political influence in the city. Later the group met violent opposition, and Pythagoras moved to nearby Metapontum, where he probably died c. 490 BCE. Pythagorean communities continued for over a century after him.","Pythagoras wrote nothing, and his followers often credited their own discoveries to him, so it is very hard to separate the man from the legend. The earliest reports, from writers such as Xenophanes and Herodotus, present him as a religious teacher who believed souls are reborn. Later biographies, written centuries afterwards, add miracles. Scholars treat these as legends, not history."],"timeline":[[-570,"Born on Samos, c. 570 BCE"],[-530,"Moves to Croton in southern Italy and founds his community, c. 530 BCE"],[-510,"Croton defeats neighbouring Sybaris; Pythagoreans are influential in the city, c. 510 BCE"],[-490,"Dies, probably in Metapontum, c. 490 BCE"],[-450,"Anti-Pythagorean uprisings in southern Italy break up the communities, c. 450 BCE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Souls are reborn","body":"The best-attested teaching of Pythagoras is that the soul survives death and is reborn in other bodies, including animals. The poet Xenophanes mocked him for supposedly recognising a dead friend's voice in a beaten puppy. This belief linked to rules about diet and purity."},{"head":"Number and harmony","body":"Pythagoreans discovered that musical harmonies match simple number ratios: halve a string's length and the note rises an octave (2:1). From such findings they came to believe that number is the key to the order of the whole cosmos."},{"head":"Philosophy as a way of life","body":"For Pythagoras, wisdom was not just knowledge but a disciplined way of living, with shared meals, silence, self-examination and rules about food. This idea that philosophy should change how you live influenced Plato and many later schools."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Pythagoras discovered and proved the Pythagorean theorem.","truth":"The relationship between the sides of a right-angled triangle was known in Babylon over a thousand years earlier, and there is no reliable evidence that Pythagoras himself proved it. The credit came from later tradition."},"startHere":"Since he wrote nothing, start with Aristotle's Metaphysics, Book 1, chapter 5, which describes what the Pythagoreans believed about number, or the Pythagoras section of a modern collection of early Greek philosophy.","critics":"Heraclitus attacked Pythagoras by name, saying that learning many things does not teach understanding, and Aristotle objected that Pythagoreans confused numbers, which are abstract, with the physical things they describe.","legacy":"The Pythagorean conviction that the world has a mathematical structure inspired Plato and, much later, scientists such as Kepler. His name lives on in mathematics, and his idea of philosophy as a way of life shaped ancient schools.","quote":null},"heraclitus":{"life":["Heraclitus lived in Ephesus, a large Greek city on the coast of what is now Turkey, around 500 BCE. Ephesus was under Persian control for much of his life. Earlier thinkers from the same coast, like Thales and Anaximander, had already tried to explain the world through one basic stuff, such as water. Heraclitus knew their work and rejected much of it, along with the poets Homer and Hesiod.","He came from an old aristocratic family. According to a later story, he gave up a hereditary title to his brother. He wrote a single book, apparently made up of short, striking sayings, and is said to have placed it in the temple of Artemis. Only around a hundred fragments survive, quoted by later writers. Ancient readers already found him hard to understand, and he was nicknamed 'the Obscure'.","The surviving fragments show a proud, sharp-tongued thinker who mocked the crowd, the poets and other philosophers, including Pythagoras. Many colourful stories about his life and death come from a biography written some 700 years later and are probably invented, sometimes from his own sayings. The fragments are best read as carefully crafted riddles meant to make readers think, not as a complete system."],"timeline":[[-535,"Born in Ephesus, c. 535 BCE"],[-500,"At the height of his activity; writes his book, c. 500 BCE"],[-475,"Dies, c. 475 BCE"],[-360,"Plato's dialogue Cratylus reports his river saying, c. 360 BCE"],[1903,"Hermann Diels publishes the standard numbered collection of his fragments"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Everything is in flux","body":"Heraclitus said we cannot step into the same river twice, because new waters keep flowing in. The world is constant change. Yet the river stays the same river precisely because its waters change: its identity depends on the flow."},{"head":"The unity of opposites","body":"Opposites belong together. The road up and the road down are one and the same road. Health is pleasant because of sickness, rest because of weariness. What looks like conflict is part of a deeper unity."},{"head":"The logos","body":"Logos means word, account or reason. Heraclitus claimed there is a rational order that governs everything, which most people fail to notice even though it is common to all. Wisdom is grasping this hidden pattern."},{"head":"Fire and strife","body":"He described the cosmos as an ever-living fire that is kindled and put out in measures. He also said war or strife is the father of all things, meaning that tension between forces drives change and holds the world together, like the tension in a bow or a lyre string."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Heraclitus taught that nothing is stable and the world is pure chaos.","truth":"He stressed that change follows a fixed measure and a rational order, the logos. The famous summary 'everything flows' (panta rhei) comes from later writers, not his own fragments."},"startHere":"Read the fragments in any short modern collection of early Greek philosophy. Start with those on the river, the road up and down, and the logos; each is a sentence or two.","critics":"Aristotle accused Heraclitus of denying the law of non-contradiction, the principle that something cannot both be and not be in the same respect; Parmenides, his near contemporary, argued that change itself is impossible.","legacy":"His ideas of flux and the unity of opposites influenced Plato and the Stoics, who took over his fire and logos. Much later Hegel and Nietzsche both admired him as a thinker of becoming and conflict.","quote":null},"epicurus":{"life":["Epicurus was born in 341 BCE on the island of Samos to Athenian parents. He grew up just as Alexander the Great was conquering the Persian Empire. After Alexander's death in 323 BCE, his generals fought over his empire, and Greek cities lost much of their independence. Many people felt powerless in public life, and philosophies that promised peace of mind became popular.","At eighteen Epicurus went to Athens for military service. He studied philosophy, including the atomism of Democritus, and later taught at Mytilene and Lampsacus. Around 306 BCE he bought a house with a garden in Athens and set up a school there, known as the Garden. He taught and wrote there until his death in 270 BCE, after a painful illness, which he described in a letter as eased by memories of conversations with friends.","Epicurus was said to have written about 300 works, but almost all are lost. Three letters and a list of key teachings survive because a later biographer copied them. The Garden welcomed women and slaves, which was unusual. Its members lived simply, valued friendship and honoured Epicurus as a guide. His enemies spread rumours that he was a glutton, but his own writings praise bread and water."],"timeline":[[-341,"Born on Samos to Athenian parents"],[-323,"Arrives in Athens for military service"],[-311,"Begins teaching at Mytilene, then Lampsacus, c. 311 BCE"],[-306,"Founds the Garden in Athens, c. 306 BCE"],[-270,"Dies in Athens"],[1417,"Lucretius' poem on Epicurean philosophy is rediscovered in a German monastery"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Pleasure as the absence of pain","body":"Epicurus held that pleasure is the good, but he meant mainly freedom from bodily pain and mental disturbance. The highest pleasure is a calm mind, called ataraxia. A simple meal with friends, without worry, beats a feast followed by a hangover and debts."},{"head":"Sort your desires","body":"Some desires are natural and necessary, like food and shelter. Some are natural but not necessary, like fancy food. Others are empty, like craving fame or luxury, and they can never be satisfied. Cut back to the first group and you will be content easily."},{"head":"Death is nothing to us","body":"When we exist, death is not here; when death is here, we no longer exist. Since there is no experience after death, it cannot hurt us. Fearing it is like being upset about the years before you were born."},{"head":"A world of atoms","body":"Following Democritus, Epicurus taught that everything is made of tiny atoms moving in empty space. The gods exist but do not interfere in human affairs. This was meant to free people from fear of divine punishment."},{"head":"Friendship","body":"Of all the things wisdom provides for a happy life, Epicurus said, friendship is the greatest. Friends give security and joy, and a life shared with them is far better than wealth or status."}],"misconception":{"myth":"An Epicurean is someone devoted to luxury, fine food and indulgence.","truth":"Epicurus advised a simple life, limited desires and avoiding excess, because over-indulgence brings pain and anxiety later. His idea of pleasure was mostly calm and freedom from fear."},"startHere":"Read the Letter to Menoeceus. It is only a few pages long and covers his views on the gods, death, desire and the pleasant life.","critics":"Stoics and the Roman writer Cicero argued that pleasure cannot be the highest good, because a good life is about virtue and duty, and that simply lacking pain is not the same thing as pleasure.","legacy":"Through Lucretius' poem On the Nature of Things, Epicurean atomism influenced early modern science and thinkers such as Gassendi. His approach to death and desire is still used in modern ethics and in therapy-like practical philosophy.","quote":{"text":"Death, therefore, the most awful of evils, is nothing to us, seeing that, when we are, death is not come, and, when death is come, we are not.","src":"Letter to Menoeceus","tr":"R. D. Hicks"}},"diogenes":{"life":["Diogenes came from Sinope, a Greek trading city on the southern coast of the Black Sea, and lived in the 4th century BCE. He spent most of his life in Athens and Corinth, at a time when Macedon, under Philip II and Alexander the Great, was taking control of Greece. Socrates was executed in 399 BCE, when Diogenes was probably a child, and philosophers in Athens were arguing over his legacy.","According to ancient reports, Diogenes was forced to leave Sinope after a scandal involving the defacing of coins, possibly linked to his father, who ran the city's mint. In Athens he lived in poverty, reportedly sleeping in a large clay storage jar. Later stories say he was captured by pirates and sold as a slave in Corinth, where he died c. 323 BCE. His followers became known as Cynics.","Our main source is a biography by Diogenes Laertius, written about 500 years later and full of anecdotes, so few details are certain. The stories agree on his style: he taught by provocative action as much as words, such as eating in the marketplace or carrying a lamp in daylight to look for an honest man. 'Cynic' comes from the Greek for dog, an insult he embraced."],"timeline":[[-412,"Born in Sinope, c. 412 BCE (some sources suggest c. 404 BCE)"],[-336,"Alexander becomes king of Macedon; later stories place his meeting with Diogenes in Corinth"],[-323,"Dies in Corinth, c. 323 BCE"],[-300,"Zeno of Citium, who studied with Diogenes' follower Crates, begins teaching Stoicism in Athens, c. 300 BCE"],[230,"Diogenes Laertius writes the Lives, our main source for his sayings, c. 230 CE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Live according to nature","body":"Diogenes held that happiness comes from living simply and naturally, like animals do, needing only what nature requires. According to one story, when he saw a child drinking from its hands he threw away his cup, realising he did not need it."},{"head":"Customs are not sacred","body":"Much of what society treats as proper, such as wealth, status and etiquette, he saw as artificial and often foolish. He broke rules in public on purpose to show they had no real authority. Shaming people into thinking was part of his teaching."},{"head":"Self-sufficiency through training","body":"Diogenes trained himself to endure cold, hunger and insults so that nothing outside him could control his happiness. Reportedly he would hug cold statues in winter. The fewer your needs, the freer you are."},{"head":"Citizen of the world","body":"Asked where he came from, Diogenes is reported to have said he was a citizen of the world (kosmopolites). He refused to define himself by a single city, which was a striking idea at a time when one's city meant everything."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Diogenes lived in a barrel.","truth":"Ancient sources say he lived in a pithos, a large clay storage jar used for wine or grain. Wooden barrels were not common in Greece at that time."},"startHere":"Read Book 6 of Diogenes Laertius' Lives of the Eminent Philosophers, the section on Diogenes. It is a collection of short anecdotes and sayings, quick and often funny to read.","critics":"Critics have long argued that rejecting all social customs is parasitic: Diogenes depended on a city he scorned, begging from people who worked and followed its rules.","legacy":"Diogenes inspired the Cynic movement, which in turn shaped Stoicism, and his idea of being a citizen of the world is an ancestor of modern cosmopolitanism. He remains the classic image of the philosopher who mocks power and pretension.","quote":null},"marcus_aurelius":{"life":["Marcus Aurelius was born in Rome in 121 CE, when the Roman Empire was at its largest and, by many accounts, most stable. Emperors in this period chose and adopted their successors. Greek philosophy, especially Stoicism, was widely studied by educated Romans, and Greek was the language of philosophy even for Latin speakers. Philosophy was seen less as an academic subject than as training for how to live.","Marcus was from a wealthy family and caught the attention of Emperor Hadrian, who arranged in 138 that the next emperor, Antoninus Pius, would adopt him. He became emperor in 161, at first sharing power with Lucius Verus. His reign was difficult: a deadly epidemic, the Antonine plague, swept the empire from about 165, and he spent many years fighting Germanic peoples on the Danube frontier. He died in 180 while on campaign, and his son Commodus succeeded him.","From a young age Marcus studied philosophy and was strongly influenced by the writings of the former slave Epictetus. During his campaigns he kept notebooks in Greek, reminding himself of Stoic principles. These are what we now call the Meditations. They were not meant for publication, which is why they are repetitive, personal and honest about his struggles with anger, fatigue and other people."],"timeline":[[121,"Born in Rome"],[138,"Adopted by Antoninus Pius at Hadrian's direction, as future emperor"],[161,"Becomes emperor, sharing power with Lucius Verus"],[165,"The Antonine plague begins spreading through the empire, c. 165"],[170,"Campaigning on the Danube; writes much of the Meditations, c. 170–180"],[180,"Dies on campaign; his son Commodus succeeds him"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Focus on what you control","body":"Following Epictetus, Marcus reminds himself that other people's actions, illness and fame are outside his control, while his judgements and choices are his own. Being stuck in traffic is not up to you; how you respond is."},{"head":"It is our judgements that disturb us","body":"Marcus argues that events do not harm us; our opinions about them do. If you take away the thought 'I have been wronged', the hurt goes with it. This does not mean ignoring events, but questioning the story we tell about them."},{"head":"Everything passes","body":"He often reflects that famous emperors are forgotten and every life is brief. This is not meant to depress, but to free him from chasing reputation and to focus on acting well now."},{"head":"We are made for each other","body":"For Marcus, all humans share in reason and form one community. We are made to work together like hands or feet. So even when people are rude, his duty is to help them, not to hate them."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Marcus Aurelius wrote the Meditations as a self-help book for the public.","truth":"They were private notebooks written to himself, probably never meant to be read by others. Even the title is not his own; the Greek manuscripts call them simply 'to himself'."},"startHere":"Read Book 2 of the Meditations, which opens with his morning reminder about dealing with difficult people. It is short and gives a clear taste of his practical Stoicism.","critics":"Critics of Stoicism since antiquity, such as Aristotle's followers, argued that it wrongly treats strong emotions like grief as mistakes to be removed rather than fitting responses to real losses.","legacy":"The Meditations is one of the most widely read works of ancient philosophy and a key text in today's popular revival of Stoicism. Its methods resemble those of cognitive behavioural therapy, whose founders acknowledged a debt to the Stoics.","quote":{"text":"Begin the morning by saying to thyself, I shall meet with the busy-body, the ungrateful, arrogant, deceitful, envious, unsocial.","src":"Meditations, Book 2, section 1","tr":"George Long"}},"seneca":{"life":["Lucius Annaeus Seneca was born c. 4 BCE in Corduba (Córdoba) in Roman Spain, into a wealthy family, and was brought to Rome as a child. He lived under the first Roman emperors, from Augustus to Nero. Emperors held near-total power, and senators lived in danger of accusation, exile or death if they fell out of favour. Stoic philosophy was popular among the Roman elite as a guide to keeping one's integrity in such times.","Seneca became a famous speaker and senator. In 41 CE Emperor Claudius exiled him to Corsica. In 49 he was recalled to tutor the young Nero. When Nero became emperor in 54, Seneca served as one of his chief advisers and grew extremely rich. As Nero became more violent, Seneca withdrew from court in 62. In 65 he was accused of joining a plot against Nero and was ordered to kill himself, which he did.","Seneca was a prolific writer: essays on anger, mercy and the shortness of life; the Letters to Lucilius, written in his final years; and tragedies for the stage. His prose is lively and full of quotable lines. His great wealth and service to Nero sat uneasily with his praise of simple living, and he was accused of hypocrisy in his own lifetime. He admitted in his writings that he was not a sage, only someone trying to improve."],"timeline":[[-4,"Born in Corduba, Spain, c. 4 BCE"],[41,"Exiled to Corsica by Emperor Claudius"],[49,"Recalled to Rome to tutor the young Nero"],[54,"Nero becomes emperor; Seneca serves as a leading adviser"],[62,"Withdraws from public life"],[65,"Ordered by Nero to take his own life after a failed conspiracy"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Time is our most precious possession","body":"In On the Shortness of Life Seneca argues that life is long enough if we use it well, but we waste it on busyness, ambition and other people's demands. We guard our money carefully but give away our hours to anyone."},{"head":"Anger is a temporary madness","body":"In On Anger he argues that anger is never useful: it clouds judgement and causes more harm than the offence. His advice includes delaying your response, since anger cools with time, and remembering that you too make mistakes."},{"head":"Rehearse misfortune","body":"Seneca recommends imagining losses like poverty or exile in advance, even practising living simply for a few days. When hardship comes, it will not catch you off guard, and you will see that you can bear it."},{"head":"Philosophy is practice, not talk","body":"For Seneca, philosophy is a daily training of the mind, like medicine for the soul. In his letters he urges his friend to read a little, reflect each evening on his day, and judge himself by what he does, not by what he says."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Being Stoic, for Seneca, meant feeling nothing and keeping a stiff upper lip.","truth":"Seneca accepted that involuntary first reactions, like a shudder or tears, are natural. What he wanted to remove were passions built on false judgements, such as the belief that a loss is a disaster that ruins your life."},"startHere":"Read On the Shortness of Life. It is a short essay, easy to follow, and directly about how you spend your days.","critics":"In his own lifetime, as the historian Tacitus reports, opponents attacked Seneca as a hypocrite who preached simplicity while piling up a vast fortune under Nero; philosophers have also questioned the Stoic claim that only virtue is truly good.","legacy":"Seneca's essays and letters were widely read in the Middle Ages and the Renaissance, influencing Montaigne and others, and his tragedies shaped Elizabethan drama. He is central to the modern revival of practical Stoicism.","quote":null},"zhuangzi":{"life":["Zhuangzi, whose personal name was Zhuang Zhou, lived c. 369 to 286 BCE in the Warring States period of China. The old Zhou order had collapsed and powerful states fought constantly for control. Many thinkers, including Confucians, Mohists and legalists, competed to advise rulers on how to bring order. Zhuangzi stood apart from this competition, often mocking it, and seems to have had little interest in holding office himself.","According to the historian Sima Qian, Zhuangzi came from Meng, in the state of Song, and briefly held a minor post looking after a lacquer tree garden. Sima Qian also tells a story that he turned down an offer to become chief minister of the state of Chu. In a version of this story in the Zhuangzi itself (chapter 17), he says he would rather drag his tail in the mud like a living turtle than be a dead one honoured in a temple. Little else is known.","The book called the Zhuangzi has 33 chapters in the version edited by Guo Xiang around 300 CE. Scholars generally think the first seven, the Inner Chapters, are closest to Zhuangzi himself, and the rest were added by followers and others. The writing is unusual: funny stories, talking animals, strange characters and paradoxes, rather than straightforward argument. Zhuangzi appears in some stories as a witty, unconventional character."],"timeline":[[-369,"Born in Meng, state of Song, c. 369 BCE"],[-320,"Active as a thinker and writer, c. 320 BCE"],[-286,"Dies, c. 286 BCE"],[300,"Guo Xiang edits the text into the 33-chapter version read today, c. 300 CE"],[742,"Tang emperor Xuanzong honours the book with the title True Classic of Southern Florescence"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Everything depends on perspective","body":"A frog in a well thinks the sky is a small circle. Zhuangzi suggests that our judgements of big and small, right and wrong, beautiful and ugly depend on where we stand. A fish and a human disagree on what a good home is, and neither is simply right."},{"head":"The butterfly dream","body":"Zhuangzi dreams he is a butterfly, then wakes and wonders whether he is a man who dreamt of being a butterfly, or a butterfly now dreaming it is a man. The story questions how firmly we can know who we are and what is real."},{"head":"Effortless skill","body":"Cook Ding carves an ox so smoothly that his knife never dulls, because he follows the natural joints rather than hacking. After years of practice, skill becomes spontaneous. This is a picture of living in harmony with the Dao."},{"head":"The usefulness of the useless","body":"A huge gnarled tree is useless for timber, so no one cuts it down, and it lives a long life. Zhuangzi suggests that chasing usefulness and status can destroy us, while being 'useless' by the world's standards can keep us free."},{"head":"Accepting change and death","body":"Zhuangzi treats death as one transformation among many, like the change of seasons. In one story he drums and sings after his wife's death, explaining that after first grief he saw her death as part of the natural process."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Zhuangzi was simply a follower of Laozi, and both founded one clear school called Daoism.","truth":"The grouping of Laozi and Zhuangzi under the label 'Daoist' was made by Han dynasty historians centuries later. The two books differ a lot in style and focus, and Zhuangzi seems more interested in personal freedom than in advising rulers."},"startHere":"Read chapter 3, Nourishing the Lord of Life, with the story of Cook Ding, and the butterfly dream at the end of chapter 2. Both are short and give the flavour of his thought.","critics":"The Confucian philosopher Xunzi complained that Zhuangzi was blinded by Heaven, meaning nature, and did not understand the human world of effort, learning and social order.","legacy":"The Zhuangzi shaped Chinese literature, painting and poetry, and was a major influence on Chan (Zen) Buddhism. Its playful scepticism draws modern readers interested in relativism and perspective.","quote":null},"mencius":{"life":["Mencius, whose name was Meng Ke, lived c. 372 to 289 BCE in the Warring States period of China, when rival states fought endlessly for supremacy. He came from the small state of Zou, near Confucius' home state of Lu. Many schools of thought competed for rulers' attention, and Mencius saw himself as defending Confucius' teachings against rivals such as the Mohists and Yang Zhu.","According to Sima Qian, Mencius studied with followers of Confucius' grandson Zisi. Like Confucius, he travelled between states trying to persuade rulers to govern humanely. The book that bears his name records conversations with King Hui of Liang and King Xuan of Qi, among others. He held an honorary post in Qi for a time, but no ruler adopted his programme, and he eventually withdrew to teach.","The Mencius, in seven books, was probably compiled by his students, perhaps with his involvement. It shows him as confident, sharp in argument and willing to lecture kings to their faces. A famous later story says his mother moved house three times to find a good environment for him; this is tradition rather than established fact. The book's lively dialogues are one reason it became a classic of Chinese prose."],"timeline":[[-372,"Born in the state of Zou, c. 372 BCE"],[-320,"Advises King Hui of Liang, c. 320 BCE"],[-319,"Goes to the state of Qi and advises King Xuan, c. 319 BCE"],[-289,"Dies, c. 289 BCE"],[1190,"Zhu Xi publishes the Mencius as one of the Four Books"],[1313,"The Four Books become the basis of the Chinese civil service examinations"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Human nature is good","body":"Mencius argued that everyone is born with the beginnings of goodness. His famous example: anyone who suddenly saw a child about to fall into a well would feel alarm and compassion, not because they want a reward but because it is in their nature."},{"head":"The four sprouts","body":"Humans have four 'sprouts': compassion, shame, respect and a sense of right and wrong. These grow into the virtues of humaneness, righteousness, propriety and wisdom, if they are nurtured. Like plants, they can be stunted by a bad environment."},{"head":"The bare mountain","body":"Ox Mountain was once wooded, but people cut its trees and cattle grazed its shoots, so it became bare. Seeing it, you might think it was never forested. Likewise, a person who seems wicked has had their natural goodness cut down, not lacked it."},{"head":"Rulers must care for the people","body":"Mencius held that the people matter most and the ruler least. A ruler who oppresses the people loses the Mandate of Heaven, and removing such a tyrant is not murder, since he has ceased to be a true king."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Mencius thought people are already good, so moral education is unnecessary.","truth":"He held that people have only sprouts of goodness, which must be carefully cultivated through reflection, education and a decent environment, or they wither."},"startHere":"Read the passage on the child and the well, in Book 2A, chapter 6, then his debate with Gaozi about human nature at the start of Book 6A. Both are short and show his central argument.","critics":"Xunzi, a later Confucian, argued the opposite: that human nature is bad and goodness comes only through effort, ritual and teachers; the thinker Gaozi in the Mencius itself argues that nature is neither good nor bad.","legacy":"From the Song dynasty, Mencius was ranked second only to Confucius, and his book became one of the Four Books studied by every candidate for office in China for centuries. His view of human goodness remains central to Confucian ethics.","quote":{"text":"All men have a mind which cannot bear to see the sufferings of others.","src":"Mencius, Book 2A, chapter 6","tr":"James Legge"}},"nagarjuna":{"life":["Nagarjuna lived in India, probably in the 2nd century CE, most likely in the south. By then Buddhism was several centuries old and had split into many schools with detailed theories about the basic elements of reality. A new movement, Mahayana Buddhism, was emerging, with texts called the Perfection of Wisdom sutras that stressed emptiness. Nagarjuna gave this movement its sharpest philosophical defence.","Almost nothing is reliably known about his life. Later Buddhist biographies, written centuries afterwards, are full of legends, such as visits to the underwater realm of serpent beings called nagas to recover lost scriptures. Many scholars link him to the Satavahana dynasty of south India, since a surviving Letter to a Friend, attributed to him, is addressed to a king, probably of that dynasty. He founded the Madhyamaka, or Middle Way, school of Buddhist philosophy.","His main work is the Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way, about 450 short verses in 27 chapters. It is dense and argumentative, working through ideas like causation, motion, time and the self, and showing that each leads to contradictions if things are assumed to have fixed natures. Many other works are attributed to him; scholars accept only some, such as The Dispeller of Disputes, as genuine."],"timeline":[[150,"Born, probably in south India, c. 150 CE"],[200,"Writes the Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way, c. 200 CE"],[250,"Dies, c. 250 CE"],[409,"Kumarajiva translates the Verses with a commentary into Chinese"],[600,"Chandrakirti writes an influential commentary on the Verses, c. 600 CE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Emptiness","body":"Nagarjuna argued that nothing has svabhava, an intrinsic nature that makes it what it is all by itself. A table exists only in dependence on its parts, its maker and our concept 'table'. To say it is 'empty' is to say it lacks independent existence, not that it does not exist."},{"head":"Emptiness is dependent arising","body":"For Nagarjuna, emptiness and the Buddha's teaching of dependent arising are the same thing seen from two sides. Because things arise from conditions, they cannot have a fixed self-nature. And because they lack a fixed nature, they can change, which makes freedom from suffering possible."},{"head":"Two truths","body":"There is conventional truth, the everyday world of tables, people and causes, and ultimate truth, which is emptiness. The conventional is not false: we need it to live and to teach. But it should not be mistaken for a description of things as they are in themselves."},{"head":"A middle way between extremes","body":"Nagarjuna rejected both eternalism, the view that things exist permanently in their own right, and nihilism, the view that nothing exists at all. Emptiness avoids both: things exist, but only dependently."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Nagarjuna taught that nothing exists, so his philosophy is nihilism.","truth":"He explicitly rejected nihilism. Emptiness means things lack independent, fixed natures, not that they are unreal; he warned that grasping emptiness wrongly is like picking up a snake by the wrong end."},"startHere":"Read chapter 24 of the Fundamental Verses, on the Four Noble Truths, in a translation with notes. Here Nagarjuna answers the objection that emptiness destroys Buddhism and explains why it does the opposite.","critics":"Hindu philosophers of the Nyaya school, whose objections Nagarjuna answers in The Dispeller of Disputes, argued that if everything is empty then his own statements are empty too and cannot prove or refute anything.","legacy":"Madhyamaka became one of the two main schools of Mahayana philosophy and is central to Tibetan Buddhism and to Chan (Zen) traditions. Western philosophers increasingly study him for his views on causation, language and the self.","quote":null},"shankara":{"life":["Adi Shankara lived in India, probably in the early 8th century CE; a traditional date of 788 to 820 is also common, but many scholars now prefer c. 700 to 750. Hindu thought at this time was divided among many schools, and Buddhism and Jainism were serious rivals. One school, Vedanta, based itself on the Upanishads, ancient texts about the nature of the self and ultimate reality. Shankara became its most influential interpreter.","According to tradition, Shankara was born in Kalady in Kerala, became a renouncer as a boy, and studied under a teacher named Govinda. He is said to have travelled across India debating rivals and founding monasteries, and to have died young, at about 32. These biographies were written centuries later and include legends, such as miraculous debates and travels, so details of his life are uncertain. Even his dates rest mainly on tradition and on hints in his writings.","What is secure is his writing. His commentaries on the Brahma Sutras, the main Upanishads and the Bhagavad Gita set out a precise, closely argued system. A Thousand Teachings is an independent work, part in prose and part in verse, partly for teaching students. Hundreds of other hymns and texts are attributed to him, but scholars think many were written by later followers."],"timeline":[[700,"Born, traditionally in Kalady, Kerala, c. 700 CE (a traditional date is 788)"],[730,"Writes his commentary on the Brahma Sutras, his major work, c. 730"],[750,"Dies, c. 750 CE"],[1017,"Birth of Ramanuja, traditionally dated 1017, who would become his leading Vedanta critic"],[1893,"Swami Vivekananda presents Vedanta to the West at the Parliament of Religions in Chicago"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Atman is Brahman","body":"Shankara taught Advaita, meaning non-dualism. Your true self, Atman, is identical with Brahman, the single ultimate reality. The sense that you are a separate, limited individual is a mistake."},{"head":"Superimposition","body":"We project false features onto reality, as when someone in dim light mistakes a rope for a snake and feels real fear. In the same way we project individuality and change onto the one unchanging self. The cure is not to fight the snake but to see the rope."},{"head":"Levels of reality","body":"The everyday world of people, objects and duties is real at the practical level, and we must live in it properly. But from the highest standpoint only Brahman is fully real. The world is maya, an appearance that can be neither simply called real nor simply unreal."},{"head":"Liberation through knowledge","body":"For Shankara, freedom from the cycle of rebirth (moksha) comes from knowledge, not from rituals or good deeds alone. Study of the Upanishads under a teacher, with reflection and meditation, removes the ignorance that hides our identity with Brahman."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Shankara taught that the world is a pure illusion, so nothing we do matters.","truth":"He held that the world is fully real at the everyday level, where ethics and duties apply. It is only from the ultimate standpoint that it is seen as dependent on Brahman, an appearance rather than something independent."},"startHere":"Read the prose part of A Thousand Teachings (Upadeshasahasri). It is written as dialogues between teacher and student and is far easier than his long commentaries.","critics":"Ramanuja, the leading philosopher of a rival Vedanta school, objected that the ignorance that supposedly creates the world of many things cannot belong to Brahman, which is pure knowledge, nor to individual selves, which on Shankara's view are not ultimately real.","legacy":"Advaita Vedanta became the best-known school of Indian philosophy, especially after modern teachers like Vivekananda presented it to the world. Monasteries claiming descent from Shankara remain important in Hinduism today.","quote":null},"augustine":{"life":["Augustine was born in 354 CE in Thagaste, a town in Roman North Africa, in today's Algeria. The Roman Empire had recently become officially Christian, but older religions and philosophies were still strong. During his life the western empire weakened; in 410 the city of Rome was sacked by the Visigoths. When he died in 430, the Vandals were besieging his own city of Hippo.","His mother Monica was a devout Christian; his father became one only late in life. Augustine studied in Carthage and became a teacher of rhetoric, the art of persuasive speaking, in Carthage, Rome and Milan. For about nine years he followed Manichaeism, a religion that saw the world as a battle between good and evil powers. In Milan he read Platonist philosophy and heard Bishop Ambrose preach. In 386 he converted to Christianity, and was baptised in 387. He returned to Africa and became bishop of Hippo in 395 or 396.","Augustine wrote a huge amount: over a hundred books, hundreds of letters and sermons. In the Confessions he tells the story of his life up to his conversion, addressed to God, with an openness about his own faults that was unusual for the time. As bishop he spent much of his energy in debates with rival Christian groups, especially the Donatists and the Pelagians."],"timeline":[[354,"Born in Thagaste, Roman North Africa"],[373,"Reads Cicero's Hortensius and is drawn to philosophy"],[384,"Becomes professor of rhetoric in Milan"],[386,"Converts to Christianity in Milan"],[395,"Becomes bishop of Hippo, c. 395"],[397,"Begins writing the Confessions, c. 397"],[413,"Begins The City of God, prompted by the sack of Rome in 410"],[430,"Dies in Hippo during the Vandal siege"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The restless heart","body":"Augustine argued that humans are made for God, so nothing finite, such as money, pleasure or fame, can fully satisfy us. We keep chasing the next thing because we are really looking for something infinite."},{"head":"Evil as an absence","body":"If God is good and created everything, where does evil come from? Augustine answered that evil is not a thing at all, but a lack of good, as blindness is a lack of sight. Moral evil comes from free wills turning away from higher goods towards lower ones."},{"head":"What is time?","body":"In Confessions Book 11 Augustine notes that he knows what time is until someone asks him. The past no longer exists and the future does not yet exist, so time seems to live in the mind, as memory, attention and expectation."},{"head":"The two cities","body":"In The City of God he describes two communities defined by two loves: the earthly city, built on love of self, and the city of God, built on love of God. They are mixed together in history and will only be separated at the end of time."},{"head":"Grace","body":"Against Pelagius, Augustine argued that since the fall of Adam humans cannot become good by their own effort alone. They need God's grace. This view shaped later Christian debates about free will and salvation."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Augustine thought the body and the material world are evil.","truth":"He rejected that Manichaean view. He held that everything God created, including the body, is good; evil lies in a disordered will, not in matter."},"startHere":"Read Books 1 to 2 of the Confessions, about his childhood and the famous stolen pears, then Book 11 on time. They show both the story-teller and the philosopher.","critics":"Pelagius and his followers, such as Julian of Eclanum, argued that Augustine's views on original sin and grace undermine human freedom and responsibility, since we cannot be blamed for failing to do what we cannot do.","legacy":"Augustine was the most influential thinker in Western Christianity for a thousand years, shaping both Catholic and Protestant theology. The Confessions is often called the first Western autobiography, and his reflections on time and the self are still discussed by philosophers.","quote":{"text":"Thou madest us for Thyself, and our heart is restless, until it repose in Thee.","src":"Confessions, Book 1, chapter 1","tr":"E. B. Pusey"}},"avicenna":{"life":["Avicenna, in Arabic Ibn Sina, was born in 980 near Bukhara, in today's Uzbekistan, then ruled by the Persian Samanid dynasty. The Islamic world at this time was politically divided but culturally rich. Greek works of philosophy, science and medicine had been translated into Arabic, and scholars such as al-Farabi had built on them. Avicenna wrote mostly in Arabic, with some works in Persian.","According to his own autobiography, he mastered logic, mathematics and medicine as a teenager. He struggled with Aristotle's Metaphysics, reading it many times, until a cheap commentary by al-Farabi bought at a bookseller's stall made it clear. Having treated the Samanid ruler, he was allowed to use the royal library. After the Samanids fell, he moved from court to court, working as a physician and at times as a vizier, or chief minister, in Hamadan and later Isfahan. He died in Hamadan in 1037.","He wrote huge amounts, often at night after a day of official duties, and while travelling or in hiding during political trouble. His students recorded that he wrote parts of his great encyclopedia, The Book of Healing, without books to hand. The Canon of Medicine organised all known medicine of the time and became a standard textbook in the Islamic world and in Europe for centuries."],"timeline":[[980,"Born near Bukhara"],[997,"Treats the Samanid ruler and gains access to the royal library, c. 997"],[1025,"Completes The Canon of Medicine, c. 1025"],[1027,"Completes The Book of Healing, c. 1027"],[1037,"Dies in Hamadan"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The floating man","body":"Imagine you were created in mid-air, blindfolded, limbs spread so they touch nothing, with no sensations at all. Avicenna argued you would still be aware that you exist. So your self-awareness does not depend on your body, which suggests the soul is distinct from it."},{"head":"Essence and existence","body":"Knowing what something is differs from knowing that it exists. You can understand what a unicorn is without it existing. Avicenna concluded that, for ordinary things, existence is something added to their essence, which they must receive from a cause."},{"head":"The Necessary Existent","body":"Things that might or might not exist need a cause to exist. The chain of causes cannot simply go on with nothing that grounds it, so there must be a being whose existence is necessary in itself. Avicenna identified this being with God."},{"head":"Medicine as a science","body":"In the Canon, Avicenna set out rules for testing drugs, such as trying them on simple cases and checking that effects are consistent. He tried to make medicine systematic, drawing on Greek sources like Galen and his own experience."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the floating man passage from the opening of the psychology section of The Book of Healing, available in short anthologies of Islamic philosophy. It is a single thought experiment that shows his style of reasoning.","critics":"Al-Ghazali, in The Incoherence of the Philosophers, argued that Avicenna's views that the world is eternal, that God knows only universals, and that resurrection is not bodily contradict Islam and are not proven by reason.","legacy":"Avicenna's philosophy became the main framework for later philosophy in the Islamic world, especially in Iran. Translated into Latin, his metaphysics influenced Aquinas and other medieval Europeans, and the Canon was taught in European universities into the 17th century.","quote":null},"averroes":{"life":["Averroes, in Arabic Ibn Rushd, was born in 1126 in Córdoba, in al-Andalus, the Muslim-ruled part of Spain. During his life al-Andalus and North Africa came under the Almohads, a reforming Berber dynasty. Córdoba was a major centre of learning, where Muslim, Jewish and Christian scholars lived, though not always peacefully. Maimonides was born in the same city about a decade later.","Averroes came from a family of distinguished judges and trained in Islamic law, medicine and philosophy. Around 1169 the philosopher Ibn Tufayl introduced him to the Almohad caliph, who asked him to explain Aristotle's difficult works. Averroes spent much of his life doing so, while also serving as a judge in Seville and Córdoba and as court physician. In 1195 he fell from favour, was banished and some of his books were burned. He was restored shortly before his death in Marrakesh in 1198.","Averroes wrote commentaries on nearly all of Aristotle's works, at three levels of detail, from short summaries to long line-by-line explanations. He also wrote on law and medicine. His aim was to recover what Aristotle really meant, stripping away what he saw as distortions by earlier interpreters like Avicenna. In Latin Europe he became known simply as 'the Commentator', just as Aristotle was known as 'the Philosopher'."],"timeline":[[1126,"Born in Córdoba, al-Andalus"],[1169,"Introduced to the Almohad caliph and asked to explain Aristotle; becomes a judge in Seville, c. 1169"],[1179,"Writes The Decisive Treatise, c. 1179"],[1180,"Writes The Incoherence of the Incoherence, c. 1180"],[1182,"Becomes court physician to the caliph"],[1195,"Falls from favour and is banished; some of his books are burned"],[1198,"Dies in Marrakesh"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Truth does not contradict truth","body":"In The Decisive Treatise Averroes argued that the Quran itself commands people to reflect on the world, so philosophy is required for those capable of it. Since revelation and demonstration are both true, they cannot really conflict."},{"head":"Reading scripture wisely","body":"When a passage of scripture seems to conflict with a proven conclusion, Averroes held it should be interpreted non-literally. But this interpretation is for the learned and should not be broadcast to people who might be confused by it."},{"head":"Different arguments for different people","body":"Averroes distinguished three kinds of argument: strict demonstration for philosophers, debate for theologians, and persuasion through images and stories for the general public. Each kind can lead people to the same truth in a way they can grasp."},{"head":"Defending natural causes","body":"Against al-Ghazali, Averroes argued that denying real causal powers in things, such as fire's power to burn, destroys knowledge. If things had no natures, we could not know what anything is or predict anything."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Averroes taught a 'double truth': that something can be true in philosophy but false in religion.","truth":"He argued the opposite, that there is one truth reached in different ways. The 'double truth' idea was charged against some later Latin 'Averroists' by their critics, not stated by Averroes himself."},"startHere":"Read The Decisive Treatise. It is short, clearly argued, and sets out his view of how philosophy and religion fit together.","critics":"Thomas Aquinas, in On the Unity of the Intellect (1270), attacked the Averroist reading of Aristotle that there is a single intellect shared by all humans, arguing that it makes nonsense of the plain fact that this individual person thinks.","legacy":"His commentaries reshaped how Aristotle was read in medieval Europe and in Jewish philosophy. Today he is often invoked as a symbol of rational inquiry within Islam.","quote":null},"alghazali":{"life":["Abu Hamid al-Ghazali was born in 1058 in Tus, in Khorasan in north-east Iran. The region was ruled by the Seljuk Turks, who had taken control of Baghdad and governed in the name of the Abbasid caliph. The Seljuk vizier Nizam al-Mulk founded a network of colleges, the Nizamiyya, to train scholars. Philosophers following Avicenna were influential, and religious and political groups such as the Ismailis competed for loyalty.","Al-Ghazali studied in Nishapur under the leading theologian al-Juwayni. In 1091 Nizam al-Mulk appointed him to teach at the Nizamiyya in Baghdad, one of the most prestigious posts in the Islamic world. Around 1095, in a crisis he describes in his autobiography, he lost the ability to speak and teach. He left Baghdad, gave away his wealth and spent years as a Sufi, a follower of the Islamic mystical path, in Damascus, Jerusalem and Mecca. He later taught again briefly in Nishapur and died in Tus in 1111.","Al-Ghazali was a prolific writer on law, theology, philosophy and spiritual life. His autobiography, Deliverance from Error, describes his search for certainty with unusual personal honesty. He was a master of the philosophers' own methods: before criticising them, he wrote a clear summary of their views, The Aims of the Philosophers. Ironically, in Latin translation this summary led some medieval Europeans to think he was a follower of Avicenna."],"timeline":[[1058,"Born in Tus, Khorasan"],[1091,"Appointed to teach at the Nizamiyya college in Baghdad"],[1095,"Completes The Incoherence of the Philosophers; in crisis, leaves Baghdad"],[1100,"Writes The Revival of the Religious Sciences, c. 1096–1106"],[1106,"Returns to teaching at Nishapur"],[1108,"Writes Deliverance from Error, c. 1108"],[1111,"Dies in Tus"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The limits of the philosophers","body":"In The Incoherence of the Philosophers, al-Ghazali argued that philosophers like Avicenna claimed demonstrative proof for views they had not proved. On three points, including that the world has no beginning, he judged their views incompatible with Islam."},{"head":"Cause and effect","body":"When cotton touches fire and burns, we see one event follow another, but we never see the fire making the cotton burn. Al-Ghazali argued that the connection is not necessary: God creates the burning. The regular pattern is a habit God follows, not a law binding Him."},{"head":"The search for certainty","body":"In Deliverance from Error he describes doubting the senses, since the eye sees a star as a small coin-sized thing, and even reason. He says he was freed from this doubt not by argument but by a light God cast into his heart."},{"head":"Knowledge by experience","body":"Al-Ghazali argued that knowing about the spiritual path differs from living it, as knowing the definition of drunkenness differs from being drunk. Sufism, in his view, offers direct experience of God that theory alone cannot give."},{"head":"Logic is neutral","body":"Although he criticised the philosophers' metaphysics, al-Ghazali accepted their logic and mathematics as sound. He helped make Aristotelian logic a standard tool in Islamic theology and law."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Al-Ghazali single-handedly ended philosophy and science in the Islamic world.","truth":"This is disputed by most historians. He endorsed logic and mathematics, philosophy continued after him with figures such as Averroes, Suhrawardi and Mulla Sadra, and science flourished in some regions for centuries more."},"startHere":"Read Deliverance from Error. It is short, personal and tells the story of his doubts, his study of the philosophers and his turn to Sufism.","critics":"Averroes replied in The Incoherence of the Incoherence that al-Ghazali misunderstood the philosophers and that denying natural causes makes knowledge of the world impossible.","legacy":"Al-Ghazali is one of the most respected scholars in Sunni Islam, and The Revival is still widely read. His argument about causation anticipated themes later discussed by Hume.","quote":null},"maimonides":{"life":["Moses Maimonides, in Hebrew Moshe ben Maimon, was born in Córdoba, in Muslim Spain, probably in 1138 (some sources give 1135). His father was a respected rabbinic scholar. In 1148 the Almohads, a strict Islamic dynasty, took Córdoba and pressured Jews and Christians to convert or leave. Like many Jews, his family left. The Jewish world of his time was spread across Muslim and Christian lands and used Arabic as well as Hebrew.","The family wandered for years, living in Fez in Morocco for a time, then reached the Land of Israel in 1165 and settled in Egypt, near Cairo. There Maimonides became a leader of the Jewish community and answered legal questions from Jews around the world. After the loss of his brother, who had supported the family through trade, he worked as a doctor and later served at the court of Saladin's vizier. He died in 1204.","Maimonides was a scholar of law, a philosopher and a physician, writing in Arabic and Hebrew. His Mishneh Torah organised all of Jewish law into one clear code. His philosophical masterpiece, The Guide for the Perplexed, written in Arabic, was addressed to a student troubled by conflicts between scripture and philosophy. He also wrote medical treatises on diet, asthma and poisons."],"timeline":[[1138,"Born in Córdoba, al-Andalus, probably 1138 (some sources say 1135)"],[1148,"Almohads conquer Córdoba; his family later leaves"],[1165,"Reaches the Land of Israel, then settles in Egypt"],[1168,"Completes his Commentary on the Mishnah"],[1180,"Completes the Mishneh Torah, c. 1180"],[1190,"Completes The Guide for the Perplexed, c. 1190"],[1204,"Dies in Egypt"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Saying what God is not","body":"Maimonides argued that human words cannot describe God's essence. We can only say what God is not: not physical, not limited, not ignorant. Each denial brings us closer to understanding, like guessing an object by ruling out what it is not."},{"head":"Reading scripture with reason","body":"When the Bible speaks of God's hand or God sitting, it uses figurative language suited to ordinary readers. Since reason shows God is not a body, these passages should be read as metaphors. Scripture and sound philosophy, rightly understood, agree."},{"head":"Creation and the limits of proof","body":"Aristotle held the world had no beginning. Maimonides argued that neither its eternity nor its creation can be strictly proven by reason. Since the question is open, he accepted creation, as scripture teaches."},{"head":"Reasons for the commandments","body":"Maimonides held that the laws of the Torah have reasons, such as improving the body, the soul or society, even when we do not know them. Law is not arbitrary but aims at human perfection."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the introduction to The Guide for the Perplexed and the chapters on negative attributes in Part 1 (around chapters 50–60). They explain who the book is for and his approach to describing God.","critics":"Some contemporaries feared his rationalism undermined traditional faith, and in the 1230s his philosophical works were banned and burned in parts of France; the later Jewish philosopher Hasdai Crescas attacked his reliance on Aristotle's physics.","legacy":"Maimonides is the most influential Jewish thinker of the Middle Ages; the Mishneh Torah remains a central legal text, and the Guide influenced Aquinas and, later, Spinoza.","quote":null},"aquinas":{"life":["Thomas Aquinas was born c. 1225 at Roccasecca, near Naples, in the Kingdom of Sicily. Western Europe was then seeing the rise of universities, especially in Paris, and the arrival of Aristotle's works in Latin translation, often with commentaries by Muslim and Jewish thinkers. Some church leaders saw Aristotle as a threat to the faith. New religious orders, such as the Dominicans, were dedicated to preaching and study.","Thomas came from a noble family, who placed him at the abbey of Monte Cassino as a boy, hoping he would become its abbot. At the University of Naples he met Aristotle's works and the Dominicans, and joined them c. 1244. His family was so opposed that his brothers seized him and held him at home for about a year. He then studied under Albert the Great in Paris and Cologne, became a master of theology in Paris in 1256, and taught in Paris and Italy.","Aquinas wrote over eight million words in about twenty years, often dictating to several secretaries. He was large and quiet, and fellow students reportedly nicknamed him 'the dumb ox'. In December 1273 he stopped writing after an experience during Mass; according to a later account he said that everything he had written seemed like straw. The Summa Theologiae was left unfinished, and he died in 1274."],"timeline":[[1225,"Born at Roccasecca, near Naples, c. 1225"],[1244,"Joins the Dominican order; his family holds him captive for about a year"],[1256,"Becomes master of theology at the University of Paris"],[1259,"Begins the Summa contra Gentiles, c. 1259"],[1265,"Begins the Summa Theologiae, c. 1265"],[1273,"Stops writing after an experience during Mass"],[1274,"Dies at Fossanova on his way to the Council of Lyon"],[1323,"Declared a saint"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Faith and reason in harmony","body":"Aquinas held that reason can discover some truths about God, such as that God exists, while others, like the Trinity, are known only by revelation. Because both come from God, true reason and true faith cannot contradict each other."},{"head":"The Five Ways","body":"Aquinas gave five short arguments that God exists, based on features of the world we observe: change, causation, things that could fail to exist, degrees of goodness, and order in nature. For example, every change is caused by something else, and the chain must begin with a first unchanged source of change."},{"head":"Natural law","body":"Humans can work out basic moral rules by reflecting on their nature: we are inclined to preserve life, raise children, live in society and seek truth. The first principle is to do good and avoid evil. Human laws that contradict natural law are not truly laws."},{"head":"Essence and existence","body":"Building on Avicenna, Aquinas said that in every created thing, what it is (its essence) is distinct from the fact that it is (its existence). Only in God are they the same. So everything else depends on God for its existence at every moment."},{"head":"Happiness is seeing God","body":"Following Aristotle, Aquinas held that humans seek happiness. But no created good, like wealth or pleasure, fully satisfies, so perfect happiness lies only in knowing God directly after death."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Aquinas thought his Five Ways prove everything Christians believe about God.","truth":"The Five Ways aim only to show that there is a first cause, a necessary being and so on, which people call God. Aquinas held that truths like the Trinity can be known only through revelation, not proof."},"startHere":"Read the Five Ways in the Summa Theologiae, Part I, question 2, article 3. It fits on two pages and shows his style of setting out objections before answering them.","critics":"David Hume and Immanuel Kant attacked arguments like the Five Ways: Hume asked why a chain of causes needs a first cause at all, and Kant argued that the argument from contingency secretly relies on the weaker ontological argument.","legacy":"Aquinas became the leading theologian of the Catholic Church, and his synthesis of Aristotle and Christianity, known as Thomism, is still taught. His natural law theory remains influential in ethics, law and debates about human rights.","quote":null},"anselm":{"life":["Anselm was born in 1033 in Aosta, in the Alps of northern Italy. Europe at this time was changing fast: monasteries were the main centres of learning, and the Norman Conquest of England took place in 1066. Popes and kings were starting to clash over who had the right to appoint bishops, a struggle known as the Investiture Controversy, which shaped Anselm's later life.","As a young man Anselm left home and, in 1060, became a monk at the abbey of Bec in Normandy, where the scholar Lanfranc taught. He became prior in 1063 and abbot in 1078. His major philosophical works, including the Monologion and Proslogion, were written there. In 1093 he became Archbishop of Canterbury. His disputes with Kings William II and Henry I over church rights led to two periods of exile. He died at Canterbury in 1109.","Anselm was known as a gentle, thoughtful teacher, and much of what we know about him comes from a biography by his secretary Eadmer, who knew him well. He wrote short, carefully argued works, often in the form of meditations or dialogues with students. He preferred arguments that rely on reason alone, rather than quotations from authorities, while writing as a believer."],"timeline":[[1033,"Born in Aosta, northern Italy"],[1060,"Becomes a monk at the abbey of Bec, Normandy"],[1076,"Writes the Monologion"],[1078,"Writes the Proslogion, c. 1077–1078, and becomes abbot of Bec"],[1093,"Becomes Archbishop of Canterbury"],[1098,"Completes Why God Became Man while in exile"],[1109,"Dies at Canterbury"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Faith seeking understanding","body":"Anselm's motto was 'faith seeking understanding'. He did not try to reason his way into belief from nothing; as a believer, he wanted to understand what he already believed, using reason as far as it could go."},{"head":"The ontological argument","body":"God is that than which nothing greater can be conceived. Even a fool who denies God understands this idea. But a being that exists in reality is greater than one that exists only in the mind. So if God existed only in the mind, we could conceive something greater, which is a contradiction. Therefore God exists."},{"head":"Why God became man","body":"Anselm argued that human sin was an offence against God's honour that humans owe but cannot repay, while only God could repay it. So the one who repays must be both God and human. This 'satisfaction' theory shaped Western thinking about Christ's death."},{"head":"Freedom as rightness","body":"Anselm defined freedom not as the ability to choose evil, but as the power to keep one's will upright for its own sake. On this view, being unable to sin would not make one less free."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Anselm wrote the ontological argument as a proof to convince atheists.","truth":"The Proslogion is written as a prayer by a believer seeking to understand his faith. The argument does address 'the fool' who denies God, but its main purpose was reflection, not conversion."},"startHere":"Read chapters 2 to 4 of the Proslogion, then Gaunilo's short reply On Behalf of the Fool. Together they take about half an hour and show the argument and its first critic.","critics":"The monk Gaunilo replied that the same reasoning would prove that a perfect island exists; later Kant argued that existence is not a real property that can make a concept greater.","legacy":"The ontological argument is still debated nearly a thousand years later and has been defended or attacked by Descartes, Leibniz, Kant and modern logicians. His motto, faith seeking understanding, came to define scholastic theology.","quote":null},"machiavelli":{"life":["Niccolò Machiavelli was born in Florence in 1469, during the Renaissance. Italy was divided into rival city-states, kingdoms and papal territories, and from 1494 it was invaded repeatedly by French, Spanish and imperial armies. Florence itself switched between rule by the powerful Medici family and periods as a republic. Politics was violent, alliances shifted constantly, and the popes were themselves major military players. Humanist scholars were reviving the study of ancient Rome.","In 1494 the Medici were expelled from Florence. In 1498 Machiavelli became a senior official of the new republic, running diplomatic missions to the French king, the pope and the ruthless commander Cesare Borgia, and organising a citizen militia. In 1512 the Medici returned and he lost his job. In 1513 he was wrongly suspected of conspiracy, imprisoned and tortured, then released. He retired to his farm near Florence and began writing, though he never regained high office. He died in 1527.","In a letter of December 1513 he describes spending his days at the inn playing cards, then putting on formal clothes in the evening to 'converse' with the ancients through their books. From this came The Prince and the Discourses on Livy. He also wrote comedies, including The Mandrake, a history of Florence, and The Art of War. The Prince was published only in 1532, after his death."],"timeline":[[1469,"Born in Florence"],[1498,"Becomes a senior official of the Florentine republic"],[1502,"On diplomatic mission to Cesare Borgia"],[1512,"Medici return to power; he is dismissed"],[1513,"Imprisoned and tortured, then released; writes The Prince"],[1517,"Working on the Discourses on Livy, c. 1517"],[1527,"Dies in Florence"],[1532,"The Prince is published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Politics as it really is","body":"Machiavelli said he would write about how people actually behave, not imagined ideal states. A ruler who always acts as he 'should' among people who do not will come to ruin. Politics must be studied like the real world, not a sermon."},{"head":"Virtù and fortune","body":"Fortune, or chance, controls about half of what happens, he wrote, but the other half is up to us. Virtù is not moral virtue but a mix of skill, boldness and adaptability. Like building dykes before a river floods, a wise ruler prepares for bad luck."},{"head":"Better feared than loved","body":"It is best to be both loved and feared, but if you must choose, Machiavelli argued it is safer to be feared, because love is easily broken while fear of punishment holds. Yet a ruler must avoid being hated, especially by taking people's property."},{"head":"Free republics","body":"In the Discourses Machiavelli praises republics, especially ancient Rome. He argued that conflict between nobles and common people, if channelled through laws, kept Rome free and strong. Citizen armies, he thought, are more loyal than paid mercenaries."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Machiavelli wrote that the ends justify the means.","truth":"That phrase is not in his works. In chapter 18 of The Prince he observes that people judge rulers by results; whether that endorses any means is debated, and in chapter 8 he says that killing fellow citizens and betraying friends may win power but not glory."},"startHere":"Read chapters 15 to 18 of The Prince. In a few pages they set out his break with traditional moral advice to rulers and his famous views on fear, love and keeping promises.","critics":"From the 16th century many condemned him as a teacher of tyranny: the Catholic Church banned his works in 1559, and Frederick II of Prussia wrote the Anti-Machiavel; others, like Rousseau, argued The Prince was really a warning about how tyrants behave.","legacy":"Machiavelli is often seen as the founder of modern political science, because he separated the study of power from religion and moral ideals. His Discourses also shaped later republican thought, including, some historians argue, the American founders.","quote":null},"montaigne":{"life":["Michel de Montaigne was born in 1533 in the Château de Montaigne near Bordeaux, in south-west France. His family had become rich through trade and had recently joined the nobility. His adult life was overshadowed by the French Wars of Religion between Catholics and Protestants, which brought massacres and civil war. Europeans were also discovering the Americas, which raised questions about how other peoples lived.","Montaigne's father had him taught Latin as his first language, before French. He studied law and served as a magistrate in Bordeaux, where he formed a close friendship with the writer Étienne de La Boétie, who died in 1563. In 1571, aged 38, he retired to his estate and began writing in a tower library. The first edition of the Essays appeared in 1580. He then travelled through Germany, Switzerland and Italy, served two terms as mayor of Bordeaux, and kept revising his book until his death in 1592.","Montaigne made himself the subject of his book, writing about his habits, illnesses, reading and doubts. He kept adding to his essays rather than correcting them, so later layers sometimes disagree with earlier ones. He had a beam in his library painted with sayings, many sceptical, and his motto was the question 'What do I know?'. Though a Catholic, he was trusted by both sides and acted as a go-between in the religious wars."],"timeline":[[1533,"Born at the Château de Montaigne, near Bordeaux"],[1563,"His close friend Étienne de La Boétie dies"],[1571,"Retires to his estate to read and write"],[1580,"First edition of the Essays, in two books, is published"],[1581,"Elected mayor of Bordeaux"],[1588,"New edition of the Essays adds the third book"],[1592,"Dies at his château"]],"ideas":[{"head":"What do I know?","body":"Montaigne doubted that humans can reach certain knowledge, since our senses, reason and customs are unreliable. In his longest essay, Apology for Raymond Sebond, he asks how we can be so sure we are superior to animals. When I play with my cat, he wonders, who knows if she is not passing time with me more than I with her?"},{"head":"Custom shapes us","body":"Much of what we call natural or reasonable is just local custom. In Of Cannibals he notes that Europeans called the peoples of Brazil barbarians, while Europeans themselves tortured people over religion. Everyone calls barbarous whatever is not their own practice."},{"head":"Studying yourself","body":"Montaigne said he was himself the matter of his book. By looking honestly at one ordinary, changeable person, his moods, his kidney stones, his contradictions, he believed he was describing the human condition, since each person bears the whole form of humanity."},{"head":"Learning to live","body":"In an early essay Montaigne says that to philosophise is to learn to die, echoing the Stoics. Later he moved towards simply living well and calmly: enjoying the present, accepting our bodies and limits, and not fretting too much about death."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Montaigne's 'essays' were formal, structured arguments like a school essay.","truth":"The French word essai means an attempt or trial. His pieces wander, digress and change their minds; he was testing his judgement on a topic rather than proving a thesis."},"startHere":"Read Of Cannibals, from Book 1 of the Essays. It is short, lively and shows his curiosity, his scepticism and his sharp eye for European hypocrisy.","critics":"Blaise Pascal, who learned much from him, still condemned his 'foolish project' of portraying himself, and criticised his scepticism and relaxed attitude to death as unworthy of a Christian.","legacy":"Montaigne invented the essay as a literary form and influenced writers from Shakespeare and Bacon to Emerson and Woolf. His honest self-examination and doubts about European superiority make him feel strikingly modern.","quote":null},"bacon":{"life":["Francis Bacon was born in London in 1561, during the reign of Elizabeth I. England was a Protestant country, expanding its trade and exploration. Universities still taught mainly Aristotle's natural philosophy, but new discoveries, from the compass to the voyages to the Americas, suggested the ancients had not known everything. Bacon wanted a new start for learning, one that would build knowledge step by step from careful observation rather than from the authority of ancient books.","His father was Lord Keeper of the Great Seal, a top royal official. Bacon went to Trinity College, Cambridge, at about 12, then trained as a lawyer and entered Parliament. His rise was slow under Elizabeth but fast under James I: he became Solicitor General, Attorney General and, in 1618, Lord Chancellor, the highest legal office. In 1621 he was found guilty of accepting gifts from people with cases before him, removed from office and fined. He spent his last years writing and died in 1626.","Bacon was a lawyer and politician first, and wrote philosophy alongside a demanding public career. He planned a vast project, the Great Instauration, to reform all knowledge, but completed only parts, including the Novum Organum. His Essays, short and full of crisp advice, were very popular. According to a later story by John Aubrey, he caught his fatal illness while stuffing a chicken with snow to test whether cold preserves meat."],"timeline":[[1561,"Born in London"],[1597,"Publishes the first edition of his Essays"],[1605,"Publishes The Advancement of Learning"],[1618,"Becomes Lord Chancellor"],[1620,"Publishes the Novum Organum"],[1621,"Convicted of taking bribes and removed from office"],[1626,"Dies near London"],[1627,"New Atlantis is published after his death"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The idols of the mind","body":"Bacon listed four kinds of mental error. Idols of the tribe are biases all humans share, like noticing hits and ignoring misses. Idols of the cave are personal quirks. Idols of the marketplace come from loose use of words. Idols of the theatre come from accepting grand systems of philosophy."},{"head":"A new method of induction","body":"Instead of leaping from a few cases to grand conclusions, Bacon urged gathering many observations, organising them in tables, and especially looking for cases where the effect is absent. One clear counterexample can rule out a hypothesis, however many cases seem to support it."},{"head":"Knowledge for human benefit","body":"Bacon held that the point of studying nature is to improve human life, to relieve misery and increase power over nature. Inventions like printing, gunpowder and the compass, he noted, had changed the world more than any philosophy."},{"head":"Organised research","body":"In New Atlantis Bacon imagined Salomon's House, an institution where teams of researchers carried out experiments and shared findings. This picture of collaborative science helped inspire the founding of the Royal Society in 1660."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Francis Bacon secretly wrote Shakespeare's plays.","truth":"This theory was popular in the 19th century, but historians and literary scholars reject it; there is no good evidence for it, and the plays are firmly attributed to Shakespeare."},"startHere":"Read the aphorisms on the four Idols in Book 1 of the Novum Organum. They are short numbered paragraphs and still work as a guide to everyday bias.","critics":"Later philosophers of science, notably Karl Popper, argued that science does not start from neutral observation as Bacon suggested, but from bold guesses that are then tested; others note that Bacon undervalued mathematics, which proved central to the new science.","legacy":"Bacon became a symbol of the experimental method, honoured by the founders of the Royal Society and the Enlightenment. His catalogue of biases anticipates modern psychology's study of how we deceive ourselves.","quote":{"text":"Reading maketh a full man; conference a ready man; and writing an exact man.","src":"Essays, Of Studies","tr":"original English"}},"pascal":{"life":["Blaise Pascal was born in 1623 in Clermont, in central France. In his lifetime France became the most powerful state in Europe, and new science was overturning old views of the universe. Galileo and Descartes were reshaping physics and mathematics. In religion, French Catholics were divided, including over Jansenism, a strict movement stressing human sinfulness and God's grace, centred on the convent of Port-Royal.","Pascal was educated at home by his father and showed exceptional talent early. At 16 he wrote a respected essay on geometry. In his late teens he designed a mechanical calculator to help his father with tax accounts. He carried out experiments on air pressure and the vacuum and, with Pierre de Fermat, laid the foundations of probability theory in 1654. That November he had an intense religious experience, recorded on a note he kept sewn into his coat. Afterwards he devoted himself mostly to religious writing and died in 1662, aged 39.","Pascal was in poor health for much of his life. His Provincial Letters, published anonymously in 1656–57, defended the Jansenists with sharp irony against the Jesuits and are admired as models of French prose. He was working on a defence of Christianity when he died. His notes, on scraps of paper, were published in 1670 as the Pensées, meaning 'Thoughts'; their intended order is still debated."],"timeline":[[1623,"Born in Clermont, France"],[1640,"Publishes his Essay on Conic Sections, aged 16"],[1642,"Begins work on his mechanical calculator"],[1648,"An experiment he designed, carried out on the Puy de Dôme by his brother-in-law Florin Périer, shows air pressure falls with height"],[1654,"Works out probability theory with Fermat; has his religious experience in November"],[1656,"Begins publishing the Provincial Letters"],[1662,"Dies in Paris"],[1670,"The Pensées are published from his notes"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The wager","body":"Reason cannot prove whether God exists, but we must choose how to live. If you bet on God and are right, you gain infinite happiness; if wrong, you lose little. If you bet against God and are wrong, you lose everything. So, Pascal argued, the rational bet is on God."},{"head":"The heart has its reasons","body":"Pascal thought we know some things not by argument but by the 'heart', meaning direct intuition. We know we are not dreaming and that space has three dimensions this way, even though reason cannot prove it. Faith, too, is felt by the heart."},{"head":"Greatness and misery","body":"Humans are tiny and fragile in an infinite universe: a reed, the weakest thing in nature, yet a thinking reed. We are wretched, but we know we are wretched, and that knowledge is our greatness. Pascal saw this contradiction as a clue to the human condition."},{"head":"Diversion","body":"Most human unhappiness, Pascal observed, comes from not being able to sit quietly in a room. We fill our time with games, work and noise to avoid thinking about death and our condition. Today's endless scrolling fits his description well."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Pascal's wager claims to prove that God exists.","truth":"The wager is not a proof of God. It is an argument about what it is rational to do when reason cannot settle the question, and Pascal saw it as a way to move unbelievers towards seeking faith."},"startHere":"Read the fragment on the wager (often titled Infinite: nothing) and the fragments on the thinking reed and diversion in the Pensées. They are short and show both his argument and his vision of human life.","critics":"Denis Diderot objected that an imam could make the same wager for Islam; this 'many gods' objection asks which God to bet on, while others argue that believing just for a reward is not real faith.","legacy":"Pascal's work on probability helped found decision theory, and the wager is often described as one of the first uses of expected-value reasoning about a life choice. The Pensées influenced later religious and existentialist writers who stressed anxiety and the limits of reason.","quote":{"text":"The heart has its reasons, which reason does not know.","src":"Pensées, 277","tr":"W. F. Trotter"}},"gibbard":{"life":["Allan Gibbard was born in 1942 in Providence, Rhode Island. He grew up in post-war America and trained at a time when English-speaking philosophy was closely tied to logic, economics and decision theory, the mathematical study of rational choice. At Harvard, John Rawls was reviving serious philosophical work on justice and ethics. Gibbard's background in mathematics and his contact with Rawls both shaped the kind of philosopher he became.","He studied mathematics at Swarthmore College, graduating in 1963, then taught mathematics and physics in Ghana with the Peace Corps for two years. He completed his PhD at Harvard in 1971, supervised by Rawls. After posts at the University of Chicago and the University of Pittsburgh, he moved to the University of Michigan in 1977 and stayed until he retired in 2016. In 1973 he proved a result in voting theory now known as the Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem. His later books built a detailed theory of what words like 'ought' and 'rational' mean.","Gibbard works where ethics, economics and the study of meaning meet, and he often uses tools from decision theory to make loose ideas precise. His books are careful and demanding rather than popular. He gave the Tanner Lectures on Human Values at Berkeley in 2006, published as Reconciling Our Aims, and in 2009 was elected to the US National Academy of Sciences, an unusual honour for a philosopher."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in Providence, Rhode Island"],[1971,"Completes his PhD at Harvard under John Rawls"],[1973,"Publishes his theorem showing that voting systems can be manipulated"],[1977,"Joins the University of Michigan"],[1990,"Publishes Wise Choices, Apt Feelings"],[2003,"Publishes Thinking How to Live"],[2006,"Gives the Tanner Lectures at Berkeley, later published as Reconciling Our Aims"],[2009,"Elected to the US National Academy of Sciences"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Norm-expressivism","body":"Gibbard holds that calling something rational, or a feeling fitting, does not describe a fact in the world. It expresses your acceptance of a set of norms that allow it. If you say it makes sense to be angry with a friend who lied to you, you are endorsing rules that permit anger in cases like that."},{"head":"Judgements as plans","body":"In Thinking How to Live he treats 'ought' judgements as a kind of plan. Deciding what someone ought to do, even in an imagined situation, is like making a contingency plan for being in their place. The logic of planning then explains how such judgements can contradict each other and support arguments."},{"head":"Every fair vote can be gamed","body":"His 1973 theorem shows that any voting system choosing among three or more options, where every option can win and no single voter is a dictator, will sometimes reward voting tactically. For example, a voter may do better by backing a second choice to block a disliked winner than by voting honestly."},{"head":"Why humans have norms","body":"Gibbard offers an evolutionary story. Humans live by cooperating, and cooperation needs shared expectations about what to do and feel. Talking about what is fitting or required helps groups reach agreement, which is why normative discussion is so central to human life."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Begin with the opening chapters of Wise Choices, Apt Feelings (1990), where Gibbard sets out his central idea in comparatively plain terms before the technical machinery arrives.","critics":"The main challenge is the Frege-Geach problem, first pressed by Peter Geach against earlier expressivists: if 'lying is wrong' only expresses an attitude, it is hard to explain what it means inside 'if lying is wrong, then...', where nothing is being endorsed. Gibbard's planning account is an answer, but critics such as Mark Schroeder argue serious difficulties remain.","legacy":"With Simon Blackburn, Gibbard made expressivism one of the leading theories in metaethics, the study of what moral claims are. The Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem remains a standard result taught in economics and political science.","quote":null},"churchland":{"life":["Paul Churchland was born in 1942 in Vancouver, Canada. He grew up as computing and brain science were developing quickly. He studied at a time when philosophers such as Wilfrid Sellars and W. V. O. Quine were arguing that philosophy should work hand in hand with science rather than stand apart from it. These ideas, together with new findings about how neurons work, shaped his whole career.","He took his BA at the University of British Columbia in 1964 and his PhD at the University of Pittsburgh in 1969, under Sellars. He taught at the University of Manitoba for about fifteen years, then moved in 1984 to the University of California, San Diego, where he chaired the department and is now emeritus. His 1981 paper 'Eliminative Materialism and the Propositional Attitudes' made him the best-known defender of eliminativism. Later books used artificial neural networks as a model of how brains represent the world.","Churchland writes in a lively, direct style, and several of his books are aimed at students and general readers; Matter and Consciousness is still a widely used textbook. He has worked closely with the philosopher Patricia Churchland, and the two are often discussed together as founders of neurophilosophy. At San Diego he held joint appointments that put him alongside neuroscientists and cognitive scientists."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in Vancouver, Canada"],[1969,"Completes his PhD at Pittsburgh under Wilfrid Sellars"],[1979,"Publishes Scientific Realism and the Plasticity of Mind"],[1981,"Publishes 'Eliminative Materialism and the Propositional Attitudes'"],[1984,"Publishes Matter and Consciousness and moves to UC San Diego"],[1989,"Publishes A Neurocomputational Perspective"],[1995,"Publishes The Engine of Reason, the Seat of the Soul"],[2012,"Publishes Plato's Camera"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Folk psychology is a theory","body":"Everyday talk of beliefs, desires and intentions is how we explain and predict each other: 'She took an umbrella because she believed it would rain.' Churchland argues this is a theory, handed down from our ancestors. Like any theory, it could turn out to be false."},{"head":"Eliminative materialism","body":"Science has sometimes dropped old concepts rather than explained them, as chemistry dropped 'phlogiston'. Churchland argues that a mature brain science may do the same to beliefs and desires: rather than finding them in the brain, it may replace them with better categories describing patterns of neural activity."},{"head":"Brains work with patterns, not sentences","body":"Churchland argues that the brain represents things as patterns of activity across huge numbers of neurons, not as sentences stored in the head. A face or a colour can be treated as a point in a space of possible patterns, and learning is the slow adjustment of connections between neurons."},{"head":"Perception can be retrained","body":"In his first book he argued that what we perceive depends on the concepts we have learned. In principle we could learn to perceive the world directly in the terms of advanced science, for example feeling temperature as molecular motion."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Eliminative materialists claim that people have no minds or are not conscious.","truth":"Churchland accepts that there are mental processes, including conscious experience; he holds that they are brain processes and that our everyday words for them will probably be replaced by better scientific ones."},"startHere":"Read the chapters on reductive and eliminative materialism in Matter and Consciousness (1984). The book is short, clear and surveys the main theories of mind before arguing for his own.","critics":"Critics such as Lynne Rudder Baker argued that eliminativism defeats itself: if there are no beliefs, Churchland cannot believe or assert that eliminativism is true. Jerry Fodor and others replied that folk psychology is a highly successful theory with no sign of being overturned.","legacy":"Churchland helped make neuroscience and neural network models central to the philosophy of mind. Eliminativism remains the standard reference point for the most radical views about the mental.","quote":null},"block":{"life":["Ned Block was born in 1942 in Chicago. He trained at Harvard when functionalism, the idea that mental states are defined by what they do rather than what they are made of, much like software that can run on different hardware, was becoming the leading theory of mind. His teacher Hilary Putnam had helped launch it. The growth of computers and artificial intelligence made the question of whether a machine could think or feel newly urgent.","He completed his PhD at Harvard in 1971 under Putnam and taught at MIT from 1971 to 1996, chairing its philosophy section from 1989 to 1995. In 1996 he moved to New York University as professor of philosophy and psychology. His 1978 paper 'Troubles with Functionalism' challenged the theory he had been trained in. In 1995 he drew a distinction between two kinds of consciousness that became standard. His more recent work on perception led to The Border Between Seeing and Thinking (2023).","Block is known for arguing from experiments in psychology and neuroscience as well as from thought experiments, and for engaging closely with scientists. He gave the William James Lectures at Harvard in 2012 and the John Locke Lectures at Oxford in 2013, and received the Jean Nicod Prize in 2013. He has also served as a judge for the Loebner Prize, a contest based on the Turing test."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in Chicago"],[1971,"Completes his PhD at Harvard under Hilary Putnam; begins teaching at MIT"],[1978,"Publishes 'Troubles with Functionalism'"],[1981,"Publishes 'Psychologism and Behaviorism', introducing the Blockhead machine"],[1995,"Distinguishes phenomenal and access consciousness"],[1996,"Moves to New York University"],[2002,"Publishes 'The Harder Problem of Consciousness'"],[2023,"Publishes The Border Between Seeing and Thinking"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Two kinds of consciousness","body":"Block separates phenomenal consciousness, what an experience is like (the redness of red, the sting of pain), from access consciousness, information being available for reasoning, speech and action. They can come apart: you may hear a drill outside for some time before you notice it and can report it."},{"head":"The China brain","body":"Imagine the whole population of China, each person with a radio, organised to copy the functional organisation of a human brain for an hour. Functionalism says this system would have a mind, yet most people doubt it would feel any pain. Block uses this to argue that the right functional organisation may not be enough for experience."},{"head":"Blockhead","body":"Imagine a machine that passes the Turing test by looking up a pre-stored reply to every possible conversation. It would behave intelligently but plainly would not be intelligent. Block concludes that intelligence depends on how behaviour is produced, not just on the behaviour itself."},{"head":"The harder problem","body":"Suppose a robot works like us at the level of information processing but is built from very different materials. Block argues we may have no way, even in principle, to tell whether it is conscious, because all our evidence about consciousness comes from creatures made like us."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first part of 'Troubles with Functionalism' (1978), which introduces the China brain thought experiment. It is widely reprinted in introductory anthologies on philosophy of mind.","critics":"Daniel Dennett argued that the split between phenomenal and access consciousness is confused: once you explain access, nothing extra is left to explain. Others reply that our feeling of a rich experience beyond what we can report may itself be an illusion.","legacy":"Block's distinction between phenomenal and access consciousness became standard vocabulary in consciousness science. The China brain and Blockhead are classic thought experiments in debates about minds and artificial intelligence.","quote":null},"mccann":{"life":["Hugh McCann was an American philosopher whose career unfolded in the late 20th and early 21st centuries. At that time the philosophy of action, the study of what makes a bodily movement count as something a person does, was a busy field. Many followed Donald Davidson in thinking that actions are caused by beliefs and desires. McCann questioned this and also worked on the philosophy of religion.","He took a BA at Villanova University and an MA and PhD at the University of Chicago, writing his dissertation on basic actions, trying and bodily movement. He began teaching part-time at Northern Illinois University and spent most of his career at Texas A&M University, where he became head of the department and later professor emeritus. He also held visiting posts at the University of Virginia and the University of Notre Dame. He died in 2016.","McCann brought the same questions to both of his main fields: what it means for something to be done by an agent, and what it means for God to create. His essays on action were gathered in The Works of Agency (1998). He also wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's entry on divine providence and co-wrote a textbook on critical thinking."],"timeline":[[1979,"Publishes 'Nominals, Facts and Two Conceptions of Events'"],[1988,"Publishes, with Jonathan Kvanvig, an essay on God's conservation of the world"],[1998,"Publishes The Works of Agency"],[2001,"Publishes the Stanford Encyclopedia entry on divine providence"],[2012,"Publishes Creation and the Sovereignty of God"],[2016,"Dies"],[2017,"The volume he edited, Free Will and Classical Theism, is published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Decisions are not caused","body":"McCann argues that a decision is not an event pushed into being by earlier beliefs and desires. It is something you do. Your reasons explain why you decided, but they do not cause the decision the way one billiard ball moves another."},{"head":"Intentional through and through","body":"When you decide to raise your hand, you do not first decide and then separately intend the deciding. McCann holds that acts of will carry their intention within them. This is what makes them yours, rather than things that merely happen inside you."},{"head":"Creation is not ordinary causation","body":"McCann held that God creates and sustains everything at every moment, including our free decisions. Because this creating is not cause and effect between events in time, he argued, it does not compete with or undermine human freedom: God gives our decisions their existence without forcing them."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on divine providence, which is freely available online and lays out the main positions he was responding to.","critics":"Critics of uncaused decisions, such as Alfred Mele, press the 'luck objection': if nothing before a decision settles it, it is hard to see how the choice is controlled rather than random. Others argue that if God creates every decision, human freedom is lost however the creating is described.","legacy":"McCann is best known for a strong view of divine sovereignty combined with libertarian freedom, a combination that continues to be debated in philosophy of religion.","quote":null},"crispin_wright":{"life":["Crispin Wright was born on 21 December 1942 in Surrey, England. He studied in the 1960s, when Oxford and Cambridge were centres of the philosophy of language and when Michael Dummett was arguing that old debates about realism, whether things exist independently of us, were really debates about meaning and truth. Dummett's work on Frege and on realism shaped Wright's career.","He studied moral sciences at Trinity College, Cambridge, gaining a BA in 1964 and a PhD in 1968, then a BPhil at Oxford. He was a fellow of All Souls College, Oxford, until 1978, when he became Professor of Logic and Metaphysics at the University of St Andrews. There he founded the Arché research centre in 1998. He later held posts at New York University, the University of Aberdeen and the University of Stirling.","Wright is known for building research groups and collaborations, most notably his long partnership with Bob Hale on the philosophy of mathematics and the Arché centre, which brought together researchers in logic, language and metaphysics. His writing is technical and closely argued, and much of it engages with Wittgenstein and Dummett. He was elected a Fellow of the British Academy in 1992 and of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in 2012."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in Surrey, England"],[1978,"Becomes Professor of Logic and Metaphysics at St Andrews"],[1980,"Publishes Wittgenstein on the Foundations of Mathematics"],[1983,"Publishes Frege's Conception of Numbers as Objects"],[1992,"Publishes Truth and Objectivity; elected to the British Academy"],[1998,"Founds the Arché research centre at St Andrews"],[2001,"Publishes The Reason's Proper Study with Bob Hale"],[2004,"Publishes 'Warrant for Nothing (and Foundations for Free?)'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Numbers from a definition","body":"Wright revived Frege's plan to base arithmetic on logic. He starts from Hume's Principle: the number of Fs equals the number of Gs exactly when the Fs and Gs can be paired off one to one, as cups and saucers can. He argued that this principle, plus logic, yields basic arithmetic and shows that numbers are real objects."},{"head":"Truth varies by area","body":"In Truth and Objectivity Wright argues that debates over realism should be settled area by area. Whether a claim about comedy, morality or physics is 'true' in a strong, objective sense depends on features such as whether disagreements in that area must involve someone making a mistake."},{"head":"Entitlement without evidence","body":"Some basic assumptions, such as that there is an external world or that our senses mostly work, cannot be proved without circularity. Wright argues we are nonetheless entitled to trust them, because all inquiry depends on them and we have no reason to doubt them."},{"head":"Proofs that cannot transmit","body":"Wright argued that some valid arguments cannot pass justification from premises to conclusion. G. E. Moore's 'here is a hand, so there is an external world' fails in this way, because trusting the premise already assumes the conclusion."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Try the introduction and first chapter of Truth and Objectivity (1992), where Wright explains why debates over realism should be fought area by area. It is demanding but sets out his general outlook.","critics":"Against his Frege-style programme, George Boolos and Michael Dummett raised the 'bad company' problem: Frege's own Basic Law V looks just like Hume's Principle but leads to contradiction, so we need a principled way of telling good definitions of this kind from bad ones.","legacy":"Wright and Hale founded neo-logicism, the leading modern attempt to ground mathematics in logic. His ideas about entitlement and about truth varying by area remain central in epistemology and the philosophy of language.","quote":null},"savitt":{"life":["Steven Savitt is a philosopher of physics who works on the nature of time. His field grew from Einstein's special theory of relativity (1905) and Hermann Minkowski's 1908 recasting of it as a four-dimensional 'spacetime'. These theories imply that observers moving relative to each other disagree about which distant events happen at the same time. That raises hard questions about whether there is a single, shared 'now'.","Savitt has spent his career in the philosophy department at the University of British Columbia in Vancouver. In 1995 he edited Time's Arrows Today, a collection in which physicists and philosophers discussed why time seems to have a direction. From 2000 onward he published a series of papers on whether the passage of time can fit into relativity, and he wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on being and becoming in modern physics.","Savitt works at the meeting point of physics and metaphysics, testing philosophical claims about time against what relativity actually says rather than against everyday intuition. His papers are short and closely argued, often responding directly to other philosophers of physics. Some of his central proposals about local becoming were developed together with the philosopher Richard Arthur."],"timeline":[[1995,"Edits Time's Arrows Today"],[2000,"Publishes 'There's No Time Like the Present (in Minkowski Spacetime)'"],[2001,"Publishes 'A Limited Defense of Passage'"],[2002,"Publishes 'On Absolute Becoming and the Myth of Passage'"],[2006,"Publishes 'Presentism and Eternalism in Perspective'"],[2009,"Publishes 'The Transient nows'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Relativity has no universal now","body":"In relativity, two events that are simultaneous for one observer need not be simultaneous for another moving past. So there seems to be no single 'present' spread across the whole universe. Savitt asks what this means for the idea that only the present is real."},{"head":"The rivals may not really disagree","body":"Presentism says only present things exist; eternalism says past, present and future things all exist. Savitt argues that for this to be a real dispute there must be one clear sense of 'exists' that one side asserts and the other denies, and that such a sense is hard to find."},{"head":"Time can pass locally","body":"Savitt and Richard Arthur suggest that passage happens along the path of each object through spacetime, measured by its own clock. Your 'present' is a small region tied to your own history, not a slice through the whole universe."},{"head":"A defence of passage","body":"J. M. E. McTaggart argued in 1908 that time's passage is contradictory, since every event would be past, present and future. Savitt argued that this argument depends on using the word 'is' in more than one sense."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Being and Becoming in Modern Physics', which is free online and surveys the whole debate, including his own proposals.","critics":"Many metaphysicians, including defenders of presentism such as Dean Zimmerman, argue that the dispute between presentism and eternalism is a real one and should not be dissolved.","legacy":"Savitt has helped shape the debate over whether relativity leaves room for the passage of time, especially through the idea of local becoming.","quote":null},"steiner":{"life":["Hillel Steiner was born in 1942 and studied at the University of Toronto. He came to political philosophy in the 1970s, when John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) defended a welfare state and Robert Nozick's Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) answered with a libertarian case for a minimal state. Steiner took ideas from the libertarian side but drew egalitarian conclusions from them.","He spent his career at the University of Manchester, where he is now Emeritus Professor of Political Philosophy. His book An Essay on Rights (1994) won the Political Studies Association's prize for best book, and he was elected a Fellow of the British Academy in 1999. With Peter Vallentyne he edited two volumes that defined left-libertarianism as a school of thought.","Steiner is known for a precise, logical style. He treats rights almost like a mathematical system that must fit together without contradiction, and he defends the 'choice' or 'will' theory of rights, on which having a right means having control over someone else's duty. His work sits between the libertarian and egalitarian traditions and is engaged with by both."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born"],[1994,"Publishes An Essay on Rights, which wins the Political Studies Association's best book prize"],[1998,"Publishes A Debate over Rights with Matthew Kramer and Nigel Simmonds"],[1999,"Elected a Fellow of the British Academy"],[2000,"Co-edits Left-Libertarianism and Its Critics with Peter Vallentyne"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Self-ownership","body":"Like right-libertarians, Steiner holds that each person fully owns themselves: their body, talents and labour. No one may be forced to work for others, and what you make by your own efforts from your fair share of resources is yours."},{"head":"Natural resources belong to all","body":"Land, minerals and other things no one made are different. Steiner argues everyone has an equal claim to their value. Someone who holds more than an equal share owes the difference to others, for example through a fund paid out equally to everyone."},{"head":"Rights must fit together","body":"Steiner argues that a proper set of rights must be 'compossible', meaning all of them can be respected at once. If my right to use a field and your right to use the same field could clash, at least one of them is not a real right."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Libertarianism is always a right-wing view that accepts unlimited inequality.","truth":"Left-libertarians such as Steiner accept full self-ownership but hold that natural resources belong to everyone equally, which supports substantial redistribution."},"startHere":"Read the chapters of An Essay on Rights (1994) on justice and natural resources, where he sets out the case for equal shares of the earth.","critics":"G. A. Cohen argued that self-ownership itself is the problem: if the talented fully own their abilities, equal shares of resources cannot secure real equality between people born with very different capacities.","legacy":"Steiner is one of the founders of modern left-libertarianism, and his strict account of how rights fit together is a standard reference in the theory of rights.","quote":null},"unger":{"life":["Peter Unger was born in 1942 in the United States. He trained in the 1960s, when English-speaking philosophy focused on careful analysis of language and when Oxford was a world centre for it. He began his career working on scepticism, the view that we know far less than we think, and later moved into metaphysics, ethics and the philosophy of mind.","He took a BA in philosophy at Swarthmore College in 1962 and a doctorate at Oxford in 1966, where he studied under A. J. Ayer. He spent most of his career as a professor at New York University. His first book, Ignorance (1975), argued for radical scepticism. Living High and Letting Die (1996) turned to the ethics of giving to the poor. In Empty Ideas (2014) he argued that his own field had produced little real knowledge.","Unger is known for following arguments to startling conclusions, such as that he himself might not exist, and for changing his views openly over his career: from scepticism, to the view that many philosophical questions have no definite answer, to a defence of the soul. His writing is informal and full of invented examples meant to test the reader's intuitions."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in the United States"],[1966,"Completes his doctorate at Oxford under A. J. Ayer"],[1975,"Publishes Ignorance: A Case for Scepticism"],[1984,"Publishes Philosophical Relativity"],[1990,"Publishes Identity, Consciousness and Value"],[1996,"Publishes Living High and Letting Die"],[2006,"Publishes All the Power in the World"],[2014,"Publishes Empty Ideas"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Nothing is really flat","body":"Unger argued that some words set an absolute standard. Nothing is truly flat if something could be flatter. He claimed 'know' works the same way: knowing requires complete certainty, which we almost never have, so we know almost nothing."},{"head":"Distance does not excuse us","body":"Unger compared a person who will not help a bleeding stranger because it would ruin their car's leather seats with someone who throws away a charity letter asking for money to save children's lives. Most people judge the first harshly and the second hardly at all. Unger argues the cases are morally alike, and that we are misled by how vivid and close the victim is."},{"head":"We should give much more","body":"From this he concluded that well-off people are obliged to give away a large share of their wealth to life-saving charities, even at considerable cost to themselves."},{"head":"Doubts about ordinary things","body":"Using puzzles about vagueness, such as how many grains make a heap, Unger argued in 1979 that ordinary things like stones and tables, and even he himself, might not strictly exist."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Living High and Letting Die (1996), which sets out his paired cases and the challenge they pose for our everyday morality.","critics":"Frances Kamm argued at length that physical distance and nearness to a victim can make a genuine moral difference, so our differing reactions to the paired cases are not simply errors.","legacy":"Living High and Letting Die is, with Peter Singer's work, a standard philosophical source for the argument that the affluent should give much more to help distant people.","quote":null},"bonjour":{"life":["Laurence BonJour was born in 1943 in the United States. He worked in epistemology, the study of knowledge and justified belief, at a time when many philosophers were turning to 'externalism': the idea that a belief can be justified by being reliably produced, even if the believer cannot tell that it is. BonJour became one of the main defenders of the opposing, more traditional view.","He took degrees in philosophy and political science at Macalester College and a PhD at Princeton in 1969, supervised by Richard Rorty. He taught at the University of Texas at Austin and then the University of Washington, where he is now emeritus. His first book, The Structure of Empirical Knowledge (1985), defended coherentism. He later abandoned that view, and In Defense of Pure Reason (1998) argued for the power of a priori reasoning.","BonJour is known for clear writing and for changing his mind publicly when he came to think his earlier arguments failed, moving from defending coherentism to rejecting it. He also wrote a textbook for students, co-edited an anthology of philosophical problems, and co-wrote a debate book with Ernest Sosa that sets internalist and externalist views side by side."],"timeline":[[1943,"Born"],[1969,"Completes his PhD at Princeton under Richard Rorty"],[1980,"Publishes his clairvoyance objection to externalism"],[1985,"Publishes The Structure of Empirical Knowledge"],[1998,"Publishes In Defense of Pure Reason"],[2002,"Publishes the textbook Epistemology: Classic Problems and Contemporary Responses"],[2003,"Publishes Epistemic Justification, a debate with Ernest Sosa"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Seeing that it must be so","body":"BonJour argues we can grasp some truths by reason alone, such as that nothing can be red and green all over at the same time. You do not need to check every object; you can see why it must be true. This is a priori justification."},{"head":"No reasoning without reason","body":"He argues that we could not justify anything without a priori insight. Even learning from experience depends on it: moving from 'the sun has always risen' to 'it will rise tomorrow' requires seeing that the inference is a good one."},{"head":"The clairvoyant","body":"BonJour imagined a man with a reliable psychic power who has no idea he has it and believes, out of the blue, that the president is in New York. The belief is reliably formed and true, but BonJour argued it is not justified, because from the man's own point of view it is irrational."},{"head":"From coherence to foundations","body":"BonJour first held that beliefs are justified by fitting together into a coherent system. He later concluded that a coherent system could still float free of the world, and turned to a view on which experience provides a direct foundation."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the chapters on a priori knowledge in his textbook Epistemology: Classic Problems and Contemporary Responses (2002), written for students and giving his own views clearly.","critics":"Michael Devitt, a naturalist, argued against BonJour that there is no a priori knowledge: rational 'insight' is mysterious, and all our knowledge, including logic and mathematics, is ultimately tested by experience.","legacy":"BonJour's clairvoyant case is one of the best-known objections to reliabilism, and his work did much to revive serious defences of a priori knowledge.","quote":null},"michael_moore":{"life":["Michael S. Moore was born in 1943 in the United States. He works in the philosophy of criminal law, a field that asks when people deserve punishment and what makes someone responsible for a crime. In the mid-20th century, many lawyers and criminologists treated punishment mainly as a tool for deterrence or rehabilitation. Moore became a leading defender of the older idea that punishment should give people what they deserve.","He took an AB at the University of Oregon and a JD and SJD at Harvard Law School. He taught law at the University of Kansas, the University of Southern California, Berkeley and the University of Pennsylvania, where he co-founded an Institute for Law and Philosophy, and later at the University of San Diego. In 2002 he moved to the University of Illinois, holding joint posts in law and philosophy. He was editor-in-chief of the journal Law and Philosophy from 2002 to 2010.","Moore is known for writing long, systematic books that bring metaphysics and the philosophy of action into legal questions. He holds that law should be interpreted in the light of real moral facts, a version of natural law theory. His career has bridged law schools and philosophy departments, and he co-founded programmes in law and philosophy at several universities."],"timeline":[[1943,"Born"],[1984,"Publishes Law and Psychiatry"],[1989,"Becomes professor of law and philosophy at the University of Pennsylvania"],[1993,"Publishes Act and Crime"],[1997,"Publishes Placing Blame"],[2002,"Moves to the University of Illinois"],[2009,"Publishes Causation and Responsibility"],[2020,"Publishes Mechanical Choices"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Punishment because it is deserved","body":"Moore is a retributivist: the main reason to punish a guilty person is that they deserve it. Deterring others may be a welcome side effect, but it is not what justifies the punishment."},{"head":"The argument from guilt","body":"Moore asks you to imagine committing a terrible crime yourself. Most people would feel they deserved to suffer for it. If we take that feeling seriously for ourselves, he argues, we should take it seriously for others too."},{"head":"Results matter","body":"Moore argues that a person who kills deserves more punishment than one who tries to kill and misses by chance. What actually happens in the world is part of what we are responsible for, not just what we intended."},{"head":"Actions and causes","body":"In Act and Crime and Causation and Responsibility he argues that a criminal act is a willed bodily movement, and that legal responsibility should rest on real causal relations, not on policy choices dressed up as causation."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Retributivism is just a polite name for revenge.","truth":"Moore separates desert from the urge for revenge. He warns that resentment can distort our judgements and holds that punishment must be limited to what the offender actually deserves."},"startHere":"Read his essay 'The Moral Worth of Retribution' (1987), reprinted in Placing Blame, which gives the argument from guilt in plain terms.","critics":"Larry Alexander and Kimberly Kessler Ferzan argued against Moore that results should not affect punishment: a would-be killer who misses by luck is just as culpable as one who succeeds, so they should be punished equally.","legacy":"Moore is one of the leading modern defenders of retributivism and helped make the philosophy of action central to criminal law theory.","quote":null},"hursthouse":{"life":["Rosalind Hursthouse was born on 10 November 1943 in Bristol, England, and was educated in New Zealand. In mid-20th-century English-speaking ethics, the main theories were utilitarianism, which judges actions by their results, and Kantian ethics, which judges them by duties and rules. Elizabeth Anscombe and Philippa Foot argued for a return to Aristotle's focus on character, and Hursthouse developed their ideas into a full theory.","She took a BA and MA at the University of Auckland, then a BPhil and DPhil at Somerville College, Oxford, finishing in 1974. In 1975 she joined the Open University in Britain, where she taught for about 25 years and was head of department from 1991 to 1997. In 2002 she became Professor of Philosophy at the University of Auckland, later becoming professor emerita. Her On Virtue Ethics (1999) is a standard statement of the theory.","Hursthouse is known for showing how virtue ethics applies to real moral issues, such as abortion and the treatment of animals, and for writing textbooks for Open University students as well as research. With Glen Pettigrove she wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on virtue ethics. She was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of New Zealand in 2016."],"timeline":[[1943,"Born in Bristol, England"],[1974,"Completes her DPhil at Oxford"],[1975,"Joins the Open University"],[1991,"Publishes 'Virtue Theory and Abortion'"],[1999,"Publishes On Virtue Ethics"],[2000,"Publishes Ethics, Humans and Other Animals"],[2002,"Becomes Professor of Philosophy at the University of Auckland"],[2016,"Elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of New Zealand"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Right action and the virtuous person","body":"Hursthouse defines a right action as what a virtuous person would characteristically do in the circumstances. Instead of starting with rules or results, ethics starts with what kind of person you should be: honest, kind, courageous, fair."},{"head":"Virtue ethics does guide action","body":"Each virtue and vice gives a rule of thumb: do what is honest, do not do what is cruel or cowardly. Hursthouse calls these v-rules and argues they guide action as well as other theories' rules do."},{"head":"Abortion and character","body":"Rather than asking only about the status of the fetus or the woman's rights, Hursthouse asks what a decision shows about the person. A choice made callously or light-mindedly can be wrong even if it violates no one's rights, while one made seriously in hard circumstances may not be."},{"head":"Virtues help humans flourish","body":"Following Foot, she argues that virtues are the traits a human being needs to live well as a social, rational animal, much as a wolf needs to hunt with its pack. This gives virtue ethics an objective basis in human nature."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Virtue ethics is only about character and cannot tell you what to do.","truth":"Hursthouse built a definition of right action from the virtues and showed how virtue and vice terms give concrete guidance, including on contested issues like abortion."},"startHere":"Read 'Virtue Theory and Abortion' (1991), a short article showing how virtue ethics handles a real and divisive moral issue.","critics":"Robert Johnson argued that some things we ought to do, such as working to overcome our own vices, are things a fully virtuous person would never do, so 'what a virtuous person would do' cannot define right action for the rest of us.","legacy":"On Virtue Ethics became a standard account of neo-Aristotelian virtue ethics, and Hursthouse's work helped make virtue ethics one of the three main approaches in normative ethics.","quote":null},"pchurchland":{"life":["Patricia Churchland was born on 16 July 1943 in Oliver, British Columbia, Canada. She came to philosophy when it was largely separate from brain science, and when many philosophers held that the mind could be studied without looking at the brain at all. From the 1970s she argued that this was a mistake and that philosophers of mind should learn neuroscience.","She studied at the University of British Columbia, the University of Pittsburgh and Somerville College, Oxford. She taught at the University of Manitoba from 1969 to 1984, then moved to the University of California, San Diego, where she later chaired the department and is now emerita. She has also been an adjunct professor at the Salk Institute since 1989. Neurophilosophy (1986) named and launched a field. She received a MacArthur Fellowship in 1991.","Churchland is known for learning laboratory neuroscience herself and for working with scientists, including Terrence Sejnowski, with whom she wrote The Computational Brain. She often works with the philosopher Paul Churchland. Her later books, such as Braintrust, Touching a Nerve and Conscience, are written for general readers and apply brain science to morality and the self."],"timeline":[[1943,"Born in Oliver, British Columbia"],[1969,"Begins teaching at the University of Manitoba"],[1984,"Moves to UC San Diego"],[1986,"Publishes Neurophilosophy"],[1991,"Receives a MacArthur Fellowship"],[1992,"Publishes The Computational Brain with Terrence Sejnowski"],[2011,"Publishes Braintrust"],[2019,"Publishes Conscience"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Study the brain to understand the mind","body":"Churchland argues that the mind is what the brain does, so questions about memory, perception, free will and the self cannot be settled without neuroscience. Philosophy and brain science should develop together, each correcting the other."},{"head":"Everyday psychology may be revised","body":"Our everyday categories, such as belief and desire, may be refined or even replaced as brain science advances, in the way 'heat' came to be understood as molecular motion."},{"head":"Morality grows from caring","body":"In Braintrust she argues that morality is rooted in brain systems for attachment that evolved in mammals for caring for offspring. Hormones such as oxytocin help extend that care to mates, kin and wider groups. Moral rules build on this basic urge to care."},{"head":"Doubts about the hard problem","body":"Churchland is sceptical of claims that consciousness poses a special problem that science can never solve. She argues that such claims rest on our present ignorance, not on a proof of impossibility."}],"misconception":{"myth":"If neuroscience explains morality in terms of brain chemistry, then morality is an illusion.","truth":"Churchland argues the reverse: understanding the brain basis of caring explains why morality matters so much to us, without showing that it is unreal."},"startHere":"Read Touching a Nerve (2013), written for general readers, which explores what brain science means for the self, free will and death.","critics":"Jerry Fodor argued that psychology cannot be reduced to neuroscience, because the same mental state could be realised in very different physical systems, so it must be studied at its own level.","legacy":"Churchland founded neurophilosophy as a field, and it is now normal for philosophers of mind to engage directly with neuroscience.","quote":null},"watson":{"life":["Gary Watson is an American philosopher of action and moral responsibility. He began publishing in the 1970s, soon after Harry Frankfurt's work on freedom of the will (1971) and P. F. Strawson's 'Freedom and Resentment' (1962). Frankfurt had suggested that freedom depends on how your desires relate to each other; Strawson had tied responsibility to feelings like resentment and gratitude. Watson's work responded to both.","His 1975 paper 'Free Agency' became a classic, and he followed it with influential papers on weakness of will, on what makes someone a fitting target of blame, and on the 'two faces' of responsibility. He taught at several Californian universities, finishing his career at the University of Southern California. His essays were collected in Agency and Answerability (2004), and he edited the widely used anthology Free Will.","Watson is known for careful, essay-length work rather than big books, and for revising his own earlier views when he thought they failed. He was elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in 2014, and a volume of Oxford Studies in Agency and Responsibility was devoted to discussion of his work by other philosophers."],"timeline":[[1975,"Publishes 'Free Agency'"],[1977,"Publishes 'Skepticism about Weakness of Will'"],[1982,"Edits the anthology Free Will"],[1987,"Publishes 'Responsibility and the Limits of Evil'"],[1996,"Publishes 'Two Faces of Responsibility'"],[2004,"Publishes Agency and Answerability"],[2014,"Elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Valuing versus wanting","body":"Watson distinguishes what you value, your judgements about what is good, from what merely moves you. You act freely when your actions flow from your values. Someone who, in a fit of rage, feels like smashing a phone but does not value doing it is unfree to the extent that the urge wins."},{"head":"Two faces of responsibility","body":"Watson separates two ideas. An action is attributable to you if it expresses who you are and what you care about. You are accountable if it is fair to hold you to demands and to blame or sanction you. The two can come apart."},{"head":"The limits of blame","body":"Watson discussed a real murderer, Robert Harris, whose crimes were horrifying but whose childhood was one of terrible abuse. Learning his history makes our anger waver. Watson used the case to show tensions in tying responsibility to our reactive feelings."},{"head":"Weakness or compulsion?","body":"When someone gives in to a desire against their better judgement, we call it weakness if they could have resisted and compulsion if they could not. Watson argued it is surprisingly hard to say what this difference consists in."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Free Agency' (1975), a short paper that sets out the valuing versus wanting distinction clearly; it is reprinted in his anthology Free Will.","critics":"Alfred Mele and others argued that views like Watson's ignore history: if someone's values were implanted by brainwashing, they would act from their values yet seem unfree. Watson himself later admitted that valuing and desiring cannot be separated as cleanly as his early paper suggested.","legacy":"Watson's distinction between attributability and accountability is now standard in debates on moral responsibility, and 'Free Agency' remains one of the most cited papers on free will.","quote":null},"adams_mm":{"life":["Marilyn McCord Adams was born on 12 October 1943 in Oak Park, Illinois. She worked in two fields that were often kept apart in her time: medieval philosophy and the analytic philosophy of religion. From the 1970s, philosophers such as Alvin Plantinga were reviving rigorous debate over God's existence and the problem of evil, and Adams joined that debate with deep knowledge of medieval thinkers.","She studied at the University of Illinois and took a PhD at Cornell in 1967, later adding a ThM from Princeton Theological Seminary. She taught philosophy at UCLA from 1972 to 1993 and was ordained an Episcopal priest in 1987. She then taught historical theology at Yale Divinity School, and from 2004 to 2009 was Regius Professor of Divinity at Oxford, the first woman to hold the post. She later taught at the University of North Carolina and Rutgers. She died on 22 March 2017 in Princeton.","Adams combined scholarship with ministry, serving as a priest and as a canon of Christ Church, Oxford. She was married to the philosopher Robert Merrihew Adams, with whom she edited The Problem of Evil (1990). Her two-volume William Ockham is a major study of that thinker, and she served as president of the Society of Christian Philosophers."],"timeline":[[1943,"Born in Oak Park, Illinois"],[1967,"Completes her PhD at Cornell"],[1972,"Begins teaching at UCLA"],[1987,"Publishes William Ockham; ordained an Episcopal priest"],[1999,"Publishes Horrendous Evils and the Goodness of God"],[2004,"Becomes Regius Professor of Divinity at Oxford"],[2006,"Publishes Christ and Horrors"],[2017,"Dies in Princeton, New Jersey"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Horrendous evils","body":"Adams focused on the worst evils: those so terrible that taking part in them, as victim or perpetrator, gives a person reason to doubt their life could be worth living. Examples include torture and the betrayal of one's deepest loyalties."},{"head":"Good for each person","body":"Many responses to the problem of evil point to a greater good overall, such as free will or a richer world. Adams argued this is not enough: a good God must be good to each individual, so each person's life must be a great good for them on the whole."},{"head":"Defeating horrors","body":"Adams argued that God can defeat horrors by bringing the sufferer into a relationship with God so good that the horror becomes part of a meaningful whole. She saw God's own suffering in Christ as a way of identifying with victims."},{"head":"No one is lost","body":"Because God must be good to every person, Adams held that in the end all people will be saved. A God who left anyone in a life dominated by horror would not be good to them."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read her article 'Horrendous Evils and the Goodness of God' (1989), short and widely reprinted, which sets out the core of her view.","critics":"Critics, including many atheist philosophers of religion, argue that appealing to goods in an afterlife no one can observe makes her view impossible to test, and that even a later good does not explain why God permits horrors in the first place.","legacy":"Adams changed the debate on the problem of evil by insisting it be faced from the point of view of each individual sufferer, and her Ockham scholarship remains standard.","quote":null},"blackburn":{"life":["Simon Blackburn was born on 12 July 1944 in Chipping Sodbury, England. He trained when English-speaking moral philosophy was split between realists, who held that moral facts exist independently of us, and emotivists, who held that moral claims merely express feelings. Blackburn took inspiration from David Hume and set out to show how a view based on attitudes could still make sense of moral truth.","He studied at Trinity College, Cambridge, graduating in 1965, and took his doctorate at Churchill College in 1969. He then spent about two decades as a fellow of Pembroke College, Oxford, and edited the journal Mind from 1984 to 1990. He moved to the University of North Carolina in 1990 and returned to Cambridge as professor of philosophy from 2001 until 2011. He was president of the Aristotelian Society in 2009–2010.","Blackburn writes for general readers as well as specialists. His Oxford Dictionary of Philosophy and his introduction Think are widely used, and he has written short books on lust, truth, Hume and Plato's Republic. He often appears in newspapers and broadcasts, and he is a patron of Humanists UK. He was elected a Fellow of the British Academy in 2002."],"timeline":[[1944,"Born in Chipping Sodbury, England"],[1969,"Completes his doctorate at Cambridge"],[1984,"Publishes Spreading the Word; becomes editor of Mind"],[1990,"Moves to the University of North Carolina"],[1994,"Publishes The Oxford Dictionary of Philosophy"],[1998,"Publishes Ruling Passions"],[1999,"Publishes Think"],[2001,"Returns to Cambridge as professor of philosophy"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Projecting attitudes","body":"Following Hume, Blackburn holds that when we call something cruel or wrong we are not detecting a property out there, as we detect colour. We are voicing an attitude of disapproval, which we then 'project' onto the world in the way we talk."},{"head":"Quasi-realism","body":"Blackburn's distinctive aim is to show that, even so, we are entitled to talk as realists do: to say moral claims are true or false, that we can be mistaken, and that some views are better than others. All this, he argues, can be explained in terms of attitudes."},{"head":"Wrong whatever we think","body":"Someone might object that on his view kicking dogs for fun would be fine if we all approved of it. Blackburn replies that saying 'it would still be wrong' is itself a moral attitude, and one he holds. So his view does not make morality depend on our opinions."},{"head":"An argument from supervenience","body":"Moral facts always go with natural facts: two situations alike in every natural way cannot differ in rightness. Blackburn argued that realists struggle to explain why this must hold, while his view explains it easily."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Expressivists like Blackburn think morality is just personal opinion, so anything goes.","truth":"Blackburn holds that we can argue, improve our moral views and rightly condemn others; his theory is about what moral claims are, not a licence for relativism."},"startHere":"Think (1999) is his introduction to philosophy for beginners. For his own view of ethics, Being Good (2001) is short and non-technical.","critics":"Peter Geach's Frege-Geach problem asks how attitude-expressing claims can work inside 'if... then' arguments. James Dreier raised the problem of 'creeping minimalism': if quasi-realism succeeds, it becomes hard to say how it differs from realism at all.","legacy":"Blackburn's quasi-realism is one of the leading positions in metaethics, and his books and dictionary have introduced philosophy to a very wide readership.","quote":null},"davis_angela":{"life":["Angela Y. Davis was born on 26 January 1944 in Birmingham, Alabama, then one of the most racially segregated cities in the United States. Her neighbourhood was the target of so many racist bombings that it was nicknamed 'Dynamite Hill'. The civil rights movement and later the Black Power movement formed the background to her early life and thought.","She graduated from Brandeis University in 1965, where she studied with the philosopher Herbert Marcuse, then did graduate work in Frankfurt and took an MA at UC San Diego in 1968. In 1969 the University of California fired her from UCLA for being a member of the Communist Party; a court overturned this, but she was dismissed again in 1970. That year guns registered to her were used in a deadly courtroom attack. She was charged, spent many months in jail and was acquitted by a jury in 1972. She ran for US vice-president for the Communist Party in 1980 and 1984.","Davis taught at UC Santa Cruz from 1991, directing its feminist studies programme, and retired in 2008 as distinguished professor emerita. In 1997 she co-founded Critical Resistance, which campaigns to abolish what it calls the prison-industrial complex. She combines academic writing with public speaking and activism, and many of her books are collected lectures and interviews aimed at a general audience."],"timeline":[[1944,"Born in Birmingham, Alabama"],[1965,"Graduates from Brandeis University"],[1970,"Dismissed from UCLA; charged after a courtroom attack and arrested"],[1972,"Acquitted of all charges"],[1981,"Publishes Women, Race & Class"],[1991,"Joins UC Santa Cruz"],[1997,"Co-founds Critical Resistance"],[2003,"Publishes Are Prisons Obsolete?"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Race, class and gender together","body":"In Women, Race & Class Davis argues that the experiences of Black women cannot be understood by looking at race, sex or class alone. For example, she showed how the early women's suffrage movement often sidelined Black women and working-class women."},{"head":"The prison-industrial complex","body":"Davis argues that prisons in the United States have become tied up with business and political interests that profit from locking people up. Prison numbers grew far faster than crime, she points out, and the burden falls hardest on poor people and people of colour."},{"head":"Prisons and the legacy of slavery","body":"She traces links between prisons and slavery, such as the US Constitution's Thirteenth Amendment, which banned slavery except as punishment for crime, and the leasing of convicts as forced labour in the South after the Civil War."},{"head":"Abolition as building","body":"Borrowing W. E. B. Du Bois's phrase 'abolition democracy', Davis argues that abolishing prisons means building institutions that make them unnecessary: good schools, health care, housing and new ways of responding to harm."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Prison abolition means opening all the prison doors tomorrow and doing nothing about violent crime.","truth":"Davis describes abolition as a long-term project of reducing reliance on prisons while building other ways to prevent and respond to harm."},"startHere":"Read Are Prisons Obsolete? (2003), a short book of about a hundred pages that sets out her case clearly for general readers.","critics":"Many criminologists and policy scholars argue that some offenders pose real dangers that require secure confinement, and that reforming prisons is more realistic and safer than abolishing them.","legacy":"Davis is a central figure in Black feminist thought and in the modern prison abolition movement, whose ideas have spread widely in activism and academia.","quote":null},"scruton":{"life":["Roger Scruton was born on 27 February 1944 in Buslingthorpe, Lincolnshire, and grew up in post-war England, attending grammar school in High Wycombe. In the 1960s and 1970s most British intellectuals leaned left, and conservatism had little standing in universities. Scruton later said that watching the student riots in Paris in May 1968 turned him towards conservatism.","He studied at Jesus College, Cambridge, and taught at Birkbeck College, London, from 1971 to 1992, becoming Professor of Aesthetics. He also trained as a barrister. From 1982 to 2001 he edited the conservative Salisbury Review. From 1979 he helped run secret seminars for dissidents in communist Czechoslovakia and was detained and expelled in 1985. He later taught at Boston University and held various research posts. He was knighted in 2016.","Scruton wrote more than fifty books, from philosophy and political theory to novels, and also composed music. He lived for many years on a farm in Wiltshire. In 2018 he was made chair of a government commission on beautiful building. He was dismissed in 2019 after controversy over a magazine interview, then reappointed. He died of cancer on 12 January 2020."],"timeline":[[1944,"Born in Buslingthorpe, Lincolnshire"],[1971,"Begins teaching at Birkbeck College, London"],[1980,"Publishes The Meaning of Conservatism"],[1982,"Becomes founding editor of The Salisbury Review"],[1985,"Detained and expelled from Czechoslovakia"],[1997,"Publishes The Aesthetics of Music"],[2016,"Knighted"],[2020,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Conserving what we inherit","body":"Scruton argued that institutions, customs and places we inherit carry wisdom built up over generations. They are far easier to destroy than to create, so change should be careful and gradual."},{"head":"Love of home","body":"He called the attachment to one's home and community oikophilia, from the Greek for 'household'. He saw it as the root of conservatism and also of real environmental care, since people protect the places they love."},{"head":"Beauty matters","body":"Scruton held that beauty is a real value, not just a matter of taste. In architecture and everyday surroundings, ugliness damages how we live together, which is why he campaigned for building in harmony with traditional streets and towns."},{"head":"Hearing movement in music","body":"In The Aesthetics of Music he argued that when we listen to music we hear tones as moving, rising and falling through a kind of imagined space. That experience, not the sounds alone, is what music is about."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Scruton's conservatism was mainly a defence of free markets.","truth":"Scruton was wary of unrestrained markets and consumerism, which he thought could erode communities and places; his conservatism centred on attachment, tradition and beauty."},"startHere":"Read How to Be a Conservative (2014) for his politics, or Beauty (2009) for his aesthetics. Both are short and written for general readers.","critics":"Critics on the left argue that appeals to inherited tradition and national home can defend unjust hierarchies and exclude minorities and newcomers.","legacy":"Scruton was among the most prominent conservative philosophers in the English-speaking world, and his work on beauty and architecture influenced British planning debates.","quote":null},"dutton":{"life":["Denis Dutton was born on 9 February 1944 in Los Angeles. He worked in aesthetics, the philosophy of art, at a time when many critics held that art and taste were shaped entirely by culture. From the 1990s, evolutionary psychology was offering a different picture, explaining human behaviour partly through instincts shaped by natural selection, and Dutton applied it to art.","He studied at the University of California, Santa Barbara, gaining a BA in 1966 and a PhD in 1975, and taught at several American universities. In 1984 he moved to the University of Canterbury in Christchurch, New Zealand, where he taught for the rest of his career. He edited the journal Philosophy and Literature and in 1998 founded Arts & Letters Daily, a popular website linking to essays and reviews. The Art Instinct appeared in 2009. He died of cancer in Christchurch on 28 December 2010.","Dutton was a lively public figure. He ran a 'Bad Writing Contest' that mocked obscure academic prose, was a founding member and first chair of the New Zealand Skeptics, and served on the board of Radio New Zealand. He also founded a print-on-demand publisher. His wide reading and taste for debate showed in Arts & Letters Daily, which he edited himself."],"timeline":[[1944,"Born in Los Angeles"],[1975,"Completes his PhD at UC Santa Barbara"],[1979,"Publishes 'Artistic Crimes', on forgery"],[1984,"Moves to the University of Canterbury, New Zealand"],[1998,"Founds the website Arts & Letters Daily"],[2009,"Publishes The Art Instinct"],[2010,"Dies in Christchurch, New Zealand"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Art is an achievement","body":"Dutton argued that a work of art is a kind of performance: we value it partly for the skill it shows. That is why a perfect forgery is worth less than the original. It may look the same, but it represents a different, lesser achievement."},{"head":"Art is universal","body":"Every known human society has art. Dutton listed features found everywhere, such as skill, pleasure, style, criticism and imaginative experience, and argued that this pattern points to a shared human nature rather than mere local custom."},{"head":"An evolved taste for landscapes","body":"Dutton noted that people across very different cultures tend to prefer pictures of open land with scattered trees, water and paths. He suggested this preference echoes the kind of landscape our ancestors found safe and rich in food."},{"head":"Art as display","body":"Drawing on Darwin's idea of sexual selection, Dutton argued that making art may have helped our ancestors show off intelligence, skill and creativity to potential mates, much as a peacock displays its tail."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Evolutionary aesthetics claims our taste in art is fixed by our genes.","truth":"Dutton argued that evolved instincts shape what we tend to enjoy, but he stressed that cultures develop these tendencies in very different ways."},"startHere":"Read his essay 'Artistic Crimes' (1979), which uses the case of the forger Han van Meegeren to ask why a perfect fake is worth less than the original.","critics":"Critics argue that evolutionary stories about art are hard to test and risk becoming 'just-so stories', and that the great variety of art across cultures is better explained by history and culture than by instinct.","legacy":"The Art Instinct is a leading statement of evolutionary aesthetics, and Arts & Letters Daily became a widely read guide to ideas online.","quote":null},"widerker":{"life":["David Widerker is an Israeli philosopher who works on free will, action and metaphysics. In 1969 Harry Frankfurt published imagined cases that seemed to show you can be morally responsible even if you could not have done otherwise. This challenged an old principle tying responsibility to alternatives, and it set off a long debate in which Widerker became a leading voice.","He studied philosophy and mathematics at Bar-Ilan University, gaining a BA in 1970, an MA in 1975 and a PhD in 1982 on the logic of events and actions. He has spent his career at Bar-Ilan, becoming a lecturer in 1984 and full professor in 2002. He held research posts at Cornell, Notre Dame, Toronto, the University of British Columbia, Berlin and Amsterdam. His 1995 paper in The Philosophical Review made him central to the free will debate.","Widerker works mainly through journal articles rather than books, often co-written with colleagues. With Michael McKenna he edited Moral Responsibility and Alternative Possibilities (2003), a standard collection on the Frankfurt debate. He has also written on fatalism and God's foreknowledge, and has led free will research groups in Israel, Canada and Germany."],"timeline":[[1982,"Completes his PhD at Bar-Ilan University"],[1984,"Begins teaching at Bar-Ilan"],[1990,"Publishes 'Troubles with Ockhamism' in The Journal of Philosophy"],[1995,"Publishes 'Libertarianism and Frankfurt's Attack on the Principle of Alternative Possibilities'"],[2002,"Publishes 'Farewell to the Direct Argument'; becomes full professor"],[2003,"Co-edits Moral Responsibility and Alternative Possibilities"],[2006,"Publishes 'Libertarianism and the Philosophical Significance of Frankfurt Scenarios'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The dilemma for Frankfurt cases","body":"In a Frankfurt case, a hidden device would force your choice if you showed a sign of choosing otherwise. Widerker asked: is the sign connected to your choice by determinism or not? If it is, the case assumes determinism and cannot persuade libertarians. If it is not, the device cannot know in time what you will do, and you could have done otherwise."},{"head":"What should you have done instead?","body":"Widerker argued that blaming someone makes sense only if there was something else they should have done. If, in a Frankfurt case, no alternative was open, it is unfair to blame them. Others named this the 'W-defence' after him."},{"head":"Against the direct argument","body":"Peter van Inwagen argued that if no one is responsible for the past and the laws of nature, no one is responsible for what follows from them. Widerker challenged the logic of this argument."},{"head":"Libertarians can welcome Frankfurt","body":"In later work Widerker argued that libertarians, who hold that freedom requires indeterminism, could accept that alternatives are not needed for responsibility, while still holding that determinism rules responsibility out."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his 1995 Philosophical Review paper on Frankfurt's attack, which sets out the dilemma clearly and started a large debate; it is reprinted in his co-edited collection.","critics":"Derk Pereboom, Alfred Mele and David Robb designed new Frankfurt-style cases that do not rely on a prior sign, intended to escape Widerker's dilemma; John Martin Fischer also argued against the W-defence.","legacy":"Widerker's dilemma reshaped the debate on Frankfurt cases, and every major treatment since has had to answer it.","quote":null},"duff":{"life":["R. A. (Antony) Duff was born in 1945 in Britain. He works on the philosophy of criminal law and punishment. In the late 20th century debate was dominated by two camps: those who justified punishment by its future benefits, such as deterring crime, and retributivists who justified it by desert. Duff developed a third view, in which punishment is a form of moral communication.","He spent most of his career at the University of Stirling in Scotland, where he is now professor emeritus, and later also taught at the University of Minnesota Law School. Trials and Punishments (1986) set out his early view, which he developed in Punishment, Communication, and Community (2001). With colleagues he led large research projects on the criminal trial and on criminalisation, producing a series of co-written books.","Duff is known for collaborative work across philosophy and law, often co-writing with legal scholars. He is a Fellow of the British Academy and of the Royal Society of Edinburgh, and wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on legal punishment. His later work turned from punishment itself to the question of what conduct the criminal law should cover in the first place."],"timeline":[[1945,"Born"],[1986,"Publishes Trials and Punishments"],[1990,"Publishes Intention, Agency and Criminal Liability"],[1996,"Publishes Criminal Attempts"],[2001,"Publishes Punishment, Communication, and Community"],[2004,"First volume of The Trial on Trial, written with colleagues, appears"],[2007,"Publishes Answering for Crime"],[2018,"Publishes The Realm of Criminal Law"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Punishment as communication","body":"Duff argues that punishment should communicate to an offender the censure they deserve, as a message to a fellow citizen. The aim is not just to inflict pain but to get the offender to understand and accept that what they did was wrong."},{"head":"Secular penance","body":"The hard treatment of punishment, such as community service, works like a penance. It gives the offender a way to take the wrong seriously and to show apology to the community, much as a sincere apology is often accompanied by doing something to make amends."},{"head":"Crimes as public wrongs","body":"A crime is a wrong that concerns the whole community, not just the victim. That is why the state, on behalf of all citizens, calls the offender to account, rather than leaving it to the victim to sue."},{"head":"Answering at trial","body":"Duff sees a criminal trial as calling the defendant to answer a charge as a fellow citizen. When a state has treated some citizens very unjustly, he argues, it may lack the standing to call them to account."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Legal Punishment', freely available, which surveys the main theories before his own communicative view.","critics":"Andrew von Hirsch agreed that punishment should express censure but argued the state has no business trying to make offenders repent, since that intrudes on their conscience; it should only state its disapproval.","legacy":"Duff's communicative theory is one of the main modern accounts of punishment, and his work shaped how philosophers think about criminal trials and criminal law.","quote":null},"pettit":{"life":["Philip Pettit was born in 1945 in Ballygar, Ireland. He trained when political philosophy was dominated by debates about liberty understood as non-interference, the absence of others getting in your way. From the 1980s historians such as Quentin Skinner were recovering an older 'republican' tradition, from ancient Rome to the American revolutionaries, and Pettit turned it into a modern theory.","He studied at Maynooth and took a PhD at Queen's University Belfast, then taught at University College Dublin, Cambridge and Bradford. For many years he was at the Australian National University. In 2002 he moved to Princeton, where he became Laurance S. Rockefeller University Professor of Politics and Human Values, while keeping a post at ANU. Republicanism (1997) set out his theory of freedom. He also wrote widely on philosophy of mind, ethics and group agency.","Pettit is known for collaboration and for public engagement. He has co-written books with philosophers, economists and criminologists. His ideas influenced the Spanish government of José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero, which he advised and later wrote about. He was made a Companion of the Order of Australia in 2017 and is a Fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences."],"timeline":[[1945,"Born in Ballygar, Ireland"],[1993,"Publishes The Common Mind"],[1997,"Publishes Republicanism: A Theory of Freedom and Government"],[2002,"Moves to Princeton University"],[2010,"Publishes A Political Philosophy in Public Life, on his work with the Spanish government"],[2011,"Publishes Group Agency with Christian List"],[2012,"Publishes On the People's Terms"],[2014,"Publishes Just Freedom"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Freedom as non-domination","body":"You are free, Pettit argues, only if no one has the power to interfere with you at will. A slave with a kind master who never interferes is still unfree, because the master could interfere at any moment. Freedom is about others' power over you, not just what they actually do."},{"head":"The eyeball test","body":"Pettit offers a simple test: citizens are free when they can look one another in the eye without reason for fear or deference. A worker who must flatter the boss to keep their job fails the test."},{"head":"Contestatory democracy","body":"To stop the state itself becoming a master, citizens need ways to challenge its decisions: courts, ombudsmen, a free press and appeals. Democracy is not just voting but the power to contest how we are governed."},{"head":"Groups as agents","body":"With Christian List, Pettit argues that organised groups such as companies or committees can be real agents with their own beliefs and goals. Majority voting on separate questions can produce an inconsistent set of group views, so groups need procedures to stay coherent."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Republicanism in Pettit's sense is the ideology of the US Republican Party.","truth":"Pettit's republicanism is a much older tradition of thought about freedom and self-government, going back to ancient Rome; it has no connection to any modern party."},"startHere":"Read Just Freedom (2014), written for general readers, which explains freedom as non-domination with everyday examples.","critics":"Matthew Kramer and other liberal critics argue that non-domination is not a new kind of freedom, since a master's power can be captured by the likelihood of interference, which non-interference views already count.","legacy":"Pettit made neo-republicanism a major position in political philosophy and one of the few recent theories to directly shape a national government's programme.","quote":null},"david_friedman":{"life":["David D. Friedman was born on 12 February 1945, the son of the economist Milton Friedman and the economist Rose Friedman. He came of age in the 1960s, when American libertarianism was emerging as a movement. Most libertarians defended a minimal state limited to police, courts and defence. Friedman went further and argued that even these could be provided by markets.","He took a BA in chemistry and physics at Harvard in 1965 and a PhD in theoretical physics at the University of Chicago in 1971, then moved into economics without a formal degree in it. He taught economics and law at several universities and spent many years at Santa Clara University School of Law, where he is professor emeritus. The Machinery of Freedom (1973) made his name, and later books applied economics to law.","Friedman argues mainly through economics rather than appeals to natural rights, and he is open about the weak points of his own proposals. His books on economics, such as Hidden Order, are written for general readers and use everyday examples. He has also written novels and published work on medieval cookery and historical re-creation."],"timeline":[[1945,"Born"],[1971,"Completes a PhD in theoretical physics at the University of Chicago"],[1973,"Publishes The Machinery of Freedom"],[1979,"Publishes a study of private law enforcement in medieval Iceland"],[1986,"Publishes Price Theory"],[1996,"Publishes Hidden Order"],[2000,"Publishes Law's Order"],[2019,"Publishes Legal Systems Very Different from Ours"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Private protection agencies","body":"Friedman imagines a society where people buy protection from competing private firms, much as they buy insurance. When customers of different agencies disagree, the agencies settle the dispute through arbitrators they have agreed on in advance, since fighting would be costly for both."},{"head":"Law from the market","body":"In such a system, law itself would be produced by contract between agencies and arbitrators. Different customers could buy different legal rules, for instance on whether drug use is punished, and rules would compete like products."},{"head":"History as evidence","body":"Friedman studied medieval Iceland, where for centuries law was enforced without a state executive: wrongdoers owed compensation, and claims could be sold to others able to enforce them. He used it to argue that private law enforcement is workable."},{"head":"Results, not rights","body":"Unlike many libertarians, Friedman argues mainly from consequences: anarcho-capitalism would work better for people than a state. He openly admits hard cases, especially national defence, which is difficult for markets to supply."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Anarcho-capitalism means a society with no law at all.","truth":"Friedman's proposal is a society with law and courts, provided by competing private firms rather than by the state."},"startHere":"Read the section of The Machinery of Freedom (1973) that explains how private police, courts and law could work. The chapters are short and written for general readers.","critics":"Robert Nozick argued in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) that competing protection agencies would naturally merge into a single dominant agency, in effect a state. Tyler Cowen later argued that agencies able to cooperate would also be able to form a cartel.","legacy":"Friedman is one of the main theorists of anarcho-capitalism, and his books helped popularise the economic analysis of law.","quote":null},"arneson":{"life":["Richard Arneson was born in 1945 in the United States. He entered political philosophy in the 1970s, just after John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) reshaped the field. Philosophers were asking what exactly justice requires us to make equal: income, resources, opportunities or well-being. Arneson became a leading voice in this debate over what it means to treat people as equals.","He took his PhD at the University of California, Berkeley, in 1975, and has taught at the University of California, San Diego, since 1973, chairing the department from 1992 to 1996. His 1989 paper 'Equality and Equal Opportunity for Welfare' became a founding text of luck egalitarianism. He later moved towards prioritarianism. He served as president of the Pacific Division of the American Philosophical Association.","Arneson works mainly through journal articles. He is known for openly revising his positions in response to criticism, and for writing on a wide range of topics, from exploitation in Marx to paternalism, democracy and the value of human life. He has spent almost his whole career at one university, where he has supervised many graduate students in political philosophy."],"timeline":[[1945,"Born"],[1973,"Begins teaching at UC San Diego"],[1975,"Completes his PhD at Berkeley"],[1981,"Publishes 'What's Wrong with Exploitation?'"],[1989,"Publishes 'Equality and Equal Opportunity for Welfare'"],[1992,"Becomes chair of the UC San Diego philosophy department"],[2000,"Publishes 'Luck Egalitarianism and Prioritarianism'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Equal opportunity for welfare","body":"Arneson argued that justice should give everyone an equal chance of a good life. Inequalities caused by bad luck, such as being born with a disability, are unjust. Inequalities that come from people's own free choices, such as deciding to gamble, are not."},{"head":"Luck versus choice","body":"This became known as luck egalitarianism. Think of two people with the same opportunities: one works hard and one chooses not to. The difference in their incomes may be fair. Two people whose incomes differ only because one fell ill is a different matter."},{"head":"Priority to the worse off","body":"Arneson later argued that what matters is not equality as such but improving people's lives, with extra weight given to helping those who are worse off. Gains to the badly off count for more than equal gains to the well off."},{"head":"Democracy as a tool","body":"Arneson argued that democracy is not just in itself. It is justified because, and to the extent that, it tends to produce good outcomes for people. If another system reliably did better, democracy would lose its claim."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Equality and Equal Opportunity for Welfare' (1989), a short paper that set off the luck egalitarian debate.","critics":"Elizabeth Anderson, in 'What Is the Point of Equality?' (1999), argued that luck egalitarianism is harsh and demeaning: it would leave a reckless driver to suffer without help, and it requires the state to judge people's choices and their talents.","legacy":"Arneson is one of the founders of luck egalitarianism and a major defender of prioritarianism, two of the main positions in contemporary theories of distributive justice.","quote":null},"worrall":{"life":["John Worrall was born on 27 November 1946 in Leigh, Lancashire, England. He studied at the London School of Economics when it was a centre of the philosophy of science, home to Karl Popper and Imre Lakatos. Their debates, and those with Thomas Kuhn, about how science changes and whether we should believe its theories, formed the background to his work.","He took his degrees at the LSE, completing a PhD in 1976 under Lakatos. After Lakatos's death in 1974 he co-edited his unpublished work, including Proofs and Refutations. He edited the British Journal for the Philosophy of Science from 1974 to 1983 and spent his career at the LSE as Professor of Philosophy of Science, retiring in 2019. His 1989 paper on structural realism launched a major research programme. He was president of the British Society for the Philosophy of Science in 2010–2011.","Worrall is known for using detailed episodes from the history of science, especially the wave theory of light and the periodic table, to test philosophical claims. He has also written critically about how evidence is ranked in medicine, and about the relationship between science and religion. Many of his former students now teach philosophy of science."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born in Leigh, Lancashire"],[1974,"Becomes editor of the British Journal for the Philosophy of Science"],[1976,"Completes his PhD; Lakatos's Proofs and Refutations, co-edited by Worrall, is published"],[1978,"Co-edits Lakatos's Philosophical Papers"],[1989,"Publishes 'Structural Realism: The Best of Both Worlds?'"],[2010,"Becomes president of the British Society for the Philosophy of Science"],[2019,"Retires from the LSE"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Structural realism","body":"Worrall argues that we should believe what successful theories say about the structure of the world, as expressed in their equations, but not necessarily what they say about the nature of the things involved."},{"head":"The case of light","body":"In the 19th century Augustin Fresnel explained light as waves in an 'ether'. The ether turned out not to exist, but Fresnel's equations survived intact into Maxwell's later theory. What lasted was the mathematical structure, not the picture of what light is."},{"head":"The best of both worlds","body":"Realists say science's success would be a miracle if its theories were not roughly true. Sceptics point to the many past theories that were successful but false. Worrall argues that structural realism respects both points: structure is retained across theory change, even when the story about what exists is not."},{"head":"Doubts about randomised trials","body":"Worrall has argued that randomised controlled trials, often called the gold standard in medicine, do not automatically give better evidence than other well-designed studies, and that their special status should be argued for, not assumed."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his 1989 paper 'Structural Realism: The Best of Both Worlds?', which is short, clear and uses the history of the theory of light as its main example.","critics":"Stathis Psillos argued that the structure of a theory cannot be neatly separated from its claims about the nature of things. Others revived an objection from the mathematician M. H. A. Newman (1928): knowing only the structure of the world tells us almost nothing.","legacy":"Worrall founded modern structural realism, now one of the main positions in the debate over whether to believe scientific theories.","quote":null},"field":{"life":["Hartry Field was born on 30 November 1946 in Boston. He trained at Harvard when W. V. O. Quine and Hilary Putnam were arguing that, since mathematics is indispensable to physics, anyone who believes physics must believe that numbers and other mathematical objects exist. Much of Field's career has been an attempt to resist that conclusion.","He studied mathematics at the University of Wisconsin, then took an MA and PhD in philosophy at Harvard, finishing in 1972. That year his paper 'Tarski's Theory of Truth' became widely discussed. He taught at Princeton, the University of Southern California and the CUNY Graduate Center before moving to New York University in 1997. Science Without Numbers (1980) won the Lakatos Award. Later books dealt with truth, indeterminacy and the logical paradoxes.","Field is known for technically demanding work that takes on large philosophical questions with the tools of logic and mathematics, and for changing his views on truth over time. He gave the John Locke Lectures at Oxford in 2008, is a Fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences, and has also held a research post at the University of Birmingham."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born in Boston"],[1972,"Completes his PhD at Harvard; publishes 'Tarski's Theory of Truth'"],[1980,"Publishes Science Without Numbers"],[1986,"Science Without Numbers wins the Lakatos Award"],[1989,"Publishes Realism, Mathematics and Modality"],[1997,"Moves to New York University"],[2001,"Publishes Truth and the Absence of Fact"],[2008,"Publishes Saving Truth from Paradox; gives the John Locke Lectures"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Physics without numbers","body":"In Science Without Numbers Field rewrote Newton's theory of gravity without mentioning numbers, using only points of space and time and relations between them. If physics can be done this way, he argued, science does not force us to believe in numbers."},{"head":"Maths as a useful fiction","body":"Field holds that mathematical claims like '2 + 2 = 4' are not literally true, since there are no numbers, but they are true in the story of mathematics, as 'Sherlock Holmes lives on Baker Street' is true in the stories."},{"head":"Why fiction is so useful","body":"Field argued that mathematics is 'conservative': adding it to a theory about the physical world never lets you prove a new claim about the physical world that you could not have proved without it. It is a powerful shortcut, not a source of new facts."},{"head":"Saving truth from paradox","body":"The liar sentence, 'This sentence is false', seems true if false and false if true. Field proposes a logic that gives up the rule that every claim is either true or not true, which lets us keep a simple notion of truth without contradiction."}],"misconception":{"myth":"If mathematics is a fiction, then it is useless or mathematicians are wrong.","truth":"Field holds that mathematics is extremely useful and that mathematicians are right about what follows within mathematics; he denies only that mathematical objects literally exist."},"startHere":"Read the short opening chapters of Science Without Numbers (1980), where Field explains what he is trying to do and why mathematics might be dispensable.","critics":"Defenders of the Quine-Putnam indispensability argument reply that Field's method has not been extended beyond Newtonian gravity to modern physics; David Malament pointed out early that quantum mechanics poses special difficulties.","legacy":"Field made fictionalism about mathematics a serious position and has shaped debates on truth, deflationism and the paradoxes.","quote":null},"kit_fine":{"life":["Kit Fine was born on 26 March 1946 in Britain. He trained when modal logic, the logic of necessity and possibility, was transforming philosophy. Saul Kripke and others had given it a clear framework of 'possible worlds'. Fine's teacher Arthur Prior was a pioneer of the logic of time. Fine went on to work across logic, metaphysics, language and philosophy of mathematics.","He studied philosophy, politics and economics at Balliol College, Oxford, graduating in 1967, and took a PhD at the University of Warwick in 1969, supervised by Prior. He taught at Edinburgh, UC Irvine, the University of Michigan and UCLA before joining New York University in 1997, where he is Silver Professor of Philosophy and Mathematics. His 1994 paper 'Essence and Modality' changed how metaphysicians think about necessity. He has edited the Journal of Symbolic Logic.","Fine is known for combining technical logic with metaphysical questions, and for producing several new frameworks rather than a single system. He has published well over a hundred articles, many of them long and demanding. He is a Corresponding Fellow of the British Academy and a Fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born"],[1969,"Completes his PhD at Warwick under Arthur Prior"],[1975,"Publishes 'Vagueness, Truth and Logic'"],[1977,"Publishes Worlds, Times and Selves with Arthur Prior"],[1994,"Publishes 'Essence and Modality'"],[1997,"Joins New York University"],[2005,"Publishes Modality and Tense"],[2020,"Publishes Vagueness: A Global Approach"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Essence comes first","body":"Many philosophers defined essence in terms of necessity. Fine reversed this. Socrates necessarily belongs to the set whose only member is Socrates, yet belonging to that set is no part of what Socrates is. So necessity is not enough for essence; instead, necessities flow from the essences of things."},{"head":"Truth on every sharpening","body":"In his 1975 paper Fine treated a vague word like 'bald' as one whose meaning could be sharpened in many acceptable ways. A sentence is true if it comes out true on every sharpening, false if false on every one, and neither in borderline cases. He later developed a different 'global' approach."},{"head":"Grounding","body":"Fine helped develop the idea of grounding: some facts hold in virtue of others. The fact that a ball is red and round holds because it is red and because it is round. Grounding is a relation of explanation, not just necessary connection."},{"head":"Arbitrary objects","body":"Mathematicians say 'let n be an arbitrary number'. Fine developed a theory of such arbitrary objects to explain how this kind of reasoning works."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Essence and Modality' (1994), a short paper whose Socrates example shows clearly why essence and necessity come apart.","critics":"Timothy Williamson argued against Fine's 1975 treatment of vagueness that we should keep classical logic and say that vague words have sharp boundaries we simply cannot know.","legacy":"Fine's work on essence and grounding helped launch a revival of metaphysics focused on explanation, and his logical work is widely used.","quote":null},"woodward":{"life":["James Woodward was born in 1946 in the United States. He works in the philosophy of science on causation and explanation. For much of the 20th century, philosophers tried to analyse causation using laws of nature or probabilities. In the 1990s, statisticians and computer scientists such as Judea Pearl, Peter Spirtes, Clark Glymour and Richard Scheines developed new mathematical tools for causal reasoning, which Woodward drew on.","He took a BA in mathematics at Carleton College in 1968 and a PhD in philosophy at the University of Texas at Austin in 1977. He taught for many years at the California Institute of Technology, becoming J. O. and Juliette Koepfli Professor of the Humanities in 2001. In 2010 he moved to the Department of History and Philosophy of Science at the University of Pittsburgh. Making Things Happen (2003) won the Lakatos Award.","Woodward is known for working closely with scientists' actual methods, including in biology, psychology and neuroscience, and for testing philosophical ideas against how researchers really reason. He was president of the Philosophy of Science Association from 2010 to 2012 and was elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in 2016."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born"],[1977,"Completes his PhD at the University of Texas at Austin"],[1988,"Publishes 'Saving the Phenomena' with James Bogen"],[2003,"Publishes Making Things Happen"],[2005,"Making Things Happen wins the Lakatos Award"],[2010,"Moves to the University of Pittsburgh"],[2016,"Elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences"],[2021,"Publishes Causation with a Human Face"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Causes are handles","body":"Woodward's interventionist theory says X causes Y if intervening to change X would change Y. Flicking a light switch changes whether the light is on, so the switch is a cause. Changing the reading on a barometer does not change the weather, so the barometer is not a cause of storms."},{"head":"Explanation answers what-if questions","body":"A good explanation tells you how the outcome would have been different if things had been different. Explaining why a ball landed where it did means showing how its landing spot depends on speed and angle."},{"head":"Stable relationships, not strict laws","body":"Woodward argues that explanations in biology or economics do not need exceptionless laws. They need relationships that stay stable when we intervene, at least within some range."},{"head":"Data versus phenomena","body":"With James Bogen he distinguished data, such as many slightly different thermometer readings, from phenomena, such as the melting point of lead. Theories explain phenomena; data are evidence for them."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Interventionism says something is a cause only if humans can actually experiment on it.","truth":"Woodward's interventions are hypothetical: the Moon causes the tides because the tides would change if the Moon's position changed, even though no one can move it."},"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Causation and Manipulability', which explains the interventionist theory clearly and is free online.","critics":"Critics point out that the theory explains causation using interventions, which are themselves causal, so it cannot reduce causation to anything non-causal. Woodward accepts this, arguing that the account is still informative.","legacy":"Woodward's interventionism has become the most widely used theory of causation in philosophy of science and is applied across biology, psychology and the social sciences.","quote":null},"dancy":{"life":["Jonathan Dancy was born on 8 May 1946 in England. He trained when moral philosophy was dominated by theories built around general principles, such as utilitarianism's 'maximise happiness' or Kant's tests for duties. Most philosophers assumed that good moral thinking meant finding the right principle and applying it. Dancy became the leading critic of the idea that ethics needs such principles at all.","He studied classics and philosophy at Corpus Christi College, Oxford, graduating in 1969 with a double first, and taught for a year at Pembroke College. In 1971 he went to Keele University, where he became a professor in 1991. He later held posts at the University of Reading and the University of Texas at Austin. He wrote textbooks on epistemology and on Berkeley before Moral Reasons (1993) set out his particularism. He was elected a Fellow of the British Academy in 2016.","Dancy is known for close attention to how reasons actually work in everyday cases, using many small examples rather than grand theory. He also co-edited an anthology on the philosophy of action and edited a collection of essays on Derek Parfit's work. He wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on moral particularism, which is a standard introduction to the view."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born in England"],[1971,"Begins teaching at Keele University"],[1985,"Publishes An Introduction to Contemporary Epistemology"],[1993,"Publishes Moral Reasons"],[2000,"Publishes Practical Reality"],[2004,"Publishes Ethics Without Principles"],[2016,"Elected a Fellow of the British Academy"],[2018,"Publishes Practical Shape"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Reasons depend on context","body":"Dancy argues that a feature that is a reason in one case can be no reason, or even a reason against, in another. That an act would give someone pleasure usually counts in its favour. But a torturer's pleasure counts against the torture. He calls this holism about reasons."},{"head":"Ethics without principles","body":"If reasons shift with context, no general principle like 'lying is always wrong' can be relied on. Moral skill is the ability to see what matters in a particular situation, not to apply rules."},{"head":"Favourers and enablers","body":"Dancy distinguishes the features that count in favour of an act from conditions that let them do so. That I promised is a reason to help you move house. That my promise was not forced out of me is not an extra reason, but it enables the promise to count."},{"head":"Reasons are facts, not beliefs","body":"In Practical Reality he argues that your reason for taking an umbrella is that it is raining, a fact about the world, not your belief that it is raining, a state of your mind."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Particularism means there are no moral reasons and anything goes.","truth":"Dancy holds that there are real moral reasons and right answers; he denies only that general principles are needed to find them."},"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Moral Particularism', a clear and free introduction to the view written by Dancy himself.","critics":"Brad Hooker argued that moral principles are needed for people to predict and trust each other's behaviour. Sean McKeever and Michael Ridge argued in Principled Ethics (2006) that even if reasons depend on context, principles that take context into account are still possible.","legacy":"Dancy made moral particularism a serious position, and his holism about reasons is now discussed throughout ethics and the theory of reasons.","quote":null},"waller":{"life":["Bruce Waller was born on 20 August 1946 in the United States. He worked on free will and moral responsibility. Most philosophers have held that people can deserve praise and blame, whether or not determinism is true. A small group of sceptics argued otherwise, and Waller became one of the most persistent of them, drawing on psychology and biology as well as philosophy.","He took his PhD at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill in 1979 and taught at Elon College before moving in 1990 to Youngstown State University in Ohio, where he taught until retiring in 2019 and chaired the Department of Philosophy and Religious Studies. Freedom Without Responsibility (1990) set out his position, which he developed in books including Against Moral Responsibility (2011) and The Stubborn System of Moral Responsibility (2014). He died on 8 February 2023.","Waller wrote many textbooks for students on critical thinking, ethics and philosophy, reflecting a long commitment to teaching. His arguments drew heavily on psychology, criminology and animal behaviour, and he wrote in a direct style for non-specialists as well as philosophers. He applied his views to criminal justice, arguing for less punishment and more attention to causes."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born"],[1979,"Completes his PhD at the University of North Carolina"],[1990,"Publishes Freedom Without Responsibility; joins Youngstown State University"],[1998,"Publishes The Natural Selection of Autonomy"],[2011,"Publishes Against Moral Responsibility"],[2014,"Publishes The Stubborn System of Moral Responsibility"],[2020,"Publishes The Injustice of Punishment"],[2023,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"No one deserves blame","body":"Waller argues that our characters and abilities, including our capacity for self-control, come from genes and circumstances we did not choose. So no one ultimately deserves blame or praise for what they do."},{"head":"Look for causes, not culprits","body":"Blame, he argues, makes us stop asking why something happened. When a nurse makes a mistake, asking what in the system made the error likely, such as tiredness or confusing labels, prevents future errors far better than punishing the nurse."},{"head":"Freedom without responsibility","body":"Waller does not deny that we have a kind of freedom. Like other animals, we explore options and choose among them, which he calls natural autonomy. What we lack is the special kind of freedom that would make us deserve blame."},{"head":"Why the system is stubborn","body":"Waller argues that belief in moral responsibility is held in place by a network of other beliefs, such as the belief that the world is just, and by a strong urge to strike back at those who harm us."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Denying moral responsibility means people could do whatever they like without consequences.","truth":"Waller holds that we can still protect people from harm and hold people to obligations; he rejects only the idea that wrongdoers deserve to suffer."},"startHere":"Read the opening chapters of Against Moral Responsibility (2011), where he sets out his case and answers the most common objections.","critics":"Compatibilists such as Daniel Dennett argue that holding people responsible is a valuable social practice that works well and is justified even if determinism is true.","legacy":"Waller helped build the case for scepticism about moral responsibility and influenced later sceptics who argue for reforming punishment.","quote":null},"zagzebski":{"life":["Linda Zagzebski was born in 1946 in the United States. She came to epistemology, the study of knowledge, as philosophers were struggling with Edmund Gettier's 1963 cases, which showed that a justified true belief can fall short of knowledge. At the same time, virtue ethics was reviving Aristotle's focus on character. Zagzebski brought these two developments together.","She took a BA at Stanford, an MA at Berkeley and a PhD at UCLA in 1979. She taught at Loyola Marymount University from 1979 to 1999, then moved to the University of Oklahoma, where she held the Kingfisher College Chair of the Philosophy of Religion and Ethics. Virtues of the Mind (1996) set out her virtue epistemology. She gave the Wilde Lectures at Oxford in 2010 and the Gifford Lectures at St Andrews in 2015.","Zagzebski works across epistemology, ethics and the philosophy of religion, and often uses ideas from one field to solve problems in another. She has served as president of the American Philosophical Association's Central Division, the American Catholic Philosophical Association and the Society of Christian Philosophers, and was elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in 2022."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born"],[1979,"Completes her PhD at UCLA; begins teaching at Loyola Marymount"],[1991,"Publishes The Dilemma of Freedom and Foreknowledge"],[1994,"Publishes 'The Inescapability of Gettier Problems'"],[1996,"Publishes Virtues of the Mind"],[1999,"Moves to the University of Oklahoma"],[2012,"Publishes Epistemic Authority"],[2017,"Publishes Exemplarist Moral Theory"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Gettier problems cannot be escaped","body":"Zagzebski argued that as long as justification does not guarantee truth, Gettier cases can always be built. Take a justified belief, add a stroke of bad luck that would make it false, then a stroke of good luck that makes it true anyway. The belief is true by accident, so it is not knowledge."},{"head":"Virtues of the mind","body":"She modelled intellectual virtues, such as open-mindedness, intellectual courage and carefulness, on Aristotle's moral virtues. A good thinker, like a good person, has the right motives and acts reliably on them."},{"head":"Knowledge as success through virtue","body":"Zagzebski defines knowledge as true belief reached because of an act of intellectual virtue. An archer who hits the target because of skill, not a lucky gust of wind, is a good comparison."},{"head":"Learning from exemplars","body":"In Exemplarist Moral Theory she argues that ethics should start from people we admire, such as saints, heroes and sages. 'Good' means roughly 'like that person', and we learn what is good by noticing what such people do."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'The Inescapability of Gettier Problems' (1994), a short paper that sets out her argument in a few clear pages.","critics":"Virtue reliabilists such as Ernest Sosa and John Greco argue that knowledge does not require good motives: simply seeing a cup on a table gives knowledge without any act of intellectual virtue.","legacy":"Zagzebski is a founder of virtue epistemology, and her argument about Gettier cases is widely cited as a reason to doubt that knowledge can be defined as justified true belief plus a simple fix.","quote":null},"david_miller":{"life":["David Miller was born on 8 March 1946 in England. He studied political theory in the late 1960s and early 1970s, when John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) was reviving the subject. Many political philosophers treated nationality as irrelevant or even dangerous to justice. Miller became the leading defender of 'liberal nationalism', the view that national ties have moral weight.","He studied at Selwyn College, Cambridge, then took a BPhil and DPhil at Balliol College, Oxford, writing a thesis on social justice that became his first book in 1976. He lectured at the University of Lancaster and the University of East Anglia before becoming an Official Fellow of Nuffield College, Oxford, and later Professor of Political Theory. His books cover social justice, market socialism, nationality, global justice and immigration.","Miller combines philosophical argument with evidence from the social sciences about how ordinary people actually think about justice, and he holds that political philosophy should take such views seriously. He is a Fellow of the British Academy, and he wrote the short Political Philosophy: A Very Short Introduction and an early book on anarchism."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born in England"],[1976,"Publishes Social Justice"],[1989,"Publishes Market, State, and Community"],[1995,"Publishes On Nationality"],[1999,"Publishes Principles of Social Justice"],[2007,"Publishes National Responsibility and Global Justice"],[2016,"Publishes Strangers in Our Midst"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Nations as ethical communities","body":"Miller argues that a nation is a real community whose members share a public culture and a history. Like families, nations create special obligations: we owe more to fellow citizens than to strangers, though we owe something to everyone."},{"head":"Three principles of justice","body":"Miller holds that justice has no single rule. In close communities, like families, need comes first. In working relationships, desert matters, so people should get what they earn. Among citizens, equality of status is key. Which principle applies depends on the relationship."},{"head":"National responsibility","body":"Nations can be collectively responsible for the results of their choices, for example their economic policies. This, Miller argues, limits how much richer countries owe poorer ones, though everyone everywhere is owed basic human rights."},{"head":"Controlling immigration","body":"In Strangers in Our Midst he argues that democratic states may decide how many immigrants to admit, to protect their public culture and self-government, while still owing strong duties to refugees."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Defending nationality is the same as defending xenophobia or ethnic nationalism.","truth":"Miller's liberal nationalism is based on a shared public culture open to newcomers, not on ethnicity, and it insists on human rights for all."},"startHere":"Read Political Philosophy: A Very Short Introduction (2003), his own brief and accessible guide to the subject, before tackling On Nationality.","critics":"Cosmopolitans such as Charles Beitz and Simon Caney argue that nationality is morally arbitrary, like class or birthplace, so it should not limit what justice requires across borders. Joseph Carens argues for much more open borders.","legacy":"Miller is the leading theorist of liberal nationalism, and his pluralist account of social justice and his work on immigration are central to current debates.","quote":null},"carroll":{"life":["Noël Carroll was born in 1947 in the United States. He came of age as film was becoming accepted as a serious art form and a university subject. At the same time, philosophy of art in the English-speaking world was turning away from abstract definitions of beauty towards particular arts, popular culture and the emotions that films, novels and plays stir in their audiences.","He studied philosophy at Hofstra University and the University of Pittsburgh, then cinema studies at New York University, where he wrote a dissertation on Buster Keaton's film The General. He later earned a second doctorate, in philosophy, at the University of Illinois at Chicago. He held named professorships at Temple University and the University of Wisconsin–Madison, and since 2007 he has been Distinguished Professor of Philosophy at the CUNY Graduate Center in New York.","Alongside his early academic work, Carroll spent years as a journalist, writing about film, theatre, performance and the visual arts. His books range widely, covering horror films, mass art, humour, criticism and Keaton's physical comedy. He often starts from an ordinary experience, such as being frightened by a monster we know is not real, and builds a theory that stays close to how audiences actually respond."],"timeline":[[1947,"Born in the United States"],[1988,"Publishes Mystifying Movies, a critique of academic film theory"],[1990,"Publishes The Philosophy of Horror"],[1996,"Publishes the essay Moderate Moralism"],[1998,"Publishes A Philosophy of Mass Art"],[1999,"Publishes Philosophy of Art: A Contemporary Introduction"],[2007,"Becomes Distinguished Professor of Philosophy at the CUNY Graduate Center"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Moderate moralism","body":"Carroll argues that a moral flaw in a work can sometimes be an artistic flaw too. If a novel asks us to admire a cruel hero and we rightly refuse, the work has failed on its own terms, because it could not win the response it was aiming for. But not every moral flaw spoils a work, and moral value is only one of many things art can have."},{"head":"The paradox of horror","body":"Why do people enjoy films full of disgusting monsters? Carroll's answer is curiosity. Horror stories are built around beings that should not exist, and the pleasure comes from discovering, step by step, what the monster is and how it can be defeated. The disgust is the price we pay for that discovery."},{"head":"Mass art","body":"Carroll defines mass art as art made with technologies of mass production and distribution, such as film, television and recorded music, and designed to be easily understood by large audiences. He argues against critics who dismiss it, holding that being easy to access is a design goal, not automatically a defect."},{"head":"Identifying art through stories","body":"Instead of a single definition of art, Carroll suggests that we usually identify new works as art by telling a historical narrative. We show how an artist, responding to earlier art, made sensible choices that led to the new piece. This helps explain how strange new works, such as conceptual art, come to be accepted."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Start with his essay Moderate Moralism (1996), reprinted in Beyond Aesthetics (2001). It is short, uses clear examples, and sets out his best-known view on art and morality.","critics":"James Anderson and Jeffrey Dean replied with 'moderate autonomism', arguing that a moral flaw is never an artistic flaw as such, even if it sometimes happens to go with one. On the other side, Berys Gaut's 'ethicism' holds that moral flaws always count as artistic flaws.","legacy":"Carroll helped make film, horror and popular art respectable subjects for analytic philosophy, and his moderate moralism remains a standard position in debates about art and ethics.","quote":null},"papineau":{"life":["David Papineau was born in 1947 in Como, Italy. He belongs to a generation of British-based philosophers who take modern science as the starting point for philosophy, a view called naturalism. When he began his career, brain science was advancing quickly, and many philosophers were asking whether consciousness could fit into a world made only of physical things.","He took a degree in mathematics at the University of Natal in South Africa before studying philosophy at Cambridge, where he completed his PhD under Ian Hacking. He taught at several universities, including Cambridge, before becoming Professor of Philosophy of Science at King's College London. He also teaches at the CUNY Graduate Center in New York. He has been president of the British Society for the Philosophy of Science, the Mind Association and the Aristotelian Society.","Papineau writes for general readers as well as specialists. He co-wrote the illustrated guide Introducing Consciousness (2000), and in Knowing the Score (2017) he used sport to shed light on philosophical questions about skill, teamwork and fairness. His work moves between philosophy of mind, philosophy of science, probability and the theory of how thoughts come to be about things."],"timeline":[[1947,"Born in Como, Italy"],[1987,"Publishes Reality and Representation"],[1993,"Publishes Philosophical Naturalism"],[2002,"Publishes Thinking about Consciousness"],[2013,"Serves as president of the Aristotelian Society (2013–2014)"],[2017,"Publishes Knowing the Score, on the philosophy of sport"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The causal argument for physicalism","body":"Conscious states, such as a pain, clearly cause bodily movements, such as pulling your hand away. But physiology suggests every bodily movement already has a complete physical cause. Papineau argues that unless pains are pulling the same strings twice, the simplest conclusion is that the pain just is a brain state."},{"head":"Phenomenal concepts","body":"We can think about an experience in two ways: in scientific terms, as a brain process, or from the inside, by calling up what it feels like. Papineau argues that these are two different concepts for one and the same thing. Because the two ways of thinking feel so different, it seems possible to have the brain state without the feeling, but that appearance does not show they are really separate."},{"head":"Why zombies seem possible","body":"A 'zombie' is an imagined being physically just like you but with no inner experience. Papineau says zombies only seem possible because our inner-feeling concepts are not linked to our scientific concepts. The seeming gap lies in our ways of thinking, not in the world."},{"head":"Content from biological function","body":"How can a brain state be about something, such as water or danger? Papineau, like Ruth Millikan, argues that mental states get their meaning from the job they were selected to do, by evolution or learning. A belief is about water because it has the function of guiding us towards water."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Begin with Introducing Consciousness (2000), the illustrated guide he wrote with Howard Selina. It surveys the problem of consciousness quickly and shows where his own view fits.","critics":"David Chalmers argued that the 'phenomenal concept strategy' fails: either phenomenal concepts can themselves be explained physically, in which case they cannot explain the gap, or they cannot, in which case they bring the problem back.","legacy":"Papineau is one of the clearest defenders of physicalism about the mind, and his causal argument and his use of phenomenal concepts are standard reference points in debates about consciousness.","quote":null},"stump":{"life":["Eleonore Stump was born in 1947. She trained during a revival of philosophy of religion in English-speaking universities, when Christian philosophers increasingly used the tools of analytic philosophy, a style that stresses clear argument and precise language, to examine their beliefs. She has also worked to bring medieval thinkers, especially Thomas Aquinas, back into modern debates.","She studied classical languages at Grinnell College, biblical studies at Harvard and medieval studies at Cornell, where she earned her PhD in 1975. She taught at Oberlin College, Virginia Tech and the University of Notre Dame before moving in 1992 to Saint Louis University, where she is Robert J. Henle Professor of Philosophy. She gave the Gifford Lectures at Aberdeen in 2003, and has been president of the Society of Christian Philosophers, the American Catholic Philosophical Association and the Central Division of the American Philosophical Association.","Stump combines skills rarely found together: close reading of Latin and Greek texts, interpretation of biblical stories, and careful analytic argument. Much of her early work was written with the philosopher Norman Kretzmann, including a 1981 paper on what it means for God to be eternal. Her later books argue that stories, not only arguments, can give us a kind of knowledge of persons and of suffering."],"timeline":[[1947,"Born"],[1975,"Earns PhD in medieval studies at Cornell"],[1981,"Publishes 'Eternity' with Norman Kretzmann"],[1992,"Joins Saint Louis University"],[2003,"Gives the Gifford Lectures in Aberdeen and publishes Aquinas"],[2010,"Publishes Wandering in Darkness"],[2018,"Publishes Atonement"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Suffering and closeness to God","body":"Stump argues that the greatest good for a person is loving union with God. Suffering can sometimes open a person to this closeness, for example by breaking down pride or self-reliance. On her view, God allows suffering only when it is the best available way to bring about this good for the person who suffers."},{"head":"Knowledge through stories","body":"Some knowledge cannot be fully put into statements. Knowing your best friend is different from knowing facts about them. Stump argues that narratives, such as the biblical stories of Job or Abraham, convey this kind of personal knowledge, which is why she uses stories, not just arguments, when thinking about suffering."},{"head":"God's eternity","body":"With Norman Kretzmann, Stump defended the traditional view that God exists outside time, not at every moment of it. All of time is present to God at once, rather as a whole road is visible from a mountain top. They argued this view is coherent and helps explain how God can know the future without fixing it in advance."},{"head":"Aquinas for modern readers","body":"Stump has argued that Thomas Aquinas's views on mind, ethics and human nature are still worth taking seriously. Her book Aquinas (2003) presents his thought as a set of live philosophical positions, not just a historical system."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the chapters on the Book of Job in Wandering in Darkness (2010). They show both her way of reading stories and her central answer to the problem of suffering.","critics":"Critics have objected that her account works best for adults who can come to see their suffering as drawing them to God, and says little about the suffering of animals, young children or people destroyed by what they endure. Stump herself limits her account to adult human beings with normal mental capacities.","legacy":"Stump is one of the leading Christian philosophers of her generation, and she has done much to bring Aquinas and biblical narrative into analytic philosophy of religion.","quote":null},"kitcher":{"life":["Philip Kitcher was born in London in 1947. He trained at a time when philosophy of science was being shaken by Thomas Kuhn's claim that science advances through revolutions, not steady accumulation. Public quarrels over evolution, creationism and sociobiology, the attempt to explain human behaviour through evolution, were also opening up, giving philosophers of science a chance to play a public role.","He studied mathematics and the history and philosophy of science at Cambridge, then earned a PhD at Princeton in 1974, where his teachers included Carl Hempel and Thomas Kuhn. He taught at the University of Vermont, the University of Minnesota and the University of California, San Diego, before moving to Columbia University, where he became John Dewey Professor of Philosophy. His work moved from mathematics and biology to ethics, religion and literature.","Kitcher has often written for readers outside philosophy. Abusing Science (1982) took apart the arguments of creationists, and Vaulting Ambition (1985) criticised popular sociobiology. Later he turned to pragmatism, the American tradition of John Dewey that judges ideas by how well they help us solve problems. He has also written books on Wagner's operas, Thomas Mann's Death in Venice and James Joyce's Finnegans Wake."],"timeline":[[1947,"Born in London"],[1974,"Earns PhD at Princeton"],[1982,"Publishes Abusing Science: The Case Against Creationism"],[1985,"Publishes Vaulting Ambition, a critique of sociobiology"],[1993,"Publishes The Advancement of Science"],[2011,"Publishes The Ethical Project"],[2014,"Publishes Life After Faith"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The symmetry argument","body":"Most people hold the religion they were brought up in, and believers in every tradition acquire their faith in much the same way: through parents, community and religious experience. Kitcher argues that since these routes are alike, no tradition can claim better support than the others. So the confident believer in any one of them has no good reason to think theirs is the true one."},{"head":"Ethics as a human project","body":"Kitcher tells a story of ethics beginning among small groups of early humans whose limited sympathy for one another caused conflict. Rules and shared practices were invented to repair these failures, and they have been revised ever since. On this view ethics is not discovered in God's commands or in abstract truths; it is an ongoing project that can make progress."},{"head":"Secular humanism with a soft edge","body":"In Life After Faith, Kitcher defends a secular humanism that does not mock religion. He accepts that religion supplies community, ritual and comfort, and argues that a secular society must find other ways to meet those needs."},{"head":"Well-ordered science","body":"Which research gets funded is a moral and political choice. Kitcher argues that science should pursue the questions that informed, democratic deliberation would judge most important, not only those chosen by scientists or markets."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Start with Life After Faith (2014), a short book based on lectures, which sets out his case against religious belief and his picture of secular humanism in plain language.","critics":"Alvin Plantinga argued that holding one religion while knowing others disagree is not irrational, and that a belief's origin in upbringing does not by itself show it is unjustified; the same symmetry worry, he suggested, would apply to many moral and philosophical beliefs too.","legacy":"Kitcher has shaped philosophy of science, philosophy of biology and the public debate over creationism, and his naturalistic account of ethics is an important version of pragmatism today.","quote":null},"levinson":{"life":["Jerrold Levinson was born in New York City in 1948. In the 1960s and 1970s, philosophers of art faced a puzzle. Artists such as Marcel Duchamp had exhibited ordinary objects, like a urinal, as art, and older definitions based on beauty or imitation no longer seemed to work. Philosophers looked for new ways to say what makes something a work of art.","He took a degree in philosophy and chemistry at MIT in 1969 and a PhD in philosophy at the University of Michigan in 1974. He joined the University of Maryland in 1976, became Distinguished University Professor there in 2004, and became professor emeritus in 2018. He was president of the American Society for Aesthetics from 2001 to 2003 and held the International Francqui Chair at KU Leuven in 2010–2011.","Music is at the centre of much of his work. He has written on what a musical work is, how music expresses emotion and how listeners actually follow music as it unfolds. He also edited The Oxford Handbook of Aesthetics (2003), a standard reference work, and has published several collections of essays on art, music and value."],"timeline":[[1948,"Born in New York City"],[1974,"Earns PhD at the University of Michigan"],[1976,"Joins the University of Maryland"],[1979,"Publishes 'Defining Art Historically'"],[1990,"Publishes Music, Art, and Metaphysics"],[2001,"Becomes president of the American Society for Aesthetics (2001–2003)"],[2004,"Named Distinguished University Professor at Maryland"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Defining art historically","body":"Something is art, Levinson argues, if someone who has the right to it seriously intends it to be regarded in some way that earlier artworks have correctly been regarded. A urinal becomes art when it is offered for the kind of attention we give to sculptures. This ties art to its own history instead of to any fixed feature such as beauty."},{"head":"Musical works are 'indicated' structures","body":"A musical work is not just a pattern of sounds. It is a sound pattern as indicated by a particular composer at a particular time. So if two composers in different centuries happened to write exactly the same notes, they would have written two different works, with different meanings and achievements."},{"head":"Hypothetical intentionalism","body":"What does a novel or poem mean? Levinson's answer is: what an ideal, well-informed reader would best guess the author intended, given the work and its context. The author's private, actual intentions do not settle the matter if the work itself does not convey them."},{"head":"Listening in the moment","body":"In Music in the Moment, Levinson argues that understanding music mainly consists in following it from moment to moment, hearing how each passage grows out of the last. Grasping the large-scale form of a symphony matters much less than music theorists often assume."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his essay 'Defining Art Historically' (1979), reprinted in Music, Art, and Metaphysics. It is short and lays out one of the main modern answers to the question 'What is art?'.","critics":"Noël Carroll and other 'actual intentionalists' argued that a work means what its author actually intended, when that intention fits the text, not what an imagined reader would guess. Others asked how the historical definition can handle the very first artworks, which had no earlier art to look back to; Levinson has replied to this himself.","legacy":"Levinson's historical definition of art and his work on the nature of musical works are standard positions in aesthetics, and he did much to make philosophy of music a major field.","quote":null},"wegner":{"life":["Daniel Wegner (1948–2013) was a social psychologist who worked in the United States. He began his career as psychology was moving away from behaviourism, which studied only outward behaviour, towards the experimental study of thought. His questions about mental control and the feeling of will overlapped with long-running debates in philosophy about free will and the mind.","He earned his PhD at Michigan State University and taught at Trinity University in Texas and at the University of Virginia before moving to Harvard in 2000. In 1987 he and colleagues published a study in which people asked not to think of a white bear found it hard to stop. This led to his theory of ironic processes. His book The Illusion of Conscious Will (2002) brought his work to a wide audience. He died in 2013.","Wegner was known for simple, inventive experiments on everyday mental life, such as trying not to think of something, or feeling that we caused an event that someone else in fact caused. He also developed the idea of transactive memory. His last book, The Mind Club, written with Kurt Gray, appeared after his death."],"timeline":[[1948,"Born"],[1987,"Publishes the 'white bear' study on thought suppression"],[1989,"Publishes White Bears and Other Unwanted Thoughts"],[2000,"Joins the psychology department at Harvard"],[2002,"Publishes The Illusion of Conscious Will"],[2013,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The feeling of willing is an inference","body":"Wegner argued that we feel we willed an action when a thought about it comes just before the action, fits it, and has no obvious rival cause. The real causes of both the thought and the action are unconscious brain processes. So the feeling of will is the mind's interpretation of what happened, not a direct view of the cause."},{"head":"Evidence from errors","body":"Wegner collected cases where the feeling of will and actual causation come apart. People using a Ouija board move the pointer but feel they are not doing it. In his experiments, people could be led to feel they had caused a movement that a hidden helper in fact produced."},{"head":"Ironic processes","body":"When you try not to think of something, part of your mind keeps checking whether you are thinking of it, and that check keeps bringing the thought back. This is why trying hard not to think of a white bear, or of food when dieting, can backfire, especially when you are tired or stressed."},{"head":"Transactive memory","body":"Couples, families and teams share the work of remembering. Each person remembers some things and also who knows the rest, like a shared filing system. Losing a partner can therefore mean losing part of one's memory."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Wegner showed that our thoughts and intentions play no role in what we do.","truth":"Wegner argued that the conscious feeling of willing is not a direct readout of what causes an action; he did not deny that the brain processes behind our intentions produce our actions, and he thought the feeling of will usefully tracks what we are responsible for."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of The Illusion of Conscious Will (2002), which sets out his theory of apparent mental causation with many vivid everyday examples.","critics":"Philosophers such as Alfred Mele argued that cases where the feeling of will goes wrong show only that we can be mistaken about our actions, not that conscious intentions never play a causal role in ordinary action.","legacy":"Wegner's work on thought suppression and the feeling of will is widely cited in psychology, and it pushed philosophers of free will to take experimental evidence about agency seriously.","quote":null},"feldman_r":{"life":["Richard Feldman was born in 1948. He works in epistemology, the study of knowledge and justified belief. When he began his career, epistemologists were still responding to Edmund Gettier's 1963 examples showing that a justified true belief is not always knowledge, and arguing over whether justification depends on a person's evidence or on reliable processes that may lie outside their awareness.","He graduated from Cornell in 1970 and earned his PhD at the University of Massachusetts in 1975. That same year he joined the University of Rochester, where he spent his whole career. He chaired the philosophy department for 13 years, served as dean of the College of Arts, Sciences and Engineering from 2006 to 2017, and became interim president of the university in 2018.","Much of his best-known work was written with Earl Conee, with whom he defended evidentialism in a 1985 paper and a 2004 book. He also wrote widely used textbooks, including Reason and Argument and Epistemology (2003). His essays in the 2000s helped start a lively debate about what you should believe when someone equally well informed disagrees with you."],"timeline":[[1948,"Born"],[1975,"Earns PhD and joins the University of Rochester"],[1985,"Publishes 'Evidentialism' with Earl Conee"],[2003,"Publishes the textbook Epistemology"],[2004,"Publishes Evidentialism: Essays in Epistemology with Conee"],[2006,"Becomes dean of Arts, Sciences and Engineering at Rochester"],[2007,"Publishes 'Reasonable Religious Disagreements'"],[2018,"Becomes interim president of the University of Rochester"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Evidentialism","body":"Whether a belief is justified depends only on the evidence the person has. If you believe it will rain because the sky is dark and the forecast says so, your belief is justified; if you believe it because you hope it will, it is not. With Earl Conee, Feldman defended this simple-sounding view against many objections."},{"head":"Peer disagreement","body":"Suppose you and a friend who is just as informed and careful reach opposite conclusions on the same evidence. Feldman argues that, once you know this, you have no good reason to favour your own view. The reasonable response is for both of you to suspend judgement."},{"head":"No reasonable disagreement on shared evidence","body":"Feldman argues that a single body of evidence supports at most one attitude to a claim. So two people who share all the same evidence cannot both be reasonable if they disagree. This applies, he suggests, even to religious and political disputes."},{"head":"Justification is internal","body":"On Feldman's view, what makes a belief justified depends on what is available to the believer's mind, their experiences and reasons, not on facts they have no access to, such as whether their senses happen to be reliable."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his essay 'Reasonable Religious Disagreements' (2007). It is clear, uses everyday examples, and states his view on disagreement directly.","critics":"Thomas Kelly argued that the original evidence on the disputed question still matters after you learn of a disagreement, so the person who reasoned correctly may keep their view. Others, including Adam Elga, noted that the 'suspend judgement' view seems to undermine itself, since philosophers disagree about it too.","legacy":"Feldman and Conee's evidentialism is one of the main theories of justification, and Feldman's papers helped make the epistemology of disagreement a major topic.","quote":null},"shusterman":{"life":["Richard Shusterman was born in Philadelphia in 1949. He grew up as rock music, television and other mass media were transforming culture, while many philosophers still treated only 'high' art, such as opera or painting, as worth serious study. American pragmatism, the tradition of John Dewey that ties ideas to practical life and experience, was beginning to revive.","At 16 he moved to Israel, studying English and philosophy at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem. He completed a doctorate at Oxford in 1979 and taught in Israel, gaining tenure at Ben-Gurion University of the Negev. In 1986 he moved to Temple University in Philadelphia, later chairing its philosophy department. Since 2004 he has been Dorothy F. Schmidt Eminent Scholar in the Humanities at Florida Atlantic University, where he founded the Center for Body, Mind, and Culture.","Shusterman began in analytic philosophy of literary criticism, then turned to Dewey's pragmatism. He treats philosophy as an art of living, not only a body of theory. He developed somaesthetics, the study of the body as the place where we experience beauty and can improve ourselves, and he qualified as a practitioner of the Feldenkrais method, a system of movement education, to explore these ideas in practice."],"timeline":[[1949,"Born in Philadelphia"],[1979,"Completes his doctorate at Oxford"],[1986,"Joins Temple University"],[1991,"Publishes 'The Fine Art of Rap'"],[1992,"Publishes Pragmatist Aesthetics"],[1997,"Introduces somaesthetics in Practicing Philosophy"],[2004,"Moves to Florida Atlantic University"],[2008,"Publishes Body Consciousness"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Rap can be fine art","body":"Shusterman argued that rap deserves serious artistic attention. Its sampling of older records, its wordplay and its political commentary show complex skill and meaning. Dismissing it as mere entertainment, he argued, reflects social prejudice more than a judgement of quality."},{"head":"Art as lived experience","body":"Following Dewey, Shusterman holds that art is best understood as intense, meaningful experience, not as objects kept in museums. This means popular arts that people actively enjoy and dance to can be just as real a source of aesthetic value as concert music."},{"head":"Somaesthetics","body":"Our bodies are how we perceive, act and feel. Shusterman proposes a field that studies the body both in theory and in practice, from the science of perception to disciplines such as yoga or martial arts. Improving bodily awareness, he argues, can improve thinking, perception and the quality of life."},{"head":"Philosophy as a way of life","body":"Like ancient philosophers, Shusterman thinks philosophy should help people live better, not only produce theories. He treats the self as something one can shape, much as an artist shapes a work."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his chapter on rap in Pragmatist Aesthetics (1992), which grew from 'The Fine Art of Rap'. It shows his method by closely reading actual songs.","critics":"Critics in the tradition of Theodor Adorno's attack on the 'culture industry' argue that commercial popular art mostly offers standardised, passive pleasures, so celebrating it risks confusing popularity with value. Others question whether bodily training belongs inside philosophy at all.","legacy":"Shusterman helped bring popular art into serious aesthetics and founded somaesthetics, now an international field with its own journal and conferences.","quote":null},"craig":{"life":["William Lane Craig was born in Peoria, Illinois, in 1949. He trained during the 1970s, when a revival of arguments for the existence of God was under way in analytic philosophy, led by figures such as Alvin Plantinga and Richard Swinburne. At the same time, public debates in America over science, secularism and religion were growing louder.","He studied at Wheaton College and Trinity Evangelical Divinity School, then earned a PhD in philosophy at the University of Birmingham under John Hick, published as The Kalam Cosmological Argument (1979). He later earned a doctorate in theology at the University of Munich under Wolfhart Pannenberg. He has taught at Talbot School of Theology at Biola University and at Houston Christian University, and founded the ministry Reasonable Faith.","Craig is known well beyond academic philosophy for his public debates on God's existence with atheists such as Christopher Hitchens, Sam Harris and Lawrence Krauss. He presents tightly structured arguments in a set format, and is a central figure in Christian apologetics, the defence of faith by reason. His academic work also covers God's relation to time, divine foreknowledge and the theory of relativity."],"timeline":[[1949,"Born in Peoria, Illinois"],[1977,"Earns PhD at the University of Birmingham"],[1979,"Publishes The Kalam Cosmological Argument"],[1984,"Earns doctorate in theology at the University of Munich"],[1994,"Publishes Reasonable Faith"],[2009,"Debates Christopher Hitchens on the existence of God"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The kalam argument","body":"Whatever begins to exist has a cause. The universe began to exist, both because an actual infinite series of past events is impossible and because Big Bang cosmology points to a beginning. So the universe has a cause, which Craig argues must be timeless, spaceless, enormously powerful and personal."},{"head":"Life without God is absurd","body":"If there is no God and no life after death, Craig argues, then everyone and everything ends in nothing, and no act makes any final difference. Without God there would be no objective meaning, value or purpose. He thinks people cannot live consistently with this view."},{"head":"The moral argument","body":"If God does not exist, objective moral values and duties do not exist. But they do exist: torturing a child for fun is really wrong, not just disliked. So, Craig argues, God exists."},{"head":"Middle knowledge","body":"Craig defends Molinism, the view of the 16th-century Jesuit Luis de Molina. God knows what every possible free person would freely do in every possible situation. This lets God plan history in detail without taking away human freedom."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his essay 'The Absurdity of Life without God', a chapter of Reasonable Faith. It is short and states the view in his tagline directly.","critics":"Graham Oppy and Wes Morriston have argued that the kalam's premises are not as secure as Craig claims: it is unclear that an infinite past is impossible, and the step from a cause of the universe to a personal God is weak. Many critics also reject the claim that meaning or morality needs God, pointing to secular accounts such as Susan Wolf's.","legacy":"Craig revived the kalam cosmological argument as a major topic in philosophy of religion and has done more than almost anyone to shape popular debates between theists and atheists.","quote":null},"mcginn":{"life":["Colin McGinn was born in 1950 in West Hartlepool, in north-east England. He entered philosophy when Oxford was a world centre of analytic philosophy and the mind-body problem was being reopened. In 1974 Thomas Nagel had asked what it is like to be a bat, arguing that science seems unable to capture experience from the inside.","He studied psychology at the University of Manchester before turning to philosophy at Oxford, where he won the John Locke Prize. He taught at University College London, then held the Wilde Readership in Mental Philosophy at Oxford, and later taught at Rutgers University and the University of Miami. His 1989 paper 'Can We Solve the Mind-Body Problem?' made his central idea widely known.","McGinn writes in a clear, direct style and has often written for general readers in magazines and newspapers. His interests are wide: besides the mind, he has written on ethics, imagination, Shakespeare, disgust and the philosophy of language. He described his path into the subject in the memoir The Making of a Philosopher (2002)."],"timeline":[[1950,"Born in West Hartlepool, England"],[1982,"Publishes The Character of Mind"],[1989,"Publishes 'Can We Solve the Mind-Body Problem?'"],[1999,"Publishes The Mysterious Flame"],[2002,"Publishes the memoir The Making of a Philosopher"],[2006,"Publishes Shakespeare's Philosophy"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Cognitive closure","body":"Every mind has limits. A dog cannot understand algebra, not because algebra is magic but because dogs lack the right mental equipment. McGinn suggests human minds may be 'cognitively closed' to how the brain produces consciousness in the same way."},{"head":"A natural but hidden link","body":"McGinn holds that consciousness arises from the brain by entirely natural means. There is some property of the brain that explains it. We simply may never be able to grasp that property, because neither looking inward nor studying the brain from outside reveals it."},{"head":"Dissolving the puzzle","body":"If McGinn is right, the mind-body problem is not a sign that the mind is spiritual or that physics is incomplete. It is a fact about our limits as thinkers. This would explain why centuries of effort have made so little progress."}],"misconception":{"myth":"McGinn thinks consciousness is supernatural or that the mind is a non-physical soul.","truth":"McGinn insists consciousness is a natural product of the brain; his claim, known as 'mysterianism', is only that humans may lack the mental capacity to understand how the brain produces it."},"startHere":"Read The Mysterious Flame (1999), written for general readers. It explains the mind-body problem and his answer to it without technical language.","critics":"Owen Flanagan, who coined the label 'new mysterians', and Daniel Dennett argued that the view is premature defeatism: we cannot know in advance that a problem is beyond us, and many problems once thought unsolvable, such as the nature of life, were later solved.","legacy":"McGinn's mysterianism is one of the standard positions in debates about consciousness, and it made the idea of limits on human understanding a serious topic in philosophy of mind.","quote":null},"simmons":{"life":["A. John Simmons was born in 1950 in the United States. He came into political philosophy in the 1970s, soon after John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) revived the subject. A central old question was whether citizens have a moral duty to obey the law simply because it is the law of their country, a question that goes back to Socrates in Plato's Crito.","He studied at Princeton and took his graduate degrees at Cornell. In 1976 he began teaching at the University of Virginia, where he became Commonwealth Professor of Philosophy and Professor of Law, and chaired both the philosophy department and the Program in Political and Social Thought. His first book, Moral Principles and Political Obligations (1979), made his reputation. He has served on the editorial staff of the journal Philosophy & Public Affairs since 1982.","Simmons is a leading interpreter of John Locke, and much of his own thinking builds on Locke's view that legitimate authority must rest on the consent of the governed. He gave the Auguste Comte Lectures at the London School of Economics in 2013. Outside the university, he taught ethics for several years as a consultant at the FBI National Academy."],"timeline":[[1950,"Born"],[1976,"Begins teaching at the University of Virginia"],[1979,"Publishes Moral Principles and Political Obligations"],[1992,"Publishes The Lockean Theory of Rights"],[1993,"Publishes On the Edge of Anarchy"],[2001,"Publishes Justification and Legitimacy"],[2005,"Publishes Is There a Duty to Obey the Law? with Christopher Heath Wellman"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Philosophical anarchism","body":"Simmons argues that no existing state has shown that its citizens have a general moral duty to obey its laws. You should not steal, but that is because stealing is wrong, not because the law forbids it. The law itself does not add a new moral reason."},{"head":"Why the usual theories fail","body":"Simmons tested each standard argument for a duty to obey. Most people never consented to their state; simply living where you were born is not real consent. Arguments from gratitude, fair play or the benefits we receive, he argues, either do not apply to most citizens or do not create a duty to obey every law."},{"head":"Justification versus legitimacy","body":"A state can be justified, meaning it is good, useful and better than having no state. But it is legitimate over a particular person only if that person has accepted its authority. Simmons argues that most states are, at best, justified but not legitimate over most of their citizens."},{"head":"Lockean rights","body":"Simmons has given a detailed reconstruction of John Locke's theory of natural rights, property and consent, showing how Locke's ideas still bear on modern questions about borders, property and authority."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Philosophical anarchists like Simmons want to abolish the state or encourage people to break the law.","truth":"Simmons's anarchism is a claim about moral obligation: he denies a general duty to obey, but he does not conclude that states must be resisted or overthrown, and he accepts that many laws should be followed for independent moral reasons."},"startHere":"Read his half of Is There a Duty to Obey the Law? (2005), a debate book with Christopher Heath Wellman. It states his case compactly and sets it against a direct opponent.","critics":"George Klosko defended the 'fair play' principle, arguing that people who receive essential public goods, such as security, owe their share of cooperation even without consenting. Jeremy Waldron and others argued that a natural duty to support just institutions can ground political obligation without consent.","legacy":"Simmons made philosophical anarchism a serious position in mainstream political philosophy, and his arguments are the starting point for most modern work on political obligation.","quote":null},"shapiro":{"life":["Stewart Shapiro was born in 1951 in the United States. He trained in the decades after the great crises in the foundations of mathematics, when logicians such as Kurt Gödel had shown limits to what formal systems can prove. Philosophers were still asking what mathematical objects such as numbers are, and how we can know about things we cannot see or touch.","He studied mathematics and philosophy at Case Western Reserve University and took a master's degree in mathematics at the State University of New York at Buffalo, where he went on to earn a PhD in philosophy under the logician John Corcoran. He spent most of his career at Ohio State University, where he became O'Donnell Professor of Philosophy. He was elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in 2021.","Shapiro's work combines technical logic with philosophical questions. He defended second-order logic, a more powerful logic that can talk about properties as well as objects, in Foundations without Foundationalism (1991). He also wrote an accessible textbook, Thinking about Mathematics (2000), and later turned to vagueness and to logical pluralism, the idea that there may be more than one correct logic."],"timeline":[[1951,"Born"],[1978,"Earns PhD at the State University of New York at Buffalo (c.)"],[1991,"Publishes Foundations without Foundationalism"],[1997,"Publishes Philosophy of Mathematics: Structure and Ontology"],[2000,"Publishes Thinking about Mathematics"],[2006,"Publishes Vagueness in Context"],[2014,"Publishes Varieties of Logic"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Mathematics is about structure","body":"Shapiro argues that mathematics studies structures, patterns of relations, not individual objects. The natural numbers form one structure; geometry and group theory study others. What matters is how the parts relate, not what they are made of."},{"head":"Numbers as positions","body":"A number is like a position in a team. 'Goalkeeper' names a role that any suitable player can fill; similarly, the number 2 is simply the place that comes after 1 and before 3 in the number pattern. It has no inner nature beyond its place in the structure."},{"head":"In defence of second-order logic","body":"Ordinary first-order logic talks only about objects. Second-order logic can also talk about properties and collections of objects. Shapiro argued that mathematicians need this stronger logic to describe structures like the natural numbers precisely, even though it lacks some neat technical features of first-order logic."},{"head":"Vagueness depends on context","body":"Is a man with a certain number of hairs bald? Shapiro suggests that in borderline cases, competent speakers are free to go either way, and what has been said in a conversation shapes what counts as correct. This explains why there seems to be no fixed sharp line."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Thinking about Mathematics (2000), an introductory textbook that surveys the main views in philosophy of mathematics before setting out his own structuralism.","critics":"John Burgess and Jukka Keränen raised the 'identity problem': in some structures, such as the complex numbers, two positions (i and -i) play exactly the same role, so if objects are nothing but positions it is unclear what makes them two different things.","legacy":"Shapiro's ante rem structuralism is one of the leading accounts of what mathematics is about, and his defence of second-order logic shaped later debates in philosophy of logic.","quote":null},"scheffler":{"life":["Samuel Scheffler was born in 1951. He began his career when moral philosophy was dominated by debates about consequentialism, the view that we should always bring about the best overall outcome. Critics such as Bernard Williams had argued that this leaves no room for a person's own life, loves and commitments. Scheffler set out to find a middle path.","He studied at Harvard and earned his PhD at Princeton in 1977 under Thomas Nagel. He taught at the University of California, Berkeley, for 31 years before moving in 2008 to New York University, where he is University Professor of Philosophy and Law. His first book, The Rejection of Consequentialism (1982), set out his position. His Tanner Lectures at Berkeley became the book Death and the Afterlife (2013).","Scheffler's work moves from the structure of moral theory to questions about equality, liberalism and tradition, and later to time, death and future generations. His books often begin with an ordinary attitude, such as why we care about people who will live long after us, and ask what it reveals about our values."],"timeline":[[1951,"Born"],[1977,"Earns PhD at Princeton"],[1982,"Publishes The Rejection of Consequentialism"],[2001,"Publishes Boundaries and Allegiances"],[2008,"Moves from Berkeley to New York University"],[2013,"Publishes Death and the Afterlife"],[2018,"Publishes Why Worry About Future Generations?"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The personal prerogative","body":"Scheffler accepts that it is always permissible to do the most good. But he argues that morality also gives each person a 'prerogative' to give extra weight to their own projects. You may spend time on your music or your family even if that time could do slightly more good elsewhere."},{"head":"No absolute side-constraints","body":"Many moral theories forbid some acts, such as killing an innocent person, even to prevent more killings. Scheffler found these 'constraints' much harder to justify than the prerogative. His hybrid view keeps the prerogative but drops the constraints."},{"head":"The collective afterlife","body":"Imagine you knew that all human life would end 30 days after your own death. Scheffler argues that many of your projects, like curing a disease or writing a book, would suddenly seem pointless. This suggests we depend on future people, whom we will never meet, for our lives to feel meaningful."},{"head":"Valuing as caring","body":"To value something, such as a friendship or a tradition, is not just to think it good. It involves emotional attachment and seeing it as giving you reasons to act. Scheffler uses this idea to explain loyalty and partiality."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Given the title The Rejection of Consequentialism, Scheffler is an opponent of consequentialism.","truth":"His book rejects only part of consequentialism: he keeps the idea that it is always permissible to do the most good, while adding a prerogative to favour one's own projects."},"startHere":"Read the first lecture of Death and the Afterlife (2013). It opens with a striking thought experiment about the end of humanity and is easy to follow.","critics":"Shelly Kagan, in The Limits of Morality (1989), argued that a prerogative to favour oneself cannot be justified without also accepting the constraints Scheffler rejects, so his hybrid is unstable.","legacy":"Scheffler's 'agent-centred prerogative' became a standard term in ethics, and his work on the collective afterlife opened new discussion of our ties to future generations.","quote":null},"davies_b":{"life":["Brian Davies was born in 1951 and trained in England. He is a Dominican friar, a member of the Catholic religious order founded in the 13th century to which Thomas Aquinas belonged. He came to philosophy of religion when many analytic philosophers pictured God as a person much like us but without limits, an approach Davies came to reject.","He studied theology at the University of Bristol and took his doctorate at King's College London in 1976. From 1982 to 1995 he taught theology and philosophy at Blackfriars, the Dominican house of studies in Oxford, and served as its Regent. In 1995 he became Distinguished Professor of Philosophy at Fordham University in New York. His textbook An Introduction to the Philosophy of Religion (1982) has gone through four editions.","Davies is best known as an interpreter of Thomas Aquinas, on whom he has written many books, including guides to the Summa Theologiae and the Summa Contra Gentiles. He writes clearly for students and returns often to one theme: that much modern talk about God quietly treats God as one more thing in the universe."],"timeline":[[1951,"Born"],[1976,"Earns PhD at King's College London"],[1982,"Publishes An Introduction to the Philosophy of Religion"],[1992,"Publishes The Thought of Thomas Aquinas"],[1995,"Becomes Distinguished Professor of Philosophy at Fordham"],[2006,"Publishes The Reality of God and the Problem of Evil"],[2014,"Publishes a guide and commentary on Aquinas's Summa Theologiae"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Classical theism","body":"Davies defends the traditional view of God found in Aquinas. God is not a very large or powerful being among others. God is the reason why there is anything at all, utterly simple and unchanging, and quite unlike any creature."},{"head":"God is not a moral agent","body":"A moral agent, like a parent or a doctor, has duties and can do better or worse at meeting them. Davies argues that God is not that kind of being and has no duties to creatures. So God is good in the sense of being the source of all goodness, not in the sense of being a well-behaved person."},{"head":"Rethinking the problem of evil","body":"The usual problem of evil asks why a morally perfect being would allow suffering. If God is not a moral agent, Davies argues, the question rests on a confusion, much like asking whether the number seven is kind. This does not make suffering less real, but it changes what we can ask of God."},{"head":"Creation as ongoing dependence","body":"For Aquinas and Davies, creation does not just mean starting the universe long ago. It means that everything depends on God at every moment simply for existing, as a song depends on the singer while it is being sung."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the chapter on evil in An Introduction to the Philosophy of Religion. It explains the standard debate and then shows clearly why Davies thinks it rests on a mistake.","critics":"Theists who think of God as a perfect person, such as Richard Swinburne, hold that God is good in a moral sense and acts for reasons. Critics on both sides worry that Davies's view leaves 'God is good' with too little content to comfort or guide believers.","legacy":"Davies is one of the most widely read modern interpreters of Aquinas, and his work helped revive classical theism as a live position in analytic philosophy of religion.","quote":null},"wolf":{"life":["Susan Wolf was born in 1952. She trained at Princeton in the 1970s, when analytic moral philosophy was returning to large questions about how to live after decades focused on the meaning of moral words. She has worked on three linked topics: how much morality demands of us, free will and responsibility, and what makes a life meaningful.","She studied philosophy and mathematics at Yale and earned her PhD at Princeton in 1978 under Thomas Nagel. She taught at Harvard, the University of Maryland and Johns Hopkins University, where she chaired the philosophy department, before moving in 2002 to the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill as Edna J. Koury Professor of Philosophy. Her essay 'Moral Saints' (1982) is still widely read and taught.","Wolf writes in a plain, conversational style and often starts from examples drawn from ordinary life and fiction. She has co-edited a book on love in philosophy, film and fiction. Rather than building a grand system, she typically defends a moderate position between more extreme views, showing what each side gets right."],"timeline":[[1952,"Born"],[1978,"Earns PhD at Princeton"],[1982,"Publishes 'Moral Saints'"],[1987,"Publishes 'Sanity and the Metaphysics of Responsibility'"],[1990,"Publishes Freedom Within Reason"],[2002,"Joins the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill"],[2010,"Publishes Meaning in Life and Why It Matters"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Moral saints","body":"Imagine someone whose every act is as morally good as possible. Wolf argues that such a person would be oddly unattractive: they would have no time for cooking, music or jokes that are not useful to others. So moral perfection should not be the single ideal for a human life."},{"head":"Meaning: loving what is worth loving","body":"A meaningful life, Wolf argues, comes when subjective attraction meets objective attractiveness. You must care about your activities, and they must also be worth caring about. Someone devoted to helping refugees or mastering an instrument has meaning; someone passionate only about counting blades of grass does not, however happy they are."},{"head":"The Reason View of freedom","body":"Wolf argues that we are responsible when we can act in line with good reasons. This creates an asymmetry: a person who does the right thing deserves praise even if they could not have done otherwise, but blame requires that they could have recognised and acted on the right reasons."},{"head":"Sanity and responsibility","body":"Wolf imagines JoJo, the son of a cruel dictator, raised to share his father's values. JoJo acts on his own deepest desires, yet we hesitate to blame him. She concludes that responsibility needs 'sanity', the ability to see right from wrong, not only acting on one's own desires."}],"misconception":{"myth":"In 'Moral Saints' Wolf argues that morality does not really matter.","truth":"She argues that morality should not be the only ideal shaping a life, not that it is unimportant; she accepts that we have real moral obligations."},"startHere":"Read her essay 'Moral Saints' (1982). It is short, witty and full of examples, and it introduces her concern with how morality fits into a good life.","critics":"Critics of her view of meaning ask who decides which activities are objectively worth caring about, and worry that the standard will reflect the tastes of academics. Others reply that 'Moral Saints' misreads what moral theories actually demand.","legacy":"Wolf's work made meaning in life a mainstream topic in analytic philosophy, and her JoJo case and 'Moral Saints' are standard readings in ethics courses.","quote":null},"fischer":{"life":["John Martin Fischer was born in 1952 in the United States. He entered philosophy soon after Harry Frankfurt's 1969 paper challenged an old assumption: that you are responsible for what you did only if you could have done otherwise. Frankfurt's examples reshaped the free will debate, and Fischer built much of his career on them.","He studied at Stanford and earned his PhD at Cornell in 1982. He taught at Yale for almost a decade before moving to the University of California, Riverside, where he became Distinguished Professor of Philosophy. In 2017 he was named a University Professor of the University of California. He was president of the Pacific Division of the American Philosophical Association, and from 2012 he led the Immortality Project, a three-year research programme funded by the John Templeton Foundation.","Fischer works patiently through examples and objections, and some of his main ideas were developed with others, notably with Mark Ravizza in Responsibility and Control (1998). Alongside free will, he has written extensively on death: whether death is bad for the one who dies, whether living forever would be good, and what near-death experiences show."],"timeline":[[1952,"Born"],[1982,"Earns PhD at Cornell"],[1994,"Publishes The Metaphysics of Free Will"],[1998,"Publishes Responsibility and Control with Mark Ravizza"],[2012,"Begins leading the Immortality Project"],[2013,"Serves as president of the APA Pacific Division"],[2020,"Publishes Death, Immortality, and Meaning in Life"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Semicompatibilism","body":"Determinism is the view that every event is fixed by earlier causes. Fischer leaves open whether determinism rules out the ability to do otherwise. But he argues that moral responsibility does not need that ability, so we can be responsible even if determinism is true."},{"head":"Frankfurt cases","body":"Suppose a scientist has secretly fitted a device to Jones's brain that would make him vote for a certain candidate if he were about to vote otherwise. Jones votes for that candidate on his own, so the device never acts. Jones could not have done otherwise, yet he seems responsible. Fischer uses such cases to separate responsibility from alternative possibilities."},{"head":"Guidance control","body":"What responsibility does need, Fischer argues, is 'guidance control'. You must act from your own mechanism of decision, one that would respond to good reasons if they were present. A person acting under hypnosis or compulsion lacks this; an ordinary person choosing for reasons has it."},{"head":"Immortality need not be boring","body":"Against Bernard Williams, who argued that an endless life would become tedious, Fischer suggests that some pleasures, such as good food, music or friendship, can be enjoyed again and again. So an immortal life could still be worth living."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Fischer's chapter in Four Views on Free Will (2007), written with Robert Kane, Derk Pereboom and Manuel Vargas. It sets out semicompatibilism clearly and shows the rival views beside it.","critics":"Derk Pereboom's 'manipulation argument' imagines a person whose reasons-responsive decisions were designed by neuroscientists; he argues such a person is not responsible, and that ordinary determined agents are no different. Libertarians such as Robert Kane deny that Frankfurt cases succeed.","legacy":"Fischer's semicompatibilism and his account of guidance control are among the most discussed theories of moral responsibility, and he helped make death and immortality active research topics.","quote":null},"hurka":{"life":["Thomas Hurka was born in Toronto, Canada, in 1952. He entered moral philosophy when it was dominated by utilitarianism and by theories of rights. An older tradition stretching back to Aristotle, which says the good life is the full development of human nature, had fallen out of favour, and Hurka set out to give it a modern defence.","He studied at the University of Toronto and then at Oxford, where he completed a doctorate on perfectionist ethics in 1980, supervised by R. M. Hare. He taught at the University of Calgary from 1978 to 2002, then returned to the University of Toronto, where he holds the Jackman Distinguished Chair in Philosophical Studies. He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of Canada in 2001.","Hurka writes both technical philosophy and books for general readers. Principles (1993) collected short essays on everyday ethical questions, and The Best Things in Life (2011) explains for a general audience which goods make a life go well. He has also written a history of British moral philosophers from Henry Sidgwick to A. C. Ewing."],"timeline":[[1952,"Born in Toronto"],[1978,"Begins teaching at the University of Calgary"],[1980,"Completes his Oxford doctorate on perfectionist ethics"],[1993,"Publishes Perfectionism"],[2001,"Publishes Virtue, Vice, and Value"],[2011,"Publishes The Best Things in Life"],[2014,"Publishes British Ethical Theorists from Sidgwick to Ewing"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Perfectionism","body":"Hurka argues that what is good for us is developing the capacities central to human nature: our bodies, our knowledge and our ability to act rationally. A life rich in understanding and skilled achievement goes better than a life of passive pleasure, even if the person is equally content."},{"head":"Goods we need not want","body":"Knowledge and achievement are good for us whether or not we desire them. Someone who never cared about learning would still be missing something valuable. This is what separates perfectionism from theories that measure well-being by getting what you want."},{"head":"The value of achievement","body":"Achievements are especially valuable when they are difficult and when they involve organising many steps towards a goal, like climbing a mountain or writing a symphony. The challenge itself is part of what makes them worth pursuing."},{"head":"Virtue as loving the good","body":"Hurka offers a 'recursive' account of virtue. If pleasure and knowledge are good, then loving them for their own sake is also good, and that loving is what virtue is. Hating good things, or loving evil ones such as another's pain, is vice."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Best Things in Life (2011), a short book for general readers that discusses pleasure, knowledge, achievement, virtue and love with everyday examples.","critics":"John Rawls argued that a just state should not favour any particular picture of the good life, and many critics worry that perfectionism ranks some lives above others in an elitist way and invites paternalism.","legacy":"Hurka's Perfectionism is the main modern statement of perfectionist ethics, and his recursive account of virtue is widely discussed.","quote":null},"carruthers":{"life":["Peter Carruthers was born in 1952 and trained in Britain. His career coincided with the growth of cognitive science, the combined study of the mind by psychology, neuroscience, linguistics and philosophy. He is one of the philosophers who draws most closely on experimental psychology, using its findings to test philosophical claims about thought, consciousness and self-knowledge.","He studied at the University of Leeds and completed his doctorate at Oxford under Michael Dummett. He taught at Essex, Queen's University Belfast, St Andrews and Oxford, and became Professor of Philosophy at the University of Sheffield, where he founded and directed the Hang Seng Centre for Cognitive Studies. In 2001 he moved to the University of Maryland.","Carruthers is willing to follow arguments to unpopular conclusions. In The Animals Issue (1992) he argued that animals lack moral rights of their own. His view on whether animals are conscious has shifted over time: in Human and Animal Minds (2019) he argued that there may be no fact of the matter, and that consciousness is not what decides moral standing anyway."],"timeline":[[1952,"Born"],[1992,"Publishes The Animals Issue"],[1996,"Publishes Language, Thought and Consciousness"],[2000,"Publishes Phenomenal Consciousness"],[2001,"Moves to the University of Maryland"],[2006,"Publishes The Architecture of the Mind"],[2011,"Publishes The Opacity of Mind"],[2019,"Publishes Human and Animal Minds"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Morality as a contract","body":"Carruthers holds a contractualist view: moral rules are those that rational agents would agree to live by. Animals cannot take part in such an agreement, so they have no moral rights of their own. Cruelty to animals is still wrong, he argues, because it shows and fosters a bad character."},{"head":"Higher-order theory of consciousness","body":"On Carruthers's view, an experience is conscious when it is available to be the object of a thought about that very experience. Seeing red becomes conscious seeing because it can be noticed as a seeing of red. This aims to explain consciousness in terms of ordinary mental functions."},{"head":"We interpret ourselves","body":"We usually think we know our own beliefs and decisions directly. Carruthers argues, using psychological experiments, that we often learn them by interpreting our own behaviour and inner speech, much as we read other people. This is why people confidently give wrong reasons for their own choices."},{"head":"A mind of many modules","body":"Carruthers defends 'massive modularity': the mind is made up of many specialised systems, each shaped by evolution for a task, such as recognising faces or tracking cheaters. Human flexibility comes from how these modules work together."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapters of The Animals Issue (1992), which state his contractualist argument plainly and answer the obvious objections.","critics":"Mark Rowlands argued that if contracting parties chose rules without knowing whether they would be human or animal, as in John Rawls's 'veil of ignorance', they would protect animals. Others, including Peter Singer, press the problem that contract views struggle to protect humans who also cannot take part in agreements, such as infants.","legacy":"Carruthers is a leading figure in empirically informed philosophy of mind, and his higher-order theory and self-interpretation view are central to debates on consciousness and self-knowledge.","quote":null},"tronto":{"life":["Joan Tronto was born in 1952. She trained as a political theorist in the 1970s, when feminist thinkers were asking why work traditionally done by women, such as raising children and nursing the sick, was treated as private and left out of theories of justice. In 1982 the psychologist Carol Gilligan argued that many people reason about morality through care and relationships, starting a debate about an ethics of care.","She studied at Oberlin College and earned her PhD at Princeton in 1981. She taught at Bowdoin College and then at Hunter College and the Graduate Center of the City University of New York, before becoming professor of political science at the University of Minnesota. Her book Moral Boundaries (1993) argued for treating care as a political concept, and Caring Democracy (2013) extended the argument to democratic politics.","Tronto has often worked with others. With Berenice Fisher she wrote an early and much-cited definition of care, and she co-edited a reader on women and politics. Several of her works have been translated into French, and her ideas are widely used by scholars of nursing, social policy and public administration as well as by political theorists."],"timeline":[[1952,"Born"],[1981,"Earns PhD at Princeton"],[1987,"Publishes 'Beyond Gender Difference to a Theory of Care'"],[1990,"Publishes a definition of care with Berenice Fisher"],[1993,"Publishes Moral Boundaries"],[2013,"Publishes Caring Democracy"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Care is everywhere","body":"With Berenice Fisher, Tronto defined care very broadly: everything we do to maintain, continue and repair our world so that we can live in it as well as possible. That includes cooking, cleaning, nursing and teaching, but also maintaining roads and water systems. On this view care is central to human life, not a private extra."},{"head":"Phases of care","body":"Tronto divides care into phases: noticing a need (caring about), taking responsibility for it (taking care of), doing the actual work (care-giving), and the response of the person cared for (care-receiving). Later she added 'caring with', when care becomes shared and builds trust. Each phase has its own virtue, such as attentiveness or responsibility."},{"head":"Moral boundaries","body":"Tronto argues that three boundaries have kept care out of politics: between morality and politics, between a detached 'moral point of view' and real involvement, and between public and private life. Redrawing these boundaries lets care be discussed as a matter of justice."},{"head":"Privileged irresponsibility","body":"People with power can often pay others, typically poorer women and migrants, to do care work and then ignore that work. Tronto calls this 'privileged irresponsibility'. A caring democracy would openly debate how responsibilities for care are shared."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The ethics of care is a special 'women's morality' based on women's natural nurturing instincts.","truth":"Tronto argued, from her 1987 essay onwards, that care should not be tied to gender at all; she treats it as a human practice and a political question about how care work is distributed."},"startHere":"Read the chapter of Moral Boundaries (1993) where she defines care and sets out its phases. It is the core of her theory and is often reprinted on its own.","critics":"Critics argue that care is naturally partial, focused on people close to us, and so cannot do the work of impartial justice in politics. Some feminists also worry that praising care risks keeping women in caring roles.","legacy":"Tronto turned the ethics of care from a theory of personal morality into a political theory, and her definition and phases of care are used across politics, nursing and social policy.","quote":null},"waldron":{"life":["Jeremy Waldron was born in New Zealand in 1953. He trained in both law and philosophy as legal and political philosophy in the English-speaking world was being reshaped by John Rawls and Ronald Dworkin. A major question of the time was how far courts, not elected legislatures, should settle disputed questions about rights.","He studied philosophy and law at the University of Otago in New Zealand and took his doctorate at Oxford. He has taught at the University of California, Berkeley, Columbia University and New York University, where he is University Professor in the School of Law. From 2010 to 2014 he also held the Chichele Professorship of Social and Political Theory at Oxford.","Waldron writes on a wide range of topics: property, the rule of law, torture, homelessness, human dignity and hate speech. He is known for taking legislatures seriously as places where free and equal citizens settle disagreements, at a time when many theorists focused on judges. He often writes for general readers, including essays in the New York Review of Books."],"timeline":[[1953,"Born in New Zealand"],[1988,"Publishes The Right to Private Property"],[1999,"Publishes Law and Disagreement and The Dignity of Legislation"],[2006,"Publishes 'The Core of the Case Against Judicial Review'"],[2010,"Becomes Chichele Professor of Social and Political Theory at Oxford"],[2012,"Publishes The Harm in Hate Speech"],[2017,"Publishes One Another's Equals"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Disagreement is permanent","body":"Reasonable people disagree deeply about rights, such as abortion or free speech, and that will not go away. Since we still need common rules, Waldron calls this the 'circumstances of politics'. The fair way to decide is a procedure that gives each citizen an equal say."},{"head":"Against strong judicial review","body":"In a working democracy with healthy institutions, Waldron argues, it is wrong for judges to overrule laws passed by elected legislatures on rights questions. Judges disagree too, often by narrow votes, so letting them decide does not remove disagreement; it just takes the decision away from citizens."},{"head":"The harm in hate speech","body":"Hate speech, such as posters saying a minority group is subhuman, attacks people's standing as equal members of society. Waldron argues that it undermines the public assurance everyone needs that they will be treated decently, so there is a case for some legal limits."},{"head":"Dignity as high rank for all","body":"Dignity was once a privilege of nobles. Waldron argues that modern human rights extend that high rank to everyone, so that every person is treated as a kind of noble before the law."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'The Core of the Case Against Judicial Review' (2006), a long but clear article that states his main argument and answers objections step by step.","critics":"Ronald Dworkin and other defenders of judicial review argued that courts can protect minorities and basic rights against majorities, and that democracy itself requires some rights to be beyond the reach of ordinary votes. On hate speech, Dworkin and others argued that bans undermine the legitimacy of laws by silencing some citizens.","legacy":"Waldron is one of the most influential legal philosophers of his generation, and his case against judicial review is central to debates about constitutions and democracy.","quote":null},"pogge":{"life":["Thomas Pogge was born in 1953. He studied at Harvard under John Rawls, whose A Theory of Justice (1971) set out principles of justice for a single society. By the 1980s and 1990s, globalisation, international trade rules and persistent extreme poverty were raising the question of whether such principles should apply to the whole world.","He earned his PhD at Harvard in 1983 with a dissertation on Kant, Rawls and global justice, supervised by Rawls. His first book, Realizing Rawls (1989), argued for extending Rawls's ideas to the global level. He taught at Columbia University and later became Leitner Professor of Philosophy and International Affairs at Yale, where he directs the Global Justice Program. World Poverty and Human Rights (2002) set out his main argument.","Pogge pairs philosophical argument with practical proposals. With the economist Aidan Hollis he designed the Health Impact Fund, a scheme to reward drug companies according to how much their medicines improve health, so that new drugs can be sold cheaply. He also helped launch Academics Stand Against Poverty and was involved with Giving What We Can from its start in 2009."],"timeline":[[1953,"Born"],[1983,"Earns PhD at Harvard under John Rawls"],[1989,"Publishes Realizing Rawls"],[2002,"Publishes World Poverty and Human Rights"],[2008,"Publishes The Health Impact Fund with Aidan Hollis"],[2010,"Publishes Politics as Usual"],[2013,"Receives the Gregory Kavka Prize in Political Philosophy"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Harm, not just failure to help","body":"Most people think the rich have at most a duty to help the poor, a duty of charity. Pogge argues that rich countries do something worse: they help design and enforce global rules that keep people poor. That makes poverty a matter of harming, which we have a strict duty to stop."},{"head":"The global institutional order","body":"Rules on trade, intellectual property and finance are made largely by powerful countries. Pogge points to examples such as the 'resource privilege', which lets anyone who seizes power in a country sell its oil or minerals legally. This rewards dictators and gives them reasons to fight for control."},{"head":"Human rights and institutions","body":"Pogge argues that a human right is mainly a claim on social institutions. A global order violates human rights when people could easily be spared severe poverty under different rules, and the rules are foreseeably keeping them poor."},{"head":"The Health Impact Fund","body":"Drug companies earn profits by charging high prices under patents, which puts medicines out of reach for the poor. Pogge and Hollis propose an optional fund that pays companies by how much health their drug produces, so they can sell it at cost and still be rewarded."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his article 'World Poverty and Human Rights' (2005) in the journal Ethics & International Affairs, a short summary of the argument of his 2002 book.","critics":"Mathias Risse argued that the global order has in fact helped reduce poverty, and that poverty depends mostly on the quality of a country's own institutions. Others question whether the causal links Pogge draws between global rules and poverty are as strong as he claims.","legacy":"Pogge made global justice a central topic in political philosophy and helped shift debate about world poverty from charity towards questions of harm and institutional responsibility.","quote":null},"mcmahan":{"life":["Jeff McMahan was born in the United States in 1954. He entered moral philosophy as Derek Parfit and others were transforming practical ethics, applying careful reasoning to life-and-death questions such as abortion, euthanasia, self-defence and war. The Cold War debate about nuclear weapons was also a pressing public issue.","He studied English at the University of the South in Tennessee, went to Oxford as a Rhodes Scholar, and completed his doctorate at Cambridge in 1986. He taught at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign from 1986 to 2003 and at Rutgers University from 2003 to 2014. In 2014 he became White's Professor of Moral Philosophy at Oxford.","His first books, on British nuclear weapons and on Ronald Reagan's foreign policy, addressed current politics, and his later work stays close to real moral problems. He is known for following arguments where they lead, even to conclusions many find uncomfortable, as when he asked in a 2010 essay whether we should prevent predators from killing their prey if we could."],"timeline":[[1954,"Born"],[1981,"Publishes British Nuclear Weapons: For and Against"],[1986,"Completes doctorate at Cambridge"],[2002,"Publishes The Ethics of Killing: Problems at the Margins of Life"],[2003,"Moves to Rutgers University"],[2009,"Publishes Killing in War"],[2014,"Becomes White's Professor of Moral Philosophy at Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Soldiers are not morally equal","body":"The traditional view says soldiers on both sides of a war are equally entitled to fight, whatever the justice of their cause. McMahan rejects this. A soldier invading another country without just cause has no right to kill the defenders, just as a robber has no right to kill a homeowner defending herself."},{"head":"Liability to be harmed","body":"Why may you harm an attacker? McMahan's answer is that a person becomes liable to defensive harm when they are morally responsible for an unjust threat. Their own choices cost them their usual protection, which is why a homeowner may use force against a burglar but not against an innocent passer-by."},{"head":"Time-relative interests","body":"How bad is a death for the one who dies? McMahan argues it depends partly on how strongly that individual is psychologically connected to their own future. Since a fetus or newborn has few such connections, its death is a smaller loss to it than the death of an adult, though that is not the only thing that matters."},{"head":"The problem of predation","body":"If animal suffering matters, McMahan asks, should we, if we safely could, stop predators from killing prey? He treated this as a serious question to show how far our views about animals stretch."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Killing in War (2009), which states his challenge to the moral equality of soldiers using clear analogies with ordinary self-defence.","critics":"Michael Walzer, whose Just and Unjust Wars (1977) defended the equality of combatants, and later writers such as Seth Lazar argued that McMahan's view ignores the realities of war, where soldiers cannot easily judge their cause and where mutual rules limit killing.","legacy":"McMahan led the 'revisionist' movement in just war theory, and his work on killing, self-defence and the margins of life is central to modern practical ethics.","quote":null},"sunstein":{"life":["Cass Sunstein was born in 1954 in Concord, Massachusetts. He trained as a lawyer as American law schools were absorbing ideas from economics. From the 1970s psychologists such as Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky had shown that people make systematic errors in judgement, findings that fed into behavioural economics. Sunstein became one of the main figures applying them to law and public policy.","He studied at Harvard College and Harvard Law School, then clerked for Supreme Court Justice Thurgood Marshall. He taught at the University of Chicago Law School for 27 years before moving to Harvard in 2008, where he is Robert Walmsley University Professor. From 2009 to 2012 he served in the Obama administration as head of the Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs, which oversees federal regulation. He received the Holberg Prize in 2018.","Sunstein is extremely prolific, having written or co-written dozens of books on constitutional law, regulation, risk, group polarisation, social media and even Star Wars. His best-known book, Nudge (2008), written with the economist Richard Thaler, reached a very wide audience. Later, with Daniel Kahneman and Olivier Sibony, he wrote Noise (2021), on unwanted variation in human judgement."],"timeline":[[1954,"Born in Concord, Massachusetts"],[1978,"Graduates from Harvard Law School"],[2001,"Publishes Republic.com"],[2008,"Publishes Nudge with Richard Thaler and moves to Harvard"],[2009,"Becomes head of the Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs (to 2012)"],[2018,"Receives the Holberg Prize"],[2021,"Publishes Noise with Daniel Kahneman and Olivier Sibony"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Choice architecture","body":"Every choice is presented in some way, and the way matters. Putting fruit at eye level in a school canteen leads more children to choose it. Since someone must decide how choices are arranged, Sunstein argues, it is better to arrange them deliberately to help people."},{"head":"Nudges","body":"A nudge is a change in how choices are presented that steers people without forbidding anything or changing costs much. Examples include automatic enrolment in a pension scheme, with a simple way to opt out, or a reminder text before an appointment."},{"head":"Libertarian paternalism","body":"Sunstein and Thaler argue it is possible to steer people towards choices that make their own lives better while fully preserving their freedom to choose otherwise. The phrase sounds like a contradiction, but the idea is that defaults can help without forcing."},{"head":"Group polarisation","body":"When like-minded people discuss an issue together, they tend to end up holding more extreme views than they started with. Sunstein argues that online echo chambers can intensify this effect and damage democratic debate."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Nudges are ways of taking away people's choices or forcing them to do what the government wants.","truth":"By Sunstein and Thaler's definition, a nudge must leave all options open and be easy to avoid; whether particular nudges are manipulative is a real debate, but bans and mandates are not nudges."},"startHere":"Read the introduction to Nudge (2008), which opens with the canteen example and explains choice architecture and libertarian paternalism in plain terms.","critics":"Jeremy Waldron, reviewing Sunstein's Why Nudge?, argued that nudging treats people as objects to be steered rather than as reasoners to be persuaded, and so fails to respect their dignity. Others warn that governments can misuse nudges or get them wrong.","legacy":"Sunstein helped bring behavioural economics into law and government; 'nudge units' have been set up by many governments, and he is among the most cited legal scholars in the United States.","quote":null},"kagan":{"life":["Shelly Kagan was born in Skokie, Illinois, in 1954. He trained when moral philosophers were arguing about consequentialism, the view that the right act is whichever produces the best outcome. Most people believe morality also lets us favour ourselves and forbids certain acts, such as killing one to save five. Kagan set out to test whether those beliefs can be defended.","He studied at Wesleyan University and earned his PhD at Princeton in 1982 under Thomas Nagel; his dissertation became The Limits of Morality (1989). He taught at the University of Pittsburgh and the University of Illinois at Chicago before moving in 1995 to Yale, where he is Clark Professor of Philosophy. He also wrote a widely used textbook, Normative Ethics (1998).","Kagan became known far beyond academic philosophy through his Yale course on death, recorded in 2007 and released free online through Open Yale Courses; the book Death (2012) grew out of it. He is known as a lively lecturer who enjoys arguing both sides of a question, and he took part in a public debate with William Lane Craig on whether God is necessary for morality."],"timeline":[[1954,"Born in Skokie, Illinois"],[1982,"Earns PhD at Princeton"],[1989,"Publishes The Limits of Morality"],[1995,"Joins Yale University"],[1998,"Publishes Normative Ethics"],[2007,"Records his Yale course on death, later released online"],[2012,"Publishes Death and The Geometry of Desert"],[2019,"Publishes How to Count Animals, More or Less"]],"ideas":[{"head":"No permission to favour yourself","body":"Most people think you may spend money on a holiday even though it could save a life elsewhere. Kagan argues that the usual defences of this permission fail. If he is right, morality requires us to do as much good as we can, which is far more than ordinary morality asks."},{"head":"Doubts about constraints","body":"Ordinary morality also says some acts are forbidden even if they produce the best outcome, such as killing one person to save five. Kagan examines the standard arguments for such 'constraints' and argues that none of them work."},{"head":"Why death is bad","body":"Kagan defends the 'deprivation' account: death is bad for you mainly because it takes away the good things you would otherwise have had. He uses it to answer the ancient Epicurean argument that death cannot harm you because when it comes you no longer exist."},{"head":"Animals count, but not equally","body":"In How to Count Animals, More or Less, Kagan argues that animals' interests matter morally, but that beings with richer mental lives may count for more. He defends a 'hierarchy' view between equal treatment of all creatures and ignoring animals."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Death (2012) or watch the first lectures of his free Open Yale course on death. Both are aimed at beginners and show his step-by-step style of argument.","critics":"Many argue that a morality with no room to put oneself first is too demanding to be true. Bernard Williams argued that such demands would destroy a person's integrity, and Samuel Scheffler defended a 'prerogative' to favour one's own projects.","legacy":"The Limits of Morality is a standard reference in debates about the demands of morality, and Kagan's course on death made him one of the most widely followed philosophy teachers online.","quote":null},"pinker":{"life":["Steven Pinker was born in Montreal, Canada, in 1954. He trained as cognitive science was replacing behaviourism, which had treated the mind as a blank slate shaped by rewards and punishments. Noam Chomsky had argued that humans are born with a capacity for language, and the computer offered a new model for how a mind might work.","He studied at McGill University and earned his PhD in experimental psychology at Harvard in 1979. He taught at MIT from 1982 to 2003, then moved to Harvard, where he is Johnstone Family Professor of Psychology. His research on how children learn words and grammar, especially regular and irregular verbs such as 'walked' and 'went', led to Words and Rules (1999).","Pinker is known for writing for the general public in a clear, witty style. After The Language Instinct (1994) and How the Mind Works (1997), he took on wider debates in The Blank Slate (2002), The Better Angels of Our Nature (2011), which argued that violence has declined over history, and Enlightenment Now (2018). He has also written a guide to clear writing, The Sense of Style (2014)."],"timeline":[[1954,"Born in Montreal, Canada"],[1979,"Earns PhD at Harvard"],[1994,"Publishes The Language Instinct"],[1997,"Publishes How the Mind Works"],[2002,"Publishes The Blank Slate"],[2003,"Moves from MIT to Harvard"],[2011,"Publishes The Better Angels of Our Nature"],[2018,"Publishes Enlightenment Now"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Language is an instinct","body":"Children everywhere learn complex grammar quickly, without formal teaching, and even invent new rules no adult taught them. Pinker argues that language is a biological adaptation, like a spider's ability to spin webs, not a cultural invention we simply pick up."},{"head":"The mind as computing organs","body":"Pinker combines two ideas. The mind processes information, like a computer. And it is made of many specialised systems shaped by natural selection to solve problems our ancestors faced, such as finding food, choosing mates and judging other people."},{"head":"Against the blank slate","body":"Pinker argues that human nature is not infinitely shaped by culture. Our genes influence personality, intelligence and emotions. Admitting this, he argues, does not threaten equality or moral responsibility, since those are moral commitments, not claims that everyone is born the same."},{"head":"Consciousness may stay a puzzle","body":"Pinker separates 'access' consciousness, the information available for thinking and speaking, which science can study, from 'sentience', the felt quality of experience. He suggests the second may be a problem human minds are not built to solve."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Pinker claims violence has disappeared and that the world no longer has serious problems.","truth":"He argues that rates of violence have fallen over the long run, not that violence has ended, and he acknowledges that declines can reverse."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of How the Mind Works (1997), which lays out the computational and evolutionary picture of the mind with lively examples.","critics":"Jerry Fodor, in The Mind Doesn't Work That Way (2000), argued that Pinker's picture cannot explain how the mind integrates information across everything it knows. Critics of The Better Angels of Our Nature, such as the philosopher John Gray, questioned his reading of the historical data and his optimism.","legacy":"Pinker brought cognitive science and evolutionary psychology to a vast general audience and remains a leading public defender of reason, science and Enlightenment values.","quote":null},"williamson":{"life":["Timothy Williamson was born in Uppsala, Sweden, in 1955, and was educated in England. He trained at Oxford in the 1970s, when philosophy there was strongly shaped by Michael Dummett, who held that questions about reality turn on questions about language and meaning. Williamson came to reject much of this, arguing that philosophy is mainly about the world, not about words or concepts.","He took a first-class degree in mathematics and philosophy at Balliol College, Oxford, in 1976 and a DPhil in 1980. He lectured at Trinity College Dublin, was a fellow of University College, Oxford, and was Professor of Logic and Metaphysics at Edinburgh from 1995 to 2000. From 2000 to 2023 he was Wykeham Professor of Logic at Oxford. He was president of the Aristotelian Society in 2004–2005.","Williamson is known for rigorous arguments, often using formal logic, and for defending bold positions that others had dismissed as obviously false. He also writes for general readers: Tetralogue (2015) is a dialogue between four strangers on a train arguing about truth and relativism, and Doing Philosophy (2017) explains how philosophy works."],"timeline":[[1955,"Born in Uppsala, Sweden"],[1980,"Completes his Oxford DPhil"],[1994,"Publishes Vagueness"],[2000,"Publishes Knowledge and Its Limits and becomes Wykeham Professor of Logic at Oxford"],[2007,"Publishes The Philosophy of Philosophy"],[2013,"Publishes Modal Logic as Metaphysics"],[2015,"Publishes Tetralogue"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Epistemicism about vagueness","body":"Is there a precise number of hairs at which a man stops being bald? Williamson argues yes: vague words have sharp boundaries, fixed by how we use them. We cannot know where the boundaries lie, because our knowledge needs a margin for error, and our use of the word is too finely balanced for us to detect the exact point."},{"head":"Knowledge first","body":"For decades philosophers tried to define knowledge as true belief plus something extra. Williamson argues this gets things backwards. Knowledge is a basic mental state that cannot be broken down, and other notions, such as belief and evidence, should be explained in terms of it."},{"head":"Your evidence is what you know","body":"Williamson argues that a person's evidence is exactly their knowledge. Something counts as your evidence when you know it, so a false belief can never be part of your evidence, however convincing it seems."},{"head":"Necessary existence","body":"In Modal Logic as Metaphysics, Williamson defends 'necessitism': everything that exists exists necessarily. You could have failed to be a person, or to be physical at all, but on his view you could not have been nothing. The view follows, he argues, from the simplest logic of possibility."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Tetralogue (2015), a short dialogue for general readers that shows his views on truth, knowledge and disagreement in action.","critics":"Many philosophers, including Crispin Wright, find epistemicism hard to believe: it seems incredible that a word like 'bald' could have a sharp boundary that nobody could ever discover, and unclear what would fix it.","legacy":"Williamson's 'knowledge first' approach reshaped epistemology, and his defence of epistemicism and necessitism made him one of the most influential analytic philosophers of his generation.","quote":null},"koch":{"life":["Christof Koch was born in 1956 in Kansas City, in the United States, and grew up in several countries, including the Netherlands, Germany and Canada. He trained in physics and biology as neuroscience was gaining new tools to record brain activity. For much of the 20th century many scientists treated consciousness as too vague for serious study, and Koch helped change that.","He studied physics at the University of Tübingen in Germany and earned a PhD in biophysics there in 1982. After working at MIT he joined the California Institute of Technology in 1986. From the late 1980s until Francis Crick's death in 2004, he worked with Crick, co-discoverer of the structure of DNA, on the brain basis of consciousness. In 2011 he joined the Allen Institute for Brain Science in Seattle, later serving as its president and chief scientist.","Koch writes for general readers as well as scientists, and his books mix science with personal reflection. In 1998 he bet the philosopher David Chalmers that a clear neural signature of consciousness would be found within 25 years; in 2023 he conceded that it had not been. He has worked with the neuroscientist Giulio Tononi on integrated information theory, and in 2024 he published Then I Am Myself the World."],"timeline":[[1956,"Born in Kansas City"],[1982,"Earns PhD in biophysics at Tübingen"],[1986,"Joins the California Institute of Technology"],[2004,"Publishes The Quest for Consciousness"],[2011,"Joins the Allen Institute for Brain Science"],[2019,"Publishes The Feeling of Life Itself"],[2023,"Concedes his 25-year bet with David Chalmers"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Neural correlates of consciousness","body":"With Francis Crick, Koch proposed a practical research programme: find the smallest set of brain events that is enough for a particular conscious experience, such as seeing a face. Instead of starting with philosophical definitions, scientists could compare brain activity when a stimulus is consciously seen and when it is not."},{"head":"Integrated information theory","body":"Koch supports Giulio Tononi's theory that consciousness is integrated information, measured by a quantity called Phi. A system is conscious to the degree that its parts causally constrain one another as a unified whole. On this view, consciousness depends on a system's physical structure, not on what it computes."},{"head":"Clever machines need not feel","body":"If consciousness depends on causal structure, a digital computer could imitate human behaviour perfectly and still feel nothing. Koch compares this to a computer simulation of a rainstorm, which never makes anything wet. Only hardware with the right kind of structure could be conscious."},{"head":"Consciousness is widespread","body":"Integrated information theory implies that consciousness comes in degrees and may be present in many animals and other systems. Koch accepts that this brings the view close to panpsychism, the idea that mind is a basic and widespread feature of nature."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Feeling of Life Itself (2019), a short book that explains integrated information theory and its consequences for animals and computers in plain language.","critics":"Computer scientist Scott Aaronson argued that integrated information theory implies that some simple grid-like structures would be more conscious than humans, which he took as a refutation. In 2023 more than a hundred researchers signed an open letter calling the theory pseudoscience, a charge its defenders strongly rejected.","legacy":"Koch, with Crick, helped make consciousness a legitimate subject for laboratory science, and he remains one of its best-known public voices.","quote":null},"dorato":{"life":["Mauro Dorato was born in 1956 and has worked mainly in Italy. He belongs to a generation of philosophers of science who take physics as the main guide to questions about time, space and the laws of nature. Einstein's theory of relativity, which denies a single universal 'now', has long challenged the everyday picture of a present moment moving forward through time.","He took degrees in philosophy and in mathematics at the University of Rome La Sapienza, then, after a Fulbright scholarship to the United States, earned his PhD in philosophy at Johns Hopkins University in 1992. After teaching in Italian schools and holding a postdoctoral post at Padua, he joined Roma Tre University, where he became full professor of philosophy of science in 2005. He is a member of the Academia Europaea.","Dorato has written on time and becoming, the laws of nature and the interpretation of quantum mechanics, often in dialogue with physicists. He served on the first steering committee of the European Philosophy of Science Association, founded in 2007, and has worked as an editor of the European Journal for Philosophy of Science. He publishes in both Italian and English."],"timeline":[[1956,"Born"],[1992,"Earns PhD in philosophy at Johns Hopkins University"],[1995,"Publishes Time and Reality"],[2005,"Becomes full professor of philosophy of science at Roma Tre University"],[2006,"Argues that the presentism/eternalism debate is irrelevant to Minkowski spacetime"],[2007,"Serves on the first steering committee of the European Philosophy of Science Association"]],"ideas":[{"head":"A debate about words","body":"Presentists say only present things exist; eternalists say past, present and future things all exist. Dorato argues that once 'exists' is made precise, the dispute falls apart. If it means 'exists now', presentism is trivially true; if it means 'exists at some time', eternalism is trivially true. Either way there is no deep disagreement about the world."},{"head":"Becoming without a universal now","body":"Relativity says there is no single 'now' shared across the whole universe. Dorato argues that we can still make sense of time passing: events come into being one after another along each object's history. Becoming is local, not a sweeping present moving through all of space."},{"head":"Physics as a guide to metaphysics","body":"Dorato holds that philosophical questions about time and laws of nature should be answered in close contact with our best physical theories. Arguments that ignore relativity or quantum mechanics risk solving problems that the actual world does not pose."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his paper 'The Irrelevance of the Presentist/Eternalist Debate for the Ontology of Minkowski Spacetime' (2006). It is short and states his main claim directly, though some background in relativity helps.","critics":"Philosophers who think the debate is genuine, such as Theodore Sider, argue that a notion of existence that is neither 'existing now' nor 'existing at some time' can be made clear, so presentists and eternalists really do disagree.","legacy":"Dorato's work is a standard reference for the view that relativity and careful analysis of language deflate debates about the reality of past and future.","quote":null},"sorensen":{"life":["Roy Sorensen was born in 1957. He works in a tradition of analytic philosophy that treats puzzles and paradoxes as clues to deep questions. Since the ancient Greeks, philosophers have wrestled with the sorites paradox: if removing one grain of sand never turns a heap into a non-heap, how can there be heaps at all?","After early posts at the University of Delaware and elsewhere, he taught at New York University from 1987, where he chaired the philosophy department, and then at Dartmouth College and at Washington University in St Louis. In 2020 he moved to the University of Texas at Austin. He has also been a professorial fellow at the University of St Andrews in Scotland.","Sorensen is known for playful, inventive writing that mixes riddles and jokes with serious argument. He has written on unusual topics such as shadows, darkness, holes and absences, including Seeing Dark Things (2008), about whether we can literally see shadows and darkness, and a philosophical history of nothing. A Brief History of the Paradox (2003) is aimed at general readers."],"timeline":[[1957,"Born"],[1987,"Joins New York University"],[1988,"Publishes Blindspots"],[1992,"Publishes Thought Experiments"],[2001,"Publishes Vagueness and Contradiction"],[2003,"Publishes A Brief History of the Paradox"],[2008,"Publishes Seeing Dark Things"],[2020,"Moves to the University of Texas at Austin"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Sharp but unknowable boundaries","body":"Sorensen defends epistemicism: a vague word like 'tall' has a precise cut-off, even though nobody can ever know where it is. Our ignorance, not any gap in reality, is what makes the boundary seem blurry. He thinks this is the price of keeping ordinary logic."},{"head":"Blindspots","body":"Some truths are perfectly consistent but cannot be known or believed by a particular person. 'It is raining but I do not believe it' could be true, yet I can never sincerely believe it. Sorensen uses such 'blindspots' to explain a range of paradoxes, including the surprise examination paradox."},{"head":"Thought experiments are real experiments","body":"Sorensen argues that thought experiments, like imagining Galileo's falling bodies, are experiments carried out in the mind. They can test theories much as laboratory experiments do, though they can also mislead in similar ways."},{"head":"We can see absences","body":"Can you see a shadow, or darkness, or a hole? Sorensen argues that we really can perceive such absences, which forces us to rethink what perception is."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read A Brief History of the Paradox (2003), written for general readers, which traces famous paradoxes from the ancient world onwards in short, entertaining chapters.","critics":"Many philosophers find it hard to believe that vague words have sharp boundaries no one can discover, and ask what in our usage could possibly fix such a precise line.","legacy":"Sorensen is a leading writer on paradoxes and vagueness, and his work on blindspots and thought experiments is widely cited.","quote":null},"wallace":{"life":["R. Jay Wallace was born in 1957. He trained when philosophers were still responding to P. F. Strawson's 1962 essay Freedom and Resentment, which argued that moral responsibility is grounded in emotions such as resentment, indignation and gratitude, not in metaphysical facts about free will. Wallace developed this idea into a full theory of responsibility.","He studied at Williams College, took a BPhil at Oxford in 1983 and earned his PhD at Princeton in 1988. He taught at the University of Pennsylvania from 1988 to 1996 and at the Humboldt University in Berlin, before joining the University of California, Berkeley, in 1999, where he chaired the philosophy department from 2005 to 2010. He was elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in 2021.","Wallace works on moral psychology, practical reason and the foundations of morality, and has close ties to German philosophy through his teaching and visiting posts in Berlin and Bielefeld. His books include The View from Here (2013), on regret and affirming one's own life, and The Moral Nexus (2019), which argues that morality is about what we owe to one another in our relationships."],"timeline":[[1957,"Born"],[1988,"Earns PhD at Princeton"],[1994,"Publishes Responsibility and the Moral Sentiments"],[1999,"Joins the University of California, Berkeley"],[2006,"Publishes Normativity and the Will"],[2013,"Publishes The View from Here"],[2019,"Publishes The Moral Nexus"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Responsibility and the reactive emotions","body":"Following Strawson, Wallace argues that holding someone responsible means being prone to feelings like resentment or indignation when they break moral expectations we accept. Blaming a friend who lied to you is not a cold judgement; it is a response tied to expectations you care about."},{"head":"Fairness requires capacity","body":"When is it fair to hold someone responsible? Wallace argues it is fair when they have the powers of reflective self-control: the ability to understand moral reasons and to guide their behaviour by them. These powers do not require freedom from determinism."},{"head":"Morality is relational","body":"In The Moral Nexus, Wallace argues that moral duties are owed to particular people. When you break a promise, you do not just do something wrong; you wrong the person you promised. Morality is a web of claims people have on each other."},{"head":"Affirmation and regret","body":"Wallace asks how we can affirm the lives we have while regretting the mistakes or injustices that led to them. A parent might regret a reckless decision yet be unable to regret the child that resulted. He explores how attachment shapes what we can coherently regret."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapters of Responsibility and the Moral Sentiments (1994), which explain the link between blame, emotion and expectations before he builds his full theory.","critics":"Incompatibilists such as Derk Pereboom argue that resentment and blame are only fair if people deserve them in a deep sense that determinism would rule out, so linking responsibility to emotions does not settle the free will problem.","legacy":"Wallace's book is one of the main developments of Strawson's approach to responsibility, and his relational view of morality is an important position in current ethics.","quote":null},"draper":{"life":["Paul Draper was born in 1957 in the United States. He trained in the 1980s, when philosophers of religion had largely accepted Alvin Plantinga's argument that evil is not logically inconsistent with God's existence. The debate moved to a new question: does the suffering in the world make God's existence less likely, even if not impossible?","He studied at the University of California, Irvine, earning his PhD in 1985 with a dissertation on the evidential problem of evil. He taught at Florida International University from 1987 to 2006 and then moved to Purdue University. He edited the journal Philo from 2007 to 2012 and has been an editor for philosophy of religion at the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy.","Draper is known for presenting the case against theism while taking theistic replies seriously. In 1997 he debated William Lane Craig on God's existence. He is credited with coining the term 'sceptical theism' for the reply that we should not expect to see God's reasons for allowing evil. He also edited God or Blind Nature? (2007), an online collection of debates."],"timeline":[[1957,"Born"],[1985,"Earns PhD at the University of California, Irvine"],[1987,"Begins teaching at Florida International University"],[1989,"Publishes 'Pain and Pleasure: An Evidential Problem for Theists'"],[1997,"Debates William Lane Craig on God's existence"],[2006,"Moves to Purdue University"],[2007,"Becomes editor of the journal Philo (to 2012)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The hypothesis of indifference","body":"Draper compares two hypotheses. Theism says a good God made the world. The 'hypothesis of indifference' says that no one, good or evil, made the world for the sake of creatures. He argues that the actual pattern of pain and pleasure we find is much more what we would expect if indifference were true."},{"head":"Evidence, not proof","body":"Draper does not claim that evil proves God does not exist. He argues that suffering is evidence against God, raising or lowering probabilities, much as a detective weighs clues. Other evidence might still outweigh it, so the question remains open."},{"head":"Pain and pleasure follow biology","body":"Pain and pleasure in living things are closely tied to survival and reproduction, as natural selection would predict. Draper argues that this is surprising if a loving God designed things for creatures' good, but just what we should expect on the indifference hypothesis."},{"head":"Sceptical theism","body":"Draper named a key theistic reply: given the limits of human understanding, we should not expect to grasp God's reasons for allowing evil. He argues this reply has costs, since it also undercuts our ability to know what God would want in other matters."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Pain and Pleasure: An Evidential Problem for Theists' (1989). It is a journal article with some probability notation, but its central comparison of two hypotheses is easy to follow.","critics":"Peter van Inwagen and Alvin Plantinga both replied to Draper, arguing that we are in no position to judge how likely the world's suffering would be if God existed, so the comparison of probabilities cannot be made with any confidence.","legacy":"Draper's 'Pain and Pleasure' is one of the most discussed modern versions of the evidential argument from evil, and his probabilistic method shaped the field.","quote":null},"metzinger":{"life":["Thomas Metzinger was born in Frankfurt, West Germany, in 1958. He belongs to a generation of philosophers who took brain science seriously, at a time when new imaging methods and experiments on the brain were changing how people thought about the mind. His central question is a very old one, what is a self?, but he approaches it with the tools of neuroscience, psychology and the study of artificial systems.","He studied philosophy, ethnology and theology at Goethe University Frankfurt, earning his doctorate in 1985 with a thesis on the mind-body problem. In 1993 he published Subjekt und Selbstmodell, the first version of his self-model theory, which he developed at full length in Being No One (2003). The Ego Tunnel (2009) presented the same ideas for general readers. From 2000 he was professor of theoretical philosophy at the University of Mainz, where he is now emeritus.","Metzinger has always worked closely with scientists. He co-founded the Association for the Scientific Study of Consciousness in 1994 and took part in experiments on 'out-of-body' illusions with neuroscientists. He has also been active in ethics: he sat on the European Commission's High-Level Expert Group on Artificial Intelligence (2018–2020) and has argued that we should not risk creating machines that can suffer. He has written that he has practised meditation for decades, the subject of his book The Elephant and the Blind (2024)."],"timeline":[[1958,"Born in Frankfurt, West Germany"],[1985,"Completes his doctorate at Goethe University Frankfurt"],[1993,"Publishes Subjekt und Selbstmodell, his first account of the self-model"],[1994,"Co-founds the Association for the Scientific Study of Consciousness"],[2003,"Publishes Being No One"],[2009,"Publishes The Ego Tunnel for a general audience"],[2018,"Joins the European Commission's High-Level Expert Group on AI (to 2020)"],[2024,"Publishes The Elephant and the Blind, on pure awareness in meditation"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The self-model","body":"Your brain builds a model of the whole organism, its body, its position and its thoughts, to help control behaviour. Metzinger calls this the phenomenal self-model. What you experience as 'me' is the content of this model, not a separate thing that has it."},{"head":"Transparency","body":"We cannot see the self-model as a model. Just as you look through a clean window without noticing the glass, you look through the self-model and simply seem to be a self. This, Metzinger argues, is why the sense of being someone feels so certain and so direct."},{"head":"No one has a self","body":"Because the self is a representation and not an object, Metzinger concludes that no one has ever been or had a self in the traditional sense. There are self-models and there is the experience of being someone, but no little owner behind them."},{"head":"The ego tunnel","body":"Conscious experience is like a tunnel built by the brain: a useful, simplified picture of reality from the inside. We take this picture for the world itself. Experiments in which people feel they are located outside their own body show how easily the brain's model can be shifted."},{"head":"Do not build suffering machines","body":"If consciousness depends on a certain kind of self-model, we might one day build artificial systems that really suffer. Metzinger has argued for a global pause on research that aims at, or risks, creating such 'synthetic phenomenology' until we understand it better."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Metzinger says that you do not exist.","truth":"He does not deny that there are people, bodies or experiences. He denies that there is a self as a thing; what exists is a brain process that models the organism and is experienced as a self."},"startHere":"Read The Ego Tunnel (2009), especially its opening chapters, which set out the self-model theory without the heavy technical language of Being No One.","critics":"Phenomenologists such as Dan Zahavi argue that every experience is lived from a first-person point of view, so there is a minimal self that a model cannot explain away. Others ask how a mere representation could produce the feeling of being someone at all.","legacy":"Metzinger's self-model theory is one of the most discussed naturalistic accounts of the self and has influenced research on body ownership, virtual reality and meditation, as well as debates about the ethics of artificial consciousness.","quote":null},"frankish":{"life":["Keith Frankish is a British philosopher of mind. He works in a field shaped by the debate over the 'hard problem' of consciousness, the question of why brain processes are accompanied by felt experience at all. Many philosophers think this problem shows that physical science cannot fully explain the mind. Frankish has become the best-known defender of a radical alternative: illusionism.","He first studied literature, ancient history and philosophy with the Open University, then took an MA and a PhD at the University of Sheffield, where his thesis became Mind and Supermind (2004), a theory of two levels of belief. He lectured for the Open University from 1999 and later worked with the University of Crete. He is now an honorary reader at Sheffield. In 2016 he edited a special issue of the Journal of Consciousness Studies on illusionism, later published as a book.","Frankish works in an open, collaborative style. He has edited several handbooks on cognitive science, on dual-process theories of reasoning and on artificial intelligence, and he has written introductory books such as Consciousness: The Basics (2021). He often debates rivals in public and in podcasts, and he has said that he began as a more conventional materialist before rejecting the idea of special felt qualities."],"timeline":[[1999,"Becomes a lecturer in philosophy at the Open University"],[2003,"Completes his PhD at the University of Sheffield"],[2004,"Publishes Mind and Supermind"],[2009,"Co-edits In Two Minds, on dual-process theories of thinking"],[2016,"Publishes 'Illusionism as a Theory of Consciousness' in a special journal issue"],[2021,"Publishes Consciousness: The Basics"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Illusionism","body":"When you see red or feel pain, it seems as if there is a private, glowing 'what it is like' quality in your mind. Frankish argues that this seeming is real but the special quality is not. The brain represents its own states as having such qualities, and we are taken in by that representation."},{"head":"From hard problem to illusion problem","body":"If felt qualities do not exist, the hard problem disappears. What remains is a scientific question: why does the brain represent its states in this misleading way? Frankish calls this the illusion problem, and thinks it can be solved like any other problem in psychology."},{"head":"Not denying experience","body":"Illusionists do not say people feel nothing. Pain still hurts in the sense that it makes you wince, avoid and complain. What they reject is an extra inner feeling over and above all of these effects."},{"head":"Mind and supermind","body":"In his earlier work Frankish argued that humans have two levels of belief. One is a basic level of automatic, unconscious states shared with animals; the other is a 'supermind' of conscious, deliberate commitments, such as deciding to accept a claim and then reasoning from it."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Illusionists claim that nobody is really conscious.","truth":"Frankish accepts that we are conscious in the sense of perceiving, thinking and reporting on our states. He denies only that experiences have special private qualities beyond what they do."},"startHere":"Read his paper 'Illusionism as a Theory of Consciousness' (2016), which sets out the view and replies to obvious objections in a few dozen readable pages.","critics":"Galen Strawson called the denial of felt experience one of the silliest claims ever made, arguing that the existence of experience is the one thing we cannot be wrong about. Critics also ask how there can be an illusion of feeling without something it feels like to have the illusion.","legacy":"Frankish has turned illusionism from a fringe position into a recognised option in consciousness studies, often discussed alongside the views of Daniel Dennett.","quote":null},"schellenberg":{"life":["J. L. Schellenberg is a Canadian philosopher of religion. He works in a field where, in the late twentieth century, the main argument against God was the problem of evil, the question of why a good God would allow suffering. Schellenberg added a new and widely debated argument: the problem of divine hiddenness, which asks why God's existence is not clear to everyone who would welcome it.","He studied religious studies and philosophy at the University of Calgary and completed a doctorate at the University of Oxford. His first book, Divine Hiddenness and Human Reason (1993), introduced the hiddenness argument. He then wrote a trilogy on the philosophy of religion: Prolegomena to a Philosophy of Religion (2005), The Wisdom to Doubt (2007) and The Will to Imagine (2009). He is a professor of philosophy at Mount Saint Vincent University in Halifax, Nova Scotia.","Schellenberg combines an argument for atheism with a positive interest in religion. He has argued that humanity may be at a very early stage of its history, so our religious ideas are probably immature. He proposes a 'skeptical religion' based on imagination and faith rather than belief, centred on the idea that there is something ultimate in reality, while leaving its nature open."],"timeline":[[1993,"Publishes Divine Hiddenness and Human Reason"],[2005,"Publishes Prolegomena to a Philosophy of Religion"],[2007,"Publishes The Wisdom to Doubt"],[2009,"Publishes The Will to Imagine"],[2013,"Publishes Evolutionary Religion"],[2015,"Publishes The Hiddenness Argument for a general readership"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The hiddenness argument","body":"A perfectly loving God would always be open to a personal relationship with us, and such a relationship requires believing that God exists. So God would make sure that anyone who is not resisting would believe. But there are people who are open to God and still do not believe. Schellenberg concludes that a perfectly loving God does not exist."},{"head":"Nonresistant nonbelief","body":"The key premise concerns people who are not turning away from God but simply find no reason to believe, such as sincere seekers or people raised in cultures with no idea of a personal God. Schellenberg argues that such people clearly exist, which is what gives the argument its force."},{"head":"Ultimism","body":"Schellenberg separates theism, the belief in a personal God, from a more general idea: that there is something ultimate in reality, of the highest value and the source of our deepest good. He thinks theism can be rejected, but ultimism should only be doubted."},{"head":"Religion in deep time","body":"Humans have existed for a tiny fraction of the time the Earth may remain habitable. Schellenberg argues that, given this, our religious thinking is probably at a very early stage, so we should be modest about current answers and open to new forms of religion."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Hiddenness Argument (2015), a short book written for non-specialists that sets out the argument step by step and replies to common objections.","critics":"Theistic philosophers such as Daniel Howard-Snyder and Michael Murray argue that God might have good reasons to stay partly hidden, for example to leave room for people to respond freely rather than under pressure. Others question whether a relationship with God really requires believing that God exists.","legacy":"Schellenberg's argument made divine hiddenness one of the main topics in the philosophy of religion, alongside the problem of evil, and has produced a large literature of replies.","quote":null},"anderson_e":{"life":["Elizabeth Anderson is an American philosopher born in 1959 and raised in Connecticut. She works in moral and political philosophy at a time when debates about equality were dominated by the question of how to share out resources fairly. Her approach brings in history, economics and social science, and asks how real social relationships, at work, in neighbourhoods and in politics, shape whether people can live as equals.","She studied philosophy and economics at Swarthmore College and completed her PhD at Harvard in 1987 under John Rawls. She has taught at the University of Michigan since 1987. Her first book, Value in Ethics and Economics (1993), questioned the idea that all values can be measured on one scale. Her essay 'What Is the Point of Equality?' (1999) became one of the most cited papers in political philosophy. Later books include The Imperative of Integration (2010), Private Government (2017) and Hijacked (2023).","Anderson is known for combining philosophy with close attention to evidence. The Imperative of Integration draws heavily on social science about racial segregation in the United States, and Private Government on the history of work. She founded Michigan's Philosophy, Politics and Economics programme and received a MacArthur Fellowship in 2019."],"timeline":[[1959,"Born; raised in Manchester, Connecticut"],[1987,"Completes her PhD at Harvard and begins teaching at the University of Michigan"],[1993,"Publishes Value in Ethics and Economics"],[1999,"Publishes 'What Is the Point of Equality?'"],[2010,"Publishes The Imperative of Integration"],[2017,"Publishes Private Government"],[2019,"Awarded a MacArthur Fellowship"],[2023,"Publishes Hijacked, on the history of the work ethic"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Relational equality","body":"For Anderson, equality is not mainly about everyone having the same amount of stuff. It is about people standing in relations of equals: no one rules over, exploits or looks down on others. A society where everyone has enough to take part as a citizen, and no caste of masters exists, is equal in the sense that matters."},{"head":"Against luck egalitarianism","body":"Some philosophers held that justice means compensating people for bad luck they did not choose, but not for bad choices. Anderson argued this is both harsh and insulting: harsh because it abandons people who made poor choices, such as an uninsured driver injured in a crash, and insulting because it requires the state to judge people's talents and failings."},{"head":"Private government","body":"Employers often control workers' speech, dress, bathroom breaks and even life outside work. Anderson argues that the workplace is a kind of government, but one where those governed have little say. We should judge it by the same standards of freedom we apply to states."},{"head":"Integration","body":"Racial segregation in housing and schools is not just a matter of separate but equal lives. Anderson argues it concentrates disadvantage, cuts groups off from opportunities and keeps stereotypes alive, so active integration is required for democracy to work."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'What Is the Point of Equality?' (1999). It is long for an article but clear, full of examples, and sets out the view she is best known for.","critics":"Luck egalitarians such as Richard Arneson replied that Anderson's critique attacked a crude version of their view, and that caring about choice and luck is compatible with protecting everyone's basic needs. Others argue that relational equality is too vague to settle how resources should be distributed.","legacy":"Anderson is the leading voice of relational egalitarianism, which has become a major school in political philosophy, and her work on workplaces and segregation has brought philosophy into public debates about work and race.","quote":null},"schechtman":{"life":["Marya Schechtman is an American philosopher who works on personal identity, the question of what makes you the same person over time. For much of the twentieth century this question was framed by puzzle cases about memory, brain transplants and teleporters. Schechtman argued that these puzzles often miss what we actually care about when we ask who someone is.","She studied at the University of Chicago and completed her PhD at Harvard in 1988. She has taught at the University of Illinois Chicago ever since, where she is now LAS Distinguished Professor of Philosophy and has also served as an associate dean. Her first book, The Constitution of Selves (1996), introduced her narrative account of the self. Staying Alive (2014) developed a broader view that ties personal identity to the whole shape of a human life.","Schechtman is also affiliated with a neuroscience laboratory at her university, and her work draws on questions from medicine and ethics, such as how to think about identity in people with dementia or in a persistent vegetative state."],"timeline":[[1988,"Completes her PhD at Harvard and joins the University of Illinois Chicago"],[1996,"Publishes The Constitution of Selves"],[2014,"Publishes Staying Alive"],[2018,"Named LAS Distinguished Professor of Philosophy"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The narrative self","body":"You make yourself a person by understanding your life as an unfolding story, in which past actions and future plans fit together. A memory or trait belongs to you in the fullest sense when it plays a part in that self-understanding, the way an episode belongs to the plot of a novel."},{"head":"Two questions about identity","body":"Schechtman separates the question of whether a later person is numerically the same as an earlier one from the question of which traits and actions are truly yours. She argues that the second question, about characterisation, is what matters most for responsibility, self-interest and how we treat each other."},{"head":"Constraints on the story","body":"Your life story cannot be just anything you like. It must fit the basic facts about the world, and you must be able to explain parts of it when asked. These limits stop the view from saying you can become anyone simply by telling a new tale."},{"head":"The person life view","body":"In Staying Alive she argues that a person is someone living a characteristically human life, woven into social relationships and practices. This allows people who cannot tell their own story, such as infants or people with advanced dementia, to remain the same person, because others sustain their place in a shared life."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the chapters of The Constitution of Selves (1996) that set out the narrative self-constitution view; they explain clearly why she thinks the traditional puzzle cases miss what matters.","critics":"Galen Strawson, in 'Against Narrativity' (2004), argued that many people are 'episodic', experiencing their lives as a series of moments rather than a story, and still live good lives, so narrative cannot be necessary for being a self.","legacy":"Schechtman is one of the main defenders of narrative views of the self, and her work has influenced bioethics as well as debates about personal identity.","quote":null},"dowe":{"life":["Phil Dowe is an Australian philosopher of science who works on causation, the relation between a cause and its effect. Since David Hume in the eighteenth century, many philosophers have doubted that we can observe any real connection between causes and effects. In the late twentieth century some philosophers, led by Wesley Salmon, tried instead to find causation in physical processes themselves.","Dowe developed the best-known version of this approach, the conserved quantity theory, in papers from the early 1990s and in his book Physical Causation (2000). He spent many years at the University of Queensland and moved to the Australian National University in 2016. He has also written on science and religion, including Galileo, Darwin, and Hawking (2005).","Dowe's work is technical but driven by a simple aim: to say what causation is in the actual physical world, using the concepts of physics, rather than by analysing how we use the word 'cause'."],"timeline":[[2000,"Publishes Physical Causation"],[2005,"Publishes Galileo, Darwin, and Hawking, on science and religion"],[2016,"Moves from the University of Queensland to the Australian National University"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Conserved quantities","body":"A causal process is the history of an object that has a conserved quantity, such as energy, momentum or electric charge. A causal interaction is when such processes meet and exchange that quantity. When a cue ball strikes another ball, momentum passes from one to the other: that is the causation."},{"head":"Real versus pseudo processes","body":"Not everything that moves is a causal process. The spot of light from a torch swept across a distant wall can appear to move faster than light, but nothing physical travels along it. Dowe's theory explains why: the spot itself carries no conserved quantity."},{"head":"An empirical analysis","body":"Dowe says he is not analysing the ordinary concept of cause. He is giving an account of what causation is in our world, based on what physics tells us, much as scientists explained what water is by discovering that it is H2O."},{"head":"Quasi-causation","body":"Some causal talk involves absences: the gardener's failure to water the plant caused it to die. No conserved quantity passes from an absence. Dowe calls these cases quasi-causation: they are not causation proper, but they depend counterfactually on real causal processes."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Start with the first chapters of Physical Causation (2000), which explain the puzzles that motivated process theories before setting out his own view.","critics":"Jonathan Schaffer argued in 'Causation by Disconnection' (2000) that many ordinary causes work by removing a connection, as when cutting a rope makes a weight fall, so causation often involves no transfer at all. Others argue the theory cannot explain causation in sciences such as economics or psychology.","legacy":"The Salmon-Dowe conserved quantity theory is the standard example of a 'process' theory of causation and appears in most surveys of the subject.","quote":null},"smilansky":{"life":["Saul Smilansky is an Israeli philosopher who works on free will and ethics. The free will debate has long been split between compatibilists, who think freedom fits with a world governed by causes, and hard determinists or sceptics, who think it does not. Smilansky argues that both sides are partly right, and that this leaves us in an uncomfortable position.","He studied philosophy at Tel Aviv University and completed his doctorate at Oxford in 1990. He is a professor in the Department of Philosophy at the University of Haifa. His book Free Will and Illusion (2000) set out his combined view and the role of illusion within it. 10 Moral Paradoxes (2007), translated into several languages, collects cases in which ethics seems to contradict itself.","Smilansky is known for writing about awkward conclusions that others avoid. He often presents his arguments as paradoxes, inviting readers to take seriously ideas that seem both plausible and absurd."],"timeline":[[1990,"Completes his doctorate at Oxford"],[2000,"Publishes Free Will and Illusion"],[2007,"Publishes 10 Moral Paradoxes"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Fundamental dualism","body":"Compatibilists are right that there is a real difference between acting freely and being forced, and that this difference matters. But sceptics are right that, at the deepest level, no one chose the character and circumstances that made them who they are. Smilansky holds that both truths must be kept together."},{"head":"Illusionism about free will","body":"If people fully absorbed the sceptic's point, Smilansky fears, they might lose respect for themselves and others, and the practice of holding people responsible could weaken. So he argues that it is a good thing that most people believe in a deeper freedom than exists. Illusion plays a positive role."},{"head":"Moral paradoxes","body":"Ethics contains real paradoxes. One example he discusses is the paradox of beneficial retirement: many people in a job could do more good by leaving it to someone better, which would mean that being mediocre at your job is a moral failing. He thinks such paradoxes show ethics is stranger than we assume."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Smilansky simply says we should lie to people about free will.","truth":"He does not recommend a deliberate campaign of deception. He argues that existing beliefs in deeper freedom are often useful and that philosophers should be cautious about destroying them."},"startHere":"Read a chapter or two of 10 Moral Paradoxes (2007), which are short, self-contained and show his method clearly.","critics":"Derk Pereboom and other free will sceptics argue that we can live well, and even better, without believing in deep freedom, because we would let go of harmful anger and blame. Others object that a philosopher who recommends illusion undermines the commitment to truth that philosophy depends on.","legacy":"Smilansky's illusionism is a recognised position in the free will debate, and his work on moral paradoxes has opened a distinctive line of inquiry in ethics.","quote":null},"driver":{"life":["Julia Driver is an American moral philosopher. She works in a period when 'virtue ethics', inspired by Aristotle, had become a major rival to consequentialism, the view that what matters morally is good and bad outcomes. Most virtue ethicists held that virtues are defined by good motives and practical wisdom. Driver argued that consequentialists could give their own account of virtue.","She studied at the University of Texas at Austin and completed her PhD at Johns Hopkins University in 1990, under Susan Wolf. She has taught at Brooklyn College, Virginia Tech, Dartmouth College and Washington University in St. Louis, and since 2019 at the University of Texas at Austin. Her books include Uneasy Virtue (2001), Ethics: The Fundamentals (2007) and Consequentialism (2012). She co-edited the journal Ethics from 2018 to 2023.","Driver is known for taking on cases that seem to embarrass standard theories, such as virtues that depend on not knowing something, and for writing clear introductory books for students."],"timeline":[[1990,"Completes her PhD at Johns Hopkins University"],[2001,"Publishes Uneasy Virtue"],[2007,"Publishes Ethics: The Fundamentals"],[2012,"Publishes Consequentialism"],[2018,"Becomes co-editor of the journal Ethics (to 2023)"],[2019,"Joins the University of Texas at Austin"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Virtues are traits that do good","body":"A character trait is a virtue if it systematically produces more good than bad, for example kindness, which tends to make people's lives better. What makes it a virtue lies in its effects in the world, not in the agent's inner reasoning or motives."},{"head":"Virtues of ignorance","body":"Some virtues seem to require not knowing something. Driver argued that modesty involves a tendency to underestimate your own worth, and that blind charity involves not noticing other people's faults. If so, virtue cannot always require full knowledge, as many Aristotelians assume."},{"head":"Objective consequentialism","body":"Driver defends the view that the rightness of an action depends on its actual consequences, not on what the agent expected. Praise and blame are separate matters: we may not blame someone who reasonably expected good results, even if the act turned out wrong."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Ethics: The Fundamentals (2007), a short introduction in which she explains the main moral theories, including her own consequentialist sympathies, for beginners.","critics":"Virtue ethicists in the Aristotelian tradition object that her view cuts virtue off from the agent's motives and wisdom, so it could count as virtuous a trait that does good only by chance or in spite of the person's intentions.","legacy":"Driver's work made consequentialist virtue theory a recognised option and her examples about modesty and ignorance are widely discussed in debates on virtue.","quote":null},"markosian":{"life":["Ned Markosian is an American metaphysician. He works on questions about time and material objects: whether the past and future exist, whether time really passes, and when several things together make up a further thing. These questions were revived in analytic philosophy in the late twentieth century after a long period in which many philosophers thought metaphysics was meaningless.","He studied at Oberlin College and completed his PhD at the University of Massachusetts Amherst in 1990 with a thesis called Does Time Pass?. He taught at several American universities, including Western Washington University from 1998, before returning to UMass Amherst as a professor in 2015. He co-wrote An Introduction to Metaphysics (2010) with John Carroll and wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on time.","Markosian has also helped build philosophy abroad: he lectured in Armenia and co-founded the Yerevan Academy for Linguistics and Philosophy in 2017."],"timeline":[[1990,"Completes his PhD, 'Does Time Pass?', at UMass Amherst"],[1998,"Publishes 'Brutal Composition'"],[2004,"Publishes 'A Defense of Presentism'"],[2008,"Publishes 'Restricted Composition'"],[2010,"Co-writes An Introduction to Metaphysics with John Carroll"],[2015,"Becomes professor of philosophy at UMass Amherst"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Presentism","body":"Only present things exist. Julius Caesar existed but does not exist now, and future people do not yet exist. Markosian argues this matches common sense, and he tries to explain how sentences about the past can still be true without past things existing."},{"head":"Time really passes","body":"Markosian defends the idea that time genuinely flows, with events moving from future to present to past, rather than all times being equally real like places on a map."},{"head":"Restricted composition","body":"When do several objects compose a further object? Some say always (so your nose and the Eiffel Tower make up an object), others say never (so there are no tables, only particles arranged table-wise). Markosian defends the middle view: only some collections, such as particles stuck together, make up something more."},{"head":"Brutal composition","body":"There may be no tidy rule that tells us exactly when composition happens. Markosian suggests the facts about which things make up an object may simply be brute: they hold without any deeper explanation."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Time' (freely available online), which lays out the main debates, including presentism, in a clear way.","critics":"Theodore Sider and others object that presentism cannot say what makes truths about the past true, since the past things they describe do not exist. Presentism also seems to clash with Einstein's theory of relativity, which denies that there is a single universal 'now'.","legacy":"Markosian is a leading defender of presentism and of restricted composition, and his papers are standard reading in courses on the metaphysics of time and objects.","quote":null},"derose":{"life":["Keith DeRose is an American epistemologist, a philosopher who studies knowledge. He works on one of the oldest puzzles in philosophy: scepticism, the worry that we cannot know anything about the external world because we cannot rule out being deceived, for example by a dream. Late twentieth-century philosophers looked for ways to explain why the sceptic's argument seems so strong and yet so wrong.","He studied at Calvin College and completed his PhD at UCLA in 1990 under Rogers Albritton. He taught at New York University and Rice University before moving to Yale, where he is Allison Foundation Professor of Philosophy. His papers 'Contextualism and Knowledge Attributions' (1992) and 'Solving the Skeptical Problem' (1995) made his name. He has since published two volumes of a planned series: The Case for Contextualism (2009) and The Appearance of Ignorance (2017).","DeRose is known for testing his theory against carefully built everyday examples, such as his 'bank cases', and for long, patient replies to critics."],"timeline":[[1990,"Completes his PhD at UCLA"],[1992,"Publishes 'Contextualism and Knowledge Attributions', with the bank cases"],[1995,"Publishes 'Solving the Skeptical Problem'"],[2009,"Publishes The Case for Contextualism"],[2017,"Publishes The Appearance of Ignorance"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Contextualism","body":"The word 'know' works a bit like 'tall' or 'here': what it takes to count as knowing depends on the context of the person speaking. In a relaxed chat, 'I know the shop is open' may be true; in a high-stakes conversation, the same words may be false because higher standards apply."},{"head":"The bank cases","body":"On Friday you see long queues at the bank and decide to deposit your cheque on Saturday, saying 'I know the bank is open then; I was there two weeks ago'. That seems fine. But if a lot depends on the deposit and your partner points out that banks change their hours, saying 'I know' now seems wrong. DeRose uses such pairs to support contextualism."},{"head":"Defusing scepticism","body":"When a sceptic mentions dreams or evil demons, the conversation's standards rise, and in that context it is true that you do not 'know' you have hands. But in ordinary contexts, your claims to know are still true. This explains why the sceptic seems convincing without making ordinary knowledge claims false."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Contextualism says that whether you know something depends on your mood or what you feel like.","truth":"DeRose's view is about the meaning of the word 'know' in a conversation. The standards are set by features of the speaker's context, such as what is at stake and which doubts have been raised, not by personal whim."},"startHere":"Read his paper 'Solving the Skeptical Problem' (1995), which shows how contextualism handles scepticism using clear examples.","critics":"Invariantists such as Jason Stanley and John Hawthorne argue that 'know' has a fixed meaning, and that stakes affect knowledge itself rather than the meaning of the word. Stephen Schiffer objected that ordinary speakers do not seem aware of the context-shifts contextualism claims.","legacy":"DeRose's work made epistemic contextualism one of the central debates in epistemology from the 1990s onwards, and the bank cases are among the most cited examples in the field.","quote":null},"norcross":{"life":["Alastair Norcross is a moral philosopher who defends utilitarianism, the view that we should make the world go as well as possible. He works in a period when utilitarianism faced many objections, especially that it is too demanding. Norcross responds by changing how the theory is framed: it ranks actions as better and worse rather than issuing strict commands.","He graduated from Oxford in 1983 and completed his PhD at Syracuse University in 1991, under Jonathan Bennett. He taught at Southern Methodist University and Rice University, and since 2007 at the University of Colorado Boulder. His 2004 paper 'Puppies, Pigs, and People' became one of the most discussed modern arguments about eating meat. Morality by Degrees (2020) sets out his scalar view in full.","Norcross is known for vivid, often provocative thought experiments, which he uses to show that common moral views are less consistent than they appear."],"timeline":[[1983,"Graduates from Oxford University"],[1991,"Completes his PhD at Syracuse University"],[2004,"Publishes 'Puppies, Pigs, and People'"],[2006,"Publishes 'Reasons Without Demands: Rethinking Rightness'"],[2007,"Joins the University of Colorado Boulder"],[2020,"Publishes Morality by Degrees"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Scalar utilitarianism","body":"Utilitarianism is usually stated as a rule: do the act with the best results, and anything else is wrong. Norcross argues the theory should only rank options from better to worse. Giving some money to charity is better than giving none and worse than giving more, without a sharp line where duty starts."},{"head":"Reasons without demands","body":"If morality is a matter of degree, then 'right' and 'wrong' are not deep features of the world. They are rough labels we use for practical purposes, such as teaching or blaming. What morality really gives us is reasons to do better."},{"head":"Fred's basement","body":"Imagine a man, Fred, who tortures puppies in his basement because a chemical from their suffering lets him enjoy chocolate. Everyone thinks this is monstrous. Norcross argues that buying factory-farmed meat is relevantly similar: it causes great suffering for a small gain in taste."},{"head":"Small contributions matter","body":"One shopper's purchase seems too small to affect how many animals are raised. Norcross replies that each purchase has a small chance of tipping a supplier's order, so its expected effect is roughly proportional to its share of the harm."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Puppies, Pigs, and People: Eating Meat and Marginal Cases' (2004), which is short, vivid and shows his style of argument.","critics":"Critics of scalar views argue that without a line between right and wrong, morality can no longer guide action or explain when blame is deserved. On the meat argument, Mark Budolfson and others have challenged the expected-effect reply, arguing that supply chains absorb small changes.","legacy":"Norcross is a leading defender of scalar consequentialism, and his 'Fred's basement' case is a standard reading in courses on animal ethics.","quote":null},"rowlands":{"life":["Mark Rowlands is a Welsh philosopher born in Newport in 1962. He works in two fields that grew rapidly in the late twentieth century: the philosophy of mind, especially the idea that the mind extends beyond the brain, and animal ethics, which asks what we owe to non-human animals.","He began studying engineering at the University of Manchester before switching to philosophy, then completed a doctorate at Oxford in 1989. He has held posts in Britain, Ireland and the United States, and is now a professor of philosophy at the University of Miami. His books include Animal Rights (1998), The Body in Mind (1999), The Philosopher and the Wolf (2008), The New Science of the Mind (2010) and Can Animals Be Moral? (2012).","Rowlands writes both academic books and popular ones. The Philosopher and the Wolf, a bestseller, is a memoir of the more than ten years he spent living and travelling with a wolf named Brenin, using that life to reflect on happiness, death and what it means to be human."],"timeline":[[1962,"Born in Newport, Wales"],[1989,"Completes his doctorate at Oxford"],[1998,"Publishes Animal Rights"],[1999,"Publishes The Body in Mind, on the extended mind"],[2008,"Publishes The Philosopher and the Wolf"],[2010,"Publishes The New Science of the Mind"],[2012,"Publishes Can Animals Be Moral?"]],"ideas":[{"head":"A contract that includes animals","body":"John Rawls imagined choosing principles of justice behind a 'veil of ignorance', not knowing your race, sex or talents. Rowlands argues that, to be consistent, you should not know your species either. If you might turn out to be a pig on a factory farm, you would choose rules that protect animals."},{"head":"Moral subjects","body":"Can animals be moral? Rowlands argues that some animals can act from moral emotions, such as compassion or concern, even if they cannot reflect on or be blamed for what they do. They are moral subjects, though not moral agents."},{"head":"The extended mind","body":"Mental processes are not confined to the head. When you use a notebook to remember, or move tiles in Scrabble to find a word, Rowlands argues that the manipulation of things in the world is part of the thinking itself."},{"head":"Learning from a wolf","body":"In The Philosopher and the Wolf, Rowlands contrasts the wolf's way of living in the moment with the human tendency to calculate and scheme. He suggests that what matters most in life are moments that cannot be bought or planned."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Philosopher and the Wolf (2008), a readable memoir that introduces many of his philosophical ideas through his life with Brenin.","critics":"Contractualists such as Peter Carruthers argue that a contract binds only beings who can make and keep agreements, so animals fall outside it, as Rawls himself assumed. Critics of the extended mind, such as Fred Adams and Ken Aizawa, argue that tools are causally linked to thinking but are not part of it.","legacy":"Rowlands has shown how social contract theory can be applied to animals, and his popular books have brought animal ethics and philosophy of mind to a wide audience.","quote":null},"daniel_bell":{"life":["Daniel A. Bell is a Canadian political theorist born in Montreal in 1964. He works in a world where liberal democracy is often treated as the only legitimate form of government. Bell has spent most of his career in East Asia, and argues that Confucian traditions offer serious alternatives that Western political theory has ignored.","He studied at McGill University and at Oxford, where his doctorate was supervised by David Miller; it became Communitarianism and Its Critics (1993). He has taught in Singapore, Hong Kong, Beijing and Shanghai, including many years at Tsinghua University. From 2017 to 2022 he was dean of the School of Political Science and Public Administration at Shandong University, and he now holds a chair in political theory at the University of Hong Kong. His books include Beyond Liberal Democracy (2006), The China Model (2015) and Just Hierarchy (2020, with Wang Pei).","Bell often writes about his own experience. The Dean of Shandong (2023) describes his time running a Chinese university department. He founded a Princeton University Press series that translates Chinese scholars into English."],"timeline":[[1964,"Born in Montreal, Canada"],[1993,"Publishes Communitarianism and Its Critics"],[2006,"Publishes Beyond Liberal Democracy"],[2015,"Publishes The China Model"],[2017,"Becomes dean at Shandong University (to 2022)"],[2020,"Publishes Just Hierarchy with Wang Pei"],[2023,"Publishes The Dean of Shandong"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Political meritocracy","body":"Bell argues that leaders can be chosen by examinations, track records and promotion through lower levels of government, rather than by national elections. The aim is rulers with ability and good character, as in the Confucian ideal of rule by the wise."},{"head":"The China model","body":"He describes China's ideal as 'democracy at the bottom, experimentation in the middle, meritocracy at the top': elections in villages, policy trials in provinces, and selected leaders at the centre. He uses this as a standard by which China's actual system can also be criticised."},{"head":"Just hierarchy","body":"Not all hierarchy is bad. Bell and Wang Pei argue that some hierarchies, between teachers and students or between states of different size, can be fair and beneficial if they serve the interests of those lower down and are not fixed for life."},{"head":"Context matters","body":"Political values like human rights should be defended in ways that fit local traditions. Bell argues that East Asian societies may justify and prioritise rights differently from Western liberals, without simply rejecting them."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Bell is simply a defender of the Chinese government as it is.","truth":"He defends an ideal of political meritocracy and argues that China's system should be judged by it, and he has criticised the gap between that ideal and actual practice, including corruption and repression."},"startHere":"Read the introduction and first chapter of The China Model (2015), which explain his case for political meritocracy and the problems he thinks it must solve.","critics":"Many critics, including liberal democrats and scholars of Chinese politics, argue that without competitive elections and a free press there is no reliable way to stop leaders abusing power, and that in practice Chinese promotion rewards loyalty rather than merit.","legacy":"Bell is one of the best-known Western thinkers to argue for Confucian political ideas, and his work has made political meritocracy a topic of debate in political theory.","quote":null},"balaguer":{"life":["Mark Balaguer is an American philosopher who works on metaphysics, the philosophy of mathematics and free will. His work has a common thread: he often argues that big metaphysical debates either have no fact of the matter or cannot be settled by philosophy alone.","He is a professor in the Department of Philosophy at California State University, Los Angeles, where he has also chaired the department. His first book, Platonism and Anti-Platonism in Mathematics (1998), argued that the main rival views of mathematics are equally defensible. Free Will as an Open Scientific Problem (2010) applied a similar strategy to free will, and Free Will (2014) is a short book for general readers in MIT Press's Essential Knowledge series.","Balaguer was named one of Cal State LA's Outstanding Professors in 2019, reflecting his reputation as a teacher."],"timeline":[[1998,"Publishes Platonism and Anti-Platonism in Mathematics"],[2010,"Publishes Free Will as an Open Scientific Problem"],[2014,"Publishes Free Will for general readers"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Torn decisions","body":"Sometimes you face a choice where your reasons seem evenly balanced, such as two job offers that each look equally good, and you just decide. Balaguer argues that if such 'torn decisions' are not determined by prior causes, they are free in the sense libertarians want."},{"head":"An open scientific question","body":"Whether torn decisions are in fact undetermined depends on how the brain works, which is a matter for neuroscience and physics. Balaguer argues there is currently no good evidence either way, so the free will question is still open and cannot be settled from the armchair."},{"head":"No fact of the matter in mathematics","body":"Platonists say numbers really exist outside space and time; fictionalists say mathematics is a useful story. Balaguer argued that the best versions of each view are equally defensible, and that there may be no fact of the matter which one is right."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Free Will (2014), a short book for beginners that explains the debate and his own torn-decision view in plain language.","critics":"Critics press the 'luck objection': if nothing determines which option you pick in a torn decision, the outcome seems a matter of chance rather than something you control.","legacy":"Balaguer is known for a distinctive, science-dependent version of libertarianism about free will and for his influential defence of fictionalism and full-blooded platonism in the philosophy of mathematics.","quote":null},"mumford":{"life":["Stephen Mumford is a British metaphysician. He works on powers and dispositions: features like the fragility of glass or the solubility of salt, which are about what things would do. For much of the twentieth century many philosophers, following Hume, treated such features as less than fully real. Mumford is part of a revival that puts powers at the centre of nature.","He studied at Huddersfield Polytechnic and completed his PhD at the University of Leeds in 1994. He spent over twenty years at the University of Nottingham, where he served as head of department and dean of the Faculty of Arts, before moving to Durham University as professor of metaphysics. His books include Dispositions (1998), Laws in Nature (2004), Getting Causes from Powers (2011, with Rani Lill Anjum) and Causation: A Very Short Introduction (2013, also with Anjum).","Mumford has a long collaboration with the Norwegian philosopher Rani Lill Anjum, with whom he has applied powers-based thinking to science and medicine. He has also written on the philosophy of sport, in Watching Sport (2011)."],"timeline":[[1994,"Completes his PhD at the University of Leeds"],[1998,"Publishes Dispositions"],[2004,"Publishes Laws in Nature"],[2011,"Publishes Getting Causes from Powers with Rani Lill Anjum, and Watching Sport"],[2013,"Publishes Causation: A Very Short Introduction with Anjum"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Powers are real","body":"A sugar cube is soluble even while it sits dry in the bowl. Mumford argues that such powers are real properties of things, not just shorthand for 'it would dissolve if put in water'. They are what make the world active."},{"head":"No laws of nature","body":"We usually imagine laws of nature as rules standing over things and governing them. In Laws in Nature, Mumford argues that we do not need such laws: the regular behaviour of things comes from their powers, and 'laws' are just our summaries of it."},{"head":"Causes tend, they do not guarantee","body":"A cause does not necessitate its effect; it tends towards it. Smoking tends to cause cancer, but not every smoker gets it, because other powers can interfere. Mumford and Anjum picture causes as forces pushing in different directions, like arrows that add up or cancel out."},{"head":"Beyond regularity","body":"Hume argued that causation is just one kind of event regularly following another. Mumford argues the reverse: regularities exist because things have powers, so causation is a real production of effects, not a mere pattern."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Causation: A Very Short Introduction (2013, with Rani Lill Anjum), which explains the main theories of causation and their own powers view clearly and briefly.","critics":"David Armstrong objected that powers seem to point towards effects that may never happen, so they involve a strange relation to things that do not exist. Humeans reply that powers are mysterious and that regularities and laws explain all we need.","legacy":"Mumford is a leading figure in the revival of powers metaphysics, and his work with Anjum has carried these ideas into the philosophy of science and medicine.","quote":null},"casal":{"life":["Paula Casal is a Spanish political philosopher. She works on distributive justice, the question of how goods like income, health and opportunities should be shared. In the late twentieth century philosophers debated whether justice requires equality, priority for the worst off, or simply that everyone has enough. Casal has made important contributions to this debate and to animal ethics.","She taught at Keele University and the University of Reading, with fellowships at Harvard, Louvain, Oxford and Stanford along the way. She is now an ICREA Research Professor at Pompeu Fabra University in Barcelona, where she co-directs a centre for animal ethics. Her article 'Why Sufficiency Is Not Enough' (2007) is one of the most cited discussions of the sufficiency view.","Casal has a strong interest in biology and great apes. She is president of the Great Ape Project in Spain, which campaigns for basic rights for chimpanzees, gorillas, orangutans and bonobos."],"timeline":[[1996,"Begins teaching at Keele University"],[2004,"Moves to the University of Reading"],[2007,"Publishes 'Why Sufficiency Is Not Enough'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Sufficiency is not enough","body":"Sufficientarians say justice requires that everyone has enough, and once that is achieved, inequalities do not matter. Casal agrees that having enough is urgent, but argues that justice cannot stop there. Above the threshold, it still matters how goods are shared and whether some have far more for no good reason."},{"head":"Thresholds and their problems","body":"Where exactly is 'enough'? If it is set low, the view allows huge inequality above it; if high, it may require large sacrifices for small gains. Casal uses such problems to argue that sufficiency must be combined with other principles, such as priority to the worse off."},{"head":"Animals and culture","body":"Casal has examined clashes between respect for cultural traditions and the welfare of animals, such as ritual slaughter or bullfighting. She argues that cultural importance does not by itself justify practices that cause animals serious suffering."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Why Sufficiency Is Not Enough' (2007, in the journal Ethics), which sets out the sufficiency view fairly before explaining its problems.","critics":"Defenders of sufficiency, such as Liam Shields, reply that above the threshold our reasons to help do not vanish but change in kind, so a refined sufficiency view can meet Casal's objections.","legacy":"Casal's critique shaped the modern debate on sufficientarianism, and she is a prominent voice for great ape rights in Spain.","quote":null},"joyce":{"life":["Richard Joyce is a philosopher who works on the nature of morality. He was born in England and raised in New Zealand. His field, metaethics, asks not what is right and wrong but what moral claims are: whether they can be true, and what could make them so. Joyce takes the evolutionary origins of human moral thinking very seriously.","He studied at the University of Auckland and completed his PhD at Princeton in 1998, under Gilbert Harman. He has worked at the University of Sheffield, the Australian National University and the University of Sydney, and since 2010 has been professor of philosophy at Victoria University of Wellington. His books include The Myth of Morality (2001), The Evolution of Morality (2006), Essays in Moral Skepticism (2016) and Morality: From Error to Fiction (2024).","Joyce is known for defending an unpopular view, that moral claims are systematically false, carefully and without dismissing what morality does for us."],"timeline":[[1998,"Completes his PhD at Princeton"],[2001,"Publishes The Myth of Morality"],[2006,"Publishes The Evolution of Morality"],[2010,"Becomes professor of philosophy at Victoria University of Wellington"],[2016,"Publishes Essays in Moral Skepticism"],[2024,"Publishes Morality: From Error to Fiction"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Moral error theory","body":"When we say 'stealing is wrong' we mean to state a fact that binds everyone, whatever they want. Joyce argues that nothing in the world has this kind of authority, so all such claims are false, in the way that claims about witches are false. He compares his view to atheism about God."},{"head":"Morality evolved","body":"Joyce suggests that the human tendency to think in moral terms may be a biological adaptation that helped our ancestors cooperate. Feeling that certain acts are simply forbidden makes it easier to resist temptation and to trust each other."},{"head":"Debunking","body":"If our moral beliefs exist because they helped our ancestors survive, they would have arisen whether or not they were true. Joyce argues this undermines any justification we have for them, however strongly we feel them."},{"head":"Moral fictionalism","body":"Even if morality is false, we need not abandon it. Joyce suggests we could keep using moral language as a useful fiction, the way people say 'the sun rose' without believing the sun moves around the Earth, because it helps us keep our commitments."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Moral error theorists think anything goes and people may as well be cruel.","truth":"Joyce argues that moral claims are false, not that cruelty is acceptable. He thinks people have strong practical reasons, based on their own interests and sympathies, to keep living by moral standards."},"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of The Myth of Morality (2001), where he explains what error theory claims and why it is not as shocking as it sounds.","critics":"Terence Cuneo and others offer a 'companions in guilt' argument: the same reasoning that rules out moral facts would also rule out facts about what we ought to believe, which seems self-defeating. Moral realists such as Russ Shafer-Landau argue that moral facts are no stranger than other normative facts.","legacy":"Joyce is the leading contemporary defender of moral error theory and moral fictionalism, and his work on evolutionary debunking arguments is central to current debates in metaethics.","quote":null},"benatar":{"life":["David Benatar is a South African philosopher born in 1966. He works in applied ethics and on questions about the value of life. He is best known for defending antinatalism, the view that it is wrong to bring new people into existence, a position with ancient roots but few modern academic defenders before him.","He studied at the University of Cape Town, where he earned his PhD, and spent his career there, becoming professor and head of philosophy; he is now emeritus. His books include Better Never to Have Been (2006), The Second Sexism (2012), Debating Procreation (2015, with David Wasserman), The Human Predicament (2017) and The Fall of the University of Cape Town (2021).","Benatar is known for defending conclusions that many find disturbing, and for arguing that readers' resistance comes largely from a built-in optimism rather than from good reasons. He has also written widely on practical issues, from circumcision to the treatment of animals."],"timeline":[[1966,"Born in South Africa"],[2006,"Publishes Better Never to Have Been"],[2012,"Publishes The Second Sexism"],[2015,"Publishes Debating Procreation with David Wasserman"],[2017,"Publishes The Human Predicament"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The asymmetry","body":"Benatar's main argument rests on an asymmetry. The absence of pain is good, even if no one is there to enjoy that absence. But the absence of pleasure is not bad unless someone exists who is deprived of it. So never existing has a clear advantage over existing: no pain, and no one who misses the pleasures."},{"head":"Coming into existence is a harm","body":"From the asymmetry, Benatar concludes that being brought into existence is always a harm, however good a life turns out. Creating a person always involves exposing them to suffering without any matching benefit compared with never existing."},{"head":"Life is worse than we think","body":"People tend to rate their lives as better than they are. Benatar points to optimism bias, adaptation to bad conditions and comparing ourselves with others. Hunger, discomfort, fatigue and disappointment fill ordinary days, but we discount them."},{"head":"Not a case for death","body":"Benatar distinguishes between lives worth starting and lives worth continuing. Once someone exists, they have interests in continuing to live, and death is usually a serious harm. His view is about not creating new lives, not about ending existing ones."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Benatar thinks people should end their lives.","truth":"He holds that it is a harm to be brought into existence, but that for most people already alive, continuing to live is better than dying. Death, in his view, is itself bad."},"startHere":"Read chapter 2 of Better Never to Have Been (2006), which presents the asymmetry argument directly.","critics":"Many philosophers reject the asymmetry, arguing that if the absence of pain is good without anyone to benefit, the absence of pleasure should count as bad in the same way. Others argue his conclusion is so implausible that it shows a premise must be false.","legacy":"Benatar made antinatalism a serious topic in academic philosophy, and his asymmetry argument is the most discussed modern argument on the ethics of having children.","quote":null},"lynch":{"life":["Michael P. Lynch is an American philosopher who works on truth and on knowledge in public life. In the late twentieth century, debates about truth were split between those who think truth is correspondence with reality, those who think it is coherence among beliefs, and 'deflationists' who think truth is no deep property at all. Lynch proposed that more than one of these could be right.","He is Board of Trustees Distinguished Professor of Philosophy at the University of Connecticut, and directed its Humanities Institute from 2014 to 2023. His books include Truth in Context (1998), True to Life (2004), Truth as One and Many (2009), In Praise of Reason (2012), The Internet of Us (2016) and Know-It-All Society (2019), which won the Orwell Award.","Lynch writes both technical philosophy and books for general readers, especially about how the internet, polarisation and arrogance affect democratic discussion."],"timeline":[[1998,"Publishes Truth in Context"],[2004,"Publishes True to Life"],[2009,"Publishes Truth as One and Many"],[2012,"Publishes In Praise of Reason"],[2016,"Publishes The Internet of Us"],[2019,"Publishes Know-It-All Society"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Alethic pluralism","body":"Truth may work differently in different areas. A claim about the weather may be true because it matches the world, while a moral claim or a legal claim may be true because it fits best with other accepted claims. Lynch's view is called alethic pluralism, from the Greek word for truth."},{"head":"Truth is one and many","body":"Truth is still one thing, defined by its role: true beliefs are the ones worth having, true claims are what we aim at when we inquire. What fills that role can differ from one subject to another, in the way that many different devices can all count as a 'clock'."},{"head":"Why truth matters","body":"In True to Life, Lynch argues that caring about truth is part of a good life and of a healthy democracy. Wanting your beliefs to be true is not a philosopher's hobby: it shows in ordinary hopes, like wanting to know whether a friend is honest."},{"head":"Intellectual humility","body":"In an age of online echo chambers, Lynch argues that we need intellectual humility: being open to the possibility that we are wrong and willing to learn from others, without giving up our convictions."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Internet of Us (2016), a readable book on knowledge and truth in the digital age that shows why his ideas about truth matter in practice.","critics":"Christine Tappolet raised the problem of 'mixed inferences': in an argument combining a moral premise and a factual premise, the conclusion must be true in some single sense, which seems to require one kind of truth after all.","legacy":"Lynch is a leading developer of pluralist theories of truth, and his public writing has made him a prominent voice on knowledge, polarisation and democracy.","quote":null},"sider":{"life":["Theodore Sider is an American metaphysician. He works in a period when analytic metaphysics had recovered from earlier doubts and was again asking big questions about time, objects and the structure of reality. He is known for applying careful logic to these questions and for defending a bold picture in which objects extend through time as well as space.","He completed his PhD at the University of Massachusetts Amherst in 1993, advised by Phillip Bricker. He has taught at the University of Rochester, Syracuse University, New York University, Cornell University and Rutgers University, where he has been Distinguished Professor of Philosophy since 2015. His books include Four-Dimensionalism (2001), Riddles of Existence (2005, with Earl Conee), Logic for Philosophy (2010), Writing the Book of the World (2011) and The Tools of Metaphysics and the Metaphysics of Science (2020).","Sider is also known as a teacher: Logic for Philosophy is widely used to train philosophy students in formal logic, and Riddles of Existence introduces metaphysics to beginners."],"timeline":[[1993,"Completes his PhD at UMass Amherst"],[2001,"Publishes Four-Dimensionalism"],[2005,"Publishes Riddles of Existence with Earl Conee"],[2010,"Publishes Logic for Philosophy"],[2011,"Publishes Writing the Book of the World"],[2020,"Publishes The Tools of Metaphysics and the Metaphysics of Science"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Four-dimensionalism","body":"Objects have temporal parts as well as spatial ones. Just as a road has different parts in different towns, you have different parts at different times: a childhood part, a present part, and so on. You are the whole thing stretched across time."},{"head":"The stage view","body":"Sider adds a twist: the word 'you' usually picks out an instantaneous stage, and you existed in the past by having an earlier stage suitably related to you. This lets him solve puzzles, such as how a statue and the lump of clay it is made of can seem to be both one thing and two."},{"head":"Carving at the joints","body":"Some ways of describing the world are better than others, not just more convenient. The concept 'electron' captures real structure; an invented concept like 'grue' (green until a certain date, then blue) does not. Sider argues that metaphysics aims to describe the world in its most fundamental terms."},{"head":"Metaphysical debates are real","body":"Some philosophers argue that disputes about whether, say, tables exist are just verbal. Sider replies that if the world has objective structure, there can be a fact about which way of speaking matches it, so such debates can be substantive."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Riddles of Existence (2005, with Earl Conee), especially its chapters on time and personal identity, which introduce his views for beginners.","critics":"Eli Hirsch argues for 'quantifier variance': many metaphysical disputes, such as whether objects have temporal parts, are merely verbal, because each side can describe the same facts in its own language. Endurantists hold that objects are wholly present at each time, which they think fits common sense better.","legacy":"Sider's Four-Dimensionalism is a standard work on persistence through time, and his idea of 'structure' has shaped debates about what metaphysics can achieve.","quote":null},"chiang":{"life":["Ted Chiang is an American science-fiction writer born in 1967 in Port Jefferson, New York. He is not an academic philosopher, but his stories are widely read and taught in philosophy for the way they explore free will, language, knowledge and what it means to be a person. More recently he has written essays on artificial intelligence.","He studied computer science at Brown University and worked as a technical writer in the software industry. His first published story, 'Tower of Babylon' (1990), won a Nebula Award. His collections Stories of Your Life and Others (2002) and Exhalation (2019) contain stories that have won many of science fiction's major awards. 'Story of Your Life' (1998) was adapted into the film Arrival (2016). In The New Yorker he published 'ChatGPT Is a Blurry JPEG of the Web' (2023) and 'Why A.I. Isn't Going to Make Art' (2024).","Chiang writes slowly and publishes rarely. Many of his stories take a single idea, such as a world where the past can be known for certain, and follow its consequences with great care."],"timeline":[[1967,"Born in Port Jefferson, New York"],[1990,"Publishes 'Tower of Babylon', which wins a Nebula Award"],[1998,"Publishes 'Story of Your Life'"],[2002,"Publishes the collection Stories of Your Life and Others"],[2016,"'Story of Your Life' is adapted into the film Arrival"],[2019,"Publishes the collection Exhalation"],[2024,"Publishes 'Why A.I. Isn't Going to Make Art' in The New Yorker"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Art is many choices","body":"Chiang argues that a work of art is the result of a huge number of decisions, about each word, brushstroke or note. When you type a short prompt into an image generator, you make only a few choices and the program fills in the rest by averaging, so the result is not art in the full sense."},{"head":"A blurry copy of the web","body":"He compares large language models to a compressed image file: they store a fuzzy summary of the text they were trained on and produce plausible-sounding reconstructions. This, he argues, explains both their fluency and why they sometimes make things up."},{"head":"Knowing the future","body":"In 'Story of Your Life', a linguist learns an alien language that lets her perceive her whole life at once, including painful events to come. The story asks whether you could freely choose a future you already know, and whether you would."},{"head":"Thought experiments as stories","body":"Many of Chiang's stories are philosophical thought experiments in narrative form. 'What's Expected of Us', for example, imagines a device that proves free will is an illusion and asks what happens to people who believe it."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Story of Your Life' in Stories of Your Life and Others (2002), then his essay 'Why A.I. Isn't Going to Make Art' (2024) to see how his fiction and his views on AI connect.","critics":"Defenders of AI art argue that people can make many choices with AI tools through repeated prompting, selection and editing, and point out that photography was once dismissed as not art for similar reasons.","legacy":"Chiang's stories are among the most admired in modern science fiction and are often used to teach philosophy, and his essays have become key references in public debate about AI and creativity.","quote":null},"harris":{"life":["Sam Harris is an American writer, neuroscientist and podcaster born in 1967 in Los Angeles. He became well known in the 2000s as one of the 'New Atheists', a group of writers who openly criticised religion after the attacks of 11 September 2001. He has since written about morality, free will and meditation.","He left Stanford University for some years, during which he studied meditation, and returned to complete a degree in philosophy in 2000. He then earned a PhD in cognitive neuroscience at UCLA in 2009. His books include The End of Faith (2004), Letter to a Christian Nation (2006), The Moral Landscape (2010), Free Will (2012) and Waking Up (2014). He began his podcast, now called Making Sense, in 2013, and in 2018 launched the meditation app Waking Up.","Harris works mainly as a public intellectual rather than an academic. He writes short, direct books aimed at general readers and often debates critics in long recorded conversations."],"timeline":[[1967,"Born in Los Angeles"],[2000,"Completes a degree in philosophy at Stanford"],[2004,"Publishes The End of Faith"],[2009,"Completes a PhD in cognitive neuroscience at UCLA"],[2010,"Publishes The Moral Landscape"],[2012,"Publishes Free Will"],[2014,"Publishes Waking Up"],[2018,"Launches the Waking Up meditation app"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Free will is an illusion","body":"Your thoughts and choices arise from causes you did not choose, such as your genes, upbringing and brain states. Harris adds that you cannot even notice where your next thought comes from: it simply appears. So the feeling that you are the author of your choices is mistaken."},{"head":"Choices still matter","body":"Lacking free will is not fatalism. Your decisions still shape what happens; they are themselves part of the causal chain. Harris argues we should still restrain dangerous people, but should give up hatred and the idea that wrongdoers deeply deserve to suffer."},{"head":"The self is an illusion","body":"Harris argues, drawing on meditation, that there is no inner self standing behind your thoughts. With practice, you can notice that consciousness is present without any 'thinker' in the middle, and this can reduce suffering."},{"head":"The moral landscape","body":"Morality is about the wellbeing of conscious creatures, so it has right and wrong answers that science can in principle help discover. Societies are like points on a landscape, with peaks of flourishing and valleys of suffering."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Harris thinks that, since there is no free will, nothing we do makes any difference.","truth":"He explicitly rejects fatalism. He argues that our choices are caused but still matter, and that effort, practice and reasons do change outcomes."},"startHere":"Read Free Will (2012), which is under a hundred pages and states his main argument directly.","critics":"Daniel Dennett, in a 2014 review of Free Will, argued that Harris attacks a version of free will no serious philosopher defends, and that the compatibilist freedom worth wanting is real. On morality, critics argue that The Moral Landscape assumes rather than proves that wellbeing is what matters.","legacy":"Harris is one of the most widely heard public voices on atheism, free will and meditation, and has introduced many people to philosophical questions through his books and podcast.","quote":null},"levy":{"life":["Neil Levy is an Australian philosopher who works on free will, moral responsibility and neuroethics, the study of ethical questions raised by brain science. He works at a time when psychology and neuroscience have produced new findings about self-control, implicit bias and the unconscious, and he draws on these findings closely.","He is a professor at Macquarie University in Sydney and a senior research fellow at the Uehiro Centre for Practical Ethics at the University of Oxford. He has been editor of the journal Neuroethics. His books include Neuroethics (2007), Hard Luck (2011), Consciousness and Moral Responsibility (2014) and Bad Beliefs (2022).","Levy is a prolific writer who moves between technical philosophy and public questions, from addiction and self-control to misinformation and why people come to hold false beliefs."],"timeline":[[2007,"Publishes Neuroethics: Challenges for the 21st Century"],[2011,"Publishes Hard Luck"],[2014,"Publishes Consciousness and Moral Responsibility"],[2022,"Publishes Bad Beliefs"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Luck everywhere","body":"Your character, talents and values come from genes and upbringing you did not choose: that is constitutive luck. And what you do at a given moment depends on which considerations happen to occur to you: that is present luck. Levy argues these two kinds of luck together leave no room for deep moral responsibility."},{"head":"No basic desert","body":"If luck goes all the way down, no one deserves praise or blame in the deep sense that would justify punishment for its own sake. Levy thinks we should reform practices of blame and punishment accordingly."},{"head":"Consciousness and responsibility","body":"Levy argues that we are responsible only for actions guided by information we were conscious of. If an implicit bias shapes your behaviour without your awareness, you are not fully responsible for its effects in the way you would be for a deliberate act."},{"head":"Bad beliefs are often rational","body":"People who hold false beliefs, such as rejecting climate science, are often not stupid or irrational. In Bad Beliefs, Levy argues they are reasonably trusting the sources around them; the real problem is a polluted information environment, which should be repaired."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of Hard Luck (2011), which sets out the problem of luck and why he thinks it undermines responsibility.","critics":"Compatibilists argue that luck does not undermine responsibility when an action flows from an agent's own reasons and values, however those values arose. Others reply that his standards for responsibility are so high that no possible agent could meet them.","legacy":"Levy is a leading figure in neuroethics and a central voice in the case for scepticism about moral responsibility.","quote":null},"kieran":{"life":["Matthew Kieran is a British philosopher of art. He works on a long-standing question in aesthetics: how a work's moral qualities relate to its value as art. Some philosophers hold that moral flaws are always artistic flaws; others think morality is irrelevant to art. Kieran defends a third, more provocative view.","He has spent his career at the University of Leeds, where he began as a teaching fellow in 1994 and became professor in 2009. His chapter 'Forbidden Knowledge: The Challenge of Immoralism' (2003) and his book Revealing Art (2005) set out his views on art, morality and understanding. He has also written on obscenity, on the vice of snobbery, and on creativity as a virtue of character, the subject of a Leverhulme Research Fellowship.","Kieran has done much public work on art. He ran workshops based on Revealing Art at Tate Britain and acted as art consultant to a Channel 4 television series on hidden artistic talent."],"timeline":[[1994,"Joins the University of Leeds as a teaching fellow"],[2003,"Publishes 'Forbidden Knowledge: The Challenge of Immoralism'"],[2005,"Publishes Revealing Art"],[2009,"Becomes professor at the University of Leeds"],[2010,"Publishes 'The Vice of Snobbery'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Cognitive immoralism","body":"Some artworks invite us to take up attitudes we would reject in life, such as sympathy for a murderer. Kieran argues that this can give us understanding we could not get otherwise, for example of how people talk themselves into evil. When it does, the moral flaw can make the work better as art."},{"head":"Art as a way of knowing","body":"Kieran holds that much of art's value lies in what it helps us understand about ourselves and the world. A painting or novel can sharpen how we see things, not just give pleasure."},{"head":"The vice of snobbery","body":"Snobbery is valuing art for the wrong reasons, such as status or price. Kieran argues this is a real vice: it distorts what we notice and enjoy, and so makes our appreciation poorer."},{"head":"Creativity as a virtue","body":"Being creative is not just producing new things; it is a trait of character. A creative person is motivated by the activity itself and has the skill and judgement to do it well, much as a courageous person acts bravely for the right reasons."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Revealing Art (2005), an introduction to the philosophy of art written for general readers that also sets out his views on art and morality.","critics":"Berys Gaut defends 'ethicism': whenever a work endorses morally bad attitudes, that is always an artistic flaw. Noël Carroll's 'moderate moralism' holds that moral defects sometimes spoil a work artistically, and critics argue that the understanding Kieran describes could be gained without the work itself endorsing the bad attitude.","legacy":"Kieran's work made immoralism a recognised position in debates on art and ethics, and his work on snobbery and creativity helped bring virtue theory into aesthetics.","quote":null},"metz":{"life":["Thaddeus Metz is an American philosopher who has spent most of his career in South Africa. He works on two areas: the meaning of life, which for a long time analytic philosophers had largely avoided, and African ethics, especially ideas linked to ubuntu, a southern African idea that a person becomes a person through others.","He completed his PhD at Cornell University in 1997. He has taught mainly at South African universities, including the University of Johannesburg, where he was a Distinguished Professor, and since 2020 the University of Pretoria. His books include Meaning in Life: An Analytic Study (2013), God, Soul and the Meaning of Life (2019) and A Relational Moral Theory: African Ethics in and Beyond the Continent (2022).","Metz is credited, alongside Susan Wolf, with helping establish the meaning of life as a field in analytic philosophy. He is known for bringing African moral ideas into conversation with Western philosophy in a systematic, analytic way."],"timeline":[[1997,"Completes his PhD at Cornell University"],[2013,"Publishes Meaning in Life: An Analytic Study"],[2019,"Publishes God, Soul and the Meaning of Life"],[2020,"Joins the University of Pretoria"],[2022,"Publishes A Relational Moral Theory"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The fundamentality theory","body":"A life is meaningful to the degree that it uses reason to engage positively with fundamental conditions of human existence. Discovering how nature works, creating art about love and death, or helping others flourish are meaningful because they address what is most basic to human life."},{"head":"Meaning without God","body":"Many people think that life can only be meaningful if God or an immortal soul exists. Metz argues that these supernatural theories face serious problems and that a naturalistic theory can explain meaning in ordinary lives."},{"head":"Ubuntu as a moral theory","body":"Drawing on African traditions, Metz argues that an act is right insofar as it honours relationships of harmony, which he analyses as sharing a way of life with others and caring for their good. Acts are wrong when they are discordant, such as lying or exploiting."},{"head":"Moral status is relational","body":"A being matters morally to the degree that it can be part of harmonious relationships, either as a subject who relates or as an object of others' care. This offers an alternative to Western views that base moral status on rationality or the capacity to feel pain."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read God, Soul and the Meaning of Life (2019), a short book that lays out the main theories of meaning in plain language.","critics":"Critics argue that the fundamentality theory favours intellectual and artistic lives over simpler ones, and that his relational ethics struggles to explain why we should not harm beings who can take part in no relationships.","legacy":"Metz has helped make the meaning of life a recognised topic in analytic philosophy and has done more than most to bring African ethics into mainstream international debate.","quote":null},"schwitzgebel":{"life":["Eric Schwitzgebel is an American philosopher of mind. He works at the meeting point of philosophy and psychology, on how well we know our own minds, what beliefs are, and how strange reality may turn out to be. He often tests philosophical claims with empirical studies.","He completed his PhD at the University of California, Berkeley, in 1997, and is a professor of philosophy at the University of California, Riverside. His books include Describing Inner Experience? (2007, with Russell Hurlburt), Perplexities of Consciousness (2011), A Theory of Jerks and Other Philosophical Misadventures (2019) and The Weirdness of the World (2024). He has written the blog The Splintered Mind since 2006.","Schwitzgebel is known for playful, accessible writing and for unusual research projects. With colleagues, he has studied whether professional ethicists behave better than other people, measuring things like how often ethics books go missing from libraries. He also writes science fiction."],"timeline":[[1997,"Completes his PhD at the University of California, Berkeley"],[2006,"Starts the blog The Splintered Mind"],[2007,"Publishes Describing Inner Experience? with Russell Hurlburt"],[2009,"Publishes a study finding ethics books go missing from libraries more often"],[2011,"Publishes Perplexities of Consciousness"],[2019,"Publishes A Theory of Jerks and Other Philosophical Misadventures"],[2024,"Publishes The Weirdness of the World"]],"ideas":[{"head":"We know our minds poorly","body":"We assume we know our own current experience better than anything. Schwitzgebel argues the reverse: ask people whether they dream in colour or how wide their visual field is in focus, and answers vary wildly. Our introspection of our own experience is often unreliable."},{"head":"Ethicists do not behave better","body":"Studies by Schwitzgebel and colleagues found that ethics professors do not behave measurably better than other professors, in voting, replying to emails or paying conference fees, and that ethics books were more likely to go missing from libraries. Knowing ethics does not seem to make people more ethical."},{"head":"Beliefs as dispositions","body":"To believe something is to be disposed to act, react and think in certain ways. Someone who says all races are equal but treats people unequally only partly believes it. This 'in-between' believing is very common."},{"head":"The weirdness of the world","body":"Every serious theory of consciousness and the cosmos implies something bizarre, whether that minds are everywhere, that we might be in a simulation, or that the universe is infinite. Since some bizarre view must be true, we should hold all of them with doubt."},{"head":"AI moral status","body":"We may soon build AI systems that some respected theories say are conscious and others say are not. Schwitzgebel warns this will leave us unsure whether we are mistreating beings who matter, and recommends not building systems whose moral status is unclear."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read A Theory of Jerks and Other Philosophical Misadventures (2019), a collection of short, lively essays that cover many of his main ideas.","critics":"Russell Hurlburt, his co-author on Describing Inner Experience?, argues that careful, trained sampling of experience at random moments gives reliable introspective reports, so Schwitzgebel's scepticism is too strong. Others argue that his findings show only that some questions about experience are badly posed.","legacy":"Schwitzgebel's work on the unreliability of introspection and the behaviour of ethicists is widely cited, and his writing on AI consciousness has become central to debates on the moral status of machines.","quote":null},"huemer":{"life":["Michael Huemer is an American philosopher who works in epistemology, ethics and political philosophy. He is known for defending positions that start from common-sense intuitions and follow them to radical conclusions, including that no government has real authority.","He studied at the University of California, Berkeley, and completed his PhD at Rutgers in 1998, supervised by Peter Klein. He is a professor of philosophy at the University of Colorado Boulder. His books include Skepticism and the Veil of Perception (2001), Ethical Intuitionism (2005), The Problem of Political Authority (2013), Approaching Infinity (2016), Dialogues on Ethical Vegetarianism (2019) and Knowledge, Reality, and Value (2021), an introduction to philosophy.","Huemer writes clearly and often for general readers, and has published a number of debate books in which he argues one side against another philosopher."],"timeline":[[1998,"Completes his PhD at Rutgers University"],[2001,"Publishes Skepticism and the Veil of Perception"],[2005,"Publishes Ethical Intuitionism"],[2013,"Publishes The Problem of Political Authority"],[2019,"Publishes Dialogues on Ethical Vegetarianism"],[2021,"Publishes Knowledge, Reality, and Value"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Phenomenal conservatism","body":"If something seems true to you, you are justified in believing it unless you have reason to doubt it. Huemer argues this simple principle underlies all reasoning: even arguments against it rely on things seeming true."},{"head":"Ethical intuitionism","body":"We know basic moral truths, such as that torturing a child for fun is wrong, by intellectual intuition, the way we know that 2 + 2 = 4. Moral facts are objective, and these intuitions give us real knowledge of them."},{"head":"The problem of political authority","body":"If a private person took your money by threat and spent it on projects they liked, we would call it theft. Governments do this through taxation, and yet we think it is legitimate. Huemer argues that no theory, including the social contract, explains this special authority, so it does not exist."},{"head":"Anarchy can work","body":"Without a state, Huemer argues, competing private security firms and arbitration services could protect rights and settle disputes, because violent conflict is costly for them. He thinks this would be more just and less dangerous than government."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Huemer thinks people should simply disobey all laws.","truth":"He argues that laws have no special authority, but many acts that laws forbid, like theft and assault, are wrong anyway, and he still thinks people usually have good reasons to obey."},"startHere":"Read Part I of The Problem of Political Authority (2013), which builds the case against government authority from everyday examples.","critics":"Daniel Layman, in a debate book with Huemer, defends the authority of the state, arguing that it secures the conditions of freedom. Many critics also doubt that private security agencies would avoid war or the rule of the strongest.","legacy":"Huemer is one of the most cited contemporary defenders of phenomenal conservatism and ethical intuitionism, and his book is a central modern work of philosophical anarchism.","quote":null},"schaffer":{"life":["Jonathan Schaffer is an American metaphysician. He works in a period when metaphysics was moving from the question of what exists to the question of what depends on what. He has been one of the leaders of this shift, and is known for defending the claim that the whole universe is more basic than its parts.","He studied at Kenyon College and completed his PhD at Rutgers in 1999, writing on causation. He taught at the University of Massachusetts Amherst from 2000, held a research position at the Australian National University from 2007, and has been at Rutgers since 2010, where he became Distinguished Professor in 2015. His papers 'On What Grounds What' (2009) and 'Monism: The Priority of the Whole' (2010) are among the most cited in recent metaphysics.","Schaffer works mainly through journal articles rather than books, and has also written influential papers on causation and knowledge, including the idea that causal and knowledge claims are implicitly contrastive."],"timeline":[[1999,"Completes his PhD at Rutgers University"],[2000,"Begins teaching at UMass Amherst"],[2007,"Takes up a research position at the Australian National University"],[2009,"Publishes 'On What Grounds What'"],[2010,"Publishes 'Monism: The Priority of the Whole' and returns to Rutgers"],[2015,"Becomes Distinguished Professor at Rutgers"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Metaphysics is about grounding","body":"The old question was what exists: numbers, tables, fictional characters? Schaffer argues that most of these things do exist, and the real task is to find out what grounds what, that is, which things are basic and which depend on them. A crowd exists, but it exists because the people in it do."},{"head":"Priority monism","body":"Many things exist, but only one is fundamental: the whole cosmos. Its parts, from galaxies down to particles, depend on it, rather than the whole being built up from them. This view is called priority monism."},{"head":"Arguments for the whole","body":"Schaffer appeals to quantum entanglement, in which a system as a whole has properties not fixed by its parts, and to the possibility that matter is infinitely divisible with no smallest parts to serve as a foundation. Both suggest the whole, not the parts, is basic."},{"head":"Contrastivism","body":"Saying 'C caused E' or 'I know P' is really saying something contrastive: C rather than C* caused E rather than E*. Schaffer argues that making the contrast explicit clears up many puzzles about causation and knowledge."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Monism means Schaffer thinks there is only one thing in the universe.","truth":"He accepts that there are many things. His claim is about priority: only one thing, the cosmos, is fundamental, and all others depend on it."},"startHere":"Read 'On What Grounds What' (2009), which explains his picture of metaphysics as the study of dependence in an accessible way.","critics":"Jessica Wilson, in 'No Work for a Theory of Grounding' (2014), argues that grounding is too vague to do useful work and that more specific relations, such as part and whole, are what matter. Others question whether quantum entanglement really supports the priority of the whole.","legacy":"Schaffer helped make grounding one of the central topics of twenty-first-century metaphysics and revived monism as a serious position.","quote":null},"seth":{"life":["Anil Seth is a British neuroscientist born in Oxford in 1972. He works on consciousness from the side of science, at a time when brain imaging and computational models have made consciousness a respectable research topic. His work has been widely read by philosophers.","He studied natural sciences at King's College, Cambridge, then took an MSc and a doctorate in computer science and artificial intelligence at the University of Sussex. After postdoctoral work at the Neurosciences Institute in San Diego (2001–2006), he returned to Sussex, where he is professor of cognitive and computational neuroscience and, since 2010, co-director of its centre for consciousness science. He is editor-in-chief of the journal Neuroscience of Consciousness and wrote Being You: A New Science of Consciousness (2021).","Seth is an active public communicator. His 2017 TED talk on how the brain constructs experience has been widely watched, and in 2025 his essay 'The Mythology of Conscious AI' won the Berggruen Prize Essay Competition."],"timeline":[[1972,"Born in Oxford, England"],[1994,"Graduates in natural sciences from Cambridge"],[2001,"Begins postdoctoral work at the Neurosciences Institute, San Diego"],[2010,"Becomes co-director of the Sussex centre for consciousness science"],[2017,"Gives a widely viewed TED talk on consciousness"],[2021,"Publishes Being You"],[2025,"Wins the Berggruen Prize Essay Competition"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Controlled hallucination","body":"Your brain does not passively receive the world; it constantly predicts what is out there and checks those predictions against sensory signals. What you experience is the brain's best guess. Seth calls perception a 'controlled hallucination': controlled because it is tied to the world by the senses."},{"head":"The real problem","body":"Rather than trying to explain why there is consciousness at all, Seth suggests explaining, predicting and controlling particular features of experience, such as why something looks red or why you feel like a self. He calls this the 'real problem', and expects the hard problem to fade as it is solved."},{"head":"The beast machine","body":"The deepest layer of the self comes from the brain predicting and regulating the body's inner state, heartbeat, temperature and blood chemistry, to keep us alive. Emotions and the feeling of being a self are rooted in this, so consciousness is tied to being a living creature."},{"head":"Doubts about conscious AI","body":"Because Seth links consciousness to life and bodily self-regulation, he doubts that cleverness alone makes a machine conscious. He warns against assuming that AI systems that speak fluently feel anything."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Calling perception a 'controlled hallucination' means Seth thinks the world is not real.","truth":"He holds that the world is real and that perception is tightly tied to it by sensory signals. The point is that what we experience is the brain's model of the world, not a direct copy."},"startHere":"Read Being You (2021), especially its first chapters, which explain the 'real problem' and controlled hallucination for general readers.","critics":"Philosophers such as David Chalmers reply that explaining, predicting and controlling features of experience still leaves the hard problem untouched. Functionalists argue that his link between consciousness and living bodies is not proven, and that a machine with the right organisation could be conscious.","legacy":"Seth is one of the most prominent scientists of consciousness, and his predictive, body-centred account has influenced both neuroscience and philosophical debate about AI consciousness.","quote":null},"bostrom":{"life":["Nick Bostrom is a Swedish philosopher born in 1973 in Helsingborg. He works on the long-term future of humanity: the risks that could end or cripple civilisation, the possibility of radically enhancing human beings, and the arrival of artificial intelligence smarter than humans. Many of these topics were seen as science fiction when he began writing about them.","He studied in Sweden and London and completed his PhD at the London School of Economics in 2000. In 1998 he co-founded the World Transhumanist Association. In 2005 he became founding director of the Future of Humanity Institute at the University of Oxford, which he led until it closed in 2024. His works include Anthropic Bias (2002), the paper 'Are You Living in a Computer Simulation?' (2003), 'The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant' (2005), Superintelligence (2014) and Deep Utopia (2024).","Bostrom works across philosophy, probability and technology, and has been unusually influential outside academia, especially among AI researchers and technology leaders."],"timeline":[[1973,"Born in Helsingborg, Sweden"],[1998,"Co-founds the World Transhumanist Association"],[2000,"Completes his PhD at the London School of Economics"],[2003,"Publishes 'Are You Living in a Computer Simulation?'"],[2005,"Founds the Future of Humanity Institute at Oxford; publishes 'The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant'"],[2014,"Publishes Superintelligence"],[2024,"Future of Humanity Institute closes; publishes Deep Utopia"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The dragon-tyrant","body":"In Bostrom's fable, a dragon demands thousands of people every day, and people learn to accept this as natural, even noble. When they finally realise they could kill it, they regret every year of delay. The dragon stands for ageing, which Bostrom thinks we should treat as a disease to cure, not a fate to accept."},{"head":"Existential risk","body":"Some catastrophes would not just kill many people but end humanity's whole future. Because that future could be so long and valuable, Bostrom argues that reducing such risks, even slightly, is one of the most important things we can do."},{"head":"Superintelligence","body":"If AI surpasses human intelligence, it might quickly improve itself further. Its goals would then decide the future, and almost any goal not carefully aligned with human values could be disastrous for us. Bostrom argues we must solve this 'control problem' before such systems are built."},{"head":"The simulation argument","body":"At least one of three things is probably true: almost all civilisations die out before they can run detailed simulations of minds; almost none that could want to; or we are very likely living in a simulation. Bostrom does not claim to know which."},{"head":"The reversal test","body":"When people resist enhancing some human trait, such as intelligence, ask whether reducing it would be better. If not, and no reason is given why the current level is ideal, the resistance may be simple bias towards the way things are."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Bostrom claims we are living in a computer simulation.","truth":"His simulation argument says only that one of three possibilities is likely true, and he has said he does not think we can know that the simulation option is the right one."},"startHere":"Read 'The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant' (2005), a short story-essay freely available online that shows his style and his case against accepting ageing.","critics":"Bernard Williams argued in 'The Makropulos Case' (1973) that an endless life would become unbearably tedious, and Leon Kass has argued that mortality gives life its seriousness and meaning. On AI, critics argue that his scenarios rest on speculative assumptions about how intelligence works.","legacy":"Bostrom helped turn existential risk and AI safety into recognised fields of research and policy, and his simulation argument is one of the most discussed thought experiments of recent decades.","quote":null},"leng":{"life":["Mary Leng is a British philosopher of mathematics and science. She works on an old question: do numbers and other mathematical objects really exist? The modern debate has been shaped by the 'indispensability argument' of W. V. Quine and Hilary Putnam, which says we must believe in mathematical objects because our best science cannot do without them.","She studied at Balliol College, Oxford, and did her graduate work at the University of Toronto. She held posts at the University of Cambridge (2002–2006) and the University of Liverpool (2006–2011), and since 2012 has been at the University of York, where she is a professor. She co-edited Mathematical Knowledge (2007) and wrote Mathematics and Reality (2010), a defence of mathematical fictionalism.","Leng has also explored whether her approach to mathematics could extend to other areas, such as whether a fictionalist about mathematics should also be one about morality."],"timeline":[[2002,"Takes up a post at the University of Cambridge"],[2006,"Moves to the University of Liverpool"],[2007,"Co-edits Mathematical Knowledge"],[2010,"Publishes Mathematics and Reality"],[2012,"Joins the University of York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Mathematical fictionalism","body":"Statements like 'there is a prime number between 10 and 12' are, strictly speaking, not true, because numbers do not exist. They are true in the story of mathematics, the way 'Sherlock Holmes lived on Baker Street' is true in the Holmes stories."},{"head":"Answering indispensability","body":"Quine and Putnam argued that since science uses mathematics, we must believe in mathematical objects. Leng replies that scientists use many idealisations they do not believe, such as frictionless planes. Mathematics can be a useful idealisation of this kind."},{"head":"Why useful fictions work","body":"If numbers do not exist, why is mathematics so useful in physics? Leng argues that mathematics works as a framework for describing physical things: what matters is that the physical world is as it would need to be if the mathematical story were true, not that the numbers exist."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Fictionalists think mathematics is made up, so its results are arbitrary or unreliable.","truth":"Leng holds that mathematical reasoning is rigorous and its results are not arbitrary; what she denies is only that mathematical objects such as numbers exist."},"startHere":"Read the introductory chapter of Mathematics and Reality (2010), which explains the indispensability argument and how a fictionalist can respond.","critics":"Mark Colyvan and Alan Baker argue that mathematics does more than describe: it sometimes explains physical facts, as in Baker's example of cicadas whose life cycles are prime numbers of years, so we should believe in what it describes.","legacy":"Leng is one of the leading defenders of mathematical fictionalism, and her book is a standard reference in debates about the indispensability argument.","quote":null},"caruso":{"life":["Gregg Caruso is an American philosopher working in the early twenty-first century, a time when neuroscience and psychology have revived old debates about whether people truly control what they do. He belongs to a growing group of 'free will sceptics', who argue that nobody has the kind of free will needed to truly deserve blame or punishment, and he has pushed this debate out of the seminar room and into discussions of criminal justice.","He studied philosophy at William Paterson University and took his MPhil and PhD at the Graduate Center of the City University of New York. He became Professor of Philosophy at SUNY Corning, a community college in New York State, and later also an Honorary Professor at Macquarie University in Australia and co-director of the Justice Without Retribution Network at the University of Aberdeen School of Law. His books include Free Will and Consciousness (2012) and Rejecting Retributivism (2021). In 2024 he moved to Fairfield University in Connecticut as director of its Patrick J. Waide Center for Applied Ethics.","Caruso writes for both specialists and the public. In 2021 he published Just Deserts, a book-length debate with Daniel Dennett, one of the best-known defenders of free will, in which the two exchange arguments and replies. He has edited several collections on free will, moral responsibility and the meaning of life in an age of neuroscience, and he is a regular voice in public debates and podcasts about free will and punishment."],"timeline":[[2012,"Publishes Free Will and Consciousness: A Determinist Account of the Illusion of Free Will"],[2013,"Edits Exploring the Illusion of Free Will and Moral Responsibility"],[2021,"Publishes Rejecting Retributivism: Free Will, Punishment, and Criminal Justice"],[2021,"Publishes Just Deserts: Debating Free Will, with Daniel Dennett"]],"ideas":[{"head":"No one deserves blame","body":"Caruso argues that our choices come from causes we did not choose, such as genes, upbringing and luck. So nobody has the kind of free will needed to deserve praise or blame in the deepest sense, which philosophers call 'basic desert'. This does not mean nothing matters; it means we must justify how we treat people in other ways."},{"head":"Against retributivism","body":"Retributivism is the view that wrongdoers should suffer because they deserve it. Caruso argues that if basic desert does not exist, then retributive punishment is unjustified. Making someone suffer simply because they 'had it coming' would be like punishing someone for an illness."},{"head":"The public health-quarantine model","body":"Building on an idea of Derek Pereboom, Caruso compares dangerous offenders to carriers of a contagious disease. We may restrain them to protect others, just as we may quarantine someone with a serious infection, but only as much as needed and with as little harm as possible. Like public health, the system should focus on preventing crime by tackling its causes, such as poverty and poor education."},{"head":"Fairness to the restrained","body":"Because offenders do not deserve to suffer, Caruso argues that those who must be restrained should be treated decently and given help to rejoin society. This, he says, would make the justice system more humane than one built on punishment."}],"misconception":{"myth":"If no one has free will, criminals should simply be let go.","truth":"Caruso argues that society may still restrain dangerous people in self-defence, as it quarantines carriers of disease. What he rejects is punishment justified by the claim that offenders deserve to suffer."},"startHere":"Read the opening exchanges of Just Deserts (2021), his debate with Daniel Dennett, which sets out both sides in plain language. His Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Skepticism About Moral Responsibility' is a good next step.","critics":"Compatibilists such as Daniel Dennett argue that we can be responsible in the sense that matters even if our choices are caused, and that a practice of holding people accountable is justified by its good effects. Others worry that a quarantine model could justify detaining people for a long time before or without any crime.","legacy":"Caruso has helped turn free will scepticism from an abstract metaphysical position into a practical programme for reforming criminal justice, linking philosophers with lawyers and criminologists.","quote":null},"weatherson":{"life":["Brian Weatherson is an Australian philosopher working in the analytic tradition in the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries. He works mainly in epistemology (the study of knowledge and belief) and philosophy of language, and also on ethics and decision theory. He is known for using formal tools, such as probability theory, while staying close to questions about how people actually think and act.","He completed his PhD at Monash University in 1998 with a thesis on uncertainty. He then taught at Syracuse University, Brown University, Cornell University and Rutgers University before moving in 2012 to the University of Michigan, where he holds the Marshall M. Weinberg Professorship in Philosophy. His book Normative Externalism appeared in 2019, followed by Knowledge: A Human Interest Story in 2024.","Weatherson has been an active presence in online philosophy. He ran the philosophy blog Thoughts, Arguments and Rants and has contributed to the group blog Crooked Timber. He has a reputation for writing papers that defend unfashionable positions with care, such as the claim that a good theory can sometimes outweigh a strong intuition against it."],"timeline":[[1998,"Completes his PhD at Monash University"],[2003,"Publishes 'What Good Are Counterexamples?'"],[2004,"Joins Brown University"],[2008,"Joins Cornell University"],[2012,"Joins the University of Michigan"],[2019,"Publishes Normative Externalism"],[2024,"Publishes Knowledge: A Human Interest Story"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Theories can beat intuitions","body":"In 'What Good Are Counterexamples?' Weatherson asks whether one odd case should be enough to sink a theory. He suggests that the old view that knowledge is justified true belief might still be right, despite the famous Gettier cases, because its simplicity and power could outweigh our intuitions about a few examples. Scientists sometimes keep a theory despite a stray result, and he argues philosophers can do the same."},{"head":"Normative externalism","body":"Suppose someone sincerely but wrongly believes that a cruel act is morally required. Weatherson argues that what matters is whether the act is actually right, not whether it fits the person's own moral beliefs. Following your own mistaken moral views does not make you blameless."},{"head":"Against hedging on moral uncertainty","body":"Some philosophers say that when you are unsure which moral theory is true, you should 'hedge', choosing the act that does best across the theories weighted by how likely you think each is. Weatherson argues against this, saying that it gives too much weight to a person's own moral opinions and can make a fetish of being right rather than doing right."},{"head":"Knowledge depends on interests","body":"Weatherson has defended the idea that whether you know something can depend partly on what is at stake for you. If you only need to catch a bus, your memory of the timetable may be enough for knowledge; if a life depends on it, the same evidence may not be."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'What Good Are Counterexamples?' (2003), a short paper that raises a question every philosophy student meets: how much should one clever example count against a whole theory?","critics":"Philosophers working on moral uncertainty, such as William MacAskill and Toby Ord, argue against his externalism that rational agents must take their uncertainty about morality into account when deciding what to do. Many epistemologists also doubt that any theory could outweigh the strong and widely shared intuitions about Gettier cases.","legacy":"Weatherson's work has shaped current debates on moral uncertainty, on the role of intuitions in philosophy and on whether practical stakes affect knowledge.","quote":null},"landemore":{"life":["Hélène Landemore is a French political theorist, born in 1976, who grew up in Normandy and began her university studies in Paris. She works in the early twenty-first century, when trust in elected politicians has fallen in many democracies and governments have begun to experiment with citizens' assemblies, groups of ordinary people chosen at random to deliberate on public questions.","She completed her PhD at Harvard in 2008 with a thesis applying the idea of collective intelligence to the defence of democracy, and has taught political science at Yale since 2009. Her first major book, Democratic Reason (2013), argued that democracy can be wiser than rule by experts. Open Democracy (2020) proposed new institutions built around randomly chosen citizens. In 2021 she published Debating Democracy with Jason Brennan, one of democracy's leading critics.","Landemore combines theory with close observation of real experiments. She directly observed France's Citizens' Convention for Climate in 2019 and 2020, and in 2022 she was appointed to the governance committee of a French citizens' convention on the end of life. She writes regularly for French and American media."],"timeline":[[1976,"Born in France"],[2008,"Completes her PhD at Harvard"],[2009,"Begins teaching political science at Yale"],[2013,"Publishes Democratic Reason"],[2019,"Begins observing France's Citizens' Convention for Climate"],[2020,"Publishes Open Democracy"],[2021,"Publishes Debating Democracy, with Jason Brennan"],[2022,"Joins the governance committee of France's citizens' convention on the end of life"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Democratic reason","body":"Landemore argues that democracy is not only fair but also smart. When many people with different backgrounds think about a problem together, they notice more possibilities and catch more errors than a small group of similar experts. Her case for democracy rests on this collective intelligence."},{"head":"Diversity beats ability","body":"Drawing on work by the scientists Lu Hong and Scott Page, Landemore argues that, under certain conditions, a varied group of reasonably capable people can solve problems better than a group of the very best individuals who all think alike. A team of identical experts tends to get stuck in the same places."},{"head":"Elections are not very democratic","body":"Elections, she argues, tend to pick a small social elite: people with money, education and connections. Women, working-class people and minorities end up underrepresented. Choosing representatives by lot, as ancient Athens did for many offices, would produce bodies that look more like the people."},{"head":"Open democracy","body":"Landemore proposes 'open mini-publics': assemblies of a few hundred citizens chosen at random, who deliberate and propose laws, with the wider public able to take part and follow along. Her model rests on principles of participation, deliberation, majority rule, representation by lot and transparency."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the introduction and early chapters of Open Democracy (2020), where she explains what is wrong with elections and what randomly selected assemblies might offer instead.","critics":"Jason Brennan and other critics argue that real voters are often poorly informed and biased, so the mathematical conditions under which diverse groups outperform experts rarely hold in practice. Others doubt whether randomly chosen assemblies can be held to account, since no one elects them.","legacy":"Landemore is one of the leading defenders of 'epistemic democracy' and of citizens' assemblies, and her work is widely cited by reformers who design deliberative experiments around the world.","quote":null},"brennan":{"life":["Jason Brennan is an American philosopher, born in 1979, who works on political philosophy, ethics and the moral side of markets. He writes in the early twenty-first century, when political scientists had gathered large amounts of evidence that many voters know little about politics. Brennan made this evidence the starting point for some of the most provocative arguments in recent political philosophy.","He studied at Case Western Reserve University and the University of New Hampshire, then took his PhD at the University of Arizona under David Schmidtz. He was a research fellow and assistant professor at Brown University before moving to Georgetown University's McDonough School of Business, where he holds the Robert J. and Elizabeth Flanagan Family Professorship. The Ethics of Voting (2011) and Against Democracy (2016) made his name.","Brennan is a very productive writer, with more than twenty books, many co-written. Several take the form of debates, in which he argues one side and another philosopher argues the other, as in Debating Democracy (2021) with Hélène Landemore. He writes in a direct, combative style and defends libertarian views on markets, as well as criticising universities in Cracks in the Ivory Tower (2019, with Phil Magness)."],"timeline":[[1979,"Born in the United States"],[2011,"Publishes The Ethics of Voting"],[2012,"Publishes Libertarianism: What Everyone Needs to Know"],[2016,"Publishes Against Democracy"],[2018,"Publishes When All Else Fails, on resisting unjust governments"],[2021,"Publishes Debating Democracy, with Hélène Landemore"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The ethics of voting","body":"Brennan argues that you have no duty to vote, but if you do vote, you have a duty to vote well: for the common good and on the basis of sound evidence. Voting badly is like polluting a shared resource. So many citizens would do better to stay home."},{"head":"Hobbits, hooligans and vulcans","body":"Brennan sorts citizens into three types. Hobbits know and care little about politics; hooligans care a great deal but treat politics like supporting a sports team, ignoring evidence against their side; vulcans reason carefully and impartially. He argues most voters are hobbits or hooligans, and that taking part in politics tends to turn hobbits into hooligans rather than vulcans."},{"head":"Against democracy","body":"Brennan denies that democracy has any special value in itself. A political system is a tool, like a hammer, and we should use whichever one produces the best results. If a different system worked better, we should prefer it."},{"head":"Epistocracy","body":"Brennan suggests experimenting with epistocracy, rule by the knowledgeable. One option is giving extra weight to the votes of people who pass a basic test of political knowledge; another is letting experts estimate what an informed public would want. He presents these as ideas worth testing, not a finished blueprint."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Brennan wants to take away ordinary people's right to vote and hand power to a small elite.","truth":"He argues that badly informed people have a moral duty not to vote and that epistocratic systems deserve to be tried and compared with democracy, but he presents these as experiments to test, not a plan for rule by a fixed elite."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Against Democracy (2016), which introduces hobbits, hooligans and vulcans and sets out the evidence about voter knowledge that drives the rest of the book.","critics":"David Estlund's 'demographic objection' warns that any knowledge test would favour groups that are already privileged, such as the wealthy and well-educated, whose expertise may be biased by their interests. Hélène Landemore argues that diverse democratic groups can be wiser than experts even when individual voters are poorly informed.","legacy":"Against Democracy revived serious debate about epistocracy and forced defenders of democracy to explain why it is worth having even if voters are often ill-informed.","quote":null},"korman":{"life":["Daniel Z. Korman is an American philosopher working in the early twenty-first century, mainly in metaphysics (the study of what exists) and epistemology. He entered a debate in which many philosophers had argued that our everyday picture of objects is badly wrong: some said tables and cats do not really exist, while others said that many odd objects exist that no one notices.","He studied at Brandeis University and the University of Colorado at Boulder, and completed his PhD at the University of Texas at Austin in 2007. He taught at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign from 2007 to 2017, with a research fellowship at the Australian National University, and since 2017 has been a professor at the University of California, Santa Barbara. His book Objects: Nothing out of the Ordinary appeared in 2015.","Korman is known for clear, carefully organised writing that sets out arguments step by step. He wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on ordinary objects and an introductory textbook, Learning from Arguments (2022). With Dustin Locke he has also written on 'debunking' arguments in ethics, winning the Marc Sanders Prize in Metaethics for one of their papers."],"timeline":[[2007,"Completes his PhD at the University of Texas at Austin; joins the University of Illinois"],[2010,"Research fellow at the Australian National University"],[2015,"Publishes Objects: Nothing out of the Ordinary"],[2017,"Becomes professor at the University of California, Santa Barbara"],[2020,"Publishes 'Against Minimalist Responses to Moral Debunking Arguments' with Dustin Locke"],[2022,"Publishes the introductory textbook Learning from Arguments"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Conservatism about objects","body":"Korman defends the common-sense view that the objects we normally believe in, such as tables, trees, cats and people, really exist, while strange objects invented by philosophers do not. He calls this conservatism. It sits between eliminativism, which denies ordinary objects, and permissivism, which accepts huge numbers of odd objects."},{"head":"Rejecting weird objects","body":"Some philosophers say that if a trout and a turkey can each be an object, there is no good reason to deny a 'trout-turkey' made of the front half of one and the back half of the other. Korman argues that we can reasonably reject such objects without being arbitrary, and he answers the main arguments for accepting them one by one."},{"head":"Answering debunking arguments","body":"A debunking argument says that our beliefs about which objects exist are shaped by biology and culture, not by the facts, so we cannot trust them. Korman argues that such arguments, if they worked, would also undermine many beliefs we clearly ought to keep, and that perception still gives us good reason to believe in ordinary objects."},{"head":"Trust intuitions until defeated","body":"Korman treats our everyday intuitions about objects as evidence. They can be overturned, but only by arguments strong enough to outweigh them, and he argues that the standard arguments against ordinary objects are not that strong."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Ordinary Objects', which lays out the puzzles about objects and the main positions in a clear, organised way.","critics":"Permissivists such as Theodore Sider argue that any line between ordinary and strange objects will be arbitrary, since nature does not seem to care whether a collection of matter matches human interests. Eliminativists such as Trenton Merricks argue that ordinary objects like statues would compete with their atoms to cause effects, which counts against their existence.","legacy":"Korman's book is now a standard reference for the common-sense position in debates about what objects exist, and his work on debunking arguments has influenced metaethics as well as metaphysics.","quote":null},"cameron_r":{"life":["Ross P. Cameron is a philosopher working in metaphysics and philosophy of logic in the early twenty-first century. He writes on very general questions: what exists, what time is, what makes statements true, and whether explanations can go on forever. His work draws on the revival of ambitious metaphysics in analytic philosophy since the 1970s.","He studied at the University of Glasgow, taking an MA and MPhil there, and completed his PhD at the University of St Andrews, where he was connected with the Arché research centre. He taught at the University of Leeds and in 2014 moved to the University of Virginia, where he is a professor. His books are The Moving Spotlight: An Essay on Time and Ontology (2015) and Chains of Being: Infinite Regress, Circularity, and Metaphysical Explanation (2022).","Cameron has published widely in journals on truthmakers, possibility, existence and the nature of time, and he co-edited The Routledge Companion to Metaphysics (2009). His writing tends to take positions that many regard as old-fashioned or unpopular, such as the view that time really passes, and to show that they can be stated precisely and defended."],"timeline":[[2008,"Publishes 'Truthmakers and Ontological Commitment'"],[2009,"Co-edits The Routledge Companion to Metaphysics"],[2014,"Moves from the University of Leeds to the University of Virginia"],[2015,"Publishes The Moving Spotlight: An Essay on Time and Ontology"],[2022,"Publishes Chains of Being"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The moving spotlight","body":"Picture all of history laid out in a line, with a spotlight moving along it: whatever the light falls on is the present. Cameron defends a version of this 'moving spotlight' theory, on which the present is objectively special and time genuinely passes, yet the past and future are not simply nothing. He argues this view can be made precise and that it fits our experience of time better than rivals."},{"head":"Time really passes","body":"Many philosophers think that 'now' is like 'here': it just marks where the speaker is, with no moment more real than another. Cameron rejects this. He holds that it is a real fact which moment is present, and that this fact changes."},{"head":"Truthmakers","body":"Truthmaker theory says that true statements are made true by something in the world. Cameron uses this idea to argue that a sentence like 'there are tables' can be true without our needing to count tables among the most basic things that exist."},{"head":"Infinite regress and circles","body":"Can one thing be explained by another, which is explained by another, forever? Or can explanations go in a circle? In Chains of Being Cameron examines when such regresses and circles are really vicious and argues that they are not always as bad as philosophers have assumed."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of The Moving Spotlight (2015), which sets out the main theories of time and explains what is at stake between them.","critics":"Defenders of the 'block universe' view, which treats all times as equally real and none as privileged, argue that modern physics, especially relativity, leaves no room for an objective present moving through time. Presentists, who think only the present exists, reply from the other side that the spotlight keeps too much of past and future.","legacy":"Cameron's book is one of the main recent defences of the moving spotlight theory and has helped bring it back into serious discussion in the philosophy of time.","quote":null},"kamm":{"life":["Frances Myrna Kamm is an American moral philosopher who has worked from the late twentieth century to the present. She is one of the best-known theorists of non-consequentialism, the family of views holding that right and wrong depend on more than producing good results. Her work belongs to the era of 'trolley problem' ethics, in which philosophers compare carefully built imaginary cases to find the principles behind moral judgements.","She studied at Barnard College, graduating in 1969, and completed her PhD at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1980. She taught for many years at New York University, moved to Harvard in 2003 as Littauer Professor of Philosophy and Public Policy at the Kennedy School of Government, and in 2017 joined Rutgers University, where she is a Henry Rutgers University Professor. Her books include Morality, Mortality, Intricate Ethics (2007) and The Trolley Problem Mysteries (2015).","Kamm is famous for the 'method of cases': inventing many slightly different scenarios and asking what we would judge in each, then searching for principles that explain all the judgements. Her books are long, detailed and demanding. She has also applied her views to practical issues, including abortion, the allocation of scarce medical resources, war and terrorism, and has worked as an ethics consultant for the World Health Organization."],"timeline":[[1969,"Graduates from Barnard College"],[1980,"Completes her PhD at MIT"],[1992,"Publishes Creation and Abortion"],[1993,"Publishes Morality, Mortality, volume I: Death and Whom to Save from It"],[2003,"Joins Harvard's Kennedy School of Government"],[2007,"Publishes Intricate Ethics"],[2015,"Publishes The Trolley Problem Mysteries"],[2017,"Moves to Rutgers University"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The method of cases","body":"Kamm builds many closely related imaginary cases, such as variations on a runaway trolley, and changes one detail at a time. If our judgement changes when only that detail changes, the detail must matter morally. The aim is to uncover principles we already rely on but cannot state."},{"head":"How harm comes about matters","body":"Kamm argues that it is not enough to count how many people are saved or harmed. What matters is how the harm is related to the good. Diverting a trolley so that the good result itself causes the harm may be allowed, while using a person's harm as the tool for saving others is not. She calls her account the Principle of Permissible Harm."},{"head":"The Doctrine of Triple Effect","body":"The traditional Doctrine of Double Effect separates harm you intend from harm you merely foresee. Kamm adds a third category: acting because an effect will occur, without intending it. For example, in her party case you give a party only because you expect that your guests, feeling indebted, will help clean up the mess, though you do not give the party in order to make them feel indebted. This distinction lets her explain puzzling cases that double effect handles badly."},{"head":"People have a high moral status","body":"Kamm argues that rights against being harmed express the high value of each person. A world in which no one may be sacrificed for others, even to prevent more sacrifices, is one in which everyone's status is higher, and that is part of why such rights hold."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first Tanner Lecture in The Trolley Problem Mysteries (2015), which introduces her approach to trolley cases and is followed by replies from other philosophers.","critics":"Peter Singer and Joshua Greene argue that intuitions about invented cases may reflect evolved emotional reactions or bias rather than moral truth, so building principles on them is unreliable. Others object that her principles become so complex that they are hard to believe anyone actually follows them.","legacy":"Kamm is regarded as one of the most rigorous non-consequentialist theorists, and her detailed analyses of trolley cases have shaped how philosophers, bioethicists and some psychologists study moral judgement.","quote":null},"quinn_warren":{"life":["Warren Quinn was an American moral philosopher who worked in the 1970s and 1980s, a time when philosophers were testing traditional moral principles, such as the distinction between killing and letting die, against carefully built imaginary cases. He wrote on abortion, punishment, practical reason and above all on the moral importance of how we bring harm about.","He taught at the University of California, Los Angeles, where his colleagues included Philippa Foot, whose trolley example he helped develop into a theory. In 1989 he published two influential papers on the Doctrine of Double Effect and on the difference between doing and allowing harm. He died in 1991. His essays were collected and published after his death as Morality and Action (1993), with a foreword by Foot.","Quinn wrote few but carefully argued papers, many of which remain standard reading. Besides his work on harm, he devised a well-known puzzle about rational choice, the 'self-torturer', and argued against the idea that being rational is simply a matter of efficiently getting whatever you happen to want."],"timeline":[[1984,"Publishes 'Abortion: Identity and Loss'"],[1989,"Publishes 'Actions, Intentions, and Consequences: The Doctrine of Double Effect'"],[1989,"Publishes 'Actions, Intentions, and Consequences: The Doctrine of Doing and Allowing'"],[1990,"Publishes 'The Puzzle of the Self-Torturer'"],[1991,"Dies"],[1993,"Morality and Action published, with a foreword by Philippa Foot"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Using people versus side effects","body":"Quinn restated the Doctrine of Double Effect. He argued that it is harder to justify harm that comes from using a person as a means, by involving them in your plan, than harm that is merely a side effect. A bomber who targets civilians to spread terror uses them; one who hits a factory knowing nearby civilians will die does not, though both may be wrong."},{"head":"Doing and allowing","body":"Quinn defended the idea that harming someone is generally worse than failing to help them. He explained the difference in terms of 'positive' agency, where your action causes the harm, and 'negative' agency, where harm happens because you do not act. This, he argued, reflects each person's rightful authority over their own body and life."},{"head":"The self-torturer","body":"Imagine a device with a thousand settings, each one slightly more painful than the last, though you cannot feel the difference between neighbouring settings. You are paid a large sum for each step up. Each step seems worth taking, yet you end up in agony. Quinn used this puzzle to show that choosing well step by step can lead to a result you would never choose."},{"head":"Rationality is not just getting what you want","body":"Quinn imagined a man with an urge to turn on every radio he sees, though he sees nothing good in doing so. Satisfying such a desire, Quinn argued, is not obviously rational. Practical reason must be connected to what is actually good, not only to what we happen to want."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Actions, Intentions, and Consequences: The Doctrine of Double Effect' (1989), reprinted in Morality and Action, which states his version of the doctrine clearly and tests it on examples.","critics":"Jonathan Bennett argued that the line between intending a harm and merely foreseeing it is unclear, since almost any intended means can be redescribed so that the harm is only a side effect. Consequentialists also deny that the way a harm is brought about makes any moral difference in itself.","legacy":"Quinn's statements of the Doctrine of Double Effect and the doing and allowing distinction remain standard reference points in ethics, and the self-torturer is a well-known puzzle in decision theory.","quote":null},"wong":{"life":["David B. Wong is an American philosopher who has worked from the 1970s to the present on ethics, moral psychology and Chinese philosophy. His career spans a period in which cultural diversity became central to moral philosophy, and in which Western philosophers began to engage seriously with Confucian and Daoist thought. Wong has been one of the main figures connecting these traditions.","He studied at Macalester College and completed his PhD at Princeton University in 1977 under Gilbert Harman, himself a well-known defender of moral relativism. His first book, Moral Relativity (1984), set out a careful form of relativism, which he developed further in Natural Moralities: A Defense of Pluralistic Relativism (2006). He is the Susan Fox Beischer and George D. Beischer Professor of Philosophy at Duke University.","Wong combines analytic argument with attention to empirical research in psychology and anthropology, and with close readings of classical Chinese texts such as the Zhuangzi and the works of Mencius. He wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on Chinese ethics and has published widely on comparative ethics."],"timeline":[[1971,"Graduates from Macalester College"],[1977,"Completes his PhD at Princeton"],[1984,"Publishes Moral Relativity"],[1991,"Contributes the chapter 'Relativism' to A Companion to Ethics"],[2006,"Publishes Natural Moralities: A Defense of Pluralistic Relativism"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Pluralistic relativism","body":"Wong holds that there is more than one true morality, but not every morality is acceptable. Moralities exist to help people live together and cooperate, and human nature limits which ones can do that job. A morality that allowed torturing people for fun would fail, so relativism does not mean anything goes."},{"head":"Different emphases, both adequate","body":"Some moral traditions put rights and individual freedom first; others put community and relationships first. Wong argues that both can be adequate ways of meeting human needs, because each balances values that genuinely matter. Disagreement between them may not have a single correct answer."},{"head":"Accommodation","body":"If more than one morality can be correct, how should people with different ones live together? Wong argues that adequate moralities should include a value of accommodation: respecting others' views where possible and seeking ways to cooperate without forcing agreement."},{"head":"Learning from Chinese philosophy","body":"Wong draws on Confucian thinkers, with their emphasis on relationships and cultivating good character, and on the Daoist Zhuangzi, who warned against the obsession with being right. He uses these sources to enrich Western debates about moral motivation and disagreement."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapters of Natural Moralities (2006), where he explains why he is neither a universalist nor an 'anything goes' relativist.","critics":"Moral realists argue that deep moral disagreement does not show there is more than one correct morality, since people also disagree about scientific facts. From the other side, some relativists argue that his limits on acceptable moralities bring universal standards back in through the back door.","legacy":"Wong developed one of the most careful and widely discussed forms of moral relativism and has helped make Chinese philosophy part of mainstream debates in ethics.","quote":null},"estlund":{"life":["David Estlund is an American political philosopher working from the late twentieth century to the present. He writes about democracy, justice and the aims of political philosophy itself. His work responds to a long-standing tension: we want political decisions to be both fair to everyone and actually good, and it is not obvious that democracy can promise both.","He completed his PhD at the University of Wisconsin-Madison, taught briefly at the University of California, Irvine, and has taught at Brown University since 1991, where he is the Lombardo Family Professor of Philosophy. His main books are Democratic Authority: A Philosophical Framework (2008) and Utopophobia: On the Limits (If Any) of Political Philosophy (2019). He also edited the anthology Democracy (2002) and The Oxford Handbook of Political Philosophy (2012).","Estlund has held fellowships at Harvard's ethics programme and the Australian National University. He is known for precise arguments and memorable labels, such as the 'demographic objection' and 'utopophobia', which have entered the vocabulary of political philosophers."],"timeline":[[1991,"Begins teaching at Brown University"],[2002,"Edits the anthology Democracy"],[2008,"Publishes Democratic Authority"],[2012,"Edits The Oxford Handbook of Political Philosophy"],[2019,"Publishes Utopophobia"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Epistemic proceduralism","body":"Estlund argues that democracy is justified partly because it is a fair procedure and partly because it tends to make good decisions, somewhat better than chance. Like a jury, it can still get things wrong, but its decisions carry authority because the procedure is reasonably reliable and fair. He calls this view epistemic proceduralism."},{"head":"The expert/boss fallacy","body":"Even if some people know more about politics, it does not follow that they should be in charge. Knowing the answer does not give you the right to command others. Estlund calls the slide from 'you are an expert' to 'you are my boss' a fallacy."},{"head":"The demographic objection","body":"Suppose voters had to pass a knowledge test. The people who passed would likely be wealthier, better educated and from particular groups, and they might share hidden biases. Since reasonable people could object to such a system, Estlund argues, rule by the knowledgeable cannot be justified to everyone."},{"head":"Against utopophobia","body":"Many people dismiss a theory of justice because people are unlikely to live up to it. Estlund argues that this is a mistake: a standard can be true even if people will probably never meet it, just as it is true that people ought not to lie even though they often do. He calls the fear of idealistic theories utopophobia."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of Democratic Authority (2008), which explains why neither pure fairness nor pure expertise is enough to justify democracy.","critics":"Jason Brennan argues that the demographic objection proves too much, since similar worries would apply to democracy itself, and that we should simply use whatever system works best. Defenders of 'non-ideal' theory argue that political philosophy should focus on improving actual societies rather than on standards people will never meet.","legacy":"Estlund's account of democracy's authority and his demographic objection are standard reference points in debates over epistemic democracy and epistocracy.","quote":null},"christiano":{"life":["Thomas Christiano is an American political philosopher who has worked from the late 1980s to the present on democracy, equality and the authority of governments and international institutions. He writes in a period when political philosophers were asking whether democracy is valuable in itself or only because it tends to produce good results.","He studied in Belgium at the Université Catholique de Louvain, then at SUNY Buffalo and the University of Chicago, and completed his PhD at the University of Illinois at Chicago in 1988. After a postdoctoral fellowship at the University of Chicago, he joined the University of Arizona in 1990, where he became Professor of Philosophy and Law and later head of the philosophy department. His main books are The Rule of the Many (1996) and The Constitution of Equality (2008).","Christiano has helped shape the field through editing as well as writing. From 2011 to 2024 he was co-editor of the journal Politics, Philosophy and Economics, and he organised its annual conference for over a decade. He also wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entries on democracy and on authority."],"timeline":[[1988,"Completes his PhD at the University of Illinois at Chicago"],[1990,"Joins the University of Arizona"],[1996,"Publishes The Rule of the Many"],[2008,"Publishes The Constitution of Equality"],[2011,"Becomes co-editor of Politics, Philosophy and Economics"],[2022,"Becomes head of the philosophy department at Arizona"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Public equality","body":"Christiano argues that justice requires not only that people be treated as equals but that this can be seen by everyone. Because people disagree deeply and judge from their own point of view, a society must treat its members as equals in a way they can all recognise. Democracy, where each has an equal say, is the main way to do this."},{"head":"Democracy has intrinsic value","body":"For Christiano, democracy is not merely a useful tool for getting good outcomes. It is valuable in itself, because it is the public expression of the equal standing of citizens. This gives democratic decisions real authority, so citizens normally have a duty to obey them even when they disagree."},{"head":"The limits of democratic authority","body":"Since democracy's authority comes from public equality, it loses that authority when it violates public equality. If a majority takes away a minority's basic rights, or a group is permanently outvoted on everything that matters to it, the decisions no longer bind in the same way."},{"head":"A division of labour","body":"Christiano suggests that ordinary citizens should choose the aims of society, such as how much to value growth versus equality, while experts and officials work out the best means. This lets democracy use expertise without handing power to experts."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Democracy', which lays out the main arguments for and against democracy, including his own, in an even-handed way.","critics":"Instrumentalists such as Richard Arneson and Jason Brennan argue that democracy has no value in itself and is justified only if it produces better results than the alternatives. Others question whether public equality really requires equal votes rather than other ways of showing equal concern.","legacy":"Christiano developed one of the leading accounts of why democracy is intrinsically just, and his editorial work helped build the interdisciplinary field of politics, philosophy and economics.","quote":null},"conly":{"life":["Sarah Conly is an American philosopher who teaches at Bowdoin College in Maine. She works in ethics and political philosophy in the early twenty-first century, a time when research in behavioural economics and psychology had shown that people make predictable mistakes in their choices about health, money and risk. Governments were beginning to use 'nudges' to steer behaviour, and Conly asked whether they should go further.","Her book Against Autonomy: Justifying Coercive Paternalism (2013), published by Cambridge University Press, argued that the state should sometimes simply prevent people from making harmful choices. It drew wide attention, including reviews by Cass Sunstein and Gerald Dworkin and praise from Martha Nussbaum, and an opinion piece by Conly in The New York Times in 2013. Her second book, One Child: Do We Have a Right to More? (2016), turned to population and the environment.","Conly is known for defending openly a position that many philosophers since John Stuart Mill have regarded as indefensible. Her writing is direct and practical, focusing on concrete policies such as banning cigarettes rather than on abstract theory."],"timeline":[[2013,"Publishes Against Autonomy: Justifying Coercive Paternalism"],[2013,"Publishes 'Three Cheers for the Nanny State' in The New York Times"],[2013,"Cass Sunstein reviews Against Autonomy in The New York Review of Books"],[2016,"Publishes One Child: Do We Have a Right to More?"]],"ideas":[{"head":"We are bad at reaching our own goals","body":"Conly draws on psychology to argue that people regularly fail to do what would best achieve their own aims. We overweight the present, underestimate risks and stick with defaults. Someone who wants a long, healthy life may still keep smoking."},{"head":"Coercive paternalism","body":"Paternalism means interfering with people for their own good. Conly argues that the state may sometimes use force, such as banning a product, to help people get what they themselves want, when the benefits are large, the costs are small and the measure works. She suggests banning cigarettes as an example."},{"head":"Beyond nudges","body":"Cass Sunstein and Richard Thaler proposed 'libertarian paternalism', steering choices through nudges, such as making healthy food easier to pick, while leaving people free to choose otherwise. Conly argues that nudges are often too weak, and that if steering people is justified, sometimes stopping them is too."},{"head":"No right to many children","body":"In One Child, Conly argues that because of the environmental costs of population growth, people do not have a right to have more than one biological child, and that states could in principle discourage larger families. She does not defend forced abortion or sterilisation."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read her short New York Times piece 'Three Cheers for the Nanny State' (2013), then the first chapter of Against Autonomy, which sets out the psychological evidence and her basic argument.","critics":"Defenders of autonomy, following John Stuart Mill's harm principle, argue that people have a right to run their own lives, including making mistakes. Cass Sunstein, though sympathetic, warned that officials also make errors and that people's goals and tastes differ, so bans risk overriding legitimate choices.","legacy":"Conly's work became the leading philosophical defence of hard paternalism and is now a standard reading in debates about nudges, public health and personal freedom.","quote":null},"olson_jonas":{"life":["Jonas Olson is a Swedish philosopher working in metaethics, the study of what moral claims mean and whether they can be true. He writes in the early twenty-first century, when moral realism, the view that there are objective moral facts, was enjoying a revival. Olson became the best-known defender of a rival view, moral error theory, first proposed by the Australian philosopher J. L. Mackie in 1977.","He took an MA in practical philosophy at Lund University in 2001 and a PhD at Uppsala University in 2005. After teaching at the University of Otago in New Zealand and then at the University of Oxford from 2005 to 2008, he joined Stockholm University in 2008, where he is Professor of Practical Philosophy. His book Moral Error Theory: History, Critique, Defence appeared in 2014.","Olson is also a historian of ideas. His book traces the roots of error theory in thinkers such as David Hume and the Swedish philosopher Axel Hägerström, and he has written on Franz Brentano and Adam Smith. He co-edited The Oxford Handbook of Value Theory (2015) and an edition of Mackie's writings on justice and rights (2024)."],"timeline":[[2001,"Takes his MA at Lund University"],[2005,"Completes his PhD at Uppsala University"],[2005,"Becomes a tutorial fellow at Brasenose College, Oxford"],[2008,"Joins Stockholm University"],[2014,"Publishes Moral Error Theory: History, Critique, Defence"],[2015,"Co-edits The Oxford Handbook of Value Theory"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Moral error theory","body":"When people say 'cruelty is wrong', they mean to state a fact that holds whatever anyone thinks or wants. Olson argues that there are no such facts, so all such claims are, strictly speaking, false. Ordinary moral thinking rests on a mistake, much as talk of witches once did."},{"head":"The queerness of reasons","body":"Following Mackie, Olson argues that moral facts would have to be very strange. They would give everyone reasons to act regardless of their desires, reasons that simply exist in the world. Olson argues that such 'irreducibly normative' facts are too odd to believe in."},{"head":"Moral conservationism","body":"If moral claims are false, what should we do? Olson argues that we should not abolish moral talk, nor merely pretend to believe it. In everyday life, outside the philosophy room, we may go on believing and using moral claims because they help people cooperate and resist temptation."},{"head":"A history of the view","body":"Olson shows that error theory has a longer history than often supposed. He traces versions of it through Hume and the early twentieth-century Swedish philosopher Axel Hägerström before Mackie."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Error theorists think anything goes, so people may as well behave badly.","truth":"Error theory is a claim about whether moral statements are literally true, not advice to act immorally. Olson in fact argues that we should keep using moral thinking in daily life."},"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of Moral Error Theory (2014), which explains what the theory claims and why it is not as shocking in practice as it first sounds.","critics":"Terence Cuneo's 'companions in guilt' argument holds that if moral reasons are too strange to exist, so are reasons for belief, which would undermine the error theorist's own case. Others question whether one can keep believing moral claims while also believing they are false.","legacy":"Olson's book is now the leading modern defence of moral error theory and has made it a live option in metaethics again.","quote":null},"gaut":{"life":["Berys Gaut is a British philosopher who has worked from the 1990s to the present, mainly in aesthetics, the philosophy of art. He writes at a time when analytic philosophers had returned to old questions about whether art can be morally good or bad, and when new art forms such as film and digital media raised fresh problems.","He is Professor of Philosophy at the University of St Andrews, where he has taught for many years, and served as President of the British Society of Aesthetics until 2018. His essay 'The Ethical Criticism of Art' (1998) set out the view he calls ethicism, and his book Art, Emotion and Ethics (2007) developed it fully. A Philosophy of Cinematic Art (2010) gathers his work on film.","Gaut has done much to organise the field. With Dominic McIver Lopes he edited The Routledge Companion to Aesthetics, first published in 2001, a standard reference work. He has also written on creativity and, with Morag Gaut, a practical guide to doing philosophy with young children."],"timeline":[[1997,"Co-edits Ethics and Practical Reason with Garrett Cullity"],[1998,"Publishes 'The Ethical Criticism of Art'"],[2000,"Publishes 'Art as a Cluster Concept'"],[2001,"Co-edits The Routledge Companion to Aesthetics"],[2007,"Publishes Art, Emotion and Ethics"],[2010,"Publishes A Philosophy of Cinematic Art"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ethicism","body":"Gaut holds that if a work of art shows an ethically bad attitude, that is always an artistic flaw in it, and an ethically good attitude is always an artistic merit. A novel that invites us to find cruelty funny is worse as a novel because of that. This does not mean the moral flaw always outweighs other virtues."},{"head":"The merited response argument","body":"Works of art ask us to respond in certain ways: to pity a character, admire a hero or laugh at a joke. If the response a work asks for is not merited, for example amusement at real suffering, the work has failed in its own terms. Since moral reasons can show that a response is unmerited, moral flaws become artistic flaws."},{"head":"Art as a cluster concept","body":"Philosophers have long tried to define art by one essential feature. Gaut argues there is no such feature. Instead, there is a cluster of features, such as expressing emotion, being skilfully made, being the product of creative imagination and being intellectually challenging. Something with enough of them counts as art, even though no single one is required."},{"head":"Film as an art","body":"Gaut argues that film is a distinct art form whose possibilities come from its medium, and he examines questions such as how audiences identify with characters and who counts as the author of a film made by many people."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'The Ethical Criticism of Art' (1998), a short essay that sets out ethicism and the merited response argument clearly.","critics":"Immoralists such as Daniel Jacobson and Matthew Kieran, and later A. W. Eaton, argue that a work's moral flaws can sometimes make it better as art, for instance by getting us to understand a repugnant point of view. Noël Carroll's 'moderate moralism' holds a weaker view, that moral flaws only sometimes count against a work.","legacy":"Ethicism is one of the main positions in debates about art and morality, and the cluster account is one of the standard answers to the question of how to define art.","quote":null},"eaton":{"life":["A. W. Eaton is an American philosopher working in aesthetics and feminist philosophy in the early twenty-first century. Her work falls in a period when philosophers had returned to the question of how art and morality relate, and when feminist philosophers were examining how art and popular culture shape attitudes to gender and the body.","She teaches in the philosophy department at the University of Illinois Chicago. Her early papers examined the moral side of famous paintings, such as Titian's Rape of Europa, and feminist arguments against pornography. Her paper 'Robust Immoralism' (2012), published in The Journal of Aesthetics and Art Criticism, was named one of the outstanding papers in aesthetics of that year.","Eaton writes about both high art and popular culture, from Renaissance nudes to television series such as The Sopranos. She has also written on fat oppression and on race and aesthetics, including the introduction to a 2019 special issue of The Journal of Aesthetics and Art Criticism on race and aesthetics."],"timeline":[[2003,"Publishes 'Where Ethics and Aesthetics Meet: Titian's Rape of Europa'"],[2007,"Publishes 'A Sensible Antiporn Feminism' in Ethics"],[2012,"Publishes 'Robust Immoralism'"],[2014,"'Robust Immoralism' is named one of the outstanding aesthetics papers of 2012"],[2016,"Publishes 'Taste in Bodies and Fat Oppression'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Robust immoralism","body":"Eaton argues that a work's moral flaw can itself be an artistic achievement. Getting an audience to like and root for a deeply immoral character, against their own moral judgement, is very hard to do. When a work manages it, the very thing that is morally troubling is also what makes it artistically impressive."},{"head":"Rough heroes","body":"Eaton's central examples are 'rough heroes' such as Tony Soprano, the mob boss in The Sopranos. He commits terrible crimes, yet viewers find themselves caring about him and hoping he succeeds. Eaton argues that this conflict in the viewer is part of the work's power."},{"head":"Art can shape attitudes","body":"Eaton argues that art does not merely reflect moral attitudes but can teach them. In her work on the female nude in Western painting, she argues that many such works present women's subordination as sexually attractive and so help make inequality seem natural."},{"head":"A careful case against pornography","body":"In 'A Sensible Antiporn Feminism', Eaton argues that the feminist case against some pornography is best understood like a public health claim: a matter of cumulative effects across a population, which needs careful empirical study, rather than a claim that a single film directly causes harm."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Robust Immoralism' (2012), which is short, uses familiar television examples and states her position clearly.","critics":"Ethicists such as Berys Gaut argue that a work inviting sympathy for evil is always worse as art for doing so, because the response it asks for is not merited. Some critics also argue that works like The Sopranos are admirable because they ultimately help us see the character's wrongdoing, not because they make us side with him.","legacy":"Eaton's robust immoralism is now one of the main positions in debates about whether moral flaws can be artistic merits, and her feminist work has influenced discussions of art, pornography and the body.","quote":null},"zangwill":{"life":["Nick Zangwill is a British philosopher working from the 1990s to the present in aesthetics, metaethics and the philosophy of mind. In aesthetics he defends views that many twentieth-century philosophers abandoned: that beauty is a real feature of things and that art is closely tied to beauty and other aesthetic qualities.","He took his PhD at the University of London and has taught at several British universities, including the University of Hull. He is an honorary research professor at University College London and at the University of Lincoln. His main books in aesthetics are The Metaphysics of Beauty (2001), Aesthetic Creation (2007) and Music and Aesthetic Reality (2015).","Zangwill is willing to defend unpopular positions in public. Besides his work on art, he has written on the foundations of morality, defending moral realism, and published a widely discussed paper in 2021 arguing that people have a moral duty to eat meat from animals that have lived good lives, though not from factory farms."],"timeline":[[1995,"Publishes 'The Creative Theory of Art'"],[2001,"Publishes The Metaphysics of Beauty"],[2007,"Publishes Aesthetic Creation"],[2015,"Publishes Music and Aesthetic Reality"],[2021,"Publishes 'Our Moral Duty to Eat Meat'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Beauty is real","body":"Zangwill argues that when we call something beautiful, we are describing it, not just reporting our feelings. Beauty and other aesthetic properties, such as elegance or gracefulness, depend on a thing's ordinary physical features, like its shapes and colours. So people can be right or wrong in their judgements of beauty."},{"head":"The creative theory of art","body":"What makes something art? Zangwill answers that a work of art is something made with the intention that it have certain aesthetic properties, by giving it certain non-aesthetic ones. A painter arranges colours in order to make the picture balanced or striking. Art is defined by this aesthetic aim."},{"head":"Moderate formalism","body":"Formalism is the view that what matters aesthetically is a work's form, what can be seen or heard in it. Zangwill defends a moderate version: many aesthetic properties depend only on what we perceive, though some, especially in representational art, also depend on things outside the work, such as its history or subject."},{"head":"Music is about sound","body":"In Music and Aesthetic Reality, Zangwill argues that musical experience is directed at the sounds themselves and their aesthetic qualities. Descriptions of music as sad or triumphant are metaphors that point to these qualities rather than literal claims about emotions in the music."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of The Metaphysics of Beauty (2001), where he explains why judgements of beauty are more than expressions of taste.","critics":"Defenders of the institutional theory, such as George Dickie, and followers of Arthur Danto point to conceptual works like Duchamp's Fountain, a urinal exhibited as art, which seem to be art without any aesthetic aim. Zangwill replies that such works are a minority and depend on the wider practice of aesthetic art.","legacy":"Zangwill is one of the leading defenders of aesthetic realism and of an aesthetic definition of art, keeping these traditional positions active in contemporary debate.","quote":null},"kelly_sd":{"life":["Sean Dorrance Kelly is an American philosopher working from the 1990s to the present. He works in phenomenology, the study of experience from the first-person point of view, and existentialism, and he has applied these traditions to perception, the meaning of life and, more recently, artificial intelligence. He is one of the main voices connecting European philosophy with analytic philosophy of mind.","He studied at Brown University and completed his PhD at the University of California, Berkeley in 1998, where he worked closely with Hubert Dreyfus, a well-known critic of artificial intelligence. He taught at Princeton from 1999 to 2006, then moved to Harvard, where he is the Teresa G. and Ferdinand F. Martignetti Professor of Philosophy. With Dreyfus he wrote All Things Shining (2011), a book for general readers about finding meaning in a secular age.","Kelly has held several senior roles at Harvard, including as faculty dean of Dunster House, one of its undergraduate residences, and from 2024 as Dean of Arts and Humanities. In 2019 he published an essay in MIT Technology Review arguing that an AI cannot be an artist, which drew wide attention."],"timeline":[[1998,"Completes his PhD at the University of California, Berkeley"],[1999,"Begins teaching at Princeton"],[2006,"Moves to Harvard"],[2011,"Publishes All Things Shining with Hubert Dreyfus"],[2019,"Publishes 'A philosopher argues that an AI can't be an artist' in MIT Technology Review"],[2024,"Becomes Harvard's Dean of Arts and Humanities"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Creativity needs a community","body":"Kelly argues that being creative is not just producing something new. A truly creative work changes how a human community understands what is valuable, and the creator must be someone who cares about that community and can be answerable to it. Because an AI is not a member of such a community, he argues, it cannot be an artist, however skilled its output."},{"head":"Meaning in a secular age","body":"In All Things Shining, Kelly and Dreyfus argue that many people today feel that life has lost meaning because they think they must choose its meaning for themselves. Drawing on Homer and other classics, they suggest that meaning can still be found in moments that sweep us up, such as great sporting performances or shared celebrations, if we learn to recognise the right ones."},{"head":"Perception is skilled","body":"Following the French phenomenologist Maurice Merleau-Ponty, Kelly argues that we perceive the world through bodily skills. We see an object as something to grasp or move around, and our sense of where things are depends on what we can do with them, not only on an inner picture."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his short essay 'A philosopher argues that an AI can't be an artist' (2019), then the introduction to All Things Shining (2011) for his broader view of meaning.","critics":"Researchers in computational creativity, such as Simon Colton, argue that whether software counts as creative should depend on what it does and how it works, not on whether it belongs to a human community. Reviewers of All Things Shining objected that its celebration of being swept up in collective moods could also fit crowds caught up in dangerous enthusiasm.","legacy":"Kelly has helped bring phenomenology into debates about perception and technology, and his essay on AI art is a standard reference for the view that creativity requires human agency.","quote":null},"hertzmann":{"life":["Aaron Hertzmann is an American computer scientist who works on computer graphics, computer vision and the relationship between technology and art. He is included here because his writing on whether computers can create art has become a reference point in philosophical debates about artificial intelligence and creativity, which grew rapidly in the 2010s and 2020s as image-generating software spread.","He studied computer science and art history at Rice University, graduating in 1996, and completed his PhD in computer science at New York University in 2001. He worked at Pixar Animation Studios and Microsoft Research, taught at the University of Toronto for about ten years, and became a Principal Scientist at Adobe Research. He is also an affiliate professor at the University of Washington.","Hertzmann has published more than a hundred research papers, including early work on computer methods that imitate artistic styles, and he is a Fellow of both the ACM and the IEEE, the main professional bodies in computing. His 2018 essay 'Can Computers Create Art?' brought his technical knowledge and his training in art history to bear on a question usually left to philosophers."],"timeline":[[1996,"Graduates from Rice University in computer science and art history"],[2001,"Completes his PhD at New York University"],[2001,"Co-authors 'Image Analogies', a method for imitating artistic styles by computer"],[2003,"Begins teaching at the University of Toronto (c.)"],[2018,"Publishes 'Can Computers Create Art?'"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Art is made by social agents","body":"Hertzmann argues that art, as we understand it, is created by people who take part in social relationships: they communicate with audiences, respond to other artists and are recognised as authors. Current computer programs do not take part in society in this way, so they are not artists."},{"head":"The camera analogy","body":"When photography was invented, many feared it would replace painters. Instead, it became a new tool, and photographers became a new kind of artist. Hertzmann argues that AI software is similar: a powerful new tool that human artists use to make new kinds of art."},{"head":"New tools change art","body":"Hertzmann points out that art has always changed with technology, from oil paint to film to digital animation. Each new tool lets artists do things they could not before, and the most interesting AI art comes from people experimenting with what the tools can do."},{"head":"Could a computer ever be an artist?","body":"Hertzmann does not rule this out for the distant future. He argues that a computer would count as an artist only if it became a social agent deserving recognition in something like the way people are, which no current system is."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Can Computers Create Art?' (2018), which is written for a general audience and is freely available online.","critics":"Computational creativity researchers such as Simon Colton argue that software can already show genuine creativity, and that refusing to call it an artist reflects human prejudice rather than anything about the work. Others reply from the opposite side that today's AI systems are not mere tools like cameras, since they produce whole images with little human control.","legacy":"Hertzmann's 'tool' view, comparing AI to the camera, is one of the most widely cited positions in public and academic debates about AI-generated art.","quote":null},"colton":{"life":["Simon Colton is a British computer scientist, born in 1973 in Nottingham, who has worked since the late 1990s in computational creativity: the study of how to build software that does things which, if people did them, would be called creative. His work raises philosophical questions about what creativity is and whether machines can have it.","He studied mathematics at Durham and Liverpool, then completed a PhD in artificial intelligence at the University of Edinburgh on HR, a program that invents mathematical concepts and conjectures. He taught at Imperial College London from 2002, becoming Reader in Computational Creativity in 2010, then moved to Goldsmiths, University of London, as Professor of Computational Creativity in 2013 and to Queen Mary University of London in 2018. He has also worked at Falmouth University and at Monash University's SensiLab in Australia.","Colton's best-known project is The Painting Fool, software that creates portraits and other pictures, which he hopes may one day be accepted as an artist in its own right. It won the British Computer Society's Machine Intelligence Award in 2007. He has written both technical papers and essays on the philosophy of creative software."],"timeline":[[1973,"Born in Nottingham, England"],[2002,"Becomes a lecturer at Imperial College London"],[2007,"The Painting Fool shares the British Computer Society's Machine Intelligence Award"],[2008,"Publishes 'Creativity Versus the Perception of Creativity in Computational Systems'"],[2013,"Becomes Professor of Computational Creativity at Goldsmiths, University of London"],[2018,"Joins Queen Mary University of London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Software can be creative","body":"Colton argues that there is no good reason in principle why software cannot be creative. If people object that a program only follows rules, he replies that humans also work within constraints and are shaped by their training. The question should be what the software actually does."},{"head":"The creativity tripod","body":"Colton suggests that people judge something creative when it shows three things: skill, appreciation of its own work and of others', and imagination. Like a tripod, creativity seems to fall over if one leg is missing. He designs software, such as The Painting Fool, to show all three."},{"head":"Perception matters","body":"Colton argues that whether people see software as creative depends heavily on how they perceive the process, not just the finished product. A program that explains its choices, for example by describing the mood it was trying to capture, is more likely to be judged creative. So builders of creative software must take this perception seriously."},{"head":"Machines in mathematics","body":"Colton's earlier program, HR, invented mathematical concepts and made conjectures, some of which proved interesting to mathematicians. This showed that software can contribute new ideas, not only calculate."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Creativity Versus the Perception of Creativity in Computational Systems' (2008), a short paper that introduces the creativity tripod and his main argument.","critics":"Sean Dorrance Kelly and Aaron Hertzmann argue that creativity, or at least being an artist, requires being a social agent in a human community, which current software is not. Others argue that apparent appreciation and imagination in software are only simulated by its designers.","legacy":"Colton is one of the founders of computational creativity as a research field, and The Painting Fool is a standard example in debates about whether machines can be artists.","quote":null},"hooker":{"life":["Brad Hooker is an American-born moral philosopher, born in 1957, who has spent most of his career in Britain. He works in normative ethics, the study of what makes actions right or wrong. In the later twentieth century, consequentialism, the view that right actions are those with the best results, faced powerful objections that it permits terrible acts. Hooker revived a version that aims to avoid them.","He began his studies at Princeton and then went to the University of Oxford, where he completed a BPhil and a DPhil between 1981 and 1986; his doctoral thesis, supervised by Derek Parfit, asked 'Why should I be moral?'. He has taught for many years at the University of Reading, where he is a professor. His main book, Ideal Code, Real World (2000), is the leading modern defence of rule consequentialism.","Hooker has also been active in the profession, serving as President of the British Philosophical Association from 2006 to 2009. He has co-edited volumes on moral particularism, well-being and moral reasons, and is known for setting out his own view alongside its rivals fairly."],"timeline":[[1957,"Born in the United States"],[1986,"Completes his DPhil at Oxford under Derek Parfit"],[2000,"Publishes Ideal Code, Real World"],[2000,"Co-edits Moral Particularism with Margaret Olivia Little"],[2006,"Becomes President of the British Philosophical Association"],[2012,"Edits Developing Deontology"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Rule consequentialism","body":"Instead of judging each act by its results, Hooker judges acts by rules. An act is right if it is allowed by the code of rules whose general acceptance by nearly everyone would produce the best results. Rules against lying, stealing and killing pass this test, so the theory supports familiar moral rules."},{"head":"Avoiding monstrous conclusions","body":"Act consequentialism seems to allow, for example, a surgeon to kill one patient to save five with the organs. Hooker argues that a society in which doctors accepted such a rule would be far worse, since no one would trust hospitals. So rule consequentialism forbids it."},{"head":"Reflective equilibrium","body":"Hooker defends his theory not by claiming that maximising good results is self-evidently the goal, but by showing that it fits and ties together the moral beliefs we are most confident in. This method, testing theories against considered judgements and adjusting both, is called reflective equilibrium."},{"head":"A duty to prevent disaster","body":"Hooker's ideal code includes a rule that we must prevent disasters even when that means breaking other rules. This lets the theory deal with extreme cases, such as lying to prevent a catastrophe."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Ideal Code, Real World (2000), or his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Rule Consequentialism', both of which explain the theory and its motivation clearly.","critics":"J. J. C. Smart objected that following a rule when breaking it would produce better results is irrational 'rule worship', and David Lyons argued that rule consequentialism, once rules allow exceptions, collapses into act consequentialism. Hooker replies that his theory does not rest on maximising good results, so it does not face this inconsistency.","legacy":"Hooker's work made rule consequentialism a serious option again in normative ethics, and his book is the standard statement of the view.","quote":null},"olson":{"life":["Eric T. Olson is an American philosopher working from the 1990s to the present on personal identity, the question of what we are and what it takes for a person to survive over time. For centuries, following John Locke, most philosophers had answered in terms of memory and psychology. Olson became the leading defender of a different answer, animalism, which says that each of us is a human animal.","He studied at Reed College and completed his PhD at Syracuse University. He is Professor of Philosophy at the University of Sheffield. His first book, The Human Animal: Personal Identity without Psychology (1997), set out animalism, and his second, What Are We? A Study in Personal Ontology (2007), compared the main answers to that question.","Olson is known for clear, patient writing that takes seemingly simple questions very seriously. He wrote the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on personal identity and has published widely on related puzzles, such as whether you were ever a fetus and what happens to us when we die."],"timeline":[[1997,"Publishes The Human Animal: Personal Identity without Psychology"],[1997,"Publishes 'Was I Ever a Fetus?'"],[2002,"Writes the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Personal Identity'"],[2003,"Publishes 'An Argument for Animalism'"],[2007,"Publishes What Are We? A Study in Personal Ontology"]],"ideas":[{"head":"We are animals","body":"Olson argues that each of us is a human animal, a biological organism. So you continue to exist as long as that animal is alive, whatever happens to your memories or personality. Your identity over time is a biological matter, not a psychological one."},{"head":"The thinking animal argument","body":"There is a human animal sitting in your chair, and it seems to be thinking your thoughts. If you were something other than that animal, there would be two thinkers in your chair, which seems absurd. And how could you know you were not the animal? The simplest answer, Olson argues, is that you are the animal."},{"head":"Was I ever a fetus?","body":"If personal identity depends on psychology, then you were never a fetus, since an early fetus has no mental life. Olson finds this hard to believe. On animalism, you were once a fetus, because the same animal that is now you once existed in that early form."},{"head":"Psychology matters, but not for identity","body":"Olson accepts that our mental lives are very important to us. But he argues that what we care about in survival and what makes us the same thing over time are different questions, and that philosophers have often confused them."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Personal Identity' for an overview, then 'An Argument for Animalism' (2003), a short statement of his main argument.","critics":"Defenders of psychological views appeal to the 'transplant' case: if your cerebrum were moved into another body, most people think you would go with it, though the animal would stay behind. Lynne Rudder Baker's 'constitution view' holds that a person is constituted by an animal without being identical to it, just as a statue is made of clay without being the same as the lump.","legacy":"Olson turned animalism from a neglected position into one of the main views on personal identity, and the thinking animal argument is now central to the debate.","quote":null},"enoch":{"life":["David Enoch is an Israeli philosopher working in moral, political and legal philosophy from the early 2000s to the present. He is known for defending robust moral realism, the view that there are objective moral facts as real and mind-independent as mathematical ones. He wrote at a time when many philosophers preferred more modest views that try to explain morality in terms of human attitudes or natural facts.","He took a BA in philosophy and a law degree at Tel Aviv University in 1993 and completed his PhD in philosophy at New York University in 2003. He became a professor of philosophy and law at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem, holding the Rodney Blackman Chair in the Philosophy of Law and co-directing its Center for Moral and Political Philosophy. In October 2023 he took up the Professorship of the Philosophy of Law at Oxford, while keeping links with the Hebrew University.","Enoch's book Taking Morality Seriously: A Defense of Robust Realism appeared in 2011. Two of his articles were chosen for The Philosopher's Annual list of the year's ten best philosophy papers. He writes in a lively, conversational style and has also been active in public debate in Israel."],"timeline":[[1993,"Completes a BA in philosophy and a law degree at Tel Aviv University"],[2003,"Completes his PhD at New York University"],[2006,"Publishes 'Agency, Shmagency'"],[2011,"Publishes Taking Morality Seriously: A Defense of Robust Realism"],[2023,"Takes up the Professorship of the Philosophy of Law at Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Robust moral realism","body":"Enoch argues that there are moral facts that hold no matter what anyone thinks, wants or agrees on. These facts are not physical facts, yet they are no less real, much as many philosophers think mathematical facts are real without being physical."},{"head":"The deliberative indispensability argument","body":"When you decide what to do, you are trying to find out what you have most reason to do, not merely discovering what you happen to want. Enoch argues that we cannot avoid this kind of deliberation, and that it only makes sense if there are normative facts to get right. Just as science justifies belief in the things our best theories need, deliberation justifies belief in normative facts."},{"head":"The argument from impartiality","body":"Imagine two friends disagree about whether to go to a film or a concert; this is a matter of taste, and it is fair to split the difference. But if they disagree about whether slavery is wrong, splitting the difference is not fair. Enoch argues that this shows we treat moral disagreements as being about facts, not preferences."},{"head":"Agency, shmagency","body":"Some philosophers try to ground morality in what is required to be an agent at all. Enoch replies: suppose I am not interested in being an agent, only a 'shmagent', something that acts but does not care about the rules of agency. Why should I care? He argues that such theories cannot answer this without assuming normative facts."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his short paper 'Why I Am an Objectivist about Ethics (And Why You Are, Too)', written for a general audience, before the opening chapter of Taking Morality Seriously.","critics":"Sharon Street's 'Darwinian dilemma' argues that evolution shaped our moral beliefs to help us survive, not to track independent moral facts, so it would be a coincidence if they matched. Enoch accepts that this epistemological challenge is serious and devotes part of his book to answering it.","legacy":"Enoch's book is one of the main modern defences of non-naturalist moral realism, and his 'shmagency' objection is a standard challenge to constructivist theories of morality.","quote":null},"greene":{"life":["Joshua Greene is an American philosopher and psychologist, born in 1974, who works at the meeting point of moral philosophy and brain science. Around 2000, new brain-scanning techniques made it possible to watch which parts of the brain are active while people make moral judgements. Greene was among the first to use them to study the trolley problem.","He studied philosophy at Harvard, graduating in 1997, and completed his PhD in philosophy at Princeton in 2002. While still a student he led a 2001 study, published in Science, that scanned people's brains as they considered moral dilemmas. After postdoctoral work in a Princeton neuroscience lab, he joined Harvard's psychology department in 2006 and became a full professor in 2014. His book Moral Tribes appeared in 2013.","Greene runs the Moral Cognition Lab at Harvard. Besides research on moral judgement, he has worked on practical projects, including ways to increase donations to highly effective charities and an online cooperative game designed to reduce hostility between political opponents."],"timeline":[[1974,"Born in the United States"],[1997,"Graduates in philosophy from Harvard"],[2001,"Publishes an influential brain-imaging study of moral dilemmas in Science"],[2002,"Completes his PhD in philosophy at Princeton"],[2006,"Joins the psychology department at Harvard"],[2008,"Publishes 'The Secret Joke of Kant's Soul'"],[2013,"Publishes Moral Tribes"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Dual-process moral judgement","body":"Greene argues that moral judgements come from two systems, like a camera with automatic settings and a manual mode. Fast, emotional responses drive judgements such as 'don't push the man off the footbridge', while slower reasoning drives judgements such as 'saving five is better than saving one'. Both are useful, but they suit different situations."},{"head":"Personal force","body":"Most people say it is acceptable to flip a switch to divert a trolley from five people onto one, but not to push a man off a footbridge to stop it. Greene's research suggests the difference comes largely from an emotional alarm triggered by harming someone up close with personal force, which may not track what actually matters morally."},{"head":"The tragedy of commonsense morality","body":"Our moral instincts evolved to help us cooperate within our own group. But different groups have different moral instincts, which makes cooperation between groups hard. Greene calls this the tragedy of commonsense morality."},{"head":"Deep pragmatism","body":"To settle disputes between groups, Greene argues, we need a shared 'common currency'. He proposes a form of utilitarianism, which he calls deep pragmatism: when our intuitions conflict, we should switch to manual mode and ask what would produce the best results for everyone."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Greene's brain scans prove that utilitarianism is correct.","truth":"Greene combines his findings with philosophical arguments about which judgements we should trust. Scientific data alone cannot settle what is right, a point stressed by critics such as Selim Berker."},"startHere":"Read Part II of Moral Tribes (2013), which introduces his camera analogy for fast and slow moral thinking in plain language.","critics":"Selim Berker argued in 2009 that the brain-imaging evidence does no real work in Greene's argument: the conclusion depends on philosophical claims about which features of cases are morally relevant. Guy Kahane and others have argued that so-called utilitarian responses in these studies often reflect a willingness to harm rather than impartial concern for others.","legacy":"Greene's work helped found the field of moral psychology that uses brain science, and his dual-process model is one of the most discussed theories of moral judgement.","quote":null},"berker":{"life":["Selim Berker is an American philosopher working in ethics and epistemology from the 2000s to the present. He writes at a time when psychologists and neuroscientists were using brain scans to study moral judgement, and some claimed that their findings showed which moral theories we should accept. Berker became known for a careful argument that such findings cannot do this on their own.","He studied physics at Harvard College, graduating in 1998, and began a doctorate in physics before switching to philosophy. He completed his PhD in philosophy at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 2007 and has taught at Harvard since 2006, first as an instructor and assistant professor and from 2013 as the John L. Loeb Associate Professor of Philosophy. He now holds Harvard's Alford Professorship of Natural Religion, Moral Philosophy, and Civil Polity. He was also a visiting fellow at Princeton's Center for Human Values.","Berker writes mainly journal articles rather than books. His 2009 paper 'The Normative Insignificance of Neuroscience' was selected as one of the ten best philosophy papers of its year. He has also written influential papers in epistemology, especially against the idea that what we should believe is determined by what produces the best overall epistemic results."],"timeline":[[1998,"Graduates in physics from Harvard College"],[2007,"Completes his PhD in philosophy at MIT and becomes assistant professor at Harvard"],[2009,"Publishes 'The Normative Insignificance of Neuroscience'"],[2013,"Publishes 'Epistemic Teleology and the Separateness of Propositions'"],[2013,"Becomes John L. Loeb Associate Professor of Philosophy at Harvard"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Brain scans cannot settle ethics","body":"Berker argues that facts about which brain areas light up when we make moral judgements cannot by themselves show which judgements are right. To get from 'this judgement comes from emotion' to 'this judgement is unreliable', you need a moral premise about what matters. Once that premise is added, the neuroscience no longer does the work."},{"head":"Testing Greene's argument","body":"Berker examined Joshua Greene's argument that our non-utilitarian intuitions are unreliable because they come from fast emotional processes. He showed that each version of the argument either depends on disputed data or rests on a moral claim, for example that personal force is morally irrelevant, that could be defended without any brain science."},{"head":"Against epistemic consequentialism","body":"Some epistemologists think we should believe whatever leads to the most true beliefs overall. Berker argues that this is like the worst forms of consequentialism in ethics: it would permit believing something false if that brought about many true beliefs elsewhere. Each belief must be judged on its own evidence."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'The Normative Insignificance of Neuroscience' (2009), which is long but clearly organised and lays out each version of the argument from neuroscience to ethics in turn.","critics":"Joshua Greene replied that empirical findings can still matter indirectly, by revealing which features our intuitions respond to; once we see that a judgement is driven by something like physical closeness, we can reflect on whether that feature really matters. Other philosophers agree with Berker in principle but think he underestimates how much science can support moral arguments.","legacy":"Berker's paper is the standard statement of the case against drawing moral conclusions directly from neuroscience, and it is widely read in courses on moral psychology.","quote":null},"voltaire":{"life":["Voltaire was born François-Marie Arouet in Paris in 1694, in the France of Louis XIV. It was a world of absolute monarchy, an official Catholic Church with real power over books and beliefs, and censors who could ban a work and have it publicly burned. Protestants were persecuted, and a writer who mocked the wrong person could be locked up without trial. At the same time, salons, coffee houses and printing presses were creating a new reading public hungry for wit and argument.","He made his name early as a poet and playwright, and was twice sent to the Bastille prison as a young man. After a quarrel with a nobleman he went to England (1726–1728), where he admired its religious toleration, Newton's science and Locke's philosophy. His Letters Concerning the English Nation praised all this, and the French edition was condemned and burned. He later lived at Cirey with the scientist Émilie du Châtelet, spent a stormy stay at the court of Frederick the Great of Prussia, and finally settled at Ferney, near the Swiss border, where he could escape French authorities quickly.","Voltaire wrote an enormous amount: plays, poems, histories, tales, pamphlets and over twenty thousand surviving letters. He often published anonymously or under false names and denied writing his own books. From Ferney he campaigned for victims of religious injustice, most famously Jean Calas, a Protestant executed in 1762 on a false charge, whose name Voltaire helped clear. His rallying cry was 'écrasez l'infâme', roughly 'crush the infamous thing', meaning intolerant, persecuting religion. He returned to Paris in triumph in 1778 and died there that year."],"timeline":[[1694,"Born François-Marie Arouet in Paris"],[1717,"Imprisoned in the Bastille for about a year over satirical verses"],[1726,"Goes to England, where he stays until 1728"],[1733,"Letters Concerning the English Nation published in London"],[1759,"Publishes Candide; settles at Ferney about this time"],[1763,"Publishes his Treatise on Tolerance after the Calas affair"],[1764,"Publishes the Philosophical Dictionary"],[1778,"Returns to Paris to great acclaim and dies there"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Toleration over fanaticism","body":"Voltaire argued that persecuting people for their beliefs is both cruel and absurd, since nobody can force real belief. He pointed to England, where many religious groups lived side by side and traded peacefully. A society that lets people worship differently, he thought, is more peaceful than one that tries to make everyone agree."},{"head":"Deism: God without dogma","body":"Voltaire believed in a Creator, partly because the order of nature seemed to need a designer, like a watch needs a watchmaker. But he rejected miracles, revealed scripture and the power of priests. For him, true religion came down to worshipping God and treating others justly, not to rituals or doctrines."},{"head":"Ridicule as a weapon","body":"Voltaire used humour as an argument. In Candide, the hero's tutor Pangloss keeps insisting all is for the best while disasters pile up, and the reader laughs at the idea. Satire, he found, could reach readers who would never sit through a treatise, and it was harder for censors to answer."},{"head":"Cultivate your garden","body":"Candide ends with the advice that we must cultivate our garden. After grand theories have failed to explain suffering, the characters find a kind of peace in modest, useful work. It is a practical answer to a problem that philosophy cannot neatly solve."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Voltaire said 'I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it.'","truth":"The line was written in 1906 by his biographer Evelyn Beatrice Hall as a summary of his attitude. Voltaire never wrote it, though he did campaign for freedom of thought."},"startHere":"Read Candide, a short and very funny novel that can be finished in an afternoon. It shows his attack on easy optimism and his talent for using a story to make a philosophical point.","critics":"Rousseau wrote to Voltaire in 1756 objecting that his bleak poem on the Lisbon earthquake took away people's comfort in Providence while offering nothing in its place. Later critics also noted that his own writings contain prejudiced remarks, including about Jews, which sit badly with his campaign for tolerance.","legacy":"Voltaire became the symbol of the French Enlightenment and of the writer who challenges church and state. Modern ideas of religious freedom, free speech and fair trials owe much to his campaigns.","quote":null},"hobbes":{"life":["Thomas Hobbes was born in 1588 in Malmesbury, England, the year the Spanish Armada threatened invasion; he later joked that his mother gave birth to twins, himself and fear. He lived through the English Civil War (1642–1651), when King and Parliament fought, a king was executed and ordinary people suffered from years of violence. At the same time, the new science of Galileo and others was explaining the world as matter in motion, and Hobbes wanted to explain politics in the same way.","After studying at Oxford, Hobbes spent most of his working life as a tutor and secretary to the Cavendish family, the Earls of Devonshire, which allowed him to travel in Europe and meet leading thinkers. As conflict grew in 1640 he fled to Paris, fearing his views on royal power would get him into trouble. There he wrote Leviathan, published in 1651, and for a time tutored the exiled future King Charles II in mathematics. He returned to England and kept writing into his nineties, dying in 1679.","Hobbes came to philosophy late; according to his friend John Aubrey, he fell in love with geometry around the age of forty after reading Euclid. He admired its method of moving step by step from definitions to conclusions and tried to do the same for politics. He was combative and enjoyed controversy, and he spent years in a bitter dispute with the mathematician John Wallis. Many contemporaries suspected him of atheism, and after the Great Fire of London (1666) there were moves in Parliament to investigate his books."],"timeline":[[1588,"Born in Westport, near Malmesbury, England"],[1629,"Publishes an English translation of the Greek historian Thucydides"],[1640,"Flees to Paris as political tensions grow in England"],[1642,"Publishes De Cive (On the Citizen) in Latin"],[1651,"Publishes Leviathan; returns to England around the end of the year"],[1655,"Publishes De Corpore (On Body)"],[1679,"Dies at Hardwick Hall, Derbyshire, aged 91"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The state of nature","body":"Hobbes asked what life would be like with no government at all. Because people are roughly equal, want scarce things and fear each other, he argued it would be a war of every person against every person. No one could safely farm, trade or build, because anyone might take what they had."},{"head":"The social contract","body":"To escape this war, people agree to give up their right to do whatever they like and hand power to a sovereign: a ruler or ruling assembly. They do this out of self-interest, because security is better than constant fear. Government's authority comes from this agreement, not from God's choice of a king."},{"head":"A strong, undivided sovereign","body":"Hobbes thought the sovereign's power must be very great and not split between rival bodies, since divided power had just led England into civil war. Even a bad ruler, he argued, is usually better than a return to chaos. Subjects may still resist if the sovereign tries to kill them, because self-preservation was the whole point."},{"head":"Everything is matter in motion","body":"Hobbes was a materialist: he thought only physical bodies exist, and that thoughts and feelings are motions in the body. Desire is motion towards something, fear is motion away from it. This made him one of the first to try to build ethics and politics on a scientific picture of human beings."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hobbes believed humans are naturally evil.","truth":"Hobbes thought people are mostly driven by self-preservation and fear, not by wickedness. Conflict arises from their situation: equality, scarcity and the absence of a power to keep them all in awe."},"startHere":"Read chapter 13 of Leviathan, only a few pages long, which describes the state of nature and contains his famous description of life without government. Then read chapters 14 and 17 to see how he gets from that war to the state.","critics":"John Locke objected that it makes no sense to escape the danger of other people by handing unlimited power to one ruler, which he compared to avoiding polecats and foxes by running into the arms of a lion. Others have argued that people are more naturally cooperative than Hobbes allowed.","legacy":"Hobbes is usually seen as the founder of the modern social contract tradition, later developed by Locke, Rousseau and Rawls. Modern game theory and the study of cooperation still use his state of nature as a starting point.","quote":{"text":"And the life of man, solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short.","src":"Leviathan, ch. 13","tr":"original English (spelling modernised)"}},"descartes":{"life":["René Descartes was born in 1596 in La Haye, a small town in western France that is now named Descartes after him. He grew up as the old scientific picture inherited from Aristotle was being shaken by new discoveries, such as Galileo's observations with the telescope. Europe was also torn by religious war: the Thirty Years' War began in 1618. Many educated people felt that old certainties had gone and wondered whether anything could be known for sure.","He was educated at the Jesuit college of La Flèche and took a law degree, then joined the army of Maurice of Nassau in the Netherlands in 1618. In November 1619, by his own account, he had a series of dreams that convinced him to rebuild knowledge anew. From 1628 he lived mostly in the Netherlands, where he could work quietly. He held back a book on the physical world after Galileo was condemned in 1633, but went on to publish the Discourse on the Method (1637) and the Meditations (1641). In 1649 he went to Stockholm to teach Queen Christina of Sweden, and died there in February 1650.","Descartes was a mathematician and scientist as much as a philosopher. He invented the system of plotting points with x and y coordinates that pupils still learn, and wrote on optics, weather and anatomy. He liked privacy and moved house often. He sent the Meditations to other thinkers before publishing and printed their objections with his replies, a kind of early peer review. His long exchange of letters with Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia produced some of the sharpest criticism of his views."],"timeline":[[1596,"Born in La Haye, Touraine, France"],[1619,"Has the dreams that, by his account, set his life's project"],[1628,"Moves to the Netherlands, where he lives for most of the next twenty years"],[1637,"Publishes the Discourse on the Method with essays including the Geometry"],[1641,"Publishes the Meditations on First Philosophy with Objections and Replies"],[1644,"Publishes the Principles of Philosophy"],[1649,"Publishes The Passions of the Soul and travels to Sweden"],[1650,"Dies in Stockholm"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The method of doubt","body":"Descartes decided to doubt every belief that could possibly be false, to see whether anything survived. Our senses sometimes trick us, we might be dreaming, and he even imagined a powerful evil demon deceiving us about everything. The point was not to stay doubting but to find a foundation that doubt could not shake."},{"head":"I think, therefore I am","body":"Even if a demon deceives me about everything, I must exist in order to be deceived. Whenever I am thinking, even doubting, I can be certain that I exist as a thinking thing. This was the first certainty on which Descartes tried to rebuild knowledge."},{"head":"Mind and body are different things","body":"Descartes argued that the mind is a thinking thing with no size or shape, while the body is an extended thing that takes up space. This view is called dualism. It explains why thoughts seem so unlike physical objects, but it raises the puzzle of how the two could ever affect each other."},{"head":"God guarantees clear ideas","body":"To move beyond his own existence, Descartes argued that a perfect God exists and would not deceive us. So what we perceive very clearly and distinctly, such as truths of mathematics, can be trusted. Critics later said this reasoning goes in a circle, since he uses clear ideas to prove God and God to trust clear ideas."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Descartes was a sceptic who thought we cannot know anything.","truth":"He used doubt as a tool to defeat scepticism. His aim was to find beliefs so certain that no doubt could touch them and then to build science on them."},"startHere":"Read the first two of his six Meditations, which together run to about twenty pages. They take you through the method of doubt and the discovery of 'I think' in the first person, as if you are thinking alongside him.","critics":"Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia asked in 1643 how a mind with no physical size or position could move a physical body, a problem Descartes never fully solved. Antoine Arnauld pointed out the apparent circle in using clear ideas to prove God and God to vouch for clear ideas.","legacy":"Descartes is often called the father of modern philosophy because he made 'What can I know?' the starting question. The mind–body problem he left behind is still debated in philosophy and neuroscience.","quote":{"text":"I think, therefore I am","src":"Discourse on the Method, Part IV","tr":"Elizabeth S. Haldane and G. R. T. Ross (1911)"}},"spinoza":{"life":["Baruch (also called Bento, later Benedict) de Spinoza was born in Amsterdam in 1632 to a family of Portuguese Jews whose ancestors had been forced to convert to Christianity and had fled the Inquisition. The Dutch Republic was then one of the most tolerant and commercially successful places in Europe, a centre of printing, trade and new science. Even so, there were limits: religious authorities, both Jewish and Calvinist, watched closely for dangerous ideas.","Spinoza worked in his family's import business as a young man. In 1656, at about 23, he was expelled from the Amsterdam Jewish community with an unusually harsh ban; the exact reasons were not recorded, but they concerned his views and conduct. He moved to Rijnsburg, then Voorburg, then The Hague, earning money partly by grinding lenses for microscopes and telescopes. He published his Theological-Political Treatise anonymously in 1670, and it was widely condemned. His main work, the Ethics, appeared only after his death from a lung illness in 1677.","Spinoza lived simply but was not isolated. He corresponded with scientists and philosophers, including Henry Oldenburg of the Royal Society, and had a circle of friends who discussed his drafts. In 1673 he turned down a professorship at Heidelberg, partly to protect his freedom to think as he chose. Leibniz visited him in 1676. He wrote the Ethics in the style of a geometry textbook, with definitions, axioms and numbered proofs, believing philosophy should be as rigorous as mathematics."],"timeline":[[1632,"Born in Amsterdam"],[1656,"Expelled from the Amsterdam Jewish community"],[1663,"Publishes Principles of Cartesian Philosophy, the only book under his own name in his lifetime"],[1670,"Theological-Political Treatise published anonymously; moves to The Hague"],[1673,"Declines a professorship at the University of Heidelberg"],[1677,"Dies in The Hague; the Ethics is published that year with his other unpublished writings"]],"ideas":[{"head":"God or Nature","body":"Spinoza argued that there can only be one infinite substance, the single reality that everything else depends on. He called it 'God or Nature'. On this view God is not a person outside the world who makes choices; everything that exists is part of, or a way of being of, this one reality."},{"head":"Everything follows necessarily","body":"For Spinoza, everything happens because it must, given the nature of reality, much as the angles of a triangle must add up to two right angles. There is no chance and no free will in the sense of an uncaused choice. We feel free only because we do not know the causes of our desires."},{"head":"Freedom through understanding","body":"If everything is necessary, what is freedom? Spinoza's answer is that we become free by understanding the causes that move us. Someone who understands why they feel jealous is less controlled by jealousy than someone swept along by it. Understanding turns passive emotions into active ones."},{"head":"Mind and body as one","body":"Spinoza denied Descartes' view that mind and body are two different substances. He thought they are the same thing viewed in two ways: as thought and as physical extension. This removes the puzzle of how a mind pushes a body, since they were never two things."},{"head":"Freedom of thought","body":"In the Theological-Political Treatise Spinoza argued that the Bible should be studied historically, like any other ancient text, and that the state should allow people to think and say what they like. He believed that a stable state is one that leaves people free to think."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Spinoza was a lonely hermit grinding lenses in isolation.","truth":"He lived modestly, but had friends, a discussion circle and a wide correspondence with scientists and philosophers across Europe."},"startHere":"Begin with the Appendix to Part I of the Ethics, a few pages of plain prose without the geometric proofs. It attacks the idea that nature was made for human purposes and gives a good feel for his whole outlook.","critics":"Pierre Bayle, in his widely read Historical and Critical Dictionary, attacked Spinoza's single-substance view as absurd and impious. Many others, including Leibniz, objected that his strict necessity leaves no room for genuine choice or moral responsibility.","legacy":"Spinoza became a hero to later freethinkers and is seen as a founder of biblical criticism and of the case for a secular, tolerant state. Einstein said he believed in 'Spinoza's God'.","quote":{"text":"But all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.","src":"Ethics, Part V, Proposition 42, Scholium","tr":"R. H. M. Elwes (1883)"}},"locke":{"life":["John Locke was born in 1632 in Wrington, Somerset, England. His father was a lawyer who fought on Parliament's side in the English Civil War. Locke grew up in a century of political upheaval: civil war, the execution of Charles I, the rule of Cromwell, the return of the monarchy in 1660 and, in 1688, the Glorious Revolution that removed James II. Arguments about the limits of royal power and about religious freedom were matters of life and death.","He studied at Westminster School and Christ Church, Oxford, where he trained in medicine and took an interest in the new experimental science of Robert Boyle. In 1667 he joined the household of Lord Ashley, later Earl of Shaftesbury, as doctor and adviser, and was drawn into politics. When Shaftesbury fell from power, Locke went into exile in the Netherlands (1683–1689). He returned after the Glorious Revolution, and within a year his three major works appeared. He later served on the Board of Trade and died in 1704.","Locke was cautious by nature. He published the Two Treatises of Government and the Letter Concerning Toleration anonymously and denied writing the Two Treatises for most of his life. He worked slowly, spending nearly twenty years on the Essay Concerning Human Understanding, which began after a discussion with friends ran into confusion. He also wrote on education, money and interpreting the Bible. In his later years he lived at Oates in Essex, in the household of his friend Lady Damaris Masham."],"timeline":[[1632,"Born in Wrington, Somerset, England"],[1667,"Joins the household of Lord Ashley, later Earl of Shaftesbury"],[1683,"Goes into exile in the Netherlands"],[1689,"Returns to England; the Letter Concerning Toleration, the Essay Concerning Human Understanding and the Two Treatises of Government appear (the last two dated 1690)"],[1693,"Publishes Some Thoughts Concerning Education"],[1696,"Appointed to the Board of Trade"],[1704,"Dies at Oates, Essex"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The mind as blank paper","body":"Locke argued that we are not born with any ideas already in our minds. He compared the newborn mind to white paper, which experience writes on. All our ideas come from the senses or from reflecting on what our own minds do. This view, that knowledge comes from experience, is called empiricism."},{"head":"Natural rights","body":"Locke held that, even before any government exists, people have rights to life, liberty and property, based on a natural law that reason can discover. Governments do not create these rights. Their job is to protect them."},{"head":"Government by consent","body":"Political power is legitimate only if the people consent to it. If a government seriously and repeatedly violates people's rights, it breaks its trust, and the people may resist and replace it. This argument was used to justify the Glorious Revolution and, later, the American Revolution."},{"head":"Property from labour","body":"Locke argued that when you mix your labour with something unowned, such as picking apples from a wild tree, it becomes yours. This explains how private property can arise before governments. He added limits, such as leaving enough and as good for others."},{"head":"Religious toleration","body":"Locke argued that the state should not force religious belief, because force can produce outward conformity but never sincere faith. Churches are voluntary societies and should not use the power of the state. He did, however, exclude atheists and Catholics from full toleration."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Locke coined the phrase 'tabula rasa' for the blank-slate mind.","truth":"In the Essay he mostly speaks of 'white paper'. The image of the mind as a blank writing tablet goes back to Aristotle and was used by medieval thinkers too."},"startHere":"Read chapter 2 of the Second Treatise of Government, 'Of the State of Nature', which is short and clear. Then try chapter 5, 'Of Property', to see one of his most influential and debated arguments.","critics":"Leibniz, in his New Essays, argued that the mind is not blank: it has inborn tendencies, like the veins in a block of marble that make it easier to carve one shape than another. Later critics, such as Marxists, argued that his theory of property justifies large inequalities and was used to justify taking land from Indigenous peoples.","legacy":"Locke shaped liberal democracy: the US Declaration of Independence echoes his ideas of natural rights and consent. His empiricism set the agenda for Berkeley and Hume.","quote":{"text":"Let us then suppose the mind to be, as we say, white paper, void of all characters, without any ideas.","src":"An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, Book II, ch. 1, sec. 2","tr":"original English"}},"leibniz":{"life":["Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz was born in Leipzig in 1646, just before the end of the Thirty Years' War, which had devastated the German lands. The Holy Roman Empire was split into hundreds of states, and Catholics and Protestants still distrusted each other deeply. Leibniz spent much of his life seeking to reconcile opposites: Catholic and Protestant churches, ancient and modern philosophy, and faith and science.","His father was a professor of moral philosophy, and Leibniz read widely in his father's library as a boy. He earned a doctorate in law in 1667 and entered the service of the Elector of Mainz. A diplomatic mission took him to Paris (1672–1676), where he met leading scientists and, around 1675, developed calculus. From 1676 until his death he worked for the dukes of Brunswick in Hanover as librarian, adviser and court historian. He helped found the Berlin Academy of Sciences in 1700 and became its first president. He died in Hanover in 1716, by then out of favour at court.","Leibniz was a tireless worker with interests in almost everything: mathematics, physics, law, history, linguistics, engineering, China and theology. He built one of the first calculating machines that could multiply, and he worked out binary arithmetic, the base-2 system that computers use. Much of his philosophy is scattered in letters and short essays, and he corresponded with hundreds of people. His later years were soured by a bitter dispute with Newton's supporters over who had invented calculus first; historians now agree they developed it independently."],"timeline":[[1646,"Born in Leipzig"],[1667,"Receives a doctorate in law from the University of Altdorf"],[1672,"Begins a four-year stay in Paris; develops calculus there c. 1675"],[1676,"Enters the service of the dukes of Brunswick in Hanover; visits Spinoza on the way"],[1686,"Writes the Discourse on Metaphysics"],[1710,"Publishes the Theodicy"],[1714,"Writes the Monadology"],[1716,"Dies in Hanover"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The best of all possible worlds","body":"God, being perfectly good, wise and powerful, chose to create the best world that was possible. This does not mean every part is pleasant. It means that any world with less evil would, overall, be worse, perhaps by having fewer goods or less order, in the way a painting may need dark shadows to be beautiful."},{"head":"Monads","body":"Leibniz argued that the basic building blocks of reality cannot be lumps of matter, since matter can always be divided further. Instead they are simple, soul-like units he called monads, each with its own perceptions. Everything we see as physical is made up of vast numbers of them."},{"head":"Pre-established harmony","body":"Monads do not really act on each other. Instead, God set them all up at the start so that their inner states always line up, like many clocks set to show the same time. This was his answer to how mind and body seem to interact without one pushing the other."},{"head":"Principle of sufficient reason","body":"Leibniz held that nothing happens without a reason why it is this way rather than another. If two options are exactly alike, there could be no reason to choose one, so God would never face such a choice. This principle runs through his whole philosophy."},{"head":"Identity of indiscernibles","body":"No two things can be exactly alike in every respect. If they were, they would be one and the same thing. He reportedly challenged courtiers to find two leaves that were truly identical."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Leibniz's claim that this is the best possible world means he thought everything is fine and suffering does not matter.","truth":"That is Voltaire's parody in Candide. Leibniz accepted that there is real evil and suffering, but argued that any possible world with less of it would be worse overall."},"startHere":"Read the Monadology, a set of 90 short numbered paragraphs that summarise his whole system in about twenty pages. It is compressed, so read a few paragraphs at a time.","critics":"Voltaire mocked the best-possible-world idea in Candide, especially after the 1755 Lisbon earthquake killed tens of thousands. Many readers since have felt that no amount of overall good could justify the suffering of innocent people, a version of the problem of evil.","legacy":"Leibniz's calculus notation, with its dx and the long S for integrals, is the one still used today, and his binary arithmetic underlies modern computing. His rationalism shaped German philosophy up to Kant.","quote":null},"berkeley":{"life":["George Berkeley was born in 1685 near Kilkenny, Ireland, then ruled by England, into an Anglo-Irish Protestant family. He grew up in the generation after Newton and Locke, when the new science described the world as tiny particles of matter in motion. Some people feared this 'mechanical' picture left no room for God and could lead to atheism and scepticism, the view that we cannot really know anything. Berkeley shared that worry.","He entered Trinity College Dublin at fifteen and became a fellow. While still in his twenties he wrote his main philosophical works: An Essay towards a New Theory of Vision (1709), the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) and the Three Dialogues (1713). He then travelled in Europe and spent years on a plan to found a college in Bermuda, living in Newport, Rhode Island (1729–1731) while waiting for government money that never came. In 1734 he became Bishop of Cloyne in Ireland, and he died in Oxford in 1753.","Berkeley was a clergyman for most of his adult life and saw his philosophy as a defence of religion and common sense. He was also practical and public-spirited: he wrote on Irish poverty and economics in The Querist, and late in life promoted tar-water as a medicine. He criticised the logic of Newton's calculus in The Analyst (1734), a challenge that mathematicians took seriously. The city of Berkeley in California is named after him."],"timeline":[[1685,"Born near Kilkenny, Ireland"],[1709,"Publishes An Essay towards a New Theory of Vision"],[1710,"Publishes A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge"],[1713,"Publishes Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous"],[1729,"Arrives in Newport, Rhode Island, awaiting funds for his Bermuda college"],[1734,"Becomes Bishop of Cloyne; publishes The Analyst"],[1753,"Dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"To be is to be perceived","body":"Berkeley argued that for ordinary things like tables and apples, to exist just is to be perceived. An apple is its colour, smell, taste and feel, and these are all ideas in a mind. He thought it made no sense to talk of something that exists completely unperceived."},{"head":"No material substance","body":"Locke thought there was an unseen 'matter' underneath the qualities we perceive. Berkeley argued we have no idea at all of such a thing, and that it does no work. So he concluded that only minds and their ideas exist. This view is called idealism, or immaterialism."},{"head":"God keeps the world going","body":"If things exist only when perceived, why does the tree in the empty park not vanish? Berkeley's answer is that God perceives everything all the time. The regular order of our experiences, which we call the laws of nature, is the language in which God speaks to us."},{"head":"Against abstract ideas","body":"Berkeley denied that we can form abstract ideas, such as an idea of a triangle that is neither large nor small, nor any particular shape. When we think 'triangle', we think of a particular one and use it to stand for all. He believed many philosophical puzzles came from muddled talk of abstractions."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Berkeley thought objects pop out of existence when nobody is looking at them.","truth":"He held that objects continue to exist because God always perceives them. His aim was to defend the reality of the everyday world, not to deny it."},"startHere":"Read the first of the Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous. It is a lively conversation in which Philonous (Berkeley's voice) argues the sceptical Hylas out of his belief in matter.","critics":"Hume remarked that Berkeley's arguments admit of no answer and yet produce no conviction. Thomas Reid argued that his view clashes with the common-sense beliefs we cannot help holding; Samuel Johnson's famous kick of a stone, reported by Boswell, made a similar point less carefully.","legacy":"Berkeley pushed empiricism to a startling conclusion and forced later philosophers to ask what we really mean by 'matter'. His critique of abstraction and of absolute space influenced Hume, Mach and, indirectly, Einstein.","quote":{"text":"Their esse is percipi, nor is it possible they should have any existence out of the minds or thinking things which perceive them.","src":"A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge, sec. 3","tr":"original English"}},"hume":{"life":["David Hume was born in Edinburgh in 1711. Scotland had recently joined England in the 1707 Act of Union, and over Hume's lifetime Edinburgh became a centre of what is now called the Scottish Enlightenment, with thinkers such as Adam Smith and the historian William Robertson. The Church of Scotland was still strict and powerful, and open doubt about religion could cost a man a university job. Science, trade and polite conversation were all expanding fast.","Hume entered Edinburgh University very young, tried law and then business, and moved to France (1734–1737), where at La Flèche he wrote A Treatise of Human Nature. It appeared in 1739–1740 and, in his own words, fell dead-born from the press. He recast its ideas in shorter, more readable works, including An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748). His reputation for religious scepticism blocked him from professorships at Edinburgh and Glasgow. As keeper of the Advocates' Library in Edinburgh he wrote a best-selling History of England, and he later served as a diplomat in Paris. He died in Edinburgh in 1776.","Hume was known for his good nature and was popular in Paris salons, where he was called 'le bon David'. He was a sociable host and a loyal friend, especially to Adam Smith. In 1766 he helped Rousseau find refuge in England, but Rousseau came to believe Hume was plotting against him, and the quarrel became public. Hume faced death calmly; his friend Smith wrote that he had been as near to the idea of a perfectly wise and virtuous man as human frailty allows."],"timeline":[[1711,"Born in Edinburgh"],[1734,"Moves to France, where he writes the Treatise"],[1739,"First two books of A Treatise of Human Nature published"],[1748,"Publishes the work later titled An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding"],[1752,"Becomes keeper of the Advocates' Library, Edinburgh"],[1754,"First volume of his History of England published"],[1763,"Goes to Paris as secretary to the British embassy"],[1776,"Dies in Edinburgh; the Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion appear in 1779"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ideas come from impressions","body":"Hume divided the contents of the mind into vivid impressions (what we see, hear or feel) and fainter ideas copied from them. A good test of any word is to ask which impression it comes from. If none can be found, the word may have no real meaning."},{"head":"The problem of causation","body":"When one billiard ball hits another, we see one motion followed by another, but we never see the 'necessary connection' between them. Our belief that the first causes the second comes from habit: we have seen such things happen together many times. Reason alone cannot show that the future will be like the past."},{"head":"The problem of induction","body":"We expect the sun to rise tomorrow because it always has. But any argument that the future will resemble the past would itself assume what it is trying to prove. So, Hume argued, our most basic predictions rest on custom, not on reasoning, though we cannot live without them."},{"head":"Reason serves the passions","body":"Reason can tell us facts and how to get what we want, but it cannot by itself make us want anything. Our goals come from feelings, or 'passions'. Morality too, he argued, is grounded in feelings of approval and disapproval, especially sympathy with others."},{"head":"Is and ought","body":"Hume noticed that writers often move from statements about what is the case to claims about what ought to be the case without explaining how. A list of facts about a situation does not by itself tell you what you should do. This 'is–ought gap' is still debated in ethics."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hume proved that causes do not exist.","truth":"Hume did not deny that there are causes. He argued that we cannot perceive necessary connections or prove them by reason, and that our belief in causes rests on habit, which he thought natural and unavoidable."},"startHere":"Read Sections IV and V of An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, which set out his doubts about causation and induction in clear, short chapters. Hume himself wanted to be judged by the Enquiry rather than the earlier Treatise.","critics":"Kant said Hume woke him from his 'dogmatic slumber', and argued that concepts like cause are not learned from experience but are needed to have experience at all. Thomas Reid objected that Hume's starting point, that we only ever perceive our own impressions, leads to absurd conclusions and should be rejected.","legacy":"The problem of induction remains central to the philosophy of science, and Hume's account of morality as grounded in feeling shapes many modern views. Kant, Darwin and Einstein all acknowledged his influence.","quote":{"text":"Reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions, and can never pretend to any other office than to serve and obey them.","src":"A Treatise of Human Nature, Book 2, Part 3, Section 3","tr":"original English"}},"smith":{"life":["Adam Smith was born in Kirkcaldy, a small port in Fife, Scotland, and baptised on 5 June 1723; his exact birth date is unknown. His father had died a few months before. Scotland was growing richer through trade with the Americas, and Glasgow in particular was booming from tobacco. Smith belonged to the Scottish Enlightenment, a circle of thinkers interested in how societies develop, from hunting bands to commercial nations.","He studied at Glasgow University under the philosopher Francis Hutcheson and then at Balliol College, Oxford, which he found dull. After giving public lectures in Edinburgh, he became a professor at Glasgow in 1751, teaching moral philosophy from 1752. The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759) made his name. In 1764 he left to tutor the young Duke of Buccleuch on a tour of France, where he met leading French economists. He then spent about ten years writing The Wealth of Nations (1776). From 1778 he worked as a Commissioner of Customs in Edinburgh, where he died in 1790.","Smith was famously absent-minded, and many stories about his distraction circulated, though some are later embellishments. He was a careful, slow writer who revised his books many times; he was still revising The Theory of Moral Sentiments in the year he died. He was a close friend of David Hume. Shortly before his death he had most of his unpublished papers burned, so some of his planned works, including one on law and government, are lost."],"timeline":[[1723,"Baptised in Kirkcaldy, Scotland"],[1751,"Appointed professor at the University of Glasgow"],[1759,"Publishes The Theory of Moral Sentiments"],[1764,"Leaves Glasgow to tutor the Duke of Buccleuch in France"],[1776,"Publishes An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations"],[1778,"Becomes Commissioner of Customs in Edinburgh"],[1790,"Dies in Edinburgh"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Sympathy and the impartial spectator","body":"Smith argued that we naturally share in the feelings of others by imagining ourselves in their place. We learn to judge our own conduct by imagining how a fair, well-informed onlooker, the 'impartial spectator', would see it. Conscience, on this view, is that imagined spectator inside us."},{"head":"The division of labour","body":"Smith's famous example is a pin factory: one worker alone might make barely one pin a day, but ten workers each doing one step can make thousands. Splitting work into specialised tasks hugely increases what a society can produce. It is the main source of the wealth of nations."},{"head":"Self-interest and the market","body":"In a market, people serve each other's needs by pursuing their own interest. The baker sells you bread to earn a living, not out of kindness. Smith used the phrase 'invisible hand' to describe how such choices can benefit society without anyone planning it."},{"head":"Wary of merchants and monopolies","body":"Smith warned that business people often conspire to raise prices and seek special favours from government, such as monopolies and tariffs. He attacked the 'mercantile system' that protected these interests. He also argued that the state has duties: defence, justice, some public works and help with educating ordinary people."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Adam Smith taught that greed is good and governments should do nothing.","truth":"His first book grounds morality in sympathy, and the 'invisible hand' appears only once in The Wealth of Nations. He supported several government roles and was suspicious of business interests."},"startHere":"Read Book I, chapters 1 to 3 of The Wealth of Nations, which explain the division of labour with the pin factory and the butcher and baker. Then read Part I, section 1, chapter 1 of The Theory of Moral Sentiments, 'Of Sympathy', to see the other side of his thought.","critics":"Karl Marx, building on Smith's own idea that labour creates value, argued that free market exchange hides the exploitation of workers by owners. Nineteenth-century German scholars posed 'das Adam Smith Problem', claiming that his two books contradict each other, though most scholars now think they fit together.","legacy":"The Wealth of Nations is often seen as the founding work of modern economics. His moral theory of sympathy is enjoying renewed attention among philosophers and psychologists.","quote":{"text":"It is not from the benevolence of the butcher, the brewer, or the baker, that we expect our dinner, but from their regard to their own interest.","src":"The Wealth of Nations, Book I, ch. 2","tr":"original English"}},"rousseau":{"life":["Jean-Jacques Rousseau was born in 1712 in Geneva, a small Protestant city-republic that he was proud to call his homeland. His mother died days after his birth, and his father, a watchmaker, later left the city after a quarrel. Rousseau grew up while France, its powerful neighbour, was dominated by a glittering, unequal society of aristocrats and Paris salons, which he came to see as vain and corrupt.","He left Geneva at fifteen and lived a wandering life, partly under the protection of Madame de Warens in Savoy. In 1742 he went to Paris and became friends with Diderot and the circle writing the Encyclopédie. He shot to fame in 1750 by winning a prize essay competition with the argument that the arts and sciences had corrupted morals. Then came the Discourse on Inequality (1755), a best-selling novel Julie (1761), and in 1762 both The Social Contract and Emile. These were condemned in Paris and Geneva, and he spent years on the run. He died at Ermenonville, near Paris, in 1778.","Rousseau was self-taught, prickly and often at odds with former friends, including Diderot, Voltaire and Hume. He earned money copying music and wrote an opera and a dictionary of music. His Confessions, published after his death, is one of the first modern autobiographies, and in it he admits to having given his five children to a foundling hospital, a fact his critics have often set against his writings on education."],"timeline":[[1712,"Born in Geneva"],[1742,"Moves to Paris"],[1750,"Wins the Dijon Academy prize with his Discourse on the Sciences and Arts"],[1755,"Publishes the Discourse on the Origin of Inequality"],[1762,"Publishes The Social Contract and Emile; both are condemned and he flees"],[1766,"Comes to England with Hume's help; they soon quarrel"],[1778,"Dies at Ermenonville, France"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Natural goodness, social corruption","body":"Rousseau argued that humans in their original state were not wicked, but simple, independent and moved by self-love and pity. It is society, with its comparison, competition and private property, that makes us vain, jealous and unequal. The problem is not human nature but the way we live together."},{"head":"The origin of inequality","body":"Inequality, Rousseau claimed, began when someone first fenced off land and said 'this is mine', and others believed him. Property led to rich and poor, and the rich then set up laws that protected their advantage. What looks like a natural order is really a history of human choices."},{"head":"The general will","body":"In a legitimate state, citizens together make the laws they live under, aiming at the common good rather than their private interests. Rousseau called this shared will for the common good the 'general will'. Obeying laws you have made yourself, he argued, is a kind of freedom."},{"head":"Education that follows nature","body":"In Emile, Rousseau argued that children should learn through experience and curiosity, not by memorising books and being forced into adult manners. A child should, for example, learn geography by getting lost and finding the way home. Many later ideas in child-centred education trace back to this book."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Rousseau praised the 'noble savage' and wanted us to return to the forest.","truth":"He never used the phrase 'noble savage', which comes from the poet John Dryden. He thought we could not go back to the state of nature; he wanted better societies, not a return to primitive life."},"startHere":"Read Book I of The Social Contract, which is short and opens with his famous line about chains. For a more vivid picture of his thinking, try Part Two of the Discourse on Inequality.","critics":"Critics from Benjamin Constant to Isaiah Berlin argued that the idea of a general will, and Rousseau's claim that someone may be 'forced to be free', can be used to justify crushing individuals in the name of the people. Voltaire mocked his praise of simple life as wanting us to walk on all fours.","legacy":"Rousseau inspired the French Revolution, Romanticism, and modern ideas of democracy, education and authenticity. Kant was deeply affected by him, and debates about inequality still begin from his questions.","quote":{"text":"Man is born free; and everywhere he is in chains.","src":"The Social Contract, Book I, ch. 1","tr":"G. D. H. Cole (1913)"}},"kant":{"life":["Immanuel Kant was born in 1724 in Königsberg, a busy Baltic port in East Prussia (now Kaliningrad, Russia). His parents were a harness-maker and his wife, both devout Pietists, members of a Lutheran movement that stressed inner faith and moral seriousness. Prussia under Frederick the Great was an absolute monarchy but relatively open to Enlightenment ideas. Kant's age was split between rationalists, who trusted pure reason, and empiricists like Hume, who traced all knowledge to experience.","Kant studied at the University of Königsberg, worked as a private tutor, and from 1755 lectured on a huge range of subjects, from logic to geography. His early work was mostly scientific; he proposed that the solar system formed from a rotating cloud of gas. He became professor of logic and metaphysics in 1770, then published almost nothing for over a decade. In 1781 the Critique of Pure Reason appeared, followed by the Groundwork (1785), the Critique of Practical Reason (1788) and the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790). He died in Königsberg in 1804.","Kant famously never travelled far from Königsberg, and he kept a very regular daily routine, including an afternoon walk. Yet he was no recluse: he was a sociable dinner host and a popular, witty lecturer. In 1794 the Prussian government ordered him to stop writing on religion, and he complied until the king's death. His late essay Toward Perpetual Peace (1795) proposed a federation of free states to end war."],"timeline":[[1724,"Born in Königsberg, Prussia"],[1755,"Begins lecturing at the University of Königsberg; publishes his theory of the solar system's formation"],[1770,"Becomes professor of logic and metaphysics"],[1781,"Publishes the Critique of Pure Reason (revised edition 1787)"],[1785,"Publishes the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals"],[1788,"Publishes the Critique of Practical Reason"],[1790,"Publishes the Critique of the Power of Judgment"],[1804,"Dies in Königsberg"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The mind shapes experience","body":"Kant argued that the mind is not a blank sheet. It organises everything we perceive through built-in forms, such as space, time and cause and effect. It is a little like wearing glasses you can never take off: everything you see is shaped by them. This is why we can know some things, such as that every event has a cause, before checking experience."},{"head":"Things as they are in themselves","body":"Because our minds shape experience, we can only know things as they appear to us. What things are like in themselves, apart from our way of experiencing them, we can never know. This sets limits to knowledge and, Kant argued, explains why arguments about God and the soul never settle anything."},{"head":"The categorical imperative","body":"Kant's test for right action: act only on a rule you could will everyone to follow. If I make a false promise to get a loan, I could not want everyone to do this, because then no one would believe promises. Morality commands us without conditions, whatever we happen to want."},{"head":"People as ends, not mere means","body":"Another version of his test: always treat people as ends in themselves, never merely as tools. You may use a shopkeeper's services, but you must not deceive or manipulate them. Every rational being has a dignity that has no price."},{"head":"Autonomy and enlightenment","body":"For Kant, being free means governing yourself by laws your own reason gives, not by impulse or other people's orders. In his essay What is Enlightenment? he urged readers to 'dare to know': to have the courage to use their own understanding."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The categorical imperative is just the Golden Rule, do as you would be done by.","truth":"Kant himself rejected this comparison. The Golden Rule depends on what you happen to want done to you, while his test asks whether a rule could consistently be willed as a law for all rational beings."},"startHere":"Read the Preface and Section One of the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals, which is short compared with his other works. For a gentler start, his essay What is Enlightenment? (1784) runs to only a few pages.","critics":"Hegel argued that the categorical imperative is an empty form that cannot tell us which actions are right without already assuming some content. Many readers find his claim that one may not lie even to a murderer asking where your friend is hiding, made in a 1797 essay, a sign that his ethics is too rigid.","legacy":"Kant's critical philosophy changed the agenda of almost all later Western philosophy. Modern human rights thinking draws heavily on his idea of human dignity, and his writings on peace influenced the League of Nations and the United Nations.","quote":{"text":"Act only on that maxim whereby thou canst at the same time will that it should become a universal law.","src":"Fundamental Principles of the Metaphysic of Morals (Groundwork), Second Section","tr":"T. K. Abbott (1873)"}},"burke":{"life":["Edmund Burke was born in Dublin, Ireland, in 1729. His father was a Protestant lawyer and his mother a Catholic, at a time when Catholics in Ireland faced harsh legal restrictions on owning land, voting and education. Burke later moved to London, the capital of a growing British empire that stretched from the American colonies to India. During his career Britain lost America, ruled huge territories through the East India Company, and watched the French Revolution overturn a monarchy.","Burke studied at Trinity College Dublin and went to London to train in law, but turned to writing instead. His Philosophical Enquiry into the Sublime and Beautiful (1757) made his name. He became private secretary to the Marquess of Rockingham, a leading Whig politician, and entered Parliament in 1765, where he served for nearly thirty years. He argued for conciliation with the American colonists, led the long impeachment of Warren Hastings, governor of Bengal, for abuses in India, and in 1790 published Reflections on the Revolution in France. He died in 1797.","Burke was a famous orator, known for long, richly argued speeches, though some colleagues found them tiring. His Speech to the Electors of Bristol (1774) argued that an MP should use his own judgement rather than simply follow voters' instructions. His opposition to the French Revolution broke his long friendship and alliance with Charles James Fox. Throughout his life he was accused by enemies of being a secret Catholic, and he campaigned for relief for Irish Catholics."],"timeline":[[1729,"Born in Dublin"],[1757,"Publishes A Philosophical Enquiry into the Sublime and Beautiful"],[1765,"Becomes secretary to Rockingham and enters Parliament"],[1774,"Elected MP for Bristol; gives his speech on the role of a representative"],[1775,"Speech on Conciliation with the American Colonies"],[1788,"Opens the impeachment trial of Warren Hastings"],[1790,"Publishes Reflections on the Revolution in France"],[1797,"Dies at Beaconsfield, England"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Society as a partnership across time","body":"Burke saw society as a partnership between the dead, the living and those yet to be born. Each generation inherits institutions, customs and wisdom it did not create, and holds them in trust for the future. So the living have no right to tear everything down to suit themselves."},{"head":"Distrust of abstract reason","body":"Burke doubted that any one generation, armed with abstract theories like the 'rights of man', could design a better society from scratch. Societies are too complex for that. Long-standing customs often contain practical wisdom we cannot easily put into words."},{"head":"Reform, not revolution","body":"Burke did not oppose all change. He argued that a state without the means of some change has no means of preserving itself. The right way is careful, gradual reform that keeps what works, like repairing an old house rather than demolishing it."},{"head":"The sublime and the beautiful","body":"In his early book on aesthetics, Burke separated two kinds of experience. The beautiful is small, smooth and pleasing; the sublime is vast, powerful and a little terrifying, like a storm at sea or a towering mountain. This distinction shaped Romantic art and Kant's aesthetics."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Burke was a reactionary who opposed all reform and sided with power.","truth":"He supported the American colonists' grievances, relief for Irish Catholics, and the prosecution of Warren Hastings for abuses in India. His argument was for cautious reform, not for keeping everything as it was."},"startHere":"Read the passage in Reflections on the Revolution in France where he describes society as a partnership between generations. It gives the heart of his view in a few pages.","critics":"Thomas Paine replied in Rights of Man (1791) that each generation must be free to govern itself and that Burke defended privilege and the dead hand of the past. Mary Wollstonecraft also answered him, accusing him of caring more about the fall of a queen than the suffering of the poor.","legacy":"Burke is often treated as the founder of modern conservatism, though liberals also claim parts of his thought. His warnings about revolutionary utopias are still cited in debates about rapid social change.","quote":{"text":"It becomes a partnership not only between those who are living, but between those who are living, those who are dead, and those who are to be born.","src":"Reflections on the Revolution in France","tr":"original English"}},"wollstonecraft":{"life":["Mary Wollstonecraft was born in Spitalfields, London, in 1759. Her family's fortunes declined because of her father's failed farming ventures and drinking. In her world, women could not go to university, vote or enter most professions, and married women's property legally belonged to their husbands. The few jobs open to respectable women, such as governess or lady's companion, were poorly paid and dependent. In the 1780s and 1790s, though, radical ideas about rights were spreading, especially after the French Revolution of 1789.","Wollstonecraft worked as a companion, set up a school at Newington Green with her sister and her friend Fanny Blood, and served as a governess in Ireland. She then moved to London and became a writer and reviewer for the radical publisher Joseph Johnson, rare for a woman then. In 1790 she answered Burke with A Vindication of the Rights of Men, and in 1792 she published A Vindication of the Rights of Woman. She went to Paris to observe the Revolution, and later wrote a popular travel book on Scandinavia. She died in 1797, days after giving birth to the future author Mary Shelley.","Wollstonecraft was largely self-educated and supported herself by her pen. She was passionate, outspoken and unafraid of scandal. In France she had a daughter, Fanny, with the American Gilbert Imlay, without marrying him. In 1797 she married the philosopher William Godwin. After her death Godwin published a frank memoir of her life, which led critics to attack her reputation for many decades."],"timeline":[[1759,"Born in Spitalfields, London"],[1784,"Opens a school at Newington Green with her sister and Fanny Blood"],[1787,"Publishes Thoughts on the Education of Daughters; begins working for Joseph Johnson"],[1790,"Publishes A Vindication of the Rights of Men, replying to Burke"],[1792,"Publishes A Vindication of the Rights of Woman; goes to Paris"],[1796,"Publishes Letters Written during a Short Residence in Sweden, Norway, and Denmark"],[1797,"Marries William Godwin; dies after the birth of her daughter Mary"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Women are rational beings","body":"Wollstonecraft argued that women have the same capacity for reason as men, and so the same claim to develop it. If women seem silly or weak, it is because they have been taught only to please and look pretty. Deny anyone an education and then blame them for ignorance, and you have made the fault yourself."},{"head":"Education for everyone","body":"She proposed national day schools where boys and girls would study together. Girls should learn real subjects and take exercise, not just manners and accomplishments. A good education would make women better citizens, friends and mothers, and give them the chance of independent work."},{"head":"Virtue needs independence","body":"Wollstonecraft held that you cannot be truly virtuous if you are entirely dependent on someone else's favour. A woman who must flatter a husband to survive learns cunning rather than goodness. Economic and legal independence is a moral matter, not only a practical one."},{"head":"Against the rule of the powerful","body":"In her reply to Burke she attacked inherited privilege, the slave trade and a system that cared more for property than for people. She argued that rights rest on reason, which all humans share, not on tradition or rank."}],"misconception":{"myth":"A Vindication of the Rights of Woman is mainly a demand for women's right to vote.","truth":"The book focuses on education, reason and independence. She raised the idea of women having political representation only briefly, expecting it to provoke laughter."},"startHere":"Read the Introduction and chapter 2 of A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, where she sets out her case and argues against Rousseau's view of how girls should be raised.","critics":"In her own time, critics such as Horace Walpole insulted her as unwomanly. Later feminists have objected that she accepted motherhood as women's main role and was harsh about ordinary women, blaming them for faults she said society caused.","legacy":"Wollstonecraft is widely regarded as a founder of feminist philosophy. Her argument that differences in ability stem from education and upbringing has become central to debates about equality.","quote":{"text":"I do not wish them to have power over men; but over themselves.","src":"A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, ch. 4","tr":"original English"}},"bentham":{"life":["Jeremy Bentham was born in London in 1748, the son of a prosperous lawyer. English law then was a tangle of old customs, harsh punishments and judge-made rules that few ordinary people could understand; many crimes, including some theft, could be punished by death. Prisons were filthy and disorderly. At the same time, Enlightenment thinkers were asking whether laws and institutions could be redesigned on rational lines.","A child prodigy, Bentham went to Queen's College, Oxford, at twelve and then trained as a lawyer. Listening to the lectures of the famous judge William Blackstone convinced him the legal system was built on muddle and fictions, and he never practised law. Instead he devoted his life to reforming it. A Fragment on Government (1776) attacked Blackstone, and An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (printed 1780, published 1789) set out his principle of utility. An inheritance let him write full time. He died in London in 1832.","Bentham worked obsessively, writing many pages a day, and much of his writing was left unpublished or was edited by followers. He coined many words still in use, including 'international' and 'codification'. He spent years and much money on the Panopticon, a prison design in which inmates could always be watched from a central tower, but the government dropped the project. At his request his body was dissected and his preserved skeleton, dressed in his clothes, is displayed at University College London."],"timeline":[[1748,"Born in London"],[1760,"Enters Queen's College, Oxford, aged twelve"],[1776,"Publishes A Fragment on Government"],[1786,"Stays with his brother in Russia (1786–1787), where he develops the Panopticon idea"],[1789,"Publishes An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation"],[1792,"Made an honorary citizen of the French Republic"],[1823,"Helps found the Westminster Review"],[1832,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The principle of utility","body":"Bentham argued that the right action or law is the one that produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number. Happiness means pleasure and the absence of pain. Everything else, from tradition to religious authority, must be judged by this one test."},{"head":"Pleasure and pain rule us","body":"Bentham thought humans are governed by two masters, pain and pleasure. They tell us both what we will do and what we ought to do. Any moral theory that ignores them, he argued, deals in sounds instead of sense."},{"head":"The felicific calculus","body":"Bentham proposed measuring pleasures and pains by features such as intensity, duration, certainty and how many people are affected. A lawmaker could then weigh the total effects of a policy. In a small way, cost–benefit analysis in modern government follows the same idea."},{"head":"Law reform and punishment","body":"Punishment is itself a pain, so it is only justified if it prevents more pain than it causes. Bentham argued for clear written laws, proportionate punishments, and an end to cruel and useless penalties. He also argued early on for decriminalising homosexuality, in essays unpublished in his lifetime."},{"head":"Animals count too","body":"Since what matters is pain and pleasure, Bentham argued that animals' suffering must count morally. The question, he wrote, is not whether they can reason or talk, but whether they can suffer. This made him an early forerunner of the animal welfare movement."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Bentham founded University College London.","truth":"He did not found it, though he supported its ideals of education open to all regardless of religion, and is often seen as its spiritual inspiration. His preserved body is kept there, which helped the legend grow."},"startHere":"Read chapter 1 of An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation, a few pages long, which states the principle of utility and answers objections. Chapter 4 explains how he proposed to measure pleasures and pains.","critics":"John Stuart Mill, his follower, argued that Bentham ignored differences in the quality of pleasures. Later critics, including John Rawls, objected that adding up everyone's happiness can justify sacrificing individuals for the total, failing to take seriously that people are separate persons with rights.","legacy":"Bentham founded classical utilitarianism, one of the main theories in modern ethics. His ideas shaped law reform, prison design, welfare economics and the animal welfare movement.","quote":{"text":"Nature has placed mankind under the governance of two sovereign masters, pain and pleasure.","src":"An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation, ch. 1","tr":"original English"}},"hegel":{"life":["Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was born in Stuttgart, in the Duchy of Württemberg, in 1770. Germany was then a patchwork of small states within the old Holy Roman Empire. When Hegel was nineteen the French Revolution broke out, and over the next decades Napoleon's armies swept away the old empire and redrew the map of Europe. Hegel's generation was also living through the aftermath of Kant's philosophy, which they felt had left reason divided against itself.","Hegel studied theology at the Tübingen seminary, sharing rooms with the poet Hölderlin and the philosopher Schelling. He worked as a private tutor in Bern and Frankfurt, then taught at Jena, where he finished the Phenomenology of Spirit in 1806 as Napoleon's army fought outside the town. After Jena he edited a newspaper in Bamberg and was head of a secondary school in Nuremberg, where he wrote the Science of Logic. He became professor at Heidelberg in 1816 and at Berlin in 1818, where he became the most influential philosopher in Germany. He died in Berlin in 1831, during a cholera epidemic.","Hegel was not a brilliant speaker: students reported halting, difficult lectures, yet they crowded to hear him. Several of his best-known works, including his lectures on history, art and religion, were put together after his death from his notes and students' notes. In a well-known letter of 1806 he described seeing Napoleon ride through Jena as seeing the 'world-soul' on horseback, which shows how he saw great events as carrying the movement of history."],"timeline":[[1770,"Born in Stuttgart"],[1788,"Enters the Tübingen seminary, where he befriends Hölderlin and Schelling"],[1801,"Begins teaching at the University of Jena"],[1807,"Publishes the Phenomenology of Spirit"],[1812,"First volume of the Science of Logic published (completed 1816)"],[1818,"Becomes professor of philosophy in Berlin"],[1820,"Publishes the Elements of the Philosophy of Right (dated 1821)"],[1831,"Dies in Berlin"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Dialectic: progress through conflict","body":"Hegel thought ideas develop by running into their own contradictions. A simple view, pushed far enough, shows its limits; the next stage keeps what was right in it while overcoming the problem. A child's rule 'always obey' breaks down when two adults give opposite orders, and a richer understanding grows from that clash."},{"head":"Reality as spirit","body":"For Hegel, reality is not dead matter set against a separate mind. It is 'spirit' (Geist): a whole that comes, through nature and human history, to know itself. Our art, religion and philosophy are the ways in which this self-understanding happens."},{"head":"History as the growth of freedom","body":"Hegel read world history as the story of freedom slowly being recognised. In ancient despotisms only one person was free, in Greece and Rome some were, and in the modern world, he claimed, all are recognised as free. History has a direction, even if it moves through wars and suffering."},{"head":"Recognition: master and slave","body":"In a famous passage of the Phenomenology, two people each seek to be recognised by the other. One becomes master and the other slave, but the master gains only the recognition of someone he does not respect, while the slave, through work, comes to shape the world. Self-consciousness, Hegel suggests, needs mutual recognition."},{"head":"Freedom lives in institutions","body":"Hegel argued that freedom is not just doing what you like. It is realised in social institutions, such as the family, civil society and the state, that give our choices meaning. A promise or a contract only exists because there is a shared world of rules."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hegel's method is 'thesis, antithesis, synthesis'.","truth":"Hegel almost never used these terms, which are associated more with Fichte and were popularised by later writers. His dialectic is a more complex movement in which each stage's own tensions lead beyond it."},"startHere":"Read the Introduction to his Lectures on the Philosophy of History, which was given as spoken lectures and is easier than his books. It sets out his view of history as the progress of freedom.","critics":"Kierkegaard objected that Hegel's grand system forgets the single, existing individual who must make choices. Marx said that with Hegel the dialectic was 'standing on its head' and must be turned right side up, by putting material conditions, not ideas, first; later Karl Popper accused him of preparing the ground for authoritarian states.","legacy":"Hegel shaped Marxism, existentialism, much of continental philosophy and the idea that history has a pattern. His account of recognition is still used in debates about identity and respect.","quote":null},"schopenhauer":{"life":["Arthur Schopenhauer was born in 1788 in Danzig (now Gdańsk, Poland), then a free trading city. His father was a wealthy merchant and his mother, Johanna, later became a successful novelist. He grew up in the years of the French Revolution and the Napoleonic Wars, and lived to see the failed revolutions of 1848. German philosophy at the time was dominated by Fichte, Schelling and above all Hegel, whose optimistic systems Schopenhauer despised.","His father wanted him to be a merchant and took him on long journeys through Europe. After his father's death in 1805 he gave up business and went to university at Göttingen and Berlin. His doctoral thesis, On the Fourfold Root of the Principle of Sufficient Reason, appeared in 1813. In Weimar he discussed colour theory with Goethe. His main work, The World as Will and Representation, was published at the end of 1818 but sold poorly. From 1833 he lived in Frankfurt, where fame came late, after his essays Parerga and Paralipomena (1851). He died there in 1860.","Schopenhauer lived on his inheritance and never needed a job. He was proud, sharp-tongued and often bitter about being ignored by professors. He kept to a fixed routine in Frankfurt, played the flute and was devoted to his poodles. He was one of the first major Western philosophers to read Indian thought seriously, and he admired the Upanishads and Buddhism, finding in them ideas close to his own."],"timeline":[[1788,"Born in Danzig"],[1813,"Publishes his doctoral thesis On the Fourfold Root of the Principle of Sufficient Reason"],[1818,"The World as Will and Representation published (dated 1819)"],[1820,"Lectures in Berlin, scheduling his course against Hegel's"],[1833,"Settles in Frankfurt am Main"],[1844,"Publishes a second, expanded edition of his main work"],[1851,"Publishes Parerga and Paralipomena, which brings him fame"],[1860,"Dies in Frankfurt"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The world as representation","body":"Following Kant, Schopenhauer held that the world we experience is a representation: it is shaped by our minds through space, time and causation. The objects we see are, in that sense, appearances to a subject. But this is only one side of the world."},{"head":"The world as will","body":"We know one thing from the inside: our own body, which we experience as wanting, striving and acting. Schopenhauer argued that this inner striving, which he called will, is what all reality is underneath appearances. Gravity, plant growth and animal hunger are all expressions of one blind, aimless will."},{"head":"Life swings between pain and boredom","body":"Because will is endless striving, we are always wanting something. When we lack it, we suffer; when we get it, satisfaction fades quickly and boredom sets in. Think of how a longed-for purchase soon becomes ordinary. Schopenhauer concluded that life holds more suffering than happiness."},{"head":"Escape through art and compassion","body":"Some relief is possible. In absorbing art, especially music, we briefly stop wanting and simply contemplate. In compassion we see that another's suffering is really our own, since all is one will, and this is the root of morality. The deepest release comes from ascetic denial of the will, as in the lives of saints."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Because he was a pessimist, Schopenhauer recommended suicide.","truth":"He argued the opposite: suicide does not deny the will to live but expresses it, since the person still wants life, only not this life. He favoured the quieting of desire instead."},"startHere":"Try his essay On the Suffering of the World from Parerga and Paralipomena, which is short and readable. If you want his system, read Book Four of The World as Will and Representation.","critics":"Nietzsche, once his admirer, came to see his pessimism and praise of denying the will as a sickness that turns against life. Others have asked how Schopenhauer could know that the thing-in-itself is will, given that he accepted Kant's claim that we cannot know things in themselves.","legacy":"Schopenhauer influenced Nietzsche, Freud's ideas about unconscious drives, Wagner's music and writers such as Tolstoy, Thomas Mann and Borges. He helped bring Indian philosophy into Western conversation.","quote":{"text":"The world is my idea","src":"The World as Will and Idea, Book I, sec. 1","tr":"R. B. Haldane and J. Kemp (1883)"}},"kierkegaard":{"life":["Søren Kierkegaard was born in Copenhagen in 1813, the youngest of seven children of a wealthy, devout and melancholy merchant. Denmark was a small Lutheran kingdom where nearly everyone belonged to the state church, and being a Christian was treated as a normal part of being a respectable citizen. In the universities, Hegel's philosophy was fashionable, promising a complete rational system that could explain everything, including faith.","Kierkegaard studied theology at the University of Copenhagen, where his dissertation On the Concept of Irony (1841) discussed Socrates. In 1840 he became engaged to Regine Olsen but broke it off in 1841, an event that marked him deeply and echoes through his books. He heard Schelling lecture in Berlin, then poured out an astonishing series of books between 1843 and 1846, including Either/Or and Fear and Trembling. After a public feud with the satirical paper The Corsair, he was mocked in the streets. His last years were spent in a fierce attack on the Danish church. He collapsed in the street in 1855 and died weeks later, aged 42.","Kierkegaard published many of his books under pseudonyms, such as Johannes de silentio and Johannes Climacus, each representing a different outlook on life. He wanted readers to wrestle with these views themselves rather than receive his opinions. He lived on his inheritance, walked the streets of Copenhagen talking to all sorts of people, and kept extensive journals that are now an important part of his work."],"timeline":[[1813,"Born in Copenhagen"],[1841,"Defends his thesis On the Concept of Irony; breaks his engagement to Regine Olsen"],[1843,"Publishes Either/Or, Fear and Trembling and Repetition"],[1844,"Publishes Philosophical Fragments and The Concept of Anxiety"],[1846,"Publishes Concluding Unscientific Postscript; mocked by The Corsair"],[1849,"Publishes The Sickness unto Death"],[1855,"Attacks the Danish state church; dies in Copenhagen"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Truth is subjectivity","body":"Kierkegaard argued that the most important truths are not facts you can learn and file away, like a date in history. They are truths you must live by and commit to. Knowing all the arguments about love is not the same as loving someone."},{"head":"Three stages of life","body":"He described three ways of living. The aesthetic life seeks pleasure and interest and avoids commitment. The ethical life takes on duties and builds a self through choices, like marriage or a career. The religious life stands alone before God in faith. Moving between them is not a matter of logic but a choice."},{"head":"Anxiety and despair","body":"Anxiety, for Kierkegaard, is the dizziness of freedom: the unsettling sense that we can choose. Despair is not just sadness but a failure to be one's true self, whether by refusing to be oneself or by desperately trying to be someone else. Even people who seem content may be in despair without knowing it."},{"head":"The leap of faith","body":"Faith cannot be reached by proofs or by a philosophical system. In Fear and Trembling he looks at Abraham, told to sacrifice his son, to show how faith goes beyond what ethics and reason can explain. Faith is a passionate, risky commitment, not a comfortable conclusion."},{"head":"Against the crowd","body":"Kierkegaard warned that in 'the crowd' or 'the public' no one takes personal responsibility. People adopt opinions because others hold them. He wanted each reader to become a single individual who thinks and chooses for themselves."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Kierkegaard was a secular existentialist who rejected religion.","truth":"He was a deeply committed Christian. His attacks on the church were aimed at comfortable, conventional Christianity, in the name of a more demanding faith."},"startHere":"Read the 'Exordium' (Attunement) and the 'Eulogy on Abraham' at the start of Fear and Trembling, which are short and gripping. They show his style and his question of what real faith demands.","critics":"Critics such as Martin Buber worried that Kierkegaard's idea of faith setting aside ordinary ethics, as in Abraham's case, could excuse terrible acts done in God's name. Others argue his focus on the inner individual neglects society and politics.","legacy":"Kierkegaard is often called the father of existentialism, influencing Heidegger, Sartre and Camus as well as Protestant theologians such as Karl Barth. His analyses of anxiety and despair also influenced psychology.","quote":null},"mill":{"life":["John Stuart Mill was born in London in 1806, the eldest son of the Scottish philosopher and historian James Mill, a close ally of Jeremy Bentham. Britain was becoming the world's first industrial nation, with growing cities, factories and a large empire. Only a small minority of men could vote, and women could not vote or, if married, own property in their own right. Reformers argued about how far democracy should go and what role the state should play.","Mill was educated at home by his father: he began Greek at three and was studying logic and economics before his teens. At seventeen he joined the East India Company, where he worked until 1858. At twenty he suffered a mental crisis, which he later described in his Autobiography, and found comfort in poetry, especially Wordsworth. In 1830 he met Harriet Taylor, whom he married in 1851; he credited her as a partner in his thinking. His major works include A System of Logic (1843), On Liberty (1859) and Utilitarianism (1861). He served as an MP from 1865 to 1868 and died in Avignon, France, in 1873.","Mill was a serious, generous thinker known for trying to understand opponents' views fairly. He wrote widely for newspapers and reviews on current issues. As an MP he proposed an amendment in 1867 to give women the vote on the same terms as men; it was defeated but won 73 votes. His Autobiography, published after his death, is one of the most revealing portraits of a philosopher's mind."],"timeline":[[1806,"Born in London"],[1826,"Suffers a mental crisis, later described in his Autobiography"],[1843,"Publishes A System of Logic"],[1859,"Publishes On Liberty"],[1861,"Utilitarianism appears in Fraser's Magazine (as a book in 1863)"],[1865,"Elected MP for Westminster"],[1869,"Publishes The Subjection of Women"],[1873,"Dies in Avignon, France"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The harm principle","body":"Mill argued that society may only restrict someone's freedom to prevent harm to others. Your own good, physical or moral, is not enough reason to force you. Adults may make choices that others think foolish, such as risky hobbies, as long as they do not harm anyone else."},{"head":"Freedom of thought and speech","body":"Silencing an opinion is wrong even if the opinion is false. If it is true, we lose the truth; if partly true, we lose part of it; if false, we lose the chance to understand why the truth is true. Beliefs never tested in argument become dead dogmas."},{"head":"Higher and lower pleasures","body":"Mill kept Bentham's aim of greatest happiness but argued that some pleasures are better in kind than others. Pleasures of the mind, like learning and friendship, count for more than mere physical ones. Those who have experienced both, he claimed, prefer the higher."},{"head":"Equality of the sexes","body":"In The Subjection of Women, Mill argued that the legal subordination of women to men was wrong and held back human progress. We cannot know women's 'nature', he said, because they have never been allowed to develop freely. He called for equal rights in marriage, education and politics."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Mill's liberalism means the government should never interfere in anyone's life.","truth":"The harm principle allows interference to prevent harm to others, and Mill supported compulsory education and some other state action. He also said it applies only to adults capable of making their own choices."},"startHere":"Read chapter 2 of On Liberty, 'Of the Liberty of Thought and Discussion', which gives his famous case for free speech. Chapter 2 of Utilitarianism is the best short statement of his ethics.","critics":"James Fitzjames Stephen, in Liberty, Equality, Fraternity (1873), argued that society has every right to enforce morality and that Mill's line between self-regarding and other-regarding acts cannot be drawn. Others argue that ranking pleasures by quality quietly abandons the idea that happiness is the only thing that matters.","legacy":"On Liberty remains the most cited defence of individual freedom and free speech in the liberal tradition. Mill's utilitarianism and his feminism both helped shape modern ethics and politics.","quote":{"text":"The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others.","src":"On Liberty, ch. 1","tr":"original English"}},"marx":{"life":["Karl Marx was born in 1818 in Trier, in the Prussian Rhineland, to a family of Jewish descent; his father converted to Lutheranism to keep his work as a lawyer. Europe was being transformed by the Industrial Revolution: factories, railways and growing cities, alongside crowded slums, child labour and long working days. Political freedom was limited, and in 1848 revolutions broke out across the continent, most of them soon crushed.","Marx studied law and philosophy at Bonn and Berlin, where he joined the Young Hegelians, radical followers of Hegel. He gained a doctorate in 1841 but, unable to get an academic job, turned to journalism; the newspaper he edited was shut down by the Prussian censors in 1843. In Paris in 1844 he began his lifelong partnership with Friedrich Engels. Expelled from several countries, the two wrote The Communist Manifesto in 1848. From 1849 Marx lived in London, where he spent years researching in the British Museum Reading Room. He published Volume I of Capital in 1867 and died in London in 1883.","Marx was often poor in London, supported by journalism, including for the New York Daily Tribune, and by money from Engels, whose family owned a cotton business. He was a formidable, often scathing polemicist. He helped lead the International Workingmen's Association from 1864. Much of his work stayed unfinished: Engels edited and published Volumes II and III of Capital after Marx's death, and important early writings appeared only in the twentieth century."],"timeline":[[1818,"Born in Trier, Prussia"],[1841,"Receives a doctorate from the University of Jena"],[1844,"Begins his partnership with Friedrich Engels in Paris; writes the Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts"],[1848,"Publishes The Communist Manifesto with Engels"],[1849,"Settles in London"],[1864,"Helps found the International Workingmen's Association"],[1867,"Publishes Capital, Volume I"],[1883,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Historical materialism","body":"Marx argued that the way a society produces its food, goods and shelter shapes everything else: its laws, politics, religion and ideas. Change the technology and organisation of work, as the steam engine did, and in time the whole society changes. Ideas matter, but they grow out of material life."},{"head":"Class struggle","body":"In every society so far, Marx claimed, one class has owned the means of production (land, factories, machines) and another has done the work. Their interests clash, and this conflict drives history: masters against slaves, lords against serfs, and now capitalists against wage-workers."},{"head":"Exploitation and surplus value","body":"Workers are paid a wage, but they produce more value than they are paid for. The difference, which Marx called surplus value, becomes the owner's profit. Even when wages are fair by market standards, Marx argued, the system rests on taking the unpaid part of workers' labour."},{"head":"Alienation","body":"Under capitalism, workers do not control what they make or how they make it. Work becomes something done only for a wage, cut off from their own purposes and from other people. A factory worker repeating one task all day may feel like a part of the machine rather than a person creating something."},{"head":"Capitalism changes the world","body":"Marx admired capitalism's power to transform production and to spread across the globe. But he argued it also produces recurring crises and creates the working class that will eventually overthrow it, leading to a classless society."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Marx designed the Soviet system and the one-party states that claimed his name.","truth":"Marx died in 1883, decades before the Russian Revolution, and wrote very little about how a communist society should be organised. How far later regimes followed or betrayed his ideas is still debated."},"startHere":"Read Part I of The Communist Manifesto, 'Bourgeois and Proletarians', which is short and vivid. It gives his view of history and his surprising praise for capitalism's achievements.","critics":"The Austrian economist Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk argued in 1896 that Marx's labour theory of value is inconsistent and cannot explain real prices. Karl Popper argued that Marxists kept reinterpreting their theory to fit any outcome, making it unfalsifiable.","legacy":"Marx's ideas inspired socialist and communist movements that shaped the twentieth century. His analysis of class, ideology and capitalism remains influential in sociology, history and economics, even among those who reject his politics.","quote":{"text":"The history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles.","src":"The Communist Manifesto, Part I","tr":"Samuel Moore (1888)"}},"nietzsche":{"life":["Friedrich Nietzsche was born in 1844 in Röcken, a village in Prussian Saxony; his father, a Lutheran pastor, died when he was four. He grew up as Germany was united under Prussia in 1871 and became a confident, industrialising empire. Science, especially Darwin's theory of evolution, was challenging traditional Christian belief, while many people still clung to Christian morality without the faith that had supported it.","After a strict education at Schulpforta, Nietzsche studied classical philology (the study of ancient languages and texts) at Bonn and Leipzig. In 1869, aged just 24, he became a professor at the University of Basel in Switzerland. His first book, The Birth of Tragedy (1872), was shaped by his friendship with the composer Richard Wagner, which later ended bitterly. Poor health forced him to resign in 1879, and for ten years he wandered between Swiss and Italian towns, writing his major works. In January 1889 he collapsed in Turin and never recovered his sanity. He died in Weimar in 1900.","Nietzsche suffered from severe headaches, eye problems and stomach trouble for much of his life. He wrote in aphorisms and short sections, with a vivid, provocative style, and often walked for hours while composing. His books sold very few copies during his sane life. After his collapse his sister Elisabeth Förster-Nietzsche controlled his papers; she edited them selectively and later promoted his work to German nationalists and the Nazis."],"timeline":[[1844,"Born in Röcken, Prussia"],[1869,"Appointed professor of classical philology at Basel"],[1872,"Publishes The Birth of Tragedy"],[1882,"Publishes The Gay Science, including the announcement that God is dead"],[1883,"First part of Thus Spoke Zarathustra published"],[1887,"Publishes On the Genealogy of Morality"],[1889,"Collapses in Turin"],[1900,"Dies in Weimar"]],"ideas":[{"head":"God is dead","body":"This was Nietzsche's diagnosis, not a celebration: belief in the Christian God had become unbelievable to modern Europe. He thought people had not grasped the consequences. If the foundation of Western values has gone, those values may collapse too, leading to nihilism, the sense that nothing matters."},{"head":"Master and slave morality","body":"In the Genealogy, Nietzsche argued that our ideas of good and evil have a history. Ancient nobles called themselves 'good' as an expression of strength. The weak, resenting them, created a 'slave morality' that praised humility and pity and called the strong 'evil'. He asked whether this morality is good for human flourishing."},{"head":"Will to power","body":"Nietzsche suggested that living things are driven less by survival or pleasure than by a drive to grow, overcome obstacles and express their strength. A musician practising for hours is not seeking comfort but mastery. He used this idea to reinterpret morality, knowledge and culture."},{"head":"The Übermensch","body":"In Thus Spoke Zarathustra, the 'overman' is a figure who creates new values instead of borrowing them from religion or the crowd. It is less a master race than an ideal of self-overcoming: becoming who you are."},{"head":"Eternal recurrence","body":"Nietzsche imagined a demon telling you that you must live your life again, exactly the same, infinitely many times. Would you curse him, or welcome it? The thought experiment tests whether you can say yes to your life as a whole."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Nietzsche was a proto-Nazi and an antisemite.","truth":"He repeatedly attacked antisemitism and German nationalism, and broke with Wagner partly over them. The Nazi link owes much to his sister, who edited his writings selectively and courted Hitler."},"startHere":"Read the Preface and First Essay of On the Genealogy of Morality, which is more continuous than his aphorism books. Then look up section 125 of The Gay Science, 'The Madman', a two-page parable about the death of God.","critics":"Many critics, including Bertrand Russell, argued that Nietzsche's contempt for compassion and equality offers no grounds for objecting to cruelty by the strong. Others reply that his targets were complacency and resentment rather than kindness itself.","legacy":"Nietzsche shaped existentialism, postmodern thought, psychology and modern literature. His questions about the origins of morality and the meaning of life without God remain widely discussed.","quote":null},"james":{"life":["William James was born in New York City in 1842 into a wealthy, intellectual family; his brother was the novelist Henry James. He grew up in a United States that was expanding fast and about to be torn apart by the Civil War (1861–1865). After the war, American universities were modernising, and science, especially Darwin's evolutionary theory, was changing how people thought about the mind and religion.","His father moved the family around Europe and the United States in search of good schooling. James first studied painting, then science and medicine at Harvard, earning a medical degree in 1869. He joined the naturalist Louis Agassiz on an expedition to Brazil. Around 1870 he went through a period of deep depression, helped, he said, by deciding to believe in free will. He began teaching at Harvard in 1872 and set up one of the first psychology laboratories in America. The Principles of Psychology (1890) took him twelve years. Later came The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902) and Pragmatism (1907). He died in New Hampshire in 1910.","James was a warm, lively writer and teacher who disliked jargon and wrote philosophy in a conversational style. He was open-minded to the point of taking psychical research seriously, which some colleagues thought unscientific. He gave many public lectures to non-specialist audiences, and several of his books began as such lectures. He developed pragmatism partly from ideas of his friend Charles Sanders Peirce, whom he credited."],"timeline":[[1842,"Born in New York City"],[1869,"Receives his medical degree from Harvard"],[1872,"Begins teaching at Harvard"],[1890,"Publishes The Principles of Psychology"],[1897,"Publishes The Will to Believe and Other Essays"],[1902,"Publishes The Varieties of Religious Experience, from his Gifford Lectures in Edinburgh"],[1907,"Publishes Pragmatism"],[1910,"Dies in Chocorua, New Hampshire"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Pragmatism","body":"To settle what an idea means, James asked what practical difference it would make if it were true. If two theories lead to exactly the same experiences and actions, the dispute between them is empty. Philosophy should be judged by its consequences in real life."},{"head":"Truth as what works out","body":"James argued that a true idea is one that we can verify and that guides us successfully through experience over time. Truth is not a fixed copy of reality but something that 'happens to' an idea as it proves itself. A map is true if following it gets you where you meant to go."},{"head":"The stream of consciousness","body":"In psychology James described consciousness not as a chain of separate pieces but as a continuously flowing stream. Thoughts blend, fade and carry fringes of other thoughts. The phrase was later borrowed by novelists such as Virginia Woolf and James Joyce."},{"head":"The will to believe","body":"When a question cannot be settled by evidence, yet we must choose, and the choice is important, James argued we have the right to believe on the basis of our passions. Waiting for proof is itself a choice. He applied this to religious faith and to trusting other people."},{"head":"Religious experience","body":"In The Varieties, James studied the experiences of mystics, converts and saints with sympathy. He judged religion by its fruits in people's lives rather than by its doctrines. Without claiming to prove God exists, he took such experiences seriously as evidence about human nature."}],"misconception":{"myth":"James's pragmatism says that whatever is useful or comforting to believe is true.","truth":"James held that true beliefs must also agree with reality and stand up to further experience over time. Critics read him crudely, but he insisted wishful thinking is not truth."},"startHere":"Read Lecture II of Pragmatism, 'What Pragmatism Means', which opens with a lively story about a squirrel on a tree. It shows his method at work in a few pages.","critics":"Bertrand Russell and G. E. Moore argued that James confused truth with usefulness, since a belief can be useful and false or true and useless. Others worried that The Will to Believe licenses believing whatever we wish.","legacy":"James helped found academic psychology in America and, with Peirce and Dewey, pragmatism, the most distinctively American philosophy. His work on consciousness and religion is still widely read.","quote":{"text":"'The true,' to put it very briefly, is only the expedient in the way of our thinking, just as 'the right' is only the expedient in the way of our behaving.","src":"Pragmatism, Lecture VI","tr":"original English"}},"freud":{"life":["Sigmund Freud was born in 1856 in Freiberg, Moravia (now Příbor in the Czech Republic), to a Jewish family that moved to Vienna when he was a small child. Vienna was the capital of the Austro-Hungarian Empire and a centre of medicine, music and science, but it was also a place of strict middle-class manners, especially about sex, and of rising antisemitism. Doctors were beginning to study 'nervous' illnesses, such as hysteria, that had no obvious physical cause.","Freud trained as a doctor at the University of Vienna and did research on the nervous system. In 1885–1886 he studied in Paris with the neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot, who used hypnosis on hysterical patients. Back in Vienna he worked with Josef Breuer, and together they published Studies on Hysteria (1895). Freud then developed his own method, psychoanalysis, and published The Interpretation of Dreams at the end of 1899. A movement grew around him, though key followers such as Carl Jung later broke away. He continued to revise his theories for decades. After the Nazis took over Austria in 1938 he fled to London, where he died in 1939.","Freud worked long hours seeing patients and wrote prolifically, in a clear and often literary German style; he received the Goethe Prize for literature in 1930. He used his own dreams and memories as evidence, analysing himself in The Interpretation of Dreams. From 1923 he suffered from cancer of the jaw and underwent many operations, yet kept working. In later life he turned increasingly to culture and religion, in books such as The Future of an Illusion and Civilization and Its Discontents."],"timeline":[[1856,"Born in Freiberg, Moravia"],[1885,"Studies with Charcot in Paris (1885–1886)"],[1895,"Publishes Studies on Hysteria with Josef Breuer"],[1899,"The Interpretation of Dreams published (dated 1900)"],[1909,"Lectures at Clark University in the United States"],[1923,"Publishes The Ego and the Id; diagnosed with cancer of the jaw"],[1930,"Publishes Civilization and Its Discontents"],[1939,"Dies in London after fleeing Nazi-ruled Vienna in 1938"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The unconscious mind","body":"Freud argued that much of our mental life goes on outside awareness. Wishes, fears and memories we cannot face are pushed out of consciousness, a process he called repression, but they still shape what we do. A slip of the tongue that says what we did not mean to say can reveal them."},{"head":"Id, ego and superego","body":"Freud divided the mind into three parts. The id holds basic drives and wants instant satisfaction. The superego is our internalised conscience, learned from parents and society. The ego tries to balance them against the demands of reality, like a manager caught between a demanding boss and an impatient customer."},{"head":"Dreams as disguised wishes","body":"Freud claimed that dreams express hidden wishes in disguised form, so that sleep is not disturbed. By talking through what a dream reminds them of, a patient can uncover its hidden meaning. He called dream interpretation the royal road to the unconscious."},{"head":"Religion as an illusion","body":"In The Future of an Illusion, Freud argued that religious belief grows from a childlike wish for a powerful, protecting father. Faced with a frightening world, people imagine a God who watches over them. He called it an illusion, meaning a belief driven by wishes, and hoped science would replace it."},{"head":"Civilisation and its costs","body":"Civilisation requires us to restrain our aggressive and sexual drives, Freud argued. This makes social life possible but leaves people with guilt and discontent. Progress, on this view, always comes with a psychological price."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Freud said 'Sometimes a cigar is just a cigar.'","truth":"No reliable source shows that Freud ever said this; it appears only in stories told long after his death."},"startHere":"Read the first few of his Introductory Lectures on Psycho-Analysis on slips of the tongue and everyday errors. They were given to a general audience and show his method on familiar examples.","critics":"Karl Popper argued that psychoanalysis is unfalsifiable: any behaviour can be explained after the event, so no evidence could ever count against it. Adolf Grünbaum and others argued that the clinical evidence Freud relied on cannot support his theories, since patients may be influenced by the analyst's suggestions.","legacy":"Many of Freud's specific theories are disputed or rejected by modern psychology, but his ideas of unconscious motives, defence mechanisms and the talking cure changed how people think about the mind. His influence on literature, art and everyday language is enormous.","quote":null},"einstein":{"life":["Albert Einstein was born in 1879 in Ulm, in the German Empire, to a secular Jewish family; he grew up mainly in Munich. Physics then seemed nearly complete: Newton's mechanics described motion and Maxwell's equations described light and electricity. But a few stubborn puzzles remained, especially about light and the behaviour of atoms. His life spanned two world wars, the rise of Nazism and the beginning of the nuclear age.","Einstein left school in Munich, finished his schooling in Switzerland, and studied at the Federal Polytechnic in Zurich. Unable to find an academic post, he worked from 1902 at the patent office in Bern. In 1905 he published papers on the photoelectric effect, Brownian motion and special relativity, including the equation E = mc². He then held professorships in Zurich, Prague and Berlin, where he completed general relativity in 1915. Its confirmation by eclipse observations in 1919 made him world-famous, and he received the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics. In 1933, after the Nazis came to power, he moved to Princeton, New Jersey, where he died in 1955.","Einstein worked largely through thought experiments, such as imagining riding alongside a beam of light. He engaged deeply with philosophy, reading Hume, Mach and Spinoza, and debated for decades with Niels Bohr about whether quantum mechanics gives a complete picture of reality. He was also a public figure: a pacifist for much of his life, a supporter of civil rights, and a signer, with Bertrand Russell, of a 1955 manifesto warning against nuclear war."],"timeline":[[1879,"Born in Ulm, Germany"],[1902,"Begins work at the Swiss patent office in Bern"],[1905,"Publishes papers on light quanta, Brownian motion and special relativity"],[1915,"Completes the general theory of relativity"],[1919,"Eclipse observations confirm the bending of starlight, making him famous"],[1922,"Receives the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics"],[1933,"Moves to the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton"],[1955,"Dies in Princeton"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Relativity of simultaneity","body":"Special relativity showed that whether two distant events happen 'at the same time' depends on how the observer is moving. There is no single universal 'now' that all observers agree on. This was a scientific result with deep philosophical consequences for how we think about time."},{"head":"The block universe","body":"Many philosophers and physicists have taken relativity to suggest that past, present and future are all equally real, like places on a map, a view called the block universe. In a 1955 letter on the death of his friend Michele Besso, Einstein wrote that for believing physicists the distinction between past, present and future is only a stubbornly persistent illusion."},{"head":"Spacetime and gravity","body":"General relativity treats gravity not as a force pulling at a distance but as the curving of spacetime by mass and energy. A planet orbits the Sun because it follows the straightest possible path through curved spacetime. This changed how we picture space and time themselves."},{"head":"Spinoza's God","body":"Einstein rejected a personal God who answers prayers or judges people. He said he believed in Spinoza's God, revealed in the lawful harmony of the universe. He described a kind of 'cosmic religious feeling' of awe at the order of nature."},{"head":"Reality beyond observation","body":"Einstein believed physics should describe an independent reality, not just predict measurements. He objected that quantum mechanics, which deals in probabilities, must be incomplete. His 1935 paper with Podolsky and Rosen set out the challenge."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Einstein failed mathematics at school.","truth":"He excelled at mathematics from an early age, mastering calculus as a teenager. The myth may come from his failing the general part of a university entrance exam at sixteen, while doing very well in maths and physics."},"startHere":"Read his essay 'The World As I See It', a few pages long, for his outlook on life, science and religion. For relativity, his own popular book Relativity: The Special and General Theory (1916) explains the ideas for non-specialists.","critics":"Niels Bohr argued against Einstein that quantum mechanics is complete, and later experiments testing John Bell's theorem have gone against the kind of local hidden reality Einstein hoped for. Philosophers who think the passage of time is real reject the block-universe reading of relativity.","legacy":"Relativity transformed physics, from GPS satellites to cosmology, and forced philosophers to rethink space, time and causation. Einstein remains the public image of scientific genius.","quote":null},"du_bois":{"life":["William Edward Burghardt Du Bois was born in 1868 in Great Barrington, Massachusetts, three years after the end of the American Civil War and the abolition of slavery. During Reconstruction, Black Americans briefly gained political rights in the South, but by the 1890s these were being stripped away through segregation laws, violence and lynching. Du Bois's long life ran from Reconstruction to the eve of the March on Washington in 1963.","Du Bois studied at Fisk University, a Black college in Tennessee, then at Harvard and the University of Berlin. In 1895 he became the first African American to earn a PhD from Harvard. His study The Philadelphia Negro (1899) was a pioneering work of sociology. Teaching at Atlanta University, he published The Souls of Black Folk (1903), which challenged Booker T. Washington's strategy of accepting segregation. He helped found the NAACP in 1909 and edited its magazine, The Crisis, from 1910 to 1934. Black Reconstruction appeared in 1935. In 1961 he moved to Ghana, where he died in 1963.","Du Bois was a scholar, editor, novelist, poet and activist, and wrote in many styles, from statistics to lyrical prose. He organised several Pan-African Congresses linking struggles in Africa and the Americas. His politics moved steadily left over his life. In 1951 he was indicted as an unregistered foreign agent for his peace activism and acquitted. He joined the Communist Party in 1961, shortly before leaving for Ghana to work on an Encyclopedia Africana."],"timeline":[[1868,"Born in Great Barrington, Massachusetts"],[1895,"Becomes the first African American to receive a Harvard PhD"],[1899,"Publishes The Philadelphia Negro"],[1903,"Publishes The Souls of Black Folk"],[1909,"Helps found the NAACP; edits The Crisis from 1910"],[1935,"Publishes Black Reconstruction in America"],[1961,"Moves to Ghana"],[1963,"Dies in Accra, Ghana"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Double consciousness","body":"Du Bois described the experience of Black Americans as a 'double consciousness': always seeing oneself through the eyes of a society that looks on with contempt, while also seeing oneself as one really is. One feels two-ness, being both American and Black in a country that treats these as at odds. The idea now applies to many people who live between identities."},{"head":"The veil and the color line","body":"Du Bois used the image of a veil to describe the barrier that separates Black and white Americans, hiding each from the other's full humanity. He predicted that the problem of the twentieth century would be the color line, worldwide as well as in America."},{"head":"Rights, not accommodation","body":"Booker T. Washington urged Black Americans to focus on vocational training and economic progress while accepting segregation for the time being. Du Bois argued this conceded too much: political rights, civil equality and higher education were essential and should be demanded now."},{"head":"Rewriting history","body":"In Black Reconstruction, Du Bois challenged the then-standard view that Reconstruction failed because Black people were unfit for politics. He showed freed people as active participants who built schools and democratic governments, and argued the period was overthrown by white elites and racist violence."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read chapter 1 of The Souls of Black Folk, 'Of Our Spiritual Strivings', which introduces double consciousness and the veil in a few powerful pages.","critics":"Booker T. Washington and his supporters argued that Du Bois's demands were impractical in the South of his day. Others criticised his early 'Talented Tenth' idea, that an educated Black elite should lead, as elitist, and Du Bois himself later revised it.","legacy":"Du Bois helped found the scientific study of race in sociology and shaped the civil rights and Pan-African movements. Double consciousness remains a key idea in philosophy of race and studies of identity.","quote":null},"ambedkar":{"life":["Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar was born in 1891 in Mhow, in central India, then under British rule. His family belonged to the Mahar caste, treated as 'untouchable' under the caste system. Untouchables, now often called Dalits, were barred from many temples, wells and schools, and were confined to jobs others thought polluting. At school Ambedkar had to sit apart from other pupils. India was also beginning its long struggle for independence from Britain.","With the help of scholarships, Ambedkar studied at Elphinstone College in Bombay, then at Columbia University in New York and the London School of Economics, earning doctorates in economics, and qualified as a barrister. Back in India he led campaigns against untouchability, such as the 1927 Mahad satyagraha to claim the right to draw water from a public tank. He clashed with Gandhi over separate electorates, ending in the Poona Pact of 1932. After independence he became India's first law minister and chaired the committee that drafted the Constitution. In 1956 he converted to Buddhism with hundreds of thousands of followers, and died weeks later.","Ambedkar was one of the most highly educated Indians of his time and a voracious reader who built a large personal library. He was a lawyer, economist, journalist and politician as well as a philosopher. He founded newspapers and political parties to give Dalits a voice. In 1935 he declared that although he had been born a Hindu, he would not die one, and he spent years studying religions before choosing Buddhism."],"timeline":[[1891,"Born in Mhow, central India"],[1913,"Begins graduate study at Columbia University, New York"],[1927,"Leads the Mahad satyagraha for access to public water"],[1932,"Signs the Poona Pact with Gandhi"],[1936,"Publishes Annihilation of Caste"],[1947,"Becomes India's first law minister and chairs the Constitution's drafting committee"],[1949,"The Constitution of India is adopted"],[1956,"Converts to Buddhism in Nagpur; dies in Delhi"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Caste must be annihilated","body":"Ambedkar argued that caste is not just a division of labour but a division of labourers, ranked one above another and fixed by birth. It cannot be reformed from within because it rests on religious texts that people treat as sacred. Real change requires rejecting the authority of those texts."},{"head":"Democracy as a way of life","body":"For Ambedkar, democracy is more than voting. It is a form of social life built on liberty, equality and fraternity, a sense of shared belonging among citizens. Political democracy cannot last, he warned, if society beneath it stays deeply unequal."},{"head":"Constitutional protection","body":"Ambedkar believed law and constitutions could protect vulnerable groups. The Indian Constitution abolished untouchability and allowed reserved places in education, jobs and legislatures for historically oppressed groups. He saw these as tools for turning formal equality into real equality."},{"head":"Buddhism as liberation","body":"Ambedkar reinterpreted Buddhism as a rational, ethical and social teaching focused on equality and the end of suffering in this world. In The Buddha and His Dhamma he presented the Buddha as a social reformer. His conversion gave Dalits a new religious identity outside caste."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Ambedkar single-handedly wrote the Indian Constitution.","truth":"He chaired the drafting committee and was its leading figure and defender in the Constituent Assembly, but the Constitution was a collective effort. Ambedkar himself credited others, including the constitutional adviser B. N. Rau."},"startHere":"Read Annihilation of Caste, a speech he wrote in 1936 but was not allowed to deliver. It is short, forceful and clearly argued.","critics":"Gandhi replied to Annihilation of Caste, arguing that the ancient ideal of varna (social roles) was different from the abuses of caste and that Hinduism could be reformed from within. Ambedkar answered that the scriptures themselves sanctioned caste.","legacy":"Ambedkar is revered as the chief architect of the Indian Constitution and the leading champion of Dalit rights. His critique of caste and his idea of democracy as social fraternity remain central to debates in India and beyond.","quote":null},"russell":{"life":["Bertrand Russell was born in 1872 in Wales into a famous aristocratic family; his grandfather, Lord John Russell, had twice been Prime Minister. Orphaned young, he was raised by his strict grandmother. He grew up as mathematics was being questioned at its foundations and as Britain stood at the height of its empire. His long life ran from Victorian Britain to the space age, through two world wars and the nuclear arms race.","Russell studied mathematics and philosophy at Trinity College, Cambridge. With his former teacher Alfred North Whitehead he wrote Principia Mathematica (1910–1913), an attempt to show that mathematics can be derived from logic. His article On Denoting (1905) became a model of analytic philosophy. He taught and encouraged the young Wittgenstein. His opposition to the First World War cost him his Cambridge post in 1916 and led to six months in prison in 1918. He then wrote widely on politics, education, religion and morals, published A History of Western Philosophy (1945), and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1950. He died in Wales in 1970.","Russell was a clear, witty writer who could explain hard ideas to general readers, and much of his income came from books and lectures. He was outspoken and often controversial: an appointment to teach in New York in 1940 was cancelled after protests about his views on sex and marriage. In later life he campaigned against nuclear weapons, co-signed the Russell–Einstein Manifesto (1955), and was briefly jailed again in 1961, aged 89, after a protest."],"timeline":[[1872,"Born in Trellech, Monmouthshire, Wales"],[1905,"Publishes On Denoting"],[1910,"First volume of Principia Mathematica published (with Whitehead, completed 1913)"],[1912,"Publishes The Problems of Philosophy"],[1918,"Imprisoned for six months for anti-war writing"],[1945,"Publishes A History of Western Philosophy"],[1950,"Awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature"],[1970,"Dies in Penrhyndeudraeth, Wales"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Logic as the basis of mathematics","body":"Russell tried to show that all of mathematics could be derived from a small set of logical principles. This programme, called logicism, did not fully succeed, but it led to modern logic and influenced the development of computer science."},{"head":"Russell's paradox","body":"Russell found a contradiction at the heart of early set theory. Consider the set of all sets that are not members of themselves: is it a member of itself? If yes, then no; if no, then yes. The discovery forced mathematicians to rebuild the foundations of their subject."},{"head":"The theory of descriptions","body":"What does 'The present King of France is bald' mean, when there is no King of France? Russell argued that the sentence secretly says: there is exactly one King of France, and he is bald. Since the first part is false, the whole sentence is false. Analysing language this way could dissolve many philosophical puzzles."},{"head":"Knowledge by acquaintance","body":"Russell distinguished knowing something directly, as you know a colour you are seeing, from knowing about something through descriptions, as you know about a historical figure. Most of our knowledge, he argued, is of the second kind, built up from what we are directly acquainted with."},{"head":"The value of doubt","body":"Russell argued that philosophy's value lies partly in its uncertainty. It frees us from the tyranny of custom and widens our sense of what is possible. He urged people to proportion their beliefs to the evidence."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Problems of Philosophy, especially chapter 1 ('Appearance and Reality') and the final chapter ('The Value of Philosophy'). It was written as a short introduction for general readers and is still one of the best.","critics":"P. F. Strawson, in 'On Referring' (1950), argued that Russell's theory of descriptions misreads ordinary language: a sentence about the King of France is not false but simply fails to say anything true or false. Kurt Gödel's incompleteness theorems (1931) showed that no single formal system could capture all mathematical truths, limiting Russell's logicist programme.","legacy":"Russell, with Frege, Moore and Wittgenstein, founded analytic philosophy, the dominant style in English-speaking universities. His popular writing and activism made him one of the best-known public intellectuals of the twentieth century.","quote":null},"wittgenstein":{"life":["Ludwig Wittgenstein was born in Vienna in 1889, the youngest of eight children of one of the richest industrial families in the Austro-Hungarian Empire. Their home was a centre of Viennese culture; Brahms and Mahler were among the musicians who visited. Vienna was a city of intense intellectual life, and also of tension, decline and, from 1914, war that would end the empire.","He studied engineering in Berlin and aeronautics in Manchester, became interested in the foundations of mathematics, and in 1911 went to Cambridge to study with Bertrand Russell. During the First World War he served in the Austrian army and finished the Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus, published in 1921. Believing he had solved philosophy's problems, he gave away his inheritance and became a primary schoolteacher in rural Austria. He returned to Cambridge in 1929, became professor there in 1939, and worked as a hospital porter during the Second World War. He resigned in 1947 and died in Cambridge in 1951. His Philosophical Investigations appeared in 1953.","Wittgenstein was intense, demanding and often tormented by doubts about his work and himself. He taught without notes, thinking aloud in front of small groups of students, and could be severe with people he thought were not serious. He disliked academic philosophy and urged several students to take up practical jobs. Apart from the Tractatus, he published almost nothing in his lifetime; most of his work comes from notebooks and manuscripts edited after his death."],"timeline":[[1889,"Born in Vienna"],[1911,"Goes to Cambridge to study with Bertrand Russell"],[1920,"Begins working as a primary schoolteacher in rural Austria (until 1926)"],[1921,"Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus published (English edition 1922)"],[1929,"Returns to Cambridge"],[1939,"Becomes professor of philosophy at Cambridge"],[1951,"Dies in Cambridge"],[1953,"Philosophical Investigations published after his death"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Language pictures the world","body":"In the Tractatus, Wittgenstein argued that a meaningful sentence works like a picture: its parts stand for things, and their arrangement shows a possible arrangement of things in the world. A sentence is true if the world matches the picture."},{"head":"What cannot be said","body":"On this view, ethics, the meaning of life and the nature of logic itself cannot be put into meaningful sentences. They can only be shown. The Tractatus ends by saying that what we cannot speak about we must pass over in silence."},{"head":"Meaning is use","body":"In his later work Wittgenstein abandoned the picture theory. He argued that the meaning of a word is usually its use in the language. 'Water!' can be an order, a warning or an answer, depending on what we are doing with it."},{"head":"Language-games and family resemblance","body":"Language is made of many 'language-games', activities like giving orders, telling jokes or reporting facts, each with its own rules. Words like 'game' have no single shared essence; board games, ball games and card games overlap in a network of similarities, like members of a family."},{"head":"Philosophy as therapy","body":"The later Wittgenstein thought many philosophical problems arise when language 'goes on holiday', when words are taken out of their everyday use. The philosopher's job is to remind us how words actually work, so the puzzle dissolves rather than gets solved."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The closing line of the Tractatus means that ethics and religion are worthless nonsense.","truth":"Wittgenstein told a publisher that the book's point was ethical and that the unwritten part was the important one. He thought such matters could not be stated, not that they did not matter."},"startHere":"Read sections 1 to 43 of Philosophical Investigations, which open with a scene of builders using simple words and quickly lead to his idea of meaning as use. They are short numbered remarks, so take them slowly.","critics":"Russell came to think the later Wittgenstein had given up serious thinking and made philosophy a trivial study of how words are used. Others, from Ernest Gellner to defenders of traditional metaphysics, argued that dissolving problems by appeal to ordinary language avoids the real questions.","legacy":"Wittgenstein shaped two movements: logical positivism drew on the Tractatus, and ordinary language philosophy on the Investigations. His ideas on meaning, rules and the mind remain central in philosophy of language.","quote":null},"heidegger":{"life":["Martin Heidegger was born in 1889 in Messkirch, a small Catholic town in Baden, southern Germany; his father was sexton of the local church. He grew up in a rural, religious world just as Germany was rapidly industrialising. He lived through the First World War, the unstable Weimar Republic, the Nazi dictatorship and the division of Germany after 1945. Many thinkers of his generation felt that modern technical civilisation had lost touch with something essential.","Heidegger first trained for the Catholic priesthood, then turned to philosophy at the University of Freiburg. He became assistant to Edmund Husserl, founder of phenomenology, the careful description of experience. While teaching at Marburg he published Being and Time (1927), which made him famous. In 1928 he succeeded Husserl at Freiburg. In 1933 he joined the Nazi Party and became rector of the university; he resigned the rectorship in 1934 but remained a party member until 1945. After the war he was banned from teaching until 1951. He kept writing and lecturing until his death in Freiburg in 1976; he was buried in Messkirch.","Heidegger was a charismatic teacher whose lectures drew students such as Hannah Arendt, Hans-Georg Gadamer and Herbert Marcuse. He often wrote in a difficult, invented vocabulary, reshaping German words to avoid what he saw as the misleading assumptions of ordinary philosophical language. He did much of his writing at a small hut in the Black Forest. His private notebooks, published from 2014, contain antisemitic passages that have intensified debate about his politics."],"timeline":[[1889,"Born in Messkirch, Baden, Germany"],[1923,"Becomes professor at the University of Marburg"],[1927,"Publishes Being and Time"],[1928,"Succeeds Husserl as professor at Freiburg"],[1933,"Joins the Nazi Party and becomes rector of Freiburg University"],[1934,"Resigns as rector"],[1954,"Publishes The Question Concerning Technology"],[1976,"Dies in Freiburg; buried in his home town of Messkirch"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The question of Being","body":"Heidegger asked what it means for anything to be at all. Philosophers, he argued, had studied particular beings, such as God, matter or minds, but had forgotten to ask about Being itself. His whole career was an attempt to reopen that question."},{"head":"Being-in-the-world","body":"Humans, whom he called Dasein ('being-there'), are not minds looking out at a separate world. We are always already involved in it, caring about things and using them. A carpenter hammering does not notice the hammer as an object; it only stands out as a thing when it breaks."},{"head":"Authenticity and 'the They'","body":"Most of the time we live as 'one' does: we think, enjoy and judge as people generally do. Heidegger called this anonymous crowd 'das Man', often translated 'the They'. Living authentically means owning one's choices rather than drifting along with the crowd."},{"head":"Being-towards-death","body":"Facing the certainty of our own death, which no one else can die for us, can pull us out of everyday distraction. It reveals our life as our own and finite. For Heidegger, anxiety about death is not just fear but a clue to what it is to exist."},{"head":"Technology as a way of seeing","body":"In his later work, Heidegger argued that modern technology is not just a set of tools but a way of revealing the world. It shows everything, rivers, forests and even people, as resources on standby to be used. The danger is that we forget any other way of relating to things."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his lecture The Question Concerning Technology, which is shorter and more concrete than Being and Time. Then try the Introduction to Being and Time, where he explains why the question of Being matters.","critics":"Rudolf Carnap, in a 1932 essay, held up Heidegger's phrases about 'the Nothing' as examples of meaningless pseudo-statements. Theodor Adorno attacked his language of authenticity as empty jargon, and many critics argue his philosophy cannot be separated from his support for Nazism.","legacy":"Heidegger shaped existentialism, hermeneutics, deconstruction and environmental philosophy, and influenced Sartre, Arendt, Gadamer and Derrida. His involvement with Nazism remains one of the most debated cases of a philosopher's politics.","quote":null},"sartre":{"life":["Jean-Paul Sartre was born in Paris in 1905 and lived through the two world wars, the German occupation of France and the long struggles over colonialism that followed. In the cafés and universities of mid-century Paris, philosophy was not a quiet academic subject: it was argued over in newspapers, plays and political meetings. Sartre became the best-known face of existentialism, a movement asking what it means to exist as a free individual in a world without built-in meaning.","After studying at the École Normale Supérieure, he taught philosophy in schools and spent 1933–34 in Berlin studying the phenomenology of Edmund Husserl, a method of describing experience exactly as it appears. His novel Nausea appeared in 1938. Drafted in 1939, he was taken prisoner in 1940 and released in 1941. Being and Nothingness followed in 1943, and after the war he co-founded the journal Les Temps modernes. In later decades he moved towards Marxism, writing the Critique of Dialectical Reason (1960).","Sartre wrote in almost every form: philosophy, novels, plays, biographies, essays and journalism. He was a public intellectual who took sides on the burning issues of his day, often controversially, including support for Algerian independence. In 1964 he was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature and refused it, saying a writer should not let himself be turned into an institution. His lifelong intellectual partnership with Simone de Beauvoir shaped the work of both."],"timeline":[[1905,"Born in Paris"],[1929,"Comes first in the national philosophy teaching exam (agrégation)"],[1938,"Publishes the novel Nausea"],[1940,"Captured by German forces; released the following year"],[1943,"Publishes Being and Nothingness"],[1945,"Co-founds Les Temps modernes and gives the lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism"],[1964,"Declines the Nobel Prize in Literature"],[1980,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Existence comes before essence","body":"A paper-knife is designed for a purpose before it is made. Sartre argued humans are the opposite: we exist first, and only afterwards define ourselves through what we do. There is no fixed human nature that tells you who you must be."},{"head":"Condemned to be free","body":"Because nothing fixes our nature, we cannot escape choosing. Even refusing to choose is a choice. This freedom comes with full responsibility: you cannot blame your character, upbringing or circumstances for what you decide to do with them."},{"head":"Bad faith","body":"Bad faith is lying to yourself about your own freedom. Sartre's example is a waiter who acts out the role of 'waiter' so completely that he pretends he is nothing but that role. Saying 'I had no choice' when you did is a typical case."},{"head":"Anguish and abandonment","body":"Realising that no God or fixed rule makes our choices for us produces anguish, a kind of dizziness before our own freedom. Sartre thought we should face this feeling honestly rather than hide from it in excuses."},{"head":"Freedom in a situation","body":"In his later work Sartre paid more attention to how poverty, class and history limit what people can actually do. He tried to combine existentialist freedom with a Marxist account of society, though readers disagree about how well this worked."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Existentialism is a gloomy philosophy of despair that says nothing matters.","truth":"Sartre argued the reverse in Existentialism Is a Humanism: because nothing is decided for us, our actions create meaning and value, which makes his view a call to action rather than to despair."},"startHere":"Read the short lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism (1946). It sets out his main ideas in plain language, with memorable examples such as the student torn between joining the Resistance and caring for his mother.","critics":"Maurice Merleau-Ponty argued that Sartre exaggerated how free we are, ignoring how the body, habits and social world shape our choices before we make them. Others, including Beauvoir in her own way, pushed him to take oppression more seriously.","legacy":"Sartre made 'existentialism' a household word and influenced literature, theatre, psychology and political thought. His ideas about authenticity and responsibility still shape how many people think about living an honest life.","quote":null},"beauvoir":{"life":["Simone de Beauvoir was born in Paris in 1908 into a respectable Catholic family whose fortunes had declined. Few French women then received a full university education, and women in France could not vote until 1944. She grew up in a society with firm expectations about what a woman's life should be, and much of her work examines how those expectations are formed and how they limit people.","In 1929 she came second in the national philosophy teaching exam, just behind Sartre, and became one of the youngest people ever to pass it. She taught in schools until 1943, the year her first novel, She Came to Stay, appeared. She then turned to philosophy with Pyrrhus et Cinéas (1944) and The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947). The Second Sex (1949) caused a scandal and became a founding text of modern feminism. Her novel The Mandarins won the Prix Goncourt in 1954.","Beauvoir worked across philosophy, fiction, essays and a long multi-volume autobiography, using each to explore the same questions about freedom and other people. She co-founded the journal Les Temps modernes and was politically active, later supporting campaigns for women's rights in France. Late in life she wrote Old Age (1970), applying the same careful analysis to how society treats the elderly."],"timeline":[[1908,"Born in Paris"],[1929,"Passes the agrégation in philosophy, ranked second"],[1943,"Publishes her first novel, She Came to Stay"],[1947,"Publishes The Ethics of Ambiguity"],[1949,"Publishes The Second Sex"],[1954,"Wins the Prix Goncourt for The Mandarins"],[1970,"Publishes Old Age"],[1986,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Becoming a woman","body":"Beauvoir argued that what society calls 'femininity' is not simply given by biology but is learned through upbringing, expectations and treatment. Girls are taught, from childhood, to see themselves in certain ways. This is the point of her famous line that one becomes a woman rather than being born one."},{"head":"Woman as the Other","body":"In many cultures, she observed, man is treated as the normal human being and woman is defined in relation to him, as 'the Other'. Think of how a 'doctor' is often assumed to be a man unless someone says 'woman doctor'. This makes women's freedom harder to exercise."},{"head":"The ethics of ambiguity","body":"Humans are both free minds and bodies stuck in particular situations. Beauvoir called this condition ambiguous. She argued we should not run from it by pretending to be mere things or pure spirits, but accept it and act."},{"head":"My freedom needs yours","body":"For Beauvoir, to will yourself free is also to will others free. A freedom that depends on keeping other people down is not true freedom. This makes her existentialism more social than Sartre's early work."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Beauvoir was mainly Sartre's companion and follower, applying his ideas.","truth":"She was an independent philosopher with her own views on ethics, the body and oppression, and many scholars now argue she shaped Sartre's thinking as much as he shaped hers."},"startHere":"Read the Introduction to The Second Sex. In a few pages it sets out the question 'What is a woman?' and the idea of woman as the Other, which the rest of the book develops.","critics":"Later feminists objected that The Second Sex treats the experience of educated, white, European women as if it were women's experience in general. Some also found its descriptions of the female body unfairly negative.","legacy":"The Second Sex helped launch the second wave of feminism and the now-common distinction between biological sex and socially shaped gender. Her ethical writings are increasingly studied as philosophy in their own right.","quote":null},"camus":{"life":["Albert Camus was born in 1913 in Mondovi, in French-ruled Algeria, into a poor family of European settlers. His father was killed in the First World War when Camus was a baby, and he grew up in a working-class district of Algiers with his mother, who could barely read or hear. The sun, the sea and the poverty of Algeria shaped his writing, as did the violent conflicts of the twentieth century.","A scholarship took him to the University of Algiers, where he studied philosophy, though tuberculosis cut short his academic plans. He worked as a journalist and theatre maker, then moved to France. In 1942 he published The Stranger and The Myth of Sisyphus. During the German occupation he edited the Resistance newspaper Combat. The Plague (1947) and The Rebel (1951) followed. The Rebel led to a public break with Sartre. Camus won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1957.","Camus thought of himself more as a writer and artist than a professional philosopher. He set out his ideas in essays, novels and plays, often in clear, short sentences. He was a moralist who distrusted grand political systems that justified killing in the name of a future ideal. He died in a car accident in France in January 1960, aged 46."],"timeline":[[1913,"Born in Mondovi, French Algeria"],[1942,"Publishes The Stranger and The Myth of Sisyphus"],[1943,"Begins working for the Resistance newspaper Combat (c.)"],[1947,"Publishes The Plague"],[1951,"Publishes The Rebel"],[1957,"Awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature"],[1960,"Dies in a car accident in France"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The absurd","body":"We long for meaning and clear answers, but the universe stays silent. The absurd is not the world alone or us alone, but the clash between the two. Camus thought most people feel it at some point, perhaps on an ordinary day when routine suddenly seems pointless."},{"head":"Do not escape","body":"Camus saw two ways of escaping the absurd: physical suicide, and 'philosophical suicide', where we leap to a comforting belief that the evidence doesn't support. He rejected both. The honest response is to keep living while fully aware of the absurd."},{"head":"One must imagine Sisyphus happy","body":"In Greek myth Sisyphus is condemned to roll a rock up a hill forever. Camus suggests that if Sisyphus accepts his fate with open eyes and takes his task as his own, he can find a kind of happiness in the struggle itself."},{"head":"Rebellion with limits","body":"In The Rebel, Camus argued that revolt against injustice is right, but revolutions often turn into new tyrannies when they accept murder for the sake of history. True rebellion recognises a shared human dignity and therefore sets limits on itself."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Camus was an existentialist philosopher alongside Sartre.","truth":"Camus repeatedly rejected the existentialist label. He shared some themes with Sartre but disagreed with him on freedom, history and political violence."},"startHere":"Read The Myth of Sisyphus, especially its opening section on the absurd and the short final chapter on Sisyphus. It is brief and states his central question directly: is life worth living?","critics":"Sartre and the critic Francis Jeanson attacked The Rebel in 1952, arguing that Camus judged history from a safe moral distance and offered no practical politics for the oppressed. Others have criticised his silence and caution on Algerian independence.","legacy":"Camus's idea of the absurd became one of the best-known ideas of the twentieth century, and his novels are still widely read in schools. His warnings against killing for abstract causes remain part of debates about political violence.","quote":null},"arendt":{"life":["Hannah Arendt was born in 1906 near Hanover, Germany, and grew up in Königsberg in a secular Jewish family. She came of age in the unstable Weimar Republic and watched the rise of Nazism. Her life was shaped by the catastrophes of the twentieth century: persecution, statelessness, war and the Holocaust. Much of her thinking asks how such things could happen and what politics should be.","She studied philosophy with Martin Heidegger and Karl Jaspers and wrote a doctorate on Saint Augustine, completed in 1928 and published in 1929. After a brief arrest by the Gestapo in 1933 she fled to Paris. In 1940 she was interned in a French camp, escaped, and reached New York in 1941. She was stateless until she became a US citizen in 1951, the year The Origins of Totalitarianism appeared. The Human Condition (1958) and Eichmann in Jerusalem (1963) followed.","Arendt refused to call herself a philosopher, preferring 'political theorist'. She wrote in a dense, original style and often worked as a public essayist, reporting for The New Yorker on the trial of the Nazi official Adolf Eichmann. She taught at several American universities, including the New School for Social Research in New York. She died in New York in 1975, leaving The Life of the Mind unfinished."],"timeline":[[1906,"Born near Hanover, Germany"],[1928,"Completes her doctorate on love in Saint Augustine"],[1933,"Briefly arrested by the Gestapo; flees Germany"],[1941,"Arrives in New York as a refugee"],[1951,"Publishes The Origins of Totalitarianism; becomes a US citizen"],[1958,"Publishes The Human Condition"],[1963,"Publishes Eichmann in Jerusalem"],[1975,"Dies in New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Totalitarianism is something new","body":"Arendt argued that Nazi and Stalinist rule were not just extreme tyrannies. They aimed at total control, using ideology and terror to make individuals isolated and superfluous. Camps were their central institution, testing whether human beings could be turned into mere bundles of reactions."},{"head":"The banality of evil","body":"Watching Eichmann's trial, Arendt was struck that he seemed less a monster than a careerist who spoke in clichés and did not think about what he was doing. Her point was that enormous evil can be done by people who simply fail to think, not only by people with demonic motives."},{"head":"Labour, work and action","body":"In The Human Condition she separates three activities: labour (meeting the body's needs), work (making lasting things), and action (doing and speaking together in public). She feared modern society was losing action, the activity where people show who they are and start something new."},{"head":"The right to have rights","body":"Stateless refugees, she noticed, lost their human rights in practice because no government would protect them. She concluded that the most basic right is the right to belong to a political community at all."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The 'banality of evil' means the Holocaust was a small or ordinary thing, or that Eichmann was harmless.","truth":"Arendt thought Eichmann deserved to be executed. 'Banal' describes his thoughtlessness and lack of deep motive, not the scale of the crimes, which she regarded as unprecedented."},"startHere":"Read the Postscript and the final chapter of Eichmann in Jerusalem, where she explains what she meant by the banality of evil, then her later essay Thinking and Moral Considerations.","critics":"The scholar Gershom Scholem and others criticised her tone and her harsh remarks on the Jewish councils under Nazi rule. Later historians, notably Bettina Stangneth, argued from new evidence that Eichmann was a committed Nazi ideologue rather than a thoughtless functionary.","legacy":"Arendt's analysis of totalitarianism and her phrase 'the banality of evil' are now standard reference points whenever people discuss authoritarian politics, bureaucracy and responsibility. Her ideas about public action continue to influence democratic theory.","quote":null},"fanon":{"life":["Frantz Fanon was born in 1925 in Fort-de-France, Martinique, a Caribbean island ruled by France. Its population was mostly descended from enslaved Africans, and schools taught children to see themselves as French. Fanon's lifetime saw the Second World War and then the collapse of European empires across Africa and Asia, often through violent wars of independence. Like many educated people from the French colonies, he was raised to think of France as his true cultural home, an experience he later examined critically.","At school he was taught by the poet Aimé Césaire. In 1944 he fought with the Free French army in Europe and was wounded. He then trained in medicine and psychiatry in Lyon, and published Black Skin, White Masks in 1952. In 1953 he became head of a psychiatric ward at Blida-Joinville hospital in Algeria, where he witnessed the Algerian War. He joined the independence movement, resigned his post in 1956, and worked for it from Tunis. He died of leukaemia in December 1961, just after The Wretched of the Earth appeared.","Fanon combined philosophy, psychiatry and political activism. He drew on existentialism, psychoanalysis and Marxism, but tested them against what he saw in his patients and in colonial society. He wrote with intensity and urgency. As a doctor he tried to reform hospital care, treating patients as members of a community rather than as inmates. His books mix clinical case notes, personal memory and political argument, which gives them an unusual urgency and makes them hard to classify."],"timeline":[[1925,"Born in Fort-de-France, Martinique"],[1944,"Wounded while serving with the Free French army"],[1952,"Publishes Black Skin, White Masks"],[1953,"Becomes head of a psychiatric ward at Blida-Joinville, Algeria"],[1956,"Resigns his hospital post and joins the Algerian independence struggle"],[1960,"Serves as an envoy of the Algerian provisional government in Ghana"],[1961,"Publishes The Wretched of the Earth; dies in the United States"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Colonialism enters the mind","body":"Fanon argued that colonialism does not only take land and labour. It teaches colonised people to see themselves through the coloniser's eyes, so that Black people may feel pressure to wear 'white masks' to be accepted. These are psychological wounds, not just political ones."},{"head":"Being seen as a type","body":"In a famous passage, Fanon describes a white child pointing at him and crying out in fear. In that moment he is no longer seen as a person but as a racial category. He called this experience of being fixed by another's gaze a crushing one."},{"head":"Decolonisation is a total change","body":"Fanon argued that real independence means more than swapping a European ruler for a local one. The whole colonial order has to be dismantled. He believed that because colonialism was founded on violence, its overthrow would usually involve violence too."},{"head":"Beware the new elite","body":"Fanon warned that after independence a national middle class might simply step into the colonisers' shoes and enrich itself. Many later post-colonial governments seemed to confirm this warning."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Fanon simply celebrated violence as good in itself.","truth":"Fanon analysed why colonial rule produced violent resistance and argued it was often unavoidable, but he also documented in detail the mental harm violence caused on all sides. How far he endorsed it remains debated."},"startHere":"Read chapter 5 of Black Skin, White Masks, often translated as 'The Lived Experience of the Black Man'. It is personal, vivid and shows how he blends philosophy with experience.","critics":"Hannah Arendt, in On Violence (1970), criticised the way Fanon's admirers, and Sartre's preface to The Wretched of the Earth, praised violence as creative and liberating. Others argue his psychological claims generalise too broadly from particular cases.","legacy":"Fanon became a central figure in post-colonial theory, Africana philosophy and critical race studies. Liberation movements from Africa to the Americas read him, and his writing on racism's psychological effects remains widely taught.","quote":null},"turing":{"life":["Alan Turing was born in London in 1912. His lifetime spanned the birth of modern logic and the Second World War, when mathematics suddenly became a matter of national survival. In the 1930s logicians were asking whether there could be a mechanical procedure to settle every mathematical question. Turing's answer helped define what a computer is, years before practical electronic computers existed.","He studied mathematics at King's College, Cambridge, and became a fellow there in 1935. His 1936 paper On Computable Numbers described an imaginary 'Turing machine'. After a PhD at Princeton (1938), he worked during the war at Bletchley Park, where he played a leading part in breaking German naval Enigma codes. Afterwards he designed an early computer at the National Physical Laboratory, then moved to Manchester. His 1950 paper Computing Machinery and Intelligence asked whether machines can think.","Turing was known for approaching problems from first principles, often building things himself. In his last years he worked on how patterns form in living things. In 1952 he was convicted under laws that then criminalised homosexual acts and was forced to undergo hormone treatment. He died in 1954 from cyanide poisoning; the inquest ruled it suicide. In 2013 he received a posthumous royal pardon."],"timeline":[[1912,"Born in London"],[1936,"Writes On Computable Numbers, introducing the Turing machine"],[1938,"Completes his PhD at Princeton"],[1939,"Begins codebreaking work at Bletchley Park"],[1945,"Joins the National Physical Laboratory to design a stored-program computer"],[1950,"Publishes Computing Machinery and Intelligence in the journal Mind"],[1952,"Convicted of 'gross indecency' under anti-homosexuality laws"],[1954,"Dies in Wilmslow, Cheshire"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The Turing machine","body":"Turing imagined a simple machine that reads and writes symbols on a tape following fixed rules. He showed that such a machine can carry out any step-by-step calculation that a human clerk following instructions could. This gave a precise meaning to the word 'computable'."},{"head":"Some questions have no mechanical answer","body":"Turing proved there is no general procedure that can decide, for every program, whether it will eventually stop or run forever. This is now called the halting problem. It shows that some well-defined questions are beyond the reach of any algorithm."},{"head":"The imitation game","body":"Instead of arguing about the vague question 'Can machines think?', Turing proposed a test. A judge has typed conversations with a human and a machine. If the judge cannot reliably tell which is which, Turing suggested we have no good reason to deny that the machine thinks."},{"head":"Answering the objections","body":"Turing's 1950 paper takes on objections one by one, such as 'machines can only do what we tell them' and 'machines can't be conscious'. He replied, for instance, that we only judge other people to think by how they behave, so we should apply the same standard to machines."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Turing claimed his test was a strict definition of intelligence or proof that a machine is conscious.","truth":"He offered the imitation game as a more workable replacement for a question he thought too vague to discuss usefully, and he made predictions about it rather than definitions."},"startHere":"Read Computing Machinery and Intelligence (1950). It is written for a general audience, is often witty, and its list of objections and replies is still a good map of the debate about machine minds.","critics":"John Searle's Chinese Room argument (1980) claimed a system could pass Turing's test by manipulating symbols without understanding anything, so passing the test does not show real thought.","legacy":"Turing's work is a foundation of computer science and of the philosophy of mind and artificial intelligence. The 'Turing test' remains a reference point in every debate about whether AI systems understand or think.","quote":null},"popper":{"life":["Karl Popper was born in Vienna in 1902, when the city was a centre of science, music and new ideas, including Freud's psychoanalysis, Marxism and Einstein's physics. As a young man he lived through the collapse of the Austro-Hungarian Empire after 1918, poverty, political street violence and the rise of fascism. These experiences shaped both his theory of science and his defence of open, democratic societies.","After a doctorate in 1928 he trained and worked as a schoolteacher while writing The Logic of Scientific Discovery (1934). As the Nazi threat grew he left for New Zealand in 1937, teaching in Christchurch. There he wrote The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945), which he called his war effort. In 1946 he moved to the London School of Economics, where he stayed for the rest of his career. He was knighted in 1965 and died in London in 1994.","Popper was a forceful and combative thinker who prized clarity and disliked jargon. He argued with members of the Vienna Circle, with Marxists and with Wittgenstein. He insisted that intellectual honesty means looking for the weaknesses in your own views. His influence reached well beyond philosophy, into scientific practice and politics. He also wrote an intellectual autobiography, Unended Quest (1976), which describes how his ideas developed."],"timeline":[[1902,"Born in Vienna"],[1928,"Receives his doctorate from the University of Vienna"],[1934,"Publishes Logik der Forschung (later The Logic of Scientific Discovery)"],[1937,"Moves to New Zealand to teach in Christchurch"],[1945,"Publishes The Open Society and Its Enemies"],[1946,"Joins the London School of Economics"],[1963,"Publishes Conjectures and Refutations"],[1994,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Falsifiability","body":"A theory is scientific, Popper argued, if it makes claims that could turn out false. 'All swans are white' is risky: one black swan refutes it. A theory that fits every possible outcome, whatever happens, tells us nothing about the world and so is not science."},{"head":"Conjectures and refutations","body":"Science does not grow by piling up confirmations. It grows when scientists make bold guesses and then try hard to knock them down. Theories that survive tough tests are kept for now, but never proven true once and for all."},{"head":"The problem with induction","body":"Popper accepted Hume's point that no number of observations can prove a general law. Instead of trying to justify induction, he argued science does not need it: testing and refuting guesses is enough."},{"head":"The open society","body":"Popper attacked thinkers, including Plato, Hegel and Marx, who claimed to know the laws of history or the perfect society. Because we can always be wrong, he argued, we need institutions that let us criticise rulers and remove them without bloodshed."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Popper said a theory must have been proven false to count as scientific.","truth":"He said a scientific theory must be falsifiable: it must be possible in principle to test it against observations that could show it false. Good theories are falsifiable but not yet falsified."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Conjectures and Refutations, 'Science: Conjectures and Refutations'. It explains in a lively way how he arrived at falsifiability by contrasting Einstein's risky predictions with Freud's and Marx's theories.","critics":"Thomas Kuhn argued that real scientists usually do not abandon theories when a test fails; they keep working within an accepted framework. Others pointed out, following Pierre Duhem and Quine, that a failed test never shows exactly which assumption is at fault.","legacy":"Falsifiability is still the most widely cited answer to the question of what separates science from pseudo-science, and many working scientists describe their method in Popper's terms. His defence of the open society remains a classic of liberal political thought.","quote":null},"quine":{"life":["Willard Van Orman Quine was born in Akron, Ohio, in 1908. He grew up as modern logic was transforming philosophy: thinkers such as Bertrand Russell and the logical positivists of the Vienna Circle hoped that logic and science could clear away vague metaphysics. For most of the twentieth century, American philosophy was increasingly shaped by this 'analytic' style, and Quine became one of its leading figures.","He studied mathematics at Oberlin College and earned a Harvard PhD in 1932 under Alfred North Whitehead. A travelling fellowship took him to Europe, where he met Rudolf Carnap and other logicians. He joined the Harvard faculty in 1936 and stayed there until he retired in 1978, apart from service in US naval intelligence during the Second World War. His essays On What There Is (1948) and Two Dogmas of Empiricism (1951), and the book Word and Object (1960), made his reputation.","Quine wrote in a compact, witty style and was a skilled logician as well as a philosopher. He took a scientific attitude to every question, preferring the simplest theory that fits the evidence. He disliked talk of meanings and essences that could not be tied to observable behaviour. He died in Boston in 2000. He also wrote textbooks on logic that were used by generations of students."],"timeline":[[1908,"Born in Akron, Ohio"],[1932,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1936,"Begins teaching at Harvard"],[1948,"Publishes On What There Is"],[1951,"Publishes Two Dogmas of Empiricism"],[1960,"Publishes Word and Object"],[1978,"Retires from Harvard"],[2000,"Dies in Boston"]],"ideas":[{"head":"No sharp analytic/synthetic line","body":"Many philosophers held that some sentences, like 'All bachelors are unmarried', are true just by meaning, while others depend on facts. Quine argued that no one had given a clear, non-circular account of this distinction, and that it should be given up."},{"head":"The web of belief","body":"Our beliefs face experience together, like a web, not one by one. When an observation clashes with what we expect, we can adjust different parts of the web. Even logic and mathematics, near the centre, could in principle be revised, though we prefer to change beliefs near the edge."},{"head":"Naturalised epistemology","body":"Quine thought there is no special standpoint outside science from which philosophy can justify science. Questions about how we know should be studied with the tools of science itself, such as psychology. Philosophy is continuous with science, not above it."},{"head":"Indeterminacy of translation","body":"Imagine a linguist hearing a local speaker say 'gavagai' when a rabbit runs past. Does it mean 'rabbit', 'rabbit part' or 'a stage of rabbit'? Quine argued all the behavioural evidence could fit more than one translation, so there may be no single fact about what a word means."},{"head":"To be is to be a value of a variable","body":"To find out what a theory says exists, Quine suggested looking at what it must quantify over, that is, what its 'there is' statements require. This gave a clear test for ontology, the study of what exists."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Web of Belief (1970), a short book Quine wrote with J. S. Ullian for students. Then try On What There Is, which opens with a famous puzzle about how we can talk about things that do not exist.","critics":"H. P. Grice and P. F. Strawson, in In Defense of a Dogma (1956), argued that ordinary speakers use the analytic/synthetic distinction reliably, so it cannot be as empty as Quine claimed. Others think his indeterminacy thesis would make all talk of meaning impossible.","legacy":"Quine's attack on the analytic/synthetic distinction and his naturalism changed the direction of analytic philosophy after 1950. The idea that philosophy is continuous with science is now widespread.","quote":null},"rawls":{"life":["John Rawls was born in Baltimore, Maryland, in 1921. He grew up during the Great Depression and came of age in the Second World War. Post-war America was wealthy but deeply divided over race and inequality, and in the 1960s the civil rights movement and the Vietnam War raised urgent questions about justice and civil disobedience. These were the problems that Rawls set out to address with a systematic theory.","He studied at Princeton, then served as an infantryman in the Pacific, including in the occupation of Japan. Returning to Princeton, he completed his PhD in 1950 and spent a year at Oxford. He taught at Cornell and MIT, then moved to Harvard in 1962. His essay Justice as Fairness (1958) contained the seeds of A Theory of Justice (1971), which took him about twenty years to complete. Political Liberalism (1993) and The Law of Peoples (1999) revised and extended it.","Rawls worked slowly and carefully, revising drafts over many years and responding at length to critics. He wrote in a patient, measured style and avoided public celebrity. He was known as a devoted teacher, and many of his students became leading political philosophers. He received the National Humanities Medal in 1999 and died in 2002. A shy and modest man, he rarely gave interviews and preferred to let his books speak for themselves."],"timeline":[[1921,"Born in Baltimore, Maryland"],[1950,"Completes his PhD at Princeton"],[1958,"Publishes the essay Justice as Fairness"],[1962,"Joins the Harvard faculty"],[1971,"Publishes A Theory of Justice"],[1993,"Publishes Political Liberalism"],[1999,"Publishes The Law of Peoples"],[2002,"Dies in Lexington, Massachusetts"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The veil of ignorance","body":"Imagine choosing the basic rules of society without knowing whether you will be rich or poor, talented or not, which religion or race you will have. Rawls argued that principles chosen behind this 'veil' would be fair, because no one could tilt them in their own favour."},{"head":"Equal basic liberties","body":"His first principle says everyone should have the same set of basic liberties, such as freedom of speech, conscience and political participation. These come first and may not be traded away for economic gains."},{"head":"The difference principle","body":"Inequalities in wealth and income are allowed only if they work to the benefit of the worst-off members of society. Paying doctors more might be fair if it means better healthcare for everyone, including the poorest."},{"head":"Justice as fairness, not total welfare","body":"Rawls rejected utilitarianism, the view that we should maximise total happiness. That view, he argued, ignores the separateness of persons: it could allow some people to be sacrificed if others gained more."},{"head":"Political liberalism","body":"In later work Rawls asked how citizens with very different religions and worldviews can live together. His answer was that they can agree on a shared political idea of justice without agreeing on deeper questions about life's meaning."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Rawls believed people really are ignorant and purely selfish when they think about justice.","truth":"The veil of ignorance is a thought experiment: a way of modelling impartiality by imagining what we would choose if we could not favour ourselves."},"startHere":"Read chapter 1 of A Theory of Justice, especially the opening sections on 'justice as fairness' and the original position. They set out the core idea before the book becomes more technical.","critics":"Robert Nozick, in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), argued that redistributing wealth to fit a pattern violates people's rights to what they have fairly acquired. Communitarians such as Michael Sandel objected that the people behind the veil are stripped of the commitments that make us who we are.","legacy":"A Theory of Justice revived political philosophy in the English-speaking world and is still the starting point for most debates about fairness, equality and the welfare state. The veil of ignorance is now used well beyond philosophy, in law, economics and public policy.","quote":null},"singer":{"life":["Peter Singer was born in Melbourne, Australia, in 1946. He began his career in the late 1960s and early 1970s, when the movements for civil rights, women's liberation and against the Vietnam War were challenging accepted moral views. Moral philosophy at the time was turning back towards real-world questions, and Singer became one of the leading voices of this 'applied ethics'.","He studied at the University of Melbourne and then at Oxford. His essay Famine, Affluence, and Morality (1972), written in response to the famine and refugee crisis in what became Bangladesh, argued that the wealthy have strong duties to help. Animal Liberation (1975) helped launch the modern animal welfare movement, and Practical Ethics (1979) set out his views across many issues. He worked at Monash University, founding its Centre for Human Bioethics, and joined Princeton University in 1999.","Singer writes for the general public as much as for academics, in clear, direct prose. He has popularised his arguments through books such as The Life You Can Save (2009) and The Most Good You Can Do (2015), which helped inspire the effective altruism movement. His work has often been controversial, and he has been the target of protests over his views on disability and euthanasia."],"timeline":[[1946,"Born in Melbourne, Australia"],[1972,"Publishes Famine, Affluence, and Morality"],[1975,"Publishes Animal Liberation"],[1979,"Publishes Practical Ethics"],[1999,"Joins Princeton University as professor of bioethics"],[2009,"Publishes The Life You Can Save"],[2015,"Publishes The Most Good You Can Do"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The drowning child","body":"If you walked past a shallow pond where a child was drowning, you would wade in to save them even if it ruined your clothes. Singer argues that distance makes no moral difference, so we have similar duties to people dying of poverty far away when we could help at small cost."},{"head":"Equal consideration of interests","body":"Everyone's suffering counts equally, whoever they are. A pain of the same intensity matters just as much whether it is felt by you, a stranger or an animal. This does not mean treating everyone the same, but giving their interests equal weight."},{"head":"Speciesism","body":"Singer borrowed the term 'speciesism' for favouring members of our own species simply because they are human. He compares it to racism and sexism: in each case, a morally irrelevant feature is used to discount others' suffering. This underlies his criticism of factory farming."},{"head":"Effective giving","body":"If we should help, we should help as well as we can. Singer encourages people to give a meaningful share of their income to charities that evidence shows do the most good per pound or dollar."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Singer believes animals have the same rights as humans and must be treated identically.","truth":"He is a utilitarian, not mainly a rights theorist, and asks for equal consideration of comparable interests. A pig has no interest in voting, so equal consideration does not mean equal treatment."},"startHere":"Read the essay Famine, Affluence, and Morality (1972). It is short, uses the drowning-child example, and shows his style of argument: simple premises leading to demanding conclusions.","critics":"Many argue his view is too demanding, requiring us to give until we are nearly as poor as those we help and leaving no room for personal projects. Disability rights advocates have strongly objected to his arguments about the moral status of severely disabled newborns.","legacy":"Singer is a central figure in the animal welfare movement and in effective altruism, and has influenced how many people think about charity and diet. He did much to make applied ethics a major part of academic philosophy.","quote":null},"parmenides":{"life":["Parmenides lived in Elea, a Greek colony on the coast of southern Italy, probably from about 515 to after 450 BCE. Greek thinkers before him, such as Thales and Heraclitus, had tried to explain the world by naming its basic stuff or the laws of its change. Parmenides took a different path: instead of observing nature, he asked what reason itself allows us to say about anything that exists.","Almost nothing certain is known about his life. Plato describes him visiting Athens as an old man, aged about 65, and talking with a young Socrates; if this is accurate, it fixes his dates roughly. Later ancient writers say he drew up laws for Elea, but this cannot be confirmed. His only work was a poem, usually called On Nature, of which around 150 lines survive, quoted by later authors.","Parmenides wrote in the same verse form as Homer. His poem opens with a journey: a young man is carried by a chariot to a goddess, who teaches him two paths. One is the 'Way of Truth', based on pure reasoning. The other is the 'Way of Opinion', describing the world as ordinary mortals believe it to be. This dramatic framing has made the poem hard to interpret."],"timeline":[[-515,"Born in Elea, southern Italy (c.)"],[-475,"Composes his poem, On Nature (c.)"],[-450,"According to Plato, visits Athens and meets the young Socrates (c.)"],[-450,"Dies, probably soon after this date (c.)"],[-370,"Plato writes the dialogue Parmenides, featuring him as a character (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"What is not cannot be thought","body":"The goddess says you cannot know or speak of what is not, because there is nothing there to think about. So any line of reasoning that starts from 'nothing' or 'not-being' leads nowhere."},{"head":"Being cannot change","body":"If something came into existence, it would have come from nothing, which is impossible. If it ceased to exist, it would become nothing. Parmenides concluded that what truly is must be ungenerated, unchanging and indestructible."},{"head":"Reason over the senses","body":"Our eyes and ears tell us things move, grow and die. Parmenides argued that if reason shows change is impossible, we must trust reason and treat the senses as misleading. This set up a lasting contrast between appearance and reality."},{"head":"All is one","body":"If there is only being, and nothing that is not, then nothing can divide one part of being from another. Parmenides described reality as whole, continuous and complete, like a well-rounded sphere."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the surviving fragments of the Way of Truth, especially fragments 2 to 8 in standard numbering, in a modern translation with notes. Fragment 8 contains his main argument that being is unchanging.","critics":"Plato, in his dialogue the Sophist, argued that 'what is not' can make sense if we understand it as 'what is different', and called his challenge a kind of attack on his intellectual father. Aristotle replied that 'being' is said in many ways, which dissolves the argument against change.","legacy":"Parmenides forced every later Greek thinker to explain how change and plurality are possible, leading directly to atomism and shaping Plato's theory of Forms. He is often called the founder of metaphysics and of reasoned argument about being.","quote":null},"zeno_elea":{"life":["Zeno lived in Elea, a Greek city in southern Italy, in the fifth century BCE. He was the student and defender of Parmenides, who had argued that reality is one and unchanging. Many people found this absurd. Zeno's response was to show that the common-sense view, that there are many things and that they move, leads to even greater absurdities.","According to Plato, Zeno came to Athens with Parmenides when he was about 40 and read out his book of arguments to a young Socrates. That book is lost. We know his paradoxes mainly through Aristotle, who discussed them in his Physics, and through later commentators. Later stories say Zeno resisted a tyrant and was killed after torture, but these accounts differ and cannot be trusted as fact.","Zeno's method was to take an opponent's assumption and draw out contradictory conclusions from it, a technique called reductio ad absurdum. According to a later report, Aristotle called him the inventor of dialectic, the art of argument by question and answer. His paradoxes are short, sharp and puzzling, which is why they are still discussed today. Their aim was not to describe the world but to test ideas by pushing them to their limits."],"timeline":[[-490,"Born in Elea, southern Italy (c.)"],[-450,"According to Plato, visits Athens with Parmenides and meets Socrates (c.)"],[-430,"Dies (c.)"],[-370,"Plato's Parmenides portrays Zeno defending his teacher (c.)"],[-330,"Aristotle analyses the motion paradoxes in his Physics (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Achilles and the tortoise","body":"Swift Achilles gives a tortoise a head start. By the time he reaches where the tortoise was, it has moved on a little. When he reaches that point, it has moved again. It seems he must complete endlessly many steps and so can never catch up."},{"head":"The dichotomy","body":"To walk across a room you must first go halfway, then half the remaining distance, and so on without end. If you can never finish infinitely many tasks, how can you ever arrive, or even start?"},{"head":"The arrow","body":"At any single instant, a flying arrow occupies a space exactly its own size, so at that instant it is not moving. If time is made of instants and the arrow is still at each one, when does it move?"},{"head":"Arguments against plurality","body":"Zeno also argued that if there are many things, they must be both infinitely small and infinitely large. His aim was to show that believing in many things is no less troubling than Parmenides' view that all is one."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Zeno simply believed that nothing moves and was being silly.","truth":"Zeno's paradoxes were designed as arguments against his opponents' assumptions about space, time and plurality, and they raised real problems about infinity that took mathematicians many centuries to address."},"startHere":"Since his own book is lost, start with Aristotle's Physics, Book 6, chapter 9, where the four paradoxes of motion are set out briefly, or a good modern explanation alongside it.","critics":"Aristotle replied that a distance is only potentially, not actually, divided into infinitely many parts. Many modern writers argue the motion paradoxes are answered by the mathematics of limits, which shows that an infinite series of distances can add up to a finite total, though philosophers still debate whether this settles the deeper puzzle.","legacy":"Zeno's paradoxes have challenged philosophers and mathematicians for over two thousand years and helped drive thinking about infinity, continuity and the nature of time. He is also remembered as a pioneer of rigorous argument by contradiction.","quote":null},"democritus":{"life":["Democritus came from Abdera, a Greek city in Thrace on the northern coast of the Aegean, and was probably born around 460 BCE. He lived at the same time as Socrates, in an era when Greek thinkers were trying to answer Parmenides' challenge that change and plurality are impossible. Democritus, building on his teacher Leucippus, gave one of the boldest answers: the world is made of tiny, indivisible bodies moving in empty space.","Ancient sources say he travelled widely and lived to a great age, but the details are uncertain. He was a prolific writer: later lists credit him with around 70 works on physics, mathematics, ethics, music and more. None survive complete. We know his ideas from fragments and from reports by Aristotle and later writers, who often did not separate his views from those of Leucippus.","Later tradition called Democritus 'the laughing philosopher', supposedly because he found human folly amusing; this is a later story rather than reliable history. Many of the surviving fragments are short ethical sayings, praising moderation and cheerfulness. They show a thinker interested not only in the structure of matter but also in how to live well. Later readers, from Epicurus to the scientists of the seventeenth century, often drew on both sides of his work."],"timeline":[[-460,"Born in Abdera, Thrace (c.)"],[-370,"Dies, reportedly at a great age (c.)"],[-306,"Epicurus founds his school in Athens, adopting and revising atomism (c.)"],[-55,"Lucretius's poem On the Nature of Things carries atomism to Roman readers (c.)"],[1808,"John Dalton publishes a modern chemical atomic theory, reviving the ancient name atom"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Atoms and the void","body":"Everything is made of atoms, tiny solid bodies too small to see, that cannot be cut ('atomos' means uncuttable). They move through empty space, the void. Change is just atoms rearranging, so nothing truly comes from or vanishes into nothing."},{"head":"Colour and taste are by convention","body":"Democritus said sweet, bitter, hot and cold exist 'by convention', while in reality there are only atoms and void. Honey tastes sweet because of how its atoms affect us, not because sweetness is in the atoms themselves."},{"head":"Necessity, not purpose","body":"Atoms collide and combine according to necessity, not according to any plan or goal. Worlds, including ours, form and dissolve this way. This mechanical picture leaves no role for gods designing nature."},{"head":"Cheerfulness","body":"In ethics Democritus valued euthumia, a calm and cheerful state of mind. He thought it came from moderation, from not envying others and from wanting what is within reach."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Democritus discovered the atoms of modern science.","truth":"His atomism was a philosophical theory based on argument, not experiment, and modern atoms are divisible into smaller particles. Modern scientists borrowed his word, not his evidence."},"startHere":"Read a collection of the fragments in translation, starting with the reports on atoms and void and the fragment on sweet and bitter being 'by convention'. Kirk, Raven and Schofield's The Presocratic Philosophers gives them with clear commentary.","critics":"Aristotle objected that atomism could not explain why things have the forms and purposes they do, and argued against the existence of a void. Many later thinkers asked how mindless atoms could account for thought and experience.","legacy":"Democritus's atomism shaped Epicurus and Lucretius, was revived by early modern scientists in the seventeenth century, and gave modern physics and chemistry their word 'atom'. His distinction between real properties and how things appear to us anticipated later debates about perception.","quote":null},"zeno_citium":{"life":["Zeno was born around 334 BCE in Citium, a city on Cyprus with a large Phoenician population. He lived in the age after Alexander the Great, when Greek cities had lost much of their independence to large kingdoms. Many people looked to philosophy less for theories about nature and more for a way of living calmly and well in an uncertain world. Athens remained the centre of philosophical schools.","He came to Athens as a young man, around 312 BCE. According to a later story he arrived as a merchant after a shipwreck and was drawn to philosophy by reading about Socrates. He studied with the Cynic Crates and with other teachers. Around 300 BCE he began teaching in the Stoa Poikile, the 'Painted Porch' in the Athenian marketplace, which gave his followers the name 'Stoics'. He died around 262 BCE.","Zeno wrote several works, including a Republic describing an ideal community of wise people, but none survive; we know them through quotations and reports, chiefly in Diogenes Laertius. Ancient sources describe him as austere and self-controlled. According to Diogenes Laertius, the Athenians honoured him with a golden crown and a public tomb for his virtue and for encouraging young people towards self-control."],"timeline":[[-334,"Born in Citium, Cyprus (c.)"],[-312,"Arrives in Athens and studies with the Cynic Crates (c.)"],[-300,"Begins teaching in the Painted Stoa, founding the Stoic school (c.)"],[-262,"Dies in Athens (c.)"],[-230,"Chrysippus becomes head of the Stoic school and systematises its teaching (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Virtue is the only good","body":"Health, wealth and reputation are not truly good or bad. Zeno called them 'indifferents': some are naturally preferred, like health, but none can make you happy or miserable by itself. Only virtue, a wise and just character, is good."},{"head":"Live in agreement with nature","body":"The goal of life is to live consistently with nature, meaning both your own nature as a reasoning being and the rational order of the universe. A person who lives by reason lives in harmony with the whole."},{"head":"Passions are mistaken judgments","body":"Stoics held that strong emotions like fear, greed and rage come from false beliefs, for example that losing money is a disaster. Correct the belief and the passion fades. The wise person is free of such passions."},{"head":"A world city","body":"Zeno's Republic imagined people united not by local laws but by shared reason. Later Stoics developed this into the idea that all humans are citizens of one world community."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Stoicism means suppressing your feelings and showing a stiff upper lip.","truth":"Stoics aimed to remove passions that rest on false judgments, not to bottle feelings up. They thought the wise person would still feel good emotions, such as joy and goodwill."},"startHere":"Since none of his writings survive, start with Book 7 of Diogenes Laertius's Lives of the Eminent Philosophers, which gives his life and an overview of early Stoic teaching.","critics":"Academic sceptics such as Carneades attacked the Stoic theory of knowledge, and Aristotelian critics argued it was unrealistic to say health and wealth contribute nothing to a good life.","legacy":"The school Zeno founded became one of the most influential in the Greek and Roman world and shaped later writers such as Seneca, Epictetus and Marcus Aurelius. Stoic ideas about emotions influenced modern cognitive behavioural therapy.","quote":null},"pyrrho":{"life":["Pyrrho was born around 365 BCE in Elis, in the western Peloponnese of Greece. He lived through the dramatic expansion of Macedonian power under Philip and Alexander the Great. Greek travellers were coming into contact with Persian and Indian cultures for the first time on a large scale, and older certainties about the world were being shaken. The philosophical schools of Athens, meanwhile, were arguing about whether human beings could reach certain knowledge at all.","According to Diogenes Laertius, Pyrrho was first a painter before turning to philosophy. He travelled with Alexander's army to Persia and India alongside the philosopher Anaxarchus, and ancient writers say he met Indian ascetics there. Afterwards he returned to Elis and lived simply. He is reported to have been greatly respected in his home city, which reportedly made him a high priest. He died around 275 BCE.","Pyrrho wrote nothing. His ideas reach us mainly through his student Timon of Phlius, as summarised by later writers. Ancient stories claim he was so indifferent to appearances that friends had to stop him walking off cliffs or into carts. These tales probably come from hostile or comic sources; even in antiquity the sceptic Aenesidemus denied them. More reliably, he was admired for his calm."],"timeline":[[-365,"Born in Elis, Greece (c.)"],[-334,"Alexander the Great begins his campaign in Asia, which Pyrrho joins (c.)"],[-326,"In India with Alexander's army (c.)"],[-275,"Dies in Elis (c.)"],[-80,"Aenesidemus revives scepticism under the name 'Pyrrhonism' (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Things cannot be pinned down","body":"According to Timon's summary, Pyrrho held that things are indifferent, unmeasurable and undecidable. Neither our senses nor our beliefs tell us truly or falsely how things are in themselves."},{"head":"Suspend judgment","body":"If we cannot settle how things really are, we should not commit to any opinion about it. The sceptic says neither 'it is' nor 'it is not', but holds back."},{"head":"Calm follows","body":"The reward for giving up firm beliefs is ataraxia, a calm, untroubled state of mind. Someone who does not believe poverty is truly bad does not suffer the extra torment of thinking a disaster has happened."},{"head":"A way of life, not a theory","body":"For Pyrrho scepticism was not just a clever argument but a practical attitude. Its aim was peace of mind in a world where most people tie themselves in knots defending their opinions."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Pyrrho was so sceptical he ignored danger and had to be saved from cliffs and carts.","truth":"Those stories come from later and probably hostile sources, and ancient sceptics themselves denied them; he seems to have lived an ordinary, respected life."},"startHere":"Read Book 9 of Diogenes Laertius's Lives of the Eminent Philosophers, which collects what was said about him, then the opening chapters of Sextus Empiricus's Outlines of Pyrrhonism for the later school that took his name.","critics":"Critics from the Stoics onwards argued that a person without beliefs could not act at all: you need to believe the bread is food to eat it. David Hume later argued that total scepticism cannot survive contact with everyday life.","legacy":"Pyrrho gave his name to Pyrrhonism, the most radical form of ancient scepticism, which through Sextus Empiricus shaped Montaigne, Descartes and Hume. Some scholars also explore possible links between his ideas and Indian thought.","quote":null},"epictetus":{"life":["Epictetus was born around 50 CE in Hierapolis, in Phrygia (in modern Turkey). He lived under the Roman Empire at the height of its power, a world in which the emperor could ruin anyone at will and slavery was a normal part of life. Stoicism, by now centuries old, had become a popular philosophy among Romans seeking inner freedom in an unpredictable society.","He was born a slave and was owned in Rome by Epaphroditus, a powerful freedman of the emperor Nero. While still enslaved he studied with the Stoic teacher Musonius Rufus. After he was freed he taught philosophy in Rome until the emperor Domitian expelled philosophers from the city, probably around 93 CE. Epictetus then founded a school at Nicopolis in western Greece, where he taught until his death around 135 CE.","Epictetus wrote nothing himself. His student Arrian wrote down his lectures as the Discourses, four books of which survive, and compiled a short summary, the Enchiridion or Handbook. The Discourses show a blunt, humorous and demanding teacher who scolded students for being lazy. Ancient sources say he was lame, and that he lived very simply. His teaching was aimed at practical change, not clever theory: he wanted students to live differently, not just talk well."],"timeline":[[50,"Born in Hierapolis, Phrygia, into slavery (c.)"],[70,"Studies with the Stoic Musonius Rufus in Rome (c.)"],[93,"Philosophers are expelled from Rome by Domitian; Epictetus moves to Nicopolis (c.)"],[108,"Arrian attends his lectures and records the Discourses (c.)"],[135,"Dies in Nicopolis (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The dichotomy of control","body":"Some things are up to us: our judgments, choices, desires and aversions. Others are not: our body, property, reputation and other people's actions. Much unhappiness comes from treating the second kind as if it were the first."},{"head":"It is our judgments that upset us","body":"Things do not disturb us; our opinions about them do. If someone insults you, the harm comes from your judgment that you have been harmed. Change the judgment, and the disturbance can go."},{"head":"Inner freedom","body":"A slave can be free in the way that matters, and an emperor can be a slave to fear and desire. Freedom means wanting only what is truly in your power, so that nothing outside can force you."},{"head":"Playing your role","body":"Life is like a play in which we did not choose our part. Our task is to play it well, whether we are cast as a parent, a citizen or a beggar."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Epictetus taught people to stop caring about anything outside themselves.","truth":"He taught that we should care about acting rightly in our roles towards family and community, while not resting our peace of mind on outcomes we cannot control."},"startHere":"Read the Enchiridion (Handbook). It is very short, divided into brief numbered sections, and its first chapter sets out the idea of what is and is not up to us.","critics":"Critics argue that focusing on what is 'up to us' can encourage passive acceptance of injustice, since changing social conditions is not fully in anyone's control. Others doubt that we really control our judgments and desires as much as he claimed.","legacy":"Epictetus deeply influenced the emperor Marcus Aurelius and has been read continuously ever since. His ideas helped inspire modern cognitive behavioural therapy and today's popular revival of Stoicism.","quote":{"text":"There are things which are within our power, and there are things which are beyond our power.","src":"Enchiridion, 1","tr":"Thomas Wentworth Higginson"}},"lucretius":{"life":["Titus Lucretius Carus lived in the first century BCE, in the last decades of the Roman Republic, probably from about 99 to about 55 BCE. These were violent years of civil war, political murder and dictatorship. Many Romans were deeply religious and feared the anger of the gods and punishment after death. Lucretius wrote to free his readers from these fears.","Almost nothing reliable is known about his life. His one work is On the Nature of Things, a long Latin poem in six books dedicated to a Roman named Memmius. It sets out the philosophy of the Greek thinker Epicurus. In a letter of 54 BCE, Cicero mentions Lucretius's poems to his brother, which is our earliest outside evidence. The poem seems not to have been fully finished. The manuscript was rediscovered in 1417 by the Italian scholar Poggio Bracciolini.","Lucretius combined careful argument with vivid poetic imagery, using similes from everyday life such as dust motes dancing in a sunbeam to explain atoms. Centuries later, Saint Jerome claimed he was driven mad by a love potion and killed himself. Most scholars treat this as an unreliable story, possibly invented to discredit a writer who attacked religion. What survives is the poem itself, which speaks for its author with unusual passion."],"timeline":[[-99,"Born, probably in or near Rome (c.)"],[-55,"Dies, perhaps leaving On the Nature of Things unfinished (c.)"],[-54,"Cicero mentions Lucretius's poems in a letter to his brother"],[1417,"Poggio Bracciolini rediscovers a manuscript of the poem"],[1473,"First printed edition of the poem appears in Italy (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Nothing comes from nothing","body":"Things cannot arise out of nothing or vanish into nothing. If they could, anything might spring up anywhere at any time. Instead, everything is formed from atoms that combine and separate."},{"head":"Atoms and void","body":"Following Epicurus, Lucretius argued that the world consists only of atoms moving in empty space. Thunder, disease and the shape of living things all have natural causes, not divine ones."},{"head":"The swerve","body":"Atoms sometimes swerve unpredictably by a tiny amount. Lucretius used this to explain how atoms collide to form things and how living beings can act freely rather than being driven by an unbroken chain of causes."},{"head":"Death is nothing to us","body":"The mind is made of atoms and scatters at death, so there is no one left to suffer. Lucretius adds the 'symmetry argument': we were not troubled by the eternity before we were born, so why fear the eternity after we die?"}],"misconception":{"myth":"As an Epicurean, Lucretius preached a life of luxury and indulgence.","truth":"Epicurean pleasure meant freedom from pain and anxiety, achieved through simple living, friendship and understanding nature, not feasting and excess."},"startHere":"Read the final part of Book 3 of On the Nature of Things, on why death is nothing to fear. It contains his most famous arguments and some of his finest poetry.","critics":"Cicero mocked the swerve as an arbitrary invention with no cause, introduced only to rescue free will. Christian writers rejected the poem's denial of providence and an immortal soul.","legacy":"After its rediscovery the poem helped spread atomism and naturalism in Renaissance and early modern Europe, influencing writers and scientists from Montaigne to the seventeenth-century revival of atomic theory. It remains the fullest surviving account of Epicurean physics.","quote":null},"sextus":{"life":["Sextus Empiricus probably lived in the late second or early third century CE, under the Roman Empire. We do not know where he was born or where he worked. His name suggests he belonged to the 'Empiric' school of medicine, doctors who relied on experience rather than theories about hidden causes. Philosophy at the time was divided among Stoics, Platonists, Aristotelians and Epicureans, each confident in its doctrines.","Almost nothing is known about his life beyond what his writings show. He wrote the Outlines of Pyrrhonism, in three books, and a longer set of works now grouped as Against the Mathematicians, which attacks the claims of teachers of grammar, rhetoric, geometry, logic, physics and ethics. These books are our fullest surviving account of ancient Pyrrhonian scepticism. They also contain a great deal of information about the views of the schools he was attacking, much of it found nowhere else.","Sextus presents himself as a reporter, not a dogmatist: he says he is describing how things appear to him, not asserting what is true. He collected arguments systematically, often drawing on earlier sceptics such as Aenesidemus and Agrippa. When his Outlines was translated into Latin in 1562, it became a source of intellectual upheaval in early modern Europe. Its arguments were taken up by writers trying to understand what, if anything, could be known for certain."],"timeline":[[160,"Born (c.; place unknown)"],[200,"Writes Outlines of Pyrrhonism (c.)"],[200,"Writes the works known as Against the Mathematicians (c.)"],[210,"Dies (c.)"],[1562,"Henri Estienne publishes a Latin translation of the Outlines"],[1580,"Montaigne's Essays, influenced by Sextus, first published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Equal weight on both sides","body":"For every argument there seems to be an opposing argument of equal force. Sextus called this isostheneia, equal strength. Faced with it, we find ourselves unable to choose."},{"head":"Suspension of judgment","body":"When the arguments balance, the sceptic suspends judgment (epoché), neither affirming nor denying. Sextus describes this as something that happens to him, not a rule he imposes."},{"head":"Tranquillity follows like a shadow","body":"Sextus tells the story of a painter who, unable to paint a horse's foam, threw his sponge at the picture and accidentally created the effect. In the same way, the sceptic found calm not by searching for truth but by giving up the search for certainty."},{"head":"The modes","body":"Sextus lists standard patterns of sceptical argument. The 'five modes' of Agrippa include disagreement, infinite regress, relativity, mere assumption and circular reasoning. Together they suggest any attempt to justify a belief runs into one of these problems."},{"head":"Living by appearances","body":"Sceptics still act. They follow how things seem, natural instincts, local customs and the skills of their trade, without claiming these reveal how things truly are."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Sextus claimed that we can know nothing.","truth":"He avoided even that claim, since saying 'nothing can be known' is itself a confident belief; he only reported that he found himself suspending judgment."},"startHere":"Read Book 1 of Outlines of Pyrrhonism. It explains what scepticism is, how the sceptic lives, and the modes of argument, in short numbered sections.","critics":"Critics have argued that complete suspension of judgment is either impossible to live by or self-refuting. David Hume held that Pyrrhonism could not survive ordinary life, since nature forces us to believe.","legacy":"Sextus's rediscovery in the sixteenth century helped trigger the 'sceptical crisis' that shaped Montaigne, Descartes, Bayle and Hume. His works are our main source for ancient scepticism.","quote":null},"plotinus":{"life":["Plotinus lived in the third century CE, a time of crisis for the Roman Empire, with frequent civil wars, invasions and plagues. Religion and philosophy were flourishing in this anxious world, and many people sought a deeper spiritual reality beyond everyday troubles. Plotinus built a philosophy of this kind from the works of Plato, becoming the founder of what modern scholars call Neoplatonism.","According to a later source he was born around 204 in Lycopolis, Egypt. Our main source, his student Porphyry, says he turned to philosophy at 27 in Alexandria and studied for eleven years with Ammonius Saccas. In 243 he joined the emperor Gordian III's campaign against Persia, hoping to learn Persian and Indian philosophy, but the emperor was killed. Plotinus settled in Rome around 244, began writing around 253, and died in Campania in 270.","Porphyry's Life of Plotinus describes a teacher whose seminars were open discussions rather than lectures. Porphyry says Plotinus seemed ashamed of being in a body, ate little and lived simply, and that he reached mystical union with the One four times while Porphyry knew him. Porphyry later arranged his essays into six groups of nine, the Enneads. These essays are compact and difficult, often written as answers to questions raised in his classes."],"timeline":[[204,"Born, according to a later source in Lycopolis, Egypt (c.)"],[232,"Begins studying with Ammonius Saccas in Alexandria (c.)"],[243,"Joins Gordian III's expedition against Persia"],[244,"Settles in Rome and begins teaching (c.)"],[253,"Begins writing the treatises later collected as the Enneads (c.)"],[270,"Dies in Campania, Italy"],[301,"Porphyry publishes the Enneads (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The One","body":"At the source of everything is the One, completely simple and beyond description. It is not a thing among things. Because any description would divide it, we can only say what it is not."},{"head":"Emanation","body":"Everything flows from the One the way light flows from the sun, without the source being lessened. From the One comes Intellect, the realm of Plato's Forms, and from Intellect comes Soul, which gives life to the physical world."},{"head":"Evil as lack","body":"Matter is at the furthest point from the One, where the outflowing reality runs out. Evil is not a positive force but a lack of goodness and form, like darkness is a lack of light."},{"head":"The return","body":"The human soul has come down into a body but still belongs to the higher world. Through philosophy, virtue and contemplation it can turn inward and rise back towards the One."},{"head":"Beauty points beyond itself","body":"Beautiful things move us because they share in form and unity that come from a higher source. Loving a beautiful body or artwork can be the first step towards loving beauty itself."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Plotinus thought of himself as a new kind of Platonist starting a new school.","truth":"He saw himself as simply explaining Plato's teaching correctly; the label 'Neoplatonism' was invented by modern historians."},"startHere":"Read Ennead I.6, On Beauty. Porphyry says it was the first treatise Plotinus wrote, and it introduces his ascent from physical beauty to the source of all beauty in a few readable pages.","critics":"Christian thinkers such as Augustine adopted much of his thought but rejected the idea that the world flows necessarily from the One, insisting God created freely. Philosophers have also asked how a perfectly simple One could produce anything without changing.","legacy":"Plotinus shaped Christian, Islamic and Jewish philosophy and mysticism for over a thousand years, often through Augustine and later Platonists. The Renaissance Platonism of Marsilio Ficino, who translated the Enneads into Latin, carried his ideas into early modern Europe.","quote":null},"boethius":{"life":["Boethius was born around 477 CE into a leading Roman aristocratic family, just after the last Western Roman emperor was deposed. Italy was ruled by Ostrogothic kings, Germanic rulers who kept Roman institutions running and employed Roman nobles. Knowledge of Greek was fading in the West, and Boethius set out to preserve Greek learning for Latin readers. He would become one of the main channels through which ancient philosophy reached the Middle Ages.","Raised in the household of the senator Symmachus after his father's death, he became a brilliant scholar and statesman. He was consul in 510, and in 522 his two sons were made consuls together, a great honour. He became master of offices, a senior post under King Theodoric. Around 523 he was accused of treason, apparently after defending a senator. He was imprisoned in Pavia and executed, probably in 524, after writing The Consolation of Philosophy.","Boethius planned to translate all of Plato and Aristotle into Latin and show that they agreed. He completed translations and commentaries on Aristotle's logic, wrote textbooks on arithmetic and music, and composed short theological treatises. The Consolation, written in prison, mixes prose with poems and imagines Lady Philosophy visiting him in his cell to cure his grief. The book never mentions Christ, relying on reason alone to console its author, which has puzzled readers ever since."],"timeline":[[477,"Born in Rome into an aristocratic family (c.)"],[510,"Serves as consul"],[522,"His two sons become consuls; he is appointed master of offices"],[523,"Accused of treason and imprisoned in Pavia (c.)"],[524,"Writes The Consolation of Philosophy and is executed (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The wheel of Fortune","body":"Fortune gives and takes away; that is her nature. Lady Philosophy tells Boethius he cannot complain at losing wealth and status, since they were never truly his. Relying on luck means being at its mercy."},{"head":"True happiness","body":"People seek happiness in money, power, fame and pleasure, but each is partial and insecure. True happiness is the complete good, which Boethius identifies with God. Because it lies within, no tyrant can take it away."},{"head":"Foreknowledge and freedom","body":"If God knows everything we will do, how can we act freely? Boethius answered that God is eternal, outside time, and sees all moments at once as a present. Seeing you act does not force you to act, just as watching someone walk does not make them walk."},{"head":"What is a person?","body":"Boethius defined a person as an individual substance of a rational nature. This definition became the standard one in medieval philosophy and theology."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Book 2 of The Consolation of Philosophy, where Lady Philosophy explains the nature of Fortune, then Book 5 for his answer to the problem of foreknowledge and free will.","critics":"Many philosophers doubt that placing God outside time solves the problem: if God's knowledge is infallible, our choices still seem fixed. Arthur Prior and others also questioned whether a timeless God could know what is happening now.","legacy":"The Consolation was one of the most widely read books of the Middle Ages, translated into English by King Alfred, Chaucer and Elizabeth I. Boethius's translations were for centuries the West's main access to Aristotle's logic.","quote":null},"ockham":{"life":["William of Ockham was born around 1287, probably in the village of Ockham in Surrey, England. He lived in the late Middle Ages, when universities such as Oxford and Paris were centres of intense debate in logic and theology, and when popes and emperors fought bitterly over who held supreme power. The Franciscan order he joined was itself divided over whether Christ and the apostles had owned property.","He studied and lectured at Oxford, commenting on the standard theology textbook, Peter Lombard's Sentences, but never completed the degree of master; later writers called him the 'Venerable Inceptor'. In 1324 he was summoned to the papal court at Avignon to answer charges about his teaching. There he became involved in the dispute over Franciscan poverty and concluded Pope John XXII was a heretic. In 1328 he fled, joining Emperor Louis of Bavaria, and was excommunicated. He spent his last years in Munich and died there in 1347.","Ockham was a sharp logician who wrote large works on logic, physics and theology, including the Summa Logicae. After his flight he turned almost entirely to political writing, attacking papal claims to power over secular rulers. His style is precise and analytical, and he was willing to follow arguments to unpopular conclusions. He also had a talent for clearing away confused assumptions in the debates of his time."],"timeline":[[1287,"Born, probably in Ockham, Surrey (c.)"],[1317,"Lectures on the Sentences at Oxford (c.)"],[1323,"Completes the Summa Logicae (c.)"],[1324,"Summoned to the papal court at Avignon"],[1328,"Flees Avignon and joins Emperor Louis of Bavaria; excommunicated"],[1347,"Dies in Munich"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ockham's razor","body":"Do not suppose more things exist than you need to explain something. If two explanations account for the same evidence, prefer the one that requires fewer kinds of entities. Ockham used the principle often, though it was not new and the famous wording is not his."},{"head":"Only individuals exist","body":"There is no thing called 'humanity' that exists over and above individual humans. General words like 'human' are signs in our minds and language that stand for many individuals. This view is called nominalism."},{"head":"God's absolute power","body":"Ockham stressed that God can do anything that does not involve a contradiction. The world's order depends on God's free choice, not on necessity, so we must learn how things are by experience rather than by deduction alone."},{"head":"Limits on papal power","body":"In his political works Ockham argued that the pope's authority is spiritual and limited, and that it does not give him power over emperors or over people's property and rights. He also defended the right to resist rulers, including popes, who fall into error."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Ockham's razor says the simplest explanation is always true.","truth":"It is a guideline about not positing unnecessary entities when choosing between explanations; it does not guarantee that the simpler explanation is correct, and Ockham himself held that God could make the world more complex than reason requires."},"startHere":"Read a selection from the opening chapters of the Summa Logicae on terms and signs, in a translated anthology such as Philotheus Boehner's Ockham: Philosophical Writings, which also contains passages on universals.","critics":"His contemporary Walter Chatton proposed an 'anti-razor': if fewer things are not enough to explain the truth, we must posit more. Realists argued that nominalism cannot explain why different individuals really resemble one another.","legacy":"Ockham's nominalism shaped late medieval thought, known as the 'modern way' (via moderna), and his razor became a guiding principle of scientific reasoning. His political writings contributed to later ideas about limits on authority and individual rights.","quote":null},"mozi":{"life":["Mozi, or Mo Di, lived in China in the fifth century BCE, probably from about 470 to about 391 BCE, though his dates are uncertain. This was the early Warring States period, when rival states fought constantly and rulers spent lavishly on palaces, music and funerals while ordinary people suffered. Confucius had died shortly before, and his followers were teaching the importance of ritual and family loyalty.","Very little is known about his life. He may have come from the state of Lu or Song and from a background of craftsmen. He founded a movement, the Mohists, organised under strict leaders and committed to practical action. The Mohists became known as experts in defensive warfare who helped small states resist attack. A well-known story in the Mozi tells how he walked for days to the state of Chu and talked its king out of invading Song.","The book called the Mozi was written by his followers over a long period, and many chapters exist in three versions, perhaps from different branches of the school. Later chapters show the Mohists working on logic, optics and geometry. Mozi's style of argument is plain and systematic, building a case step by step and appealing to evidence. He often uses examples from crafts, such as the carpenter's square and compass, to show the value of clear standards."],"timeline":[[-470,"Born, perhaps in the state of Lu or Song (c.)"],[-430,"Active as a teacher and founder of the Mohist movement (c.)"],[-391,"Dies (c.)"],[-300,"Later Mohists write chapters on logic and science (c.)"],[-221,"Qin unifies China; Mohism afterwards fades as an organised school (c.)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Inclusive care","body":"Mozi taught jian ai, often translated as 'universal love' but better as 'inclusive care'. We should be concerned for other people's families and states as we are for our own. Much harm, he argued, comes from partiality: caring only for 'mine' at others' expense."},{"head":"Judge by benefit","body":"A practice is right if it benefits the people: if it feeds the hungry, clothes the cold and brings order. This makes Mozi one of the earliest thinkers to judge actions by their consequences for everyone."},{"head":"Against aggressive war","body":"If stealing a pig is wrong, Mozi asked, how can stealing a whole state be glorious? Offensive war kills and impoverishes, so it is one of the greatest crimes, whatever rulers call it."},{"head":"Against waste","body":"Mozi criticised elaborate funerals, long mourning periods and costly court music. These consumed resources that could feed people, so he judged them harmful, even though Confucians valued them."},{"head":"Three tests for a teaching","body":"To check whether a doctrine is correct, Mozi asked: does it fit the deeds of the wise kings of the past, does it fit what ordinary people see and hear, and does it bring benefit when put into practice?"}],"misconception":{"myth":"Mozi's 'universal love' means feeling the same warm affection for everyone.","truth":"Jian ai is mainly about practical concern for others' welfare and refusing to harm them for your own group's gain, not an equal emotional attachment to all people."},"startHere":"Read the chapters on 'Inclusive Care' (Jian Ai) and 'Against Aggression' (Fei Gong) in a modern translation such as Burton Watson's selection. They are short and show his method of argument clearly.","critics":"Mencius attacked Mozi's inclusive care, saying it amounted to treating one's own father as no different from a stranger. Xunzi complained that Mozi focused so much on usefulness that he ignored the value of culture and refinement.","legacy":"Mohism was a major rival to Confucianism for two centuries before it faded. Rediscovered by Chinese scholars in the late Qing period, Mozi is now studied as an early consequentialist and for the later Mohists' work in logic.","quote":null},"xunzi":{"life":["Xunzi, whose personal name was Xun Kuang, came from the state of Zhao in northern China and lived roughly from 310 to 235 BCE, though his dates are uncertain. This was the late Warring States period, when warfare between rival states was at its most brutal, shortly before the state of Qin conquered all the others. Thinkers of many schools competed to advise rulers on how to bring order.","According to the historian Sima Qian, Xunzi went to the Jixia Academy in the state of Qi, a famous gathering of scholars, where he was honoured three times as its leading figure. He later went to the state of Chu, where Lord Chunshen appointed him magistrate of Lanling. He lost the post when Lord Chunshen was assassinated in 238 BCE, and he remained in Lanling until his death.","Xunzi is unusual among early Chinese thinkers in having written essays himself, set out as connected arguments rather than recorded sayings. He engaged critically with almost every rival school, and especially with Mencius. Two of his students, Han Fei and Li Si, became leading figures of the Legalist school, and Li Si served as chief minister of Qin. His book of 32 chapters was edited and arranged by the Han dynasty scholar Liu Xiang."],"timeline":[[-310,"Born in the state of Zhao (c.)"],[-255,"Appointed magistrate of Lanling in Chu by Lord Chunshen (c.)"],[-238,"Loses his post after Lord Chunshen is assassinated"],[-235,"Dies in Lanling (c.)"],[-221,"His former student Li Si helps the Qin state unify China"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Human nature is bad","body":"Left to ourselves, we are born with desires for profit and pleasure and with envy. If people simply follow these, conflict and disorder result. Goodness is not natural to us; it comes from deliberate effort."},{"head":"Becoming good by effort","body":"Just as a warped board must be steamed and pressed to become straight, people must be shaped by teachers, rituals and study. Anyone, even an ordinary person in the street, can become a sage by sustained effort."},{"head":"Ritual solves conflict","body":"Resources are limited and desires are not. The ancient sages created ritual (li) to set out roles and share goods fairly, so that people's desires can be satisfied without fighting."},{"head":"Heaven is natural","body":"Xunzi described Heaven (tian) as the regular workings of nature, which do not respond to prayer. A drought is not a sign from Heaven; good government should prepare for it. Rituals like rain dances are valuable as culture, not because they cause rain."},{"head":"Getting names right","body":"Confusion in words leads to confusion in society. Rulers should fix the meanings of names so people can communicate clearly and laws can be followed."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Xunzi's claim that 'human nature is bad' means people are wicked and hopeless.","truth":"He meant that our raw desires lead to conflict if left untrained, and he was optimistic that anyone can become good, even a sage, through learning and effort."},"startHere":"Read the opening chapter, 'Encouraging Learning', and then chapter 23, 'Human Nature Is Bad', in a modern translation such as Eric Hutton's. Together they show both his view of human nature and his faith in education.","critics":"Critics have long asked where the first sages got the ability to create ritual if human nature is bad. Later Neo-Confucians such as Zhu Xi sided with Mencius against him and treated his view of human nature as a serious error.","legacy":"Xunzi was among the most influential Confucian thinkers of the Han dynasty, and his ideas on ritual and education shaped Chinese institutions. Long overshadowed by Mencius, he is now widely studied for his rigorous arguments and his naturalistic view of Heaven.","quote":null},"zhuxi":{"life":["Zhu Xi was born in 1130 in Fujian, in south-eastern China, during the Southern Song dynasty. Northern China had recently been lost to the Jurchen Jin state, and the Song court had retreated south. Buddhism and Daoism had long offered rich philosophical systems, and Confucian scholars of the eleventh century had begun building a revived Confucianism, Neo-Confucianism, to answer them.","He passed the highest civil service examination in 1148 at about 18. He then studied with the Confucian teacher Li Tong. Zhu held relatively few government posts, preferring teaching and writing, and he restored the White Deer Grotto Academy in 1179. In 1175 he compiled Reflections on Things at Hand with Lü Zuqian and debated the thinker Lu Jiuyuan at Goose Lake. Late in life his teaching was banned as 'false learning' (1196). He died in 1200.","Zhu Xi was an enormously productive scholar who wrote commentaries on the classics, letters and recorded conversations with students. He brought together the ideas of earlier Song thinkers into a single system. He selected the Analects, the Mencius, the Great Learning and the Doctrine of the Mean as the 'Four Books', the core of Confucian education. These texts became the foundation of education and of the civil service examinations for centuries."],"timeline":[[1130,"Born in Fujian, China"],[1148,"Passes the highest civil service (jinshi) examination"],[1175,"Compiles Reflections on Things at Hand; debates Lu Jiuyuan at Goose Lake"],[1179,"Restores the White Deer Grotto Academy"],[1189,"Completes his commentaries on the Four Books (c.)"],[1196,"His teaching is banned as 'false learning'"],[1200,"Dies in Fujian"],[1313,"The Yuan dynasty makes his commentaries the basis of the civil service examinations"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Principle and qi","body":"Everything is made of qi, a vital stuff that condenses and disperses. But each thing also has li, principle: the pattern that makes it what it is and how it ought to be. A bamboo plant's qi is its material; its li is the pattern of being bamboo."},{"head":"Investigating things","body":"To become wise, study things one by one, from the classics to everyday affairs and relationships, and grasp their principle. Over time, Zhu said, understanding suddenly connects, and you see the one principle running through all things."},{"head":"Human nature is principle","body":"Our original nature is good, because it is principle itself. But it is clouded by our qi, which may be murky or clear. Learning and self-cultivation clear away the murk, like polishing a dusty mirror."},{"head":"Reverent attention","body":"Zhu emphasised jing, a steady, reverent attentiveness in everything one does. Combined with study, it keeps the mind focused and makes moral growth possible."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Zhu Xi's 'investigation of things' was a programme of natural science like modern experiments.","truth":"His investigation was mainly aimed at moral understanding through study of texts, people and affairs, although it did include interest in the natural world."},"startHere":"Read selections from Reflections on Things at Hand in Wing-tsit Chan's translation. It was designed as an introduction for students and arranges key passages by topic.","critics":"Lu Jiuyuan and, later, Wang Yangming argued that principle is found in the mind itself, so Zhu's endless study of external things was fragmenting and could separate knowing from doing. Wang famously reported failing to find principle by staring at bamboo.","legacy":"Zhu Xi's version of Confucianism became state orthodoxy in China from the fourteenth century until 1905, through the civil service exams, and deeply shaped Korea, Japan and Vietnam. He is often ranked as the most influential Chinese thinker after Confucius.","quote":null},"wangyangming":{"life":["Wang Yangming, whose personal name was Wang Shouren, was born in 1472 in Yuyao, in Zhejiang province, during the Ming dynasty. By his time Zhu Xi's version of Confucianism was official orthodoxy, studied by every examination candidate. The imperial court was often dominated by powerful eunuchs, and officials who opposed them risked their careers and lives. Many scholars studied these classics mainly to pass the examinations and gain office, which Wang came to see as a betrayal of learning.","He passed the highest examination in 1499. In 1506 he protested against the eunuch Liu Jin, was flogged, and was banished to Longchang, a remote post in Guizhou. There, in 1508, he had a sudden insight that the principle he had sought in external things was in his own mind. He later served as a governor and military commander, putting down the rebellion of the Prince of Ning in 1519. He died in 1529 on the way home from a campaign.","Wang was both a scholar and a man of action, which matched his teaching that knowledge and action are one. He taught through conversations and letters, many collected by his students as Instructions for Practical Living. He described his own youthful attempt, with a friend, to investigate the principle of bamboo by sitting in front of it for days, until both fell ill."],"timeline":[[1472,"Born in Yuyao, Zhejiang"],[1499,"Passes the highest civil service (jinshi) examination"],[1506,"Imprisoned and flogged after opposing the eunuch Liu Jin; banished to Guizhou"],[1508,"Experiences his enlightenment at Longchang"],[1518,"First part of Instructions for Practical Living published by his students"],[1519,"Suppresses the rebellion of the Prince of Ning"],[1529,"Dies while returning from a military campaign"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The mind is principle","body":"Unlike Zhu Xi, Wang held that principle is not out there in things waiting to be discovered. It is in the mind. Filial piety is not found in your father; it is in the heart that cares for him."},{"head":"Knowing and acting are one","body":"Real knowledge already includes action. Someone who says they know they should respect their parents but never does so does not truly know it yet. Wang compared it to seeing a beautiful colour: you like it in the very act of seeing it."},{"head":"Innate good knowing","body":"Everyone has liangzhi, an inborn ability to know right from wrong, like a compass within. Even a thief feels ashamed when called a thief. Moral growth is mainly a matter of clearing away selfish desires that block it."},{"head":"Practice in daily life","body":"Wang taught that self-cultivation happens in the middle of ordinary work and duties, not by retreating from the world. A judge deciding cases or a farmer in the fields can practise extending their innate knowing."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The unity of knowing and acting means that thinking about something is the same as doing it.","truth":"Wang meant that genuine moral knowledge always shows itself in action, so if no action follows, the knowledge is not yet real."},"startHere":"Read the first part of Instructions for Practical Living (Chuanxilu), in Wing-tsit Chan's translation. The early conversations with his student Xu Ai explain the unity of knowing and acting with everyday examples.","critics":"Critics such as Luo Qinshun argued that Wang's focus on the mind came close to Chan (Zen) Buddhism and risked turning morality into whatever an individual feels is right. Later Chinese scholars blamed his followers for a decline into vague, subjective learning.","legacy":"Wang's teachings became one of the main schools of Ming thought and spread to Japan as Yōmeigaku, where they influenced reformers of the nineteenth century. He remains widely read in China today.","quote":null},"dogen":{"life":["Dōgen was born in 1200 in or near Kyoto, Japan, into an aristocratic family. This was the beginning of the Kamakura period, when warriors had taken real power from the imperial court and new forms of Buddhism were spreading. Many Japanese Buddhists believed they lived in a degenerate age when enlightenment was nearly impossible, and were looking for simpler, more direct paths.","He lost both parents as a child and was ordained as a monk on Mount Hiei, centre of the Tendai school, in 1213. Troubled by the question of why practice was needed if all beings already have Buddha-nature, he studied Zen and in 1223 sailed to China. Under the Chan master Rujing he experienced what he called 'dropping off body and mind'. Back in Japan from 1227, he taught near Kyoto, then in 1243 moved to remote Echizen, where he founded the monastery later named Eiheiji. He died in 1253.","Dōgen was a demanding teacher who insisted on strict monastic discipline and the practice of seated meditation. He was also a writer of unusual philosophical depth. Unlike most Japanese monks of his time, he wrote major works in Japanese rather than classical Chinese, often bending words and grammar to express his ideas. His essays were collected as the Shōbōgenzō. These are still read today both as religious instruction and as philosophy."],"timeline":[[1200,"Born in Kyoto, Japan"],[1213,"Ordained as a monk on Mount Hiei"],[1223,"Travels to China to study Chan (Zen) Buddhism"],[1227,"Returns to Japan and writes the meditation guide Fukanzazengi"],[1233,"Founds Kōshōji near Kyoto and writes Genjōkōan"],[1243,"Moves to Echizen province, where he founds the monastery later called Eiheiji"],[1253,"Dies in Kyoto"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Just sitting","body":"Dōgen taught shikantaza, 'just sitting': seated meditation without trying to reach any goal, solve a riddle or have a special experience. You simply sit fully and attentively."},{"head":"Practice is enlightenment","body":"Most people think of meditation as a means to the end of enlightenment. Dōgen rejected this split. Practice and realisation are one: sitting is not a road to Buddhahood but the activity of Buddhahood itself."},{"head":"Being-time","body":"In the essay Uji, Dōgen argued that time is not a container that things pass through. Each thing is its own time, and each moment is complete. A pine tree is time; you, right now, are time."},{"head":"Impermanence is Buddha-nature","body":"Buddha-nature is not a hidden, unchanging essence inside us. Dōgen reread traditional texts to say that impermanence itself, the constant change of all things, is Buddha-nature."},{"head":"Enlightenment in ordinary tasks","body":"In his instructions for the monastery cook, Dōgen treated washing rice and preparing meals as complete practice, done with full attention and care. Everyday work is not a distraction from the spiritual path."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Zen is anti-intellectual and rejects words and thinking altogether.","truth":"Dōgen wrote some of the most complex philosophical texts in Japanese, carefully reinterpreting Buddhist scriptures; he valued language when used to point beyond fixed ideas."},"startHere":"Read Genjōkōan (Actualising the Fundamental Point), a short essay from the Shōbōgenzō. It contains his famous lines that to study the self is to forget the self, and introduces his main themes.","critics":"Teachers in the rival Rinzai Zen school have long argued that sitting without kōan training and a decisive breakthrough experience can become passive or empty. Some modern scholars also note tensions between his early openness to lay practice and his later stress on monastic life.","legacy":"Dōgen is regarded as the founder of the Sōtō school, one of the two largest Zen schools in Japan. In the twentieth century his writings attracted philosophers such as those of the Kyoto School, and Western students of Zen.","quote":null},"reid":{"life":["Thomas Reid was born in 1710 in Strachan, Kincardineshire, Scotland, the son of a Church of Scotland minister. He lived during the Scottish Enlightenment, when Edinburgh, Glasgow and Aberdeen became centres of science and philosophy. Fellow Scots David Hume and Adam Smith were among its leading figures. Hume's sceptical philosophy, which questioned whether we can know an external world at all, was the challenge Reid spent his life answering.","He studied at Marischal College, Aberdeen, and in 1737 became minister at New Machar. He later wrote that reading Hume's Treatise of Human Nature made him question the philosophy he had accepted. In 1751 he became a regent at King's College, Aberdeen, and helped found the Aberdeen Philosophical Society. His Inquiry into the Human Mind appeared in 1764, when he succeeded Adam Smith as professor of moral philosophy at Glasgow.","Reid retired from teaching around 1780 to concentrate on writing, producing the Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man (1785) and the Essays on the Active Powers of Man (1788). He had wide scientific interests, including mathematics and optics. His style is patient, clear and often wryly humorous. He died in Glasgow in 1796. His Glasgow lectures covered not only the mind and ethics but also politics and law."],"timeline":[[1710,"Born in Strachan, Scotland"],[1737,"Becomes minister of New Machar"],[1751,"Appointed regent at King's College, Aberdeen"],[1764,"Publishes An Inquiry into the Human Mind; succeeds Adam Smith at Glasgow"],[1785,"Publishes Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man"],[1788,"Publishes Essays on the Active Powers of Man"],[1796,"Dies in Glasgow"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Against the 'way of ideas'","body":"Descartes, Locke, Berkeley and Hume all assumed that we never perceive things directly, only 'ideas' in our minds. Reid argued this assumption was never proved and leads straight to scepticism. Drop it, and the sceptical problem loses its grip."},{"head":"Direct perception","body":"When you see a tree, you perceive the tree itself, not a picture of it in your head. Sensations play a role, but they are signs that lead the mind directly to belief in the object. This is a form of direct realism."},{"head":"Principles of common sense","body":"Some beliefs, such as that the things we clearly remember really happened and that other people have minds, are built into our nature. We cannot help believing them, and philosophy should not demand proof of them before trusting them."},{"head":"Reason is not special","body":"Hume trusted reasoning more than perception and memory. Reid replied that all our faculties come from the same source; if we distrust perception without proof, we have no grounds to trust reason either."},{"head":"Real freedom","body":"Reid argued that we are genuine causes of our own actions, which he called active power. When you decide to raise your arm, you, not a prior chain of events, are the cause."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Reid's 'common sense' means trusting popular opinion or what most people happen to believe.","truth":"He meant a limited set of basic principles built into human nature, such as trust in perception and memory, not the views of the majority on any question."},"startHere":"Read chapter 1 (the introduction) of An Inquiry into the Human Mind, where he explains how Hume led him to question the 'way of ideas', then dip into the chapters on smell and sight.","critics":"Kant, in the preface to his Prolegomena (1783), accused Reid and his followers of missing Hume's real point and of appealing to common sense as a way of avoiding hard argument. Others have charged that his first principles simply assume what needs to be shown.","legacy":"Reid founded the Scottish School of Common Sense, which dominated philosophy in Scotland, France and American colleges in the nineteenth century. Since the late twentieth century his work has been revived in epistemology and philosophy of religion.","quote":null},"peirce":{"life":["Charles Sanders Peirce was born in 1839 in Cambridge, Massachusetts, the son of Benjamin Peirce, a leading Harvard mathematician. He grew up in the American scientific world after Darwin's Origin of Species (1859), when evolution, statistics and new laboratory methods were changing how people understood knowledge. Peirce combined training in science with deep study of logic and of philosophers from Kant to the medieval logicians.","He graduated from Harvard and earned a degree in chemistry. For about thirty years he worked for the US Coast Survey, making precise measurements of gravity. In the early 1870s he took part in the 'Metaphysical Club' in Cambridge with William James and others, where pragmatism was born. His essays The Fixation of Belief (1877) and How to Make Our Ideas Clear (1878) set it out. He lectured in logic at Johns Hopkins from 1879 until he was dismissed in 1884.","Peirce never held another academic post. In 1887 he moved to Milford, Pennsylvania, where he wrote enormous amounts on logic, signs and philosophy, much of it unpublished. He was difficult to work with and lived in growing poverty. When William James popularised 'pragmatism', Peirce renamed his own view 'pragmaticism', a word he said was ugly enough to be safe from kidnappers. He died in 1914."],"timeline":[[1839,"Born in Cambridge, Massachusetts"],[1863,"Graduates in chemistry from Harvard's Lawrence Scientific School"],[1872,"Takes part in the Metaphysical Club in Cambridge (c.)"],[1877,"Publishes The Fixation of Belief"],[1878,"Publishes How to Make Our Ideas Clear"],[1879,"Begins lecturing in logic at Johns Hopkins University"],[1887,"Moves to Milford, Pennsylvania"],[1914,"Dies in Milford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The pragmatic maxim","body":"To make an idea clear, ask what practical effects you expect it to have. Calling something 'hard' means, among other things, that many other substances will not scratch it. If two ideas lead to exactly the same expected effects, they mean the same thing."},{"head":"Four ways to fix belief","body":"People settle their beliefs by tenacity (clinging to what they already think), authority (believing what they are told), the 'a priori' method (believing what seems agreeable to reason), or science. Only the method of science lets the facts, not our wishes, decide."},{"head":"Truth as the end of inquiry","body":"Peirce defined truth as the opinion that would eventually be agreed on by everyone who investigates long enough. Truth is what careful, shared inquiry would settle on in the long run."},{"head":"Fallibilism","body":"We can never be absolutely certain that any belief is true. Science is self-correcting precisely because it is open to finding its mistakes. Peirce urged: do not block the way of inquiry."},{"head":"Signs and abduction","body":"Peirce classified signs into icons (resembling their object, like a map), indices (connected to it, like smoke to fire) and symbols (linked by convention, like words). He also described abduction: guessing the best explanation, as a detective does."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Pragmatism says a belief is true if it is useful or makes you feel good.","truth":"Peirce's maxim was about clarifying what ideas mean through their practical consequences; he rejected the looser popular versions and renamed his view 'pragmaticism' to separate it from them."},"startHere":"Read The Fixation of Belief (1877). It is short, lively and clear, and shows why Peirce thought the method of science was the best way to settle doubt.","critics":"Critics asked whether truth can really be what inquiry would agree on in the long run: some facts, such as the exact number of hairs on Julius Caesar's head, may be lost forever, yet there still seems to be a truth about them. Peirce himself wrestled with this problem.","legacy":"Peirce is recognised as the founder of pragmatism, which shaped American philosophy through William James and John Dewey. His work in logic and his theory of signs make him a founding figure of semiotics, and he is widely regarded as one of the most original American philosophers.","quote":{"text":"Consider what effects, that might conceivably have practical bearings, we conceive the object of our conception to have. Then, our conception of these effects is the whole of our conception of the object.","src":"How to Make Our Ideas Clear, section II","tr":"original English"}},"dewey":{"life":["John Dewey was born in 1859 in Burlington, Vermont, just before the American Civil War, and died in 1952 in New York. His long life ran from a country of small farms and towns to an industrial world power. Factories, cities and immigration were changing American life fast, and new mass public schools were being built. Darwin's theory of evolution was the big new idea of his youth, and it shaped his view that humans are living creatures adapting to their surroundings.","Dewey earned his doctorate at Johns Hopkins in 1884 and taught at Michigan before moving to the University of Chicago in 1894. There he founded the Laboratory School in 1896 to test his ideas about learning. In 1904 he moved to Columbia University in New York, where he stayed for the rest of his career. He wrote on almost everything: logic, ethics, art, religion, politics and schools. From 1919 to 1921 he lectured in Japan and China, where his ideas on education were widely discussed.","Dewey was a public philosopher as much as an academic one. He wrote for popular magazines, helped found organisations such as the American Association of University Professors, and took part in political causes. In 1937, aged 77, he chaired a commission in Mexico that examined the Moscow trials' charges against Leon Trotsky and found them baseless. His prose is often described as heavy going, even by admirers, but his central message was practical: ideas should be judged by what they do in real life."],"timeline":[[1859,"Born in Burlington, Vermont"],[1884,"Earns his doctorate at Johns Hopkins University"],[1896,"Opens the Laboratory School at the University of Chicago"],[1904,"Moves to Columbia University in New York"],[1916,"Publishes Democracy and Education"],[1919,"Begins two years of lecturing in Japan and China"],[1937,"Chairs the commission that examines the charges against Trotsky"],[1952,"Dies in New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Thinking as problem-solving","body":"For Dewey, thinking starts when a habit breaks down and we face a problem. We form a guess, test it in action, and keep what works. A mechanic diagnosing a strange engine noise is doing in small what scientists do in large."},{"head":"Learning by doing","body":"Children learn best through real activities, such as cooking, building or gardening, that connect to their own experience. Facts memorised for a test with no link to life are quickly forgotten. Dewey did not reject teaching knowledge; he wanted it to grow out of meaningful tasks."},{"head":"Democracy as a way of life","body":"Democracy is more than voting every few years. It is a way of living together in which people share problems, talk them through and learn from one another. Schools should be small democracies where children practise this."},{"head":"Ideas as tools","body":"Dewey called his view instrumentalism: ideas are tools for coping with the world, not copies of it. A good theory, like a good hammer, is one that does its job. This is his version of the pragmatism also found in Peirce and William James."},{"head":"The public and its problems","body":"Modern life creates problems, like pollution, that affect people who had no say in causing them. These affected people form a public that needs ways to recognise its shared interests and act on them. Dewey worried that mass society made such publics hard to form."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Dewey wanted schools where children simply do whatever they like.","truth":"In Experience and Education (1938) Dewey criticised progressive schools that dropped structure and guidance. He wanted teachers to plan experiences carefully so that they lead to real growth."},"startHere":"Experience and Education (1938) is short and clear, and shows Dewey correcting misreadings of his own educational ideas.","critics":"Bertrand Russell objected that Dewey's account of truth and inquiry swapped truth for usefulness, making knowledge depend too much on human purposes. Later education critics argued that 'child-centred' methods inspired by him weakened the teaching of basic knowledge.","legacy":"Dewey shaped modern ideas about active, project-based learning and about democracy as participation. Pragmatism, which he helped build, was revived in later American philosophy by thinkers such as Richard Rorty and Hilary Putnam.","quote":null},"husserl":{"life":["Edmund Husserl was born in 1859 in Prossnitz in Moravia, then part of the Austrian Empire and now Prostějov in the Czech Republic. He grew up in a Jewish family and later became a Lutheran. He lived through the great scientific advances of the late nineteenth century, when psychology was becoming a laboratory science and many thinkers hoped to explain logic and mathematics as facts about how the mind works. Husserl came to believe this was a serious mistake.","He began as a mathematician, earning a doctorate in Vienna in 1883, then studied philosophy with Franz Brentano. He taught at Halle, then at Göttingen from 1901, where his Logical Investigations (1900–1901) made his name. In 1916 he moved to Freiburg, where the young Martin Heidegger became his assistant and later his successor. After the Nazis came to power in 1933, Husserl, as a man of Jewish birth, was excluded from university life. He died in Freiburg in 1938.","Husserl worked slowly and kept starting over, and he often called himself a perpetual beginner. He wrote tens of thousands of pages in an old German shorthand, most of it unpublished in his lifetime. After his death a Belgian priest, Herman Van Breda, smuggled these manuscripts out of Nazi Germany to Leuven, where the Husserl Archives still publish them. Much of what scholars now know about his thought comes from these papers."],"timeline":[[1859,"Born in Prossnitz, Moravia"],[1883,"Earns a doctorate in mathematics in Vienna"],[1900,"Publishes the first volume of Logical Investigations"],[1913,"Publishes Ideas I, setting out his phenomenology"],[1916,"Becomes professor at Freiburg"],[1929,"Gives the Paris lectures that become Cartesian Meditations"],[1938,"Dies in Freiburg"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Back to the things themselves","body":"Husserl's founding idea, phenomenology, is the careful description of experience exactly as it shows up to us, before theories get added. Instead of asking what a melody 'really' is in physics, you describe how you hear it: notes that linger and notes you expect."},{"head":"Consciousness is always of something","body":"Every act of mind is directed at an object: you see a tree, fear a dog, remember a holiday. Husserl, following Brentano, called this intentionality. Studying the different ways the mind can be directed at things was his main project."},{"head":"Bracketing","body":"To describe experience well, Husserl suggested we 'bracket' the question of whether the world exists, simply setting it aside. This is not doubt, as in Descartes, but a change of focus: you look at how things appear rather than at what they are made of."},{"head":"Against psychologism","body":"Logic is not a description of how people happen to think. If it were, the laws of logic could change with human brains. Husserl argued that logical truths hold whatever any mind does, a view he shared with Frege."},{"head":"The lifeworld","body":"In late work Husserl argued that science grows out of the everyday world of lived experience, which he called the lifeworld. Forgetting this, he thought, led to a crisis in which science seems to tell us nothing about meaning or value."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Phenomenology is just introspection: looking inward and reporting your feelings.","truth":"Husserl aimed to describe the general structures of any experience, such as how objects appear from sides and how time is felt, not one person's private moods. He explicitly separated phenomenology from empirical psychology."},"startHere":"The Paris Lectures, Husserl's short summary of what became Cartesian Meditations, give the main ideas in a few dozen pages.","critics":"Heidegger, his own student, argued that we are first of all involved in the world through practical activity, so a detached consciousness describing appearances is the wrong starting point. Maurice Merleau-Ponty added that the 'bracketing' Husserl wanted can never be completed.","legacy":"Husserl founded phenomenology, which shaped Heidegger, Sartre, Merleau-Ponty and much of twentieth-century Continental philosophy. His descriptions of perception and time also influence present-day cognitive science.","quote":null},"frege":{"life":["Gottlob Frege was born in 1848 in Wismar, a port town on the Baltic coast of northern Germany. He grew up as Germany was being unified into a single empire. It was a golden age for German mathematics, but the foundations of the subject were shaky: mathematicians used numbers and proofs confidently without agreeing on what numbers are or what makes a proof valid. Frege set out to fix this.","He studied at Jena and Göttingen, earned his doctorate in 1873, and taught mathematics at the University of Jena for the rest of his career. In 1879 his short book Begriffsschrift ('concept-script') introduced a new logical notation far more powerful than anything since Aristotle. He spent decades trying to show that arithmetic could be built from logic alone. In 1902, as his main work was in press, Bertrand Russell wrote to him showing that one of his basic laws led to a contradiction.","Frege's work was largely ignored in his lifetime; few people could follow his unusual two-dimensional notation. He replied to Russell's letter quickly and honestly, admitting the problem was serious, which Russell later praised. His late diary, published long after his death, shows strongly nationalist and antisemitic political views, which have troubled later readers. He retired in 1918 and died in Bad Kleinen in 1925, with his influence only beginning to spread through Russell and Wittgenstein."],"timeline":[[1848,"Born in Wismar, northern Germany"],[1873,"Earns his doctorate at Göttingen"],[1879,"Publishes Begriffsschrift, founding modern logic"],[1884,"Publishes The Foundations of Arithmetic"],[1892,"Publishes 'On Sense and Reference'"],[1902,"Receives Russell's letter revealing a contradiction in his system"],[1925,"Dies in Bad Kleinen"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Modern logic","body":"Frege invented a logic with quantifiers, words like 'all' and 'some' handled precisely. It can capture sentences like 'everyone loves someone', which old logic could not. Almost all modern logic, and much of computer science, builds on this."},{"head":"Sense and reference","body":"'The Morning Star' and 'the Evening Star' name the same planet, Venus, but they present it differently. Frege called the object the reference and the way of presenting it the sense. This explains why 'the Morning Star is the Evening Star' can be a real discovery rather than a dull truth."},{"head":"Arithmetic from logic","body":"Frege tried to show that numbers and their laws can be defined using logic alone, a programme later called logicism. Russell's paradox showed that his version was inconsistent, but the attempt produced many lasting tools."},{"head":"Thoughts are not in the head","body":"The thought that 2 + 2 = 4 is not a private mental picture. It is the same thought for everyone who grasps it, and it is true whether or not anyone thinks it. Frege insisted on keeping logic separate from psychology."},{"head":"Meaning in context","body":"Frege advised asking for the meaning of a word only in the context of a whole sentence. Words contribute to the thought a sentence expresses; they are not labels to be studied one by one."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"'On Sense and Reference' (1892) is a short essay that introduces his most famous distinction with simple examples about planets and names.","critics":"Russell's paradox, sent in 1902, showed that Frege's Basic Law V allowed a contradiction, wrecking his system as it stood. Much later, Saul Kripke argued that names do not work through senses or descriptions at all.","legacy":"Frege is often called the founder of analytic philosophy and of modern logic. His ideas on meaning shaped Russell, Wittgenstein, Carnap and the whole study of language, and his logic underlies programming languages and formal proofs.","quote":null},"moore":{"life":["George Edward Moore was born in 1873 in Upper Norwood, a suburb of London. In the Britain of his youth, the leading philosophers were idealists who held that reality is in some sense spiritual or mental, and that ordinary beliefs about separate physical things are not strictly true. At Cambridge Moore, together with his friend Bertrand Russell, rebelled against this view and helped start what became analytic philosophy.","He studied at Trinity College, Cambridge, and won a fellowship in 1898. In 1903 he published both 'The Refutation of Idealism' and Principia Ethica. After some years away he returned to Cambridge to teach in 1911 and was professor of philosophy from 1925 to 1939. He edited the leading journal Mind from 1921 to 1947. His later work defended the beliefs of common sense, most famously in 'A Defence of Common Sense' (1925) and 'Proof of an External World' (1939).","Moore was known for his intense, patient questioning. He would ask exactly what someone meant by a claim and refuse to move on until it was clear. Principia Ethica deeply influenced the Bloomsbury Group, including John Maynard Keynes and Virginia Woolf, who took from it the idea that friendship and beauty are among the greatest goods. Wittgenstein admired the puzzle now called Moore's paradox. Moore died in Cambridge in 1958."],"timeline":[[1873,"Born in Upper Norwood, London"],[1898,"Elected a fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge"],[1903,"Publishes Principia Ethica and 'The Refutation of Idealism'"],[1921,"Becomes editor of the journal Mind"],[1925,"Publishes 'A Defence of Common Sense' and becomes professor at Cambridge"],[1939,"Gives the lecture 'Proof of an External World'"],[1958,"Dies in Cambridge"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The open question argument","body":"Suppose someone defines 'good' as 'pleasant'. We can still sensibly ask: 'This is pleasant, but is it good?' Moore argued that because the question stays open for any such definition, goodness cannot be defined in natural terms. He called the mistake of trying the 'naturalistic fallacy'."},{"head":"Goodness is simple","body":"Like the colour yellow, goodness cannot be broken into parts or explained to someone who does not grasp it. We recognise it directly, by intuition. This made Moore an early modern defender of objective moral truth."},{"head":"Here is one hand","body":"Against sceptics who say we cannot know an external world exists, Moore held up his hands and said, in effect, here is one hand and here is another. He argued he was more certain of this than of any premise in a sceptic's clever argument."},{"head":"Common sense first","body":"Many everyday beliefs, such as that the Earth has existed for many years and that other people have bodies, are known to be true. Philosophy's job is to analyse what they mean, not to overturn them."},{"head":"Moore's paradox","body":"Saying 'It is raining, but I don't believe it is raining' sounds absurd, yet both parts could be true together. Moore pointed out this puzzle about belief and assertion, which still interests philosophers of language."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The 'naturalistic fallacy' means wrongly assuming that whatever is natural is good.","truth":"Moore's naturalistic fallacy is the attempt to define 'good' in terms of any other property, natural or not, such as pleasure. The 'natural means good' mistake is a different error, usually called the appeal to nature."},"startHere":"'A Defence of Common Sense' (1925) shows Moore's careful, plain method in a single essay and is easy to follow.","critics":"William Frankena argued in 1939 that the 'naturalistic fallacy' simply assumes what it sets out to prove, since a naturalist will deny that goodness is a separate property. Many have also thought that 'here is one hand' just begs the question against the sceptic.","legacy":"Moore, alongside Russell, set the style of early analytic philosophy: clear questions, careful analysis and respect for common sense. His open question argument still frames debates about whether moral facts can be explained scientifically.","quote":null},"nishida":{"life":["Nishida Kitarō was born in 1870 in a village near Kanazawa, on the Sea of Japan coast. He grew up in the Meiji era, when Japan was rapidly adopting Western science, law and education after centuries of relative isolation. Japanese thinkers faced a pressing question: how to absorb Western philosophy without simply abandoning their own Buddhist and Confucian traditions. Nishida became the first Japanese thinker to build an original philosophical system that drew on both.","He studied philosophy at Tokyo Imperial University, graduating in 1894, then spent years teaching in high schools. During this period he practised Zen meditation seriously for about a decade. His first book, An Inquiry into the Good (1911), made him famous. He taught at Kyoto Imperial University from 1910 until he retired in 1928, and the circle of thinkers around him became known as the Kyoto School. He kept writing until his death in Kamakura in June 1945.","Nishida read widely in Western philosophy, including William James, Henri Bergson, Kant and Hegel, and reworked his own system several times. His later writing is notoriously dense. His life included much personal hardship, including the deaths of several of his children. His relationship to Japanese nationalism in the war years remains debated: some read his late political essays as supporting the regime, others as quiet attempts to moderate it."],"timeline":[[1870,"Born near Kanazawa, Japan"],[1894,"Completes his studies at Tokyo Imperial University"],[1910,"Begins teaching at Kyoto Imperial University"],[1911,"Publishes An Inquiry into the Good"],[1928,"Retires from Kyoto Imperial University"],[1940,"Awarded Japan's Order of Culture"],[1945,"Dies in Kamakura"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Pure experience","body":"Before we think 'I am hearing music', there is just the hearing: no separate 'I' and no separate 'music'. Nishida called this pure experience, taking the phrase from William James. Absorbed in playing a game or a piece of music, you may notice this state for yourself."},{"head":"Subject and object come later","body":"The split between a knower and a thing known is not basic. It is carved out of a prior unified experience. This turns around the common Western starting point, found in Descartes, of a mind facing a world."},{"head":"The logic of place","body":"In later work Nishida asked where experiences and things 'are'. His answer was basho, or place: a field in which things appear and relate. The deepest such place he called absolute nothingness, an idea with roots in Buddhist thought."},{"head":"Unity of opposites","body":"Nishida held that reality includes opposites, such as one and many or self and world, that define each other without dissolving. He called this the self-identity of absolute contradictories."},{"head":"Goodness as self-realisation","body":"In An Inquiry into the Good he argued that the good is the full development of the self, which in the end means overcoming the narrow ego and uniting with reality."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Part One of An Inquiry into the Good, on pure experience, is the most approachable part of his work and introduces the idea that drives everything else.","critics":"His colleague Tanabe Hajime criticised the logic of place from around 1930 as too contemplative and religious, arguing that it neglected history, society and the nation as real forces. After 1945 others criticised the Kyoto School for its wartime political writings.","legacy":"Nishida founded the Kyoto School, the most influential movement in modern Japanese philosophy, and showed that East Asian traditions could take part in global philosophical debate on equal terms. He remains central to comparative philosophy today.","quote":null},"ayer":{"life":["Alfred Jules Ayer was born in London in 1910 and died there in 1989. In the 1920s and 30s, science seemed to be advancing on every front, while philosophy, especially metaphysics and theology, struck many young thinkers as endless argument with no way of settling anything. In Vienna a group of philosophers and scientists, the Vienna Circle, proposed a radical cure: a statement only has meaning if experience could test it.","Ayer was educated at Eton and Christ Church, Oxford. In 1933 he spent time in Vienna attending the Circle's meetings, and in 1936, aged 25, he published Language, Truth and Logic, which brought these ideas to the English-speaking world. During the Second World War he served in the Welsh Guards and in intelligence work. He became Grote Professor at University College London in 1946 and Wykeham Professor of Logic at Oxford in 1959. He was knighted in 1970.","Ayer was a lively public figure, a regular broadcaster and debater. In 1949 he debated the existence of God with the Jesuit Frederick Copleston on BBC radio. He was candid about his own early work: in a 1978 television interview he said that its main defect was that nearly all of it was false. In 1988 he described a near-death experience in hospital, while saying it did not change his disbelief in an afterlife."],"timeline":[[1910,"Born in London"],[1933,"Attends meetings of the Vienna Circle"],[1936,"Publishes Language, Truth and Logic"],[1946,"Becomes Grote Professor at University College London"],[1956,"Publishes The Problem of Knowledge"],[1959,"Becomes Wykeham Professor of Logic at Oxford"],[1970,"Knighted"],[1989,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The verification principle","body":"A statement has factual meaning only if some observation could count for or against it. 'There is a cat on the mat' passes; 'the Absolute is beyond time' does not, since no experience could ever test it. Ayer used this as a test for nonsense."},{"head":"Two kinds of meaningful statement","body":"Besides testable claims, there are statements true by definition, such as 'all bachelors are unmarried' or the truths of maths and logic. They are certain but tell us nothing new about the world. Everything else, Ayer said, is literally meaningless."},{"head":"Metaphysics is nonsense","body":"Because claims about God, the soul or ultimate reality cannot be tested, Ayer argued they are neither true nor false, just meaningless. Philosophy should stop building systems and instead clarify language and science."},{"head":"Emotivism","body":"Saying 'stealing is wrong' does not state a fact. It expresses disapproval, rather like saying 'stealing!' in a shocked tone. Moral disputes are really clashes of attitude, not of evidence."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Ayer argued that God does not exist.","truth":"In Language, Truth and Logic Ayer argued that 'God exists' is meaningless, so the atheist's 'God does not exist' is equally meaningless. He was personally an unbeliever, but his argument rejected both sides."},"startHere":"Chapter 1 of Language, Truth and Logic, 'The Elimination of Metaphysics', is short, punchy and sets out the whole programme.","critics":"The most famous objection is that the verification principle fails its own test: it is neither true by definition nor checkable by observation. Alonzo Church also showed in 1949 that Ayer's revised version of the principle let almost any statement count as meaningful.","legacy":"Ayer made logical positivism widely known in Britain and America and set a model of clear, combative philosophical writing. Although few now accept the verification principle, his emotivism was an important step toward modern expressivist theories of ethics.","quote":null},"hayek":{"life":["Friedrich August von Hayek was born in Vienna in 1899, in the last years of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. He fought in the First World War on the Italian front and then watched the empire collapse, inflation wreck savings, and rival movements of fascism and communism rise. In this world many intellectuals believed that experts planning the economy from the centre would do better than markets. Hayek spent his life arguing they were wrong.","He earned doctorates in law and political science at the University of Vienna and joined the circle around the economist Ludwig von Mises. In 1931 he moved to the London School of Economics, where he debated John Maynard Keynes. The Road to Serfdom (1944) made him famous. He later taught at the University of Chicago from 1950, then at Freiburg in Germany. In 1974 he shared the Nobel Prize in economics with Gunnar Myrdal. He died in Freiburg in 1992.","Hayek worked across economics, law, psychology and political philosophy, which was unusual even then. In 1947 he founded the Mont Pelerin Society, a meeting place for liberal economists and thinkers. For decades his ideas were unfashionable among economists, who mostly followed Keynes. His reputation revived in the 1970s, and politicians such as Margaret Thatcher cited him as an influence, though he did not hold political office himself."],"timeline":[[1899,"Born in Vienna"],[1931,"Joins the London School of Economics"],[1944,"Publishes The Road to Serfdom"],[1945,"Publishes 'The Use of Knowledge in Society'"],[1947,"Founds the Mont Pelerin Society"],[1960,"Publishes The Constitution of Liberty"],[1974,"Shares the Nobel Prize in economics"],[1992,"Dies in Freiburg, Germany"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The knowledge problem","body":"The knowledge needed to run an economy is scattered among millions of people: a shopkeeper knows her customers, a farmer knows his soil. No central planner could gather it all. Hayek argued this is the main reason planned economies fail."},{"head":"Prices carry information","body":"When tin becomes scarce, its price rises, and people everywhere use less of it without needing to know why. Prices act like a signalling system that coordinates people who never meet. Markets, on this view, are mainly ways of sharing knowledge."},{"head":"Spontaneous order","body":"Many useful things, such as language, money, common law and markets, were not designed by anyone. They grew out of countless individual actions. Hayek warned against assuming that what nobody planned must be chaotic or improved by planning."},{"head":"The road to serfdom","body":"Hayek argued that once a government tries to direct the whole economy, it must override people's own choices more and more, which slides toward tyranny. He meant this as a warning about a tendency, not as a claim that every welfare policy leads to dictatorship."},{"head":"The rule of law","body":"Freedom depends on general, known rules that apply to everyone equally, rather than officials deciding case by case. Clear rules let people plan their own lives."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hayek opposed every kind of state welfare.","truth":"In The Road to Serfdom he accepted that the state should guarantee a basic minimum of food, shelter and clothing and help organise insurance against sickness and accident. His target was central planning of the economy, not all public provision."},"startHere":"'The Use of Knowledge in Society' (1945) is a short, clear essay that sets out his central idea about prices and scattered knowledge.","critics":"Market socialists such as Oskar Lange argued in the 1930s that planners could imitate markets by adjusting prices by trial and error. Many later critics have stressed that markets can also fail badly, as with pollution or financial crises, and can produce unjust inequality.","legacy":"Hayek's arguments about knowledge and prices are now standard in economics and shaped the free-market revival of the late twentieth century. He remains a central reference point for classical liberals and libertarians.","quote":null},"anscombe":{"life":["Elizabeth Anscombe was born in 1919 in Limerick, Ireland, to English parents, and grew up in London. She came of age as the Second World War began, and moral questions about war, killing and the bombing of civilians were urgent ones. In British philosophy at the time, ethics was mostly about analysing moral words, and the main live theories were forms of utilitarianism. Anscombe thought this whole approach had gone wrong.","As a teenager she became a Roman Catholic, and her faith shaped much of her work. She studied at St Hugh's College, Oxford, then went to Cambridge, where she became a student and close friend of Ludwig Wittgenstein. After his death she translated his Philosophical Investigations (1953) and was one of his literary executors. She published Intention in 1957 and 'Modern Moral Philosophy' in 1958. From 1970 to 1986 she held the professorship of philosophy at Cambridge that Wittgenstein had once held. She died in Cambridge in 2001.","Anscombe was famously forthright. In 1939, as a student, she co-wrote a pamphlet arguing that Britain's war did not meet the conditions of a just war. In 1956 she publicly opposed Oxford giving an honorary degree to former US President Harry Truman, arguing that ordering the atomic bombings made him a murderer. She was married to the philosopher Peter Geach. Her writing is compressed and demanding, but many of her phrases set agendas for decades."],"timeline":[[1919,"Born in Limerick, Ireland"],[1939,"Co-writes a pamphlet questioning the justice of Britain's war"],[1953,"Her translation of Wittgenstein's Philosophical Investigations is published"],[1956,"Opposes Oxford's honorary degree for Harry Truman"],[1957,"Publishes Intention"],[1958,"Publishes 'Modern Moral Philosophy'"],[1970,"Becomes professor of philosophy at Cambridge"],[2001,"Dies in Cambridge"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Intention and the question 'Why?'","body":"An action is intentional if the question 'Why are you doing that?' applies to it and gets a reason as an answer. If you are sawing a plank, you can say why; if you are also making a squeaking noise, you may not even know it. Anscombe used this to map out what intentions are."},{"head":"Actions under a description","body":"The same movement can be described as 'moving my arm', 'pumping water' and 'poisoning the household'. Whether it is intentional depends on the description. This idea now runs through philosophy of action and law."},{"head":"Modern moral philosophy lost its way","body":"Anscombe argued that words like 'morally ought' only make sense if there is a lawgiver, as in religious ethics. Without belief in such a lawgiver, she said, philosophers should drop that language and return to virtues, like justice and courage, and to human flourishing."},{"head":"Against consequentialism","body":"Anscombe coined the word 'consequentialism' for views that judge acts only by their results. She held that some acts, such as deliberately killing the innocent, are ruled out whatever the consequences."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"'Modern Moral Philosophy' (1958) is short and provocative, and it explains why so many later philosophers turned to virtue ethics.","critics":"Many secular philosophers reject her claim that moral obligation needs a divine lawgiver, arguing that obligation can rest on reason, autonomy or agreements between people, as in Kantian and contractualist theories. Consequentialists also reject her absolute prohibitions as ignoring outcomes that matter.","legacy":"Anscombe's 1958 paper is widely credited with starting the modern revival of virtue ethics, and Intention founded much of today's philosophy of action. Her work on double effect still shapes debates in medical and military ethics.","quote":null},"murdoch":{"life":["Iris Murdoch was born in Dublin in 1919 and grew up in London. As a young woman she lived through the Second World War and its aftermath, working for the Treasury and then for the United Nations relief agency helping refugees in Europe. In post-war Europe the excitement was existentialism, with its stress on free choice; in Oxford, philosophy focused on language and on moral judgements as choices or commands. Murdoch found both pictures thin.","She studied classics and philosophy at Somerville College, Oxford, and taught philosophy at St Anne's College, Oxford, from 1948 to 1963. Her first book, in 1953, was a study of Sartre. Her first novel, Under the Net, appeared in 1954, and she went on to write 26 novels, winning the Booker Prize in 1978 for The Sea, The Sea. Her key philosophical essays were collected as The Sovereignty of Good in 1970.","Murdoch kept her two careers separate in method but not in concern: both her novels and her philosophy are about how people see one another and how selfishness distorts that seeing. She drew heavily on Plato and on the French thinker Simone Weil. She was made a Dame in 1987. In her last years she developed Alzheimer's disease, and she died in Oxford in 1999."],"timeline":[[1919,"Born in Dublin"],[1948,"Becomes a philosophy fellow at St Anne's College, Oxford"],[1953,"Publishes Sartre: Romantic Rationalist"],[1954,"Publishes her first novel, Under the Net"],[1970,"Publishes The Sovereignty of Good"],[1978,"Wins the Booker Prize for The Sea, The Sea"],[1992,"Publishes Metaphysics as a Guide to Morals"],[1999,"Dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Attention","body":"Moral life is not only about big choices. It happens all the time in how we look at people. Paying patient, loving and honest attention to someone, rather than seeing them through our fears and vanity, is the core of goodness."},{"head":"The mother-in-law example","body":"Murdoch imagines a woman, M, who thinks her daughter-in-law, D, is vulgar. Without saying or doing anything different, M makes herself look again and comes to see D as refreshingly simple and spontaneous. Murdoch argues that M has done something morally important, even though no outward action changed."},{"head":"Unselfing","body":"Our ego constantly spins comforting fantasies. Anything that takes us out of ourselves helps: Murdoch describes looking out of a window while anxious and seeing a kestrel, and suddenly the brooding self vanishes. Art, nature and careful study all do this."},{"head":"The sovereignty of good","body":"Goodness is real and not just something we choose. Like the sun in Plato's cave, it is what lets us see things truly. Murdoch rejected the idea that values are simply created by the will."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Murdoch was a novelist who dabbled in philosophy on the side.","truth":"She taught philosophy at Oxford for fifteen years, and The Sovereignty of Good is still widely read and taught by moral philosophers in its own right."},"startHere":"'The Idea of Perfection', the first essay in The Sovereignty of Good, contains the mother-in-law example and is the clearest way into her thought.","critics":"Critics argue that her idea of a real, transcendent Good is metaphysically mysterious and hard to square with a scientific worldview. Others worry that stressing inner vision says too little about how to decide what to actually do in hard cases.","legacy":"Murdoch helped turn moral philosophy back toward character, perception and the inner life, and influenced virtue ethics and later thinkers on moral perception. Her novels remain widely read.","quote":null},"foot":{"life":["Philippa Foot was born Philippa Bosanquet in 1920 in Owston Ferry, Lincolnshire; her mother was a daughter of the American President Grover Cleveland. She studied at Somerville College, Oxford, during the Second World War. The news of the Nazi death camps shook her deeply. It made the then-fashionable view in Oxford, that moral judgements are just expressions of attitude, seem unable to say that the camps were truly and objectively wrong.","After working as an economist during the war, she taught at Somerville for many years. In 1967 she published a paper on abortion and the doctrine of double effect, in which she introduced the runaway tram example that became the trolley problem. In 1969 she moved to the United States, teaching at several universities and mainly at UCLA, where she held a chair until 1991. Her collection Virtues and Vices appeared in 1978, and her final book, Natural Goodness, in 2001.","Foot was known for asking blunt, simple questions that others had skipped over, and for patiently changing her mind when she thought she had been wrong. She was an early supporter of the charity Oxfam, founded in Oxford in 1942. She was close friends with Iris Murdoch and Elizabeth Anscombe, and the three are often discussed together. She died in Oxford in 2010, on her 90th birthday."],"timeline":[[1920,"Born in Owston Ferry, Lincolnshire"],[1958,"Publishes 'Moral Arguments' and 'Moral Beliefs', challenging the Oxford view of ethics"],[1967,"Introduces the trolley example in a paper on the doctrine of double effect"],[1972,"Publishes 'Morality as a System of Hypothetical Imperatives'"],[1978,"Publishes Virtues and Vices"],[2001,"Publishes Natural Goodness"],[2010,"Dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The trolley problem","body":"A tram driver whose brakes have failed can steer onto a track where one person will die instead of five. Most people think steering is allowed. Yet a judge may not frame and execute one innocent person to stop a riot that would kill five. Foot asked what explains the difference."},{"head":"Doing versus allowing","body":"Foot's answer was that our duty not to harm people is stronger than our duty to help them. The driver chooses between killing one and killing five; the judge would kill one rather than let five die. This distinction remains central in medical and legal ethics."},{"head":"Natural goodness","body":"We judge a plant or animal by what is normal for its kind: an oak with shallow roots is a defective oak. Foot argued that human virtues work the same way: courage, honesty and justice are what humans need to live well together, so a cowardly or cruel person is defective as a human."},{"head":"Moral words have content","body":"Against the view that anything at all could count as 'good' if someone approved of it, Foot argued that moral words are tied to human benefit and harm. You cannot sensibly call clasping your hands three times an hour a virtue without some story about why it helps."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Foot invented the trolley problem to show that we should pull a lever to save five people.","truth":"Her original case involved a tram driver and was part of an argument about abortion and the doctrine of double effect. The famous bystander-at-a-switch version and the name 'trolley problem' came later from Judith Jarvis Thomson."},"startHere":"Her 1967 essay 'The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect', reprinted in Virtues and Vices, is short and introduces the trolley case in its original setting.","critics":"Critics of Natural Goodness argue that facts about what is normal for humans cannot settle what is good, since evolution shaped many traits, such as aggression, that we would not call virtues. Others object that what counts as human flourishing is itself disputed.","legacy":"Foot gave philosophy the trolley problem, now studied in ethics, psychology and the design of self-driving cars. She was also a leading figure in the revival of virtue ethics and of the idea that morality is grounded in human nature.","quote":null},"nozick":{"life":["Robert Nozick was born in Brooklyn, New York, in 1938, the son of a Russian Jewish immigrant. He grew up in post-war America, a time of expanding government and the growing welfare state. In 1971 his Harvard colleague John Rawls published A Theory of Justice, which argued that a just society should arrange inequalities to benefit the worst off. Nozick wrote the most famous reply.","He studied at Columbia and earned his PhD at Princeton in 1963. He joined Harvard and became a full professor at 30. In his student days he had been on the political left, but he was won over by arguments for individual liberty. Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) won the National Book Award. He then turned away from political philosophy to write on knowledge, personal identity, free will, the meaning of life and rationality. He died of cancer in 2002.","Nozick was known for an exploratory, playful style. He liked to follow an argument wherever it went, offer many possible answers rather than one final system, and invent vivid thought experiments. He said he did not want to write the same book twice, and he did not: each book tackled a different area. He taught a famous range of courses at Harvard, reportedly rarely repeating the same one."],"timeline":[[1938,"Born in Brooklyn, New York"],[1963,"Earns his PhD at Princeton"],[1969,"Publishes his paper on Newcomb's problem; becomes full professor at Harvard"],[1974,"Publishes Anarchy, State, and Utopia"],[1981,"Publishes Philosophical Explanations"],[1989,"Publishes The Examined Life"],[2002,"Dies in Cambridge, Massachusetts"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The experience machine","body":"Imagine a machine that could give you any experiences you want, a perfect life as felt from inside, while you float in a tank. Would you plug in for life? Most people say no, which Nozick took to show that we care about actually doing things and being in touch with reality, not just about pleasant feelings."},{"head":"Rights as side constraints","body":"Individuals have rights that set limits on what anyone, including the state, may do to them, even to achieve good results. You may not take one person's kidney to save two others. People are not resources for others' goals."},{"head":"Justice as entitlement","body":"A distribution is just if it came about through fair acquisition and voluntary exchange, whatever its final pattern. In his Wilt Chamberlain example, fans freely pay to watch a basketball star, who becomes rich. Nozick asks what could be unjust about that."},{"head":"The minimal state","body":"Only a 'night-watchman' state that protects against force, theft and fraud and enforces contracts can be justified. Taxing income to redistribute it is, Nozick argued, on a par with forced labour."},{"head":"A framework for utopia","body":"People want very different ideal lives. The minimal state allows many voluntary communities to exist side by side, and people can join the one that suits them."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Nozick later fully renounced libertarianism.","truth":"In The Examined Life (1989) he wrote that the libertarian position he once put forward now seemed seriously inadequate, but in later interviews he said he had not abandoned it as much as people thought. Mostly he moved on to other subjects."},"startHere":"The short passage on the experience machine in chapter 3 of Anarchy, State, and Utopia is a few pages long and one of the most discussed thought experiments in philosophy.","critics":"G. A. Cohen argued that Nozick's case rests on self-ownership and on how unowned land first became private property, both of which he challenged. Thomas Nagel complained that the book offered libertarianism without foundations, assuming strong property rights rather than arguing for them.","legacy":"Anarchy, State, and Utopia remains the main philosophical defence of libertarianism and the standard counterweight to Rawls in teaching about justice. The experience machine is a fixture of discussions about well-being.","quote":null},"thomson":{"life":["Judith Jarvis Thomson was born in New York City in 1929. She began publishing on ethics at a time when the United States was fiercely debating abortion, civil rights and the Vietnam War, and when philosophers were turning back from purely abstract questions toward urgent practical ones. The new journal Philosophy and Public Affairs, founded in 1971, was a sign of this change, and her most famous paper appeared in its first issue.","She studied at Barnard College and at Cambridge, then earned her PhD at Columbia in 1959. She taught at Barnard and from 1964 at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where she spent the rest of her career. 'A Defense of Abortion' appeared in 1971. In papers from 1976 and 1985 she named and developed the trolley problem. Her main theoretical book, The Realm of Rights, followed in 1990. She died in 2020.","Thomson's style was crisp, witty and direct, built around inventive imaginary cases. She was willing to say when she had changed her mind: in 'Turning the Trolley' (2008) she rejected the answer she had given to her own trolley case decades earlier. She also wrote on metaphysics, including the nature of actions and events, and on goodness and the nature of normative words."],"timeline":[[1929,"Born in New York City"],[1959,"Earns her PhD at Columbia University"],[1964,"Joins the Massachusetts Institute of Technology"],[1971,"Publishes 'A Defense of Abortion'"],[1976,"Coins the name 'the trolley problem' in 'Killing, Letting Die, and the Trolley Problem'"],[1990,"Publishes The Realm of Rights"],[2008,"Publishes 'Turning the Trolley', changing her earlier view"],[2020,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The violinist","body":"You wake up to find a famous violinist plugged into your kidneys; he will die if you unplug him in the next nine months. Thomson argued you are not morally required to stay plugged in, even though he has a right to life. A right to life is not a right to use someone else's body."},{"head":"Grant the premise, still argue","body":"Thomson granted, for the sake of argument, that the foetus is a person from conception. She then argued that abortion can still be permissible in many cases. This moved the debate away from the single question of when personhood begins."},{"head":"The trolley problem","body":"Thomson changed Foot's tram driver into a bystander at a switch and added the 'fat man' on a footbridge who could be pushed to stop the trolley. Most people would flip the switch but not push. She asked why, and the question has driven decades of research."},{"head":"Rights have structure","body":"In The Realm of Rights she analysed what it is to have a claim against someone, and when claims can be overridden. Infringing a right is not always wrong, but it may leave you owing compensation."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The violinist argument works by assuming that a foetus is not a person.","truth":"Thomson explicitly assumed that the foetus is a person with a right to life, to show that abortion could be defended even on that assumption."},"startHere":"'A Defense of Abortion' (1971) is short, lively and full of memorable cases; it is one of the most reprinted essays in modern philosophy.","critics":"John Finnis replied in 1973 that unplugging the violinist is letting die, while abortion usually involves killing, and that the cases differ morally. Many others argue the violinist only fits pregnancy from rape, since in voluntary sex the woman bears some responsibility for the foetus's need.","legacy":"Thomson's abortion essay is the most widely taught philosophical paper on the subject, and her versions of the trolley problem are the ones most people know. Her method of testing principles against carefully built cases became standard in applied ethics.","quote":null},"williams":{"life":["Bernard Williams was born in 1929 in Westcliff-on-Sea, Essex. He studied classics and philosophy at Balliol College, Oxford, and did national service in the Royal Air Force, flying Spitfires in Canada. In mid-century Britain moral philosophy was dominated by tidy theories, especially utilitarianism, which says to maximise good outcomes, and Kantian views built on universal rules. Williams spent his career arguing that real moral life cannot be squeezed into such systems.","He held posts in Oxford and London before becoming Knightbridge Professor of Philosophy at Cambridge in 1967, then Provost of King's College, Cambridge, from 1979 to 1987. He later taught at Berkeley and returned to Oxford as White's Professor of Moral Philosophy in 1990. He chaired a government committee on obscenity and film censorship, which reported in 1979. His key books include Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973), Moral Luck (1981) and Ethics and the Limits of Philosophy (1985). He was knighted in 1999.","Williams was known for his speed of mind, wit and range: he wrote on ancient Greek ethics, Descartes, personal identity, politics and opera. He thought philosophy should connect with history, literature and how people actually live, and was suspicious of philosophers who reduce ethics to calculation. His last major book, Truth and Truthfulness (2002), defended the value of truth against fashionable scepticism. He died in Rome in 2003."],"timeline":[[1929,"Born in Westcliff-on-Sea, Essex"],[1967,"Becomes Knightbridge Professor of Philosophy at Cambridge"],[1973,"Publishes Utilitarianism: For and Against with J. J. C. Smart"],[1979,"Becomes Provost of King's College, Cambridge"],[1981,"Publishes Moral Luck"],[1985,"Publishes Ethics and the Limits of Philosophy"],[1999,"Knighted"],[2003,"Dies in Rome"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The integrity objection","body":"Jim is told that if he shoots one villager, nineteen others will go free. Utilitarianism says the answer is obvious. Williams argued that it treats Jim as a mere channel for producing outcomes and ignores that it would be his own act, which threatens a person's integrity."},{"head":"Moral luck","body":"Two drivers are equally careless, but a child runs in front of only one of them. We judge that one far more harshly, though the difference was luck. Williams, along with Thomas Nagel, showed how luck shapes moral judgement in ways our theories ignore."},{"head":"One thought too many","body":"If a man can save either his wife or a stranger from drowning, he should not need to check that impartial morality permits saving his wife. Thinking 'it's my wife, and in such cases it is allowed' is one thought too many. Love and personal projects give reasons of their own."},{"head":"Internal reasons","body":"Williams argued that you only have a reason to do something if it connects, through sound thinking, with something you already care about. This challenged the idea that morality gives everyone reasons regardless of what they want."},{"head":"Against the morality system","body":"Williams criticised what he called 'morality', a narrow, blame-centred picture of ethics built on obligation. He preferred the older, broader question of how one should live, and concepts like courage, cruelty and shame."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Williams was a moral relativist who thought any culture's values are as good as any other's.","truth":"He called simple relativism, the view that it is wrong to criticise other cultures, incoherent. He defended only a limited 'relativism of distance' for societies so remote from ours that their outlook is not a real option for us."},"startHere":"Williams's essay in Utilitarianism: For and Against, especially the section with the examples of George and Jim, is lively and shows his style of argument.","critics":"Christine Korsgaard and John McDowell argued against his internal reasons view, holding that people can have reasons, including moral ones, that do not depend on their existing desires. Defenders of moral theory reply that without some systematic principles, ethics has no way to settle disagreements.","legacy":"Williams changed the shape of moral philosophy by making moral luck, integrity and the limits of theory central topics. Many philosophers who reject his conclusions still treat his objections as the ones any theory must answer.","quote":null},"nagel":{"life":["Thomas Nagel was born in 1937 in Belgrade, Yugoslavia, and came to the United States as a child. He belongs to the generation of American philosophers who reshaped analytic philosophy in the 1960s and 70s, when the field was moving beyond the study of language to tackle big questions directly: the mind, the meaning of life, death, and justice. Nagel became known for asking those questions in clear, non-technical prose.","He studied at Cornell, at Oxford, and at Harvard, where he earned his PhD in 1963 under John Rawls. He taught at Berkeley and Princeton before moving to New York University in 1980, where he spent most of his career. His essay 'What Is It Like to Be a Bat?' (1974) became one of the most cited papers in philosophy of mind. The View from Nowhere (1986) drew his themes together. Mind and Cosmos (2012) caused fierce controversy.","Nagel's work keeps returning to one tension: the clash between how things seem from a particular point of view and how they look from an impersonal, objective standpoint. He applies this to consciousness, ethics, free will and the meaning of life. He also wrote one of the best-known short introductions to philosophy, What Does It All Mean? (1987), and many essays for general readers."],"timeline":[[1937,"Born in Belgrade, Yugoslavia"],[1963,"Earns his PhD at Harvard"],[1970,"Publishes The Possibility of Altruism"],[1974,"Publishes 'What Is It Like to Be a Bat?'"],[1980,"Joins New York University"],[1986,"Publishes The View from Nowhere"],[2012,"Publishes Mind and Cosmos"]],"ideas":[{"head":"What it is like","body":"A creature is conscious if there is something it is like to be that creature. Bats find their way by echolocation, and we can study every detail of their brains, yet still not know what it is like for the bat. Physical science, which describes things from outside, seems to leave this out."},{"head":"The view from nowhere","body":"We can step back from our own viewpoint and try to see the world impersonally, as science does. But some things, like experience and the value of our own lives, only show up from a particular viewpoint. Nagel argues we must find room for both."},{"head":"The absurd","body":"Life feels absurd when we step back and see our serious projects as tiny and arbitrary, yet go on taking them seriously. Nagel thinks this clash is unavoidable for creatures who can view themselves from outside, and that it is better met with irony than despair."},{"head":"Moral luck","body":"We are judged for things that depend on factors outside our control, such as the results of our actions or the circumstances we happen to face. Nagel, along with Bernard Williams, showed how this sits uneasily with the idea that we are only responsible for what we control."},{"head":"Doubts about materialism","body":"In Mind and Cosmos Nagel argued that standard neo-Darwinian materialism cannot fully explain consciousness, reason and value, and that a new kind of natural explanation is needed."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The bat paper argues that consciousness is something non-physical.","truth":"Nagel said physicalism might well be true; his claim was that we do not yet have the concepts to understand how it could be true of experience."},"startHere":"What Does It All Mean? (1987) is a very short introduction to nine big questions in philosophy, written for complete beginners.","critics":"Daniel Dennett argued that we can learn a great deal about what it is like to be a bat from science, and that Nagel overstates the mystery. Mind and Cosmos drew sharp criticism from many philosophers and biologists, who argued that he underrated evolutionary explanations.","legacy":"Nagel's bat paper set the terms for later debates about consciousness, including David Chalmers's 'hard problem'. His writing on the absurd, luck and objectivity is widely read outside academic philosophy.","quote":null},"frankfurt":{"life":["Harry Frankfurt was born in 1929 in Langhorne, Pennsylvania, and worked in the American analytic tradition through the second half of the twentieth century. In the 1960s the free will debate was stuck on a familiar idea: you are only responsible for what you did if you could have done otherwise. Frankfurt challenged that assumption with a single short paper, and then went on to ask what it means for a person to care about anything at all.","He earned his PhD at Johns Hopkins University in 1954. He taught at several institutions, including Ohio State University and Rockefeller University, then at Yale from 1976 and at Princeton from 1990 until he retired in 2002. His papers of 1969 and 1971 on responsibility and freedom of the will are among the most discussed in the field. His essay 'On Bullshit', first published in 1986, became a surprise bestseller when reissued as a small book in 2005. He died in 2023.","Frankfurt wrote in short, clear essays rather than large systems, and his later work became more personal in theme: love, caring and what makes a life worth living. He was also a serious scholar of Descartes, publishing Demons, Dreamers, and Madmen in 1970. His late books, such as The Reasons of Love (2004), On Truth (2006) and On Inequality (2015), were written for general readers."],"timeline":[[1929,"Born in Langhorne, Pennsylvania"],[1954,"Earns his PhD at Johns Hopkins University"],[1969,"Publishes 'Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility'"],[1971,"Publishes 'Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person'"],[1986,"Publishes the essay 'On Bullshit'"],[1990,"Joins Princeton University"],[2005,"'On Bullshit' becomes a bestseller as a short book"],[2023,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Frankfurt cases","body":"Imagine a neuroscientist has secretly wired up Jones's brain so that if Jones were about to decide not to vote for a candidate, the device would make him vote anyway. But Jones votes for that candidate on his own, so the device never acts. Jones could not have done otherwise, yet he still seems responsible. Frankfurt concluded that responsibility does not require alternatives."},{"head":"Desires about desires","body":"Humans can want to have or not have their own desires. A smoker may want a cigarette while also wanting not to want one. Frankfurt called these second-order desires and argued that being a person, rather than just a creature with wants, involves having them."},{"head":"Freedom of the will","body":"You have free will when the desire that actually moves you is the one you want to move you. An addict who hates his craving but gives in lacks free will in this sense; one who welcomes it may not. This view is compatible with determinism."},{"head":"Bullshit versus lying","body":"A liar cares about the truth enough to hide it. A bullshitter does not care whether what he says is true or false; he just wants to create an impression. Frankfurt argued this indifference makes bullshit a greater enemy of truth than lies are."},{"head":"Enough, not equal","body":"In On Inequality Frankfurt argued that equality itself is not what matters morally. What matters is that everyone has enough to live a decent life. This view is often called sufficientarianism."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"On Bullshit (2005) is a few dozen pages long, funny and clear, and shows how careful analysis can illuminate an everyday notion.","critics":"Defenders of the 'could have done otherwise' principle, such as David Widerker and Robert Kane, argued that Frankfurt cases only work if we assume determinism or a reliable 'sign' before the choice, so they beg the question. Gary Watson argued that higher-order desires cannot settle what is truly one's own, since they too might be alien to the self.","legacy":"Frankfurt cases reshaped the free will debate, and his hierarchical account of the self remains a starting point for theories of autonomy. 'On Bullshit' gave philosophers and the public a useful term for analysing modern political and commercial speech.","quote":null},"parfit":{"life":["Derek Parfit was born in 1942 in Chengdu, China, where his parents were working as medical missionaries; the family returned to England the following year. He grew up in post-war Britain and studied modern history at Balliol College, Oxford. He turned to philosophy in the mid-1960s, when British moral philosophy was beginning to take on concrete questions about personal identity, the future and our obligations to others.","In 1967 he was elected to a fellowship at All Souls College, Oxford, a research college with no undergraduates, and he remained there for almost his whole career. He also taught regularly as a visiting professor at Harvard, New York University and Rutgers. His first book, Reasons and Persons (1984), covered rationality, personal identity and future generations. His second, On What Matters, appeared in two volumes in 2011 and a third in 2017. He died in London on 1 January 2017.","Parfit was famous for his total dedication to his work. He circulated drafts widely, invited criticism from dozens of philosophers, and revised endlessly in response. He also had a serious interest in photography, repeatedly photographing buildings in Venice and St Petersburg. Colleagues describe him as gentle and intensely serious. He thought that if he could show that ethics has objective answers, it would matter for how humanity faces its future."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in Chengdu, China"],[1967,"Elected a fellow of All Souls College, Oxford"],[1971,"Publishes 'Personal Identity'"],[1984,"Publishes Reasons and Persons"],[2011,"Publishes the first two volumes of On What Matters"],[2014,"Awarded the Rolf Schock Prize in logic and philosophy"],[2017,"Dies in London; the third volume of On What Matters appears"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The teleporter","body":"A machine scans you, destroys your body, and builds an exact copy on Mars, with all your memories. Is the copy you? Parfit argued there may be no deeper fact to settle this, and that what matters is the psychological connection, not whether it is 'really' the same person."},{"head":"Identity is not what matters","body":"Parfit argued that personal identity is less important than we think. If a process kept your memories, character and plans going, it would be nearly as good as survival, even if strictly you did not survive. He found this liberating: the wall between himself and others seemed thinner."},{"head":"The non-identity problem","body":"If we use up resources now, the people born in a century will be different individuals from those who would have been born otherwise. They cannot complain that we made them worse off, since they would not otherwise exist. Yet the policy still seems wrong, and Parfit asked why."},{"head":"The Repugnant Conclusion","body":"Some theories imply that a vast population whose lives are barely worth living would be better than a smaller, very happy one. Parfit found this unacceptable, but showed it is very hard to avoid without other strange results."},{"head":"Climbing the same mountain","body":"In On What Matters Parfit argued that Kantian, contractualist and consequentialist ethics, properly developed, largely agree. Like climbers on different sides of a mountain, they reach the same summit."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Parfit claimed that persons do not really exist.","truth":"Parfit held that persons do exist, but that their existence consists in physical and mental facts, such as connected memories and intentions, rather than in a separate soul or self."},"startHere":"Chapter 10 of Reasons and Persons, 'What We Believe Ourselves to Be', opens with the teleporter case and is gripping even for beginners.","critics":"Christine Korsgaard argued in 1989 that personal identity is grounded in the unity of agency, since we must act as one self, rather than in psychological links alone. On population ethics, many philosophers argue that some of the intuitions Parfit treated as fixed should be given up.","legacy":"Reasons and Persons transformed debates on personal identity and did much to create the field of population ethics, which now shapes thinking about climate change and the long-term future. Parfit is a major influence on the effective altruism movement.","quote":null},"mackie":{"life":["John Leslie Mackie was born in Sydney, Australia, in 1917. He studied at the University of Sydney under John Anderson, a forceful philosopher known for his realism, atheism and dislike of mystery. This tough-minded Australian style marked Mackie for life. He went on to Oriel College, Oxford, and served in the army during the Second World War. He worked in the decades when British and Australian philosophers aimed for plain, careful argument on traditional problems.","After the war he taught at Sydney, then became professor at the University of Otago in New Zealand in 1955, returned to Sydney as professor in 1959, and moved to the University of York in England in 1963. From 1967 he was a fellow of University College, Oxford. His books include The Cement of the Universe (1974) on causation, Problems from Locke (1976), and Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977). He died in Oxford in 1981, and The Miracle of Theism appeared the next year.","Mackie wrote with notable clarity and fairness. He set out opposing views at their strongest before criticising them, which is one reason his books are still assigned to students. His 1955 paper 'Evil and Omnipotence' argued that belief in an all-good, all-powerful God is logically inconsistent with the existence of evil, and it shaped the philosophy of religion for decades."],"timeline":[[1917,"Born in Sydney, Australia"],[1955,"Publishes 'Evil and Omnipotence'; becomes professor at Otago"],[1963,"Moves to the University of York"],[1967,"Becomes a fellow of University College, Oxford"],[1974,"Publishes The Cement of the Universe"],[1977,"Publishes Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong"],[1981,"Dies in Oxford"],[1982,"The Miracle of Theism is published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Error theory","body":"When people say 'cruelty is wrong', they mean to state an objective fact. But Mackie argued there are no such facts. So ordinary moral claims all share a mistake, much as claims about witches did. That is why his view is called an error theory."},{"head":"The argument from queerness","body":"Objective values would be very strange things: facts that by themselves tell anyone, whatever they want, what to do. We would also need a strange special faculty to detect them. Mackie thought it more reasonable to conclude that they do not exist."},{"head":"The argument from relativity","body":"Moral codes vary widely between societies and eras. Mackie argued this is better explained by different ways of life than by some groups seeing objective moral truths more clearly than others."},{"head":"Morality is invented","body":"Even without objective values, we still need rules so that people can live and cooperate together. Mackie argued we should consciously build a sensible morality to do that job, as the subtitle of his book suggests."},{"head":"The problem of evil","body":"If God is all-powerful and wholly good, why is there evil? Mackie argued that these beliefs cannot all be consistently held, and that the usual escapes, such as free will, do not work."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Because Mackie denied objective values, he thought anything goes and morality does not matter.","truth":"More than half of Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong develops a practical morality he thought people had good reason to adopt. He denied that moral rules are objective facts, not that they are useful or important."},"startHere":"Chapter 1 of Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong, 'The Subjectivity of Values', sets out his error theory and both main arguments in a few clear pages.","critics":"A common reply, developed by Terence Cuneo and others, is the companions-in-guilt argument: reasons for belief are just as 'queer' as moral reasons, so if Mackie's argument works it also undermines rational belief. Against his problem of evil, Alvin Plantinga's free will defence is widely thought to show that God and evil are not strictly inconsistent.","legacy":"Mackie's error theory is the standard reference point for moral anti-realism, and his writings on God and evil remain staples of philosophy of religion courses.","quote":null},"kripke":{"life":["Saul Kripke was born in 1940 in Bay Shore, New York, and grew up in Omaha, Nebraska. He worked in the analytic tradition when the dominant view, going back to Frege and Russell, held that names work like hidden descriptions, and when many philosophers, following W. V. O. Quine, were suspicious of talk about necessity and possibility. Kripke overturned both positions.","He was a prodigy in logic: while still a teenager he proved results about modal logic, the logic of 'necessarily' and 'possibly', published in 1959. He studied at Harvard, graduating in 1962, without earning a doctorate. In January 1970 he gave three lectures at Princeton, published in 1972 and as the book Naming and Necessity in 1980. He taught at Rockefeller University and at Princeton from 1977 to 1998, and later at the City University of New York Graduate Center. He died in 2022.","Kripke famously lectured without notes. Naming and Necessity was transcribed from tape recordings of talks he gave largely from memory. He published relatively little in his lifetime, and much of his influence spread through lectures, seminars and circulating manuscripts. He received the Rolf Schock Prize in 2001. His work combined the precision of a logician with a talent for simple, striking examples, such as asking whether Aristotle could have failed to teach Alexander. Many of his later lectures, on topics such as truth and reference to fictional things, were published only toward the end of his life."],"timeline":[[1940,"Born in Bay Shore, New York"],[1959,"Publishes a completeness proof in modal logic as a teenager"],[1962,"Graduates from Harvard"],[1970,"Gives the Naming and Necessity lectures at Princeton"],[1980,"Naming and Necessity is published as a book"],[1982,"Publishes Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language"],[2001,"Awarded the Rolf Schock Prize"],[2022,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Names as rigid designators","body":"The name 'Aristotle' picks out the same person in every possible situation, even ones where he never taught Alexander or wrote any books. Kripke called such words rigid designators. A description like 'the teacher of Alexander' is not rigid: in other situations it could pick out someone else."},{"head":"Names are passed along","body":"If most people only know Gödel as the man who proved incompleteness, and that turned out to be someone else's work, 'Gödel' would still refer to Gödel. Names refer through a chain of use passed from person to person back to an original naming, not through the descriptions in our heads."},{"head":"Necessary truths we discover","body":"Water is H2O. Kripke argued this is necessary: anything that is not H2O is not water. Yet it took scientists to discover it. This split apart two ideas many had treated as one: what must be true and what can be known without experience."},{"head":"Rule-following puzzle","body":"Kripke drew from Wittgenstein a sceptical puzzle: nothing in your past behaviour seems to fix that by 'plus' you meant addition rather than some strange rule that gives different answers for large numbers. He suggested meaning depends on community practice. Readers often call this view 'Kripkenstein'."},{"head":"Against mind-brain identity","body":"If pain just were a certain brain state, it would be necessarily so. Yet it seems we can imagine one without the other. Kripke used this to challenge identity theories of the mind."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Lecture I of Naming and Necessity is conversational, because it was transcribed from a talk, and introduces rigid designators with simple examples.","critics":"Gareth Evans pointed out in 1973 that the name 'Madagascar' originally referred to part of the African mainland and shifted to the island through a mistake, which a simple causal chain theory cannot explain. Many philosophers also reject his argument against mind-brain identity, holding that what we can imagine is a poor guide to what is possible.","legacy":"Kripke's work on names and necessity reshaped philosophy of language and metaphysics and revived serious talk of essences and necessity. His reading of Wittgenstein launched a large literature on meaning and rule-following.","quote":null},"lewis":{"life":["David Lewis was born in 1941 in Oberlin, Ohio, and worked in the American analytic tradition. In the 1960s many philosophers, led by W. V. O. Quine, were wary of metaphysics and of talk about what is possible or necessary. Lewis took the opposite path. He built a large, unified system of metaphysics using the tools of logic, and showed how much it could explain, even when its conclusions sounded startling.","He studied chemistry and then philosophy at Swarthmore College, and spent a year at Oxford. He earned his PhD at Harvard in 1967 under Quine, and his thesis became his first book, Convention (1969). He taught at UCLA and from 1970 at Princeton, where he remained until his death. Counterfactuals appeared in 1973, two volumes of Philosophical Papers in 1983 and 1986, and On the Plurality of Worlds in 1986. He died in 2001.","Lewis was admired for clear, plain writing and for taking objections seriously; he often stated the best case against himself. He visited Australia almost every year and had close ties to philosophers there. He wrote on an extraordinary range of topics, from causation and time travel to decision theory, mind, and the semantics of fiction, and tied them together into one connected picture."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born in Oberlin, Ohio"],[1967,"Earns his PhD at Harvard under W. V. O. Quine"],[1969,"Publishes Convention"],[1970,"Joins Princeton University"],[1973,"Publishes Counterfactuals"],[1976,"Publishes 'The Paradoxes of Time Travel'"],[1986,"Publishes On the Plurality of Worlds"],[2001,"Dies in Princeton, New Jersey"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Modal realism","body":"When we say 'I could have been a doctor', what makes that true? Lewis's answer: there really is another world, just as concrete as ours, where someone very like you is a doctor. Every way things could have been is the way some real world is. Each world is cut off in space and time from all the others."},{"head":"Counterparts","body":"You yourself exist only in this world. In other worlds there are people very similar to you, your counterparts. 'You could have been a doctor' means some counterpart of yours is one."},{"head":"Conventions","body":"Why do people in Britain drive on the left? Not because anyone signed an agreement, but because everyone expects everyone else to, and it is in each person's interest to match. Lewis used game theory to explain conventions, including the conventions of language."},{"head":"Counterfactuals","body":"'If I had dropped this glass, it would have broken' is true if, in the most similar worlds where I dropped it, it broke. This simple idea became a standard way of analysing 'what if' statements and causation."},{"head":"Time travel is consistent","body":"Lewis argued that stories of time travel need not contain contradictions. A time traveller cannot kill his own grandfather, not because something stops him by magic, but because what happened, happened."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Lewis thought other possible worlds are far-off planets or parallel universes that scientists might one day reach.","truth":"For Lewis, other worlds have no spatial, temporal or causal connection to ours at all, so no telescope or spaceship could ever reach them."},"startHere":"'The Paradoxes of Time Travel' (1976) is short, playful and a clear example of his method.","critics":"Lewis himself named the most common reaction the 'incredulous stare': most people simply find infinitely many concrete worlds unbelievable. Saul Kripke objected that if Hubert Humphrey 'could have won' the election, he cares about himself winning, not some other person in another world.","legacy":"Lewis is widely seen as the most important systematic metaphysician of the late twentieth century. His work on counterfactuals, causation and convention is standard in philosophy, linguistics and economics.","quote":null},"putnam":{"life":["Hilary Putnam was born in Chicago in 1926. He worked through the second half of the twentieth century, when analytic philosophy was absorbing rapid changes in logic, computing and science. Questions about whether minds are like computers, how words connect to the world, and whether science describes reality as it is were all being argued about fiercely, and Putnam made major contributions to each.","He studied at the University of Pennsylvania and earned his PhD at UCLA in 1951 under Hans Reichenbach. He taught at Northwestern, Princeton and MIT before moving to Harvard in 1965, where he stayed until retiring in 2000. He contributed to the solution of a famous mathematical problem, Hilbert's tenth problem. 'The Meaning of \"Meaning\"' appeared in 1975, and Reason, Truth and History in 1981. He died in 2016.","Putnam was famous for changing his mind in public, sometimes about views he himself had made popular, such as functionalism. He saw this as honest, not as weakness. In the late 1960s and early 70s he was politically active against the Vietnam War. Later in life he wrote on pragmatism, ethics and Jewish philosophy, and argued that facts and values cannot be cleanly separated."],"timeline":[[1926,"Born in Chicago"],[1951,"Earns his PhD at UCLA"],[1965,"Joins Harvard University"],[1967,"Publishes 'Psychological Predicates', setting out functionalism"],[1975,"Publishes 'The Meaning of \"Meaning\"' with the Twin Earth example"],[1981,"Publishes Reason, Truth and History"],[2016,"Dies in Arlington, Massachusetts"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Multiple realisability","body":"Humans, octopuses and perhaps future robots can all feel pain, though their bodies are built very differently. So pain cannot simply be one particular brain state. Putnam suggested instead that mental states are defined by what they do, an idea called functionalism."},{"head":"Twin Earth","body":"Imagine a planet just like Earth where the clear liquid in lakes is not H2O but a different chemical, XYZ. Your twin there thinks exactly what you think, yet when you both say 'water' you mean different things. Putnam concluded that meanings are not fixed only by what is in your head but also by your surroundings."},{"head":"The division of linguistic labour","body":"Most of us cannot tell gold from fool's gold, yet our word 'gold' still means gold, because we rely on experts who can. Meaning is partly a social achievement."},{"head":"Brains in a vat","body":"Could you be a brain in a vat fed fake experiences? Putnam argued that if you were, your word 'vat' would refer to images, not real vats, so the thought 'I am a brain in a vat' could not be true. The argument is still hotly debated."},{"head":"Science is no miracle","body":"Putnam argued early on that scientific theories must be roughly true, since otherwise their predictive success would be a miracle. This 'no miracles' argument is a standard defence of scientific realism."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"'Brains in a Vat', chapter 1 of Reason, Truth and History (1981), takes a familiar sci-fi worry and shows how a philosopher of language attacks it.","critics":"Functionalism, which Putnam helped found, was challenged by John Searle's Chinese Room and Ned Block's 'China brain' examples, which suggest a system could have the right functions without any understanding or experience. Putnam himself later rejected functionalism in Representation and Reality (1988).","legacy":"Putnam's semantic externalism and multiple realisability became basic ideas in philosophy of language and mind and in cognitive science. His willingness to revise his views is often held up as a model of philosophical honesty.","quote":null},"searle":{"life":["John Searle was born in 1932 in Denver, Colorado. He worked in the analytic tradition from the late 1950s, through the rise of computers and artificial intelligence. In the 1970s many researchers believed that the mind is essentially a computer program and that a machine running the right software would literally understand and think. Searle became the most famous critic of this idea, while also building theories of language, mind and society.","He studied at the University of Wisconsin and then at Oxford as a Rhodes Scholar, where he was influenced by J. L. Austin's work on how we do things with words. In 1959 he joined the University of California, Berkeley, where he spent his career. Speech Acts appeared in 1969. His Chinese Room argument was published in 1980. Later books include Intentionality (1983), The Rediscovery of the Mind (1992) and The Construction of Social Reality (1995). He died in 2025.","Searle wrote in a confident, plain style and enjoyed public argument, debating AI researchers, Daniel Dennett and Jacques Derrida in print. He was a vivid lecturer and a popular teacher at Berkeley for decades. He saw himself as defending common-sense views, such as the reality of consciousness, against theories he considered fashionable but confused. His books are written for intelligent general readers as well as specialists, and he often used everyday examples, such as ordering a beer or paying with a banknote, to make abstract points."],"timeline":[[1932,"Born in Denver, Colorado"],[1959,"Joins the University of California, Berkeley"],[1969,"Publishes Speech Acts"],[1980,"Publishes the Chinese Room argument in 'Minds, Brains, and Programs'"],[1983,"Publishes Intentionality"],[1992,"Publishes The Rediscovery of the Mind"],[1995,"Publishes The Construction of Social Reality"],[2025,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The Chinese Room","body":"Imagine you sit in a room with a rulebook for matching Chinese symbols. People pass in questions in Chinese; you follow the rules and pass out perfect answers, but you understand no Chinese. Searle argued a computer running a program is in the same position: following rules for symbols is not understanding."},{"head":"Speech acts","body":"Saying 'I promise to come' is not describing a promise; it is making one. Building on Austin, Searle showed that speaking is a kind of action governed by rules, and classified the main things we do with words: asserting, ordering, promising, expressing and declaring."},{"head":"Biological naturalism","body":"Consciousness is a real biological feature of the brain, caused by brain processes in the way digestion is caused by the stomach. It is neither a mysterious soul nor something to be explained away."},{"head":"Social reality","body":"A piece of paper counts as money, and a person counts as president, only because we collectively accept that it does. Searle summed this up as 'X counts as Y in context C'. Much of the social world rests on such shared acceptances."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The Chinese Room argument claims machines can never think.","truth":"Searle held that brains are machines that think, and that an artificial machine with the right causal powers could think too. His target was the claim that running a program is by itself enough for understanding."},"startHere":"'Minds, Brains, and Programs' (1980) sets out the Chinese Room in a few pages and is one of the most discussed papers in philosophy of mind.","critics":"The best-known reply, the 'systems reply', says that while the person in the room does not understand Chinese, the whole system of person, rulebook and room does. Daniel Dennett and Douglas Hofstadter pressed versions of this and argued that Searle's intuition misleads us about scale and speed.","legacy":"The Chinese Room remains the central argument in debates about whether AI systems really understand. Searle's speech act theory shaped linguistics and law, and his account of social reality influenced social theory.","quote":null},"rorty":{"life":["Richard Rorty was born in New York City in 1931 to parents who were left-wing writers and activists. He worked in American philosophy during the second half of the twentieth century, when the analytic tradition aimed to make philosophy precise and scientific, while Continental thinkers in Europe were questioning the very idea of objective foundations. Rorty became the best-known bridge, and the best-known rebel, between these camps.","He entered the University of Chicago at 15 and earned his PhD at Yale in 1956. After military service he taught at Wellesley, then at Princeton from 1961 to 1982. Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature (1979) argued that philosophy's search for firm foundations of knowledge had failed. He then moved to the University of Virginia as a professor of humanities, and later to Stanford. Contingency, Irony, and Solidarity appeared in 1989. He died in 2007.","Rorty wrote with wit and enjoyed provoking his own profession. He saw himself as continuing the pragmatism of John Dewey, whom he admired as a philosopher and as a democratic reformer. He wrote often for newspapers and magazines on American politics. His short book Achieving Our Country (1998) criticised the cultural left for neglecting economic inequality, and passages from it were widely quoted again after the 2016 US election."],"timeline":[[1931,"Born in New York City"],[1956,"Earns his PhD at Yale"],[1961,"Begins teaching at Princeton"],[1979,"Publishes Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature"],[1982,"Moves to the University of Virginia"],[1989,"Publishes Contingency, Irony, and Solidarity"],[1998,"Publishes Achieving Our Country"],[2007,"Dies in Palo Alto, California"]],"ideas":[{"head":"No mirror of nature","body":"Philosophers have pictured the mind as a mirror trying to reflect reality accurately. Rorty argued this picture is a mistake. Beliefs are tools for coping with the world, as Dewey said, not copies of it, and there is no neutral way to check them against reality from outside."},{"head":"Truth without foundations","body":"Rorty argued that we can only justify beliefs by giving reasons to other people, and that there is nothing more solid underneath. Calling a belief 'true' adds a compliment, not a deeper metaphysical relation."},{"head":"The liberal ironist","body":"An ironist knows that her deepest values, her 'final vocabulary', are products of history and could have been otherwise, yet she holds them anyway. A liberal is someone who thinks cruelty is the worst thing we do. Rorty hoped people could be both."},{"head":"Solidarity over objectivity","body":"Instead of seeking timeless truths, we should try to widen the circle of people we count as 'us'. Novels, journalism and films that show the suffering of strangers do more for this than philosophical arguments."},{"head":"Philosophy as conversation","body":"Philosophy is not a referee for all other subjects. It is one voice in an ongoing conversation of humankind, useful for redescribing things in fresh ways."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Rorty was a relativist who thought every opinion is as good as every other.","truth":"Rorty rejected that label and argued that almost nobody holds such a view. He defended liberal democratic values firmly; he only denied that they rest on foundations outside human practices."},"startHere":"His autobiographical essay 'Trotsky and the Wild Orchids' (1992), collected in Philosophy and Social Hope, explains in plain language how he arrived at his views.","critics":"Hilary Putnam and Jürgen Habermas argued that if truth and objectivity are given up, we lose the ability to criticise our own community's beliefs as mistaken. Many analytic philosophers also held that Rorty misread the authors he used for support.","legacy":"Rorty helped revive American pragmatism and encouraged dialogue between analytic and Continental philosophy. He remains one of the most widely read philosophers outside the academy.","quote":null},"dennett":{"life":["Daniel Dennett was born in Boston in 1942. His career ran alongside the growth of cognitive science, computer science and evolutionary biology, when it became possible to study the mind with experiments and models rather than only with introspection and argument. Dennett wanted philosophy to work closely with these sciences, and he spent decades trying to show how minds, meaning and free will fit into a world explained by evolution.","He studied at Harvard, where he was influenced by W. V. O. Quine, and earned his DPhil at Oxford in 1965 under Gilbert Ryle. After teaching at the University of California, Irvine, he moved in 1971 to Tufts University, where he spent the rest of his career and co-directed the Center for Cognitive Studies. His major books include Brainstorms (1978), Elbow Room (1984), Consciousness Explained (1991) and Darwin's Dangerous Idea (1995). He died in 2024.","Dennett was a prolific and combative writer who enjoyed vivid examples, coined many terms and wrote for general audiences as well as specialists. He worked with scientists in psychology, AI and biology. He was one of the 'Four Horsemen' of the 'new atheism' debates in the 2000s, alongside Richard Dawkins, Sam Harris and Christopher Hitchens, and wrote Breaking the Spell (2006) on religion as a natural phenomenon."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in Boston"],[1965,"Earns his DPhil at Oxford"],[1971,"Joins Tufts University"],[1984,"Publishes Elbow Room on free will"],[1987,"Publishes The Intentional Stance"],[1991,"Publishes Consciousness Explained"],[1995,"Publishes Darwin's Dangerous Idea"],[2024,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The intentional stance","body":"We can predict a chess computer's moves by treating it as if it wants to win and believes certain things. Dennett argued that this is all there is to having beliefs and desires: a system has them if treating it this way reliably works. This applies to people, animals and machines alike."},{"head":"No inner theatre","body":"It feels as if there is a place in the brain where everything 'comes together' for an inner viewer to watch. Dennett called this the Cartesian theatre and argued it does not exist. Instead, many brain processes produce competing 'drafts' of what is happening, with no single finish line."},{"head":"Consciousness is not what it seems","body":"Dennett argued that our sense of having private, ineffable 'qualia', such as the raw redness of red, is a kind of user illusion, like the icons on a computer screen. Consciousness is real, but it is not the mysterious inner glow we take it to be."},{"head":"Free will worth wanting","body":"Even if the world is deterministic, we have the kinds of freedom that matter: the ability to think ahead, weigh options and respond to reasons. Dennett was a leading compatibilist, holding that free will and determinism can both be true."},{"head":"Darwin's dangerous idea","body":"Natural selection is a mindless process that can produce design without a designer. Dennett called it a 'universal acid' that eats through traditional ideas, and contrasted explanations built from the ground up, 'cranes', with appeals to miracles from above, 'skyhooks'."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Dennett denied that consciousness exists.","truth":"Dennett said consciousness is real but works very differently from how it seems from the inside. What he denied was a certain philosophical picture of it, involving private, indescribable qualia."},"startHere":"'Where Am I?' (1978), a short story-like essay reprinted in Brainstorms, is a funny and memorable introduction to his way of thinking about the self.","critics":"David Chalmers, John Searle and others argued that Dennett's theory explains how the brain processes information but not why any of it feels like anything; critics joked that his book should have been called Consciousness Explained Away. Libertarians about free will argue his compatibilist freedom is not the kind needed for deep responsibility.","legacy":"Dennett helped make philosophy of mind work hand in hand with cognitive science and evolutionary biology. His terms, such as the intentional stance and intuition pump, are widely used.","quote":null},"jackson":{"life":["Frank Jackson was born in 1943 in Adelaide, Australia. He belongs to a generation of Australian philosophers who were unusually influential in the analytic tradition, especially on the question of whether the mind is simply physical. Australia in the 1950s and 60s had been a centre of materialism about the mind, through figures such as J. J. C. Smart and David Armstrong. Jackson produced the most famous argument against that view, and later changed sides.","He studied at the University of Melbourne and earned his PhD at La Trobe University. He taught at Monash University and then for many years at the Australian National University, where he also held senior leadership posts. His 1982 paper 'Epiphenomenal Qualia' introduced the case of Mary the colour scientist. He gave the John Locke Lectures at Oxford, published as From Metaphysics to Ethics (1998). He has also taught regularly at Princeton.","Jackson's work is known for its clarity and economy. He has written on conditionals, perception, ethics and the method of philosophy itself. Around the late 1990s he announced that he no longer accepted the conclusion of his own Mary argument and had become a physicalist, while still thinking the argument raised an important challenge. Few philosophers have so publicly switched sides on the problem they are most famous for."],"timeline":[[1943,"Born in Adelaide, Australia"],[1982,"Publishes 'Epiphenomenal Qualia', introducing Mary"],[1986,"Publishes 'What Mary Didn't Know'"],[1987,"Publishes Conditionals"],[1998,"Publishes From Metaphysics to Ethics, based on his John Locke Lectures"],[2003,"Publishes 'Mind and Illusion', explaining his move to physicalism"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Mary the colour scientist","body":"Mary has lived all her life in a black-and-white room but knows every physical fact about colour vision. When she leaves and sees a red tomato, does she learn something new? Jackson argued yes: she learns what seeing red is like. So, he concluded, not all facts are physical facts."},{"head":"Epiphenomenal qualia","body":"Jackson originally concluded that qualia, the felt qualities of experience, are real but have no physical effects. They would be by-products, like the shadows of a moving train. He admitted this sounded strange but thought it followed."},{"head":"Changing his mind","body":"Jackson later argued that Mary does not learn a new fact after all. Instead, she comes to represent an old physical fact in a new way. He now holds that experience is a matter of how the brain represents things, which fits a physicalist picture."},{"head":"The Canberra plan","body":"Jackson defends a method sometimes called the Canberra plan: first spell out what our everyday concepts commit us to, then ask science what, if anything, fills those roles. For example, we work out what 'pain' does in our thinking, then find what in the brain does that job."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Jackson still uses the Mary argument to show the mind is not physical.","truth":"Jackson rejected the conclusion of his own argument around the late 1990s and became a physicalist. Many other philosophers, however, still defend the original argument."},"startHere":"'Epiphenomenal Qualia' (1982) is short, and its section on Mary is one of the most accessible and discussed thought experiments in philosophy of mind.","critics":"David Lewis and Laurence Nemirow argued that Mary gains new abilities, such as recognising and imagining red, rather than new facts. Daniel Dennett argued that if Mary truly knew every physical fact, she would not be surprised at all.","legacy":"The Mary argument is one of the central arguments in debates about consciousness and is taught in almost every philosophy of mind course. Jackson's work on conceptual analysis shaped the method of much Australian and American philosophy.","quote":null},"chalmers":{"life":["David Chalmers was born in Sydney, Australia, in 1966. His career began in the 1990s, when the scientific study of consciousness was becoming respectable again after decades in which many psychologists avoided the topic. Neuroscientists were searching for the brain activity linked to awareness, and philosophers were asking whether such findings could ever fully explain it. Chalmers gave this debate its best-known framing.","He studied mathematics and computer science at the University of Adelaide, then went to Oxford before moving to Indiana University, where he earned his PhD in 1993 under Douglas Hofstadter. He held posts at Washington University in St Louis, the University of California, Santa Cruz, the University of Arizona, where he directed the Center for Consciousness Studies, and the Australian National University, before joining New York University. His talk at a 1994 conference in Tucson introduced the 'hard problem'. The Conscious Mind appeared in 1996.","Chalmers has worked across philosophy of mind, language, metaphysics and technology. With Andy Clark he wrote 'The Extended Mind' (1998). He co-founded PhilPapers, a widely used online index of philosophy research. His book Reality+ (2022) applies philosophy to virtual reality and simulations. In 1998 he made a public bet with the neuroscientist Christof Koch that the brain basis of consciousness would not be settled within 25 years, which Koch conceded in 2023."],"timeline":[[1966,"Born in Sydney, Australia"],[1993,"Earns his PhD at Indiana University"],[1994,"Introduces the 'hard problem' at the Tucson consciousness conference"],[1995,"Publishes 'Facing Up to the Problem of Consciousness'"],[1996,"Publishes The Conscious Mind"],[1998,"Publishes 'The Extended Mind' with Andy Clark"],[2022,"Publishes Reality+"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The hard problem","body":"Explaining how the brain sorts information, focuses attention or controls speech are 'easy' problems: hard work, but standard science can do it. The hard problem is why any of this is accompanied by experience at all: why seeing red feels like something rather than nothing."},{"head":"Zombies","body":"Imagine a being physically identical to you, atom for atom, but with no inner experience at all. Chalmers argues such a 'zombie' is at least conceivable. If so, experience is not fixed by physical facts alone, and physicalism is false."},{"head":"Naturalistic dualism","body":"Chalmers holds that consciousness is a fundamental feature of the world, linked to physical processes by basic laws, much as mass and charge are basic in physics. This is a dualism of properties, not of souls, and he takes panpsychism, the idea that experience exists everywhere in simple forms, seriously as an option."},{"head":"The extended mind","body":"If a man with memory problems relies on a notebook in the same way others rely on memory, the notebook is part of his mind. Chalmers and Clark argued that the mind can extend into tools, an idea now discussed in relation to smartphones."},{"head":"Virtual worlds are real","body":"In Reality+ Chalmers argues that if we are living in a simulation, our world is still real: tables and chairs exist, just made of bits. Virtual reality is a genuine reality, not an illusion."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Chalmers believes in a ghostly soul separate from the body.","truth":"Chalmers rejects soul-like substances. He holds that conscious experience is a basic property of the natural world, connected to physical processes by laws that science could in principle discover."},"startHere":"'Facing Up to the Problem of Consciousness' (1995) explains the hard problem clearly in one article and is freely available.","critics":"Daniel Dennett argued that the hard problem is an illusion that will disappear once the easy problems are solved. Many physicalists, such as Brian Loar, accept that zombies are conceivable but deny that conceivability shows they are really possible.","legacy":"The phrase 'hard problem of consciousness' has become the standard way of naming the central puzzle about the mind, in philosophy and in neuroscience. His work also helped make technology, virtual reality and AI mainstream topics in philosophy.","quote":null},"vaninwagen":{"life":["Peter van Inwagen was born in 1942 in Rochester, New York. He belongs to the generation of analytic philosophers who, from the 1970s, brought metaphysics and philosophy of religion back to the centre of the field after decades in which many thought such questions meaningless. Using the tools of modern logic, he took on old questions directly: Do we have free will? What exists? Why does God allow evil?","He studied at Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute and earned his PhD at the University of Rochester in 1969. He taught at Syracuse University for over two decades and moved to the University of Notre Dame in 1995. His 1975 paper on the incompatibility of free will and determinism set out his best-known argument, developed fully in An Essay on Free Will (1983). Material Beings (1990) defended an unusual view of what objects exist. He gave the Gifford Lectures in 2003, published as The Problem of Evil (2006).","Van Inwagen is known for clear, careful argument and dry humour, and for his willingness to accept surprising conclusions when he thinks the arguments require them. He is a Christian and has written widely in philosophy of religion. He has also written a widely used textbook, Metaphysics, first published in 1993. His writing is admired even by those who reject his conclusions, because he states each premise of an argument openly so that readers can see exactly where they disagree."],"timeline":[[1942,"Born in Rochester, New York"],[1969,"Earns his PhD at the University of Rochester"],[1975,"Publishes 'The Incompatibility of Free Will and Determinism'"],[1983,"Publishes An Essay on Free Will"],[1990,"Publishes Material Beings"],[1995,"Moves to the University of Notre Dame"],[2006,"Publishes The Problem of Evil, based on his Gifford Lectures"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The consequence argument","body":"If determinism is true, everything we do follows from the laws of nature and events long before we were born. We have no control over those laws or that distant past. So, the argument goes, we have no control over what follows from them, including our actions. Free will and determinism cannot both be true."},{"head":"Free will is real but mysterious","body":"Van Inwagen also argues that free will seems to clash with indeterminism, since an undetermined choice looks like a matter of luck. Yet he holds that we do have free will, because we are morally responsible. He accepts that how it works is a mystery."},{"head":"There are no chairs","body":"In Material Beings he asks when several small parts make up a bigger thing. His answer: only when their activity makes up a life. So organisms exist, but strictly speaking there are no chairs or tables, only particles arranged chair-wise."},{"head":"The problem of evil","body":"Van Inwagen argues that a theist need not explain exactly why God permits evil. It is enough to tell a story that might be true, involving human free will and a fallen world, to show that the argument from evil does not succeed."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"His 1975 paper 'The Incompatibility of Free Will and Determinism' sets out the consequence argument in a short, clear form; the free will chapter of his textbook Metaphysics is an easier alternative.","critics":"David Lewis argued in 1981 that the consequence argument trades on an ambiguity: a person could act in a way such that, had they done so, a law would have been different, without having the power to break a law. Compatibilists in general deny the argument's key rule about lack of control passing from premises to conclusion.","legacy":"The consequence argument is the standard argument for incompatibilism and frames most modern debate on free will. Material Beings remains a central text on the question of what objects exist.","quote":null},"gstrawson":{"life":["Galen Strawson was born in 1952 in Oxford. He works in the analytic tradition from the late twentieth century onward, on questions about free will, the self and consciousness. In his field many philosophers had come to accept that moral responsibility fits comfortably with a scientific picture of the world. Strawson argued that a certain deep kind of responsibility is impossible, whatever the truth about determinism.","He studied at Cambridge and then at Oxford, where he earned his doctorate. He taught at Oxford for many years, later at the University of Reading and the City University of New York Graduate Center, and then at the University of Texas at Austin. His first book, Freedom and Belief (1986), was followed by The Secret Connexion (1989) on Hume, Mental Reality (1994), and the 2006 paper 'Realistic Monism', which defended a form of panpsychism.","Strawson writes both technical philosophy and essays for general readers, and is known for forceful, often witty prose. He has written widely on Hume and Locke, and his essay 'Against Narrativity' (2004) challenged the popular idea that everyone lives their life as a story. He insists that the reality of conscious experience is the one thing we can be most certain of, and he has criticised views that deny it in strong terms."],"timeline":[[1952,"Born in Oxford"],[1986,"Publishes Freedom and Belief"],[1989,"Publishes The Secret Connexion on Hume and causation"],[1994,"Publishes 'The Impossibility of Moral Responsibility' and Mental Reality"],[2004,"Publishes 'Against Narrativity'"],[2006,"Publishes 'Realistic Monism', defending panpsychism"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The Basic Argument","body":"You do what you do because of the way you are. To be ultimately responsible for what you do, you would have to be responsible for the way you are. But to make yourself a certain way, you would already need to be some way, and so on back forever. Since no one can create themselves from nothing, no one is ultimately responsible."},{"head":"Determinism is not the point","body":"The Basic Argument works whether or not determinism is true. Random events in the brain would not help, since you are no more responsible for chance than for causes. This sets Strawson apart from many other sceptics about free will."},{"head":"Against narrativity","body":"Many people say a good life must be lived as a story with a plot. Strawson argues that some people are 'Episodic': they do not feel their past self as really them, and they live perfectly good lives without any life-story. He counts himself among them."},{"head":"Real physicalism","body":"Strawson argues that if physicalism is true and experience is real, then experience must be part of physical reality at the most basic level. This leads him to panpsychism: the view that some form of experience exists in the fundamental stuff of the world."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"His paper 'The Impossibility of Moral Responsibility' (1994) sets out the Basic Argument step by step in a few pages.","critics":"Compatibilists such as Daniel Dennett reply that responsibility does not require creating oneself from nothing; it requires only that we act from our reasons and can respond to criticism. On narrativity, critics such as Marya Schechtman argue that a basic sense of one's life as a connected whole is needed for being a responsible person over time.","legacy":"The Basic Argument is one of the most cited arguments against deep moral responsibility. Strawson's 'Realistic Monism' helped bring panpsychism back into serious discussion in philosophy of mind.","quote":null},"pereboom":{"life":["Derk Pereboom was born in 1957 and works in the analytic tradition in the United States. His career has run through a period in which neuroscience and psychology were increasingly cited in public debate as showing that free will is an illusion. Within philosophy, most experts remained compatibilists, holding that free will and determinism can coexist. Pereboom became the leading defender of the view that we lack the free will needed for deep blame, and that this need not be bad news.","He taught for many years at the University of Vermont before moving to Cornell University. His first major work on free will, the paper 'Determinism al Dente', appeared in 1995, followed by Living Without Free Will (2001). He contributed to the widely used Four Views on Free Will (2007) alongside John Martin Fischer, Robert Kane and Manuel Vargas. Later books include Consciousness and the Prospects of Physicalism (2011), Free Will, Agency, and Meaning in Life (2014) and Wrongdoing and the Moral Emotions (2021).","Pereboom also works on the history of philosophy, especially Kant and Spinoza, and on philosophy of mind and religion. His writing is careful and measured, and he is known for taking the practical side of his view seriously. Rather than stopping at the claim that we lack free will, he works out what criminal justice, relationships and personal meaning would look like if people took that claim to heart."],"timeline":[[1957,"Born"],[1995,"Publishes 'Determinism al Dente'"],[2001,"Publishes Living Without Free Will"],[2007,"Contributes to Four Views on Free Will"],[2011,"Publishes Consciousness and the Prospects of Physicalism"],[2014,"Publishes Free Will, Agency, and Meaning in Life"],[2021,"Publishes Wrongdoing and the Moral Emotions"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Hard incompatibilism","body":"If our actions are determined, we lack the free will needed to deserve blame or praise in the deepest sense. If they are undetermined, they are partly down to chance, which does not help. Either way, Pereboom argues, we lack basic-desert responsibility. He calls this hard incompatibilism."},{"head":"The four-case argument","body":"Imagine Plum, whose decision to kill was directly programmed by neuroscientists. Most people say he is not responsible. Then imagine cases where the manipulation becomes more distant, ending with an ordinary person whose choice was simply determined by the past. Pereboom argues there is no principled place to draw the line."},{"head":"The quarantine model","body":"We quarantine people with dangerous diseases even though they are not to blame. Similarly, society may detain dangerous offenders to protect others, but without the retribution of making them suffer because they deserve it. This also means detention should be as humane as possible."},{"head":"Meaning without free will","body":"Love, friendship, gratitude and achievement remain valuable without deep free will. We can still be glad of a friend's kindness and still reasonably hope that our efforts will make a difference. Pereboom argues that giving up anger-driven blame might even make relationships better."},{"head":"Forward-looking blame","body":"We can still hold people accountable in ways that look to the future, by asking them to reflect, apologise and change, protecting others, and repairing relationships, without supposing they deserve suffering."}],"misconception":{"myth":"If no one has free will, we would have to stop punishing criminals and let them all go.","truth":"Pereboom's quarantine model allows society to detain dangerous people to protect others. What it rejects is punishment justified by the claim that offenders deserve to suffer."},"startHere":"His chapter in Four Views on Free Will (2007) sets out his position briefly and is printed next to replies from leading opponents.","critics":"Michael McKenna's 'hard-line reply' argues that we should accept that even manipulated Plum is responsible if he meets compatibilist conditions, which blocks the four-case argument. Saul Smilansky and others argue that dropping belief in desert could undermine respect for people and be dangerous in practice.","legacy":"Pereboom has made free will scepticism a mainstream position in philosophy, and his quarantine model is the most discussed account of punishment without desert. His work links the free will debate to questions about criminal justice and personal life.","quote":null},"korsgaard":{"life":["Christine Korsgaard was born in 1952 in Chicago and belongs to the generation of American philosophers trained after John Rawls had made political and moral philosophy central again. In the late twentieth century many philosophers doubted that morality could have any real authority: perhaps moral claims were just feelings, or facts that somehow existed apart from us. Korsgaard set out to show that Immanuel Kant had a better answer than either side, and that his ideas could be restated for a modern audience.","She studied at Eastern Illinois University and then at Harvard, where she wrote her doctorate under Rawls, finishing in 1981. After teaching at Yale, the University of California at Santa Barbara and the University of Chicago, she returned to Harvard in 1991 and spent the rest of her career there. Her Tanner Lectures of 1992 became The Sources of Normativity (1996), her best-known book. Her John Locke Lectures at Oxford grew into Self-Constitution (2009), and late in her career she turned to the moral standing of animals in Fellow Creatures (2018).","Korsgaard writes as an interpreter of Kant, but she uses his ideas to tackle present-day questions rather than only explaining what he meant. Her books start from ordinary experiences, such as stopping to ask whether a desire is a good reason to act, and build up careful arguments from there. She has also written widely on Aristotle and on the history of ethics, and has supervised many younger philosophers who now work in the Kantian tradition."],"timeline":[[1952,"Born in Chicago"],[1981,"Completes her PhD at Harvard under John Rawls"],[1991,"Joins the Harvard philosophy department"],[1992,"Gives the Tanner Lectures that become The Sources of Normativity"],[1996,"Publishes The Sources of Normativity and Creating the Kingdom of Ends"],[2009,"Publishes Self-Constitution"],[2018,"Publishes Fellow Creatures, on our obligations to animals"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The normative question","body":"Korsgaard asks what gives morality its grip on us: why should I do what morality says when it costs me? She argues that a good answer must be one that a person, thinking carefully in the first person, can actually accept. Pointing to a fact 'out there' is not enough if it leaves me asking 'so what?'"},{"head":"Reflection demands reasons","body":"Humans can step back from their impulses and ask whether to act on them. If you feel like skipping a promised meeting, you can ask whether that urge is a good reason. Because we can always ask this, we cannot simply act; we need reasons, and reasons have to come from somewhere."},{"head":"Practical identity","body":"Our reasons come from the ways we value ourselves: as a parent, a friend, a teacher, a citizen. A good parent has reasons to get up at night for a crying baby that a stranger lacks. Korsgaard argues that behind all these identities lies one we cannot give up, valuing ourselves as human beings who need reasons, and this commits us to valuing humanity in others too."},{"head":"Acting makes the self","body":"In Self-Constitution she argues that by choosing and acting we give ourselves a unified character. Acting well is like a building standing well: good action holds the self together, while bad action pulls it apart. This view, sometimes called constitutivism, tries to ground ethics in what it is to be an agent at all."},{"head":"Animals as ends in themselves","body":"Kant thought we have no direct duties to animals. Korsgaard disagrees: when we value our own good as creatures who can be hurt or helped, we must also respect other creatures that have a good of their own. Fellow Creatures argues that this rules out much of how we treat animals today."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first lecture of The Sources of Normativity, which sets out the question she is trying to answer and surveys earlier answers. It is clear, short and shows her method of testing theories against the first-person point of view.","critics":"In replies printed with The Sources of Normativity, G. A. Cohen argued that her argument proves too little: a committed Mafioso could also have a practical identity that gives him reasons, so reflective endorsement alone does not yield morality. Moral realists such as Derek Parfit also argued that reasons must exist independently of what we endorse.","legacy":"Korsgaard helped make Kantian ethics a leading option in present-day moral philosophy and gave the debate about 'constructivism' (the idea that moral truths are built from the standpoint of agents) its modern shape. Her work on animals has brought Kantian thinkers into debates about animal ethics.","quote":null},"nussbaum":{"life":["Martha Nussbaum was born in New York City in 1947. She came of age during the American civil rights movement and second-wave feminism, when questions about equality and the quality of people's lives were at the centre of public debate. She trained first as a classicist, reading Greek tragedy and Aristotle in the original, and has spent her career showing that ancient ideas about living well can help with present-day problems of justice.","After studying at New York University she completed a doctorate in classics at Harvard in 1975 and taught at Harvard and Brown. The Fragility of Goodness (1986) made her name. From the mid-1980s she worked with the economist Amartya Sen at a United Nations research institute in Helsinki, which led her to develop the 'capabilities approach' to human development. Since 1995 she has taught at the University of Chicago, in both its law school and philosophy department. Her books include Women and Human Development (2000), Frontiers of Justice (2006) and Creating Capabilities (2011).","Nussbaum works across many fields at once: classics, law, political theory, literature and the study of emotions. She writes for general readers as well as specialists and often joins public debates, for instance about education, religious freedom and the treatment of animals. She has received major international prizes, including the Kyoto Prize in 2016 and the Holberg Prize in 2021."],"timeline":[[1947,"Born in New York City"],[1975,"Completes her PhD in classics at Harvard"],[1986,"Publishes The Fragility of Goodness"],[1995,"Joins the University of Chicago"],[2000,"Publishes Women and Human Development"],[2006,"Publishes Frontiers of Justice"],[2011,"Publishes Creating Capabilities"],[2016,"Receives the Kyoto Prize"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Goodness is fragile","body":"Drawing on Greek tragedy and Aristotle, Nussbaum argues that a good human life depends partly on luck. Friendship, love and political life make life rich, but they also expose us to loss. Trying to make ourselves invulnerable, as some philosophers recommended, would mean giving up much of what makes life worth living."},{"head":"The capabilities approach","body":"To judge whether a society is just, ask what each person is actually able to do and be, not just how much money they have. A woman who earns an income but cannot leave the house safely is not truly well off. The question is whether people have real opportunities, called capabilities, to live a full human life."},{"head":"A list of central capabilities","body":"Unlike Sen, Nussbaum proposes a list of ten central capabilities, including life, health, bodily integrity, using the senses and imagination, emotions, practical reason, affiliation with others, relating to other species, play and control over one's environment. A just society must bring every citizen above a minimum threshold on each one."},{"head":"Emotions are judgments","body":"In Upheavals of Thought (2001) she argues that emotions are not blind surges but evaluations of things we care about. Grief, for example, contains the judgment that someone who mattered greatly to you has been lost. So emotions can be wise or foolish and belong in ethics and politics."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Begin with the early chapters of Creating Capabilities (2011), a short book written for non-specialists that explains the capabilities approach and sets out her list of central capabilities with real examples.","critics":"Critics argue that a fixed list of capabilities is paternalistic, imposing one picture of the good life on cultures that may see things differently; Amartya Sen himself declined to endorse any fixed list for this reason. Others ask who decides where the threshold lies.","legacy":"The capabilities approach, developed by Sen and Nussbaum, has shaped how international bodies such as the United Nations Development Programme think about measuring human development. Her work also helped bring emotions and literature back into mainstream moral philosophy.","quote":null},"street":{"life":["Sharon Street is an American philosopher born in 1973. She works in metaethics, the part of philosophy that asks what moral claims are and whether they can be true. Her work belongs to a period in which evolutionary biology and psychology were being used more and more to explain human behaviour, including our moral feelings. Street asked what this means for the old idea that there are moral facts which exist independently of what anyone thinks.","She has spent most of her career teaching at New York University. Her 2006 paper 'A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value', published in the journal Philosophical Studies, became one of the most discussed papers in ethics of its time. In a series of later papers, including 'Constructivism about Reasons' (2008), she developed a positive view: that reasons and values come from the standpoint of beings who value things, rather than from a separate realm of moral facts.","Street mainly writes journal articles rather than books, and her work is known for setting out arguments in a clear, step-by-step form that others can test. Her Darwinian argument has prompted a large number of replies from philosophers defending moral realism, and it is now a standard topic in university courses on metaethics. She has also written for students and general readers on whether anything really matters if our values are products of evolution."],"timeline":[[1973,"Born"],[2006,"Publishes 'A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value'"],[2008,"Publishes 'Constructivism about Reasons'"],[2010,"Publishes 'What Is Constructivism in Ethics and Metaethics?'"],[2012,"Publishes 'Coming to Terms with Contingency', defending a Humean form of constructivism"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Evolution shaped our values","body":"Many of our deepest evaluative tendencies, such as caring for our children or thinking that we should repay kindness, are the kinds of attitudes that helped our ancestors survive and reproduce. Natural selection has had a huge influence on what we find valuable, whether or not we notice it."},{"head":"The Darwinian dilemma","body":"If there are moral facts independent of us, either evolution's influence on our values is related to those facts or it is not. If it is not, our values would be wildly off-track by luck, and we could have little moral knowledge. If it is, the realist must claim that evolution 'tracked' moral truth, but the simpler explanation is that our values helped us survive, with no need for moral facts at all."},{"head":"Constructivism about value","body":"Street's alternative is that what we have reason to do is fixed by what follows from our own values when we reflect on them consistently. Value is not discovered like a planet; it is built from the point of view of creatures who care about things. This avoids the dilemma, because there is no outside standard our values had to match."},{"head":"Living with contingency","body":"On her view, if evolution had shaped us very differently, different things would have been reasons for us. Street argues we should accept this contingency rather than fear it: it does not make our values fake, just as the fact that you might have loved a different person does not make your actual love fake."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value' (2006). It is long for a journal article, but the main argument is laid out clearly in its opening sections and needs no technical background.","critics":"Defenders of moral realism, including David Enoch and Erik Wielenberg, have argued for a 'third factor' reply: evolution favoured attitudes like caring for survival and wellbeing, and these really are good, so evolution and moral truth line up without luck. Derek Parfit also replied at length in On What Matters.","legacy":"Her Darwinian dilemma made 'evolutionary debunking arguments' one of the main topics of current metaethics. Her constructivism is a leading alternative to both moral realism and the view that morality is mere feeling.","quote":null},"goff":{"life":["Philip Goff is a British philosopher born in 1978. He works in philosophy of mind, the field that asks how consciousness, the felt experience of seeing red or tasting coffee, fits into the physical world. For much of the twentieth century most philosophers held that consciousness would eventually be explained in purely physical terms. Goff belongs to a group of thinkers who doubt this and have revived panpsychism, the old idea that some form of experience is found throughout nature.","After his doctoral studies, Goff taught in Britain and at Central European University in Budapest before moving to Durham University. His academic book Consciousness and Fundamental Reality (2017) set out his case in detail. Galileo's Error (2019) presented the same ideas for general readers and was widely reviewed. In Why? The Purpose of the Universe (2023) he turned to the question of whether the universe might have some kind of purpose, without appealing to a traditional God.","Goff is unusually active as a public philosopher. He writes for newspapers and magazines, takes part in public debates with scientists and other philosophers, and has helped organise discussions of panpsychism among researchers. His style is to present a bold hypothesis plainly and then answer the most common objections to it one by one. He is open about how speculative his proposals are, presenting panpsychism as the best available hypothesis rather than as a proven fact."],"timeline":[[1978,"Born"],[2017,"Publishes Consciousness and Fundamental Reality"],[2017,"Co-authors the revised Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on panpsychism"],[2019,"Publishes Galileo's Error for general readers"],[2023,"Publishes Why? The Purpose of the Universe"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Galileo's error","body":"Goff argues that modern science began by deliberately setting aside qualities like colour, taste and smell, treating them as being in the mind rather than in matter. That choice let physics describe the world in pure mathematics. But it means physics was never designed to explain experience, so we should not expect it to do so without rethinking matter."},{"head":"Physics describes structure only","body":"Following Bertrand Russell and Arthur Eddington, Goff points out that physics tells us what matter does, how particles behave and relate to one another, but not what matter is in itself. A chess rulebook tells you how the pieces move, not what they are made of. This leaves room for the inner nature of matter to be experiential."},{"head":"Panpsychism","body":"His proposal is that the basic constituents of the world have extremely simple forms of experience, and our rich consciousness is built out of them. This would explain why consciousness exists without either treating it as an illusion or adding a separate non-physical soul."},{"head":"Cosmic purpose","body":"In Why? he argues that the apparent fine-tuning of the laws of physics for life gives some evidence that the universe has purpose, but that the God of traditional religion faces serious problems such as the existence of suffering. He explores middle options, such as a universe that is itself conscious or a limited designer."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Panpsychism means rocks, chairs and electrons think and feel like we do.","truth":"Goff's panpsychism says only that the most basic bits of matter have some extremely simple form of experience. It does not say that a rock as a whole is conscious, and nothing like thought is attributed to particles."},"startHere":"Start with Galileo's Error (2019), especially its opening chapters, which explain the problem of consciousness and the historical story behind it in plain language.","critics":"The most pressing objection is the 'combination problem', first raised in a different form by William James: it is unclear how many tiny experiencing subjects could combine to make one unified mind like yours. Others, including illusionists such as Keith Frankish, argue there is no special problem of consciousness to solve in the first place.","legacy":"Goff has done much to make panpsychism a serious option in academic philosophy of mind and to bring the debate to a wide public. Whether it lasts will depend on whether the combination problem can be answered.","quote":null},"alfarabi":{"life":["Abu Nasr al-Farabi was born around 870, probably in Central Asia; whether his family was Turkic or Persian is disputed. He lived during the Abbasid era, when Baghdad was the centre of a great translation movement that had put much of Greek science and philosophy into Arabic. Christian, Muslim and Jewish scholars studied Aristotle side by side. Al-Farabi entered this world and set out to show how Greek philosophy and Islamic religion fit together.","He spent most of his working life in Baghdad, where he studied logic with Christian teachers in the Aristotelian tradition. He wrote commentaries on Aristotle's logic, a classification of the sciences and a large book on music theory. Around 942, during political unrest, he left Baghdad for Syria, and spent his last years largely in Damascus and Aleppo, with a probable visit to Egypt. His best-known political work, The Perfect State (Opinions of the People of the Virtuous City), dates from this late period. He died in Damascus in about 950.","Little is securely known about his personal life. Later biographers, writing centuries afterwards, describe him as living simply and caring little for wealth, and tell stories of his skill as a musician; these are best treated as legends. What is certain is the respect he earned: he was called 'the Second Teacher', after Aristotle, the First. His surviving works range widely, from logic and political philosophy to metaphysics and the theory of music."],"timeline":[[870,"Born, c., probably in Central Asia"],[942,"Leaves Baghdad for Syria, c."],[942,"Completes The Perfect State (Opinions of the People of the Virtuous City), c."],[950,"Dies in Damascus, c."],[998,"Ibn Sina reports that a short work by al-Farabi unlocked Aristotle's Metaphysics for him, c."],[1150,"His Enumeration of the Sciences is translated into Latin, c."]],"ideas":[{"head":"The virtuous city","body":"Like Plato, al-Farabi thought a city exists so that its citizens can reach true happiness, which for him is the perfection of the mind. In the virtuous city each person plays a role, like organs in a healthy body, under a ruler who knows what happiness really is. Other cities aim at wealth, honour or pleasure and so go astray."},{"head":"The philosopher-prophet ruler","body":"The ideal ruler combines the philosopher's understanding with the prophet's gift for imagery and persuasion. He grasps the truth through reason and then communicates it in a form ordinary people can follow. If no single person has all the qualities, a group of rulers can share them."},{"head":"Religion as imitation of philosophy","body":"Al-Farabi held that religion presents the same truths as philosophy, but through symbols and stories suited to the wider public. A story about paradise, for example, conveys in vivid images what philosophy explains as the soul's final perfection. This made philosophy the deeper of the two, a claim that worried many religious scholars."},{"head":"Emanation and the Active Intellect","body":"He described the universe as flowing from the First Cause through a series of intellects linked to the heavenly spheres. The lowest of these, the Active Intellect, lights up the human mind so that it can understand universal truths, much as sunlight lets the eye see colours."},{"head":"Logic as universal grammar","body":"Al-Farabi argued that logic does for reasoning what grammar does for a language, but logic applies to all languages alike. Knowing it is like knowing the rules that let you check any argument, whatever language it is made in."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Begin with The Attainment of Happiness, a short work that explains how the sciences, philosophy, religion and political rule fit together in his system. It is available in English translations of his political writings.","critics":"Al-Ghazali, in The Incoherence of the Philosophers (c. 1095), attacked al-Farabi and Ibn Sina for teaching that the world is eternal, that God does not know particular things, and that bodily resurrection is symbolic, and declared some of these views unbelief.","legacy":"Al-Farabi set the agenda for Islamic philosophy: Ibn Sina, Ibn Rushd and the Jewish philosopher Maimonides all built on him. In the twentieth century Leo Strauss and others revived interest in his political philosophy.","quote":null},"ibn_khaldun":{"life":["Ibn Khaldun was born in Tunis in 1332 to a family that had come from Seville in Muslim Spain. North Africa in his lifetime was divided between rival dynasties that rose and fell quickly, and the Black Death of 1348 to 1349 killed his parents and many of his teachers. Living through so much political upheaval, he wanted to understand why states form, flourish and collapse.","He had a long and turbulent public career, serving and sometimes plotting against rulers in Fez, Granada, Bijaya and elsewhere; in 1364 he was sent as an envoy to the Christian king Pedro of Castile. In 1375 he withdrew to the castle of Qal'at Ibn Salama, in present-day Algeria, and there in 1377 drafted the Muqaddimah ('Introduction'), the opening part of a universal history. In 1382 he moved to Cairo, where he taught and served several terms as a chief judge of the Maliki school of law. In 1401 he met the conqueror Timur outside Damascus. He died in Cairo in 1406.","Unusually for his time, Ibn Khaldun wrote an autobiography, so his career is well documented. He was ambitious and politically skilled, and often changed patrons. As a scholar he combined deep training in Islamic law and theology with a sharp eye for practical politics, economics and how people actually live. He kept revising the Muqaddimah for the rest of his life and taught from it in Cairo."],"timeline":[[1332,"Born in Tunis"],[1349,"Loses his parents to the Black Death, c."],[1364,"Serves as envoy from Granada to Pedro of Castile"],[1377,"Completes the first draft of the Muqaddimah"],[1382,"Moves to Cairo, where he later serves as a chief judge"],[1401,"Meets Timur outside Damascus"],[1406,"Dies in Cairo"]],"ideas":[{"head":"A science of society","body":"Ibn Khaldun noticed that historians often repeated impossible stories, such as armies too big for any country to feed. He proposed a new science of human social organisation ('ilm al-'umran) to test reports against what is possible in society. Before believing a report, ask whether it fits how societies actually work."},{"head":"Group feeling (asabiyya)","body":"Hardy peoples living in harsh conditions, such as desert tribes, develop strong group solidarity, which he called asabiyya. This shared loyalty lets them conquer settled peoples and found dynasties. Think of a small, tight-knit team that outplays a richer but divided rival."},{"head":"The cycle of dynasties","body":"Once in power, rulers settle in cities and grow used to luxury. Over about three generations, which he reckoned at roughly 120 years, group feeling fades, rulers hire mercenaries and raise taxes, and the dynasty becomes vulnerable to a new group with fresh solidarity. The cycle then begins again."},{"head":"Economics of labour and taxes","body":"He argued that wealth comes from human labour and that cities prosper when people can keep the fruits of their work. Early in a dynasty, low taxes encourage activity and bring in much revenue; later, high taxes discourage work and revenue falls. Modern economists have compared this to later ideas about taxation."},{"head":"Rule under religious law is best","body":"Ibn Khaldun distinguished rule based on force, rule based on reasoned policy for worldly interests, and rule guided by religious law. He held the last to be best, because it serves people's good in both this world and the next."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Ibn Khaldun was essentially a modern secular sociologist born centuries too early.","truth":"He did propose a new science of society, but he worked within an Islamic framework, served as a religious judge and held that rule guided by religious law is best. Calling him the first sociologist or economist captures something real but can hide how different his aims were."},"startHere":"Read the opening sections of the Muqaddimah on the errors of historians and the first chapters on group feeling and the rise of dynasties, available in Franz Rosenthal's English translation and its one-volume abridgement.","critics":"Modern historians have argued that his cycle of desert conquerors and decaying dynasties fits the history of North Africa that he knew well, but generalises poorly to other societies, and that 'group feeling' is too loose an idea to test.","legacy":"Rediscovered by European scholars in the nineteenth century, Ibn Khaldun is now widely studied as a forerunner of sociology, historiography and economics, and remains a major figure in Arab and Islamic thought.","quote":null},"cicero":{"life":["Marcus Tullius Cicero was born in 106 BCE at Arpinum, a country town south-east of Rome. He lived in the last decades of the Roman Republic, an age of civil wars, powerful generals such as Pompey and Julius Caesar, and fierce political rivalry. Greek philosophy was by then studied by educated Romans, but it was written almost entirely in Greek. Cicero set out to change that.","Cicero studied philosophy in Rome and in Athens and Rhodes, then rose through law and politics, becoming consul in 63 BCE, when he crushed the conspiracy of Catiline. He was exiled in 58 BCE and recalled the next year. Shut out of power under Caesar, and grieving after his daughter Tullia died in 45 BCE, he wrote most of his philosophical works in about two years, including the Academica, On the Nature of the Gods and On Duties. After Caesar's murder he attacked Mark Antony in a series of speeches, and in 43 BCE he was killed on Antony's orders.","Cicero was above all an orator and a writer of brilliant Latin prose; many of his letters survive, giving an unusually detailed picture of his hopes, vanity and doubts. In philosophy he saw himself as a follower of the sceptical Academy, weighing the arguments of each school. He also invented much of the Latin vocabulary of philosophy, including the word qualitas, 'quality'."],"timeline":[[-106,"Born at Arpinum"],[-79,"Studies philosophy and rhetoric in Athens and Rhodes, c."],[-63,"Serves as consul and suppresses Catiline's conspiracy"],[-58,"Exiled from Rome; recalled the following year"],[-45,"After his daughter's death, writes the Academica, On the Nature of the Gods and other works"],[-44,"Writes On Duties after Caesar's assassination"],[-43,"Killed on the orders of Mark Antony"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Follow the most probable","body":"As a member of the sceptical Academy, Cicero held that certainty is rarely available, but this need not paralyse us. We can follow what seems most probable or persuasive after hearing all sides, while staying open to correction. A good juror does the same: decides on the best evidence without claiming to be infallible."},{"head":"Hear every side","body":"His dialogues usually set out the views of rival schools, such as Epicureans, Stoics and Academics, through different speakers, and often leave the final judgment to the reader. He thought the best way to approach truth was to argue on both sides of a question."},{"head":"Right and useful never truly clash","body":"In On Duties, written for his son, he argues that what is morally right and what is truly advantageous never really conflict. When cheating seems profitable, the profit is an illusion, because damaging trust and justice harms the very society we depend on."},{"head":"Natural law","body":"Borrowing from the Stoics, Cicero argued that there is a true law rooted in nature and reason, the same for all peoples and all times. Laws made by states that break this higher law are not truly law. This idea later fed into theories of natural law and human rights."},{"head":"Philosophy in Latin","body":"Cicero translated and adapted Greek philosophy for Roman readers, coining Latin terms that later passed into English and other European languages. Words such as 'quality' and 'moral' owe much to his choices."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Cicero was a Stoic philosopher.","truth":"Cicero identified with the sceptical New Academy, which weighed rival views without claiming certainty. He admired and borrowed heavily from Stoic ethics, especially in On Duties, which is why he is often mistaken for one."},"startHere":"Read On Duties, Book 1, a practical guide to how to live and act well that was one of the most widely read moral books in Europe for centuries.","critics":"Ancient critics asked whether a sceptic who claims only probability can act and commit to anything consistently. In the nineteenth century the historian Theodor Mommsen and others dismissed Cicero as a vain politician and an unoriginal compiler of Greek ideas.","legacy":"Cicero shaped Western education and political thought for nearly two thousand years: reading his lost Hortensius turned Augustine to philosophy, and Renaissance humanists, John Locke and the American founders all drew on him.","quote":{"text":"True law is right reason in agreement with nature; it is of universal application, unchanging and everlasting.","src":"On the Republic, Book 3 (passage preserved by Lactantius)","tr":"Clinton W. Keyes"}},"ramanuja":{"life":["Ramanuja lived in South India in the eleventh and twelfth centuries; tradition gives his dates as 1017 to 1137, a lifespan of 120 years that many scholars doubt. He was born at Sriperumbudur, near present-day Chennai. His world was shaped by the Tamil devotional poetry of the Alvars, saints who sang of love for Vishnu, and by Vedanta, the philosophical schools that interpret the Upanishads. The most influential Vedanta view, Advaita, taught that only one formless reality exists and the world of many things is in some sense an illusion.","According to traditional biographies, Ramanuja first studied under the teacher Yadavaprakasha but broke with him over how to read the Upanishads. He later became the leader of the Sri Vaishnava community based at the great temple of Srirangam, continuing the work of the earlier teacher Yamuna. Tradition says he spent some years in what is now Karnataka, at Melkote, to escape persecution by a Chola king. His major works include the Vedarthasangraha, a summary of his views, and the Sri Bhasya, a long commentary on the Brahma Sutras.","Much of what we know about his life comes from later devotional biographies, which mix history and legend. What is well established is that he was both a philosopher and a religious organiser: he systematised temple worship and built a community of followers that has lasted to the present day. His followers still revere him as one of the greatest teachers of their tradition, and images of him are worshipped in Sri Vaishnava temples."],"timeline":[[1017,"Born at Sriperumbudur, according to traditional dating"],[1100,"Writes the Vedarthasangraha, c.; exact date uncertain"],[1100,"Completes the Sri Bhasya on the Brahma Sutras, c.; exact date uncertain"],[1137,"Dies at Srirangam, according to traditional dating"],[1268,"Birth of Vedanta Desika, who later systematises Ramanuja's school"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Qualified non-dualism","body":"Ramanuja's system is called Vishishtadvaita, 'non-dualism of the qualified'. There is only one ultimate reality, Brahman, but it is internally complex: souls and matter are real and distinct, yet they exist only as qualities or modes of Brahman. Think of a blue lotus: one thing, but its blueness is real and different from the flower that has it."},{"head":"The world as God's body","body":"Souls and the material world are related to God as a body is related to its soul. God supports them, controls them from within and exists for their sake as well. Just as your body depends on you and serves your purposes, everything depends on God, who is the inner self of all things."},{"head":"Against illusion","body":"Ramanuja argued that Advaita's doctrine of illusion (maya) cannot be made coherent. If the world is an illusion, whose illusion is it, and where does the ignorance that causes it reside? He held that scripture and perception both present the world as real."},{"head":"God with qualities","body":"Against the idea of a qualityless Absolute, Ramanuja held that Brahman is a personal God, identified with Vishnu or Narayana, possessing infinite good qualities such as knowledge, power and compassion."},{"head":"Devotion and surrender","body":"Liberation comes through bhakti, loving devotion to God, supported by knowledge and action, and in his tradition especially through prapatti, complete surrender to God's grace. The liberated soul does not dissolve into God but remains itself, enjoying God forever."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Start with the Vedarthasangraha, Ramanuja's own compact summary of his reading of the Upanishads and his objections to rival schools. It is much shorter than the Sri Bhasya.","critics":"Advaita philosophers argued that a Brahman containing real internal distinctions is no longer truly one. From the other side, Madhva argued that Ramanuja did not go far enough and that God, souls and matter are fully distinct rather than one qualified whole.","legacy":"Ramanuja is the central teacher of Sri Vaishnavism, a living tradition with millions of followers, and his defence of a personal God and the reality of the world gave a philosophical basis to the bhakti devotional movements of India.","quote":null},"protagoras":{"life":["Protagoras was born around 490 BCE in Abdera, a Greek city on the coast of Thrace. He lived in the golden age of classical Athens under Pericles, when democracy put a premium on public speaking: citizens argued their own cases in court and debated in the assembly. A new class of travelling teachers, the sophists, offered training in argument and public life for a fee. Protagoras was the first and most famous of them.","He spent his career travelling between Greek cities, visiting Athens several times and teaching the sons of wealthy families. According to later sources, he was asked to draft laws for the new Athenian colony of Thurii in southern Italy, founded around 444 BCE. He wrote books including Truth and On the Gods, but these are lost; only a few sentences survive, mostly quoted by others. He probably died around 420 BCE. Later stories that his books were burned and that he was expelled from Athens for impiety are doubted by many historians.","Most of what we know of him comes from Plato, who disagreed with him but treated him with respect. In Plato's dialogue Protagoras he appears as a confident, elderly celebrity who claims he can make young men good citizens. Plato's Theaetetus examines his most famous claim at length. Later writers such as Sextus Empiricus and Diogenes Laertius preserved a few more reports of his views, though they wrote centuries after his death."],"timeline":[[-490,"Born in Abdera, Thrace, c."],[-444,"Said by later sources to have drafted laws for the colony of Thurii, c."],[-420,"Dies, c."],[-380,"Plato writes the Protagoras, portraying him in debate with Socrates, c."],[-369,"Plato's Theaetetus examines his doctrine that man is the measure, c."]],"ideas":[{"head":"Man is the measure","body":"Protagoras claimed that human beings are the measure of all things: how things appear to each person is how they are for that person. If the wind feels cold to you and warm to me, there is no further fact about whether it is 'really' cold. This is often seen as the first statement of relativism."},{"head":"Uncertainty about the gods","body":"He began On the Gods by saying that he could not know whether the gods exist or what they are like, because the subject is obscure and human life is short. This makes him one of the earliest known agnostics."},{"head":"Two arguments on every question","body":"He taught that on every issue there are two opposing arguments. His students practised arguing both sides, a method still used in debating and law. Critics said he promised to 'make the weaker argument the stronger', which could sound like a recipe for trickery."},{"head":"Virtue can be taught","body":"In Plato's Protagoras he argues that civic virtue, such as justice and a sense of shame, can be taught, and that every citizen has some share of it. He tells a myth in which Zeus gives justice to all humans, not just experts, which explains why every citizen gets a say in Athenian democracy."},{"head":"Better, not truer","body":"In the Theaetetus, Plato has Protagoras reply that although no belief is truer than another, some are better: a wise teacher or doctor changes people's states so that better things appear to them, as a doctor makes food taste sweet again to a sick person."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Protagoras was a cynical con man who taught people to win arguments with no concern for truth or morality.","truth":"The popular image of sophists as tricksters comes mainly from their critics. Plato presents Protagoras respectfully, as someone with a serious theory of knowledge and of how civic virtue is taught."},"startHere":"Read Plato's Protagoras, especially the long speech in which Protagoras tells the myth of Prometheus and explains how virtue is taught (roughly 320c to 328d). It is lively, accessible and the best surviving portrait of him.","critics":"Plato, in the Theaetetus, argued that the doctrine refutes itself: if every belief is true for whoever holds it, then the belief of those who think Protagoras is wrong is also true, so Protagoras must concede he is wrong.","legacy":"Protagoras's sentence about man as the measure has been the starting point for debates about relativism ever since, and his idea that civic virtue can be taught to all citizens is an early defence of democratic education.","quote":{"text":"Man is the measure of all things, of the existence of things that are, and of the non-existence of things that are not.","src":"Truth (lost), as quoted in Plato, Theaetetus 152a","tr":"Benjamin Jowett"}},"madhva":{"life":["Madhva lived in South India in the thirteenth and early fourteenth centuries; tradition dates his life to 1238 to 1317, though some scholars prefer earlier dates. He was born near Udupi on the coast of present-day Karnataka. In his world the dominant philosophical school of Vedanta was Advaita, which taught that the individual self is ultimately identical with Brahman and the world of difference is unreal. Madhva rejected this completely.","According to traditional biographies, he became a monk while young, taking the name Anandatirtha, and soon clashed with his teachers over Advaita. He travelled widely in India debating rival scholars and wrote around thirty-seven works, including commentaries on the Brahma Sutras, the Bhagavad Gita and the Upanishads, and independent treatises such as the Anuvyakhyana and the Tattvasankhyana. He founded the Krishna temple at Udupi and established monasteries to carry on his teaching. Tradition says that in 1317 he disappeared while teaching rather than dying in the ordinary way.","The details of his life come mainly from a devotional biography written by a follower, the Madhva Vijaya, which includes many miracle stories that should be treated as legend. What is clear is that Madhva was a forceful debater and organiser who founded a lasting school of thought, known as Dvaita, 'dualism'. His followers still run the eight monasteries at Udupi, whose heads take turns managing worship at the Krishna temple."],"timeline":[[1238,"Born near Udupi, according to traditional dating"],[1280,"Writes the Anuvyakhyana, c.; exact date uncertain"],[1280,"Writes the Tattvasankhyana, a short list of the categories of reality, c."],[1317,"Dies or, according to tradition, disappears while teaching"],[1460,"Birth of Vyasatirtha, c., later the leading defender of Madhva's school against Advaita"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Difference is real","body":"Madhva taught that difference is built into reality. There are five basic differences: between God and souls, God and matter, one soul and another, souls and matter, and one piece of matter and another. You and I are really two, and neither of us is God."},{"head":"Independent and dependent","body":"Reality divides into two kinds: the independent, which is God (Vishnu) alone, and the dependent, which is everything else. Souls and matter exist eternally, but they depend on God for their existence and activity at every moment, as light in a room depends on the lamp."},{"head":"Knowledge we can trust","body":"Madhva held that knowledge, rightly gained, tells us how things really are. He grounded this in the 'witness' (sakshi), the self's own awareness, which he held cannot be mistaken about basic matters such as one's own existence. This realism opposed the Advaita claim that ordinary experience of plurality is error."},{"head":"Souls differ in nature","body":"Each soul has its own unchangeable nature. Some souls are fit for liberation, some will remain in the cycle of rebirth, and some, in his most controversial doctrine, are bound for eternal suffering. Even liberated souls differ in the degree of their bliss."},{"head":"Devotion within difference","body":"Because God and souls are forever distinct, liberation is not merging into God but loving, blissful relationship with Him. Knowledge of God's supremacy and devotion to Him, by His grace, are the path to liberation."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Begin with the Tattvasankhyana, a very short work that lists his categories of reality, read alongside a modern introduction such as Deepak Sarma's An Introduction to Madhva Vedanta.","critics":"Advaita thinkers such as Madhusudana Sarasvati, answering Vyasatirtha's defence of Madhva, argued that real difference cannot be coherently explained and that the Upanishads teach the identity of self and Brahman. Many readers, including later Hindu reformers, have also objected to his doctrine of eternal damnation.","legacy":"Madhva founded the Dvaita school, which is still active through the monasteries of Udupi, and his devotion to Vishnu influenced the Haridasa poet-saints of Karnataka and later Vaishnava movements.","quote":null},"vasubandhu":{"life":["Vasubandhu was an Indian Buddhist philosopher who lived probably in the fourth or fifth century CE; his exact dates are disputed and some scholars have even argued there were two authors with this name. He came from Purushapura (modern Peshawar) in Gandhara. By his time Buddhism had developed many scholastic schools, each building a detailed theory of the basic elements of experience, while the newer Mahayana movement was producing its own philosophies.","According to a traditional biography written perhaps one or two centuries later by the monk Paramartha (499 to 569), Vasubandhu first studied the Sarvastivada school's teachings in Kashmir. He then wrote the Treasury of Abhidharma (Abhidharmakosha) with his own commentary, which set out that school's system while criticising it from a rival viewpoint. Tradition says his elder brother Asanga later persuaded him to embrace Mahayana Buddhism. He then wrote short, dense works of the Yogacara school, such as the Twenty Verses and the Thirty Verses.","Vasubandhu was a systematic thinker who wrote in compact verse with prose explanations, a format that made his works easy to memorise and to comment on. His writings were translated into Chinese and Tibetan, and centuries of commentators have argued over their meaning. The Treasury of Abhidharma alone attracted many commentaries in India, and the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang translated it into Chinese. Tradition counts him, with Asanga, as a founder of the Yogacara school."],"timeline":[[350,"Born in Purushapura (Peshawar), c.; dates disputed"],[380,"Writes the Treasury of Abhidharma with its commentary, c."],[400,"Writes the Twenty Verses and Thirty Verses, c."],[430,"Dies, c."],[659,"Xuanzang compiles the Cheng weishi lun, a Chinese commentary on the Thirty Verses"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Appearance only","body":"In his Yogacara works Vasubandhu argues that what we take to be an outside world of objects is really consciousness appearing in the form of objects. The view is called vijnaptimatrata, 'appearance only' or 'consciousness only'."},{"head":"The dream argument","body":"Objectors asked how, if there are no outside objects, experiences can be fixed to particular places and times and shared by many people. Vasubandhu replied with examples from dreams: in a dream, things appear in definite places, and a dream can even have real effects on the body. So these features do not prove an outside world."},{"head":"Atoms cannot add up","body":"He argued that physical atoms make no sense. If an atom has no parts, it cannot touch other atoms on different sides, so atoms could never combine into larger objects. If it has parts, it is not really an atom. Either way, the idea of a world built from atoms collapses."},{"head":"The storehouse consciousness","body":"The Thirty Verses describe a deep layer of mind, the storehouse consciousness, which holds the 'seeds' left by past actions. These seeds ripen into future experiences, explaining how karma works and why our experience has continuity without a permanent self."},{"head":"Three natures","body":"Every experience can be seen in three ways: as imagined, the false split into a separate self and outside objects; as dependent, the flow of causes and conditions; and as perfected, that flow seen free of the false split. Awakening means moving from the first to the third."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Vasubandhu taught that nothing exists except my own private mind.","truth":"He accepted many streams of consciousness, not just one person's, and his aim was to remove the false division between self and object in order to end suffering. Scholars still debate whether his view is best called idealism or a kind of analysis of experience."},"startHere":"Read the Twenty Verses with his own short commentary, ideally in a modern annotated translation. It is brief and set out as replies to objections, so the arguments are easy to follow.","critics":"The Madhyamaka philosopher Candrakirti argued that consciousness is no more ultimately real than outside objects, so Yogacara wrongly privileges the mind. The Hindu Mimamsa philosopher Kumarila defended the existence of an outside world against Buddhist idealist arguments.","legacy":"The Treasury of Abhidharma became a standard textbook of Buddhist philosophy in India, Tibet and East Asia. His Yogacara works founded a tradition that shaped Chinese and Tibetan Buddhism and still interests philosophers of mind.","quote":null},"dharmakirti":{"life":["Dharmakirti was an Indian Buddhist philosopher of the seventh century; his dates are usually given as roughly 600 to 660, but they are uncertain. He lived in the era after the Gupta empire, when great monastic universities such as Nalanda attracted scholars from across Asia. Indian philosophers of all schools argued intensely about pramana, the reliable means of knowing, and public debates between Buddhists, Hindus and Jains were common.","Tibetan tradition holds that he was born in South India and studied at Nalanda. He was a follower, at one remove, of the earlier logician Dignaga, whose theory of knowledge he defended and greatly extended. His largest work, the Pramanavarttika ('Commentary on Valid Knowledge'), is formally a commentary on Dignaga but develops a full system. He also wrote shorter handbooks, including the Pramanaviniscaya and the Nyayabindu ('Drop of Reasoning'), and works on debate and on the existence of other minds.","Very little is reliably known about his personal life; the stories in later Tibetan histories are legends. His works show a demanding, technical thinker who wrote in compressed verse and expected readers to know the debates of every rival school. Later Buddhist commentators such as Dharmottara wrote detailed explanations of his works, and Hindu and Jain philosophers studied him closely in order to refute him. Several of his works long survived only in Tibetan translation, until Sanskrit manuscripts were found in Tibet in the twentieth century."],"timeline":[[600,"Born, c., according to Tibetan tradition in South India"],[640,"Writes the Pramanavarttika, c."],[640,"Writes the Nyayabindu, a short handbook of logic, c."],[660,"Dies, c."],[1200,"Sakya Pandita's Treasury of Reasoning helps make Dharmakirti's logic central to Tibetan study, c."]],"ideas":[{"head":"Two ways of knowing","body":"Dharmakirti held that there are only two reliable means of knowledge: perception and inference. Scripture or authority does not count on its own; a religious teaching must be tested by reason. If you are told there is fire on the hill, the reliable way to know is to see it or to infer it from smoke."},{"head":"Perception without concepts","body":"Pure perception grasps unique, momentary particulars before any labelling. Concepts such as 'cow' or 'blue' are added afterwards by the mind and are useful but never fully match the particular things. The vivid first instant of seeing is closer to reality than the word you put on it."},{"head":"Real means causally effective","body":"For Dharmakirti, to be real is to be able to produce effects. Things that can do something, like fire that burns, are real; general ideas cannot burn anything. He used this to argue that everything real is momentary, constantly arising and passing away."},{"head":"Meaning by exclusion","body":"Following Dignaga, he explained how general words work without real universals: the word 'cow' picks out things by excluding everything that is not a cow. This theory, called apoha, avoids positing an abstract 'cowness' existing in each cow."},{"head":"No creator God","body":"He argued that a permanent creator could not cause anything, because something unchanging cannot act at one time rather than another. He also rejected the claim that the Vedas are authoritative because they have no author."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Start with the Nyayabindu ('Drop of Reasoning'), a short handbook that sets out his account of perception, inference and fallacies in a clear order. Read it with a modern introduction, such as John Dunne's Foundations of Dharmakirti's Philosophy.","critics":"Hindu Nyaya philosophers, most famously Udayana in his defence of God's existence, argued that the order of the world requires an intelligent creator and that universals are real. Mimamsa thinkers defended the authority of the Vedas against him.","legacy":"Dharmakirti's system became the framework for later Indian debate across schools, and in Tibet his logic and theory of knowledge form a core part of monastic education and formal debate to this day.","quote":null},"nkrumah":{"life":["Kwame Nkrumah was born in 1909 at Nkroful in the Gold Coast, a British colony in West Africa that is now Ghana. In his lifetime almost all of Africa was ruled by European empires. After the Second World War, movements for independence grew rapidly, and African thinkers debated what free African states should look like: whether they should copy Western capitalism or Soviet communism, or build something of their own.","He went to the United States in 1935, studying at Lincoln University and the University of Pennsylvania. In 1945 he moved to London and helped organise the Fifth Pan-African Congress in Manchester. Returning home in 1947, he founded the Convention People's Party in 1949, was jailed for organising strikes, and won the 1951 election while still in prison. He led the Gold Coast to independence as Ghana in 1957, one of the first colonies in sub-Saharan Africa to do so, and became president in 1960. His rule grew increasingly authoritarian, and in 1966 he was overthrown in a coup while abroad. He died in exile in 1972.","Nkrumah was both a political leader and a writer who wanted a theory to guide action. His books mix philosophy, political economy and manifesto, and were written to persuade Africans and the wider world that political independence was only the first step towards real freedom. Several of his books, including Neo-Colonialism, were published while he was head of state, which gave them unusual attention at home and abroad."],"timeline":[[1909,"Born at Nkroful, Gold Coast"],[1935,"Travels to the United States to study"],[1945,"Helps organise the Fifth Pan-African Congress in Manchester"],[1949,"Founds the Convention People's Party"],[1957,"Leads Ghana to independence and becomes prime minister"],[1963,"Helps found the Organisation of African Unity; publishes Africa Must Unite"],[1966,"Overthrown in a military coup while abroad"],[1972,"Dies in Bucharest"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Africa must unite","body":"Nkrumah argued that small, separate African states would stay weak and easy to dominate. Only a political union of the whole continent, with shared economic planning and defence, could give Africa real power in the world."},{"head":"Neo-colonialism","body":"A country can be formally independent while its economy and politics are still controlled from outside, through foreign companies, debt, aid with strings attached and control of trade. Nkrumah called this neo-colonialism and saw it as the most dangerous form of imperialism, because it exercises power without responsibility."},{"head":"Consciencism","body":"Africa, he wrote, had been shaped by three influences: traditional African life, Islam and Euro-Christian culture. Consciencism was his name for a philosophy that would bring these together, keeping the humane values of African tradition while using modern science and industry."},{"head":"Socialism rooted in tradition","body":"Nkrumah believed traditional African societies were basically communal and egalitarian, treating each person as an end in themselves. He saw socialism as a modern way of renewing these values, rather than as a foreign import."},{"head":"Positive action","body":"In the independence struggle he promoted 'positive action', non-violent methods such as strikes, boycotts and political education, drawing partly on Gandhi's example, to pressure colonial rulers."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the introduction to Neo-Colonialism: The Last Stage of Imperialism (1965), which states his central argument in a few pages and remains widely cited in debates about global inequality.","critics":"Opponents in Ghana, including the sociologist and politician Kofi Busia, criticised him for jailing critics without trial under the Preventive Detention Act and for creating a one-party state. Later African philosophers, such as Kwame Gyekye, questioned whether traditional African societies were really socialist in the way he claimed.","legacy":"Nkrumah is honoured across Africa as a founder of Pan-Africanism and of the movement for African unity that led to today's African Union. His idea of neo-colonialism remains a key term in discussions of power between rich and poor countries.","quote":null},"hanfeizi":{"life":["Han Fei, known by his book's title Han Feizi ('Master Han Fei'), lived in the third century BCE, at the end of China's Warring States period. Seven major states fought constantly for supremacy, and philosophers competed to advise rulers on how to survive. Confucians urged rulers to govern by moral example and ritual, while others, later grouped together as 'Legalists', argued for strict laws and strong central control.","According to the historian Sima Qian, writing about a century later, Han Fei was a prince of the small, weak state of Han. He studied under the Confucian philosopher Xunzi, along with Li Si, who later became chief minister of the state of Qin. Han Fei's advice was ignored at home, so he wrote essays setting out his ideas. The king of Qin reportedly admired them. In 233 BCE Han Fei was sent to Qin as an envoy, but Li Si, perhaps out of rivalry, had him imprisoned, and he died there after being given poison.","Sima Qian also reports that Han Fei stammered and so expressed himself better in writing than in speech. His book, the Han Feizi, is a collection of essays, some probably by later hands, written in a sharp style full of short stories and examples. Many of its arguments are addressed directly to the ruler, warning him how ministers will try to deceive him, and its chapter on the difficulties of persuading a ruler is a vivid guide to court politics."],"timeline":[[-280,"Born a prince of the state of Han, c."],[-240,"Writes the essays collected in the Han Feizi, c."],[-233,"Sent to Qin as an envoy; imprisoned and dies by poison"],[-221,"Qin, using Legalist methods, unifies China"],[-213,"Burning of books under Qin, on the advice of Li Si"],[-206,"The Qin dynasty collapses, c."]],"ideas":[{"head":"People follow self-interest","body":"Han Fei assumed that people mostly act from self-interest. A carriage maker wants people to be rich, and a coffin maker wants people to die, not because one is kind and the other cruel, but because of where their profit lies. Good government should work with this fact rather than hope people will become virtuous."},{"head":"The two handles","body":"A ruler controls the state with two 'handles': rewards and punishments. They must be clear, publicly known and applied without exception, so that people know exactly what will happen if they obey or disobey the law. If ministers control rewards and punishments instead, the ruler loses power."},{"head":"Law, technique and position","body":"Han Fei combined three ideas from earlier thinkers: fa, published law; shu, techniques for checking that officials do what they claim; and shi, the power that comes from the ruler's position itself. A ruler of ordinary ability can govern well if he has all three."},{"head":"Don't wait by the stump","body":"He mocked those who wanted to govern by copying the ancient sage kings. He tells of a farmer who saw a rabbit run into a tree stump and die, then gave up farming to wait by the stump for another. Times change, and methods must change with them."},{"head":"The ruler keeps hidden","body":"The ruler should keep his own likes and dislikes hidden, so that ministers cannot flatter or manipulate him. He compares the names (claims and job titles) officials give themselves with their actual results and rewards or punishes accordingly."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Legalism was an early form of the rule of law, in which everyone, including the ruler, is bound by the law.","truth":"For Han Fei, law was a tool of the ruler. It bound subjects and officials strictly, but the ruler made the laws and stood above them."},"startHere":"Read the chapters 'The Two Handles' and 'The Five Vermin' in Burton Watson's selection Han Feizi: Basic Writings. They are short and show both his theory of control and his attack on appeals to the past.","critics":"Confucian critics, beginning with Han dynasty writers such as Jia Yi, argued that Qin's harsh Legalist rule explained why the dynasty collapsed only fifteen years after unifying China: a state held together only by fear has no loyalty to fall back on.","legacy":"Officially condemned by later Confucian orthodoxy, Han Fei's methods nonetheless shaped how Chinese imperial governments actually worked, in bureaucracy, law codes and the checking of officials. He is often compared to Machiavelli.","quote":null},"santayana":{"life":["George Santayana was born in Madrid in 1863 as Jorge Agustín Nicolás Ruiz de Santayana. His mother had earlier married an American, and in 1872 he joined her in Boston, while his father returned to Spain. He grew up between two cultures: Catholic Spain and Protestant New England. He kept his Spanish citizenship all his life and always felt somewhat an outsider in America.","Santayana studied at Harvard and in Germany, and taught philosophy at Harvard from 1889 to 1912, alongside William James and Josiah Royce. His students included T. S. Eliot and Walter Lippmann. His early books were The Sense of Beauty (1896) and the five volumes of The Life of Reason (1905 to 1906). In 1912, after an inheritance freed him from teaching, he left America for good. He lived in England and France and then settled in Rome, where he wrote Scepticism and Animal Faith (1923), the four-volume Realms of Being (1927 to 1940) and a best-selling novel, The Last Puritan (1935). He died in Rome in 1952.","Santayana was also a poet and a stylish essayist, and he wrote philosophy as literature, in long, elegant sentences rather than technical arguments. He described himself as a materialist, yet he loved religion as poetry and spent his last years living in a convent run by nuns in Rome. His autobiography, Persons and Places, published in parts in the 1940s and 1950s, describes his life with detached irony."],"timeline":[[1863,"Born in Madrid"],[1872,"Moves to Boston"],[1889,"Begins teaching philosophy at Harvard"],[1896,"Publishes The Sense of Beauty"],[1905,"Begins publishing The Life of Reason (five volumes, 1905 to 1906)"],[1912,"Leaves Harvard and America for Europe"],[1923,"Publishes Scepticism and Animal Faith"],[1952,"Dies in Rome"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Beauty is pleasure seen in the object","body":"In The Sense of Beauty he defined beauty as pleasure that we experience as if it were a quality of the thing itself. When a sunset seems beautiful, the delight is really ours, but we feel it as belonging to the sunset."},{"head":"Animal faith","body":"Santayana pushed scepticism to the limit: strictly, you cannot prove that the outside world, the past or even other people exist. But we are animals who must act, and acting already involves trusting that the world is there. He called this unavoidable trust 'animal faith'."},{"head":"The realms of being","body":"He divided reality into four 'realms': matter, the physical world that does all the causing; essence, the infinite range of possible qualities and forms; truth, the complete record of what actually happens; and spirit, the awareness that lights up some of what happens."},{"head":"Spirit is a powerless light","body":"Santayana was a materialist: matter does all the work in the world. Consciousness, or spirit, is real, but it is produced by the body and changes nothing; it is like the light of a fire, which shows the fire without fuelling it. This view is known as epiphenomenalism."},{"head":"The life of reason","body":"Reason, for Santayana, is not a cold calculating faculty but the harmonising of our many impulses into a balanced, happy life. Society, religion, art and science are all ways in which humans try, more or less well, to bring their desires into harmony."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The well-known saying that those who forget the past are doomed to repeat it comes from Winston Churchill or Edmund Burke.","truth":"The original line is Santayana's, from Reason in Common Sense (1905), the first volume of The Life of Reason, where it is part of an argument that progress depends on retaining experience."},"startHere":"Read Scepticism and Animal Faith (1923), especially its early chapters, which take doubt as far as it can go and then explain why we live by animal faith anyway. It is the best brief entry into his mature philosophy.","critics":"John Dewey, replying in 1927 to Santayana's critical review of Dewey's Experience and Nature, called Santayana's position a 'half-hearted naturalism' because it split powerless spirit off from the natural world. Others ask how a consciousness that causes nothing could be something we notice and talk about.","legacy":"Santayana is remembered as a major voice of American philosophy's classic period and as a master of philosophical prose. His naturalism and his idea of animal faith continue to interest philosophers who want to take both science and human experience seriously.","quote":null},"adorno":{"life":["Theodor W. Adorno was born in Frankfurt in 1903, the son of a Jewish wine merchant and a Catholic singer of Corsican descent. He grew up in the culture of German classical music and philosophy. His lifetime saw the collapse of the German Empire, the rise of fascism, the Holocaust and the post-war boom of consumer capitalism. These events raised the question at the heart of his work: how did a civilisation that prized reason produce such barbarism?","Adorno studied philosophy and music, and in 1925 studied composition in Vienna with the composer Alban Berg. He joined the circle of the Institute for Social Research, later called the Frankfurt School. When the Nazis took power in 1933 he lost his right to teach. He spent several years at Oxford, then went to the United States in 1938, first working in New York and then in Los Angeles, where he and Max Horkheimer wrote Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944). He returned to Frankfurt in 1949 and later directed the Institute. Clashes with radical students marked his final year. He died in 1969; Aesthetic Theory appeared after his death.","Adorno wrote in a dense, deliberately difficult style, believing that writing which is too easy to digest becomes just another product. He was also a trained composer and wrote widely on music, from Beethoven to the radio hits of his day. He also took part in empirical research, co-writing The Authoritarian Personality (1950), a large study of the psychology behind prejudice and support for fascism."],"timeline":[[1903,"Born in Frankfurt"],[1925,"Studies composition with Alban Berg in Vienna"],[1933,"Loses his right to teach under the Nazi regime"],[1938,"Emigrates to the United States"],[1944,"Completes Dialectic of Enlightenment with Max Horkheimer"],[1949,"Returns to Frankfurt"],[1966,"Publishes Negative Dialectics"],[1969,"Dies in Visp, Switzerland"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The culture industry","body":"Adorno and Horkheimer argued that films, radio, magazines and pop music are produced like any other industrial goods, to sell. The result is standardised entertainment that offers the same pleasures again and again and trains audiences to accept the world as it is. A blockbuster's formula plot is comfort, not challenge."},{"head":"Standardised music","body":"In 'On Popular Music' he argued that hit songs are built from interchangeable parts, so that listeners always know roughly what comes next. Small novelties, a catchy hook or a new sound, give an illusion of individuality while the overall form never changes."},{"head":"The dialectic of enlightenment","body":"Enlightenment reason set out to free people from myth and fear by mastering nature. But reason used only as a tool for control can turn against people too, treating them as things to be managed. Modern bureaucracy, propaganda and even the death camps showed how rational efficiency can serve barbarism."},{"head":"Non-identity","body":"Our concepts always simplify things; no label fully captures a particular person or object. Adorno's 'negative dialectics' tries to keep attention on what concepts leave out, rather than forcing everything into one neat system."},{"head":"The truth of art","body":"Serious art, such as the difficult music of Schoenberg or the plays of Beckett, refuses to be easily consumed. By resisting the demands of the market, it shows that the world could be otherwise and holds a kind of truth that society tries to suppress."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Adorno said it is impossible or forbidden to write poetry after Auschwitz.","truth":"His remark, in a 1949 essay, that writing poetry after Auschwitz is barbaric was a provocation about culture's entanglement with the society that produced the camps. He later qualified it in Negative Dialectics, and he admired post-war poets such as Paul Celan."},"startHere":"Read the chapter 'The Culture Industry: Enlightenment as Mass Deception' in Dialectic of Enlightenment. It is his most famous argument, and its examples from films and radio are easy to apply to today's media.","critics":"Jürgen Habermas, his former assistant, argued that Dialectic of Enlightenment criticises reason so totally that it undercuts the very reasoning it relies on. Scholars of popular culture, such as those in the Birmingham School, objected that audiences actively interpret and reuse mass culture rather than passively absorbing it, and many think Adorno badly misjudged jazz.","legacy":"Adorno's critique of the culture industry remains a starting point for discussions of media, advertising and entertainment, and his work on music and aesthetics is still widely studied in philosophy, musicology and sociology.","quote":null},"carnap":{"life":["Rudolf Carnap was born in 1891 in Ronsdorf, now part of Wuppertal in western Germany. He grew up as modern logic and physics were transforming how people thought about knowledge: Frege and Russell had shown how logic could express mathematics, and Einstein's relativity had overturned old certainties about space and time. Carnap wanted philosophy to become as clear and cooperative as science.","He studied at Jena, where he attended Frege's lectures on logic, and served in the First World War. In 1926 he went to the University of Vienna and became a leading member of the Vienna Circle, the group that developed logical empiricism. His Logical Structure of the World appeared in 1928. After teaching in Prague, where he wrote The Logical Syntax of Language (1934), he left Europe as the Nazis rose and moved to the United States in 1935. He taught at the University of Chicago and then UCLA, working on meaning, probability and inductive logic. He died in Santa Monica in 1970.","Carnap was known for his patience, careful precision and willingness to revise his views in response to criticism. He believed philosophy should proceed by building exact, testable frameworks rather than by grand pronouncements, and he was committed to science serving humane, socially progressive ends. His intellectual autobiography, written for a 1963 volume of essays on his work, recalls his conversations with Einstein, Wittgenstein and others."],"timeline":[[1891,"Born in Ronsdorf, Germany"],[1921,"Completes his doctorate on the philosophy of space"],[1926,"Joins the University of Vienna and the Vienna Circle"],[1928,"Publishes The Logical Structure of the World"],[1934,"Publishes The Logical Syntax of Language"],[1935,"Emigrates to the United States"],[1950,"Publishes 'Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology' and Logical Foundations of Probability"],[1970,"Dies in Santa Monica, California"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Meaning through experience","body":"With the Vienna Circle, Carnap held that a statement has factual meaning only if experience could, in principle, count for or against it. 'Water boils at 100 degrees at sea level' passes this test; claims about an Absolute beyond all experience do not."},{"head":"Overcoming metaphysics","body":"In a 1932 essay he argued that much traditional metaphysics consists of sentences that look meaningful but break the rules of logical syntax, with Heidegger's talk of 'the Nothing' as a leading example. Such sentences, he said, express an attitude to life, as art does, but state no facts."},{"head":"The principle of tolerance","body":"In logic there are no morals: anyone may build whatever language or logical system they like, as long as they state its rules clearly. The question is not which system is 'true' but which is most useful for a purpose, just as you choose metric or imperial units for convenience."},{"head":"Internal and external questions","body":"Within a framework, such as the language of numbers, the question 'Is there a prime between 10 and 20?' has a clear answer. But 'Do numbers really exist?', asked from outside any framework, is not a factual question at all. It is really a practical choice about whether to adopt the framework."},{"head":"Rational reconstruction","body":"Carnap tried to show how our concepts could be rebuilt step by step from simple experiences and logic. Philosophy's job, on this view, is 'explication': replacing vague everyday ideas with precise ones that do the same work better."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Carnap and the logical positivists thought that ethics, art and metaphysics were worthless.","truth":"Carnap said such statements have no factual content, but he held that they can express real attitudes and feelings about life, much as music does. He was himself deeply committed to moral and political values."},"startHere":"Read 'Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology' (1950), a short essay that explains his distinction between internal and external questions and shows his idea that many philosophical disputes are really choices of language.","critics":"W. V. O. Quine, in 'Two Dogmas of Empiricism' (1951), attacked Carnap's sharp distinction between truths of meaning and truths of fact, which his idea of choosing frameworks relies on. Many also objected that the verifiability criterion of meaning cannot itself be verified.","legacy":"Carnap was the most systematic logical empiricist and shaped philosophy of science and analytic philosophy across the twentieth century. Recent philosophers have revived his tolerant, practical approach to metaphysical disputes.","quote":null},"pfstrawson":{"life":["Peter Frederick Strawson was born in Ealing, London, in 1919. He studied at Oxford in the years when 'ordinary language philosophy' was flourishing there: philosophers such as Gilbert Ryle and J. L. Austin thought many philosophical puzzles came from ignoring how words are actually used. Strawson shared this attention to language, but went on to ask large questions about the basic structure of human thought.","After serving in the army during the Second World War, he became a fellow of University College, Oxford, in 1948. His paper 'On Referring' (1950) challenged Bertrand Russell's famous theory of descriptions. Individuals (1959) revived metaphysics in analytic philosophy, and 'Freedom and Resentment' (1962) changed the debate on free will. The Bounds of Sense (1966) offered an influential reading of Kant. From 1968 to 1987 he held the Waynflete Professorship of Metaphysical Philosophy at Oxford. He was knighted in 1977 and died in 2006.","Strawson was admired for the elegance and clarity of his prose and for his calm, unhurried way of arguing. He tended to defend the shared framework of everyday thought against philosophers who wanted to overturn it, preferring to describe how our concepts hang together rather than to revise them. Many of his books grew out of lectures, and he wrote for students as well as specialists, for example in Analysis and Metaphysics (1992), an introduction based on his Oxford teaching."],"timeline":[[1919,"Born in Ealing, London"],[1948,"Becomes a fellow of University College, Oxford"],[1950,"Publishes 'On Referring'"],[1959,"Publishes Individuals"],[1962,"Delivers 'Freedom and Resentment' as a British Academy lecture"],[1966,"Publishes The Bounds of Sense"],[1968,"Becomes Waynflete Professor of Metaphysical Philosophy at Oxford"],[2006,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Reactive attitudes","body":"In 'Freedom and Resentment' Strawson pointed out that our practices of blame and praise rest on attitudes like resentment, gratitude, indignation and forgiveness. If someone treads on your hand on purpose, you feel resentment; if by accident, you do not. These attitudes respond to what people's actions show about their goodwill towards us."},{"head":"Blame without metaphysics","body":"Even if science showed that determinism is true, Strawson argued, we could not and should not give up these attitudes wholesale; they are part of being human and of having relationships at all. So the free will debate cannot be settled by metaphysics alone. We do sometimes suspend them, for example towards small children or people who are seriously ill, but these are exceptions within the practice."},{"head":"Referring and presupposing","body":"Russell held that 'The present king of France is bald' is false, since no such king exists. Strawson replied that the sentence presupposes there is a king; when there is not, the question of truth or falsity simply does not arise. Using a phrase to refer is something speakers do, not something words do by themselves."},{"head":"Descriptive metaphysics","body":"Instead of trying to revise our basic picture of the world, philosophy can describe its deepest structure. In Individuals he argued that our thought depends on identifying material bodies in space and time, and on the concept of a person, a being to whom we apply both mental and physical descriptions."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Freedom and Resentment' (1962). It is a short lecture, needs no technical background and remains one of the most discussed essays on free will and moral responsibility.","critics":"Critics such as Gary Watson argued that our reactive attitudes themselves respond to facts about a person's history and capacities, as when learning of a murderer's abused childhood softens our blame, so the metaphysical questions Strawson set aside return. Russell replied sharply to 'On Referring' in 1957, arguing that Strawson had misunderstood what his theory was for.","legacy":"'Freedom and Resentment' founded a whole approach to moral responsibility based on our emotional responses, and Individuals made metaphysics respectable again in analytic philosophy.","quote":null},"dummett":{"life":["Michael Dummett was born in London in 1925. He grew up as analytic philosophy was establishing itself in Britain, and he came to believe that the philosophy of language, the study of meaning, should be the foundation of all philosophy. He also devoted much of his career to Gottlob Frege, the German logician whom he regarded as the founder of modern analytic philosophy.","After wartime service in the army, he studied at Christ Church, Oxford, and in 1950 was elected a fellow of All Souls College. His paper 'Truth' (1959) launched the debate about realism and anti-realism. Frege: Philosophy of Language (1973) made his reputation, followed by Elements of Intuitionism (1977) and The Logical Basis of Metaphysics (1991). From 1979 to 1992 he was Wykeham Professor of Logic at Oxford. He was knighted in 1999 and died in 2011.","Dummett was a man of wide interests. Alongside philosophy he wrote on voting systems and on the history of tarot cards and card games. He was a devout Catholic convert, and in the 1960s and 1970s he campaigned actively against racism and for the rights of immigrants in Britain, at times setting philosophy aside to do so. His book On Immigration and Refugees (2001) drew on that experience."],"timeline":[[1925,"Born in London"],[1950,"Elected a fellow of All Souls College, Oxford"],[1959,"Publishes the paper 'Truth'"],[1973,"Publishes Frege: Philosophy of Language"],[1977,"Publishes Elements of Intuitionism"],[1979,"Becomes Wykeham Professor of Logic at Oxford"],[1991,"Publishes The Logical Basis of Metaphysics"],[2011,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Meaning is use","body":"Dummett argued that the meaning of a sentence must be something speakers can show in how they use it. Understanding cannot be a private, hidden state; if you understand a sentence, there must be some way your understanding shows up in what you say and do."},{"head":"Truth and what we could know","body":"If understanding consists in abilities we can display, then knowing what a sentence means is knowing what would count as showing it to be true. On this anti-realist view, truth cannot completely outrun anything we could, even in principle, come to know."},{"head":"Doubting bivalence","body":"Take the sentence 'Julius Caesar sneezed exactly twice on his tenth birthday.' A realist says it is either true or false, though we will never know which. Dummett questioned this principle, called bivalence, for sentences that nothing could ever settle."},{"head":"Intuitionistic logic","body":"In mathematics, intuitionists accept a statement only when there is a proof of it, and they reject the rule that every statement is either true or false. Dummett argued that this logic, not classical logic, may be the correct one if meaning is tied to proof and evidence."},{"head":"Philosophy of language first","body":"Many old metaphysical disputes, about the past, about other minds, about mathematics, can be restated as disputes about what makes sentences true. Dummett proposed that settling these questions of meaning is the way to settle the metaphysics."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the essay 'Truth' (1959), reprinted in his collection Truth and Other Enigmas. It is short and sets up the realism debate that occupied him for the rest of his life.","critics":"Realists objected that Dummett's view would leave many statements about the unrecorded past neither true nor false, which seems absurd; Dummett himself wrestled with this problem in later work such as Truth and the Past (2004). Others, including John McDowell, rejected his requirement that understanding be fully displayed in behaviour.","legacy":"Dummett put the realism versus anti-realism debate at the centre of analytic philosophy and did more than anyone to establish Frege as a founding figure of the field.","quote":null},"kuhn":{"life":["Thomas Kuhn was born in Cincinnati, Ohio, in 1922. He trained as a physicist at a time when science enjoyed huge prestige after the Second World War, and most philosophers pictured science as steadily piling up truths by testing hypotheses. Kuhn's own historical studies led him to a very different picture, which changed how people talk about science and knowledge.","He studied physics at Harvard, completing his doctorate in 1949. While teaching a general science course designed by Harvard's president James Conant, he read Aristotle's physics and was struck that it made sense on its own terms rather than being simply bad modern physics. He turned to the history of science, publishing The Copernican Revolution in 1957. While at the University of California, Berkeley, he wrote The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962), first published in a series on the unity of science edited by Rudolf Carnap and Charles Morris. He later taught at Princeton and MIT, and died in 1996.","Kuhn was a careful historian who spent years with old scientific texts. He was often surprised and frustrated by how his ideas were read, and spent much of his later career clarifying and correcting what critics and admirers thought he had said. In The Essential Tension (1977) he collected essays answering his critics, and at his death he was working on a book developing his later views on meaning and incommensurability."],"timeline":[[1922,"Born in Cincinnati, Ohio"],[1949,"Completes his PhD in physics at Harvard"],[1957,"Publishes The Copernican Revolution"],[1962,"Publishes The Structure of Scientific Revolutions"],[1964,"Moves to Princeton University"],[1970,"Second edition of Structure adds a postscript answering critics"],[1979,"Moves to the Massachusetts Institute of Technology"],[1996,"Dies in Cambridge, Massachusetts"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Paradigms","body":"A paradigm is a shared model of good science: key examples, theories, methods and standards that a scientific community takes for granted. Newton's mechanics, for instance, told physicists for two centuries which problems mattered and what a solution looked like."},{"head":"Normal science is puzzle-solving","body":"Most of the time, scientists do not try to overturn their paradigm. They solve puzzles within it, much as someone doing a jigsaw assumes the picture is there to be completed. When a result does not fit, the first thought is usually that the experiment went wrong, not the theory."},{"head":"Anomalies, crisis and revolution","body":"Over time, anomalies, results that resist explanation, pile up. If they become serious enough, the field enters a crisis, and a new paradigm may replace the old one, as Einstein's physics replaced Newton's. Kuhn called these replacements scientific revolutions."},{"head":"Incommensurability","body":"Rival paradigms can be hard to compare directly, because key terms shift meaning and the standards for judging theories change too. 'Mass' does not mean quite the same thing for Newton and Einstein. In this sense, Kuhn said, after a revolution scientists work in a different world."},{"head":"Values guide theory choice","body":"In later work Kuhn listed shared values used to choose between theories: accuracy, consistency, scope, simplicity and fruitfulness. These guide choice without forming a rigid algorithm, because scientists can weigh them differently."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Kuhn showed that science is irrational, just a matter of fashion or power, and makes no real progress.","truth":"Kuhn insisted that science does progress, becoming steadily better at solving puzzles, and that theory choice is guided by shared values like accuracy and simplicity. He rejected the radical relativist readings of his work."},"startHere":"Read The Structure of Scientific Revolutions itself, starting with its first three chapters on normal science. It is short, written without technical jargon, and full of historical examples.","critics":"Karl Popper and Imre Lakatos argued that Kuhn made scientific change look like mob psychology rather than rational criticism. Margaret Masterman pointed out that he used 'paradigm' in many different senses, which led Kuhn to clarify the term in his 1969 postscript.","legacy":"The Structure of Scientific Revolutions is one of the most cited academic books of the twentieth century; it helped create the history and sociology of science as fields and put 'paradigm shift' into everyday language.","quote":null},"berlin":{"life":["Isaiah Berlin was born in 1909 in Riga, then part of the Russian Empire, into a Jewish family. As a child in Petrograd he witnessed the Russian Revolution of 1917, and in 1921 his family emigrated to England. His century was shaped by communism, fascism and the totalitarian regimes that justified terrible cruelty in the name of a perfect future. Much of his work asks how ideas about freedom and human perfection could lead to such results.","Berlin studied at Oxford and spent most of his life there. His biography of Karl Marx appeared in 1939. During the Second World War he wrote political reports for the British government from New York and Washington, and in 1945 he visited the Soviet Union, where he met the poets Anna Akhmatova and Boris Pasternak. The Hedgehog and the Fox appeared in 1953. As Chichele Professor of Social and Political Theory he gave his lecture Two Concepts of Liberty in 1958. He was the founding president of Wolfson College, Oxford, and president of the British Academy. He died in Oxford in 1997.","Berlin was famous as a brilliant talker and lecturer, and much of his work took the form of essays and lectures rather than systematic treatises. He wrote much of it as a historian of ideas, bringing to life thinkers such as Vico, Herder and the Russian radicals of the nineteenth century. He was knighted in 1957 and received the Order of Merit in 1971, and many of his scattered essays reached a wide audience only when later collected by his editor, Henry Hardy."],"timeline":[[1909,"Born in Riga"],[1921,"Emigrates with his family to England"],[1939,"Publishes Karl Marx: His Life and Environment"],[1945,"Visits the Soviet Union and meets Anna Akhmatova and Boris Pasternak"],[1953,"Publishes The Hedgehog and the Fox"],[1958,"Delivers Two Concepts of Liberty as his Oxford inaugural lecture"],[1966,"Becomes founding president of Wolfson College, Oxford"],[1997,"Dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Negative liberty","body":"Negative liberty is freedom from interference: the area in which you can act without others stopping you. If no one prevents you from choosing your religion or your job, you are free in this sense. Berlin saw protecting this area as essential to a decent society."},{"head":"Positive liberty","body":"Positive liberty is freedom as self-mastery: being ruled by your 'true' or 'higher' self rather than by passing impulses. Berlin warned that this idea is easily abused. A government can claim to know your true self better than you do, and then force you to be 'free', as many twentieth-century regimes did."},{"head":"Value pluralism","body":"Human values are many, and they can genuinely conflict: liberty and equality, justice and mercy, honesty and kindness. There is no single master value or formula that settles every conflict. Telling a friend a hurtful truth may protect honesty at a real cost to kindness, and something is lost either way."},{"head":"Against the perfect society","body":"If values truly conflict, then no ideal society can contain all goods at once. Berlin argued that the belief in a final, perfect solution has been used to justify sacrificing real people. Recognising pluralism supports tolerance, compromise and liberal institutions."},{"head":"Hedgehogs and foxes","body":"Borrowing a line from the Greek poet Archilochus, Berlin divided thinkers into hedgehogs, who relate everything to one central vision, and foxes, who pursue many different ends without a single system. Dante and Plato were hedgehogs; Shakespeare and Aristotle were foxes."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Berlin thought positive liberty was simply a bad idea and only negative liberty matters.","truth":"Berlin accepted that both are genuine values and that self-government matters. His warning was that positive liberty has historically been more easily twisted to justify oppression."},"startHere":"Read Two Concepts of Liberty (1958), a single lecture that sets out his distinction between negative and positive liberty and his case for pluralism.","critics":"Gerald MacCallum argued that there is really only one concept of freedom, always freedom of someone from something to do something, and Charles Taylor argued in 'What's Wrong with Negative Liberty' (1979) that freedom must include the ability to pursue what really matters to us. Others, including Leo Strauss, argued that value pluralism slides into relativism, which Berlin denied.","legacy":"Berlin's distinction between negative and positive liberty is a standard starting point for discussions of freedom, and his value pluralism remains a major position in moral and political philosophy.","quote":null},"oakeshott":{"life":["Michael Oakeshott was born in 1901 at Chelsfield in Kent, England. He lived through an age of ideologies: communism, fascism and, in Britain after 1945, ambitious plans to reshape society through central planning. Many intellectuals believed that politics should be guided by a rational blueprint. Oakeshott became one of the most original critics of that belief. He argued that most of what matters in politics cannot be written down as a plan.","He studied history at Gonville and Caius College, Cambridge, and taught there for many years. His first major book, Experience and Its Modes (1933), was a work of idealist philosophy. During the Second World War he served in the British army. In 1951 he became Professor of Political Science at the London School of Economics, succeeding the socialist Harold Laski. His essays, including 'Rationalism in Politics' and 'On Being Conservative', were collected in Rationalism in Politics (1962). After retiring from the LSE in 1969 he published On Human Conduct (1975), his most systematic work. He died in Dorset in 1990.","Oakeshott wrote in a graceful, ironic style and valued conversation, poetry and the enjoyment of present things over grand purposes. He avoided party politics and never aimed to found a school, though his ideas attracted many followers. His introduction to Hobbes's Leviathan (1946) is still read as one of the best essays on that book. He also wrote on education, describing a university as a place of conversation and learning for its own sake rather than job training."],"timeline":[[1901,"Born at Chelsfield, Kent"],[1933,"Publishes Experience and Its Modes"],[1947,"Publishes the essay 'Rationalism in Politics'"],[1951,"Becomes Professor of Political Science at the London School of Economics"],[1962,"Publishes Rationalism in Politics and Other Essays"],[1975,"Publishes On Human Conduct"],[1990,"Dies in Dorset"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Against rationalism in politics","body":"The rationalist believes that politics can be guided by abstract principles and blueprints, applied like a technique. Oakeshott thought this misunderstands how human activities work, and leads to grand schemes that ignore the experience built into existing institutions."},{"head":"Practical knowledge","body":"There are two kinds of knowledge: technical knowledge, which can be written down in rules, and practical knowledge, which is learned only by doing. A cookery book does not make a cook; the book is an abridgement of what good cooks already know. Politics, like cooking, depends mostly on practical knowledge."},{"head":"Pursuing intimations","body":"Political activity, he said, is not about imposing a vision but about attending to the hints, or 'intimations', within a society's existing traditions. Reform should develop what is already there, as when the extension of voting rights followed from principles already partly present."},{"head":"Civil and enterprise association","body":"In On Human Conduct he contrasted two ideas of the state. An enterprise association is united by a shared goal, like a company. A civil association is united only by common rules that let people pursue their own different goals. Oakeshott favoured civil association, warning that treating the state as an enterprise threatens freedom."},{"head":"The conservative disposition","body":"To be conservative, in his sense, is to prefer the familiar to the unknown and present enjoyment to utopian promise. This is a temperament, not an ideology: he argued that even an anti-planning plan, such as Hayek's, belongs to the same rationalist style of politics."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Oakeshott was a Conservative Party ideologue who was against reason itself.","truth":"He criticised 'rationalism', the belief that technical knowledge alone is enough, not reason as such. He kept his distance from party politics and criticised ideological doctrines of every kind, including free-market ones."},"startHere":"Read the essay 'On Being Conservative' (1956), collected in Rationalism in Politics. It is short, readable and sets out his idea of conservatism as a disposition rather than a doctrine.","critics":"Critics argue that his trust in tradition gives little guidance when traditions are themselves unjust, and that appeals to 'intimations' can be used to defend almost any status quo. Others note that the line between practical and technical knowledge is less sharp than he suggests.","legacy":"Oakeshott is regarded as one of the most important British political philosophers of the twentieth century, and his critique of rationalism remains central to modern conservative and liberal thought.","quote":null},"macintyre":{"life":["Alasdair MacIntyre was born in Glasgow in 1929 and died in 2025. He grew up in post-war Britain, when moral philosophy was dominated by the view that ethical statements mainly express feelings or choices. His intellectual life ran through Marxism, analytic philosophy and finally the tradition of Aristotle and Thomas Aquinas, and he became one of the sharpest critics of modern moral culture.","He studied classics at Queen Mary College, London, and philosophy at Manchester, and taught at several British universities, including Leeds, Oxford and Essex. His early work engaged closely with Marxism and Christianity. In 1970 he moved to the United States, where he taught at universities including Brandeis, Boston University, Vanderbilt and Duke, and above all Notre Dame. After Virtue (1981) made him widely known. In the 1980s he became a Catholic and a defender of Thomism, developing his views in Whose Justice? Which Rationality? (1988), Three Rival Versions of Moral Enquiry (1990) and Dependent Rational Animals (1999).","MacIntyre combined philosophy with history and sociology, arguing that moral ideas can only be understood in the social settings where they were lived. He continued to write into old age, publishing Ethics in the Conflicts of Modernity in 2016. He openly revised and criticised his own earlier positions over the decades. His writing ranges widely, from Greek philosophy and medieval theology to the ethics of modern professions and the experience of disability."],"timeline":[[1929,"Born in Glasgow"],[1953,"Publishes Marxism: An Interpretation"],[1970,"Moves to the United States"],[1981,"Publishes After Virtue"],[1988,"Publishes Whose Justice? Which Rationality?"],[1999,"Publishes Dependent Rational Animals"],[2016,"Publishes Ethics in the Conflicts of Modernity"],[2025,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Moral talk in fragments","body":"MacIntyre opens After Virtue with an imagined world where science has been destroyed and people repeat scraps of scientific language without understanding them. Modern moral language, he argues, is like that: words like 'right', 'duty' and 'virtue' are left over from older traditions, and without those traditions our debates about abortion or war go on forever without resolution."},{"head":"Practices and internal goods","body":"A practice, such as chess, farming or medicine, has goods internal to it that can only be gained by doing it well. A child paid in sweets to play chess may cheat to win the sweets; only when she comes to value the skill of chess itself does she have reason not to cheat. Virtues are the qualities that let us achieve such internal goods."},{"head":"Life as a narrative","body":"We understand a human life as a story with a beginning, middle and end. Asking 'what is good for me?' means asking how my life's story can best be lived and completed. Virtues are the qualities that sustain us on that quest."},{"head":"Rationality depends on tradition","body":"There is no neutral standpoint from which to judge all moral views. Reasoning always happens within a tradition, such as Aristotelian or liberal individualist, though traditions can still be rationally compared by how well they solve their own problems and those of rivals."},{"head":"Dependent rational animals","body":"In later work he stressed that humans are vulnerable animals who depend on others in childhood, illness and old age. Virtues of acknowledged dependence, such as giving and receiving care graciously, belong at the centre of ethics."}],"misconception":{"myth":"MacIntyre was a communitarian who defended traditional conservative politics.","truth":"He rejected the communitarian label and remained a strong critic of both the liberal state and capitalism, drawing on his earlier Marxism. His hope lay in small local communities, not in conservative national politics."},"startHere":"Read chapter 1 of After Virtue, 'A Disquieting Suggestion', and then chapter 14, 'The Nature of the Virtues', where he explains practices with the chess example.","critics":"Critics, including Martha Nussbaum in a 1989 review, argued that his account of tradition-bound rationality risks relativism and an authoritarian deference to tradition, and that he underrates what liberal societies achieve.","legacy":"After Virtue helped revive virtue ethics and Aristotelian thought in moral philosophy and has been widely read outside philosophy, influencing theologians, political theorists and debates about professions and community life.","quote":null},"sandel":{"life":["Michael Sandel was born in 1953 in Minneapolis, Minnesota. He came into philosophy shortly after John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) had made liberal ideas of justice dominant in political philosophy. Sandel became one of the leading critics of this liberalism, arguing that questions of justice cannot be separated from questions about the good life and the communities we belong to.","He studied at Brandeis University and then, as a Rhodes Scholar, at Oxford, where he completed his doctorate. He has taught at Harvard since 1980. His first book, Liberalism and the Limits of Justice (1982), criticised Rawls's view of the self. Later books include Democracy's Discontent (1996), The Case against Perfection (2007), on genetic enhancement, What Money Can't Buy (2012) and The Tyranny of Merit (2020). He served on the US President's Council on Bioethics in the early 2000s.","Sandel is best known as a teacher. His undergraduate course 'Justice' has been taken by thousands of Harvard students and was among the first Harvard courses made freely available online, reaching millions. He also presents public debates in which audiences reason together about hard moral cases, using questions rather than lectures. His BBC radio series The Public Philosopher took this approach to audiences around the world."],"timeline":[[1953,"Born in Minneapolis, Minnesota"],[1980,"Begins teaching at Harvard"],[1982,"Publishes Liberalism and the Limits of Justice"],[1996,"Publishes Democracy's Discontent"],[2009,"Publishes Justice: What's the Right Thing to Do?; his Justice course goes online"],[2012,"Publishes What Money Can't Buy"],[2020,"Publishes The Tyranny of Merit"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The unencumbered self","body":"Rawls asked us to imagine choosing principles of justice without knowing our place in society or our values. Sandel argued that this pictures the self as 'unencumbered', standing apart from all its commitments. But much of who we are, as members of a family, community or nation, is not chosen, and it shapes what we owe."},{"head":"Justice is not neutral","body":"Liberals often say the state should stay neutral between different views of the good life. Sandel argues that this is impossible on many issues: to decide about same-sex marriage, for instance, we must ask what marriage is for and what it honours. Politics should engage openly with moral and religious questions."},{"head":"What money can't buy","body":"Markets now reach into areas where they did not used to belong, such as paying children to read books or selling the right to jump queues. Sandel argues this can be unfair, because the poor have no real choice, and can corrupt goods by changing what they mean, as when paying for reading teaches children that reading is a chore."},{"head":"The tyranny of merit","body":"The idea that success simply reflects merit leads winners to believe they deserve everything they have and losers to feel they deserve their fate. Sandel argues this has fuelled resentment and populist anger, and calls for renewed respect for the dignity of work."},{"head":"Civic republicanism","body":"Sandel draws on the republican tradition, which sees freedom as sharing in self-government. Citizens need a sense of belonging, concern for the whole and a moral bond with the community whose fate is at stake."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Sandel is a communitarian who thinks the majority's values should simply be imposed on everyone.","truth":"Although often grouped with communitarians, he has distanced himself from that label. He argues that justice must engage with questions of the good, not that whatever a community currently values is right."},"startHere":"Read Justice: What's the Right Thing to Do? (2009), starting with its first chapter, which uses real cases to introduce the main theories of justice in plain language.","critics":"Defenders of Rawls argued that Sandel misread him: in Political Liberalism (1993) Rawls explained that the original position is a thought experiment, not a claim about the nature of the self. Others, such as Will Kymlicka, argued that we can revise our communal attachments, so liberal neutrality protects rather than ignores them.","legacy":"Sandel's critique of Rawls helped define the liberal-communitarian debate of the 1980s, and his teaching has brought political philosophy to an unusually large public audience worldwide.","quote":null},"mahavira":{"life":["Mahavira lived in the Ganges plain of north-east India, in roughly the same period as the Buddha. Jain tradition dates his life to 599–527 BCE, but many historians place it somewhat later, in the 5th century BCE. It was a time of growing cities and kingdoms, and of wandering teachers who rejected the priestly sacrifices of the Vedic religion and searched for release from the cycle of rebirth.","According to Jain tradition he was born Vardhamana, son of a ruling family near Vaishali. At about thirty he left home to live as a wandering ascetic, enduring some twelve years of fasting, silence and hardship. Jains hold that he then reached kevala, complete knowledge, and spent about thirty years teaching and organising communities of monks, nuns and lay followers. He died at Pava. The two main Jain branches disagree on details: Digambaras say he never married, Svetambaras that he married and had a daughter.","Almost everything known about Mahavira comes from Jain scriptures written down long after his death, so the details are traditional rather than historical record. Jains do not see him as the founder of a new religion but as the twenty-fourth and last tirthankara, a 'ford-maker' who rediscovered and taught an eternal path. His teaching was carried on orally by his disciples and later gathered into the Jain canon, the Agamas."],"timeline":[[-599,"Born near Vaishali, according to the traditional Jain dating (many historians prefer a later date)"],[-569,"Leaves home to become a wandering ascetic, at about thirty (traditional date)"],[-557,"Reaches kevala, complete knowledge, after some twelve years of austerity (traditional date)"],[-527,"Dies at Pava, traditionally aged seventy-two"],[-300,"c. Jain monks begin gathering his teachings into a canon, traditionally at a council in Pataliputra"],[453,"c. The Svetambara canon is written down at a council in Valabhi (dated 453 or 466)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Souls and karma","body":"Every living thing has a soul (jiva) that is by nature all-knowing and blissful. Our actions draw in karma, which Jains understand as a fine kind of matter that sticks to the soul and clouds it. Freedom comes from stopping new karma and burning off the old."},{"head":"Non-violence above all","body":"Ahimsa, not harming any living being, is the centre of Jain ethics. Since even tiny creatures have souls, Jain monks sweep the path before them and many lay Jains avoid root vegetables. Harm done in thought or speech counts too."},{"head":"Five great vows","body":"Monks and nuns vow non-violence, truthfulness, not stealing, chastity and non-possession. Lay followers keep lighter versions of the same vows. The idea is that attachment to things and people binds the soul, so letting go is the path to freedom."},{"head":"No creator god","body":"The universe has always existed and was not made by any god. Liberated souls are worthy of worship as examples, but they do not run the world or grant favours. Each person must free themselves by their own effort."},{"head":"Many-sidedness","body":"Jain thinkers later developed the doctrine of anekantavada, that reality has many sides and no single viewpoint captures it all. They traced it to Mahavira's way of answering questions. Like the blind men each touching one part of an elephant, rival views can each be partly right."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Mahavira founded Jainism.","truth":"Jains regard him as the last of twenty-four tirthankaras who taught an eternal path, and historians think his predecessor Parshva was probably a real teacher too. Mahavira reformed and organised an existing tradition."},"startHere":"Read the first book of the Acaranga Sutra, one of the oldest Jain texts, in a translation such as Hermann Jacobi's. It gives the earliest picture of Mahavira's teaching on non-violence and his own life as an ascetic.","critics":"Buddhist texts portray the Buddha rejecting extreme austerities as a path to freedom, an implicit criticism of the Jain way. Other Indian schools objected that treating karma as a physical substance makes moral action into something like chemistry.","legacy":"Jainism remains a living religion with several million followers. Its strict ethic of non-violence influenced Gandhi, who grew up in Gujarat among Jains, and through him modern movements for non-violent protest.","quote":null},"carneades":{"life":["Carneades was born in Cyrene, a Greek city in what is now Libya, around 214 BCE. He spent his career in Athens, then the centre of the philosophical world, where the Stoics, Epicureans, Aristotelians and Plato's Academy argued with each other. In his time the Academy had turned sceptical: rather than teaching Plato's doctrines, it specialised in showing that nobody, especially the Stoics, could back up their claims to certain knowledge.","Carneades became head of the Academy and its most famous debater. In 155 BCE Athens sent him to Rome with a Stoic and an Aristotelian to plead a legal case. While there he lectured in praise of justice one day and argued against it the next. According to Plutarch, Cato the Elder urged the Senate to send the philosophers home quickly before they corrupted Roman youth. Carneades lived to about 85.","Like Socrates, Carneades wrote nothing. His views survive through his student and successor Clitomachus, and through later writers such as Cicero and Sextus Empiricus. His followers already disagreed about whether he held any views of his own, or only argued both sides to show that no claim could be proved. He was famous for arguing against Chrysippus, the leading Stoic, and is reported to have said that without Chrysippus he would not have existed."],"timeline":[[-214,"c. Born in Cyrene, North Africa"],[-155,"Visits Rome as an envoy from Athens; lectures for and against justice"],[-129,"c. Dies in Athens, aged about 85"],[-127,"c. Clitomachus, who recorded his arguments, becomes head of the Academy"],[-45,"Cicero writes the Academica, a main source for Carneades' views"],[-44,"Cicero's On Divination and On Fate report his arguments against the Stoics"]],"ideas":[{"head":"No sure sign of truth","body":"The Stoics claimed some impressions are so clear they cannot be false. Carneades replied that for any true impression, there could be a false one exactly like it: identical twins, a dream, an illusion. So no impression carries a guarantee of its own truth."},{"head":"Living by the persuasive","body":"If nothing is certain, how can we act? Carneades answered that some impressions are more persuasive (pithanon) than others. Better still are ones that fit with all our other impressions, and best are ones we have checked carefully. A doctor or a juror acts this way every day without certainty."},{"head":"Arguing both sides","body":"Carneades often argued for a view and then against it, as in his Roman speeches on justice. The aim was to show that reason alone could not settle the question. This method shaped Cicero and, through him, later traditions of debate."},{"head":"Against fate and divination","body":"He attacked the Stoic belief that everything is fated and that the gods send signs of the future. If the future were fixed, he argued, there would be nothing up to us; and if events happen by chance, no sign could predict them."}],"misconception":{"myth":"A sceptic like Carneades must think every belief is as good as any other.","truth":"Carneades graded impressions by how persuasive and well tested they were. He denied certainty, not the difference between better and worse reasons."},"startHere":"Read the second book of Cicero's Academica (the Lucullus), which sets out the debate between Carneades' sceptical Academy and its critics. It is the fullest ancient account of his ideas on persuasive impressions.","critics":"The Stoics pressed the 'inaction' objection: someone who suspends all judgement could never act, since acting requires accepting something as true. Carneades' own followers disagreed about how far he answered this, with Clitomachus saying he never firmly endorsed even persuasive impressions.","legacy":"His theory of probable belief anticipates later ideas of acting under uncertainty, and through Cicero it reached Renaissance and early modern sceptics such as Montaigne and Hume. The 'plank of Carneades', a case of two shipwrecked men and one plank, is still used in law and ethics.","quote":null},"bharata":{"life":["Nothing reliable is known about Bharata Muni as a person. He is the traditional author of the Natyashastra, the oldest surviving Indian treatise on drama, dance and music. The work was probably composed between about 200 BCE and 200 CE, and most scholars think it grew over time with several hands contributing, so 'Bharata' may be a name attached to a tradition rather than a single writer. It comes from a world where Sanskrit drama was performed at courts and temples.","The text itself opens with a myth: the gods ask Brahma for an entertainment that all classes of people can enjoy, and Brahma creates drama as a 'fifth Veda', which he passes to the sage Bharata and his hundred sons to perform. This story explains the work's authority rather than giving history. The Natyashastra runs to some thirty-six chapters and covers almost everything about the stage.","Because the author is unknown, what we can describe is the book. It treats theatre as a craft with exact rules: how to build a playhouse, how actors should move their hands, eyes and feet, how to speak verse, how to tune instruments, and how plots should be put together. Its short passages on rasa, the emotional experience of the audience, became the starting point for over a thousand years of Indian aesthetic theory."],"timeline":[[-200,"c. Earliest layers of the Natyashastra may date from around this time"],[200,"c. The Natyashastra reaches roughly its present form"],[850,"c. Anandavardhana's Dhvanyaloka extends rasa theory from drama to poetry"],[900,"c. Commentators such as Shankuka and Bhatta Nayaka debate where rasa arises"],[1000,"c. Abhinavagupta writes the Abhinavabharati, the classic commentary on the Natyashastra"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Rasa: the taste of feeling","body":"The aim of a performance is rasa, literally 'juice' or 'flavour'. Just as a good dish blends many spices into one taste, a play blends many elements into a single emotional flavour that the audience savours. The Natyashastra lists eight rasas, including the erotic, comic, heroic and fearful."},{"head":"How rasa is produced","body":"Rasa arises from the combination of causes (the situation and characters), outward signs (tears, trembling, gestures) and passing feelings that come and go. A love scene might combine moonlight, a lover's glance and moments of shyness. Together they evoke a lasting emotion that the audience tastes as rasa."},{"head":"Lasting and passing emotions","body":"Each rasa rests on a stable emotion (sthayi bhava), such as love, grief or fear. Around it play many fleeting moods, like doubt, weariness or joy, which colour it without replacing it. A good play keeps one main emotion in view while letting others enrich it."},{"head":"Theatre as a total art","body":"For the Natyashastra, drama includes poetry, music, dance, costume and stagecraft working together. It gives detailed rules for gestures and movements, many still used in classical Indian dance forms such as Bharatanatyam."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the sixth chapter of the Natyashastra, on rasa, in Manomohan Ghosh's English translation. It is short and contains the famous formula for how rasa is produced, which every later Indian theorist discusses.","critics":"Later commentators disagreed sharply about what the rasa formula means: whether rasa lies in the original characters, the actor, or the spectator. Abhinavagupta argued that earlier readings by Bhatta Lollata and Shankuka wrongly placed it outside the audience's own experience.","legacy":"The Natyashastra is the foundation of Indian theories of art and still guides classical dance, music and theatre. The idea of rasa has also been taken up by modern film and literary critics, and compared with Aristotle's idea of catharsis.","quote":null},"patanjali":{"life":["Almost nothing is known about Patanjali, the author or compiler of the Yoga Sutras. The text's date is uncertain: estimates have ranged from the 2nd century BCE to the 5th century CE, and many scholars now favour a date around 400 CE. Indian tradition often identifies him with the grammarian Patanjali, who wrote a famous commentary on Sanskrit grammar around the 2nd century BCE, but most modern scholars treat them as different people.","The Yoga Sutras is a short work of about 195 'threads', terse sayings meant to be memorised and explained by a teacher. It gathers earlier ideas about meditation and gives them a philosophical framework close to Samkhya, one of the oldest Indian schools. Some scholars think the earliest commentary, traditionally credited to a sage called Vyasa, was written by Patanjali himself or together with the sutras.","Since we know nothing of his life, Patanjali survives only through the text, which is precise and systematic. It is divided into four parts: on concentration, on practice, on special powers, and on final freedom. Later legends describe him as an incarnation of the serpent Adishesha, but these are devotional stories, not biography. The text became the founding work of Yoga as one of the six classical schools of Indian philosophy."],"timeline":[[-150,"c. A grammarian named Patanjali writes the Mahabhashya; tradition, disputed by most scholars, identifies him with the yoga author"],[400,"c. The Yoga Sutras and its first commentary probably reach their present form"],[850,"c. Vachaspati Mishra writes an influential commentary on the Yoga Sutras"],[1025,"c. Al-Biruni translates a version of the Yoga Sutras into Arabic"],[1896,"Swami Vivekananda's Raja Yoga brings the Yoga Sutras to Western readers"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Yoga stills the mind","body":"The second sutra defines yoga as the stilling of the movements of the mind. Our thoughts, memories and perceptions are like ripples on a lake; when they settle, the water becomes clear. Yoga is first of all a discipline of attention, not of the body."},{"head":"The self as witness","body":"Following Samkhya, Patanjali distinguishes purusha, pure consciousness, from prakriti, the material world, which includes the mind. Suffering comes from confusing the two: thinking 'I am angry' when the self only witnesses the anger. Freedom (kaivalya) is the self resting apart from what it watches."},{"head":"The eight limbs","body":"Patanjali sets out eight steps: ethical restraints, personal observances, posture, breath control, withdrawing the senses, concentration, meditation and absorption. They move from outward behaviour to inner stillness. Posture is only the third step, and the sutras say little more than that it should be steady and comfortable."},{"head":"The causes of suffering","body":"Five 'afflictions' keep us bound: ignorance, the sense of 'I', attraction, aversion and clinging to life. Ignorance, mistaking the passing for the permanent, is the root of the others. Practice weakens them, much as untended weeds wither."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Patanjali's yoga is mainly a system of physical postures and stretching.","truth":"The Yoga Sutras is about meditation and freeing consciousness; postures get only a few lines. Most modern posture-based yoga developed much later, especially in the 19th and 20th centuries."},"startHere":"Read the first fifty-one sutras (Book One, on concentration) in a translation with brief notes, such as Edwin Bryant's. They are short and set out the definition of yoga and the main methods of practice.","critics":"Philosophers of the Advaita Vedanta school, such as Shankara, argued that the dualism of self and matter is mistaken, since ultimately only one reality, Brahman, exists. Buddhists denied there is any permanent witnessing self at all.","legacy":"The Yoga Sutras is the classical text of the Yoga school and is now read worldwide by yoga practitioners. Its definitions and its eight limbs shaped how both India and the West understand meditation.","quote":null},"akshapada":{"life":["Akshapada Gautama is the traditional author of the Nyaya-sutra, the founding text of the Nyaya school of Indian philosophy. Nothing is known of his life. The name Akshapada, often explained as 'eyes in the feet', has attracted later legends but no history. The sutras were probably compiled over time and reached their present form around the 2nd century CE, in a period when Hindu, Buddhist and Jain thinkers debated fiercely about knowledge, the self and the world.","Nyaya means 'method' or 'logic'. The school grew out of the practice of public debate, where scholars needed rules for good argument and ways to spot bad ones. The Nyaya-sutra is a short text in five books, which later thinkers expanded through long commentaries, beginning with Vatsyayana's around the 5th century CE. Later Nyaya thinkers merged with the closely related Vaisheshika school, which studied the categories of reality.","Since the author is unknown, what can be described is the work's character. It is practical and orderly. It lists sixteen topics a debater must master, from the means of knowledge to the tricks used in dishonest argument. Its aim is not only intellectual: the opening sutra promises that correct knowledge of these topics leads to liberation from suffering. Its tone is that of a teacher training students to argue well."],"timeline":[[150,"c. The Nyaya-sutra reaches roughly its present form (proposed dates vary widely)"],[200,"c. The Buddhist Nagarjuna challenges the Nyaya theory of the means of knowledge"],[450,"c. Vatsyayana writes the Nyaya-bhashya, the first surviving commentary"],[550,"c. Uddyotakara defends Nyaya against the Buddhist logician Dignaga"],[1325,"c. Gangesha's Tattvacintamani founds the 'New Nyaya' school"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Four means of knowing","body":"We gain knowledge through perception, inference, comparison and reliable testimony. Seeing a tree, inferring rain from dark clouds, recognising a wild ox because it was described as like a cow, and learning from a trustworthy expert are all genuine routes to knowledge."},{"head":"The five-step argument","body":"Nyaya sets out a standard proof: the hill has fire; because it has smoke; wherever there is smoke there is fire, as in a kitchen; this hill has smoke; so it has fire. The example ties the general rule to everyday experience."},{"head":"A real, knowable world","body":"Against Buddhist thinkers who held that things are fleeting or empty, Nyaya holds that the world contains real, lasting objects, and that a real self knows them. Knowledge is a relation between a knower and something that exists independently."},{"head":"Honest and dishonest debate","body":"The sutras distinguish fair discussion aimed at truth from debate aimed only at winning, and from mere destructive criticism. They list fallacies and quibbles, such as twisting an opponent's words, so that debaters can expose them."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening sutras of Book One, which list the sixteen topics and define the four means of knowledge, in a translation with Vatsyayana's commentary. They show the school's whole plan in a few pages.","critics":"Nagarjuna asked what establishes the means of knowledge themselves: if they need further proof, there is an endless regress, and if not, why do other claims need proof? Later Buddhist logicians such as Dignaga also accepted only perception and inference as means of knowing.","legacy":"Nyaya became the standard framework for logic and theory of knowledge across Indian philosophy, and other schools adopted its methods of argument. Its later 'New Nyaya' form developed a precise technical language still studied today.","quote":null},"umasvati":{"life":["Umasvati was a Jain monk who wrote the Tattvartha Sutra, the first systematic summary of Jain teaching in Sanskrit. His dates are uncertain; scholars place him anywhere between the 2nd and 5th centuries CE. By then Jainism was well established across India, but its scriptures were in Prakrit, and Sanskrit had become the shared language of philosophical debate between Hindu, Buddhist and Jain thinkers.","Little is reliably known of his life. The Svetambara branch of Jainism credits him with an auto-commentary whose closing verses say he belonged to a particular lineage of monks and wrote in the city of Kusumapura. The Digambara branch calls him Umasvami and traces his lineage differently. Both branches, which disagree on much else, accept the Tattvartha Sutra as authoritative.","The work itself shows a careful, compressing mind. In about 350 short sutras arranged in ten chapters, it covers the nature of the soul, the structure of the universe, karma, ethics and liberation. It was written to be memorised and explained, and it became the most commented-on text in Jain philosophy. Many Jains still recite it in full on religious occasions."],"timeline":[[300,"c. Writes the Tattvartha Sutra (dates proposed range from the 2nd to 5th centuries)"],[500,"c. The Digambara monk Pujyapada writes the Sarvarthasiddhi commentary"],[750,"c. Akalanka writes the Tattvartha-rajavarttika commentary"],[850,"c. Vidyananda writes a long verse commentary on the Tattvartha Sutra"],[1994,"Nathmal Tatia's English translation, That Which Is, is published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The three jewels","body":"The first sutra states that right faith, right knowledge and right conduct together make the path to liberation. None works alone: knowing what is right without acting on it, or acting without understanding, will not free the soul."},{"head":"Seven realities","body":"Umasvati sets out seven basic truths: souls, non-soul matter, the inflow of karma, bondage, the stopping of inflow, the shedding of karma, and liberation. Together they describe how a soul gets trapped and how it gets free, much like a boat that leaks, fills, is patched and then bailed out."},{"head":"Karma as subtle matter","body":"Passions such as anger, pride, deceit and greed make karmic matter stick to the soul. Austerity, meditation and restraint burn it off. This makes ethics practical: every act either adds weight to the soul or lightens it."},{"head":"Souls help one another","body":"One sutra says that the function of souls is to help one another (parasparopagraho jivanam). Modern Jains have taken this as a motto for an ethic of mutual care and ecological interdependence."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of the Tattvartha Sutra in Nathmal Tatia's translation. It introduces the three jewels, the seven realities and the Jain theory of knowledge in a few pages.","critics":"Hindu and Buddhist philosophers rejected the Jain view that karma is a physical substance and that souls vary in size with the bodies they occupy. Within Jainism, Svetambaras and Digambaras disagree over the wording of some sutras and over which commentary is his own.","legacy":"The Tattvartha Sutra is the one text accepted by all Jain sects and remains the standard introduction to Jain philosophy. It is still memorised and recited in Jain communities.","quote":null},"ashari":{"life":["Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari was born in Basra, in present-day Iraq, around 873–874, a descendant of a companion of the Prophet. The Abbasid empire was then the centre of Islamic learning, and theologians argued over how far human reason could judge questions of faith. The Mu'tazila school held that reason could tell right from wrong on its own and that God must act justly by standards we can grasp; traditionalists distrusted rational theology altogether.","For many years al-Ash'ari studied with al-Jubba'i, the leading Mu'tazili of Basra. Around the age of forty, traditionally about 912, he publicly broke with the school. A later story says he was moved by dreams of the Prophet and by a question about three brothers whose fates God had decided. He then used the Mu'tazilis' own methods of argument to defend traditional Sunni beliefs, and spent his later years in Baghdad, where he died around 935–936.","Al-Ash'ari wrote many works, though only a few survive. His Maqalat al-Islamiyyin is a careful, fairly neutral survey of the views of Muslim sects, still a key source for historians. His Kitab al-Luma' sets out his theology in argued form. He sought a middle path: firm on scripture, but willing to use rational argument to defend it. Later thinkers who followed his approach became known as Ash'arites."],"timeline":[[874,"Born in Basra (some sources give 873)"],[912,"c. Breaks publicly with the Mu'tazili school, traditionally around this year"],[936,"Dies in Baghdad (some sources give 935)"],[1095,"c. Al-Ghazali, the most famous Ash'arite, writes The Incoherence of the Philosophers"],[1180,"c. Ibn Rushd (Averroes) attacks Ash'arite views in The Incoherence of the Incoherence"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Good is what God commands","body":"For al-Ash'ari, actions are good because God commands them, not commanded because they are good. Nothing outside God sets a standard he must meet. If God had commanded lying, lying would be right; he did not, so it is wrong."},{"head":"Acquisition of acts","body":"God creates every event, including human actions. Yet humans 'acquire' (kasb) their acts and are responsible for them. The idea tries to keep both God's total power and human accountability, though critics found it hard to understand."},{"head":"'Without asking how'","body":"The Qur'an speaks of God's hand and throne. Al-Ash'ari accepted such descriptions as true but insisted they not be pictured like human body parts, and not explained away as mere metaphors either: they are affirmed bila kayfa, 'without asking how'."},{"head":"The uncreated Qur'an","body":"Against the Mu'tazila, he held that the Qur'an is God's eternal speech, not something God created in time. This was the position of traditionalists such as Ahmad ibn Hanbal, and al-Ash'ari defended it with philosophical argument."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Ash'arite theology rejected reason in favour of blind faith.","truth":"Al-Ash'ari and his followers used rational argument (kalam) extensively; they denied only that reason alone can establish what is morally right without revelation."},"startHere":"Read Richard McCarthy's translation of the Kitab al-Luma' in The Theology of al-Ash'ari. It is his clearest statement of his views, set out as arguments and replies.","critics":"The Mu'tazila objected that if goodness is whatever God commands, calling God good or just becomes empty, an old problem first raised in Plato's Euthyphro. Ibn Rushd criticised the Ash'arite denial of natural causation as undermining science.","legacy":"The Ash'ari school became one of the main schools of Sunni theology, followed by thinkers such as al-Juwayni and al-Ghazali, and is still taught in many Islamic institutions today.","quote":null},"abhinavagupta":{"life":["Abhinavagupta lived in Kashmir around 950–1020, when the valley was a major centre of Sanskrit learning. Scholars there produced important work on poetics, and on Shaivism, a family of traditions that worship the god Shiva. Some of these traditions were tantric, using ritual and meditation to realise one's identity with the divine. Kashmir's scholars also wrote on grammar, logic and drama, and Abhinavagupta drew on all of these fields in his own writing.","Born into a learned Brahmin family, he studied with many teachers, which he records in his works: grammar and logic with his father, dramaturgy with Bhatta Tauta, poetics with Bhatta Induraja, and Shaiva philosophy with teachers of the Pratyabhijna ('Recognition') school. He chose not to marry and devoted himself to study and teaching. His works include the vast Tantraloka, a summary of tantric Shaivism; two commentaries on the Pratyabhijna texts of Utpaladeva; and his commentaries on the Natyashastra and on Anandavardhana's Dhvanyaloka.","He worked as a synthesiser, drawing together many traditions, and as a careful critic of his predecessors, often reporting their views before refuting them. Several of his works carry dates, the last around 1014–15, but his death is unrecorded. A later legend says he walked into a cave with his disciples and was never seen again. His writing is dense and technical, aimed at trained readers."],"timeline":[[950,"c. Born in Kashmir"],[990,"c. Writes the Locana, his commentary on Anandavardhana's Dhvanyaloka"],[1000,"c. Writes the Abhinavabharati, his commentary on the Natyashastra"],[1000,"c. Writes the Tantraloka, a vast summary of tantric Shaivism (date approximate)"],[1014,"Completes the Ishvarapratyabhijna-vimarshini, one of his last datable works (1014–15)"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Rasa as savouring","body":"Abhinavagupta argued that rasa, the emotion of art, exists in the spectator's own experience, not in the characters or the actor. When we watch a tragedy we do not feel grief as we would at a real funeral; we savour sorrow itself, in a way that is pleasurable."},{"head":"Generalised emotion","body":"In art, emotions are freed from personal ties: the grief we taste is not 'my' grief about 'my' loss, but grief as such. This 'generalisation' explains why sad plays can be enjoyable and why people from different backgrounds can share the same response."},{"head":"Peace as a rasa","body":"Bharata listed eight rasas. Abhinavagupta defended a ninth, shanta, the rasa of peace or tranquillity, and argued it is in a sense the basis of all the others, since the calm absorption of aesthetic experience resembles spiritual freedom."},{"head":"Liberation as recognition","body":"In the Pratyabhijna philosophy he championed, all reality is the consciousness of Shiva, and each person already is that consciousness. Freedom does not require becoming something new, only recognising who you are, like someone who meets a person they have heard about and suddenly realises who it is."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his comments on the rasa sutra in the sixth chapter of the Abhinavabharati, available in translated extracts in anthologies of Indian aesthetics. This is the classic statement of his theory of aesthetic experience.","critics":"His contemporary Mahimabhatta rejected the theory of dhvani (suggestion), on which Abhinavagupta built, arguing that what poetry 'suggests' is simply inferred. Later writers in different schools also disputed whether shanta could be a rasa, since a peaceful state seems hard to stage.","legacy":"His account of rasa became the dominant theory in Indian aesthetics, and his Shaiva writings define Kashmir Shaivism as it is studied and practised today.","quote":null},"kenko":{"life":["Yoshida Kenkō lived in Japan around 1283–1352, during the fall of the Kamakura shogunate and the bitter split between rival imperial courts. Old aristocratic culture was losing ground to the warrior class, and Buddhist teaching on mujō, impermanence, shaped how many educated people saw the world. Poetry, especially the 31-syllable waka, remained the prized art of the court. Kyoto remained the centre of court culture, while real power lay with warriors.","According to the traditional account, Kenkō was born Urabe Kaneyoshi into a family of Shinto priests, served at the imperial court, and in his late twenties or early thirties took Buddhist vows and became a monk, living sometimes in retreats near Kyoto. Records show he had become a monk by around 1313. He was respected as a waka poet. Recent Japanese scholarship, notably by Ogawa Takeo, argues that much of this traditional biography was put together in later centuries and is unreliable, so the details of his life are uncertain.","Kenkō is remembered for one book, Tsurezuregusa ('Essays in Idleness'), probably written mostly around 1330–1332. It consists of 243 short pieces, some a sentence long, others a few pages, on nature, manners, death, friendship, taste and Buddhist practice. It seems to have been little known in his lifetime but became a classic in the 17th century, when printed editions and commentaries spread it widely."],"timeline":[[1283,"c. Born, according to the traditional dating"],[1313,"By this year records show he has become a Buddhist monk"],[1330,"c. Writes most of Essays in Idleness (c. 1330–1332)"],[1352,"c. Last records of him alive"],[1600,"c. Printed editions and commentaries in the Edo period make the book a classic"],[1967,"Donald Keene's English translation is published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Impermanence makes things precious","body":"If we lived forever, Kenkō suggests, nothing would move us. It is because life is uncertain and brief that it is valuable. A sunset matters partly because it will soon be gone."},{"head":"Beauty in the incomplete","body":"In a famous passage he asks whether we should look at cherry blossoms only in full bloom, or the moon only when the sky is clear. Blossoms about to open, or scattered petals, and a moon half hidden by cloud, can move us more. Imagination fills what is missing."},{"head":"The charm of the worn","body":"Kenkō prefers things that show age and use: a book with frayed covers, an old house that is not too tidy. Perfect matching sets and brand-new things can seem shallow. This taste for the imperfect fed later Japanese ideals of beauty."},{"head":"Don't postpone what matters","body":"Death comes without warning, from behind, he says, so a person who means to seek the Buddhist path should not wait until other business is finished. Many essays apply this to everyday life: do what matters now rather than someday."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read sections 7 and 137 of Essays in Idleness in Donald Keene's or Meredith McKinney's translation. Section 7 explains why impermanence gives life its value, and section 137 is his best-known passage on the beauty of the incomplete.","critics":"The 18th-century scholar Motoori Norinaga criticised Kenkō's preference for scattered blossoms and clouded moons as an affected, Buddhist-influenced pose, arguing that people naturally love blossoms in full bloom and the full moon.","legacy":"Essays in Idleness is one of the classics of Japanese literature, still widely read in schools. Its ideas of beauty in transience and imperfection became central to later Japanese aesthetics, often discussed under terms like wabi-sabi.","quote":null},"filmer":{"life":["Robert Filmer was born around 1588 in East Sutton, Kent, the eldest son of a gentry family. He lived through the growing conflict between the Stuart kings and Parliament over who held final authority in England. The question of where political power comes from, from God, from the people, or from ancient law, was argued in sermons, pamphlets and law courts, and led eventually to civil war.","Filmer studied at Trinity College, Cambridge, and at Lincoln's Inn, and was knighted by James I in 1619. He wrote Patriarcha probably in the 1620s or 1630s, almost certainly before the civil war began in 1642, but it circulated only in manuscript. A royalist, he had his house plundered by parliamentary forces, was imprisoned in Leeds Castle for some years, and had his estates seized. In 1648 and the following years he published several political tracts. He died at East Sutton in 1653.","Filmer was a country gentleman and scholar rather than a public figure. His tracts are short and polemical, picking holes in the theories of Hobbes, Milton, Grotius and others. He was also sceptical in other areas: in 1653 he published a tract warning juries against convicting people of witchcraft on weak evidence. Patriarcha became famous only after Tory supporters of the crown printed it in 1680."],"timeline":[[1588,"c. Born at East Sutton, Kent"],[1619,"Knighted by James I"],[1642,"c. Patriarcha is probably complete by the start of the civil war"],[1648,"Publishes The Anarchy of a Limited or Mixed Monarchy"],[1652,"Publishes Observations Concerning the Originall of Government, criticising Hobbes, Milton and Grotius"],[1653,"Publishes a tract against hasty witchcraft convictions; dies at East Sutton"],[1680,"Patriarcha is published during the Exclusion Crisis"],[1689,"Locke's Two Treatises of Government attack Patriarcha"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Kings as fathers","body":"God gave Adam authority over his children and the whole earth, and this fatherly power passed down to kings. A king relates to his people as a father to his family: he rules them for their good, but they cannot vote him out."},{"head":"No one is born free","body":"Filmer attacked the new idea that people are naturally free and equal. Every child is born under a father's authority, so natural liberty is a fiction. Government is as natural as the family and needs no contract to create it."},{"head":"Consent cannot work","body":"If power came from the consent of the people, when did everyone consent? Did women and children? Must every new generation agree again? Filmer argued that consent theories collapse into impossibility or into rule by a majority that never had the right to bind the rest."},{"head":"Sovereignty cannot be split","body":"A 'mixed' or 'limited' monarchy, sharing power between king and Parliament, is incoherent: someone must have the final say, and whoever does is the real sovereign. Divided sovereignty means anarchy."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Patriarcha, where Filmer sets out his attack on natural liberty and the idea that power comes from the people. It is short and shows exactly what Locke was answering.","critics":"John Locke devoted the whole First Treatise of Government to refuting Filmer, arguing that Adam's authority, if it existed, could not be traced to any present king, and that a father's power over children ends when they grow up. Algernon Sidney also wrote a long reply.","legacy":"Filmer is now read mainly because Locke and Sidney answered him, but historians see him as the clearest English defender of patriarchal monarchy. His questions about how consent could actually work are still raised by critics of social contract theory.","quote":null},"elisabeth":{"life":["Elisabeth of Bohemia was born in Heidelberg in 1618, eldest daughter of Frederick V, Elector Palatine, and Elizabeth Stuart, daughter of James I of England. Her father briefly accepted the crown of Bohemia, earning the nickname 'Winter King', but was defeated in 1620 at the start of the Thirty Years War. The family lived in exile in The Hague, a lively centre of learning in the Dutch Republic.","Elisabeth was highly educated, with Latin, mathematics and philosophy. In 1643 she wrote to René Descartes asking how a soul that has no size or shape could move a body. Their correspondence lasted until his death in 1650. He dedicated his Principles of Philosophy (1644) to her, and their exchanges on emotion led to his Passions of the Soul (1649). In 1667 she became abbess of the Lutheran convent at Herford in Westphalia, where she governed until her death in 1680.","Elisabeth published nothing herself; we know her thought through her letters, which were printed only in 1879. They show a sharp, persistent questioner who was not satisfied by polite answers. Besides the mind-body problem she discussed free will, the role of the passions in a good life, and Machiavelli's politics. As abbess she offered shelter to religious minorities, and the Quakers William Penn and Robert Barclay visited her."],"timeline":[[1618,"Born in Heidelberg"],[1620,"Her family goes into exile after the Battle of White Mountain"],[1643,"Begins her correspondence with Descartes"],[1644,"Descartes dedicates his Principles of Philosophy to her"],[1649,"Descartes publishes The Passions of the Soul, which grew out of their letters"],[1667,"Becomes abbess of Herford"],[1680,"Dies at Herford"],[1879,"Her letters to Descartes are first published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"How can mind move body?","body":"Descartes said the mind is a thinking thing with no extension, while the body is extended matter. Elisabeth pointed out that pushing seems to require contact and extension. If I decide to raise my arm, how does something with no size or position make the arm move? This is the classic interaction problem."},{"head":"Better to give the soul extension?","body":"When Descartes replied that the union of mind and body is simply known through experience, Elisabeth said she found it easier to grant matter and extension to the soul than to accept that an immaterial thing could move a body. She left the question open rather than accepting a weak answer."},{"head":"The body affects reason","body":"Elisabeth noted that illness, fatigue and emotion can cloud judgement, and doubted that reason can always master them. Happiness, she argued, depends partly on health and circumstances, not only on having the right attitude."},{"head":"Virtue in a messy world","body":"She questioned whether we can know enough to act well, since doing so would require weighing countless consequences. Her letters push Descartes towards a more practical ethics, suited to people in real situations with limited information."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Elisabeth was simply an admiring pupil of Descartes.","truth":"She was an independent critic whose objections Descartes struggled to answer, and her statement of the mind-body problem is still the standard starting point for discussing it."},"startHere":"Read her first two letters to Descartes from May and June 1643, with his replies, in Lisa Shapiro's edition. They take only a few pages and contain the mind-body objection in her own words.","critics":"Descartes answered that mind and body form a real union, known clearly through the senses rather than through intellect, so asking how they interact uses the wrong kind of thinking. Scholars still debate whether Elisabeth was hinting at materialism or simply showing that dualism was unfinished.","legacy":"Her objection is the classic statement of the interaction problem that any dualist theory of mind must answer. Recent work on early modern women philosophers has made her one of the best-known examples.","quote":null},"bossuet":{"life":["Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet was born in Dijon in 1627 into a family of lawyers. He lived through the long reign of Louis XIV, when France was the strongest power in Europe and the monarchy was gathering power to itself. Religious questions were also political: Catholics and Protestants still disputed bitterly, and the French church debated how independent it should be from the pope.","Educated by Jesuits and in Paris, Bossuet was ordained in 1652 and became famous as a preacher, especially for his funeral orations. He was made Bishop of Condom in 1669 and in 1670 became tutor to the Dauphin, the king's heir. For the prince he wrote the Discourse on Universal History (1681) and a treatise on politics drawn from scripture, published after his death. In 1681 he became Bishop of Meaux and in 1682 drafted the French clergy's declaration limiting papal power.","Bossuet was a controversialist as well as a preacher. He wrote against Protestantism in his History of the Variations of the Protestant Churches (1688), supported the revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 1685, and in the 1690s led the attack on the mystical 'Quietism' defended by Archbishop Fénelon, which the pope condemned in 1699. He died in Paris in 1704."],"timeline":[[1627,"Born in Dijon"],[1652,"Ordained a priest"],[1669,"Becomes Bishop of Condom"],[1670,"Appointed tutor to the Dauphin"],[1681,"Publishes the Discourse on Universal History; becomes Bishop of Meaux"],[1682,"Drafts the Declaration of the Clergy of France"],[1704,"Dies in Paris"],[1709,"Politics Drawn from the Very Words of Holy Scripture is published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Royal power comes from God","body":"Kings are God's ministers on earth, so to resist them is to resist God. Subjects owe obedience even to bad kings, though they may pray and plead. This is the theory of the divine right of kings."},{"head":"Absolute but not arbitrary","body":"Bossuet distinguished absolute government, where the king answers to no human court, from arbitrary government, where a ruler ignores law and treats property as his own. The king is bound by God's law, by reason and by the kingdom's laws, and will answer to God."},{"head":"The king as father","body":"Royal authority is fatherly: kings should care for their people as parents do children, with gentleness as well as power. A king who lives only for himself betrays the purpose of his office."},{"head":"Providence in history","body":"In the Discourse on Universal History, the rise and fall of empires, from Egypt to Rome, is directed by God towards the history of the true religion. Events that look like chance are part of a plan."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Divine right meant the king could do whatever he liked.","truth":"Bossuet insisted that the king must rule by law and reason for the good of his people, and answers to God; he denied only that subjects may lawfully rebel."},"startHere":"Read Book Three of the Politics Drawn from the Very Words of Holy Scripture, on the nature of royal authority, in Patrick Riley's translation. It gives his whole case for sacred, fatherly and absolute monarchy.","critics":"Locke and other contract theorists argued that legitimate power rests on consent and that people may resist tyrants. Voltaire criticised the Discourse on Universal History for ignoring China, India and much of the world to fit history into a biblical story.","legacy":"Bossuet is the classic theorist of French absolute monarchy and a master of French prose; his sermons and funeral orations are still read as literature.","quote":null},"malebranche":{"life":["Nicolas Malebranche was born in Paris in 1638, the youngest child of a royal official. He grew up as the new philosophy of Descartes was transforming French thought, replacing the old school philosophy of Aristotle with a mechanical picture of nature and a sharp division between mind and matter. The challenge was to fit this new science with Catholic faith.","Because of a deformed spine he was educated at home, then studied philosophy and theology in Paris. In 1660 he joined the Oratory, a religious order, and was ordained in 1664. That year, according to his early biographer, reading Descartes' Treatise on Man excited him so much that he had to stop for palpitations. He spent the next decade studying and published The Search after Truth (1674–75). His Treatise on Nature and Grace (1680) sparked a long dispute with Antoine Arnauld. He lived and worked in the Oratory's Paris house until his death in 1715.","Malebranche was a priest-philosopher who saw no conflict between reason and faith. He corresponded with Leibniz, followed developments in mathematics and science, and was elected an honorary member of the Academy of Sciences in 1699. He revised his main work repeatedly in response to critics. His style is clear and often elegant, and many of his works take the form of dialogues or replies to objections."],"timeline":[[1638,"Born in Paris"],[1660,"Joins the Oratory"],[1664,"Ordained a priest; reads Descartes' Treatise on Man"],[1674,"Publishes the first volume of The Search after Truth (completed 1675)"],[1680,"Publishes the Treatise on Nature and Grace, starting his dispute with Arnauld"],[1688,"Publishes the Dialogues on Metaphysics and Religion"],[1699,"Elected an honorary member of the Academy of Sciences"],[1715,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Only God truly causes","body":"When one billiard ball hits another, we see one event follow another but never see any power passing between them. Malebranche argued that only God has real causal power. The collision is just the 'occasion' on which God moves the second ball. This view is called occasionalism."},{"head":"Mind and body don't interact","body":"The same goes for mind and body. When I decide to raise my arm, my will does not move it; God moves it on the occasion of my choice. This dissolves Descartes' puzzle of how two different substances could act on each other."},{"head":"We see all things in God","body":"We do not perceive objects directly, nor do our minds make ideas themselves. The ideas through which we know things exist in God, and we see them there. This explains how finite minds can grasp eternal truths like those of geometry."},{"head":"God acts by general laws","body":"God acts through simple, general laws rather than constant special decisions. This explains why rain falls on the sea as well as on fields, and why some evils happen: a world run by simple laws is worthier of God than one with fewer evils but endless exceptions."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first three of the Dialogues on Metaphysics and Religion. They present his main ideas in lively conversation and are much shorter than The Search after Truth.","critics":"Antoine Arnauld argued that the 'ideas in God' were needless middlemen between minds and things. Leibniz objected that occasionalism turns all of nature into a perpetual miracle.","legacy":"Malebranche's argument that we never observe necessary connections between causes and effects anticipated Hume's account of causation, and his views shaped Berkeley's idealism.","quote":null},"holbach":{"life":["Paul-Henri Thiry, later Baron d'Holbach, was born in 1723 in Edesheim in the Palatinate, in western Germany. He lived through the French Enlightenment, when writers in Paris challenged the authority of church and crown in the name of reason and science. Open criticism of religion was dangerous: books could be burned and authors imprisoned. Holbach himself was born German but became fully French in language and outlook.","Holbach was raised partly by a wealthy uncle in Paris, studied at the University of Leiden in the 1740s, and settled in Paris, inheriting his uncle's fortune and title in 1753. He wrote or translated hundreds of articles, mainly on chemistry and geology, for Diderot's Encyclopédie and translated German scientific works. From the 1760s he published a stream of attacks on religion, all anonymous or under false names. The System of Nature (1770), issued under the name of a dead academician, was condemned to be burned.","Holbach was best known in his lifetime as a generous host. For decades his house in the rue Royale held twice-weekly dinners where Diderot, Grimm and visitors such as Hume, Adam Smith and Beccaria discussed ideas freely. Scholars think friends, especially Diderot and Naigeon, helped with some of his books. He died in Paris in January 1789, months before the Revolution."],"timeline":[[1723,"Born in Edesheim, in the Palatinate"],[1744,"Begins studies at the University of Leiden"],[1752,"c. Begins contributing articles to the Encyclopédie"],[1753,"Inherits his uncle's fortune and title"],[1770,"Publishes The System of Nature under a false name"],[1772,"Publishes Good Sense, a shorter version of his case against religion"],[1789,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Only matter exists","body":"Everything that exists is matter in motion, governed by natural laws. Humans are part of nature, not set apart by an immaterial soul. Thinking is something the brain does, as digestion is something the stomach does."},{"head":"No free will","body":"Every choice is caused by our temperament, upbringing and circumstances, which are themselves caused. You may feel free choosing tea over coffee, but the choice follows from causes you did not pick. Holbach thought accepting this would make us more tolerant and less cruel in punishing."},{"head":"Religion breeds fear","body":"Belief in gods arose from ignorance and fear of natural forces, and priests and kings used it to control people. Holbach argued that religion makes people anxious and intolerant, and that morality would be stronger without it."},{"head":"Morality from human nature","body":"Ethics should be based on what makes people happy in society, not on divine commands. We are social creatures who need each other, so self-interest properly understood leads us to be just and kind."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Part One, Chapter 1 of The System of Nature, on nature and its laws, which sets out his materialism in a few pages. His shorter Good Sense covers the case against religion more briskly.","critics":"Voltaire, though no friend of the church, rejected Holbach's atheism and argued that the order of nature points to a designer. Frederick the Great wrote a reply objecting that if all actions are determined, praising and blaming people makes no sense.","legacy":"The System of Nature is one of the first open defences of atheism and materialism in modern Europe, and it influenced later materialists, including Marx. His salon helped spread Enlightenment ideas across Europe.","quote":null},"paine":{"life":["Thomas Paine was born in Thetford, Norfolk, in 1737, the son of a Quaker corset-maker. He lived in an age of revolutions: the American colonies broke from Britain, and France overthrew its monarchy. Cheap printing allowed pamphlets to reach ordinary readers, and Paine became the most widely read political writer of his time. His plain, forceful style was written for artisans and farmers as much as for the educated.","Paine tried several trades and was twice dismissed as an excise officer. In 1774, with a letter of introduction from Benjamin Franklin, he sailed to Philadelphia. In January 1776 he published Common Sense, urging American independence; it sold in huge numbers. He then wrote The American Crisis papers for the revolutionary cause. Back in Europe he defended the French Revolution in Rights of Man (1791–92), was charged with seditious libel in Britain, and fled to France, where he was elected to the National Convention.","In France Paine opposed executing Louis XVI and was imprisoned during the Terror in 1793–94, narrowly escaping the guillotine. He wrote The Age of Reason, attacking organised religion, around this time. That book cost him many American friends, and when he returned to the United States in 1802 he was widely shunned. He died in New York in 1809, and only a handful of people attended his funeral."],"timeline":[[1737,"Born in Thetford, Norfolk"],[1774,"Emigrates to Philadelphia"],[1776,"Publishes Common Sense and the first American Crisis paper"],[1791,"Publishes Rights of Man, Part One, replying to Burke"],[1792,"Elected to the French National Convention; convicted of seditious libel in Britain in his absence"],[1793,"Imprisoned in Paris during the Terror (released 1794)"],[1794,"Publishes The Age of Reason, Part One"],[1809,"Dies in New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Hereditary rule is absurd","body":"Nobody would choose a doctor or a ship's captain because his father had the job. Paine argued it is equally foolish to inherit the right to rule a nation. Monarchy, he said, began in conquest and survives by habit."},{"head":"Each generation rules itself","body":"Against Burke, who valued tradition, Paine argued that the dead have no authority over the living. A constitution made a century ago cannot bind people today; each generation has the right to decide its own government."},{"head":"Natural rights","body":"Every person has rights simply by being human, not as a gift from government. Government exists to protect those rights, and a government that does not is illegitimate."},{"head":"Deism, not churches","body":"In The Age of Reason Paine affirmed belief in one God, known through nature and reason, but rejected the Bible as revelation and attacked churches as human inventions used for power and profit."},{"head":"Welfare from common land","body":"In Agrarian Justice (1797) he argued that since the earth was originally common property, landowners owe a ground rent to the community. He proposed using it to pay every young adult a lump sum and every older person a pension, an early plan for social welfare."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Paine was an atheist.","truth":"Paine was a deist who said plainly that he believed in one God and hoped for life after death; he attacked churches and the Bible, not belief in God."},"startHere":"Read Common Sense, especially the sections 'Of Monarchy and Hereditary Succession' and 'Thoughts on the Present State of American Affairs'. It is short, punchy and was written for ordinary readers.","critics":"Edmund Burke, whom Rights of Man answers, argued that societies are partnerships between the dead, the living and the unborn, and that abstract rights ignore the slowly built wisdom of institutions.","legacy":"Paine helped turn the American dispute with Britain into a demand for independence, and his ideas of universal human rights and of welfare funded by land shaped later radical and democratic movements.","quote":{"text":"My own mind is my own church.","src":"The Age of Reason, Part One, ch. 1","tr":"original English"}},"de_maistre":{"life":["Joseph de Maistre was born in 1753 in Chambéry, in Savoy, then part of the Kingdom of Sardinia, which was ruled from Turin. He grew up in the late Enlightenment and saw the French Revolution overturn church and monarchy in the name of reason and the rights of man. His life's work was a sustained attack on the Revolution and the ideas behind it.","Trained as a lawyer like his father, he served in the Senate of Savoy and in his youth was an active Freemason with liberal sympathies. When French troops invaded Savoy in 1792 he fled, and lived as an exile. In Lausanne he wrote Considerations on France (1797). From 1803 to 1817 he was the King of Sardinia's envoy in St Petersburg, where he wrote his major works. He returned to Turin and died there in 1821.","Maistre wrote with a vivid, provocative style, mixing sharp argument with deliberate paradox. His most famous passages, on the executioner and on war as something divine, were written to shock. He was well read in philosophy, theology and history, and argued with Bacon, Locke and Rousseau directly. He also wrote thousands of letters, many of them sharp reports on Russian and European politics."],"timeline":[[1753,"Born in Chambéry, Savoy"],[1792,"Flees Savoy after the French invasion"],[1797,"Publishes Considerations on France"],[1803,"Arrives in St Petersburg as Sardinian envoy"],[1817,"Leaves Russia and returns to Turin"],[1819,"Publishes Du Pape (On the Pope)"],[1821,"Dies in Turin; The St Petersburg Dialogues is published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Constitutions can't be written","body":"Real constitutions grow slowly from history, custom and religion; they cannot be invented on paper. The French revolutionaries thought they could design a state from scratch, Maistre argued, and produced only chaos."},{"head":"There is no 'Man' in general","body":"Maistre mocked talk of the universal rights of man. He said he had met Frenchmen, Italians and Russians, but never 'man' as such. People are shaped by their nation, faith and history, and laws must fit particular peoples."},{"head":"Authority, not reason, holds society together","body":"Reason alone breeds doubt and endless argument. Societies need authority that is not questioned: a monarch, and above all the pope, whose infallibility Maistre defended as the keystone of order."},{"head":"The Revolution as punishment","body":"Maistre saw the Revolution as an act of providence, God punishing France for its sins and impiety. History is not just human choices; a hidden divine plan works through events, even terrible ones."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Considerations on France, especially the chapters on the Revolution as providence and on the 'principle generating constitutions'. It is his shortest major work and his most direct reply to the Revolution.","critics":"Liberal critics, then and since, saw his defence of unquestioned authority as a recipe for tyranny. Isaiah Berlin famously, and controversially, presented him as a forerunner of fascism; other scholars argue this reads the 20th century back into a Catholic royalist.","legacy":"Maistre is a founding figure of the conservative and counter-revolutionary tradition, and influenced Catholic political thought and later writers such as Baudelaire and Carl Schmitt.","quote":null},"godwin":{"life":["William Godwin was born in 1756 in Wisbech, Cambridgeshire, the son of a Dissenting minister, a Protestant outside the Church of England. He came of age in the 1780s and 1790s, when the French Revolution inspired British radicals and alarmed the government, which soon prosecuted reformers for sedition. Reformers dreamed that reason could remake society, and Godwin became their boldest philosopher.","Godwin trained for the ministry at Hoxton Academy and served as a minister, but lost his faith and moved to London to write. In 1793 he published An Enquiry Concerning Political Justice, which made him famous overnight. His novel Caleb Williams (1794) dramatised its ideas about power and injustice. In 1797 he married Mary Wollstonecraft; she died that year after giving birth to their daughter, the future Mary Shelley. His candid Memoirs of her (1798) caused a scandal.","Godwin's fame faded as Britain turned against radicalism, and he spent his later life in debt, writing novels, histories and children's books and running a children's bookshop. He replied at length to Malthus's theory of population. In 1833 he was given a small government post, and he died in London in 1836. He was a methodical, cautious man in person, who believed in change through calm discussion rather than revolt."],"timeline":[[1756,"Born in Wisbech, Cambridgeshire"],[1793,"Publishes An Enquiry Concerning Political Justice"],[1794,"Publishes the novel Caleb Williams"],[1797,"Marries Mary Wollstonecraft; their daughter Mary is born and Wollstonecraft dies"],[1798,"Publishes his Memoirs of Wollstonecraft; Malthus's Essay on Population answers Godwin"],[1820,"Publishes Of Population, his reply to Malthus"],[1836,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Impartial benevolence","body":"We should act to produce the greatest good, counting everyone equally. In Godwin's famous example, if a fire threatens both the archbishop Fénelon, whose work benefits thousands, and a chambermaid, you should save Fénelon, even if the chambermaid is your mother."},{"head":"Government is an evil","body":"Government rests on force and stops people from thinking for themselves. As people become more rational, Godwin argued, laws and government will become less necessary and can gradually disappear. He is often called the first modern anarchist thinker."},{"head":"Truth will win","body":"Reason and free discussion are powerful: if people hear the truth clearly enough, they will come to act on it. So Godwin opposed violent revolution and even political parties, trusting instead in patient argument."},{"head":"Against private hoarding","body":"If someone needs my spare coat more than I do, justice says it should go to them. Godwin argued that property should go where it does the most good, though he wanted this achieved by people's free choice, not by force."}],"misconception":{"myth":"As an early anarchist, Godwin called for overthrowing the government by force.","truth":"Godwin rejected violence and even organised political agitation; he expected government to fade away as people became more rational through education and discussion."},"startHere":"Read Book II, Chapter 2 of Political Justice, on justice, which contains the Fénelon example and sets out his impartial view of morality. It is short and easy to argue with.","critics":"Thomas Malthus argued in his Essay on the Principle of Population (1798) that Godwin's equal society would collapse because population grows faster than food. Many readers also objected that impartiality that ignores family ties asks the impossible of human nature.","legacy":"Godwin influenced the Romantic poets, especially Shelley, as well as later anarchists and utilitarians. Caleb Williams is often seen as an early crime thriller.","quote":null},"constant":{"life":["Benjamin Constant was born in Lausanne, Switzerland, in 1767, into a Protestant family of French origin. He lived through the French Revolution, the Terror, Napoleon's empire and the restored Bourbon monarchy. The burning question of his age was how to have free government after both revolutionary terror and imperial dictatorship had claimed to speak for the people. Many feared that any strong government would turn into a new despotism.","Constant studied at Erlangen and Edinburgh. In 1794 he met Germaine de Staël, and they were political and intellectual partners for many years. He served in the Tribunate under Napoleon until purged in 1802. In 1815 he denounced Napoleon, then days later accepted his invitation to draft a liberal constitution during the Hundred Days, a turn that damaged his reputation. He published his novel Adolphe in 1816 and from 1819 served as a liberal deputy. He died in Paris in 1830.","Constant was a political journalist, parliamentarian and novelist as well as a theorist. His Principles of Politics (1815) and his 1819 lecture on ancient and modern liberty gave classic form to liberal ideas. He also worked for decades on a long history of religion. His diaries show a restless, self-critical and often indecisive man. He was also a heavy gambler, which kept him often in debt."],"timeline":[[1767,"Born in Lausanne"],[1794,"Meets Germaine de Staël"],[1802,"Removed from the Tribunate by Napoleon"],[1815,"Drafts a liberal constitution for Napoleon during the Hundred Days; publishes Principles of Politics"],[1816,"Publishes the novel Adolphe"],[1819,"Gives his lecture on ancient and modern liberty; elected a deputy"],[1830,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Two kinds of liberty","body":"For citizens of ancient Athens or Sparta, liberty meant sharing directly in public decisions, even if the state controlled private life. For moderns, liberty means a protected private sphere: freedom of belief, speech, work and property. Constant argued that modern commercial societies need the second kind."},{"head":"Copying the ancients is dangerous","body":"The Jacobins tried to impose ancient-style civic virtue on modern France, and the result was the Terror. Constant blamed Rousseau's ideas, misapplied, for encouraging the belief that the people's will could override every individual right."},{"head":"Limited sovereignty","body":"No authority, even the people's, is unlimited. There is a part of each person's life that no government has the right to enter. Whoever holds power, a king or a majority, must be bound by individual rights."},{"head":"Representative government","body":"Since modern people are busy with private lives, they delegate political work to representatives, as a rich person hires a steward. But they must watch those representatives closely, or they will lose the freedom they mean to enjoy."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Constant wanted people to retreat into private life and leave politics to others.","truth":"He ended his famous lecture by warning that giving up political participation endangers private liberty, and urged combining both kinds of freedom."},"startHere":"Read The Liberty of the Ancients Compared with that of the Moderns (1819). It is a single lecture, clearly argued, and the source of his best-known distinction.","critics":"Republican thinkers argue that Constant underrated active citizenship and that freedom requires more than being left alone. Marxist critics saw his modern liberty as the freedom of property owners in a commercial society.","legacy":"Constant is a founder of modern liberalism. His distinction between two kinds of liberty shaped Isaiah Berlin's famous contrast between negative and positive liberty.","quote":null},"schleiermacher":{"life":["Friedrich Schleiermacher was born in 1768 in Breslau, then in Prussian Silesia, the son of a Reformed army chaplain. He grew up as the Enlightenment's critical reason and Kant's philosophy were challenging traditional Christianity, and as German Romanticism was emerging. Many educated people had come to see religion as outdated superstition. He set out to show these cultured sceptics that they had misunderstood what religion is.","He was educated in schools of the pietist Moravian Brethren, whose warm piety stayed with him, though he came to doubt their doctrines, and then at the University of Halle. In 1796 he became a hospital chaplain in Berlin and joined the Romantic circle around Friedrich Schlegel. There he wrote On Religion: Speeches to Its Cultured Despisers (1799). He became professor at Halle in 1804 and from 1810 was a founding professor at the new University of Berlin, while also preaching at Trinity Church. His The Christian Faith appeared in 1821–22.","Schleiermacher was a scholar of wide range: he translated almost all of Plato into German, lectured on ethics, psychology, dialectics and education, and gave influential lectures on hermeneutics, the art of interpretation. He was also a popular preacher and active in Prussian church and political reform. He died in Berlin in 1834. He helped bring about the union of Lutheran and Reformed churches in Prussia in 1817."],"timeline":[[1768,"Born in Breslau"],[1796,"Becomes hospital chaplain in Berlin"],[1799,"Publishes On Religion: Speeches to Its Cultured Despisers"],[1804,"Publishes the first volume of his Plato translation; becomes professor at Halle"],[1810,"Becomes a founding professor at the University of Berlin"],[1821,"Publishes The Christian Faith (1821–22)"],[1834,"Dies in Berlin"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Religion is not doctrine or morals","body":"Against both rationalists and moralists, Schleiermacher argued that religion is not chiefly a set of beliefs to be proved, nor a support for good behaviour. It is a basic way of experiencing reality, as distinct as art or science."},{"head":"The feeling of absolute dependence","body":"At the root of religion is an awareness that we, and everything, depend wholly on something beyond us. We feel partly free and partly dependent in ordinary life, but in relation to the whole we are utterly dependent. 'God' names what that feeling points to."},{"head":"Doctrine as expression","body":"Christian doctrines are attempts to put religious experience into words. So theology should start from the experience of the Christian community, especially of redemption through Christ, rather than from proofs or bare authority."},{"head":"The art of interpretation","body":"Understanding a text is not automatic. We must grasp both the language and the author's individual mind, moving back and forth between parts and whole. This 'hermeneutic circle' applies to the Bible, to Plato, and to any piece of writing."}],"misconception":{"myth":"By grounding religion in 'feeling', Schleiermacher reduced it to emotion or sentiment.","truth":"His word Gefühl means an immediate self-awareness underlying all thinking and acting, not a passing emotion like joy or sadness."},"startHere":"Read the Second Speech of On Religion, 'On the Essence of Religion'. It is his famous attempt to say what religion really is, written for sceptical readers.","critics":"Hegel mocked the idea that religion is a feeling of dependence, saying that a dog would then be the best Christian. In the 20th century Karl Barth argued that Schleiermacher made theology about human experience instead of God's revelation.","legacy":"Schleiermacher is often called the father of modern liberal Protestant theology, and his work on hermeneutics shaped later thinkers such as Dilthey and Gadamer.","quote":null},"krause":{"life":["Karl Christian Friedrich Krause was born in 1781 in Eisenberg, in Thuringia. He belonged to the generation of German idealists after Kant, when Fichte, Schelling and Hegel were building ambitious systems that tried to explain all of reality from a single principle. Krause attempted his own system, but never won the fame of his rivals. Like them, he wrote about God, nature, law and history in one connected scheme.","He studied at Jena under Fichte and Schelling, gained his doctorate in 1801 and lectured there briefly. Lacking students, he moved to Dresden, where he taught music and other subjects and, in 1805, joined the Freemasons. His books on Masonic history and ideals brought him into conflict with the lodges. He failed to win a professorship in Berlin, taught as an unsalaried lecturer at Göttingen from 1823, and moved to Munich in 1831, where he died in 1832.","Krause was a prolific writer on metaphysics, ethics, law, aesthetics, music and language. He invented many new German terms to express his ideas, which made his work hard to read. He was an idealist in practice too, writing about a future federation of all humanity and about the rights of animals and of women. Some of his works were published only after his death by his students."],"timeline":[[1781,"Born in Eisenberg, Thuringia"],[1801,"Receives his doctorate at Jena"],[1805,"Joins the Freemasons"],[1811,"Publishes Das Urbild der Menschheit (The Ideal of Humanity)"],[1823,"Begins lecturing at Göttingen"],[1828,"Publishes his Lectures on the System of Philosophy"],[1832,"Dies in Munich"],[1860,"Julián Sanz del Río's Ideal de la Humanidad brings Krause's ideas to Spain"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Panentheism","body":"Krause coined the term panentheism: everything is in God, but God is more than everything. Unlike pantheism, which says God simply is the world, panentheism holds that the world is part of God, the way a wave is in the sea without being the whole sea."},{"head":"The ideal of humanity","body":"Humanity is meant to grow into a harmonious whole, as the parts of a body work together. Krause imagined families, nations and peoples joining in ever wider unions, ending in a worldwide league of humanity."},{"head":"Law serves human development","body":"Law exists to provide the conditions each person needs to develop fully. This gave rights a positive content: not only protection from interference, but access to education and the means to live well."},{"head":"Respect for all beings","body":"Since everything is in God, all beings have worth. Krause argued for duties towards animals and for equal standing for women, unusual views for his time."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"There are few English translations of Krause. A beginner can start with the opening chapters of The Ideal of Humanity, available in W. Hastie's English translation (1900), which sets out his vision of humanity's development.","critics":"Critics, then and now, found his invented vocabulary obscure and his system hard to distinguish from the pantheism of Schelling and Hegel. In Spain, Catholic opponents attacked Krausism as a disguised pantheism hostile to the church.","legacy":"Little read in Germany, Krause became very influential in Spain and Latin America through 'Krausism', which shaped liberal education reform, including Spain's Institución Libre de Enseñanza, and ideas of social law.","quote":null},"stirner":{"life":["Max Stirner was the pen name of Johann Caspar Schmidt, born in Bayreuth, Bavaria, in 1806. The nickname came from his high forehead (Stirn). He lived in the decades after Hegel, when young radical followers of Hegel, the 'Young Hegelians', were criticising religion, the state and society in Berlin and arguing about what should replace Christianity. Their discussions were fierce and often ended in personal quarrels.","Stirner studied at Berlin, where he heard Hegel lecture, and at Erlangen and Königsberg, but his studies were broken off and his teaching qualification was limited. From 1839 he taught at a private school for girls in Berlin. He joined 'the Free', a group of radicals meeting at a Berlin wine bar, where Engels and Bruno Bauer were also regulars. In late 1844 he published The Ego and Its Own and soon gave up teaching.","The book caused a brief stir; Marx and Engels wrote a long attack on it, though it was not published in their lifetimes. Stirner then sank into obscurity. A dairy business failed, he translated works by Adam Smith and Jean-Baptiste Say, and he was twice briefly jailed for debt. He died in poverty in Berlin in 1856. Almost no image of him survives except a sketch made by Engels from memory."],"timeline":[[1806,"Born Johann Caspar Schmidt in Bayreuth"],[1826,"Enters the University of Berlin, where he hears Hegel"],[1839,"Begins teaching at a girls' school in Berlin"],[1844,"Publishes The Ego and Its Own (dated 1845)"],[1845,"c. Marx and Engels write a long attack on him in The German Ideology"],[1852,"Publishes a History of Reaction"],[1856,"Dies in Berlin"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Spooks in the head","body":"God, Humanity, the State, Morality and even Freedom are 'spooks': abstract ideas that people treat as more real than themselves and then serve. A person who sacrifices their happiness for the idea of duty is, for Stirner, possessed by a ghost."},{"head":"Humanism is religion in disguise","body":"Feuerbach and other radicals had replaced God with 'Man' as the highest value. Stirner said this changes nothing: you are still told to serve something above you, now called humanity instead of God."},{"head":"Ownness","body":"The goal is not freedom from this or that, but 'ownness': being your own, owning your thoughts and acts. You can love a friend or work for a cause, as long as you do it because you want to, not because a fixed idea commands it."},{"head":"The union of egoists","body":"Instead of the state or society, which claim authority over individuals, Stirner proposed voluntary 'unions' that people join for their own purposes and leave when they wish, like friends who play a game together."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Stirner's egoism just means being selfish and trampling on others.","truth":"Stirner's egoist can be generous and loving; what he rejects is being ruled by fixed ideas such as duty or Humanity, not caring for other people."},"startHere":"Read the opening section of The Ego and Its Own, 'All Things Are Nothing to Me', and the chapter 'The Possessed', in David Leopold's edition. They show his style and his idea of 'spooks' in a few pages.","critics":"Marx and Engels argued in The German Ideology that Stirner's 'unique individual' is an abstraction, since people are shaped by their social and economic conditions. Others object that if all values are 'spooks', Stirner's own preference for ownness is one too.","legacy":"Stirner influenced individualist anarchism and is often discussed as a forerunner of Nietzsche and existentialism, though whether Nietzsche read him is disputed.","quote":null},"proudhon":{"life":["Pierre-Joseph Proudhon was born in Besançon, eastern France, in 1809, to a poor family of a cooper and a cook. He lived through France's early industrial age, the revolutions of 1830 and 1848, and the Second Empire of Napoleon III. Socialist ideas were spreading among workers who saw wealth grow while their own lives stayed hard. Many argued about whether reform should come from the state or from workers themselves.","Proudhon left school early and became a printer and typesetter, teaching himself Greek, Hebrew and theology from the books he set. A scholarship from the Besançon Academy let him study in Paris, and in 1840 he published What Is Property?, becoming the first person to call himself an anarchist. Elected to the National Assembly in 1848, he tried to found a People's Bank offering interest-free credit. His attacks on Louis-Napoleon led to three years in prison. In 1858 he fled to Belgium to avoid another sentence, returning in 1862. He died near Paris in 1865.","Proudhon was a self-taught, combative writer who loved paradox and argued with everyone, including other socialists. Marx at first praised him and then attacked him savagely. His private notebooks and some published writings contain hostile views of Jews and of women's equality, which later admirers have had to confront. He wrote over a dozen major works, on economics, religion, war and federalism."],"timeline":[[1809,"Born in Besançon"],[1840,"Publishes What Is Property?"],[1846,"Publishes System of Economic Contradictions"],[1847,"Marx attacks him in The Poverty of Philosophy"],[1848,"Elected to the National Assembly"],[1849,"Imprisoned for attacking Louis-Napoleon (released 1852)"],[1858,"Flees to Belgium after the publication of On Justice"],[1865,"Dies at Passy, near Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Property is theft","body":"Proudhon's famous slogan targets property that lets owners collect income without working: rent, interest and profit on others' labour. A landlord who lives off rents takes part of what tenants produce. He did not object to people keeping the house they live in or the tools they use."},{"head":"Possession versus property","body":"Proudhon distinguished possession, the right to use what you actually work or live in, from property in the sense of a right to extract income from others. He wanted to keep the first and abolish the second."},{"head":"Mutualism","body":"Workers should exchange goods and services through free contracts and cooperatives, backed by banks that lend without interest. This 'mutualism' aimed to end exploitation without a powerful state."},{"head":"Anarchy as order","body":"Anarchy does not mean chaos but order without a ruler. Society can be organised from below by contracts among individuals and groups, rather than imposed from above by government."},{"head":"Federation","body":"In later work he argued that power should be spread among small self-governing units, joined in federations by agreement, so that no central authority could dominate."}],"misconception":{"myth":"'Property is theft' means Proudhon wanted to abolish all personal belongings.","truth":"He defended possession of homes, land and tools by those who use them; he attacked income from ownership alone, such as rent and interest. Late in life he even defended property as a check on state power."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of What Is Property?, which explains his method and the famous slogan. It is lively and short.","critics":"Marx argued in The Poverty of Philosophy that Proudhon misunderstood economics and that reforming exchange and credit could not end capitalism, which rests on how production is organised.","legacy":"Proudhon is a founding figure of anarchism and influenced the early labour movement, especially in France, as well as later ideas of cooperatives and federalism.","quote":null},"huxley":{"life":["Thomas Henry Huxley was born in 1825 in Ealing, near London, the son of a schoolteacher. He lived through the Victorian age, when science was becoming a profession and Darwin's theory of evolution was challenging traditional views of humanity's place in nature. Debates over science and religion filled journals and lecture halls. Universities were still dominated by the Church of England, and science was only beginning to enter school teaching.","Huxley had only about two years of school and largely taught himself. He trained in medicine and in 1846 sailed as assistant surgeon on HMS Rattlesnake to Australia and New Guinea, studying marine animals; this work earned him election to the Royal Society in 1851. After Darwin's Origin of Species (1859) he became evolution's most prominent public defender, earning the nickname 'Darwin's bulldog'. He published Evidence as to Man's Place in Nature in 1863 and coined the word 'agnostic' in 1869.","Huxley was a gifted lecturer who spoke to working men as readily as to scientists. He served on the London School Board, on many royal commissions, and as President of the Royal Society from 1883 to 1885. Late in life he wrote mainly essays on philosophy, religion and ethics, ending with his Romanes Lecture, Evolution and Ethics (1893). He died in Eastbourne in 1895."],"timeline":[[1825,"Born in Ealing"],[1846,"Sails on HMS Rattlesnake as assistant surgeon (returns 1850)"],[1851,"Elected Fellow of the Royal Society"],[1860,"Clashes with Bishop Samuel Wilberforce over evolution at Oxford"],[1863,"Publishes Evidence as to Man's Place in Nature"],[1869,"Coins the word 'agnostic'"],[1893,"Gives the Romanes Lecture, Evolution and Ethics"],[1895,"Dies in Eastbourne"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Agnosticism","body":"Huxley coined 'agnostic' for someone who holds that we do not, and perhaps cannot, know whether God exists. For him it was a method more than a belief: follow the evidence as far as it goes, and do not claim certainty where there is none."},{"head":"Humans are animals","body":"In Man's Place in Nature he compared human anatomy with that of apes and showed the differences are smaller than those between apes and monkeys. Humans belong in the animal kingdom, though that does not make them less worthy."},{"head":"Ethics against the cosmic process","body":"Evolution works by ruthless competition, but human ethics asks us to help the weak. Huxley argued that morality is not copying nature but resisting it, as a gardener holds back weeds to grow a garden."},{"head":"Consciousness as by-product","body":"Huxley suggested that consciousness may be like the whistle of a steam engine: produced by the machine, but not driving it. This view, later called epiphenomenalism, says mental states are effects of brain processes, not causes."}],"misconception":{"myth":"At the 1860 Oxford debate Huxley crushed Bishop Wilberforce with a famous one-line retort about apes.","truth":"No exact record of the exchange exists; the best-known versions were written down decades later, and accounts differ about what was said and who came off best."},"startHere":"Read his short essay 'Agnosticism' (1889), where he explains why he coined the word. It is clear, combative and shows his view of evidence and belief.","critics":"The clergyman Henry Wace argued in 1889 that 'agnostic' was just a polite name for an unbeliever who refused to admit it. On ethics, critics asked how morality could oppose evolution if it is itself a product of evolution.","legacy":"Huxley did much to win acceptance for evolution and to professionalise British science and science education, and his word 'agnostic' is now in common use.","quote":{"text":"In matters of the intellect, follow your reason as far as it will take you, without regard to any other consideration.","src":"Agnosticism (1889)","tr":"original English"}},"hanslick":{"life":["Eduard Hanslick was born in Prague in 1825, then part of the Austrian Empire. He lived through a lively period in central European music, when Wagner and Liszt argued that music should express ideas, stories and emotions, while others, including Brahms, held to traditional forms. Concert criticism in newspapers carried real influence over public taste. Vienna, where he lived, was one of the musical capitals of Europe.","Hanslick studied law in Prague and Vienna and worked for a time as a civil servant while writing music criticism. In 1854 he published On the Musically Beautiful, which went through many editions. He began lecturing at the University of Vienna in 1856 and became professor there, among the first chairs in music history and aesthetics. From 1864 he was the chief music critic of Vienna's Neue Freie Presse, a post he held for most of the rest of his life. He died near Vienna in 1904.","Hanslick was the most powerful critic in Vienna, witty and widely read. He championed Brahms and opposed Wagner's music dramas and later Bruckner. Wagner resented him, and in an early draft of The Mastersingers of Nuremberg the pedantic critic later called Beckmesser was named 'Veit Hanslich'. Hanslick also wrote memoirs and a history of concert life in Vienna. His reviews could make or damage a composer's reputation in the city."],"timeline":[[1825,"Born in Prague"],[1854,"Publishes On the Musically Beautiful"],[1856,"Begins lecturing at the University of Vienna"],[1861,"Becomes a professor at the University of Vienna"],[1864,"Becomes music critic of the Neue Freie Presse"],[1904,"Dies at Baden, near Vienna"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Music's content is musical","body":"Music does not tell stories or depict definite emotions. Its content is the play of melody, harmony and rhythm itself, which Hanslick called 'tonally moving forms'. A Bach fugue is about its own unfolding, as a pattern in a kaleidoscope is about itself."},{"head":"Music can't represent definite feelings","body":"Love, anger or hope have objects: I love someone, I hope for something. Music has no way to show such objects, so it cannot represent these feelings. The same melody could fit words of joy or of grief."},{"head":"Music can capture motion","body":"What music can capture is the dynamic side of feeling: speeding up, slowing down, rising, falling, tension and release. This is why music seems emotional, without carrying a specific emotion."},{"head":"Our reactions are not the standard","body":"That a piece makes you cry says something about you, not about the music's beauty. Hanslick argued that judging music should focus on the work itself, not on listeners' personal feelings."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hanslick thought music was cold and had nothing to do with feeling.","truth":"He accepted that music moves listeners deeply; he denied only that representing feelings is what music is about or what makes it beautiful."},"startHere":"Read Chapter 2 of On the Musically Beautiful, 'The \"Representation of Feelings\" Is Not the Content of Music', in Lee Rothfarb and Christoph Landerer's translation. It contains his main argument and examples.","critics":"Wagner and his supporters held that music should express drama and emotion. Later philosophers such as Peter Kivy, though sympathetic to formalism, argued that sadness or cheerfulness can be heard as real features of the music's form, which Hanslick's view struggles to allow.","legacy":"Hanslick founded musical formalism, the view that music's value lies in its structure, and remains a starting point for debates about music and emotion.","quote":null},"tolstoy":{"life":["Leo Tolstoy was born in 1828 on his family's estate of Yasnaya Polyana, south of Moscow, into an old aristocratic family. He lived through the last decades of serfdom in Russia, its abolition in 1861, and the rise of revolutionary movements. Russian writers argued over whether their country should follow Western Europe or its own path. From 1859 Tolstoy ran a school for peasant children on his own estate.","Tolstoy left Kazan University without a degree, served in the army in the Caucasus and in the Crimean War, and won fame with stories drawn from his experience. He wrote War and Peace (1865–69) and Anna Karenina (1875–77). Around 1880 he went through a crisis about the meaning of life, described in A Confession, and turned to a radical Christianity based on the Sermon on the Mount. The Orthodox Church excommunicated him in 1901.","In his later years Tolstoy wrote essays and stories promoting non-violence, simple living and refusal to cooperate with state violence, and in What Is Art? (1897–98) he set out a new theory of art. He corresponded with the young Gandhi. In 1910, aged 82, he left home secretly and died of pneumonia days later at the railway station of Astapovo."],"timeline":[[1828,"Born at Yasnaya Polyana"],[1854,"Serves in the Crimean War, including the siege of Sevastopol (1854–55)"],[1869,"Completes War and Peace"],[1877,"Completes Anna Karenina"],[1882,"Completes A Confession"],[1894,"Publishes The Kingdom of God Is Within You"],[1897,"Publishes What Is Art? (1897–98)"],[1910,"Leaves home and dies at Astapovo"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Art as infection of feeling","body":"Art is one person passing on a feeling they have experienced so that others feel it too. A boy who meets a wolf and tells the story so that listeners feel his fear is making art. If the feeling does not pass to the audience, it is not art, however skilful."},{"head":"Good art unites people","body":"Art is judged first by whether it truly infects, and then by the feelings it spreads. The best art spreads feelings that bring people together as brothers and sisters, whether religious feelings or simple shared joys any person can understand."},{"head":"Against elite art","body":"Much admired art, Tolstoy argued, is obscure, made for a small upper class, and spreads only pride or lust. He rejected much of Shakespeare and Wagner, and even dismissed most of his own earlier novels as bad art."},{"head":"Do not resist evil by force","body":"Tolstoy took Jesus' command not to resist evil by violence literally. Christians should refuse military service, oaths and cooperation with state violence. This idea influenced Gandhi's non-violent resistance."},{"head":"History is not made by great men","body":"In War and Peace he argued that events like Napoleon's invasion of Russia result from the actions of countless ordinary people, not the decisions of leaders who only imagine they are in control."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Tolstoy's moral and religious views were those of the Russian Orthodox Church.","truth":"He rejected most church doctrines, including the Trinity, miracles and the sacraments, and was excommunicated in 1901."},"startHere":"Read Chapter 5 of What Is Art?, where Tolstoy gives his definition of art as the passing on of feeling. It is short and sets up everything else in the book.","critics":"Critics have objected that his theory leaves out art that conveys ideas or explores form rather than feeling, and that it condemns much of the world's greatest art, including his own novels.","legacy":"Tolstoy is among the greatest novelists, and his ideas on non-violence influenced Gandhi and later movements for peaceful resistance. His theory of art is still a standard reference point for expression theories.","quote":null},"mach":{"life":["Ernst Mach was born in 1838 in Chirlitz, Moravia, now part of the Czech Republic, then in the Austrian Empire. He worked when physics seemed close to complete, built on Newton's mechanics, and when debates were growing over whether atoms were real and whether science describes hidden realities or only what we observe. Physicists also argued about whether absolute space and time existed.","Mach studied physics in Vienna, earning his doctorate in 1860. He taught at Graz and in 1867 became professor of experimental physics at Prague, where he stayed for nearly thirty years. He did important work on perception, on how we sense motion and balance, and on supersonic flight: in 1887 he photographed the shock waves of a speeding bullet. The ratio of an object's speed to the speed of sound is now called the Mach number. In 1895 he took a chair in philosophy of the inductive sciences at Vienna.","A stroke in 1898 left him partly paralysed, and he retired in 1901, though he kept writing. Mach was both an experimenter and a historian of science; his Science of Mechanics (1883) traces the history of physics critically to show which concepts are really supported by experience. He died near Munich in 1916. He wrote clearly for general readers as well as specialists, and his popular science lectures were widely translated."],"timeline":[[1838,"Born in Chirlitz, Moravia"],[1860,"Gains his doctorate in physics in Vienna"],[1867,"Becomes professor of experimental physics at Prague"],[1883,"Publishes The Science of Mechanics"],[1886,"Publishes The Analysis of Sensations"],[1887,"Publishes photographs of shock waves around a supersonic bullet"],[1895,"Takes a chair in philosophy at the University of Vienna"],[1916,"Dies near Munich"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Science as economy of thought","body":"Scientific laws are compact summaries of many observations, like a timetable summarises thousands of train journeys. Their job is to save mental effort, not to reveal hidden essences behind experience."},{"head":"Everything is built from sensations","body":"Colours, sounds, pressures and other sensations are the basic elements of the world. 'Bodies' and 'selves' are relatively stable bundles of these elements. Physics and psychology study the same elements, just from different angles."},{"head":"The self cannot be saved","body":"There is no fixed inner self behind our experiences. The 'I' is a changing collection of memories, feelings and sensations held together loosely. Mach thought accepting this could make people less anxious about death and less selfish."},{"head":"Against absolute space","body":"Newton argued that a spinning bucket of water shows motion relative to absolute space. Mach replied that the motion might be relative to the distant stars. Einstein later named this 'Mach's principle' and credited Mach as an influence."},{"head":"Suspicion of atoms","body":"Mach doubted the reality of atoms, since they could not be observed, and treated them at most as useful models. This put him at odds with Boltzmann and Planck."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of The Analysis of Sensations, 'Introductory Remarks: Antimetaphysical'. It explains in plain terms his view of the self and of science.","critics":"Max Planck argued that physics aims at a picture of the world independent of human senses, and that Mach's view would make science merely a record of perceptions. Lenin attacked his views in Materialism and Empirio-criticism (1909) as idealism.","legacy":"Mach influenced Einstein's early thinking about space and motion and inspired the logical positivists of the Vienna Circle, whose founding society was named after him.","quote":null},"sidgwick":{"life":["Henry Sidgwick was born in Skipton, Yorkshire, in 1838, the son of a clergyman and headmaster. He lived through Victorian England's crisis of faith, when Darwin and biblical scholarship led many educated people to doubt Christianity, and when utilitarians such as John Stuart Mill were reshaping moral and political thinking. Many educated people wanted to know whether morality could stand without religious foundations.","Sidgwick studied at Rugby and at Trinity College, Cambridge, where he became a fellow in 1859. In 1869 he resigned his fellowship because he could no longer honestly affirm the Church of England's doctrines, which fellows had to accept; the requirement was abolished soon after. The Methods of Ethics appeared in 1874. He became Knightbridge Professor of Moral Philosophy at Cambridge in 1883 and also wrote on economics and politics. He died in 1900.","Sidgwick was known for patient, fair-minded argument, often more willing to state problems than to claim he had solved them. He worked hard for women's higher education and helped found Newnham College, Cambridge. He also co-founded the Society for Psychical Research in 1882, hoping to find evidence for life after death, which he thought morality might need. He married Eleanor Balfour, who later became principal of Newnham."],"timeline":[[1838,"Born in Skipton, Yorkshire"],[1859,"Elected a fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge"],[1869,"Resigns his fellowship over religious tests"],[1874,"Publishes The Methods of Ethics"],[1875,"Newnham Hall, which he helped found, opens for women students"],[1882,"Becomes first president of the Society for Psychical Research"],[1883,"Becomes Knightbridge Professor of Moral Philosophy"],[1900,"Dies in Terling, Essex"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Three methods of ethics","body":"Ordinary moral reasoning uses three methods: egoism (do what is best for yourself), intuitionism (follow self-evident rules like 'keep promises') and utilitarianism (do what is best for everyone). Sidgwick examined each carefully to see which holds up."},{"head":"Common sense points to utility","body":"When we look closely at common moral rules, they turn out vague and full of exceptions. When should a promise be broken? Sidgwick argued that we settle such cases by asking what produces the most happiness, so common sense morality is unconsciously utilitarian."},{"head":"The point of view of the universe","body":"Reason shows that my good counts for no more than anyone else's when viewed impartially. This self-evident principle of benevolence supports utilitarianism."},{"head":"The dualism of practical reason","body":"Yet it also seems reasonable to put my own good first. When the two clash, for example when helping strangers costs me dearly, reason seems to back both sides. Sidgwick could find no way to reconcile them without a God who rewards the good, which he could not prove."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the Preface to the sixth edition of The Methods of Ethics, where Sidgwick describes his own route through Mill, Kant and intuitionism. Then try Book III, Chapter 13, on philosophical intuitionism.","critics":"F. H. Bradley attacked his hedonism, the view that pleasure is the only good, in Mr. Sidgwick's Hedonism (1877). Bernard Williams later criticised his suggestion that utilitarians may keep some moral truths from the public, calling it 'Government House utilitarianism'.","legacy":"The Methods of Ethics is widely considered one of the most careful works of moral philosophy in English and a model for modern analytic ethics, admired by Rawls and Parfit and still used by utilitarians such as Peter Singer.","quote":{"text":"The good of any one individual is of no more importance, from the point of view (if I may say so) of the Universe, than the good of any other.","src":"The Methods of Ethics, Book III, ch. 13","tr":"original English"}},"henry_george":{"life":["Henry George was born in Philadelphia in 1839, into a modest religious family. He lived through America's rapid industrialisation after the Civil War, when railways, cities and fortunes grew fast but so did poverty, slums and labour unrest. In the West he watched land prices soar as speculators bought up land ahead of settlers. Labour strikes and economic slumps made many ask why progress did not help the poor.","George left school at about fourteen and went to sea, sailing as far as Australia and India. In 1858 he settled in California, where he worked as a printer and journalist, often in poverty. Observing how the arrival of railways in California raised land values while wages stayed low led him to write Progress and Poverty, published in 1879. It became an international bestseller.","George moved to New York in 1880 and became a famous lecturer, touring Britain and Ireland. In 1886 he ran for mayor of New York as a labour candidate, coming second ahead of the young Theodore Roosevelt. He replied in print to Pope Leo XIII's encyclical on labour in 1891. He ran for mayor again in 1897 and died of a stroke days before the election."],"timeline":[[1839,"Born in Philadelphia"],[1855,"Goes to sea, sailing to Australia and India"],[1858,"Arrives in California"],[1879,"Publishes Progress and Poverty"],[1880,"Moves to New York"],[1886,"Runs for mayor of New York, finishing second"],[1891,"Publishes The Condition of Labor, an open letter to Pope Leo XIII"],[1897,"Dies in New York during his second mayoral campaign"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Progress brings poverty","body":"As societies grow richer, why does poverty persist and even deepen? George's answer was that the gains of progress are absorbed by rising land values. A new railway makes nearby land valuable, and landowners collect the gain through higher rents, while workers' wages stay low."},{"head":"Land is different","body":"Things people make, like houses or tools, rightly belong to their makers. Land was made by no one, and its value comes mostly from the community around it. So the value of land belongs to everyone."},{"head":"Tax land, not work","body":"George proposed taxing the rental value of land heavily, while removing taxes on wages, trade and buildings. This 'single tax' would discourage speculators from holding land empty and would fund public needs without punishing work."},{"head":"Free trade","body":"George opposed tariffs, arguing they raise prices for ordinary people and protect special interests. His Protection or Free Trade (1886) was widely read."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Henry George wanted the government to take over all land.","truth":"He proposed leaving land in private hands and taxing its rental value instead, arguing it was not necessary to confiscate land, only its rent."},"startHere":"Read the Introduction to Progress and Poverty, 'The Problem', which sets out the puzzle he is trying to solve. Then read Book VI, Chapter 2, 'The True Remedy'.","critics":"Many economists, including Alfred Marshall, argued that land value is hard to separate from improvements and that a land tax alone could not fund modern government. Pope Leo XIII's Rerum Novarum (1891) defended private ownership of land, prompting George's reply.","legacy":"George inspired the 'Georgist' movement and many land value tax schemes around the world, and economists across the spectrum still praise land taxes as efficient. The board game that became Monopoly began as Lizzie Magie's Landlord's Game, made to illustrate his ideas.","quote":{"text":"We must make land common property.","src":"Progress and Poverty, Book VI, ch. 2","tr":"original English"}},"kropotkin":{"life":["Peter Kropotkin was born in Moscow in 1842 into an old princely family. He grew up in the Russia of the tsars, where serfdom was abolished in 1861 but political freedom was absent and radical movements faced harsh repression. In Western Europe, socialists and anarchists were debating how a society without exploitation could work. Many radicals of his generation, especially among the young nobility, felt guilt at their privilege and went 'to the people'.","Kropotkin trained in the elite Corps of Pages and served as a page to Tsar Alexander II. Instead of a court career he chose service in Siberia, where he made geographical expeditions and was struck by how animals and peasant communities cooperated to survive. He resigned in 1867, studied in St Petersburg and worked as a geographer. After joining revolutionary circles he was arrested in 1874, made a famous escape from prison in 1876, and fled abroad.","In exile he was expelled from Switzerland and imprisoned in France from 1883 to 1886, then lived in England for three decades, writing for scientific journals and anarchist papers. His books include The Conquest of Bread (1892), Mutual Aid (1902) and Memoirs of a Revolutionist (1899). He returned to Russia in 1917, criticised the Bolsheviks, and died near Moscow in 1921."],"timeline":[[1842,"Born in Moscow"],[1862,"Begins military and scientific service in Siberia"],[1874,"Arrested for revolutionary activity"],[1876,"Escapes from prison and flees Russia"],[1883,"Imprisoned in France (released 1886)"],[1892,"Publishes The Conquest of Bread"],[1902,"Publishes Mutual Aid"],[1921,"Dies at Dmitrov, near Moscow"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Mutual aid in evolution","body":"Darwin's followers often stressed competition, but Kropotkin argued that cooperation is just as important. Animals that hunt in packs, warn each other or share food often survive better than solitary rivals. Mutual aid is a factor of evolution."},{"head":"Cooperation in human history","body":"Human history shows the same pattern: clans, village communities, medieval guilds and trade unions were all forms of mutual aid. The modern state, Kropotkin argued, destroyed many of these and replaced them with centralised control."},{"head":"Anarchist communism","body":"Since everything is produced by the shared work of many generations, goods should be shared according to need, not wages. Free communes and voluntary associations would organise production and distribution without a state."},{"head":"Bread for all first","body":"In The Conquest of Bread he argued that the first task of any revolution is to make sure everyone has food, shelter and clothing. Abundance is possible if production aims at human needs rather than profit."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Kropotkin denied that there is competition or struggle in nature.","truth":"He accepted that struggle exists, but argued that cooperation within species is an equally important factor in survival, which earlier Darwinians had underrated."},"startHere":"Read the Introduction and first chapter of Mutual Aid, on cooperation among animals. It is clear, full of examples, and shows how he connected science and politics.","critics":"He was answering T. H. Huxley's 1888 essay on the struggle for existence, which stressed competition. The anarchist Errico Malatesta later criticised Kropotkin's optimism that nature guarantees a harmonious society, and his support for the Allies in the First World War.","legacy":"Kropotkin is a leading theorist of anarchism, and his work on cooperation anticipated later research in biology on the evolution of altruism and cooperation.","quote":null},"clifford":{"life":["William Kingdon Clifford was born in Exeter in 1845, the son of a bookseller. He lived in the Victorian age, when Darwin's theory and new mathematics were reshaping how educated people understood the world, and when science and religion were openly debated at gatherings like the Metaphysical Society, where Clifford sparred with clergy and scientists alike. Many young scientists of his generation openly questioned religious belief.","Clifford studied mathematics at Trinity College, Cambridge, graduating second wrangler in 1867, and became a fellow the following year. In 1871 he became professor of applied mathematics at University College London and in 1874 a Fellow of the Royal Society. He did original work in geometry, inventing what are now called Clifford algebras, and suggested that matter and motion might be explained by the curvature of space, an idea later developed in Einstein's general relativity.","Clifford was a celebrated lecturer and essayist who wrote on ethics, religion and science for general readers. He was known for his energy, humour and outspoken unbelief. Overwork weakened his health, and he died of tuberculosis in Madeira in 1879, aged only thirty-three. His friends published his Lectures and Essays the same year. He married the writer Lucy Lane in 1875."],"timeline":[[1845,"Born in Exeter"],[1867,"Graduates second wrangler at Cambridge"],[1871,"Becomes professor of applied mathematics at University College London"],[1874,"Elected Fellow of the Royal Society"],[1877,"Publishes 'The Ethics of Belief'"],[1879,"Dies in Madeira; Lectures and Essays published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The ethics of belief","body":"Believing is not a private matter. A shipowner who convinces himself his old ship is safe, without checking, and lets it sail with emigrants who then drown, is guilty, and would be guilty even if the ship had arrived safely. He had no right to believe on such evidence."},{"head":"A duty to inquire","body":"We must examine our beliefs and seek evidence, because beliefs guide actions that affect others, and because careless believing makes society credulous. Avoiding questions in order to keep a comforting belief is a moral failure."},{"head":"Limits of authority","body":"We may rely on other people's testimony, but only when we have reason to think they know what they are talking about and are honest. A belief is not justified just because it is old or widely held."},{"head":"Mind-stuff","body":"Clifford suggested that the basic material of the world might have a mental side, a 'mind-stuff', of which consciousness is a complex form. This early version of panpsychism has recently attracted new interest."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'The Ethics of Belief' (1877), especially its first section with the shipowner example. It is short, vivid and still widely assigned.","critics":"William James replied in 'The Will to Believe' (1896) that when a question is live and cannot be settled by evidence, such as religious faith, refusing to believe is itself a risky choice, and that Clifford's rule would stop us from ever finding some truths.","legacy":"His principle is the classic statement of 'evidentialism', the view that belief should match evidence, and frames debates on faith and the ethics of belief. In mathematics, Clifford algebras are used in physics and computer graphics.","quote":{"text":"It is wrong always, everywhere, and for anyone, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence.","src":"The Ethics of Belief, section 1","tr":"original English"}},"bradley":{"life":["Francis Herbert Bradley was born in Clapham, near London, in 1846, son of an evangelical clergyman. He worked in late Victorian and Edwardian England, when British philosophy, long dominated by the empiricism of Mill, turned towards the idealism of Kant and Hegel. Bradley became the leading British idealist. Idealists held that reality is in some sense spiritual or experiential, and that the world forms a connected whole.","Bradley studied at University College, Oxford, and in 1870 was elected to a fellowship at Merton College, which he held for life. It carried no teaching duties. In 1871 a kidney illness left him in fragile health, and he lived a quiet, retired life in college, often spending winters in the south of England or France for his health. He published Ethical Studies in 1876, The Principles of Logic in 1883, and Appearance and Reality in 1893.","Bradley wrote with irony and polemical force, often mocking his opponents. He rarely took part in public life and did not lecture, yet his books dominated British philosophy around 1900. In 1924 he became the first philosopher awarded the Order of Merit, and he died in Oxford later that year. His brother A. C. Bradley was a famous Shakespeare critic."],"timeline":[[1846,"Born in Clapham"],[1870,"Elected to a fellowship at Merton College, Oxford"],[1876,"Publishes Ethical Studies"],[1883,"Publishes The Principles of Logic"],[1893,"Publishes Appearance and Reality"],[1914,"Publishes Essays on Truth and Reality"],[1924,"Awarded the Order of Merit; dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Relations lead to regress","body":"If a lump of sugar is white and sweet, what links the whiteness and sweetness? A relation. But then what links the relation to each quality? Another relation, and so on forever. Bradley concluded that a world of separate things in relations cannot be ultimately real."},{"head":"Reality as one whole","body":"Ultimate reality, which Bradley called the Absolute, is a single, harmonious whole of experience. Ordinary things, times and selves are 'appearances': not illusions, but partial and inconsistent views of that whole."},{"head":"Degrees of truth","body":"No single statement is wholly true, because each picks out only part of reality. But some are truer than others, depending on how complete and consistent they are. A scientist's account of a flower is truer than a child's, though neither is the whole story."},{"head":"My station and its duties","body":"In Ethical Studies he argued that we find our moral identity in our role in a community, as parent, worker, citizen. Morality is not obeying abstract rules but realising yourself through your place in society, though he saw limits to this view too."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the essay 'My Station and Its Duties' in Ethical Studies. It is his most readable work and shows his criticism of both hedonism and abstract duty.","critics":"Bertrand Russell and G. E. Moore led a revolt against Bradley around 1900, arguing that relations are real and that the world consists of many independent things. William James also attacked his monism.","legacy":"Bradley was the dominant figure in British idealism, and the reaction against him helped create analytic philosophy. T. S. Eliot wrote his doctoral thesis on Bradley's philosophy.","quote":{"text":"Metaphysics is the finding of bad reasons for what we believe upon instinct, but to find these reasons is no less an instinct.","src":"Appearance and Reality, Preface","tr":"original English"}},"bernstein":{"life":["Eduard Bernstein was born in Berlin in 1850, the son of a Jewish railway engine driver. He grew up as Germany became a united industrial power and a large workers' movement formed. That movement took Karl Marx as its guide and expected capitalism to collapse in a crisis that would bring socialism. Bernstein spent much of his life asking whether that forecast still fitted the facts he could see around him.","He worked as a bank clerk and joined the socialist party in 1872. When Bismarck's Anti-Socialist Laws of 1878 drove socialists underground, Bernstein went into exile, first in Zurich, where he edited the party paper Der Sozialdemokrat, and from 1888 in London, where he became close to Friedrich Engels. In the late 1890s he published articles questioning Marx's predictions, then the book The Preconditions of Socialism (1899). This started the 'revisionism' dispute. He returned to Germany in 1901 and sat in the Reichstag for several terms. He came to oppose the war credits during the First World War, voting against them from 1915 and died in Berlin in 1932.","Bernstein was a journalist and party organiser rather than an academic. His method was to compare Marxist predictions with statistics on wages, small businesses and economic crises, and to say plainly where they failed. He was willing to criticise the party's official doctrine in public, which made him many enemies, but he stayed loyal to the socialist movement all his life."],"timeline":[[1850,"Born in Berlin"],[1872,"Joins the German socialist movement"],[1881,"Becomes editor of Der Sozialdemokrat in exile in Zurich"],[1888,"Expelled from Switzerland; moves to London, where he works closely with Engels"],[1899,"Publishes The Preconditions of Socialism, starting the revisionism debate"],[1901,"Returns to Germany"],[1902,"Elected to the Reichstag for the first time"],[1932,"Dies in Berlin"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Capitalism is not collapsing","body":"Marx expected small businesses to disappear, workers to grow poorer and crises to get worse until the system broke. Bernstein pointed to evidence that the middle classes were not vanishing and that workers' conditions were improving in places. So socialists should not wait for a final crash."},{"head":"The movement matters more than the end goal","body":"Bernstein said that the 'final goal' of socialism meant little to him compared with the day-to-day movement for better conditions. What counts is steady progress: a shorter working day, safer factories, stronger unions. Socialism is something built step by step, not a future event to wait for."},{"head":"Democracy as means and end","body":"For Bernstein, democracy was not just a tool for winning power but part of what socialism is. Workers should use the vote, parliaments, trade unions and co-operatives to change society peacefully. A revolution that overrode democracy would destroy the thing socialists were trying to achieve."},{"head":"An ethical case for socialism","body":"Bernstein doubted that history guarantees socialism. Instead, he argued, socialism should be defended because it is just, drawing on Kant's idea of treating people as ends in themselves. That shifts the argument from prediction to moral choice."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the preface and the final chapter of The Preconditions of Socialism (translated as Evolutionary Socialism), where Bernstein sums up his case for gradual reform. They show what he was arguing against and why.","critics":"Rosa Luxemburg answered him in Social Reform or Revolution (1899), arguing that reforms can soften capitalism but never abolish it, so giving up revolution means giving up socialism. Karl Kautsky also defended Marxist orthodoxy against him inside the party.","legacy":"Bernstein is often seen as a founder of modern social democracy, the idea of reaching socialist goals through elections and reform. When the German Social Democratic Party formally dropped Marxism at Bad Godesberg in 1959, it moved in the direction he had argued for.","quote":null},"wilde":{"life":["Oscar Wilde was born in Dublin in 1854 to a well-known Irish family: his father was a surgeon and his mother a poet. He grew up in Victorian Britain and Ireland, a world of strong moral codes in public life. Many critics of the time judged a book or painting by whether it improved its audience. Wilde became the best-known voice of 'aestheticism', the view that art should be valued for its beauty, not its lessons.","He studied at Trinity College Dublin and then at Magdalen College, Oxford, where he learned from the writings of John Ruskin and Walter Pater. He made his name as a wit and lecturer, touring America in 1882. His novel The Picture of Dorian Gray appeared in 1890 and as a book in 1891, the same year as Intentions, his collection of essays on art. Comedies such as The Importance of Being Earnest (1895) made him famous. In 1895 a failed libel case led to his conviction for 'gross indecency' with men and two years of hard labour. He died in Paris in 1900.","Wilde loved paradox: saying something that sounds absurd in order to show a hidden truth. Many of his essays are written as playful dialogues in which one speaker turns common sense upside down. Behind the jokes were serious ideas about art, criticism and individual freedom. In prison he wrote a long letter later published as De Profundis, and after his release The Ballad of Reading Gaol (1898)."],"timeline":[[1854,"Born in Dublin"],[1878,"Wins Oxford's Newdigate Prize for poetry"],[1882,"Lecture tour of the United States"],[1891,"Publishes The Picture of Dorian Gray as a book, and Intentions"],[1895,"The Importance of Being Earnest opens; later that year convicted and imprisoned"],[1897,"Released from prison and leaves for France"],[1898,"Publishes The Ballad of Reading Gaol"],[1900,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Art for art's sake","body":"Wilde held that a work of art should be judged by its beauty and craft, not by whether it teaches good behaviour. A novel about a wicked man is not a wicked novel. In the preface to Dorian Gray he says books are simply well written or badly written."},{"head":"Life imitates art","body":"In 'The Decay of Lying' a speaker argues that art shapes how we see the world, rather than copying it. Before painters showed London fogs, he jokes, people did not really notice them. The serious point is that our perception is trained by the images and stories we absorb."},{"head":"Criticism is creative","body":"In 'The Critic as Artist' Wilde argues that good criticism is not a dull report on a work but a new work of art in its own right. A critic who writes about a painting builds something fresh from it, as the painter built something from nature."},{"head":"Individualism and socialism","body":"In 'The Soul of Man under Socialism' (1891) Wilde argued that ending poverty would free people to develop their own personalities. He wanted socialism for the sake of individual freedom, and warned against any authority, including a socialist state, that tells people how to live."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Wilde said 'Be yourself; everyone else is already taken.'","truth":"This line, and many other witty sayings attributed to him online, does not appear in any of his writings. His real epigrams are in his plays, his novel and the preface to Dorian Gray."},"startHere":"Read the short preface to The Picture of Dorian Gray, a page of sharp sayings about art, and then the dialogue 'The Decay of Lying' in Intentions. Together they give his view of art in his own playful voice.","critics":"Leo Tolstoy, in What Is Art? (1897), attacked aestheticism directly: he argued that art matters because it passes feelings from person to person and can unite people morally, so art cut off from morality is empty.","legacy":"Wilde's defence of art's independence from moral lessons shaped modern debates about censorship and artistic freedom. His essays are still read as early statements of the idea that criticism and interpretation are creative acts.","quote":{"text":"There is no such thing as a moral or an immoral book. Books are well written, or badly written. That is all.","src":"The Picture of Dorian Gray, Preface","tr":"original English"}},"tucker":{"life":["Benjamin Tucker was born in 1854 near New Bedford, Massachusetts, into a family with Quaker connections. He grew up in post-Civil War America, a time of fast industrial growth, railway monopolies and fierce argument over money and banking. American radicals such as Josiah Warren and William B. Greene had already argued for individual sovereignty and free banking. Tucker joined these ideas to those of the French anarchist Pierre-Joseph Proudhon.","As a young man he translated Proudhon's What Is Property? (1876). In 1881 he founded the journal Liberty, which he edited until 1908 and which became the main voice of 'individualist anarchism' in America. His essays were collected in Instead of a Book (1893). In later years he moved towards the egoism of Max Stirner, whose book he helped publish in English in 1907. A fire in 1908 destroyed his New York bookshop and stock, and he moved to France and later Monaco, where he died in 1939.","Tucker was mainly an editor and polemicist. He wrote crisp, combative replies to readers and opponents and was known for strict logic. He also published translations of European radical writers, giving American readers access to work they could not otherwise read. He rejected violence as a strategy and argued that change would come through education, passive resistance and the spread of alternative institutions such as mutual banks."],"timeline":[[1854,"Born in South Dartmouth, Massachusetts"],[1876,"Publishes his English translation of Proudhon's What Is Property?"],[1881,"Founds the journal Liberty"],[1888,"Publishes 'State Socialism and Anarchism' in Liberty"],[1893,"Publishes Instead of a Book"],[1908,"A fire destroys his bookshop; Liberty ends and he leaves for Europe"],[1939,"Dies in Monaco"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Equal liberty","body":"Each person may do whatever they like as long as they do not invade the equal freedom of others. Tucker took this as his basic principle. Anything beyond defending people from invasion, he argued, is not a proper use of force."},{"head":"The state is the main monopolist","body":"Tucker blamed poverty on four legal monopolies: over money and banking, over land, over tariffs and over patents. Remove these state-backed privileges, he said, and free competition would push interest, rent and profit towards zero, so workers would keep the full product of their labour."},{"head":"Anarchists as consistent liberals","body":"Tucker presented anarchism as the logical end of American liberal ideas. If government that governs least is best, then the best is none at all. He contrasted this with state socialism, which he saw as giving the state even more power."},{"head":"Voluntary association","body":"Public needs such as defence, he argued, could be met by voluntary associations that people choose to join and pay for. No one should be taxed or ruled without consent."}],"misconception":{"myth":"All anarchists want to abolish private property and markets.","truth":"Tucker defended markets and property in what people produce, and he argued that land should belong to those who actually occupy and use it. He quarrelled sharply with communist anarchists over this."},"startHere":"Read the essay 'State Socialism and Anarchism', which is short and sets out how Tucker's anarchism differs from Marxist socialism while sharing its concern for workers.","critics":"Communist anarchists such as Peter Kropotkin rejected his defence of markets and wages. From the other side, the libertarian economist Murray Rothbard admired him but argued that his labour theory of value and his 'occupancy and use' rule for land were mistaken economics.","legacy":"Tucker is the main figure of American individualist anarchism. His journal Liberty is a key source for historians, and modern libertarians and market anarchists often look back to him.","quote":{"text":"The Anarchists are simply unterrified Jeffersonian Democrats.","src":"State Socialism and Anarchism","tr":"original English"}},"duhem":{"life":["Pierre Duhem was born in Paris in 1861. French science at the time was proud and centralised: a career depended on approval from a few senior figures in Paris. Physics was changing fast, with thermodynamics (the science of heat and energy) growing in importance, while scientists argued about whether atoms were real. Duhem worked in the middle of these debates.","He entered the École Normale Supérieure in 1882. His first doctoral thesis, on thermodynamics, was rejected in 1884, reportedly because it contradicted the views of the powerful chemist Marcellin Berthelot. He took a doctorate later and taught at Lille and Rennes before settling at Bordeaux in 1894, where he stayed until his death in 1916. He never received a Paris chair. Besides physics, he wrote The Aim and Structure of Physical Theory (1906) and huge histories of science, including the multi-volume Système du monde.","Duhem was a devout Catholic and a combative, independent scholar. He was a working physicist who thought hard about his own practice. His historical research took him into medieval manuscripts, where he found thinkers such as Jean Buridan and Nicole Oresme discussing ideas about motion long before Galileo. He published widely in physics, especially thermodynamics, hydrodynamics and elasticity, and saw his history and philosophy of science as serving one goal: understanding what physics can and cannot achieve."],"timeline":[[1861,"Born in Paris"],[1882,"Enters the École Normale Supérieure"],[1884,"First doctoral thesis rejected"],[1894,"Becomes professor at Bordeaux"],[1906,"Publishes The Aim and Structure of Physical Theory"],[1908,"Publishes To Save the Phenomena"],[1913,"First volume of Le Système du monde appears"],[1916,"Dies at Cabrespine, in southern France"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Theories organise, not explain","body":"For Duhem a physical theory is a set of mathematical statements that summarises and orders experimental laws. It does not reveal the hidden causes behind appearances, which is a job for metaphysics. A good theory is like a well-designed filing system for what we observe."},{"head":"Tests involve many assumptions","body":"When an experiment fails to match a prediction, we cannot tell which single hypothesis is to blame. The prediction rested on the theory under test, plus assumptions about the instruments and other theories. So no single hypothesis in physics can be tested on its own."},{"head":"No crucial experiments","body":"It follows that no experiment can decisively prove one theory and refute its rival, because each side can adjust other assumptions. Scientists choose using 'good sense', a trained judgement that cannot be turned into rules."},{"head":"Science has a medieval past","body":"Duhem's historical work argued that modern physics did not appear suddenly with Galileo. Medieval scholars in Paris had developed key ideas about motion, so the story is one of gradual growth rather than a sudden break."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Duhem held the same sweeping view as the later 'Duhem-Quine thesis', that any belief at all can be kept if we change enough others.","truth":"Duhem made his point about theories in physics, and he thought scientists' good sense would settle which hypothesis to give up. W. V. O. Quine later extended the idea far more widely."},"startHere":"Read Part II, Chapter 6 of The Aim and Structure of Physical Theory, 'Physical Theory and Experiment', where he argues that hypotheses cannot be tested one at a time. It is the most influential part of the book.","critics":"Scientific realists object that theories which successfully predict new, surprising effects are best explained by being roughly true, so theories do describe reality. Karl Popper also criticised instrumentalist views like Duhem's for treating theories as mere tools.","legacy":"His point about testing became central to twentieth-century philosophy of science through Quine. His histories founded the study of medieval science, though later historians have debated how much continuity he claimed.","quote":null},"whitehead":{"life":["Alfred North Whitehead was born in 1861 in Ramsgate, Kent, the son of a clergyman and schoolmaster. He grew up in late Victorian England, when physics seemed settled and mathematics was being rebuilt on careful logical foundations. In his lifetime, relativity and quantum theory overturned that settled picture, and Whitehead concluded that philosophy needed a new account of nature.","He studied mathematics at Trinity College, Cambridge, from 1880 and stayed on as a fellow. With his former student Bertrand Russell he wrote Principia Mathematica (1910–13), an attempt to derive mathematics from logic. He then taught in London, moving into the philosophy of science. In 1924, aged 63, he accepted a post in philosophy at Harvard. There he wrote Science and the Modern World (1925), Process and Reality (1929), based on his Gifford Lectures, and Adventures of Ideas (1933). He died in Cambridge, Massachusetts, in 1947.","Whitehead's career had three phases: mathematician, philosopher of science and metaphysician. He was a respected teacher and wrote on education too, warning against 'inert ideas', facts learned without being used. His later books coin many new terms, which makes them hard going, but they aim at a single picture of nature, mind and God. He and Russell later drifted apart, over philosophy and over the First World War, which Russell opposed."],"timeline":[[1861,"Born in Ramsgate, Kent"],[1880,"Goes up to Trinity College, Cambridge"],[1910,"First volume of Principia Mathematica, with Russell"],[1924,"Becomes professor of philosophy at Harvard"],[1925,"Publishes Science and the Modern World"],[1929,"Publishes Process and Reality"],[1933,"Publishes Adventures of Ideas"],[1947,"Dies in Cambridge, Massachusetts"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Reality is process","body":"Whitehead held that the basic units of the world are not lasting lumps of matter but brief events of becoming, which he called 'actual occasions'. A rock looks permanent, but it is really a long chain of such events, each inheriting from the last."},{"head":"Misplaced concreteness","body":"We often mistake abstractions for the real thing, such as treating 'matter at a point in space' as more real than the lived world. Whitehead called this the fallacy of misplaced concreteness. Scientific abstractions are useful, but they are simplified selections from something richer."},{"head":"Feeling runs all the way down","body":"Every actual occasion takes in, or 'prehends', its past and responds to it. Whitehead saw this as a simple form of experience, present in some degree throughout nature, so mind is not a strange add-on to a dead material world."},{"head":"A God who changes","body":"In Whitehead's scheme God offers each occasion its possibilities and is also affected by what the world does. God is not an unchanging ruler but a partner who preserves and is enriched by everything that happens."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the early chapters of Science and the Modern World, especially 'The Origins of Modern Science' and 'The Century of Genius', which trace how the scientific picture of matter arose. They are far easier than Process and Reality and introduce his main worries.","critics":"Many analytic philosophers judged his metaphysics too obscure to test and rejected his whole style of system-building. Defenders of traditional theism object that a God who changes and depends on the world cannot be perfect.","legacy":"Principia Mathematica shaped modern logic. His later metaphysics founded 'process philosophy' and, through Charles Hartshorne and others, process theology; it also influences some work in ecology and philosophy of mind.","quote":{"text":"The safest general characterization of the European philosophical tradition is that it consists of a series of footnotes to Plato.","src":"Process and Reality, Part II, Chapter 1, Section 1","tr":"original English"}},"hilbert":{"life":["David Hilbert was born in 1862 in Königsberg, Prussia, the city of Kant. He worked in the decades when mathematics was being made rigorous, and when paradoxes in set theory around 1900 made people worry that its foundations were unsafe. He became perhaps the most influential mathematician of his time. Göttingen, where he spent most of his career, drew mathematicians and physicists from across the world.","He studied at Königsberg and taught there before moving to the University of Göttingen in 1895, where he stayed for the rest of his career and made Göttingen a world centre of mathematics. His Foundations of Geometry (1899) rebuilt geometry on precise axioms. At the Paris congress of 1900 he presented a list of open problems, 23 in the published version, that guided much later research. In the 1920s he launched 'Hilbert's programme' to prove mathematics consistent. Gödel's results of 1931 showed it could not succeed in its original form. He watched the Nazi regime drive many colleagues out of Göttingen after 1933 and died there in 1943.","Hilbert worked across almost all of mathematics, from number theory to physics, and gathered many students around him. He was optimistic about reason: in a 1930 radio address he rejected the idea that some questions must remain unknowable forever, declaring that we must know and we will know. His students included Hermann Weyl, and the term 'Hilbert space', now basic to quantum physics, honours his work on infinite-dimensional spaces."],"timeline":[[1862,"Born in Königsberg, Prussia"],[1895,"Becomes professor at Göttingen"],[1899,"Publishes Foundations of Geometry"],[1900,"Presents his famous list of problems in Paris (10 in the lecture, 23 in the published version)"],[1926,"Publishes 'On the Infinite', setting out his programme"],[1930,"Retires and gives his Königsberg address"],[1934,"First volume of Foundations of Mathematics, with Paul Bernays"],[1943,"Dies in Göttingen"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The axiomatic method","body":"Hilbert showed how to build a field from a small set of precise starting rules (axioms) and deduce everything else. What the words 'point' or 'line' refer to does not matter, only the relations the axioms set up. This made it possible to study axioms themselves: are they consistent, and are they independent of each other?"},{"head":"Mathematics as formal systems","body":"To secure mathematics, Hilbert proposed writing its proofs as finite strings of symbols governed by exact rules. Then proofs become objects that can themselves be studied mathematically, a field he called proof theory."},{"head":"Prove consistency safely","body":"Hilbert wanted to show, using only simple finite reasoning that everyone accepts, that these systems can never prove a contradiction. That would justify using the infinite in mathematics, which he defended against critics such as Brouwer."},{"head":"No ignorabimus","body":"Against the claim that some questions will never be answered, Hilbert held that every clear mathematical problem can in principle be solved. This optimism drove his famous list of problems."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hilbert thought mathematics is just a meaningless game played with symbols.","truth":"Hilbert treated simple finite mathematics as fully meaningful. He used formal symbol systems as a tool to show that the more abstract, infinite parts of mathematics were safe to use."},"startHere":"Read 'On the Infinite' (1926), a lecture written for a general audience of mathematicians, which explains why he wanted to secure the infinite and how his programme would do it.","critics":"Kurt Gödel's incompleteness theorems (1931) showed that no consistent system strong enough for arithmetic can prove its own consistency, which defeated the programme in its original form. L. E. J. Brouwer rejected the whole approach, holding that only mentally constructed mathematics is meaningful.","legacy":"Proof theory and the modern axiomatic style of mathematics come from Hilbert. His question about whether there is a mechanical method for deciding mathematical statements led to Alan Turing's work and the theory of computation.","quote":null},"okakura":{"life":["Okakura Kakuzō (also known as Okakura Tenshin) was born in Yokohama in 1863, just before Japan's Meiji Restoration of 1868. The new government rushed to modernise on Western lines, and many traditional arts and temple treasures were neglected or sold abroad. Okakura spent his life arguing that Asian art and culture had their own value and must be preserved.","He learned English as a child and graduated from Tokyo University (later Tokyo Imperial University) in 1880. Working with the American scholar Ernest Fenollosa, he surveyed ancient temple art for the government. He helped found the Tokyo School of Fine Arts and became its director, but was forced out in 1898 and then founded the Japan Art Institute. He travelled to India in 1901–02, where he met Swami Vivekananda and Rabindranath Tagore. From 1904 he worked at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, on its Chinese and Japanese collections. He died in Japan in 1913.","Okakura wrote his best-known books in English for Western readers, with an elegant and sometimes ironic style. He was an organiser of institutions as much as a writer, shaping how Japanese art was taught, collected and displayed in both Japan and the United States. In The Book of Tea he complained that Westerners had called Japan barbarous while it practised the arts of peace, and civilised only once it won battles in Manchuria."],"timeline":[[1863,"Born in Yokohama"],[1880,"Graduates from Tokyo University (later Tokyo Imperial University)"],[1890,"Becomes director of the Tokyo School of Fine Arts"],[1898,"Leaves the school and founds the Japan Art Institute"],[1903,"Publishes The Ideals of the East in London"],[1904,"Begins working at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston"],[1906,"Publishes The Book of Tea in New York"],[1913,"Dies in Japan"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The worship of the imperfect","body":"The Book of Tea presents the tea ceremony as a philosophy of life. Its beauty lies in simple, worn or uneven things, like a cracked bowl or a plain room. Leaving something unfinished invites the imagination to complete it."},{"head":"Teaism draws on Taoism and Zen","body":"Okakura links tea culture to Taoist ideas of emptiness and to Zen Buddhism. A room or a cup is useful because of the empty space within it. Attention to small everyday acts becomes a way of practising this outlook."},{"head":"Asia is one","body":"The Ideals of the East opens with the claim that Asia is one. Okakura argued that the cultures of India, China and Japan share a spiritual unity and a love of the ultimate, which he contrasted with the West's focus on means and machinery."},{"head":"Japan as Asia's museum","body":"He suggested that Japan, never conquered, had preserved layers of Asian art and thought that had been lost elsewhere. Its temples hold traces of Indian and Chinese traditions side by side."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The Book of Tea is a practical guide to making and serving tea.","truth":"It is a short essay on culture and aesthetics written for Western readers. It says little about technique and uses tea as a window onto Asian ideas of beauty and simplicity."},"startHere":"Read The Book of Tea, especially the first chapter, 'The Cup of Humanity', and the chapter on Taoism and Zen. The whole book is short and written in clear English.","critics":"Later scholars argue that Okakura presented an idealised, simplified 'East' set against the West, a mirror image of Western stereotypes. Critics also note that the slogan 'Asia is one' was later used to support Japanese imperial expansion, though he wrote it as a claim about culture.","legacy":"The Book of Tea remains one of the most widely read introductions to Japanese aesthetics in the West. In Japan he is remembered as a key figure in preserving traditional art and shaping modern Japanese painting.","quote":{"text":"Teaism is a cult founded on the adoration of the beautiful among the sordid facts of everyday existence.","src":"The Book of Tea, Chapter 1, 'The Cup of Humanity'","tr":"original English"}},"vivekananda":{"life":["Swami Vivekananda was born Narendranath Datta in Calcutta (now Kolkata) in 1863, into a prosperous professional family. Bengal was then the centre of British India and of an intellectual revival: reformers debated how Hindu traditions should meet Western science, Christianity and colonial rule. As a student he read Western philosophy and joined the reformist Brahmo Samaj, but he was searching for someone who had experienced God directly.","In 1881 he met the mystic Sri Ramakrishna, who became his teacher. After Ramakrishna's death in 1886, he and other disciples became monks, and he spent several years travelling across India on foot. In 1893 he spoke at the World's Parliament of Religions in Chicago, where his speeches made him famous. He lectured in the United States and Britain, founding the Vedanta Society of New York. Returning to India in 1897, he founded the Ramakrishna Mission, which combined monastic life with social service. He died at Belur, near Calcutta, in 1902, aged 39.","Vivekananda was a powerful public speaker, and much of his work survives as lectures taken down by listeners, later gathered into books such as Raja Yoga and Jnana Yoga. He combined spiritual teaching with practical concerns, urging Indians to build strength, education and self-respect and to serve the poor. His collected lectures, letters and conversations fill several volumes, published as The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda."],"timeline":[[1863,"Born Narendranath Datta in Calcutta"],[1881,"First meets Sri Ramakrishna"],[1886,"Ramakrishna dies; he and other disciples take up monastic life"],[1893,"Speaks at the World's Parliament of Religions in Chicago"],[1896,"Publishes Raja Yoga"],[1897,"Returns to India and founds the Ramakrishna Mission"],[1902,"Dies at Belur Math, near Calcutta"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Each soul is divine","body":"Following Advaita Vedanta, a school of Hindu philosophy, Vivekananda taught that the true self (Atman) is one with Brahman, the ultimate reality. Everyone is divine underneath, and the aim of life is to realise this. Weakness and fear come from forgetting what we really are."},{"head":"Many paths, one goal","body":"Religions differ in their practices and beliefs, but he held that sincere followers of each are moving towards the same truth. He compared them to rivers flowing to one sea. Tolerance was not enough: he wanted religions to accept one another as true paths."},{"head":"Four yogas","body":"He presented four main spiritual paths suited to different temperaments: knowledge (jnana), devotion (bhakti), selfless work (karma) and meditation (raja yoga). A thoughtful person might start with study, an emotional one with devotion. All lead to the same realisation."},{"head":"Service as worship","body":"Because God is present in every person, serving people in need is a form of worship. This 'practical Vedanta' shaped the Ramakrishna Mission, which runs schools, hospitals and disaster relief."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Vivekananda simply passed on traditional Hinduism unchanged to the West.","truth":"He gave Hindu ideas a new, modern form, stressing Advaita Vedanta, social service and agreement between religions. Scholars often call this 'neo-Vedanta' because it reshaped older traditions."},"startHere":"Read his short speeches at the 1893 Parliament of Religions in Chicago, especially the opening address and the 'Paper on Hinduism'. They are brief and show the message that made him famous.","critics":"The German Indologist Paul Hacker described his teaching as 'neo-Hinduism', arguing that it mixed Western ideas into Hindu tradition. Other critics say his claim that all religions lead to one truth is itself a particular theology that ranks Advaita Vedanta above other views, including other Hindu schools.","legacy":"Vivekananda did much to introduce yoga and Vedanta to the West and inspired Indian nationalists. His birthday is celebrated in India as National Youth Day, and the Ramakrishna Mission he founded remains active worldwide.","quote":{"text":"Sectarianism, bigotry, and its horrible descendant, fanaticism, have long possessed this beautiful earth.","src":"Response to Welcome, World's Parliament of Religions, Chicago, 11 September 1893","tr":"original English"}},"mctaggart":{"life":["John McTaggart Ellis McTaggart was born in London in 1866. In late Victorian Britain the leading philosophers were idealists, who held, following Hegel, that reality is ultimately spiritual or mental. Cambridge, where McTaggart spent his career, was soon to become the home of the analytic reaction against idealism led by his younger colleagues G. E. Moore and Bertrand Russell.","He studied at Trinity College, Cambridge, and became a fellow in 1891 and a lecturer in 1897, a post he held until 1923. He wrote three books on Hegel, then Some Dogmas of Religion (1906), which argued against belief in God while defending personal immortality. His article 'The Unreality of Time' (1908) became his most famous work. He set out his own system in The Nature of Existence, whose first volume appeared in 1921; the second was edited by C. D. Broad and published after his death in London in 1925.","McTaggart was known for careful, step-by-step deductive arguments from a few premises to surprising conclusions. He was an unusual combination: an atheist who believed that souls are eternal and that love between persons is the highest reality. His students and colleagues, including Broad, treated his arguments with great seriousness even when they rejected his conclusions."],"timeline":[[1866,"Born in London"],[1891,"Elected a fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge"],[1896,"Publishes Studies in the Hegelian Dialectic"],[1906,"Publishes Some Dogmas of Religion"],[1908,"Publishes 'The Unreality of Time' in the journal Mind"],[1921,"First volume of The Nature of Existence"],[1925,"Dies in London"],[1927,"Second volume of The Nature of Existence published, edited by C. D. Broad"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Two ways of ordering time","body":"McTaggart said events can be ordered as past, present and future (the A-series), or as earlier and later than each other (the B-series). 'The battle was yesterday' uses the A-series; 'the battle was before the treaty' uses the B-series. He argued that real change needs the A-series."},{"head":"Time is unreal","body":"Every event is past, present and future, but these properties exclude each other. Saying an event is these at different times just brings in time again, starting an endless regress. Since the A-series is contradictory and time needs it, McTaggart concluded time does not exist."},{"head":"Immortality without God","body":"In Some Dogmas of Religion he argued that belief in a personal, all-powerful God is unjustified. Yet he held that souls may exist forever, and his later system tries to prove that reality consists of eternal selves."},{"head":"Reality as love","body":"In The Nature of Existence, what truly exists is a community of spirits related to one another by love. What we experience as a changing material world is a distorted appearance of that timeless reality."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the article 'The Unreality of Time' (1908). It is short, clearly argued and still sets the terms of modern debates about time.","critics":"C. D. Broad argued that the contradiction disappears once we see that no event has all three A-properties at once. Many later philosophers accept McTaggart's distinction but deny his conclusion, holding either that the B-series alone is enough for real time or that the present is privileged in a consistent way.","legacy":"His A-series and B-series terminology is still standard in the philosophy of time, where 'A-theorists' and 'B-theorists' debate whether the passage of time is real. His paper remains one of the most discussed in twentieth-century metaphysics.","quote":null},"scheler":{"life":["Max Scheler was born in Munich in 1874, to a Lutheran father and a Jewish mother. He came of age in the Germany of the early twentieth century, when phenomenology, the careful description of how things appear in experience, was taking shape around Edmund Husserl. It was a time of heated debate about values, religion and the meaning of modern life, made sharper by the First World War.","He studied in Munich, Berlin and Jena, where he took his doctorate in 1897. Around 1900 he converted to Catholicism. He met Husserl in 1901 and became one of the leading phenomenologists. After losing his teaching post in Munich in 1910, he worked as a private scholar and wrote his major books, including Ressentiment (1912) and Formalism in Ethics and Non-Formal Ethics of Values (1913–16). From 1919 he taught at Cologne. In the 1920s he moved away from the Church and turned to philosophical anthropology. He died in 1928, shortly after accepting a chair at Frankfurt.","Scheler was a prolific and energetic writer who ranged across ethics, religion, psychology, sociology and politics. Contemporaries described him as a brilliant lecturer. He wrote quickly and often revised his positions, so his work contains several distinct phases. His interests changed over his life, from Catholic-inspired ethics in the 1910s to a later metaphysics in which God develops together with the world. He was also a founder of the sociology of knowledge, which studies how social conditions shape what people think."],"timeline":[[1874,"Born in Munich"],[1897,"Completes his doctorate at Jena"],[1901,"Meets Edmund Husserl"],[1912,"Publishes Ressentiment"],[1913,"First part of Formalism in Ethics, and The Nature of Sympathy, published"],[1919,"Becomes professor at Cologne"],[1928,"Publishes The Human Place in the Cosmos; dies in Frankfurt"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Values are real","body":"Scheler held that values such as the noble, the beautiful and the holy are not invented by us. We discover them, much as we discover colours. A selfless act has a value we can grasp directly, whatever anyone happens to think about it."},{"head":"We feel values","body":"Values are grasped not by reasoning but by a kind of feeling that is still a form of knowledge. When you are moved by an act of courage, you are perceiving something real about it. Feelings can be mistaken, but they can also be accurate."},{"head":"A ranking of values","body":"Values form a fixed order. From lowest to highest: the pleasant, vital values like health, spiritual values like truth and beauty, and the holy. Moral goodness consists in preferring higher values to lower ones."},{"head":"Ressentiment","body":"Ressentiment is a lasting, suppressed resentment that leads people to devalue what they cannot have, like the fox calling the grapes sour. Nietzsche thought Christian morality came from such resentment. Scheler agreed that ressentiment distorts values but argued that true Christian love comes from strength, not envy."},{"head":"Kinds of sympathy","body":"In The Nature of Sympathy he distinguishes sharing a feeling with someone, feeling for them, and simply catching an emotion as a crowd does. Understanding others, he argued, is often direct: we see grief in a face."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Ressentiment, a short book that engages Nietzsche directly and shows Scheler's skill at describing emotional life. It is far easier than the large Formalism book.","critics":"Critics ask how we can check intuitions of value when people sincerely rank values differently: if one person feels that honour outranks life and another does not, Scheler offers little way to settle the dispute. Even sympathetic successors, such as Nicolai Hartmann, revised his ranking.","legacy":"Scheler shaped phenomenological ethics and founded modern philosophical anthropology. Karol Wojtyła, later Pope John Paul II, wrote a study of his ethics, and his work on empathy still informs philosophy and psychology.","quote":null},"ross":{"life":["William David Ross was born in 1877 in Thurso, in the far north of Scotland, and spent part of his early childhood in India, where his father worked in education. He belonged to a generation of Oxford philosophers who reacted against both grand idealist systems and utilitarianism. In ethics, many of them held that we know basic moral truths directly, without deriving them from a single theory.","He studied at Edinburgh University and Balliol College, Oxford, and became a fellow of Oriel College in 1902. He became one of the leading Aristotle scholars of his century, editing the Oxford translations of Aristotle and publishing commentaries on the Greek texts. His own ethics appeared in The Right and the Good (1930) and Foundations of Ethics (1939). He served as Provost of Oriel from 1929 to 1947 and as Vice-Chancellor of Oxford during the Second World War. He was knighted in 1938 and died in Oxford in 1971.","Ross was a careful, modest writer who argued from ordinary moral convictions rather than from bold first principles. He also did much public and administrative work, including during the First World War in the Ministry of Munitions. His work on Aristotle and his own ethics were linked: like Aristotle, he took the considered opinions of thoughtful people as the starting point of moral theory."],"timeline":[[1877,"Born in Thurso, Scotland"],[1902,"Becomes a fellow of Oriel College, Oxford"],[1923,"Publishes his book Aristotle"],[1929,"Becomes Provost of Oriel College"],[1930,"Publishes The Right and the Good"],[1938,"Knighted"],[1939,"Publishes Foundations of Ethics"],[1971,"Dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Several basic duties","body":"Ross listed duties of fidelity (keeping promises), reparation (making up for wrongs), gratitude, justice, beneficence (doing good), self-improvement and non-maleficence (not harming). None can be reduced to another. Morality has more than one source."},{"head":"Prima facie duties","body":"Each of these is a duty 'at first appearance': it really binds us, but it can be outweighed. If you promised to meet a friend but come across a car accident, the duty to help outweighs the promise. The promise still matters, which is why you owe your friend an apology."},{"head":"Judgement, not formulas","body":"There is no rule for ranking the duties in every case. We must weigh them in the situation using practical judgement. Ross thought this matches how thoughtful people actually reason about moral dilemmas."},{"head":"Against utilitarianism","body":"If a promise produced slightly less total good than breaking it, utilitarianism says break it. Ross argued this ignores the special obligation created by a promise. What we owe depends on our past acts and relationships, not only on future results."},{"head":"Self-evident moral truths","body":"Ross held that basic duties are known in the way mathematical axioms are: anyone who reflects maturely sees they are true. We do not need a proof that we ought to keep promises."}],"misconception":{"myth":"A prima facie duty is only an apparent duty, not a real one.","truth":"For Ross it is a real moral reason that always counts, even when it is outweighed. What is merely provisional is our verdict about what to do overall, not the duty itself."},"startHere":"Read Chapter 2 of The Right and the Good, 'What Makes Right Acts Right?'. It sets out the list of prima facie duties and his case against utilitarianism in a few clear pages.","critics":"Critics say that without a method for weighing duties, his theory gives no guidance in hard cases and can make decisions look arbitrary. J. L. Mackie, in Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), attacked the idea of moral intuition as requiring a strange special faculty.","legacy":"Ross's pluralism is a standard option in ethics courses and was revived by later philosophers such as Robert Audi. Medical ethics' principles approach, which weighs several duties such as avoiding harm and respecting autonomy, follows a similar pattern. His Aristotle editions are still used.","quote":null},"bell":{"life":["Clive Bell was born in 1881 in Berkshire, England, into a wealthy family whose money came from coal mining. He came of age as modern art was breaking with tradition. Painters such as Cézanne, Matisse and Picasso no longer tried to copy what the eye sees, and the British public found their work shocking.","At Trinity College, Cambridge, from 1899 he befriended Thoby Stephen, Lytton Strachey and Leonard Woolf. In 1907 he married Thoby's sister Vanessa, a painter, and became part of the Bloomsbury Group of writers and artists, which included Virginia Woolf and the critic Roger Fry. Bell helped with the Second Post-Impressionist Exhibition in London in 1912. His book Art (1914) set out the theory of 'significant form'. A pacifist, he opposed the First World War. He went on writing criticism, including Since Cézanne (1922) and Civilization (1928), and died in London in 1964.","Bell was a critic and essayist rather than an academic philosopher. He wrote with confidence and enjoyed bold, provocative claims, which helped make Art widely read and widely argued about. Many of his ideas grew out of conversations with Roger Fry, whose writings on form in painting developed alongside his. In Civilization (1928) he argued that a civilised society depends on a leisured minority with refined tastes, a view many readers found elitist."],"timeline":[[1881,"Born in Berkshire, England"],[1899,"Goes up to Trinity College, Cambridge"],[1907,"Marries the painter Vanessa Stephen"],[1912,"Helps with the Second Post-Impressionist Exhibition in London"],[1914,"Publishes Art"],[1922,"Publishes Since Cézanne"],[1964,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Significant form","body":"Bell asked what all real works of visual art have in common, from a Persian bowl to a Cézanne. His answer was significant form: lines, shapes and colours combined in a way that moves us. This, he said, is what makes something art."},{"head":"The aesthetic emotion","body":"Significant form produces a special feeling, the aesthetic emotion, which differs from ordinary emotions such as sadness or excitement. Some people are sensitive to it and some are not, which Bell thought explained disputes about art."},{"head":"Subject matter does not matter","body":"What a picture shows is irrelevant to its value as art. A painting of a battle moves us as art because of its forms, not because of the story. To appreciate art, he said, we need bring nothing from life except a sense of form and colour."},{"head":"A defence of modern art","body":"Bell's theory explained why abstract and post-impressionist painting could be great art, even though it did not copy nature well. It also let him admire art from very different cultures on the same terms."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Part I, Chapter 1 of Art, 'The Aesthetic Hypothesis', which sets out his theory of significant form in a few lively pages.","critics":"Many critics have pointed out that the theory seems circular: significant form is defined as what causes the aesthetic emotion, and the aesthetic emotion as what significant form causes. Others object that subject matter and meaning plainly matter in much great art, such as religious painting.","legacy":"Bell's book helped British audiences accept modern painting and became a classic statement of formalism, the view that art's value lies in its formal qualities. It is still a standard starting point in courses on the definition of art.","quote":null},"brouwer":{"life":["Luitzen Egbertus Jan Brouwer was born in 1881 in Overschie, near Rotterdam, in the Netherlands. He worked at a time of crisis in the foundations of mathematics: around 1900, paradoxes in set theory showed that reasoning freely about infinite collections could lead to contradiction. Mathematicians split over how to respond, and Brouwer led the most radical answer.","He studied at the University of Amsterdam, where his doctoral thesis of 1907, On the Foundations of Mathematics, began his attack on mainstream views. In 1908 he argued that the law of excluded middle, the principle that every statement is either true or false, is not reliable for infinite collections. He also proved major theorems in topology, the study of shape, including his fixed-point theorem. He became professor at Amsterdam in 1912. In the 1920s his conflict with Hilbert grew bitter, and in 1928 Hilbert had him removed from the editorial board of a leading journal. He died in 1966 after being struck by a car.","Brouwer was a strong-willed and combative figure. His early work had a mystical side: in Life, Art and Mysticism (1905) he expressed a deep pessimism about society and language. His philosophy of mathematics grew from the view that true mathematics happens in the mind, prior to any language. In 1934 he founded the journal Compositio Mathematica."],"timeline":[[1881,"Born in Overschie, near Rotterdam"],[1907,"Completes his doctoral thesis, On the Foundations of Mathematics"],[1908,"Argues that the law of excluded middle is unreliable"],[1912,"Becomes professor at the University of Amsterdam"],[1928,"Lectures in Vienna; removed from the Mathematische Annalen board"],[1966,"Dies in Blaricum, the Netherlands"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Mathematics is mental construction","body":"For Brouwer, mathematical objects exist only once a mind has built them. A number or a proof is a mental act, not something waiting in a timeless realm. Language and logic merely record these constructions, often imperfectly."},{"head":"Rooted in the experience of time","body":"Mathematics begins from our basic awareness of time passing: one moment splitting into two. From this 'two-ity' we can build the natural numbers and then much more. This is what Brouwer meant by intuition."},{"head":"Doubting the excluded middle","body":"Normally we assume every statement is true or false. Brouwer argued this is unjustified for infinite collections: to assert that a number with some property exists, you must be able to construct it. Showing that its non-existence leads to a contradiction is not enough."},{"head":"Choice sequences","body":"Brouwer introduced sequences of numbers generated step by step, with no complete list given in advance. He used them to rebuild a theory of the continuum, the real number line, on constructive terms."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Intuitionism means doing mathematics by gut feeling or hunches.","truth":"'Intuition' here means our basic awareness of time and mental construction. Brouwer's standards were stricter than ordinary mathematics, not looser."},"startHere":"Read 'Intuitionism and Formalism' (1912), his inaugural lecture at Amsterdam. It is short and sets out the contrast between his view and Hilbert's.","critics":"Hilbert argued that banning the law of excluded middle would cripple mathematics, comparing it to taking the telescope from the astronomer. Most mathematicians agreed that Brouwer's restrictions gave up too many useful results.","legacy":"Brouwer's ideas led to intuitionistic logic, formalised by his student Arend Heyting, and to constructive mathematics. These now matter in computer science, where a constructive proof can be turned into a program.","quote":null},"schlick":{"life":["Moritz Schlick was born in Berlin in 1882. He grew up as physics was transformed by relativity and quantum theory, which made many thinkers ask what scientific knowledge really is. In 1920s Vienna, a group of scientists and philosophers set out to make philosophy as clear and rigorous as science. Vienna, where he did his most important work, was then a centre of new ideas in science, economics, psychoanalysis and the arts.","He studied physics and earned his doctorate under Max Planck in 1904. His book Space and Time in Contemporary Physics (1917) was one of the first philosophical accounts of Einstein's relativity, and was admired by Einstein. He published General Theory of Knowledge in 1918. In 1922 he took a chair in Vienna, where from 1924 he led the discussion group later called the Vienna Circle. He met Ludwig Wittgenstein in 1927 and was strongly influenced by him. His Problems of Ethics appeared in 1930. In 1936 he was shot dead on the steps of the University of Vienna by a former student.","Schlick was known as a calm, courteous leader who held together a group of strong personalities. He insisted on clear language and took ethics more seriously than some of his colleagues, treating it as a field that science could help explain. He invited Rudolf Carnap to Vienna in 1926 and corresponded with Einstein for many years."],"timeline":[[1882,"Born in Berlin"],[1904,"Completes his doctorate in physics under Max Planck"],[1917,"Publishes Space and Time in Contemporary Physics"],[1918,"Publishes General Theory of Knowledge"],[1922,"Becomes professor in Vienna"],[1924,"Begins the meetings that become the Vienna Circle"],[1930,"Publishes Problems of Ethics"],[1936,"Murdered in Vienna by a former student"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Meaning is verification","body":"Schlick held that the meaning of a statement is the way it could be checked by experience. 'The kettle is boiling' means something because we know what would show it true or false. Statements that nothing could ever check are not false but meaningless."},{"head":"Philosophy as clarification","body":"Philosophy, he said, is not a science with its own facts. It is the activity of making clear what our statements mean. Once meaning is clear, the sciences can find out whether the statements are true."},{"head":"Freedom versus compulsion","body":"Free will does not mean that our actions are uncaused. It means we act from our own desires rather than under compulsion, such as being threatened or locked up. So freedom and causal determinism are compatible."},{"head":"Blame looks forward","body":"We hold people responsible to influence their future behaviour. Praise and blame make sense where they can change what someone does, so a person is responsible when their actions can be shaped this way."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Chapter 7 of Problems of Ethics, 'When Is a Man Responsible?'. It is short, clear and still widely reprinted in collections on free will.","critics":"C. A. Campbell, in 'Is \"Freewill\" a Pseudo-Problem?' (1951), argued that Schlick's account changes the subject: ordinary people mean by freedom that they could really have done otherwise, which determinism rules out. The verification principle was also criticised for failing its own test, since it cannot itself be verified by experience.","legacy":"Schlick's leadership of the Vienna Circle helped launch logical empiricism, which shaped twentieth-century philosophy of science. His compatibilist account of free will remains a standard reference point.","quote":null},"jaspers":{"life":["Karl Jaspers was born in Oldenburg, northern Germany, in 1883. He lived through the German Empire, the First World War, the Weimar Republic, Nazi rule and Germany's rebuilding after 1945. These upheavals shaped his questions about freedom, guilt, communication and the limits of human knowledge. He came to philosophy through medicine, asking how a doctor can understand the inner lives of patients.","He first studied law, then medicine, and worked at the psychiatric clinic in Heidelberg. His General Psychopathology (1913) was a major work in psychiatry. He then turned to philosophy and became a professor at Heidelberg in the early 1920s. His three-volume Philosophy appeared in 1932. Because his wife was Jewish, the Nazis removed him from his post in 1937 and later banned him from publishing. After the war he wrote The Question of German Guilt (1946). In 1948 he moved to Basel, Switzerland, and wrote widely on history, politics and the atomic bomb. He died in Basel in 1969.","Jaspers suffered from a chronic lung and heart illness from his youth, which forced him to live and work under a strict routine. He placed great weight on open, honest dialogue, and his former student Hannah Arendt remained a lifelong friend and correspondent. He remained in Germany throughout the Nazi period, and he and his wife lived under threat of deportation until the war ended."],"timeline":[[1883,"Born in Oldenburg, Germany"],[1913,"Publishes General Psychopathology"],[1919,"Publishes Psychology of Worldviews"],[1932,"Publishes his three-volume Philosophy"],[1937,"Removed from his professorship by the Nazi regime"],[1946,"Publishes The Question of German Guilt"],[1948,"Moves to the University of Basel"],[1969,"Dies in Basel"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Limit situations","body":"Some situations cannot be escaped or solved: death, suffering, struggle, guilt. Jaspers called them limit situations. Facing them honestly, instead of hiding from them in routine, is how we become fully ourselves."},{"head":"Existenz","body":"Science can describe us as bodies, minds and members of society. But each person also has a free, inner self, which Jaspers called Existenz, that no science can capture. We discover it only by choosing and acting."},{"head":"Transcendence and ciphers","body":"There is something beyond the world and ourselves, which Jaspers called Transcendence. It can never be known as an object. We glimpse it only through 'ciphers': symbols, myths, works of art and experiences that point beyond themselves."},{"head":"The Axial Age","body":"In The Origin and Goal of History (1949) he observed that between roughly 800 and 200 BCE, in China, India, Persia, Israel and Greece, great thinkers independently began asking questions about humanity's place in the world. He saw this as the spiritual foundation of later civilisations."},{"head":"Kinds of guilt","body":"After 1945 he distinguished criminal, political, moral and metaphysical guilt. Only some Germans were criminally guilty, he argued, but all citizens shared political responsibility for the regime they lived under."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Way to Wisdom, a set of twelve short radio talks first broadcast in 1949, which introduce his main ideas, such as limit situations and Transcendence, in plain language.","critics":"Critics, especially from analytic philosophy, argue that ideas such as the Encompassing and ciphers of Transcendence are too vague to be assessed. Historians have also questioned whether the Axial Age was a real, unified turning point or a pattern imposed after the fact.","legacy":"Jaspers shaped existential philosophy and modern psychiatry's method of understanding patients' experience. His idea of the Axial Age remains widely debated, and his writing on German guilt influenced discussions of collective responsibility.","quote":null},"tillich":{"life":["Paul Tillich was born in 1886 in Starzeddel, then in eastern Germany and now in Poland, the son of a Lutheran pastor. He grew up in a confident Protestant culture that was shattered by the First World War. The war and the crises that followed convinced him that theology had to speak to people's real anxieties and to culture, politics and art.","He studied in Berlin, Tübingen and Halle and was ordained a Lutheran minister in 1912. He served as an army chaplain in the First World War, an experience that changed him deeply. In the 1920s he taught at several German universities and became a leading 'religious socialist'. He criticised the Nazis, was dismissed in 1933 and emigrated to the United States. He taught at Union Theological Seminary in New York, then Harvard and Chicago. His Systematic Theology appeared in three volumes from 1951 to 1963, alongside popular books such as The Courage to Be (1952). He died in Chicago in 1965.","Tillich worked on the boundary between theology and philosophy, and between religion and culture. He drew on existentialism, depth psychology and modern art. In America his books and sermons reached a wide public beyond the churches. He described himself as living 'on the boundary', the title of a short autobiographical essay: between Germany and America, faith and doubt, church and secular culture."],"timeline":[[1886,"Born in Starzeddel, Germany"],[1912,"Ordained as a Lutheran minister"],[1929,"Becomes professor at Frankfurt"],[1933,"Dismissed by the Nazi government; emigrates to New York"],[1951,"First volume of Systematic Theology"],[1952,"Publishes The Courage to Be"],[1957,"Publishes Dynamics of Faith"],[1965,"Dies in Chicago"]],"ideas":[{"head":"God as being-itself","body":"Tillich held that God is not one being among others, not even the highest. God is being-itself, the power of being in everything that exists. To picture God as a superperson in the sky makes God just another object in the world."},{"head":"Faith as ultimate concern","body":"Faith is being grasped by what concerns us ultimately, what we treat as giving life its final meaning. Everyone has some ultimate concern. If it is money, success or a nation, it is an idol, because it is finite and will let us down."},{"head":"Religious language is symbolic","body":"Since God is not an object, everything we say about God is symbolic, not literal. Symbols such as 'Father' point beyond themselves and share in what they point to. They can grow, and they can die when they stop speaking to people."},{"head":"The courage to be","body":"Humans face anxiety about death, meaninglessness and guilt. Courage is affirming oneself in spite of these threats. Tillich argued that such courage draws on being-itself, even for people who feel they have lost belief in God."},{"head":"The method of correlation","body":"Theology should start from the questions raised by human existence, such as fear of death or loss of meaning, and show how Christian symbols answer them. Question and answer shape each other."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Because Tillich said that God does not exist, he was really an atheist.","truth":"He meant that God does not exist as a thing alongside other things; God is the ground of all existence. He remained a Christian theologian and preacher throughout his life."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Dynamics of Faith, 'What Faith Is'. It is short, clear and explains his idea of ultimate concern.","critics":"The philosopher Paul Edwards argued in 'Professor Tillich's Confusions' (Mind, 1965) that much of his talk about being-itself has no clear meaning. Many Christian thinkers also objected that an impersonal 'ground of being' cannot be the personal God of the Bible who hears prayers.","legacy":"Tillich was among the most widely read theologians of the twentieth century, especially in America. His idea of faith as ultimate concern has been used in law and religious studies to think about what counts as a religion.","quote":null},"broad":{"life":["Charlie Dunbar Broad was born in 1887 in Harlesden, then on the edge of London. He belonged to the Cambridge generation shaped by Bertrand Russell, G. E. Moore and J. M. E. McTaggart. Early-twentieth-century physics, psychology and even research into claims of telepathy raised new questions about matter, mind and time, and Broad tried to treat them all with equal care.","He went to Trinity College, Cambridge, first studying natural sciences and then philosophy, and became a fellow in 1911. He taught at St Andrews and Dundee, then became professor at Bristol in 1920. He returned to Trinity in 1923 and held the Knightbridge Professorship of Moral Philosophy from 1933 to 1953. His main books include Scientific Thought (1923), The Mind and Its Place in Nature (1925), Five Types of Ethical Theory (1930) and a long study of McTaggart's philosophy. He died in Cambridge in 1971.","Broad was known for extremely clear, thorough writing: he liked to list every possible answer to a question and test each one in turn. He also took psychical research seriously and twice served as president of the Society for Psychical Research, while remaining cautious about its claims. He was openly sceptical of fashionable movements, including the later philosophy of Wittgenstein, and preferred detailed analysis to sweeping claims."],"timeline":[[1887,"Born in Harlesden, London"],[1911,"Elected a fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge"],[1920,"Becomes professor of philosophy at Bristol"],[1923,"Publishes Scientific Thought; returns to Trinity"],[1925,"Publishes The Mind and Its Place in Nature"],[1930,"Publishes Five Types of Ethical Theory"],[1933,"Becomes Knightbridge Professor of Moral Philosophy at Cambridge"],[1971,"Dies in Cambridge"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The growing block of time","body":"In Scientific Thought Broad argued that the past and present are real, while the future is nothing at all yet. Reality grows as new events come into being at the present edge. Yesterday's breakfast still exists in the block of history; tomorrow's does not."},{"head":"Emergence","body":"Broad defended 'emergentism': some properties of complex wholes, such as chemical behaviour or consciousness, cannot be predicted even from perfect knowledge of their parts. Each level of nature may obey laws of its own."},{"head":"Mapping the mind-body options","body":"In The Mind and Its Place in Nature he set out a careful list of possible theories of mind and body and weighed each. His method showed how many views are logically open, rather than assuming the answer in advance."},{"head":"Deontological and teleological ethics","body":"In Five Types of Ethical Theory Broad distinguished theories that judge acts by their results (teleological) from those that judge some acts right or wrong in themselves (deontological). These terms are still used in every ethics course."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Five Types of Ethical Theory, then any one of its studies of Spinoza, Butler, Hume, Kant or Sidgwick. They show his clear, even-handed method.","critics":"Against the growing block view, Craig Bourne and David Braddon-Mitchell argued that if past times are as real as the present, people in the past would also believe they are at the present edge, so we cannot know that we are. Jaegwon Kim later argued that emergent mental properties would have no causal work left to do.","legacy":"Broad's growing block theory is still a leading position in the philosophy of time, and his version of emergentism is a reference point for current debates on consciousness. His terms 'deontological' and 'teleological' became standard vocabulary in ethics.","quote":null},"collingwood":{"life":["Robin George Collingwood was born in 1889 at Cartmel Fell in the Lake District, England. His father, W. G. Collingwood, was an artist and the secretary of John Ruskin. Collingwood grew up among painting, music and archaeology. At Oxford, where he spent his career, the 'realist' philosophers were then attacking idealism, and Collingwood came to see much of their method as cut off from history.","He studied at University College, Oxford, and became a fellow of Pembroke College in 1912. Alongside philosophy, he became a leading archaeologist and historian of Roman Britain. His philosophical books include Speculum Mentis (1924), An Essay on Philosophical Method (1933), The Principles of Art (1938), An Autobiography (1939) and An Essay on Metaphysics (1940). In 1935 he became Waynflete Professor of Metaphysical Philosophy. Strokes from 1938 onward weakened his health, and he died at Coniston in 1943. The Idea of History was published after his death, in 1946.","Collingwood combined philosophy with practical fieldwork, excavating Roman sites and studying inscriptions. He wrote with energy and often with sharp criticism of his colleagues, and many of his ideas came from reflecting on his own practice as a historian. He was also a capable musician and painter, and his experience of making art informed The Principles of Art. In An Autobiography he attacked Oxford realism fiercely, which left him isolated among his colleagues."],"timeline":[[1889,"Born at Cartmel Fell, Lancashire"],[1912,"Becomes a fellow of Pembroke College, Oxford"],[1924,"Publishes Speculum Mentis"],[1935,"Becomes Waynflete Professor of Metaphysical Philosophy at Oxford"],[1938,"Publishes The Principles of Art"],[1939,"Publishes An Autobiography"],[1943,"Dies at Coniston"],[1946,"The Idea of History published"]],"ideas":[{"head":"History as re-enactment","body":"To understand a past action, the historian must rethink the thoughts behind it. To explain why Caesar crossed the Rubicon, you reconstruct his reasoning about his situation. History studies events from the inside, not just as outward happenings."},{"head":"Art proper versus craft","body":"Collingwood separated real art from craft. A craft uses skill to produce a planned result, as an advertisement aims to make you buy. Art proper is the artist working out, and so expressing, an emotion that was unclear before the work was made."},{"head":"Amusement and magic","body":"Much of what passes for art is really 'amusement art', which stirs emotions for enjoyment, or 'magic art', which stirs emotions for practical use, as anthems do. These have their place, but neither is art proper."},{"head":"Question and answer","body":"Every statement is an answer to a question. To understand what someone meant, you must work out what question they were answering. This idea influenced how historians of ideas read old texts."},{"head":"Absolute presuppositions","body":"Every way of thinking rests on basic assumptions that are not themselves proved, such as the assumption that every event has a cause. Metaphysics, for Collingwood, is the historical study of what these assumptions have been in different periods."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read An Autobiography (1939). It is short, readable and explains how his ideas about history and philosophy grew from his own work.","critics":"Critics argue that re-enactment fits individual decisions but not large social and economic changes that no one intended, which historians also need to explain. His theory of art has been criticised for ruling out much that people value as art, including skilled entertainment.","legacy":"Collingwood is a central figure in the philosophy of history, and his question-and-answer method influenced the historian of ideas Quentin Skinner. His expression theory of art is still a standard position in aesthetics.","quote":{"text":"All history is the history of thought.","src":"The Idea of History, Epilegomena","tr":"original English"}},"horkheimer":{"life":["Max Horkheimer was born in 1895 in Stuttgart to a wealthy Jewish family that owned textile factories. He grew up as Germany industrialised and lived through the First World War, the failed revolutions after it and the rise of Nazism. These events led him to ask why the working class had not overthrown capitalism and why modern societies could slide into barbarism.","Expected to take over the family business, he instead studied philosophy and psychology, completing a doctorate on Kant in Frankfurt in 1922. In 1930 he became director of Frankfurt's Institute for Social Research, the base of what became known as the Frankfurt School. When the Nazis came to power in 1933 the institute moved into exile, settling at Columbia University in New York. His essay 'Traditional and Critical Theory' appeared in 1937. With Theodor Adorno he wrote Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944, published as a book in 1947). He returned to Frankfurt after the war, served as rector of the university and died in 1973.","Horkheimer was above all an organiser and director of collaborative research, bringing together philosophers, economists, psychologists and sociologists. Much of his best-known work was written jointly or as essays rather than large treatises. His long partnership with Theodor Adorno was the core of the Frankfurt School, and he secured the funding that kept its members working through exile. In later life his writing grew more pessimistic and more open to religious ideas, such as a longing for something wholly other than the world as it is."],"timeline":[[1895,"Born in Stuttgart"],[1922,"Completes his doctorate on Kant at Frankfurt"],[1930,"Becomes director of the Institute for Social Research"],[1933,"The institute goes into exile; later relocates to New York"],[1937,"Publishes 'Traditional and Critical Theory'"],[1947,"Dialectic of Enlightenment (with Adorno) and Eclipse of Reason published"],[1951,"Becomes rector of the University of Frankfurt"],[1973,"Dies in Nuremberg"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Critical theory","body":"Ordinary 'traditional' theory describes society as it is, as if from outside. Critical theory recognises that thinkers are part of society and aims to change it, freeing people from domination. Its goal is not just to explain the world but to ask whose interests the existing order serves."},{"head":"The dialectic of enlightenment","body":"Enlightenment reason promised to free people from myth and fear. Horkheimer and Adorno argued that the same reason, used to control nature, turns into a tool for controlling people. Modern progress and modern barbarism can grow from the same root."},{"head":"The culture industry","body":"Films, radio and popular music are produced like factory goods, all following formulas. This 'culture industry' offers endless small variations that feel like choice while training people to accept the way things are."},{"head":"Instrumental reason","body":"In Eclipse of Reason Horkheimer argued that reason has shrunk to working out the best means for given ends. It can tell you how to build a bomb efficiently but not whether you should. Questions about which ends are worth pursuing are dismissed as mere preference."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Traditional and Critical Theory' (1937), the essay that defines the Frankfurt School's project. It is demanding but shorter and clearer than Dialectic of Enlightenment.","critics":"Jürgen Habermas, a later Frankfurt School thinker, argued in The Philosophical Discourse of Modernity (1985) that Dialectic of Enlightenment criticises reason so completely that it undercuts the reason its own critique relies on. Others say the culture industry thesis underestimates how audiences actively interpret and reshape popular culture.","legacy":"Horkheimer shaped the Frankfurt School and gave 'critical theory' its name and programme. The approach influences sociology, media studies and political philosophy today.","quote":null},"hartshorne":{"life":["Charles Hartshorne was born in 1897 in Kittanning, Pennsylvania, the son of an Episcopal clergyman. His long life spanned almost the whole twentieth century. In that time most academic philosophers in America grew sceptical about metaphysics and about God, while Hartshorne kept arguing that careful reasoning about God was both possible and important. His main influence was Whitehead, whose process philosophy he developed in his own way.","He studied at Haverford College, served as a medical orderly in France during the First World War and took his doctorate at Harvard in 1923. After study in Germany, where he heard Husserl and Heidegger, he returned to Harvard, worked as an assistant to Alfred North Whitehead and co-edited the collected papers of C. S. Peirce. He taught at the University of Chicago from 1928 to 1955, then at Emory University and, from 1962, at the University of Texas at Austin. His books include Man's Vision of God (1941) and The Logic of Perfection (1962). He died in Austin in 2000, aged 103.","Hartshorne was also a serious ornithologist. His book Born to Sing (1973) studied birdsong, which he saw as a kind of music that birds enjoy. He kept writing and publishing philosophy into his nineties. He saw his birdsong research and his philosophy as linked: both reflected his belief that feeling and enjoyment run throughout nature."],"timeline":[[1897,"Born in Kittanning, Pennsylvania"],[1923,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1928,"Joins the University of Chicago"],[1941,"Publishes Man's Vision of God"],[1962,"Publishes The Logic of Perfection; moves to the University of Texas at Austin"],[1973,"Publishes Born to Sing, a study of birdsong"],[1984,"Publishes Omnipotence and Other Theological Mistakes"],[2000,"Dies in Austin, Texas"]],"ideas":[{"head":"God has two sides","body":"Hartshorne's 'dipolar' God is unchanging in some ways, such as always being perfectly loving, and changing in others, such as experiencing new events as they happen. A loving parent's character stays the same, but their experience changes as their child grows. Hartshorne thought God is like this, to the highest degree."},{"head":"The world is in God","body":"He defended panentheism: the world is included within God, though God is more than the world. Every event adds to God's experience, so what we do matters to God."},{"head":"Persuasive, not controlling, power","body":"Since every creature has some freedom, God cannot decide everything that happens. God influences the world by offering possibilities and drawing creatures towards good. This, Hartshorne argued, helps explain evil without making God its author."},{"head":"A modal ontological argument","body":"Hartshorne revived Anselm's second argument for God. If a perfect being exists, it exists necessarily; so if it is even possible, it exists. The debate then turns on whether such a being is really possible."},{"head":"Experience everywhere","body":"Following Whitehead, he held that all real individuals, down to atoms and cells, have some degree of feeling. Rocks are not conscious, but they are made of tiny events that each experience something."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Omnipotence and Other Theological Mistakes (1984). It is short and written for general readers, and it explains clearly where he thinks traditional theology goes wrong.","critics":"Defenders of classical theism, such as Thomist philosophers, argue that a God who changes and depends on the world cannot be perfect or the ultimate cause of everything. Critics of his ontological argument note that it can be run in reverse: if it is possible that no perfect being exists, the same logic shows that none does.","legacy":"Hartshorne turned Whitehead's metaphysics into 'process theology', developed further by John Cobb and David Ray Griffin. Along with Norman Malcolm, he revived serious philosophical interest in the ontological argument.","quote":null},"rahner":{"life":["Karl Rahner was born in Freiburg im Breisgau, Germany, in 1904. He worked as the Catholic Church faced modern thought: historical study of the Bible, new philosophies such as Heidegger's, and a world in which many people no longer shared Christian belief. Church teaching was then dominated by a rigid textbook theology, and Rahner tried to rethink it from the inside.","He joined the Jesuit order in 1922 and was ordained a priest in 1932. He studied philosophy at Freiburg, attending Heidegger's seminars, but his thesis on Thomas Aquinas was rejected by his supervisor; it was published in 1939 as Spirit in the World. He taught at Innsbruck, interrupted when the Nazis closed the faculty. From 1954 his essays appeared as Theological Investigations, eventually many volumes. At the Second Vatican Council (1962–65) he served as an expert adviser. He later taught at Munich and Münster, published Foundations of Christian Faith (1976) and died in Innsbruck in 1984.","Rahner wrote mainly in essays responding to specific questions, so his output is enormous and scattered. His prose is famously dense, but he also wrote prayers and spiritual works for ordinary readers. His brother Hugo Rahner was also a Jesuit scholar. Some of Karl's ideas drew suspicion from Church authorities in the early 1960s, shortly before the Council made him one of its most influential voices."],"timeline":[[1904,"Born in Freiburg im Breisgau, Germany"],[1922,"Joins the Jesuit order"],[1932,"Ordained a priest"],[1939,"Spirit in the World published"],[1954,"First volume of Theological Investigations"],[1962,"Begins work as an adviser at the Second Vatican Council"],[1976,"Publishes Foundations of Christian Faith"],[1984,"Dies in Innsbruck"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Reaching beyond every object","body":"Whenever we know or choose something, Rahner argued, we are also reaching beyond it towards an unlimited horizon. The restless sense that nothing finite ever fully satisfies us points to God, the 'holy mystery' at the edge of all experience."},{"head":"Grace in every life","body":"God offers grace to every human being, not only to members of the Church. Rahner called this built-in openness to God the 'supernatural existential'. Ordinary experiences of love, honesty and hope can be moments of grace."},{"head":"Anonymous Christians","body":"A person who has never heard of Christ, or rejects the Christianity they have met, may still accept God's grace by living with faithful love. Rahner said such people can be saved through Christ without knowing it."},{"head":"God as God is to us","body":"'Rahner's Rule' says that the Trinity as revealed in history is the Trinity as God truly is. We do not need speculation behind revelation: God really gives God's own self to us."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Rahner's idea of 'anonymous Christians' means all religions are equally true.","truth":"Rahner held that salvation always comes through Christ, and that Christianity is its fullest expression. His point was that God's grace can reach people outside the visible Church."},"startHere":"Read the introduction and first chapters of Foundations of Christian Faith, which present his whole approach in one place, or his short essay 'Anonymous Christians' in Theological Investigations, Volume 6.","critics":"Hans Urs von Balthasar criticised him in Cordula (1966), arguing that his view weakens the need for explicit faith and mission. Others, including non-Christians, objected that calling people 'anonymous Christians' labels them with a belief they do not hold.","legacy":"Rahner was one of the most influential Catholic theologians of the twentieth century and helped shape Vatican II's more open attitude towards other religions and the modern world. His approach remains central in Catholic theology courses.","quote":null},"rand":{"life":["Ayn Rand was born Alisa Rosenbaum in St Petersburg, Russia, in 1905. As a girl she lived through the Russian Revolution of 1917, when the Bolsheviks confiscated her father's pharmacy. This experience of a collectivist state that claimed to act for the people while crushing individuals shaped everything she later wrote. She took the name Ayn Rand when she began writing in America.","She studied at Petrograd State University, graduating in 1924, and left for the United States in 1926. She worked in Hollywood, including as a screenwriter, and published her first novel, We the Living, in 1936. The Fountainhead (1943), about an uncompromising architect, made her famous, and Atlas Shrugged (1957) became a lasting bestseller. She then set out her philosophy, Objectivism, in non-fiction essays, including The Virtue of Selfishness (1964). She died in New York in 1982.","Rand presented her ideas mainly through novels with heroic characters and long speeches, and later through essays and lectures. She built an organised movement around Objectivism, but had few ties to academic philosophy, which she criticised sharply. Her circle in New York included the future economist Alan Greenspan and the psychologist Nathaniel Branden, who ran lectures promoting Objectivism until a public break with Rand in 1968."],"timeline":[[1905,"Born Alisa Rosenbaum in St Petersburg"],[1924,"Graduates from Petrograd State University"],[1926,"Emigrates to the United States"],[1936,"Publishes We the Living"],[1943,"Publishes The Fountainhead"],[1957,"Publishes Atlas Shrugged"],[1964,"Publishes The Virtue of Selfishness"],[1982,"Dies in New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Reality and reason","body":"Rand held that reality exists independently of anyone's wishes, and that reason, working from the evidence of the senses, is our only way of knowing it. Faith and feelings are not sources of knowledge."},{"head":"Rational self-interest","body":"Each person's own life is their highest value, and morality is the guide to living it well. Rand called this the 'virtue of selfishness'. By selfishness she meant pursuing one's long-term rational interests through productive work, not grabbing what one can."},{"head":"Against self-sacrifice","body":"Rand rejected altruism, the view that we have a duty to live for others. She also rejected sacrificing others to oneself. People should deal with each other as traders, exchanging value for value by mutual consent."},{"head":"Rights and capitalism","body":"Since force stops people acting on their own judgement, the only proper role of government is to protect individual rights through police, courts and defence. Rand defended laissez-faire capitalism, a free market with no government intervention, as the only moral social system."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Rand's 'selfishness' means doing whatever you want regardless of others.","truth":"She rejected using force or fraud against others and saw honesty, justice and productive work as virtues. Her egoism is about pursuing one's own rational long-term interests, which she believed never require harming others."},"startHere":"Read the essay 'The Objectivist Ethics', the first chapter of The Virtue of Selfishness. It is the most direct statement of her moral theory, without the length of the novels.","critics":"Robert Nozick, himself a defender of free markets, argued in 'On the Randian Argument' (1971) that her steps from 'life is the ultimate value' to egoism and to individual rights do not follow. Many philosophers also object that she misread the thinkers she attacked, especially Kant.","legacy":"Rand's novels still sell widely and have had a large influence on American libertarian and pro-market politics. Academic philosophers mostly ignored her, though a small group of scholars now studies and develops Objectivism.","quote":null},"koestler":{"life":["Arthur Koestler was born in Budapest in 1905, in the last years of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. His life followed the great political conflicts of the twentieth century: Zionism, communism, fascism, the Spanish Civil War and the Cold War. He wrote about all of them from experience. Born into a Hungarian-speaking Jewish family, he moved with his parents to Vienna as a teenager.","He studied in Vienna and then went to Palestine in 1926. He became a journalist and joined the Communist Party in 1931. Reporting the Spanish Civil War, he was captured by Franco's forces in 1937 and sentenced to death, but freed after international protest. He left the Communist Party in 1938. His novel Darkness at Noon (1940), about a loyal Bolshevik destroyed in Stalin's show trials, made him famous. After internment in France he settled in Britain. Later he turned to science and creativity, in The Sleepwalkers (1959), The Act of Creation (1964) and The Ghost in the Machine (1967). He died in London in 1983.","Koestler wrote in Hungarian, German and English, moving from journalism to fiction to popular science. He campaigned against the death penalty in Britain, notably in Reflections on Hanging (1956). He was a restless writer who took up unpopular causes and changed his views openly. He saw himself as a writer who had learned from his own political mistakes, and his autobiographies, such as Arrow in the Blue (1952), trace that path."],"timeline":[[1905,"Born in Budapest"],[1931,"Joins the Communist Party"],[1937,"Captured in the Spanish Civil War and sentenced to death; later released"],[1938,"Leaves the Communist Party"],[1940,"Publishes Darkness at Noon"],[1959,"Publishes The Sleepwalkers"],[1964,"Publishes The Act of Creation"],[1983,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ends and means","body":"Darkness at Noon shows how a revolutionary who believes the end justifies any means can end up confessing to crimes he did not commit. Koestler argued that a politics that sacrifices real people for a future ideal destroys its own aims."},{"head":"Bisociation","body":"In The Act of Creation he argued that jokes, scientific discoveries and art share one pattern: linking two frames of thought that normally do not meet. A pun joins two meanings of a word; a discovery joins two fields. He called this 'bisociation'."},{"head":"Snobbery about originals","body":"In his essay 'The Anatomy of Snobbery' Koestler asked why a painting loses value when it turns out to be a forgery, though it looks just the same. He argued that preferring the original in such cases is snobbery, valuing a name or a story rather than what we see."},{"head":"Science advances by sleepwalking","body":"The Sleepwalkers told the story of astronomy from the Greeks to Newton. Koestler argued that discoverers such as Kepler often reached the truth through odd beliefs and lucky errors, not a steady march of pure logic."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Darkness at Noon. It is gripping as a novel, and its interrogation scenes set out the central moral argument about ends and means.","critics":"Nelson Goodman, in Languages of Art (1968), argued, against views like Koestler's, that knowing a picture is a fake rightly changes how we look at it, because it trains us to see differences we had missed. The biologist Peter Medawar wrote a sharply critical review of The Act of Creation, finding its scientific claims loose.","legacy":"Darkness at Noon remains one of the most important novels about totalitarianism and shaped Western views of Stalinism. His essay on forgery is still a standard reference in aesthetics debates about fakes.","quote":null},"godel":{"life":["Kurt Gödel was born in 1906 in Brünn (now Brno, in the Czech Republic), then part of Austria-Hungary. He studied in Vienna in the 1920s, when the Vienna Circle was trying to put philosophy on a scientific footing and Hilbert was trying to prove that mathematics is free of contradiction. Gödel's results changed both projects.","He entered the University of Vienna in 1924 and attended meetings of the Vienna Circle. His doctoral thesis (1929) proved the completeness of first-order logic. In 1931 he published his incompleteness theorems. Later he showed that two disputed axioms of set theory, the axiom of choice and the continuum hypothesis, cannot be disproved from the standard axioms. In 1940 he left Austria for the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, where he became a permanent member and later professor, and a close friend of Albert Einstein. He died in Princeton in 1978.","Gödel published little, but each paper was carefully finished. He was cautious about stating his philosophical views in print, and much of his philosophy of mathematics is known from a few essays and from his unpublished notes and conversations. He was deeply interested in philosophy, especially Leibniz and later Husserl, and believed that philosophy could one day become as rigorous as mathematics."],"timeline":[[1906,"Born in Brünn, Austria-Hungary"],[1929,"Completes his doctoral thesis proving the completeness of first-order logic"],[1931,"Publishes the incompleteness theorems"],[1940,"Leaves Europe for the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton"],[1947,"Becomes a US citizen; publishes 'What Is Cantor's Continuum Problem?'"],[1949,"Describes rotating universes in which time travel to the past is possible"],[1978,"Dies in Princeton"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Incompleteness","body":"Any consistent formal system strong enough to do basic arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove. Gödel built a statement that, in effect, says 'I am not provable in this system'. If the system is consistent, that statement is true but unprovable."},{"head":"No proof of consistency from within","body":"His second theorem shows that such a system cannot prove its own consistency. This defeated Hilbert's hope of securing all of mathematics with a simple internal proof that it never contradicts itself."},{"head":"Mathematics is discovered","body":"Gödel was a Platonist: he held that mathematical objects such as sets exist independently of us and that we perceive them through a kind of mathematical intuition. Questions like the continuum hypothesis have real answers, even if current axioms cannot settle them."},{"head":"Minds and machines","body":"In a 1951 lecture he argued that either the human mind exceeds any machine, or there are mathematical problems humans can never solve. He thought the first option likely, but held that his theorems alone did not prove it."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Gödel proved that nothing can be known for certain, or that truth is relative.","truth":"His theorems concern formal systems of a certain kind. They show that provability in such a system falls short of truth, which Gödel took as evidence that mathematical truth is objective."},"startHere":"Read Ernest Nagel and James Newman's short book Gödel's Proof, which explains the incompleteness theorems step by step for non-specialists, before trying Gödel's own 1931 paper.","critics":"Paul Benacerraf, in 'Mathematical Truth' (1973), posed the challenge to Gödel's Platonism: if mathematical objects are abstract and cause nothing, it is unclear how we could ever come to know about them. Many logicians also reject arguments, inspired by Gödel, that his theorems prove minds are not machines.","legacy":"The incompleteness theorems are among the most important results in logic and shaped computer science, philosophy of mind and philosophy of mathematics. Gödel remains the central example of a philosophically committed mathematical Platonist.","quote":null},"goodman":{"life":["Nelson Goodman was born in 1906 in Somerville, Massachusetts. He belonged to the generation of American philosophers who absorbed logical empiricism, the scientific philosophy of the Vienna Circle, and then reshaped it. He worked on questions that seem far apart, the logic of induction and the nature of art, and treated both with the same precision.","He studied at Harvard, graduating in 1928, and during the Great Depression ran an art gallery in Boston while working towards his doctorate, which he completed in 1941. After army service in the Second World War, he taught at the University of Pennsylvania from 1946 to 1964. He published The Structure of Appearance (1951) and Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1954). After a period at Brandeis, he returned to Harvard in 1968, where he had founded Project Zero, a research group on arts education. Languages of Art (1968) and Ways of Worldmaking (1978) followed. He died in 1998.","Goodman wrote in a compressed, witty style and loved building precise systems with as few assumptions as possible. He collected art seriously and was involved in staging multimedia performances, so his work on art drew on practical experience. He often worked with W. V. O. Quine, with whom he wrote 'Steps Toward a Constructive Nominalism' (1947), an attempt to do without abstract objects altogether."],"timeline":[[1906,"Born in Somerville, Massachusetts"],[1941,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1946,"Begins teaching at the University of Pennsylvania"],[1954,"Publishes Fact, Fiction, and Forecast, introducing 'grue'"],[1967,"Founds Harvard Project Zero"],[1968,"Publishes Languages of Art; returns to Harvard"],[1978,"Publishes Ways of Worldmaking"],[1998,"Dies in Massachusetts"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The new riddle of induction","body":"Define 'grue' as: green if first observed before some future date, blue otherwise. Every emerald seen so far is both green and grue, so the evidence fits 'all emeralds are grue' as well as 'all emeralds are green'. Goodman's puzzle is why we rightly project 'green' and not 'grue'."},{"head":"Art as a symbol system","body":"Pictures, scores, poems and dances are symbols that refer to the world in different ways. A painting can depict a storm and express turbulence. Understanding art is a form of understanding, not just enjoyment."},{"head":"Fakes and seeing","body":"If you learn that one of two identical-looking pictures is a fake, that knowledge changes how you should look, because it teaches you that there may be differences to find. So even an undetected forgery is not aesthetically equal to the original."},{"head":"Autographic and allographic arts","body":"A painting can be forged, because its identity depends on who actually made it. A symphony cannot be forged in the same way: any performance that follows the score is the symphony. Goodman called these autographic and allographic arts."},{"head":"Ways of worldmaking","body":"Goodman argued that we make many 'versions' of the world, in science, art and everyday speech, and there is no single way the world is apart from all of them. He called this view irrealism."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Chapter 3 of Fact, Fiction, and Forecast, 'The New Riddle of Induction'. It is short, playful and one of the most discussed puzzles in modern philosophy.","critics":"Israel Scheffler, his colleague, objected to worldmaking that we did not make the stars, so talk of making worlds is either false or merely about making descriptions. Critics of Languages of Art note its strange consequence that a performance with a single wrong note is not strictly a performance of the work.","legacy":"'Grue' remains a central problem in the philosophy of science and induction. Languages of Art made the analysis of symbols a major approach in aesthetics, and Project Zero continues as a research centre on learning and the arts.","quote":null},"qutb":{"life":["Sayyid Qutb was born in 1906 in the village of Musha in Upper Egypt. Egypt was then under strong British influence, and its educated classes argued over how far to adopt Western ideas. In his lifetime Egypt gained fuller independence, saw a military coup in 1952 and came under the rule of Gamal Abdel Nasser.","He memorised the Qur'an as a child, studied at Dar al-Ulum teachers' college in Cairo and worked for the Ministry of Education. At first he was a poet and literary critic. In the late 1940s he turned to Islam as a social programme, publishing Social Justice in Islam (1949). From 1948 to 1950 he studied education in the United States and returned deeply critical of American society. In the early 1950s he joined the Muslim Brotherhood. After a 1954 attempt on Nasser's life, the Brotherhood was crushed and Qutb was imprisoned. In prison he wrote much of In the Shade of the Qur'an and Milestones (1964). Briefly released, he was arrested again and executed in 1966.","Qutb wrote in a vivid literary style, and before his religious turn he was an early admirer of Naguib Mahfouz's fiction. His most radical works were written in prison, under harsh conditions and after seeing fellow Brotherhood members tortured. Before turning to religious and political writing, he published poetry, novels and an account of his village childhood."],"timeline":[[1906,"Born in Musha, Upper Egypt"],[1948,"Travels to the United States to study education"],[1949,"Publishes Social Justice in Islam"],[1954,"Imprisoned after the crackdown on the Muslim Brotherhood"],[1964,"Publishes Milestones; briefly released from prison"],[1965,"Arrested again"],[1966,"Executed in Cairo"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Sovereignty belongs to God","body":"Qutb taught that only God has the right to make law (hakimiyya). Any system in which humans make laws for themselves, whether democracy, socialism or monarchy, puts people in God's place."},{"head":"Modern jahiliyya","body":"Jahiliyya traditionally means the 'ignorance' of Arabia before Islam. Qutb applied it to the modern world, including societies whose people called themselves Muslim, if they were not governed by God's law. This was the most controversial step in his thought."},{"head":"A vanguard","body":"In Milestones he called for a committed vanguard of believers to separate themselves from jahili society and work to establish an Islamic order. He saw this as following the path of the first Muslim community."},{"head":"Islam as a complete system","body":"In Social Justice in Islam he argued that Islam offers its own full answer to social and economic questions, so Muslims do not need capitalism or communism. He criticised Western societies as materialist and morally empty."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Qutb's views simply restate mainstream, traditional Islamic teaching.","truth":"His use of jahiliyya to condemn societies that called themselves Muslim was new and controversial. Many Muslim scholars, including leaders of the Muslim Brotherhood, rejected it."},"startHere":"Read the introduction and first chapter of Milestones, which set out his ideas of God's sovereignty and modern jahiliyya in a short, direct form. It is best read alongside a historical introduction to his context.","critics":"Within the Muslim Brotherhood, the book Preachers, Not Judges, attributed to its leader Hasan al-Hudaybi, rejected declaring fellow Muslims unbelievers, a practice associated with readings of Qutb. Liberal and secular critics argue that his idea of God's sovereignty leaves no room for democratic self-government or religious freedom.","legacy":"Qutb is one of the most influential Islamist thinkers of the twentieth century. His writings inspired many Islamist movements, including some militant jihadist groups, though how far his ideas lead to violence is debated by scholars.","quote":null},"hart":{"life":["Herbert Lionel Adolphus Hart was born in 1907 in Harrogate, Yorkshire, into a Jewish family. When he began work, the leading theory in English legal thought, from Jeremy Bentham and John Austin, said that law is simply the commands of a sovereign backed by threats. At Oxford after 1945, philosophers were developing new methods of analysing ordinary language, and Hart applied them to law.","He studied classics and philosophy at New College, Oxford, and then practised as a barrister in London for several years. During the Second World War he worked in British military intelligence, MI5. Afterwards he returned to Oxford to teach philosophy and in 1952 became Professor of Jurisprudence. The Concept of Law (1961) became the most influential work of legal philosophy in English. He also debated Lon Fuller on law and morality and Lord Devlin on the legal enforcement of morals. He resigned his chair in 1968, became Principal of Brasenose College and died in Oxford in 1992.","Hart combined a lawyer's practical knowledge with a philosopher's attention to how words are used. He wrote clearly and fairly, and supported liberal law reform, such as decriminalising homosexuality in Britain. His later work included editions of Bentham and Essays on Bentham (1982). A long reply to Dworkin, left unfinished, was published after his death as a postscript to the second edition of The Concept of Law (1994)."],"timeline":[[1907,"Born in Harrogate, Yorkshire"],[1932,"Begins practising as a barrister"],[1952,"Becomes Professor of Jurisprudence at Oxford"],[1958,"Publishes 'Positivism and the Separation of Law and Morals', debating Lon Fuller"],[1961,"Publishes The Concept of Law"],[1963,"Publishes Law, Liberty, and Morality, answering Lord Devlin"],[1973,"Becomes Principal of Brasenose College"],[1992,"Dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Rules, not commands","body":"Hart rejected the idea that law is just orders backed by threats, like a gunman demanding money. A gunman obliges you through fear; law creates obligations. Law consists of social rules that people accept as standards for behaviour."},{"head":"Primary and secondary rules","body":"Primary rules tell people what to do, such as 'do not steal'. Secondary rules are rules about rules: how to recognise valid law, how to change it, and how to settle disputes. A legal system is the union of the two."},{"head":"The rule of recognition","body":"Every legal system has a basic rule, accepted by officials, for identifying what counts as law. In Britain it says, roughly, that what Parliament enacts is law. Its existence is a social fact about how officials behave."},{"head":"Law and morality are separate","body":"Whether a rule is law is one question; whether it is good is another. Keeping them apart, Hart argued, lets us say clearly that some laws are too wicked to obey."},{"head":"Limits on enforcing morality","body":"Against Lord Devlin, Hart argued that the law should not punish private conduct just because most people think it immoral. Following John Stuart Mill, he held that preventing harm to others is a much better reason."}],"misconception":{"myth":"As a legal positivist, Hart thought people must obey any law, however unjust.","truth":"Hart held the opposite: separating law from morality lets us see that a valid law can be morally wrong and that we may have good reason to disobey it."},"startHere":"Read Chapters 5 and 6 of The Concept of Law, where he sets out primary and secondary rules and the rule of recognition. Chapter 1 also gives a clear overview of the questions.","critics":"Ronald Dworkin, his successor at Oxford, argued that law includes moral principles as well as rules, so judges in hard cases are not simply making new law. Lon Fuller argued that law has an 'inner morality', such as rules being public and clear, so law and morality cannot be fully separated.","legacy":"The Concept of Law set the agenda for modern legal philosophy, and the debate between Hart and Dworkin remains its central dispute. His arguments against enforcing morality influenced liberal law reform in Britain.","quote":null},"wimsatt":{"life":["William Kurtz Wimsatt was born in Washington, D.C., in 1907. He worked during the rise of the 'New Criticism' in American universities, which taught readers to study a poem's own words and structure closely, rather than the poet's life or the reader's feelings. Wimsatt became one of its most careful theorists. Its leading figures included Cleanth Brooks and John Crowe Ransom.","He studied at Georgetown University and then taught school before taking his doctorate at Yale. His first book, The Prose Style of Samuel Johnson, appeared in 1941. He joined the Yale English department in 1939 and stayed there for the rest of his career. With the philosopher Monroe Beardsley he wrote 'The Intentional Fallacy' (1946) and 'The Affective Fallacy' (1949). His essays were collected in The Verbal Icon (1954). With Cleanth Brooks he wrote Literary Criticism: A Short History (1957). He died in New Haven in 1975.","Wimsatt was a scholar of eighteenth-century literature, especially Samuel Johnson and Alexander Pope, as well as a theorist. He was known for precise, closely argued essays, and he was a committed Catholic, which shaped his interest in poetry's lasting value. His partnership with Beardsley, a philosopher, was an early example of literary critics and philosophers working together on questions of meaning."],"timeline":[[1907,"Born in Washington, D.C."],[1939,"Joins the Yale English department"],[1941,"Publishes The Prose Style of Samuel Johnson"],[1946,"Publishes 'The Intentional Fallacy' with Monroe Beardsley"],[1949,"Publishes 'The Affective Fallacy' with Beardsley"],[1954,"Publishes The Verbal Icon"],[1957,"Publishes Literary Criticism: A Short History with Cleanth Brooks"],[1975,"Dies in New Haven, Connecticut"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The intentional fallacy","body":"It is a mistake to judge a poem by what its author meant to do. A poem works or fails on the page; if the author meant something the words do not convey, the intention did not succeed. Interviews with the poet are not a short cut to what the poem means."},{"head":"The affective fallacy","body":"It is equally a mistake to judge a poem by the feelings it produces in readers. A poem that makes one reader weep and leaves another cold is the same poem. Criticism should study the poem, not reactions to it."},{"head":"Meaning is public","body":"A poem's meaning is fixed by the shared meanings of its words, grammar and culture, which any competent reader can access. Private facts about the author count only if they help explain how the words are used."},{"head":"The poem as verbal icon","body":"Wimsatt saw a poem as a 'verbal icon': a structure of words that presents meaning concretely, the way an image does. Its sound, metaphor and form are part of what it says."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Wimsatt and Beardsley said the author's life and intentions are completely irrelevant to reading a poem.","truth":"They argued that intention is not the standard for judging a poem's success or meaning. They allowed evidence about the author's use of words and the culture of the time when it helps explain what the text says."},"startHere":"Read 'The Intentional Fallacy', the opening essay of The Verbal Icon. It is short, clear and still widely taught.","critics":"E. D. Hirsch, in Validity in Interpretation (1967), argued that without the author's intention there is no fixed meaning to be right or wrong about, so interpretation becomes arbitrary.","legacy":"'The Intentional Fallacy' is one of the most cited essays in literary theory and started a debate about intention and meaning that continues in both literary studies and philosophy of art.","quote":null},"cioran":{"life":["Emil Cioran was born in 1911 in Răşinari, a village in Transylvania, then part of Austria-Hungary and after 1918 of Romania. His father was an Orthodox priest. He grew up amid the nationalism and political extremism of interwar Romania, and as a young man was drawn into it. He later spent most of his life in Paris as an exile.","He studied philosophy at the University of Bucharest and published his first book, On the Heights of Despair, in 1934. He studied in Berlin from 1933 to 1935. In the 1930s he wrote in support of the fascist Iron Guard, including in The Transfiguration of Romania (1936), views he later repudiated and regretted. In 1937 he moved to Paris on a scholarship and never returned to Romania. After the war he switched to writing in French: A Short History of Decay (1949) won a literary prize, and later books included The Trouble with Being Born (1973). He died in Paris in 1995.","Cioran suffered from severe insomnia from his youth and said it shaped his thinking. He lived simply in Paris without an academic post and turned down most of the prizes offered to him. He wrote in short fragments and aphorisms rather than systematic arguments. His friends in Paris included the playwright Eugène Ionesco and the historian of religion Mircea Eliade, both also Romanian exiles."],"timeline":[[1911,"Born in Răşinari, Transylvania"],[1934,"Publishes On the Heights of Despair in Romanian"],[1936,"Publishes The Transfiguration of Romania, later repudiated"],[1937,"Moves to Paris"],[1949,"Publishes A Short History of Decay, his first book in French"],[1960,"Publishes History and Utopia"],[1973,"Publishes The Trouble with Being Born"],[1995,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The misfortune of birth","body":"Most people fear death, but Cioran held that the real misfortune is having been born at all. Everything painful in life follows from that first event. Not existing would have spared us everything."},{"head":"Lucidity hurts","body":"Seeing clearly, without illusions about progress, meaning or salvation, brings suffering. Yet Cioran valued this lucidity above comforting beliefs, even while doubting that it does us any good."},{"head":"Against fanaticism","body":"In A Short History of Decay he argued that the most dangerous people are those with firm convictions. Doubt and scepticism may be uncomfortable, but they rarely lead to persecution."},{"head":"Fragments, not systems","body":"Cioran distrusted philosophical systems as attempts to tidy up a disordered life. He wrote in short fragments, which can contradict each other, to stay true to changing moods and insights."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Cioran urged people to commit suicide.","truth":"He wrote often about suicide, but presented the thought of it as something that made life bearable, a reminder that one could leave at any time. He never recommended it and lived to 84."},"startHere":"Open The Trouble with Being Born at any page and read a few aphorisms at a time. The book has no argument to follow, so it rewards slow, random reading.","critics":"Philosophers often object that his fragments assert bleak conclusions without arguing for them, and that his pessimism contradicts itself by being so lively. Critics have also examined his early support for the Iron Guard, asking how far his later scepticism was a reckoning with it.","legacy":"Cioran is one of the best-known pessimist writers of the twentieth century and a master of French prose style. His idea that birth is the real misfortune is often cited in modern discussions of antinatalism, the view that it is wrong to bring people into existence.","quote":null},"prior":{"life":["Arthur Norman Prior was born in 1914 in Masterton, New Zealand. Formal logic in the mid-twentieth century usually treated statements as timelessly true or false. Prior asked how logic could handle tense: the difference between 'it is raining', 'it was raining' and 'it will rain'. Ancient and medieval logicians had discussed tense, but modern logic had largely set the problem aside.","He studied philosophy at the University of Otago under John Findlay, considered entering the Presbyterian ministry, and served in the Royal New Zealand Air Force during the Second World War. In 1946 he became a lecturer at Canterbury University College in Christchurch, replacing Karl Popper, and later its professor. His early book Logic and the Basis of Ethics (1949) dealt with whether 'ought' can be derived from 'is'. His John Locke Lectures at Oxford in 1956 became Time and Modality (1957). He moved to Manchester in 1959 and to Balliol College, Oxford, in 1966. He died in Trondheim, Norway, in 1969, the night before he was due to lecture there.","Prior worked largely from New Zealand, far from the main centres of philosophy, teaching himself much of modern logic from books. He drew on medieval and ancient logicians as well as modern ones, and wrote with lively, plain examples. His wife, Mary Prior, worked with him on some papers, including one on the logic of questions."],"timeline":[[1914,"Born in Masterton, New Zealand"],[1946,"Becomes a lecturer at Canterbury University College, Christchurch"],[1949,"Publishes Logic and the Basis of Ethics"],[1956,"Gives the John Locke Lectures at Oxford"],[1957,"Publishes Time and Modality"],[1959,"Becomes professor at the University of Manchester"],[1967,"Publishes Past, Present and Future"],[1969,"Dies in Trondheim, Norway"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Tense logic","body":"Prior created tense logic, a formal system with operators meaning 'it was the case that' and 'it will be the case that'. Instead of treating time as a list of dates, it builds past and future into the logic of statements themselves."},{"head":"Presentism","body":"Prior held that only the present is real. Past things existed and future things will exist, but they do not exist now. Talk about Julius Caesar is really talk about how things were."},{"head":"Thank goodness that's over","body":"After a painful event, we say 'thank goodness that's over'. We are not thankful for a timeless fact such as 'the pain ends before this date'. Prior argued that this relief only makes sense if the present is really special, so tense is real."},{"head":"No ought from is","body":"In Logic and the Basis of Ethics he studied attempts to derive moral conclusions from factual premises. He developed logical arguments about whether this can be done, adding to the debate started by David Hume."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his short paper 'Thank Goodness That's Over' (1959), reprinted in Papers in Logic and Ethics. It is only a few pages and gives a vivid case for the reality of tense.","critics":"B-theorists such as D. H. Mellor argued that tensed beliefs are needed for timing our actions and feelings but do not show that reality itself is tensed. Others argue that Einstein's relativity, which allows no single universal 'now', counts against presentism.","legacy":"Tense logic grew into temporal logic, now used in computer science to check that programs and hardware behave correctly over time. Prior is also a founder of modern presentism in the philosophy of time.","quote":null},"beardsley":{"life":["Monroe Curtis Beardsley was born in 1915 in Bridgeport, Connecticut. He worked when American philosophy was turning to careful analysis of language and argument, and when literary critics were debating how poems should be read. He brought the two together, becoming one of the founders of modern analytic aesthetics, the philosophy of art and beauty.","He studied at Yale, earning his BA in 1936 and his PhD in 1939. After teaching at Mount Holyoke and Yale, he taught at Swarthmore College for over twenty years and then at Temple University in Philadelphia until his death. With W. K. Wimsatt he wrote 'The Intentional Fallacy' (1946) and 'The Affective Fallacy' (1949). His major book, Aesthetics: Problems in the Philosophy of Criticism (1958), was followed by a history of aesthetics in 1966. He served as president of the American Society for Aesthetics. He died in Philadelphia in 1985.","Beardsley was also a popular teacher of reasoning: his textbooks Practical Logic and Thinking Straight (both 1950) taught generations of students to analyse arguments. His philosophy aimed to give critics clear, testable standards for their judgements. With his wife, the philosopher Elizabeth Lane Beardsley, he edited a well-known series of philosophy textbooks, and he was widely respected for clear, fair-minded argument."],"timeline":[[1915,"Born in Bridgeport, Connecticut"],[1939,"Completes his PhD at Yale"],[1946,"Publishes 'The Intentional Fallacy' with W. K. Wimsatt"],[1950,"Publishes Practical Logic and Thinking Straight"],[1958,"Publishes Aesthetics: Problems in the Philosophy of Criticism"],[1966,"Publishes Aesthetics from Classical Greece to the Present"],[1983,"Publishes 'An Aesthetic Definition of Art'"],[1985,"Dies in Philadelphia"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The work speaks for itself","body":"Beardsley argued that a critic should interpret and judge the work itself, not the artist's intentions or the audience's feelings. What a poem means depends on its words and their public meanings."},{"head":"Aesthetic experience","body":"Art's value lies in the aesthetic experience it can give. Beardsley described such experience as focused, unified, intense and complex, as when you become absorbed in a piece of music and everything in it seems to hang together."},{"head":"Reasons in criticism","body":"Critics can give real reasons for their judgements. Beardsley proposed three general criteria: unity, complexity and intensity. Calling a novel 'well unified' is a genuine reason to value it, not just a statement of taste."},{"head":"An aesthetic definition of art","body":"In later work he defined an artwork as something made with the intention of giving it the capacity to provide aesthetic experience. This tied the definition of art to its purpose, against views that make art a matter of social institutions."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the essay 'The Intentional Fallacy', written with Wimsatt. It is short and introduces his central claim that the work, not the artist's mind, is the object of criticism.","critics":"George Dickie, in 'Beardsley's Phantom Aesthetic Experience' (1965), argued that there is no special kind of experience that art produces, so Beardsley's theory rests on a phantom. Dickie went on to defend an institutional theory of art against Beardsley's aesthetic definition.","legacy":"Beardsley's Aesthetics helped make the philosophy of art an established field in analytic philosophy. Debates about intention, aesthetic experience and critical reasons still often start from his positions.","quote":null},"barthes":{"life":["Roland Barthes was born in 1915 in Cherbourg, France. His father, a naval officer, was killed in a sea battle in 1916, and Barthes grew up with his mother in Bayonne and then Paris. He came of age in a France shaped by war and occupation and, after 1945, by intense debate about Marxism, existentialism and the new 'structuralist' study of signs: the idea that culture works like a language, whose meanings depend on systems of differences.","Tuberculosis kept him in sanatoriums for much of the 1940s and delayed a normal academic career. After teaching posts in Romania and Egypt, he published Writing Degree Zero (1953) and Mythologies (1957), a collection of short essays decoding everyday French culture. From 1962 he taught at the École pratique des hautes études in Paris. 'The Death of the Author' (1967) and S/Z (1970) took him beyond strict structuralism. In 1976 he was elected to a chair of literary semiology at the Collège de France.","Barthes wrote mostly in short forms, such as essays, fragments and notes, rather than long systematic treatises. His later books, including The Pleasure of the Text (1973), A Lover's Discourse (1977) and Camera Lucida (1980), became more personal; Camera Lucida reflects on photography and on the death of his mother in 1977. In February 1980 he was struck by a laundry van while crossing a Paris street, and he died a month later."],"timeline":[[1915,"Born in Cherbourg, France"],[1953,"Publishes Writing Degree Zero"],[1957,"Publishes Mythologies"],[1967,"'The Death of the Author' first appears, in English, in the US magazine Aspen"],[1970,"Publishes S/Z and Empire of Signs"],[1976,"Elected to a chair at the Collège de France"],[1980,"Publishes Camera Lucida; dies in Paris after a street accident"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The myths of everyday life","body":"In Mythologies Barthes analysed wrestling, wine, toys, advertising and magazine covers. A 'myth', in his sense, is a message that makes something cultural look natural and obvious. His best-known example is a magazine cover showing a young Black soldier saluting the French flag, which quietly tells readers that the French empire is good and its subjects loyal."},{"head":"Signs carry second meanings","body":"A sign has a literal meaning and a further, suggested meaning. A photo of a sports car shows a car, but in an advert it also says 'freedom' or 'success'. Barthes taught readers to notice these second meanings, which do most of the persuading."},{"head":"The death of the author","body":"Barthes argued that readers should stop treating the author's life and intentions as the single key that unlocks a text. A text is woven from many earlier writings, phrases and codes of the culture, and its meanings come together in the reader. Hence his claim that the birth of the reader comes at the cost of the death of the author."},{"head":"Readerly and writerly texts","body":"In S/Z he contrasted 'readerly' texts, which hand the reader a finished meaning to consume, with 'writerly' texts, which invite the reader to help produce the meaning. He preferred the second kind, because they make reading an active, creative task."},{"head":"Studium and punctum","body":"In Camera Lucida he distinguished two ways a photograph affects us. The studium is our general, cultural interest in what a photo shows; the punctum is a small detail that 'pricks' a particular viewer and moves them personally, such as a stranger's hands or shoes."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Barthes claimed that authors do not matter at all, so a text can mean anything a reader wants.","truth":"He was attacking the habit of treating the author's biography and intentions as the final explanation of a text. He still treated texts as built from shared codes and conventions, which shape and limit what readers make of them."},"startHere":"Read a few essays from Mythologies, such as 'The World of Wrestling', and then 'Myth Today' at the end of the book: the first shows his method on an entertaining subject, and the second explains the theory behind it.","critics":"E. D. Hirsch argued that if the author's intended meaning is set aside, there is no standard for saying one interpretation is more valid than another. Seán Burke, in The Death and Return of the Author (1992), argued that Barthes and other theorists kept relying on the idea of the author in their own practice.","legacy":"Barthes helped make semiotics a tool for reading popular culture, and his ideas about readers making meaning shaped literary theory, media studies and cultural studies.","quote":null},"weitz":{"life":["Morris Weitz was born in 1916 in Detroit, Michigan. He worked in the middle of the twentieth century, when English-speaking philosophy was being reshaped by 'analytic' methods: close study of how concepts and words are actually used. Ludwig Wittgenstein's Philosophical Investigations, published in 1953, was especially influential, and Weitz applied its ideas to the arts. At the same time, modern artists were breaking the rules that older theories had used to define art.","He studied at Wayne State University and then the University of Michigan, where he earned his PhD in 1943 with a thesis on Bertrand Russell's method of analysis. He taught at the University of Washington and Vassar College, then at Ohio State University from 1954 to 1969, and finally at Brandeis University until his death in 1981. His best-known paper, 'The Role of Theory in Aesthetics', appeared in 1956.","Weitz was as interested in literature as in visual art. His books include Philosophy of the Arts (1950), Philosophy in Literature (1963) and Hamlet and the Philosophy of Literary Criticism (1964), which examined how critics argue about Shakespeare's play. He also edited a widely used anthology, Problems in Aesthetics (1959), and held visiting posts at Columbia, Cornell and Harvard."],"timeline":[[1916,"Born in Detroit, Michigan"],[1943,"Completes his PhD at the University of Michigan"],[1950,"Publishes Philosophy of the Arts"],[1954,"Joins Ohio State University"],[1956,"Publishes 'The Role of Theory in Aesthetics'"],[1964,"Publishes Hamlet and the Philosophy of Literary Criticism"],[1969,"Moves to Brandeis University"],[1981,"Dies in Boston"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Art is an open concept","body":"Some concepts are 'closed': they have a fixed set of conditions, as 'triangle' does. Weitz argued that 'art' is 'open', because artists keep making new kinds of work that stretch it. Any definition that fitted today's art would be broken by tomorrow's, so no definition can succeed."},{"head":"Family resemblances","body":"Borrowing Wittgenstein's example of games, Weitz argued that artworks do not share one common feature. Instead they share overlapping similarities, like members of a family who resemble each other in different ways. We decide whether a new case is art by comparing it with works already accepted."},{"head":"Theories as recommendations","body":"Famous theories, such as 'art is expression of emotion' or 'art is significant form', fail as definitions. But Weitz thought they are still valuable: each one is really an argument for paying attention to certain features of art, and so teaches us how to look."},{"head":"Describing versus praising","body":"Saying 'this is a work of art' can be a plain description of what kind of thing it is, or it can be praise. Weitz argued that confusing these two uses causes much muddle in arguments about what counts as art."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Weitz held that anything at all can be art, so the word means nothing.","truth":"He argued that 'art' has no fixed definition, not that it has no meaning. We apply it through recognisable similarities to agreed examples, just as we apply the word 'game'."},"startHere":"Read 'The Role of Theory in Aesthetics' (1956) itself: it is a short, clearly argued journal article, reprinted in most aesthetics anthologies.","critics":"Maurice Mandelbaum (1965) argued that Weitz only looked at visible features; artworks might share a hidden, relational property, such as how they were made or the role they play, which a definition could capture. Later 'institutional' and 'historical' definitions by George Dickie and Jerrold Levinson took this route.","legacy":"Weitz's paper set the agenda for decades of debate on whether art can be defined, and most later definitions of art were written partly as answers to him.","quote":null},"chisholm":{"life":["Roderick Chisholm was born in 1916 in North Attleboro, Massachusetts. He studied at Brown and then Harvard at a time when American philosophy was dominated by logical empiricism, which was suspicious of talk about the mind and of traditional metaphysics. Chisholm went against this trend, defending common-sense views about knowledge, the self and free action, and drawing on the Austrian philosopher Franz Brentano, whose work he helped bring to English-speaking readers.","After earning his Harvard PhD in 1942, he joined the Brown faculty in 1947 and stayed there for the rest of his career. His books include Perceiving (1957), Theory of Knowledge (1966, with revised editions in 1977 and 1989), Person and Object (1976) and The First Person (1981). His 1964 lecture 'Human Freedom and the Self' became a classic defence of free will.","Chisholm had a distinctive style: short, numbered definitions and principles, tested against counterexamples and revised again and again over many years. He trained many students who became well-known philosophers, including Richard Taylor and Keith Lehrer. In the 1950s he debated with Wilfrid Sellars, in a published exchange of letters, whether thought can be explained in physical terms. He also edited and helped translate works by Brentano. He died in 1999."],"timeline":[[1916,"Born in North Attleboro, Massachusetts"],[1942,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1947,"Joins the faculty of Brown University"],[1957,"Publishes Perceiving"],[1964,"Gives the lecture 'Human Freedom and the Self'"],[1966,"Publishes Theory of Knowledge"],[1976,"Publishes Person and Object"],[1999,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Agent causation","body":"When we act freely, Chisholm argued, the action is not caused by earlier events such as desires or brain states. It is caused by the agent, the person, directly. Like a 'prime mover unmoved', the agent makes something happen without anything causing them to do so."},{"head":"The problem of the criterion","body":"To pick out which of our beliefs are knowledge, it seems we need a test; but to know which test is right, it seems we must already know some examples. Some philosophers start with a test; Chisholm, like Thomas Reid and G. E. Moore, started with examples we are sure of, such as knowing we have hands, and built principles from them."},{"head":"Foundations of knowledge","body":"Chisholm held that some beliefs are justified simply by how things appear to us right now, such as 'I seem to see something red'. These 'self-presenting' states need no further evidence and support the rest of what we know."},{"head":"Thought is about things","body":"Following Brentano, Chisholm argued that the mark of the mental is 'intentionality': thoughts are about things, even things that do not exist, like unicorns. He argued that this feature cannot be translated into purely physical language."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Human Freedom and the Self' (1964), a short lecture that sets out the case for agent causation and is widely reprinted in free-will anthologies.","critics":"An objection going back to C. D. Broad asks how a lasting agent, rather than some event in the agent, could cause something to happen at one particular moment. Peter van Inwagen and others argue that agent causation does not remove the worry that an undetermined choice is a matter of luck.","legacy":"Chisholm helped make metaphysics and the theory of knowledge respectable again in American philosophy, and his agent-causal view remains a main option in debates about free will.","quote":null},"richard_taylor":{"life":["Richard Taylor was born in 1919 in Charlotte, Michigan, and studied at the University of Illinois. After the attack on Pearl Harbor he joined the US Navy and served as a submarine officer in the Pacific during the Second World War. He belonged to a generation of American philosophers trained after the war in the careful analytic style, but he used it on large, old questions: fate, freedom, God and the meaning of life.","After the war he studied at Oberlin College and then at Brown University, where he completed his PhD under Roderick Chisholm in 1951. He taught at Brown, briefly at Columbia, and then at the University of Rochester from 1965 to 1985, with many visiting posts elsewhere. His textbook Metaphysics (1963) was read by generations of students. His article 'Fatalism' (1962) caused wide debate, and Good and Evil (1970) set out an ethics built on human desires and needs.","Taylor was also a serious beekeeper. He kept around 300 hives near Cayuga Lake in New York, produced mainly comb honey, and wrote practical books such as The Joys of Beekeeping. He sold honey from a roadside stand that ran on an honour system. In later years he defended an ethics of personal excellence drawn from the ancient Greeks, in Virtue Ethics (1991) and Restoring Pride (1996). He died at home in Trumansburg, New York, in 2003."],"timeline":[[1919,"Born in Charlotte, Michigan"],[1951,"Completes his PhD at Brown under Roderick Chisholm"],[1962,"Publishes the article 'Fatalism'"],[1963,"Publishes Metaphysics"],[1965,"Joins the University of Rochester"],[1970,"Publishes Good and Evil"],[1996,"Publishes Restoring Pride"],[2003,"Dies in Trumansburg, New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Meaning comes from within","body":"In the myth, Sisyphus is condemned to roll a stone up a hill forever. Taylor asks us to imagine the gods giving Sisyphus a deep desire to roll stones: his endless task would then be meaningful to him. Taylor concluded that the meaning of life lies in our own will and the activities we care about, not in some lasting outcome."},{"head":"The fatalist argument","body":"Taylor set out an argument that whatever will happen was already true beforehand, so nothing we do can make a difference to it. He pointed out that most people accept that we cannot change the past, and challenged them to find a real difference that keeps the same reasoning from applying to the future."},{"head":"A theory of agency","body":"In Metaphysics Taylor argued that both strict determinism and the idea of uncaused choices fail to fit how we experience acting. Like Chisholm, he leaned towards the view that agents themselves, not events inside them, are the source of their actions, while admitting this view is puzzling."},{"head":"An ethics of aspiration","body":"Late in life Taylor argued that modern ethics, with its focus on duty and rules, has lost the ancient Greek ideal of excellence. He defended pride, in the sense of justified self-respect based on real achievement, as a virtue rather than a sin."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the chapter 'The Meaning of Life' from Good and Evil (1970), a short, vivid essay built around the myth of Sisyphus that is reprinted in many anthologies.","critics":"Susan Wolf argued that subjective satisfaction is not enough for a meaningful life: a Sisyphus made to love pointless stone-rolling would still be living a pointless life. Meaning, she argues, needs engagement with something of real, objective worth.","legacy":"Taylor's Metaphysics introduced generations of students to fate, freedom and God, and his Sisyphus essay remains a standard reading on the meaning of life.","quote":null},"hare":{"life":["Richard Mervyn Hare was born in 1919 in Backwell, Somerset, in England. He studied classics and philosophy at Balliol College, Oxford, but the Second World War interrupted his studies. He volunteered for the Royal Artillery and was captured by the Japanese at the fall of Singapore in 1942, remaining a prisoner until the war ended. He returned to an Oxford where 'ordinary language' philosophy, the close study of how words are used, was dominant.","Hare was a fellow and tutor at Balliol from 1947 to 1966, then White's Professor of Moral Philosophy at Oxford from 1966 to 1983. The Language of Morals (1952) set out his theory that moral judgements are a kind of instruction. Freedom and Reason (1963) added that they must be universalisable, and Moral Thinking (1981) built these ideas into a form of utilitarianism. After Oxford he taught at the University of Florida until 1994.","Hare believed moral philosophy should help people reason about real problems, and he wrote widely on practical questions, collected in books such as Essays on Political Morality (1989) and Essays on Bioethics (1993). While a prisoner of war he wrote a manuscript on ethics, which was never published. Among his students was Peter Singer, who developed his own version of Hare's preference utilitarianism. Hare died in Ewelme, Oxfordshire, in 2002."],"timeline":[[1919,"Born in Backwell, Somerset"],[1942,"Captured by the Japanese at the fall of Singapore"],[1947,"Becomes fellow and tutor at Balliol College, Oxford"],[1952,"Publishes The Language of Morals"],[1963,"Publishes Freedom and Reason"],[1966,"Becomes White's Professor of Moral Philosophy at Oxford"],[1981,"Publishes Moral Thinking"],[2002,"Dies in Ewelme, Oxfordshire"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Moral judgements prescribe","body":"When you say 'you ought to tell the truth', Hare argued, you are not mainly describing a fact. You are telling someone what to do, much as a command does. This is why moral words guide action: anyone who sincerely accepts 'I ought to do this' and then does not do it, when able, does not really accept it."},{"head":"Universalisability","body":"If I say I ought to do something in a situation, I am committed to saying anyone in exactly the same situation ought to do it too. If I think it is fine for me to jump the queue, I must accept others jumping it in the same circumstances. Moral words cannot be used to make special exceptions for myself."},{"head":"Putting yourself in others' shoes","body":"Combining these two ideas, Hare argued that to make a moral judgement I must imagine myself in the position of everyone affected and ask what I would accept if I were them. Doing this properly means giving equal weight to everyone's preferences, which leads to a form of utilitarianism."},{"head":"Two levels of moral thinking","body":"In daily life we should follow simple, well-chosen rules, such as keeping promises, because we lack time and are prone to bias. At the 'critical' level, an ideal thinker he called the 'archangel', with full knowledge and no bias, would test those rules and settle conflicts between them. Real people are partly archangels and partly 'proles' who must rely on rules."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Because moral judgements are like commands, Hare thought morality is just personal preference and anything goes.","truth":"Hare argued that the logic of moral words, especially universalisability, places strong limits on moral reasoning, so that careful reasoners would be pushed towards the same answers. He saw himself as defending rational moral argument, not undermining it."},"startHere":"Read Part I of The Language of Morals (1952), which explains clearly why moral language works more like instruction than description.","critics":"Philippa Foot argued that moral words have descriptive content that cannot be stretched at will: you cannot make any arbitrary behaviour morally wrong simply by prescribing against it. Bernard Williams and others objected that the two-level view asks people to follow rules while privately regarding them as mere tools for producing good results.","legacy":"Hare was one of the most influential moral philosophers of the twentieth century, and his mix of careful analysis of moral language with utilitarian reasoning shaped later work, notably that of Peter Singer.","quote":null},"smart":{"life":["John Jamieson Carswell Smart was born in 1920 in Cambridge, England, the son of an astronomer, and grew up in Scotland. He studied at the University of Glasgow and, after wartime service in the British Army, at Oxford under Gilbert Ryle. In 1950, aged 29, he became professor of philosophy at the University of Adelaide, and he spent the rest of his life in Australia, where he became central to a confident, science-minded philosophical culture.","At Adelaide his colleague U. T. Place persuaded him that sensations are brain processes, a view Smart had at first resisted. Smart's paper 'Sensations and Brain Processes' (1959) set out this 'identity theory' and made it famous. In ethics he defended act utilitarianism in An Outline of a Utilitarian System of Ethics (1961), later printed with Bernard Williams's reply as Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973). He moved to La Trobe University in 1972 and the Australian National University in 1976.","Smart wanted philosophy to fit the picture of the world given by physics. His Philosophy and Scientific Realism (1963) defended the view that the world is made up of the things physics describes. He also wrote on time, defending the 'block universe' view on which past, present and future are equally real. With Place and D. M. Armstrong he was a founding figure of what came to be called Australian materialism. He died in Melbourne in 2012."],"timeline":[[1920,"Born in Cambridge, England"],[1950,"Becomes professor of philosophy at the University of Adelaide"],[1956,"Publishes 'Extreme and Restricted Utilitarianism'"],[1959,"Publishes 'Sensations and Brain Processes'"],[1961,"Publishes An Outline of a Utilitarian System of Ethics"],[1963,"Publishes Philosophy and Scientific Realism"],[1973,"Publishes Utilitarianism: For and Against with Bernard Williams"],[2012,"Dies in Melbourne"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Sensations are brain processes","body":"Smart argued that a sensation, such as a pain or an after-image, simply is a process in the brain. This is a scientific discovery, like finding that lightning is an electrical discharge. Since science explains everything else physically, it would be strange to leave sensations as extra, non-physical 'danglers'."},{"head":"Act utilitarianism","body":"The right action is the one that produces the most happiness overall. Rules such as 'keep your promises' are useful rules of thumb, because they usually lead to good results and save time."},{"head":"Against rule worship","body":"If, in a particular case, breaking a rule would clearly do more good than keeping it, Smart argued that insisting on the rule anyway is 'rule worship': treating the rule as sacred when its whole point was to produce good results."},{"head":"The block universe","body":"Smart argued that time is in many ways like space. Just as 'here' only marks where the speaker is, 'now' only marks when the speaker is. All times are equally real, and the sense that time 'flows' comes from our point of view, not from the world itself."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The identity theory claims that the word 'pain' means the same as some description of brain activity.","truth":"Smart insisted the two expressions differ in meaning; his claim was that they turn out to refer to the same thing, as 'the morning star' and 'the evening star' both turned out to name the planet Venus."},"startHere":"Read 'Sensations and Brain Processes' (1959), a short and clearly written paper that sets out the identity theory and answers objections one by one.","critics":"In Utilitarianism: For and Against, Bernard Williams argued that act utilitarianism ignores personal integrity, since it could require someone to do something terrible whenever that would lead to slightly better results. In philosophy of mind, Hilary Putnam argued that pain cannot be one kind of brain process, because creatures with very different brains could also feel pain.","legacy":"Smart, with Place and Armstrong, made the mind-brain identity theory a central option in philosophy of mind, and his debate with Williams remains a standard way of teaching utilitarianism.","quote":null},"alston":{"life":["William Payne Alston was born in 1921 in Shreveport, Louisiana. He first trained as a musician, earning a degree in piano from Centenary College of Louisiana in 1942, and during the Second World War he played in a US Army band. He came to philosophy at a time when many philosophers regarded religious belief as either meaningless or plainly irrational, a view he spent much of his later career challenging.","He earned his PhD at the University of Chicago in 1951 with a thesis on Whitehead. He taught at the University of Michigan from 1949 to 1971, then at Rutgers, the University of Illinois and, from 1980 to 1992, Syracuse University. His early work was in the philosophy of language, including Philosophy of Language (1964). Later he turned to the theory of knowledge and philosophy of religion, above all in Perceiving God (1991).","Alston returned to Christian faith in mid-life and became a leader in the revival of philosophy of religion among analytic philosophers. He was a co-founder and president of the Society of Christian Philosophers and helped found its journal, Faith and Philosophy. He also wrote major works on what makes beliefs justified and on truth, defending a realist view in A Realist Conception of Truth (1996). He died in 2009."],"timeline":[[1921,"Born in Shreveport, Louisiana"],[1951,"Completes his PhD at the University of Chicago"],[1964,"Publishes Philosophy of Language"],[1980,"Moves to Syracuse University"],[1991,"Publishes Perceiving God"],[1993,"Publishes The Reliability of Sense Perception"],[1996,"Publishes A Realist Conception of Truth"],[2009,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Experiencing God as perception","body":"Many people report that God has been directly present to them, comforting or guiding them. Alston argued that such experiences have the same basic structure as sense perception: something appears to a person. So they can justify beliefs like 'God is comforting me', just as seeing a tree justifies 'there is a tree'."},{"head":"Belief-forming practices","body":"We form beliefs through established practices, such as sense perception, memory and reasoning. Alston argued that Christian religious experience, as interpreted within a community, is another such practice. It is reasonable to trust a well-established practice unless we have good reason to think it unreliable."},{"head":"No escape from circularity","body":"Any attempt to prove that sense perception is reliable ends up relying on sense perception, for example by checking what we see with what we touch. Alston argued that this kind of circularity affects all our basic ways of forming beliefs, so religious experience should not be held to a stricter standard."},{"head":"Our limited view of evil","body":"Replying to William Rowe, Alston argued in 1991 that human understanding is so limited that we are in no position to conclude that God could have no good reason for allowing a particular evil. Not seeing a reason is not good evidence that there is none."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Begin with the opening chapter of Perceiving God (1991), which sets out the comparison between sense perception and experience of God before the more technical parts of the book.","critics":"Critics such as Richard Gale and Michael Martin argued that experience of God lacks the cross-checks that sense perception has, since others cannot test your experience by looking. Alston himself treated the variety of world religions, each with its own practice of religious experience, as the most serious difficulty for his view.","legacy":"With Alvin Plantinga and Nicholas Wolterstorff, Alston helped make the rationality of religious belief a serious topic in analytic philosophy again, and his work on justification remains important in the theory of knowledge.","quote":null},"nyerere":{"life":["Julius Kambarage Nyerere was born around 1922 in Butiama, in what was then the British-ruled territory of Tanganyika in East Africa. His father was a chief of the small Zanaki people. Nyerere grew up as colonial rule was being challenged across Africa and Asia, and as newly independent nations debated whether to follow Western capitalism, Soviet communism or a path of their own.","He trained as a teacher at Makerere College in Uganda and later studied at the University of Edinburgh, graduating in 1952. In 1954 he founded the Tanganyika African National Union (TANU), which led the country peacefully to independence in 1961. He became prime minister, then president from 1962, and in 1964 Tanganyika united with Zanzibar to form Tanzania. The Arusha Declaration of 1967 committed the country to 'ujamaa' socialism. He stepped down as president in 1985.","Tanzanians called him Mwalimu, Swahili for 'teacher', his first profession. He explained his policies in plainly written essays and speeches, and translated two of Shakespeare's plays, Julius Caesar and The Merchant of Venice, into Swahili. He promoted Swahili as the national language, which helped unite a country of more than a hundred ethnic groups. He died in London in 1999."],"timeline":[[1922,"Born in Butiama, Tanganyika (c.)"],[1954,"Founds the Tanganyika African National Union (TANU)"],[1961,"Tanganyika becomes independent; Nyerere becomes prime minister"],[1962,"Becomes president; publishes 'Ujamaa: The Basis of African Socialism'"],[1964,"Tanganyika and Zanzibar unite to form Tanzania"],[1967,"The Arusha Declaration commits Tanzania to ujamaa"],[1985,"Steps down as president"],[1999,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ujamaa: socialism as familyhood","body":"Ujamaa is Swahili for 'familyhood'. Nyerere argued that traditional African societies already lived by socialist values: everyone worked, the community shared what it had, and no one hoarded wealth while others went hungry. Socialism, for him, was first an attitude of mind, like the care members of an extended family show each other."},{"head":"Socialism without class war","body":"Nyerere rejected the Marxist idea that socialism must come from struggle between classes. He argued that Africa had no class history like Europe's, and that the real danger was new individualism and greed brought by colonial capitalism."},{"head":"Self-reliance","body":"The Arusha Declaration argued that Tanzania should develop through its people's own work and land, not by depending on money and foreign aid. It also set a code forbidding political leaders from holding company shares or renting out houses, so that leaders could not grow rich from their positions."},{"head":"Education for self-reliance","body":"Nyerere argued that schools inherited from colonial rule trained a small elite for office jobs. Instead, education should prepare most young people for life and cooperative work in their own villages, and teach them to serve the community."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Nyerere's ujamaa was simply Soviet-style Marxism transplanted to Africa.","truth":"Nyerere explicitly rejected class struggle and Marxist 'scientific socialism' as the basis of his policy, grounding it instead in values he found in African family life, although his government did nationalise banks and major industries after 1967."},"startHere":"Read the short essay 'Ujamaa: The Basis of African Socialism' (1962), only a few pages long, which sets out his core idea in everyday language.","critics":"Economists and historians point out that Tanzania's economy struggled under ujamaa and that the villagisation campaign of the 1970s often moved people by force; James C. Scott used it as a case study of failed top-down planning in Seeing Like a State (1998). Others question whether pre-colonial African societies were as equal as Nyerere described.","legacy":"Nyerere is remembered as a founder of African socialism and pan-African politics and for building a stable national identity in Tanzania; his essays remain central texts in African political philosophy.","quote":null},"hick":{"life":["John Hick was born in 1922 in Scarborough, Yorkshire. He began studying law at Hull but, after an evangelical conversion as a student, turned towards theology. During the Second World War he was a conscientious objector and served with the Friends' Ambulance Unit. His career unfolded in an increasingly multi-faith Britain and America, where questions about how different religions relate to each other became pressing.","He studied philosophy at Edinburgh and earned his doctorate at Oxford in 1950, then served for a few years as a Presbyterian minister in Northumberland. He taught at Cornell, Princeton Theological Seminary and Cambridge, then was a professor at Birmingham from 1967 to 1982 and at Claremont Graduate University in California from 1979 to 1992. Faith and Knowledge (1957) and Evil and the God of Love (1966) made his name; his Gifford Lectures became An Interpretation of Religion (1989).","Hick's thinking moved over time from evangelical conservatism to a pluralist view of the world's religions. His views drew heresy proceedings more than once, including a dispute in the early 1960s over his refusal to affirm the virgin birth when he joined a US Presbyterian body; the church's judicial committee ruled in his favour. In Birmingham he worked with multi-faith groups and campaigned against racism. He edited the much-debated collection The Myth of God Incarnate (1977), and died in Birmingham in 2012."],"timeline":[[1922,"Born in Scarborough, Yorkshire"],[1950,"Completes his doctorate at Oxford"],[1957,"Publishes Faith and Knowledge"],[1966,"Publishes Evil and the God of Love"],[1967,"Becomes a professor at the University of Birmingham"],[1977,"Edits The Myth of God Incarnate"],[1989,"Publishes An Interpretation of Religion, based on his Gifford Lectures"],[2012,"Dies in Birmingham"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Soul-making","body":"Drawing on the early Christian writer Irenaeus, Hick argued that the world is not meant to be a paradise but a place where people can grow into moral and spiritual maturity. Courage needs real danger, and compassion needs real suffering. A world without pain would be a world where no one could develop these virtues."},{"head":"Epistemic distance","body":"Hick argued that God keeps a certain distance, so that the world can be read either religiously or not. If God's existence were obvious, people would be overwhelmed and could not freely come to faith. The world's ambiguity leaves room for a free response."},{"head":"Faith as 'experiencing-as'","body":"Just as a drawing can be seen as a duck or as a rabbit, Hick argued that life as a whole can be experienced as an encounter with God or not. Faith is this way of interpreting experience, not belief in a theory without evidence."},{"head":"Checking after death","body":"Philosophers influenced by logical positivism claimed religious statements are meaningless because nothing could confirm them. Hick replied with a parable of two travellers on one road: one believes it leads to a heavenly city, the other does not. They cannot settle it during the journey, but when they turn the last corner, one will be proved right. So belief in God could in principle be confirmed after death."},{"head":"Religious pluralism","body":"Hick proposed that the great world religions are different, culturally shaped responses to one ultimate reality, which he called 'the Real'. Like Kant's distinction between things as they are and things as they appear to us, the Real is experienced as the personal God of Christianity, as Allah, or as non-personal Brahman or Nirvana."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hick claimed all religions are basically the same.","truth":"He held that religions differ deeply in their teachings and practices, but that the great traditions are different human responses, shaped by culture, to the same ultimate reality."},"startHere":"Read the chapter on the problem of evil in his short textbook Philosophy of Religion, which gives the soul-making view in a few clear pages, before trying Evil and the God of Love.","critics":"D. Z. Phillips and others objected that treating suffering as a tool for moral growth is itself morally troubling, and that the sheer amount of suffering, such as that of young children or in the Holocaust, far exceeds anything soul-making could require. Against his pluralism, critics such as Alvin Plantinga argue that the religions make conflicting claims that cannot all be true.","legacy":"Hick's soul-making view is one of the standard responses to the problem of evil, and he set the terms of modern debate about religious pluralism.","quote":null},"sibley":{"life":["Frank Noel Sibley was born in London in 1923. He belonged to the generation of British philosophers trained at Oxford just after the Second World War, when 'ordinary language' philosophy, which looks closely at how we actually use words, was at its height. Aesthetics, the philosophy of art and beauty, was then a neglected subject in English-speaking philosophy, and Sibley helped bring the same careful methods to how we talk about art.","He studied at Oxford, where Gilbert Ryle was a leading influence (his first paper was a review of Ryle's The Concept of Mind), and then taught at Cornell University in the United States, where his best-known paper, 'Aesthetic Concepts', appeared in the Philosophical Review in 1959. In the 1960s he moved to the newly founded Lancaster University as its first professor of philosophy, and stayed there for more than three decades. Further papers, including 'Aesthetic and Non-aesthetic' (1965) and 'Objectivity and Aesthetics' (1968), developed his views.","Sibley published relatively little, almost all of it carefully argued journal articles, and he never brought out a book in his lifetime. He died in 1996. His papers were gathered after his death in Approach to Aesthetics (2001), which has kept his work in wide use."],"timeline":[[1923,"Born in London"],[1955,"Publishes 'Seeking, Scrutinizing and Seeing' in Mind"],[1959,"Publishes 'Aesthetic Concepts'"],[1965,"Publishes 'Aesthetic and Non-aesthetic'"],[1968,"Publishes 'Objectivity and Aesthetics'"],[1996,"Dies"],[2001,"His collected papers appear as Approach to Aesthetics"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Seeing aesthetic qualities takes taste","body":"Anyone with normal eyesight can see that a painting has curved lines and pale colours. But to see that it is graceful, delicate or garish takes something more, which Sibley called taste or sensitivity. Aesthetic words like these pick out qualities that not everyone notices."},{"head":"No rules guarantee beauty","body":"Sibley argued that no list of plain, non-aesthetic features guarantees an aesthetic quality. Pale colours and soft curves may make one picture delicate, but in another picture the same features might make it weak or insipid. Such features can count towards a quality, but never settle it."},{"head":"Dependence without rules","body":"Even so, aesthetic qualities depend on non-aesthetic ones: change the lines and colours, and the grace may vanish. The dependence is real but cannot be captured in rules, which is why aesthetic judgement cannot be done by checklist."},{"head":"Criticism as getting others to see","body":"Critics cannot prove their judgements by logic. Instead they point to features, compare works, use metaphors and even gestures, to help others see the quality for themselves. Sibley thought this is a real and rational activity, even without proofs."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Aesthetic Concepts' (1959): its opening sections, full of everyday examples of words like 'delicate' and 'garish', are easy to follow and set out the whole idea.","critics":"Ted Cohen argued in 1973 that Sibley's line between aesthetic and non-aesthetic concepts cannot be drawn clearly, since many words seem to fall on both sides of it. Others questioned whether aesthetic words really lack any conditions for their correct use.","legacy":"Sibley's distinction between aesthetic and non-aesthetic qualities set the terms for later debates about aesthetic properties, taste and criticism in analytic aesthetics.","quote":null},"flew":{"life":["Antony Flew was born in London in 1923, the son of a Methodist minister. During the Second World War he learned Japanese and worked in Royal Air Force intelligence. He studied at Oxford just after the war, when Gilbert Ryle and 'ordinary language' philosophy were dominant, and when the logical positivists' challenge, that religious claims might be meaningless because nothing could ever count against them, was widely discussed.","His short paper 'Theology and Falsification' (1950) became one of the most reprinted philosophical texts of the century. He taught at Oxford, Aberdeen, Keele (1954–1971) and Reading (1973–1982), writing on Hume, philosophy of mind and social questions. God and Philosophy (1966) and The Presumption of Atheism (1976) made him a leading voice of atheism. In 2004 he announced that he had come to believe in God: a creator who does not intervene in the world.","Flew was a lively public debater on religion, education and politics, and a defender of free-market liberalism. He also wrote a popular guide to clear reasoning, Thinking about Thinking (1975). His late book There Is a God (2007), written with Roy Abraham Varghese, caused controversy when a journalist reported that much of it had been drafted by Varghese, though Flew said the views in it were his own. He died in Reading in 2010."],"timeline":[[1923,"Born in London"],[1950,"Presents 'Theology and Falsification'"],[1955,"Co-edits New Essays in Philosophical Theology with Alasdair MacIntyre"],[1966,"Publishes God and Philosophy"],[1976,"Publishes The Presumption of Atheism"],[2004,"Announces that he now believes in a creator God"],[2007,"Publishes There Is a God with Roy Abraham Varghese"],[2010,"Dies in Reading"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The invisible gardener","body":"Two explorers find a clearing with flowers and weeds. One says a gardener tends it; they watch, set up fences and dogs, but no gardener ever appears. The believer keeps adding qualifications: the gardener is invisible, silent, cannot be felt. Flew asked how such a gardener differs from no gardener at all."},{"head":"Death by a thousand qualifications","body":"Flew's point was that a claim which rules nothing out says nothing. If believers keep saying 'God loves us' whatever happens, even in terrible suffering, he asked what would ever count against it. A statement compatible with every possible event seems empty."},{"head":"The presumption of atheism","body":"Just as a court presumes innocence until guilt is proved, Flew argued that discussion about God should start from not believing, until good reasons are given. Here 'atheist' simply means someone without belief in God, so the burden of proof lies with the believer."},{"head":"The 'No true Scotsman' move","body":"Flew named a common bad argument. Someone says no Scotsman puts sugar on his porridge; shown a Scotsman who does, they reply that no true Scotsman would. Redefining a group to exclude every counterexample protects a claim only by emptying it."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Flew converted to Christianity at the end of his life.","truth":"He became a deist: he accepted a creator God, something like Aristotle's God, but continued to reject divine revelation, miracles and life after death."},"startHere":"Read 'Theology and Falsification' (1950): only a few pages long, it opens with the parable of the invisible gardener.","critics":"In the same discussion, R. M. Hare replied that religious beliefs are not hypotheses but 'bliks', basic ways of seeing the world, and Basil Mitchell argued that believers do admit evidence against their faith but may reasonably keep trusting, as a resistance fighter might trust a mysterious stranger despite his puzzling behaviour.","legacy":"Flew's falsification challenge shaped decades of debate about whether religious language is meaningful, and his 'presumption of atheism' and 'No true Scotsman' remain familiar tools in arguments about belief and reasoning.","quote":null},"danto":{"life":["Arthur Coleman Danto was born in 1924 in Ann Arbor, Michigan, and grew up in Detroit. He served in the US Army during the Second World War, in North Africa and Italy. He worked as a philosopher in New York in the decades when it was the centre of the art world, and when artists such as Andy Warhol made works that looked exactly like ordinary objects, raising the question of what makes something art at all.","After studying at Wayne State University, he earned his PhD at Columbia, where he taught from 1951 until he retired in 1992. Early books dealt with history, action and knowledge, including Analytical Philosophy of History (1965) and Nietzsche as Philosopher (1965). Seeing Warhol's Brillo Box at a 1964 exhibition led to his paper 'The Artworld' (1964) and later to The Transfiguration of the Commonplace (1981). From 1984 to 2009 he was art critic for The Nation magazine.","Alongside his early philosophy career Danto was also an artist, making woodcut prints through the 1950s. This gave him an unusual double career: he wrote technical philosophy for academics and readable criticism for the public, reviewing exhibitions for decades. His Mellon Lectures were published as After the End of Art (1997). He died in New York in 2013."],"timeline":[[1924,"Born in Ann Arbor, Michigan"],[1951,"Begins teaching at Columbia University"],[1964,"Publishes 'The Artworld' after seeing Warhol's Brillo Box"],[1965,"Publishes Analytical Philosophy of History"],[1981,"Publishes The Transfiguration of the Commonplace"],[1984,"Becomes art critic for The Nation"],[1997,"Publishes After the End of Art"],[2013,"Dies in New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Identical-looking objects","body":"Warhol's Brillo Box looks just like the cartons of Brillo soap pads in a supermarket, yet one is art and the other is not. Danto concluded that what makes something art cannot be anything you can see. His method was to imagine pairs of identical-looking objects, only one of which is art, and ask what the difference is."},{"head":"The artworld","body":"To see something as art, Danto argued, requires an 'atmosphere' of art theory and a knowledge of the history of art. Warhol's Brillo Box could not have been art in 1600, because the ideas that let it make a point about art did not yet exist."},{"head":"Art as embodied meaning","body":"In his later view, an artwork is about something and embodies that meaning in its material form. Understanding a work means interpreting what it is about and how its form expresses that, which is why art always calls for interpretation."},{"head":"The end of art","body":"Danto argued that the history of Western art had told a story with a direction: first towards more accurate representation, then towards discovering what art itself is. With works like Brillo Box that story ended, and art entered a 'post-historical' period in which any style is allowed."},{"head":"Narrative sentences","body":"In his philosophy of history Danto noticed sentences like 'The Thirty Years' War began in 1618'. They describe an event in terms of later events that no one at the time could know. This shows why historians can say true things about the past that no eyewitness could have said."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Danto said art had come to an end and no more real art would be made.","truth":"He meant that a particular historical story about art's progress had ended, not that art would stop. He thought artists were now free to work in any style, with no single direction art had to follow."},"startHere":"Read 'The Artworld' (1964), a short essay in which he uses Warhol's Brillo Box to ask what makes something art.","critics":"A common objection is that 'being about something and embodying that meaning' also fits many things that are not art, such as advertisements, flags or book covers, so it does not on its own mark out art. Others doubt that art history ever had the single direction his 'end of art' story requires.","legacy":"Danto's question of what separates an artwork from an identical-looking ordinary object became a central puzzle of the philosophy of art, and his criticism brought philosophical ideas into public discussion of contemporary art.","quote":null},"salmon":{"life":["Wesley Charles Salmon was born in 1925 in Detroit, Michigan. He worked in the philosophy of science in the decades after the Second World War, when the leading account of scientific explanation was Carl Hempel's: to explain an event is to show that it follows from laws of nature, or is made highly probable by them. Salmon became the most influential critic of this view and offered an alternative based on causation.","He studied at Wayne University in Detroit and the University of Chicago (MA 1947), and earned his PhD at UCLA in 1950 under Hans Reichenbach. He taught at Brown, Indiana University, the University of Arizona (1973–1981) and the University of Pittsburgh, where in 1983 he succeeded Hempel as University Professor. His books include Statistical Explanation and Statistical Relevance (1971) and Scientific Explanation and the Causal Structure of the World (1984).","Salmon was also a gifted teacher of reasoning. His short textbook Logic (1963) and his edited collection Zeno's Paradoxes (1970) brought difficult topics to students, and his long survey Four Decades of Scientific Explanation (1989) remains a standard guide to the debate he helped shape. He retired from Pittsburgh in 1999 and was killed in a car accident in Ohio in 2001."],"timeline":[[1925,"Born in Detroit, Michigan"],[1950,"Completes his PhD at UCLA under Hans Reichenbach"],[1967,"Publishes The Foundations of Scientific Inference"],[1971,"Publishes Statistical Explanation and Statistical Relevance"],[1981,"Moves to the University of Pittsburgh"],[1984,"Publishes Scientific Explanation and the Causal Structure of the World"],[1998,"Publishes Causality and Explanation"],[2001,"Dies in a car accident"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Explaining is not predicting","body":"On Hempel's view, a good explanation shows the event was to be expected. Salmon gave a famous counterexample: a man who takes birth control pills does not become pregnant. The pills make his not becoming pregnant certain, yet they explain nothing, because they are irrelevant to men."},{"head":"Statistical relevance","body":"Salmon argued that what matters for explanation is not high probability but relevance: the factors that change the probability of an event. A treatment can explain a recovery even if it raised the chance of recovery only from low to moderate."},{"head":"Causal processes and pseudo-processes","body":"A moving car is a real causal process; its shadow racing along beside it is a 'pseudo-process'. Dent the car and the dent travels with it; 'mark' the shadow by passing it over a wall and the change does not carry on. Real causal processes can carry such marks, or, in his later view, conserved quantities such as energy."},{"head":"Explaining shows how the world works","body":"For Salmon, to explain something is to show the causal mechanisms that produce it: the processes and interactions that lead up to the event. Understanding comes from seeing how the world's machinery operates, not just from fitting an event under a general law."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his chapter 'Scientific Explanation' in the textbook Introduction to the Philosophy of Science (1992), written for students, which surveys Hempel's view, the main counterexamples and Salmon's own causal approach.","critics":"Philip Kitcher defended a rival view on which explanation works by unifying many facts under a few patterns of argument, rather than by tracing causes. Others argue that causal-process accounts struggle with explanations by absences, such as a plant dying because no one watered it, where no process carries anything from cause to effect.","legacy":"Salmon helped move the philosophy of science from the logical form of explanations to causes and mechanisms, and today's mechanism-based accounts of explanation in biology and neuroscience build on his work.","quote":null},"armstrong":{"life":["David Malet Armstrong was born in Melbourne in 1926. He studied at the University of Sydney under John Anderson, a Scottish-born philosopher known for a hard-headed realism holding that there is only one world, the world of space and time. After further study at Oxford he returned to Australia, where he became, with J. J. C. Smart, a leader of a distinctive Australian school of materialist and realist philosophy.","He taught at Birkbeck College in London and the University of Melbourne before holding the Challis Chair of Philosophy at the University of Sydney from 1959 to 1991. A Materialist Theory of the Mind (1968) argued that mental states are brain states. Universals and Scientific Realism (1978) and What Is a Law of Nature? (1983) built a systematic metaphysics, continued in A World of States of Affairs (1997) and Truth and Truthmakers (2004).","Armstrong's Sydney years included a bitter dispute in the early 1970s over the teaching of Marxism and feminism, which led to the philosophy department splitting in two in 1973; he was on the traditionalist side. Philosophically he was known for building a complete system rather than tackling isolated puzzles, and for writing in plain, direct prose. He died in 2014."],"timeline":[[1926,"Born in Melbourne"],[1959,"Takes up the Challis Chair of Philosophy at the University of Sydney"],[1968,"Publishes A Materialist Theory of the Mind"],[1973,"The Sydney philosophy department splits in two"],[1978,"Publishes Universals and Scientific Realism"],[1983,"Publishes What Is a Law of Nature?"],[1997,"Publishes A World of States of Affairs"],[2014,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The mind is the brain","body":"Armstrong defined a mental state as an inner state of a person apt to produce certain behaviour. Thirst, for example, is the inner state that typically causes drinking. Science then tells us what fills that role, and it turns out to be states of the brain."},{"head":"Real properties in things","body":"Armstrong held that properties such as having a certain mass or charge are 'universals': one and the same property is wholly present in each thing that has it. But unlike Plato he held that no universal exists apart from things, and that science, not language, tells us which universals there are."},{"head":"Laws of nature as links between properties","body":"A law is not just a pattern that happens to hold. It is a real relation of 'necessitation' between universals: being copper necessitates conducting electricity. This explains why laws support predictions, while accidental patterns, such as every coin in my pocket being silver, do not."},{"head":"Truthmakers","body":"Every truth, Armstrong argued, is made true by something in the world. The truth 'this apple is red' is made true by a state of affairs: the apple's having the property of redness. Asking 'what makes this true?' became a powerful tool in metaphysics."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his short book Universals: An Opinionated Introduction (1989), written for students, which compares the main theories of properties and argues for his own.","critics":"Bas van Fraassen, in Laws and Symmetry (1989), argued that Armstrong's 'necessitation' relation is mysterious: calling it necessitation does not explain why it guarantees regularities. David Lewis made a similar complaint, preferring a view on which laws summarise the patterns in the world.","legacy":"Armstrong's materialism about the mind and his theories of universals, laws and truthmakers helped revive systematic metaphysics in analytic philosophy, and made Australia known for it.","quote":null},"feinberg":{"life":["Joel Feinberg was born in Detroit in 1926. He worked in a period when English-speaking philosophers were returning to practical questions of law and politics, prompted by debates over civil rights, the Vietnam War, and the legal treatment of homosexuality, pornography and drugs. A central question was the one John Stuart Mill had posed: when may a state use the criminal law to limit people's freedom?","He studied at the University of Michigan, completing his PhD in 1957, and taught at Brown, Princeton, UCLA and Rockefeller University before moving to the University of Arizona in 1977, where he stayed until he retired in 1994. His essays on rights, responsibility and punishment were collected in Doing and Deserving (1970). His major work is the four-volume The Moral Limits of the Criminal Law (1984–1988).","Feinberg was known for clear, patient writing and for testing principles against many concrete cases, some invented and some drawn from real law. His anthology Reason and Responsibility, first published in 1965, introduced generations of students to philosophy. His essay 'The Nature and Value of Rights' (1970) and his work on the rights of animals and future generations were widely discussed. He died in Tucson in 2004."],"timeline":[[1926,"Born in Detroit, Michigan"],[1957,"Completes his PhD at the University of Michigan"],[1965,"Publishes the anthology Reason and Responsibility"],[1970,"Publishes Doing and Deserving and 'The Nature and Value of Rights'"],[1977,"Moves to the University of Arizona"],[1984,"Publishes Harm to Others, first volume of The Moral Limits of the Criminal Law"],[1986,"Publishes Harm to Self"],[2004,"Dies in Tucson, Arizona"]],"ideas":[{"head":"A sharper harm principle","body":"Following Mill, Feinberg held that the state may criminalise conduct that harms others. He defined harm as a wrongful setback to someone's interests. Winning a fair race sets back the other runners' interests, but does not wrong them, so it is not harm in this sense."},{"head":"The offence principle","body":"Feinberg argued that seriously offending people in public, for example by disgusting or shocking behaviour they cannot avoid, may also be restricted. But it must be weighed carefully: how intense the offence is, whether people could easily look away, and how valuable the offending conduct is. He used imagined scenes on a crowded bus, where passengers cannot leave, to test these limits."},{"head":"Soft paternalism","body":"The state may step in to check that a person's risky choice is truly voluntary, for instance by warning someone who seems unaware that a bridge is unsafe. But if the person understands the risk and still chooses it, Feinberg argued, the law should not stop them harming themselves."},{"head":"Why rights matter","body":"Feinberg imagined 'Nowheresville', a world where people are kind and dutiful but no one has rights. What is missing is the ability to claim what is owed to you, to stand up as an equal and demand it. Having rights, he argued, is closely linked to self-respect and human dignity."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'The Nature and Value of Rights' (1970), a short essay that imagines a world without rights, called Nowheresville, to show why being able to claim what is due to you matters.","critics":"Legal moralists, from Patrick Devlin to later writers such as Robert P. George, argue that the law may also enforce shared morality even where no one is harmed. Others question whether the harm and offence principles can settle hard cases, since so much depends on how 'harm' and 'wrong' are defined.","legacy":"The Moral Limits of the Criminal Law is the most detailed modern working-out of Mill's harm principle and a standard reference in legal philosophy and law teaching.","quote":null},"dickie":{"life":["George Dickie was born in 1926 in Palmetto, Florida. He served in the US Marine Corps from 1944 to 1946, then studied at Florida State University and at UCLA, where he earned his PhD in 1959. He worked when modern art was testing every boundary: Marcel Duchamp's urinal and Andy Warhol's Brillo boxes made it hard to say what makes something art, and philosophers such as Morris Weitz doubted that art could be defined at all.","Dickie taught at Washington State University and briefly at the University of Houston before joining the University of Illinois at Chicago in 1965, where he taught for thirty years. His paper 'The Myth of the Aesthetic Attitude' (1964) attacked a popular theory of how we experience art. In Art and the Aesthetic (1974) he set out his institutional theory of art, which he revised in The Art Circle (1984). He also wrote Evaluating Art (1988) and The Century of Taste (1996).","Dickie was willing to revise his own views in response to critics, as his reworking of the institutional theory shows. He served as president of the American Society for Aesthetics from 1993 to 1994. Colleagues remembered him as kind, good-humoured and generous with students. After retiring in 1995 he returned to Florida, where he died in 2020."],"timeline":[[1926,"Born in Palmetto, Florida"],[1959,"Completes his PhD at UCLA"],[1964,"Publishes 'The Myth of the Aesthetic Attitude'"],[1965,"Joins the University of Illinois at Chicago"],[1974,"Publishes Art and the Aesthetic, setting out the institutional theory"],[1984,"Publishes The Art Circle"],[1988,"Publishes Evaluating Art"],[2020,"Dies in Bradenton, Florida"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The institutional theory of art","body":"Dickie argued that what makes something art is not how it looks but its place in a social practice, the 'artworld'. A urinal in a hardware store is plumbing; the same kind of object, presented by an artist to the art public as Duchamp's Fountain was, becomes an artwork."},{"head":"The art circle","body":"In his revised theory, an artwork is something made to be presented to an art public, an artist is someone who takes part in making artworks, and so on. Dickie admitted that these definitions point to each other in a circle, but argued the circle is harmless, because we learn these ideas together, as a connected practice."},{"head":"No special aesthetic attitude","body":"Some theorists claimed that appreciating art requires a special mental state, such as 'disinterested' or 'distanced' attention. Dickie argued that there is no such special state, only paying attention or being distracted. A jealous husband watching Othello who thinks only of his own wife is not using a different attitude; he is simply not attending to the play."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The institutional theory says art is whatever powerful critics, museums or gallery owners declare it to be.","truth":"Dickie's 'artworld' is an informal practice that anyone can take part in, including an artist working alone; no official body confers art status, and in his later version no act of conferring is needed at all."},"startHere":"Read 'The Myth of the Aesthetic Attitude' (1964), a short and lively paper, and then the opening chapters of The Art Circle (1984) for his mature theory.","critics":"Critics pointed out that the theory is circular, and Richard Wollheim posed a dilemma: either the artworld has reasons for counting something as art, in which case those reasons, not the institution, make it art; or it has no reasons, in which case its verdicts are arbitrary.","legacy":"The institutional theory became one of the main modern definitions of art, and it shaped how philosophers, sociologists and art historians think about the social setting of art.","quote":null},"rothbard":{"life":["Murray Newton Rothbard was born in 1926 in the Bronx, New York City. He grew up during the New Deal and the Second World War, when the US federal government grew enormously, and his politics formed in opposition to that growth. From 1949 he attended the seminar of the Austrian economist Ludwig von Mises in New York, which shaped his economics for the rest of his life.","He studied mathematics and economics at Columbia and earned his PhD there in 1956. His treatise Man, Economy, and State (1962) set out Austrian economics, and America's Great Depression (1963) blamed the slump on government and central-bank policy. He taught for many years at the Polytechnic Institute in Brooklyn and, from 1986, at the University of Nevada, Las Vegas. He helped found the Cato Institute (1977) and the Ludwig von Mises Institute (1982).","Rothbard was a tireless organiser and writer who moved between political alliances in pursuit of libertarian goals: with the conservative 'Old Right' in the 1950s, with the anti-war New Left in the 1960s, in the Libertarian Party in the 1970s and with 'paleoconservatives' in the 1990s. His books For a New Liberty (1973) and The Ethics of Liberty (1982) set out the case for anarcho-capitalism. He died in New York in 1995."],"timeline":[[1926,"Born in the Bronx, New York City"],[1949,"Begins attending Ludwig von Mises's seminar"],[1956,"Completes his PhD at Columbia"],[1962,"Publishes Man, Economy, and State"],[1973,"Publishes For a New Liberty"],[1977,"Co-founds the Cato Institute"],[1982,"Publishes The Ethics of Liberty; helps found the Mises Institute"],[1995,"Dies in New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Self-ownership","body":"Rothbard's starting point is that each person owns their own body and life. No one else, not another person and not the state, has a rightful claim to control them."},{"head":"The non-aggression principle","body":"No one may start the use of force, or threaten force, against another person or their property. Defending yourself against aggression is allowed; starting it never is. Rothbard applied this one rule to every question of politics."},{"head":"The state as organised theft","body":"If a private gang took part of your income under threat of prison, we would call it theft. Rothbard argued that taxation is the same thing done by the state, and that governments get no moral exemption from the rules that bind everyone else."},{"head":"Anarcho-capitalism","body":"Rothbard argued that everything the state does, including police, courts and defence, could be supplied by competing private firms paid voluntarily, with disputes settled by private arbitration. The result would be a society of free markets with no state at all."},{"head":"Property by first use","body":"Following John Locke, Rothbard held that unowned land and resources become someone's property when they first put them to use, for example by farming empty land. After that, property can only change hands by gift or voluntary exchange."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first two chapters of For a New Liberty (1973), which set out self-ownership and the non-aggression principle in plain language for general readers.","critics":"Robert Nozick, in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), argued that a minimal state could arise from private protection agencies without violating anyone's rights, so anarchism is not required. Others argue that competing protection firms would become warring gangs or a new state, and many philosophers reject absolute self-ownership because it leaves no room for enforceable duties to help others.","legacy":"Rothbard is the main founder of modern anarcho-capitalism and of the radical wing of American libertarianism, and his ideas continue through the Mises Institute and libertarian movements worldwide.","quote":null},"christie":{"life":["Nils Christie was born in Oslo in 1928 and was a teenager during the German occupation of Norway in the Second World War. After the war the Scandinavian countries built generous welfare states, and many criminologists there asked whether prisons and punishment fitted with those humane ideals. Christie became one of the leading voices in this questioning, and one of the best-known criminologists of his time.","He worked as a journalist in the late 1940s and studied sociology at the University of Oslo. His master's thesis (1952) studied Norwegians who had served as guards in wartime camps for Yugoslav prisoners in Norway, asking how ordinary people came to treat others brutally. After a doctorate on young offenders (1959), he became Norway's first professor of criminology in 1966. 'Conflicts as Property' (1977), Limits to Pain (1981) and Crime Control as Industry (1993) brought him international attention.","Christie wrote in a plain, conversational style for the general public as well as for scholars, and often took part in Norwegian debates on prisons and drug policy. He criticised drug prohibition and the growth of imprisonment, especially in the United States. He preferred to speak of 'unwanted acts' rather than 'crime', to remind readers that what counts as crime is a social decision. He died in Oslo in 2015."],"timeline":[[1928,"Born in Oslo"],[1952,"Completes a study of Norwegian guards in wartime prison camps"],[1966,"Becomes Norway's first professor of criminology"],[1977,"Publishes 'Conflicts as Property'"],[1981,"Publishes Limits to Pain"],[1986,"Publishes 'The Ideal Victim'"],[1993,"Publishes Crime Control as Industry"],[2015,"Dies in Oslo"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Conflicts as property","body":"Christie argued that conflicts belong to the people involved in them, but the state and professionals such as lawyers have 'stolen' them. In a trial, the victim of a burglary is pushed to the side and may never meet the burglar. Both lose the chance to talk, understand and settle what happened, and the community loses a chance to discuss its values."},{"head":"Limits to pain","body":"Punishment, Christie said, is the deliberate delivery of pain, and we should call it that instead of hiding it behind technical language. Once we see it honestly, we should use as little of it as possible and prefer responses such as compensation, mediation and dialogue."},{"head":"Crime is not a natural thing","body":"Acts are not crimes in themselves; they become crimes when society chooses to treat them that way. If a family member takes money from your purse, you rarely call the police. The closer and more connected people are, the less likely they are to treat unwanted acts as crime."},{"head":"The ideal victim","body":"Some victims are readily recognised and sympathised with, such as an elderly woman robbed by a stranger on her way home. Others, such as a drunk man assaulted by an acquaintance, are much less likely to be seen as 'real' victims. Christie showed how this shapes public attitudes and justice."},{"head":"Crime control as an industry","body":"Christie warned that prisons and security can become an industry with its own interest in growth, always needing more 'raw material' in the form of prisoners. He pointed to rising imprisonment in the United States as a sign of where this could lead."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Conflicts as Property' (1977), a short lecture published as a journal article, which explains his core idea with everyday examples and is often treated as a founding text of restorative justice.","critics":"Critics, including 'left realist' criminologists such as Jock Young, argued that this approach can underplay the real harm crime does, especially to poor communities. Others worry that informal settlement between victim and offender may be unfair when the two are unequal in power, or unsuitable for serious violent crime.","legacy":"Christie's ideas helped inspire restorative justice and victim-offender mediation, and he remains a key reference for critics of mass imprisonment.","quote":null},"hirsch_ed":{"life":["Eric Donald Hirsch Jr. was born in 1928 in Memphis, Tennessee. He began as a scholar of English Romantic poetry at a time when American literary studies were dominated by the 'New Criticism', which told readers to focus on the text itself and set aside the author's intentions. Later, critics influenced by Roland Barthes and Jacques Derrida went further, questioning whether texts have any fixed meaning at all. Hirsch became a leading defender of the author.","He studied at Cornell and earned his PhD at Yale, where he taught English from 1956 to 1966 before moving to the University of Virginia. His early books dealt with Wordsworth and Blake. Validity in Interpretation (1967) and The Aims of Interpretation (1976) set out his theory of meaning. From the 1980s he turned to school education, publishing Cultural Literacy (1987) and founding the Core Knowledge Foundation.","Hirsch's educational work grew out of his research on reading. He argued that understanding a text depends heavily on background knowledge that writers assume their readers share, and that children from poorer homes are held back when schools do not teach it explicitly. The Core Knowledge curriculum, which sets out specific content for each school year, has been adopted by many American schools. He continued the argument in The Schools We Need (1996) and Why Knowledge Matters (2016)."],"timeline":[[1928,"Born in Memphis, Tennessee"],[1957,"Completes his PhD at Yale"],[1966,"Moves to the University of Virginia"],[1967,"Publishes Validity in Interpretation"],[1976,"Publishes The Aims of Interpretation"],[1987,"Publishes Cultural Literacy"],[1996,"Publishes The Schools We Need and Why We Don't Have Them"],[2016,"Publishes Why Knowledge Matters"]],"ideas":[{"head":"A text means what its author meant","body":"Hirsch argued that the meaning of a text is what its author intended to convey by those words. If we give that up, there is no standard for saying one reading is better than another, and interpretation becomes a matter of taste."},{"head":"Meaning versus significance","body":"Hirsch separated the fixed meaning of a text from its significance: how that meaning relates to something else, such as a reader, a period or a situation. A poem you read at fifteen and again at fifty means the same thing, but its significance for you may change completely."},{"head":"Interpretation as testing hypotheses","body":"We can never be certain what an author meant, Hirsch admitted. But interpretations can be tested against evidence, such as the author's other writings, the language of the time and the text as a whole, much as a detective tests theories. Some readings are more probable than others."},{"head":"Cultural literacy","body":"Writers constantly assume that readers know things they do not explain: historical events, famous phrases, basic science. Hirsch argued that reading well depends on this shared background, and he gave a list of some 5,000 items as a rough sketch of what literate Americans take for granted."},{"head":"Knowledge, not just skills","body":"Hirsch argued that reading comprehension is not a general skill that can be practised on any material. A child who knows a lot about baseball understands a report of a baseball game far better than a stronger reader who does not. So schools should teach a broad, ordered body of knowledge."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Cultural Literacy calls for children to memorise a list of disconnected facts.","truth":"Hirsch presented his list as a rough sketch of the background knowledge literate readers take for granted, and argued that it should be taught through a coherent, knowledge-rich curriculum, not drilled as isolated facts."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of Validity in Interpretation (1967), 'In Defense of the Author', which states his case against theories that dismiss the author directly and clearly.","critics":"Followers of Hans-Georg Gadamer argue that interpretation is always a meeting between the text and the reader's own situation, so there is no original meaning that stands apart from history; earlier, W. K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley had called reliance on the author's intention the 'intentional fallacy'. In education, critics argued that Hirsch's list reflected a narrow, mainly white and Western canon.","legacy":"Hirsch is the best-known defender of authorial intention in literary theory, and his Core Knowledge ideas have influenced curriculum reform in the United States and in England.","quote":null},"chomsky":{"life":["Avram Noam Chomsky was born in 1928 in Philadelphia. He studied linguistics at the University of Pennsylvania under Zellig Harris, at a time when American linguistics and psychology were dominated by behaviourism, the view that science should study only observable behaviour and that language is a set of learned habits. Chomsky's work challenged this view and helped start the 'cognitive revolution', which brought the mind back as a subject of science.","After a junior fellowship at Harvard, he joined the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1955 and remained there for over sixty years. Syntactic Structures (1957) introduced generative grammar, and his 1959 review of B. F. Skinner's Verbal Behavior became a landmark attack on behaviourism. Aspects of the Theory of Syntax (1965) and The Minimalist Program (1995) reshaped his theory over time. In 2017 he moved to the University of Arizona.","From the 1960s Chomsky also became one of the best-known critics of US foreign policy, beginning with his opposition to the Vietnam War in essays such as 'The Responsibility of Intellectuals' (1967). With Edward Herman he wrote Manufacturing Consent (1988), on how news media filter information. He has published dozens of political books alongside his technical linguistics, giving him an unusually large public audience."],"timeline":[[1928,"Born in Philadelphia"],[1955,"Joins the Massachusetts Institute of Technology"],[1957,"Publishes Syntactic Structures"],[1959,"Publishes his review of B. F. Skinner's Verbal Behavior"],[1965,"Publishes Aspects of the Theory of Syntax"],[1967,"Publishes 'The Responsibility of Intellectuals'"],[1988,"Publishes Manufacturing Consent with Edward Herman"],[1995,"Publishes The Minimalist Program"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Generative grammar","body":"A language lets us make and understand an unlimited number of new sentences. Chomsky argued that this requires a finite set of rules in the mind that can generate them all. His example 'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously' is grammatical but meaningless, showing that grammar is partly separate from meaning."},{"head":"The poverty of the stimulus","body":"Children learn the complex grammar of their language quickly, from limited and messy speech, and without being taught the rules. Chomsky argued that what they hear is too poor to explain what they come to know, so much of the structure must already be built into the human mind."},{"head":"Universal grammar","body":"Chomsky proposed that all humans share an inborn language faculty: general principles that limit what a human language can be. Experience then fixes the details, so a child raised in Tokyo learns Japanese and one raised in Lima learns Spanish."},{"head":"Competence and performance","body":"Chomsky separated a speaker's knowledge of their language (competence) from how they actually use it (performance), which is affected by memory limits, tiredness and slips of the tongue. Linguistics, he argued, should study the knowledge, not just the noisy behaviour."},{"head":"The mind-body problem dissolved","body":"Descartes could set mind against body because he had a clear idea of body as matter in contact. Since Newton introduced forces acting at a distance, Chomsky argues, there has been no fixed idea of 'physical'. So the old mind-body problem cannot even be stated clearly, and the mind should simply be studied like any other part of nature."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Chomsky claims babies are born knowing a particular language, such as English or Chinese.","truth":"He holds that children are born with a language faculty: general principles that shape what human languages can be. The particular language is learnt from what the child hears."},"startHere":"Read the opening lectures of Language and Problems of Knowledge (1988), talks he gave to a general audience in Nicaragua, which explain his main questions about language and mind without technical apparatus.","critics":"Usage-based linguists such as Michael Tomasello argue that children can learn language from general learning abilities and rich social interaction, without an inborn universal grammar. Daniel Everett has argued that the Amazonian language Pirahã lacks features, such as embedding clauses inside clauses, that Chomsky's theory treats as universal.","legacy":"Chomsky turned linguistics into a branch of cognitive science, and his ideas shaped psychology, philosophy of mind and computer science; his political writing also made him one of the most widely read intellectuals of his time.","quote":null},"noddings":{"life":["Nel Noddings was born in 1929 in Irvington, New Jersey. She came to philosophy through teaching rather than through a standard academic route. Her major work appeared in the 1980s, when feminist thinkers, including the psychologist Carol Gilligan, were arguing that traditional moral theory, built around rules, rights and justice, had overlooked ways of thinking about morality more often associated with women, such as attention to relationships and care.","Before her academic career Noddings spent many years as a schoolteacher, mostly teaching mathematics. She earned her PhD in philosophy of education at Stanford in 1973 and joined Stanford's faculty in 1977, staying until 1998. Caring: A Feminine Approach to Ethics and Moral Education (1984) made her name. Later books included The Challenge to Care in Schools (1992) and Starting at Home (2002), which applied care ethics to social policy.","Noddings wrote many books on ethics, education, happiness, peace and religion. She served as president of the Philosophy of Education Society, and her ideas were widely used in teacher training. Her writing stayed close to classroom life, asking what it would mean for schools to put caring relationships at their centre. She died in 2022."],"timeline":[[1929,"Born in Irvington, New Jersey"],[1973,"Completes her PhD at Stanford"],[1977,"Joins the Stanford faculty"],[1984,"Publishes Caring: A Feminine Approach to Ethics and Moral Education"],[1992,"Publishes The Challenge to Care in Schools"],[2002,"Publishes Starting at Home"],[2022,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ethics begins in caring relations","body":"Noddings argued that morality starts not with abstract principles but with relationships between a carer ('the one-caring') and the person cared for. The basic moral question is not 'what rule applies?' but 'how should I respond to this person in front of me?'"},{"head":"Attention and motivational shift","body":"When we truly care, we attend closely to the other person and feel our motivation shift towards their needs and projects. A teacher who notices a quiet student struggling, and puts her own plans aside to help, shows what Noddings meant."},{"head":"The cared-for completes the relation","body":"Caring is not finished just because the carer means well. The person cared for must receive and respond to the care, even by a smile or a sign of growth. Care that is not received, or is unwanted, has failed as care."},{"head":"Natural and ethical caring","body":"Natural caring is the care we give without effort, as a parent comforts a crying baby. Ethical caring is what we draw on when we do not feel like caring: we remember being cared for ourselves and choose to act from that ideal."},{"head":"Schools built around care","body":"Noddings argued that schools should organise learning around 'centres of care': care for oneself, for people near and far, for animals and plants, for the human-made world and for ideas. Continuity of teachers and students over years would help caring relationships grow."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Care ethics claims that women are naturally more moral than men.","truth":"Noddings called her approach 'feminine' because it drew on experiences traditionally associated with women, but she held that caring is a human capacity that men can and should develop."},"startHere":"Read the introduction and first chapter of Caring (1984), which explain the caring relation through everyday examples from family and school life.","critics":"Claudia Card and other feminist critics warned that an ethics centred on care risks endorsing self-sacrifice by women and has trouble with obligations to distant strangers; Noddings's early suggestion that our obligations to people far away whom we cannot meet are limited was widely criticised.","legacy":"Noddings was one of the founders of care ethics and its leading voice in education, and her work shaped how many teachers think about their relationships with students.","quote":null},"held":{"life":["Virginia Held was born in 1929 in Mendham, New Jersey. Her philosophical career unfolded alongside the women's movement of the 1960s and 1970s, which asked why moral and political theory had so little to say about family life, childcare and the work traditionally done by women. She became a central figure in feminist philosophy and in developing the ethics of care into a full moral theory.","She earned her PhD in philosophy at Columbia in 1968. She taught at Hunter College in New York from 1965 until she retired in 2001, and at the City University of New York Graduate Center, where she was named Distinguished Professor in 1996. Her books include The Public Interest and Individual Interests (1970), Rights and Goods, Feminist Morality (1993) and The Ethics of Care (2006).","Held worked across ethics and social and political philosophy, and wrote on topics many moral philosophers ignored, such as the role of the media in public life and the morality of political violence. In How Terrorism Is Wrong (2008) she argued that terrorism should be judged by the same standards we use for other political violence, including war. She served as president of the Eastern Division of the American Philosophical Association in 2001–2002, and died in 2026."],"timeline":[[1929,"Born in Mendham, New Jersey"],[1965,"Begins teaching at Hunter College, New York"],[1968,"Completes her PhD at Columbia"],[1970,"Publishes The Public Interest and Individual Interests"],[1993,"Publishes Feminist Morality"],[2006,"Publishes The Ethics of Care"],[2008,"Publishes How Terrorism Is Wrong"],[2026,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Care as practice and value","body":"For Held, care is both a practice, the real work of looking after people, and a value, a standard by which we judge relationships. Every one of us was cared for as a child, so care is not a special topic for some people but a basic part of all human life."},{"head":"Justice grows within care","body":"Held argued that care is more basic than justice: without care, no children would survive to become persons with rights. Justice matters greatly, but it works inside a wider web of caring relations. A family can be perfectly fair and still cold and uncaring."},{"head":"People are relational","body":"Traditional moral and political theories often picture people as independent individuals pursuing their own interests. Held argued that this is a distortion: we are interdependent, shaped by our relationships, and moral theory should start from that fact."},{"head":"Care beyond the family","body":"Held argued that care ethics should shape public life too: healthcare, education, childcare and international relations. Some activities, such as caring for children or the sick, should not be run purely by market values of profit and competition."}],"misconception":{"myth":"The ethics of care only applies to private life, such as families and friendships.","truth":"Held argued that care should also guide politics, economics and international relations, including decisions about public services, markets and the media."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of The Ethics of Care (2006), which sets out in plain terms what is distinctive about care ethics and how it differs from Kantian and utilitarian theories.","critics":"Critics in the liberal tradition argue that care, which favours those close to us, cannot ground the impartial rights and fairness needed among strangers, so justice must remain the basic political value. Some feminists also worry that emphasising care may reinforce the expectation that women should do caring work.","legacy":"Held helped turn care ethics from a theory of personal relationships into a full moral and political theory, influencing work on healthcare, social policy and global justice.","quote":null},"bourdieu":{"life":["Pierre Bourdieu was born in 1930 in Denguin, a village in the Béarn region of south-west France, the son of a farmer who became a postal worker. He rose through the French school system to the elite École normale supérieure in Paris, an unusual path for someone from his background. His experience as an outsider in elite institutions shaped his lifelong interest in how education and culture pass on social inequality.","After passing the agrégation, France's top teaching examination, in philosophy in 1954, he did military service in Algeria during its war of independence and stayed to teach at the University of Algiers. His studies of Algerian society turned him from philosophy to anthropology and sociology. Back in France he wrote The Inheritors (1964) and Reproduction (1970) on education, Outline of a Theory of Practice (1972) and Distinction (1979). In 1981 he was elected to the chair of sociology at the Collège de France.","Bourdieu ran large research teams and combined statistics, interviews and fieldwork with theory. In the 1990s he became a prominent public intellectual, supporting striking workers in 1995 and criticising free-market policies and the media in short, popular books such as On Television (1996). The Weight of the World (1993) gathered interviews with people experiencing social suffering. He died in Paris in 2002."],"timeline":[[1930,"Born in Denguin, south-west France"],[1955,"Begins military service in Algeria (c.)"],[1958,"Publishes Sociology of Algeria"],[1964,"Publishes The Inheritors with Jean-Claude Passeron"],[1972,"Publishes Outline of a Theory of Practice"],[1979,"Publishes Distinction"],[1981,"Elected to the chair of sociology at the Collège de France"],[2002,"Dies in Paris"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Habitus","body":"Habitus is the set of habits, tastes and ways of seeing that we absorb from our upbringing and social position. It guides how we act without our thinking about it, like a feel for the game. Someone raised among the wealthy knows without effort how to behave at a formal dinner; someone who was not may feel awkward and out of place."},{"head":"Many kinds of capital","body":"Bourdieu argued that people hold different kinds of resources, or 'capital': economic capital (money), cultural capital (education, knowledge, refined taste), social capital (useful connections) and symbolic capital (prestige). These can be turned into one another, as when wealthy parents pay for schooling that later earns their children good jobs."},{"head":"Taste classifies","body":"In Distinction, large surveys showed that preferences in music, food, art and sport match social class and education. The tastes of dominant groups come to count as 'refined', and other tastes as 'vulgar'. Taste therefore sorts people and helps keep social differences in place."},{"head":"Schools reproduce inequality","body":"Schools reward the cultural capital that children from educated homes bring with them, such as familiarity with books, art and formal language. Because this looks like natural talent, schools make inherited advantage appear to be merit."},{"head":"Fields","body":"Bourdieu saw society as made up of 'fields', such as art, science, politics or academic life. Each field has its own rules and prizes, and people compete within it for position, using whatever forms of capital they have."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Bourdieu argued that people consciously use their tastes to show off and climb the social ladder.","truth":"He held that taste mostly works below conscious strategy, through the habitus formed in upbringing. People sincerely like what they like, and the social sorting happens largely without anyone intending it."},"startHere":"Read the lecture 'Social Space and Symbolic Space', the first chapter of Practical Reason (1994), which gives a short, clear summary of the ideas behind Distinction.","critics":"Jacques Rancière, in The Philosopher and His Poor (1983), and others argued that Bourdieu's theory leaves too little room for people to resist or rise above their social conditioning. Later sociologists, such as Richard Peterson, found that high-status people increasingly enjoy both elite and popular culture, weakening the tight link between class and taste that Distinction described.","legacy":"Bourdieu is one of the most cited social scientists in the world, and his ideas of habitus, cultural capital and fields are standard tools in sociology, education, anthropology and cultural studies.","quote":null},"shoemaker":{"life":["Sydney Shoemaker was born in 1931 in Boise, Idaho. He studied at Reed College and then at Cornell University, which in the 1950s was a leading American centre for the philosophy of Ludwig Wittgenstein, through teachers such as Norman Malcolm. Philosophers of the time were reconsidering old questions about the self, memory and the mind using careful analysis of how our concepts work.","He completed his PhD at Cornell in 1958 under Malcolm, with a thesis that became his first book, Self-Knowledge and Self-Identity (1963). After teaching at Ohio State University from 1957 to 1960, he returned to Cornell in 1961 and spent the rest of his career there, becoming Susan Linn Sage Professor of Philosophy in 1978. He gave the John Locke Lectures at Oxford in the early 1970s. His essays were collected in Identity, Cause, and Mind (1984) and The First-Person Perspective (1996).","Shoemaker worked mainly through long, carefully argued articles, often built around imaginative thought experiments, such as brain transplants or a world in which time passes without any change. He connected personal identity, self-knowledge, philosophy of mind and metaphysics into a unified view. His last book, Physical Realization (2007), addressed how mental properties relate to physical ones. He died in Ithaca, New York, in 2022."],"timeline":[[1931,"Born in Boise, Idaho"],[1958,"Completes his PhD at Cornell under Norman Malcolm"],[1963,"Publishes Self-Knowledge and Self-Identity"],[1968,"Publishes 'Self-Reference and Self-Awareness'"],[1969,"Publishes 'Time without Change'"],[1980,"Publishes 'Causality and Properties'"],[1984,"Publishes Personal Identity with Richard Swinburne"],[2022,"Dies in Ithaca, New York"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The brain transplant case","body":"Shoemaker imagined two men, Brown and Robinson, whose brains are removed for surgery; Brown's brain is mistakenly put into Robinson's body. The survivor, 'Brownson', has Brown's memories and personality. Most people judge that Brownson is Brown, which suggests that personal identity follows psychology, not the body."},{"head":"Quasi-memory","body":"A classic objection says that memory cannot explain personal identity, because remembering an experience already assumes it was yours. Shoemaker answered by defining 'quasi-memory': a memory-like state caused in the right way by a past experience, without assuming whose experience it was. Ordinary memory is then just quasi-memory of one's own past."},{"head":"Immune to misidentification","body":"If I say 'I am in pain', I might conceivably be wrong about the pain, but I cannot be wrong that it is I who am feeling it. By contrast, if I see a bruised leg in a tangle of limbs, I might mistake someone else's leg for mine. Shoemaker argued that this special feature of first-person knowledge shows something deep about the self."},{"head":"Time without change","body":"Shoemaker imagined a world divided into three regions that each 'freeze' completely at regular intervals, observed by people in the other regions. Its inhabitants could have good reason to believe that a year had passed in which nothing at all changed. So time without change is at least conceivable."},{"head":"Properties as powers","body":"A property, Shoemaker argued, is defined by the causal powers it gives to things. Being knife-shaped, for example, gives an object the power to cut butter, along with many other powers. On this view laws of nature are not accidents but follow from what properties are."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his half of Personal Identity (1984), written with Richard Swinburne as a debate, in which he defends the psychological view of the self in accessible prose against Swinburne's view of the soul.","critics":"Bernard Williams, in 'The Self and the Future' (1970), argued that body-swap thought experiments pull our intuitions in opposite directions depending on how they are described. Eric Olson and other 'animalists' argue that each of us is a human animal, and an animal does not go wherever its psychology goes.","legacy":"Shoemaker's thought experiments and his idea of quasi-memory set the terms for modern debate on personal identity, and his work on self-knowledge and on properties as powers remains widely discussed.","quote":null},"mbiti":{"life":["John Samuel Mbiti was born in 1931 in Mulango, in the Kitui area of Kenya, then a British colony, and grew up among the Akamba people. He began his academic life as African countries were winning independence, and as African scholars started to challenge European accounts that had dismissed African traditional religions as primitive superstition or as something Christianity simply had to replace.","He studied at Makerere University College in Uganda and at Barrington College in Rhode Island, and earned a PhD in theology at Cambridge in 1963. Ordained an Anglican priest, he taught religion and theology at Makerere from 1964 to 1974, where he wrote African Religions and Philosophy (1969) and Concepts of God in Africa (1970). From 1974 to 1980 he directed the World Council of Churches' Ecumenical Institute at Bossey, Switzerland.","He then served as a parish minister in Burgdorf, Switzerland, from 1980 to 1996, while also teaching at the University of Bern. Mbiti wrote poetry and collected Akamba stories and African proverbs. In retirement he translated the New Testament from Greek into Kikamba, his mother tongue; it was published in 2014. He died in Burgdorf in 2019."],"timeline":[[1931,"Born in Mulango, Kitui, Kenya"],[1963,"Completes his PhD in theology at Cambridge"],[1964,"Begins teaching at Makerere University, Uganda"],[1969,"Publishes African Religions and Philosophy"],[1970,"Publishes Concepts of God in Africa"],[1974,"Becomes director of the Ecumenical Institute at Bossey, Switzerland"],[2014,"His Kikamba translation of the New Testament is published"],[2019,"Dies in Burgdorf, Switzerland"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Religion runs through all of life","body":"Mbiti argued that in traditional African societies there is no sharp line between religious and everyday life. Farming, birth, marriage, politics and death are all bound up with beliefs about God, spirits and ancestors, so religion is a way of life rather than a separate activity on special days."},{"head":"I am, because we are","body":"Mbiti summed up African views of the person in the formula: I am, because we are; and since we are, therefore I am. A person becomes who they are through the community, and what happens to one member happens to the whole group, much as a family shares one member's success or shame."},{"head":"The living-dead","body":"People who have died recently, and are still remembered by name by those who knew them, remain part of the family as the 'living-dead'. They are honoured and consulted. When no one living remembers them any more, they pass into the wider world of spirits."},{"head":"Sasa and Zamani","body":"Using two Swahili words, Mbiti described traditional time as having two main parts: Sasa, the lived present with its recent past and near future, and Zamani, the deep past into which everything eventually moves. He claimed that the far future played little part in traditional thinking."},{"head":"God is known everywhere in Africa","body":"In Concepts of God in Africa, Mbiti gathered evidence that peoples across the continent believed in a supreme God, often seen as creator and sustainer of the world. He argued that this showed African religions deserved the same respect as other world religions."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the early chapter on the concept of time and the later chapter on the individual and the community in African Religions and Philosophy (1969); together they contain his best-known ideas.","critics":"Okot p'Bitek argued that Mbiti dressed African beliefs in Christian and Greek concepts. Philosophers such as Paulin Hountondji criticised 'ethnophilosophy', the practice of describing a whole people's supposed shared worldview as if it were a philosophy, a category in which Mbiti's work is often placed, and several African scholars disputed his account of time.","legacy":"Mbiti's books became standard texts on African religion worldwide and helped establish African traditional religions as a serious academic subject; his formula about community and personhood is among the most quoted ideas in African philosophy.","quote":null},"penrose":{"life":["Roger Penrose was born in 1931 in Colchester, England. He is a mathematician and physicist rather than a professional philosopher, but his work bears directly on philosophical questions about the mind, mathematics and reality. He studied at University College London and at Cambridge, where he completed a PhD in algebraic geometry in 1957, at a time when Einstein's general relativity was becoming an active research field again.","In 1965 he proved that, according to general relativity, a collapsing star can form a singularity, the core of a black hole; this work later earned him a share of the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics. He taught at Birkbeck College, London, and from 1973 was Rouse Ball Professor of Mathematics at Oxford. He developed twistor theory and discovered the non-repeating 'Penrose tilings'. The Emperor's New Mind (1989) and Shadows of the Mind (1994) argued that consciousness cannot be computation.","Penrose's popular books mix serious mathematics with philosophy and are illustrated with his own hand-drawn diagrams. With the anaesthesiologist Stuart Hameroff he proposed a theory linking consciousness to quantum processes in tiny structures inside brain cells called microtubules. He shared the Wolf Prize in Physics with Stephen Hawking in 1988, was knighted in 1994, and later wrote The Road to Reality (2004), a guide to the laws of physics."],"timeline":[[1931,"Born in Colchester, England"],[1957,"Completes his PhD at Cambridge"],[1965,"Publishes his theorem on the formation of black hole singularities"],[1973,"Becomes Rouse Ball Professor of Mathematics at Oxford"],[1989,"Publishes The Emperor's New Mind"],[1994,"Publishes Shadows of the Mind; knighted"],[2004,"Publishes The Road to Reality"],[2020,"Shares the Nobel Prize in Physics"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Gödel and the mind","body":"Kurt Gödel proved that any consistent set of rules for arithmetic leaves some true statements that the rules cannot prove. Penrose, building on an earlier argument by J. R. Lucas, argued that human mathematicians can see that such statements are true. So, he concluded, human understanding is not just following a set of rules."},{"head":"Consciousness is not computation","body":"If understanding goes beyond any algorithm, then no computer program, however large or clever, could truly understand or be conscious. Penrose does not think the mind is supernatural: the brain is physical, but it must use physical processes that no computer can imitate."},{"head":"A need for new physics","body":"Penrose argues that our best physics is incomplete, especially in how quantum theory describes measurement. He proposes that a new process linked to gravity, which he calls objective reduction, is non-computable, and that the brain makes use of it."},{"head":"Three worlds","body":"Penrose describes three linked 'worlds': the physical world, the world of conscious minds, and a Platonic world of mathematical truths. He holds that mathematical truths are discovered, not invented, and that how these three worlds connect is a deep mystery."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Penrose argues that the brain is a kind of quantum computer.","truth":"Quantum computers still follow algorithms, so they could not do what he thinks minds do. He argues instead that consciousness depends on a not-yet-understood physical process, linked to gravity and quantum theory, that is non-computable."},"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of The Emperor's New Mind (1989), which uses the question of whether a computer can have a mind to introduce his argument before the heavier mathematics.","critics":"Logicians and philosophers including Solomon Feferman and Hilary Putnam argued that the Gödel argument fails, because it assumes humans can know that their own mathematical reasoning is consistent, which they cannot. Physicists such as Max Tegmark have argued that delicate quantum states in the warm, wet brain would be destroyed far too quickly to affect thought.","legacy":"Penrose transformed the mathematics of black holes and spacetime, and his books turned the question of whether minds are computers into a public debate; most philosophers reject his Gödel argument, but it remains a standard topic.","quote":null},"kenny":{"life":["Anthony Kenny was born in Liverpool in 1931. He trained for the Catholic priesthood at the English College in Rome, where he studied the philosophy of Thomas Aquinas at the Gregorian University, and was ordained in 1955. He then studied at Oxford, at a time when the influence of Ludwig Wittgenstein dominated British philosophy. Kenny became one of the first to bring careful analytic methods to the study of Aquinas and medieval thought.","After serving as a curate in Liverpool, he returned to the lay state in 1963, having come to doubt central Catholic teachings. He became a fellow of Balliol College, Oxford, in 1964 and was Master of Balliol from 1978 to 1989, then Warden of Rhodes House until 1999. He was President of the British Academy from 1989 to 1993, chaired the board of the British Library from 1993 to 1996, and was knighted in 1992.","Kenny wrote dozens of books, on Wittgenstein, Aristotle, Aquinas, Descartes, free will, the mind and religion, and even used statistics to study questions of authorship in ancient texts. He was known for very clear writing for both specialists and general readers, above all in his four-volume A New History of Western Philosophy (2004–2007). He described his own path in A Path from Rome (1985). He died in 2026."],"timeline":[[1931,"Born in Liverpool"],[1963,"Returns to the lay state after eight years as a priest"],[1969,"Publishes The Five Ways"],[1973,"Publishes Wittgenstein"],[1978,"Becomes Master of Balliol College, Oxford"],[1979,"Publishes The God of the Philosophers"],[2004,"Publishes the first volume of A New History of Western Philosophy"],[2026,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Honest agnosticism","body":"Kenny argued that neither the case for God nor the case against has been made. Believers who claim to know that God exists, and atheists who claim to know that God does not, both claim more than the evidence supports. The honest position, he concluded, is to admit we do not know."},{"head":"The Five Ways examined","body":"In The Five Ways Kenny studied Aquinas's five famous arguments for God in detail. He argued that they depend on outdated medieval physics, such as Aristotle's account of motion, and so do not work as they stand, even though he greatly admired Aquinas's philosophy of mind."},{"head":"The God of the philosophers","body":"Kenny argued that the attributes philosophers give to God do not fit together. In particular, knowing in advance everything people will freely do seems incompatible with human freedom and with God being unchanging. So there cannot be a being with exactly the attributes traditional natural theology describes."},{"head":"The mind as a capacity","body":"Drawing on Aristotle, Aquinas and Wittgenstein, Kenny held that the mind is not a thing, either a soul-substance or the brain. It is a capacity: the ability to think and to choose, which shows itself in how people speak and act."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read What I Believe (2006), a short and personal book in which he explains why he is an agnostic and sets out his views on ethics, the mind and religion.","critics":"Theistic philosophers such as Richard Swinburne and Alvin Plantinga argue that the traditional divine attributes can be stated consistently, for example by defining omniscience as knowing everything that it is logically possible to know, which may not include future free choices.","legacy":"Kenny was among the leading historians of philosophy of his generation; he helped bring Aquinas and medieval philosophy into conversation with analytic philosophy, and his New History is widely used as an introduction.","quote":null},"dworkin":{"life":["Ronald Dworkin was born in 1931 in Worcester, Massachusetts. He studied at Harvard, at Oxford as a Rhodes Scholar, and at Harvard Law School. He worked in a period when legal philosophy in the English-speaking world was dominated by H. L. A. Hart's legal positivism, which held that law is a system of rules identified by social facts and separate from morality, and when American courts were deciding major civil-rights cases.","After clerking for the judge Learned Hand and working at a New York law firm, he taught at Yale Law School from 1962. In 1969 he succeeded Hart as Professor of Jurisprudence at Oxford, holding the post until 1998, while also teaching at New York University and later at University College London. His books include Taking Rights Seriously (1977), Law's Empire (1986), Life's Dominion (1993), Sovereign Virtue (2000) and Justice for Hedgehogs (2011).","Dworkin was also a public intellectual who wrote for decades in The New York Review of Books on Supreme Court cases, abortion, euthanasia, free speech and affirmative action, applying principles to hard current cases. He received the Holberg Prize in 2007 and the Balzan Prize in 2012. He died in London in 2013."],"timeline":[[1931,"Born in Worcester, Massachusetts"],[1962,"Begins teaching at Yale Law School"],[1967,"Publishes 'The Model of Rules', his challenge to Hart"],[1969,"Succeeds H. L. A. Hart as Professor of Jurisprudence at Oxford"],[1977,"Publishes Taking Rights Seriously"],[1986,"Publishes Law's Empire"],[2011,"Publishes Justice for Hedgehogs"],[2013,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Principles, not just rules","body":"Against Hart, Dworkin argued that law contains principles as well as rules. In a famous New York case, a young man murdered his grandfather to inherit from him; the will was valid under the rules, but the court refused to let him inherit, relying on the principle that no one may profit from their own wrong. Such principles, Dworkin argued, are part of the law."},{"head":"Rights as trumps","body":"Individual rights, Dworkin argued, 'trump' ordinary reasons of collective welfare. The government cannot override someone's right to free speech simply because doing so would make most people slightly happier."},{"head":"Law as integrity","body":"Judges should read the law as a coherent whole that expresses consistent principles. Dworkin compared this to a 'chain novel', in which each writer adds a chapter that must fit what came before while making the story as good as possible. His imaginary ideal judge, Hercules, does this perfectly."},{"head":"Choices versus bad luck","body":"In Sovereign Virtue Dworkin argued that a just society should make people bear the costs of their own choices, such as choosing leisure over work, but should compensate them for bad luck they could not control, such as being born with a disability. He used an imagined auction on a desert island to work out what fair shares would look like."},{"head":"The unity of value","body":"An ancient saying holds that the fox knows many things, but the hedgehog knows one big thing. In Justice for Hedgehogs Dworkin took the hedgehog's side: he argued that true values, such as liberty, equality and justice, do not really conflict but support one another when properly understood."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'The Model of Rules', reprinted in Taking Rights Seriously (1977) as 'The Model of Rules I', which uses the inheritance case to show why law needs principles as well as rules.","critics":"Hart replied, in a postscript to The Concept of Law published after his death in 1994, that principles can be part of law because a society's accepted test for law can point to them, so positivism survives. Others argue that the ideal judge Hercules hides how much real judges' own values decide hard cases.","legacy":"Dworkin was the leading critic of legal positivism, and the Hart-Dworkin debate shapes how legal philosophy is taught; his ideas about choice and luck influenced the 'luck egalitarian' movement in political philosophy.","quote":null},"rowe":{"life":["William Leonard Rowe was born in Detroit in 1931. As a teenager he became an evangelical Christian and planned to become a minister, enrolling at the Detroit Bible Institute. He left after one of its teachers was dismissed over his theological views, and moved to Wayne State University to study philosophy. His career coincided with the revival of the philosophy of religion in analytic philosophy, as believers and unbelievers debated arguments for and against God in technical detail.","He studied at Chicago Theological Seminary, where critical study of the Bible gradually loosened his fundamentalist beliefs, and earned his PhD at the University of Michigan in 1962. After briefly teaching at the University of Illinois, he joined Purdue University in 1962 and spent his career there. His books include Religious Symbols and God (1968), a study of the theologian Paul Tillich, The Cosmological Argument (1975) and Philosophy of Religion: An Introduction (1978).","Rowe's 1979 article 'The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism' became one of the most discussed papers in the philosophy of religion. He kept refining its argument for decades in response to critics, for example in 'The Evidential Argument from Evil: A Second Look' (1996). He was known for treating opposing views fairly: The Cosmological Argument defended that argument against many standard objections, though he did not finally accept it. He died in 2015."],"timeline":[[1931,"Born in Detroit, Michigan"],[1962,"Completes his PhD at Michigan and joins Purdue University"],[1968,"Publishes Religious Symbols and God"],[1975,"Publishes The Cosmological Argument"],[1978,"Publishes Philosophy of Religion: An Introduction"],[1979,"Publishes 'The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism'"],[1996,"Publishes 'The Evidential Argument from Evil: A Second Look'"],[2015,"Dies in Lafayette, Indiana"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The evidential argument from evil","body":"Older versions of the problem of evil claimed that evil logically contradicts God's existence. Rowe made a more modest claim: an all-powerful, all-good being would prevent any intense suffering it could prevent without losing a greater good. There seems to be suffering like that, so it is probable that no such being exists."},{"head":"The fawn in the forest fire","body":"Rowe's example: lightning starts a forest fire, and a fawn is trapped, terribly burned, and lies in agony for several days before dying. No one sees it, and no good seems to come from it. Rowe asked what reason an all-good God could have for allowing it."},{"head":"Friendly atheism","body":"Rowe distinguished kinds of atheism. A 'friendly atheist' believes there is no God but accepts that some believers are rational in believing, given their own evidence and experience. Rowe counted himself as a friendly atheist."},{"head":"The cosmological argument reconsidered","body":"Rowe argued that many standard objections to the cosmological argument, including some from Hume, fail. But the argument rests on the principle that everything has an explanation, and since we do not know that principle is true, the argument does not prove God exists."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Rowe claimed that evil proves God does not exist.","truth":"He argued only that certain evils make God's existence improbable, given our evidence, and he accepted that a believer could rationally believe in God; he called his own position 'friendly atheism'."},"startHere":"Read 'The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism' (1979): it is short, clearly argued, and introduces both the fawn example and the idea of friendly atheism.","critics":"Stephen Wykstra (1984) and William Alston (1991) replied with what is now called sceptical theism: given how limited human understanding is, our failing to see a good reason for the fawn's suffering is weak evidence that there is none. Rowe answered with revised versions of his argument.","legacy":"Rowe's argument defined the modern evidential problem of evil and made 'pointless suffering' and sceptical theism central topics in the philosophy of religion.","quote":null},"ginet":{"life":["Carl Ginet was born in 1932. He studied at Occidental College in California and then at Cornell University, where he earned his PhD in 1960 with a dissertation on reasons, causes and free will. He worked during the period when analytic philosophers revived the free-will debate with new arguments about whether determinism, the view that every event is fixed by earlier events and the laws of nature, leaves room for real choice.","He taught at Ohio State, the University of Michigan and the University of Rochester before joining Cornell's Sage School of Philosophy in 1971, where he stayed until he retired in 1999. His book Knowledge, Perception, and Memory (1975) dealt with the theory of knowledge, and On Action (1990) set out his theory of free action. He has also written widely cited papers on moral responsibility, on statements that perform acts, and on Kripke's reading of Wittgenstein.","Ginet's work is known for its precision and for contributing to several debates at once. One of his examples became famous through another philosopher: Alvin Goldman's 1976 paper on perceptual knowledge credited Ginet with the 'fake barn' case, now a standard example in the theory of knowledge. Ginet was also one of the first to state a version of what became known as the consequence argument against compatibilism, in his 1966 paper 'Might We Have No Choice?'."],"timeline":[[1932,"Born"],[1960,"Completes his PhD at Cornell"],[1966,"Publishes 'Might We Have No Choice?'"],[1971,"Joins Cornell's Sage School of Philosophy"],[1975,"Publishes Knowledge, Perception, and Memory"],[1990,"Publishes On Action"],[1999,"Retires from Cornell"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Free choices are uncaused","body":"Ginet argued that a free choice is a simple mental act that nothing causes, not even the agent. What makes it an action, rather than something that merely happens to us, is its distinctive feel: it seems to the person that they are directly making it happen."},{"head":"Reasons explain without causing","body":"We can explain a choice by giving the reason for it, as in 'she raised her hand in order to vote'. Ginet argued that such an explanation is true when the person acted with that intention, and does not require the reason to have caused the choice. So freedom and rational explanation can go together without causation."},{"head":"The consequence argument","body":"If determinism is true, our actions are consequences of the laws of nature and events in the distant past. We have no choice about the laws or about the distant past. So, the argument goes, we have no choice about our actions either. Ginet gave an early version of this argument against compatibilism, the view that freedom fits with determinism."},{"head":"Fake barns","body":"Henry drives through the countryside and sees a barn, and he is right: it is a barn. But unknown to him the area is full of fake barn fronts, and he could easily have been fooled. Does he know he sees a barn? Many say no, which suggests that knowledge depends partly on one's surroundings."},{"head":"Defending alternative possibilities","body":"Harry Frankfurt argued that people can be responsible for what they do even when they could not have done otherwise. Ginet, in a 1996 paper, defended the older principle that responsibility requires being able to do otherwise, arguing that Frankfurt's examples do not succeed."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read On Action (1990), starting with its opening chapter on what an action is; the book is short and builds his view step by step.","critics":"Randolph Clarke and other critics argue that if nothing causes a choice, then nothing about the agent settles which way it goes, so the choice looks like a matter of luck rather than control. Compatibilists reject the consequence argument, often by questioning its key rule about what we 'have no choice about', as David Lewis did.","legacy":"Ginet is the leading defender of a non-causal account of free will, and his fake barn case and early consequence argument are standard reference points in the theory of knowledge and the free-will debate.","quote":null},"wolterstorff":{"life":["Nicholas Wolterstorff was born in 1932 in Bigelow, Minnesota, and grew up in a Dutch Reformed community. He studied at Calvin College in Michigan, a college of the Christian Reformed Church, and then at Harvard. His career unfolded as analytic philosophers who were also religious believers began to argue that belief in God need not be defended on terms set by secular philosophy.","He completed his Harvard PhD in 1956 with a thesis on Whitehead, taught briefly at Yale, and then spent about thirty years at Calvin College from 1959, alongside his friend Alvin Plantinga. In 1989 he became Noah Porter Professor of Philosophical Theology at Yale, retiring in 2002. His Gifford Lectures of 1995 became Thomas Reid and the Story of Epistemology (2001). He has also served as president of the Society of Christian Philosophers.","Wolterstorff's work ranges unusually widely. Reason within the Bounds of Religion (1976) and Faith and Rationality (1983), edited with Plantinga, helped launch 'Reformed epistemology'. Works and Worlds of Art and Art in Action (both 1980) dealt with art, and Justice: Rights and Wrongs (2008) grounded human rights in the worth that God's love gives to each person. Lament for a Son (1987), a short book of grief written after his son died in a climbing accident, has been read widely outside philosophy."],"timeline":[[1932,"Born in Bigelow, Minnesota"],[1956,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1959,"Begins teaching at Calvin College"],[1976,"Publishes Reason within the Bounds of Religion"],[1983,"Publishes Faith and Rationality, edited with Alvin Plantinga"],[1987,"Publishes Lament for a Son"],[1989,"Becomes Noah Porter Professor of Philosophical Theology at Yale"],[2008,"Publishes Justice: Rights and Wrongs"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Rational belief without proof","body":"Wolterstorff argued that belief in God can be rational even when it is not based on any argument. We rationally trust our memories and what other people tell us without proving them first; belief in God can be like that."},{"head":"Against classical foundationalism","body":"Classical foundationalism says a belief is rational only if it is self-evident, beyond doubt, or proved from such beliefs. Wolterstorff argued that this standard would rule out most ordinary beliefs, and that it fails its own test, since it is neither self-evident nor proved."},{"head":"Innocent until proven guilty","body":"Instead of asking believers to prove their beliefs first, Wolterstorff proposed that a person is entitled to a belief unless they have good reason to give it up. Beliefs, like people accused in court, are innocent until proven guilty."},{"head":"Control beliefs","body":"In Reason within the Bounds of Religion he argued that every scholar has 'control beliefs': deep commitments that guide which theories they find acceptable. A Christian scholar may properly let faith play this role, just as others are guided by their own basic commitments."},{"head":"Rights grounded in worth","body":"In Justice: Rights and Wrongs he argued that rights are claims grounded in the worth of persons. Every human being, he held, has a worth that comes from being loved by God, and this grounds human rights that belong to everyone equally."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Reformed epistemology says religious belief needs no reasons and cannot be criticised.","truth":"Wolterstorff holds that belief in God can be rational without argument, but that it can still be overturned by good reasons against it, just as an ordinary belief can."},"startHere":"Read his essay 'Can Belief in God Be Rational If It Has No Foundations?' in Faith and Rationality (1983), which sets out the core case of Reformed epistemology.","critics":"Critics raised what Plantinga called the 'Great Pumpkin objection': if belief in God can be rational without argument, it seems almost any belief could be, such as a child's belief in the Great Pumpkin. Evidentialists argue that responsible believers should match the strength of their beliefs to the evidence and arguments available.","legacy":"Wolterstorff helped found Reformed epistemology, and his work on justice, art and religion in public life has made him one of the most influential Christian philosophers of his generation.","quote":null},"martin_m":{"life":["Michael Martin was an American philosopher born in 1932. He grew up in a United States where religious belief was the norm and open atheism was rare in public life. In academic philosophy after the Second World War, careful analysis of language and evidence was in fashion, and arguments about God were usually treated as a side topic. Martin brought those analytic tools to the question of God's existence and became one of the best-known defenders of atheism among professional philosophers.","Before university he served with the US Marine Corps in Korea. He then studied business at Arizona State University, took a master's in philosophy at the University of Arizona and earned his PhD at Harvard in 1962. After a short spell at the University of Colorado he joined Boston University in 1965 and stayed there for the rest of his career. His early books were on the philosophy of science, social science and law. From 1990, with Atheism: A Philosophical Justification, he turned mainly to religion.","Martin was known for thoroughness. His books work through the main arguments for God one by one and reply to each, and he also built positive arguments that God does not exist. He took part in public debates with Christian thinkers, including a written internet debate published in 2000. He edited The Cambridge Companion to Atheism (2006), which helped make atheism a standard subject in university courses. He died in 2015."],"timeline":[[1932,"Born in the United States"],[1962,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1965,"Joins Boston University"],[1990,"Publishes Atheism: A Philosophical Justification"],[1991,"Publishes The Case Against Christianity"],[2003,"Co-edits The Impossibility of God with Ricki Monnier"],[2006,"Edits The Cambridge Companion to Atheism"],[2015,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Negative and positive atheism","body":"Martin separated two kinds of atheism. Negative atheism is simply not believing in God; positive atheism is believing that there is no God. He defended both, arguing that the first follows from the failure of the arguments for God and the second from arguments against God."},{"head":"Burden of proof","body":"If there is no good evidence for something, Martin argued, we should not believe in it, just as we do not believe in a planet nobody has detected. So if all the arguments for God fail, the rational stance is disbelief, not a neutral shrug."},{"head":"God as impossible","body":"Martin argued that some traditional descriptions of God contradict each other. For example, a God who knows everything would know what it feels like to envy or to fear, yet a perfect being could never feel these things. If a description is contradictory, nothing can fit it."},{"head":"Evil as evidence","body":"He held that the amount and kind of suffering in the world, such as animal pain over millions of years, makes the existence of an all-powerful, all-good God unlikely. He replied in detail to the main religious explanations of evil."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the introduction and opening chapters of Atheism: A Philosophical Justification, where he explains what atheism is, sets out negative and positive atheism and explains why he thinks the burden of proof lies with the believer.","critics":"Theist philosophers such as Alvin Plantinga argued that belief in God can be reasonable without arguments, much as belief in other minds is, so the lack of a proof does not make disbelief the default.","legacy":"Martin's books gave atheism a systematic philosophical defence at a time when most work in the philosophy of religion came from believers. His Cambridge Companion remains a standard starting point for the subject.","quote":null},"plantinga":{"life":["Alvin Plantinga was born in 1932 in Ann Arbor, Michigan, into a family of Dutch Reformed (Calvinist) background; his father was also a philosophy teacher. In the mid-twentieth century many philosophers thought belief in God had been shown to be meaningless or irrational, and religious believers rarely defended their faith in leading journals. Plantinga became the central figure in changing this, helping to make the philosophy of religion a busy and respected field again.","He studied at Calvin College and the University of Michigan and took his PhD at Yale in 1958. He taught at Wayne State University, then at Calvin College from 1963 to 1982, and then at the University of Notre Dame until 2010, before returning to Calvin. His books include God and Other Minds (1967), The Nature of Necessity (1974), God, Freedom, and Evil (1974) and a trilogy on 'warrant' ending with Warranted Christian Belief (2000). He gave the Gifford Lectures in 2005 and received the Templeton Prize in 2017.","Plantinga is known for rigorous, technical arguments, often built with the tools of modal logic (the logic of possibility and necessity), combined with a willingness to defend Christian belief openly. He was a founding figure in the Society of Christian Philosophers and wrote an influential 1984 essay, 'Advice to Christian Philosophers', urging believers to start from their own convictions. He is also known for lively, witty prose and for engaging his critics at length."],"timeline":[[1932,"Born in Ann Arbor, Michigan"],[1958,"Completes his PhD at Yale"],[1963,"Begins teaching at Calvin College"],[1974,"Publishes The Nature of Necessity and God, Freedom, and Evil"],[1982,"Moves to the University of Notre Dame"],[2000,"Publishes Warranted Christian Belief"],[2011,"Publishes Where the Conflict Really Lies, on science and religion"],[2017,"Receives the Templeton Prize"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The free will defence","body":"Critics such as J. L. Mackie claimed that God and evil are logically incompatible. Plantinga replied that a world with creatures who are truly free is worth more than a world of puppets, and even God cannot make free creatures always choose the good. If that is possible, there is no contradiction between God and evil."},{"head":"Defence, not theodicy","body":"Plantinga distinguished a 'defence', which only shows that God and evil could both exist, from a 'theodicy', which claims to know God's actual reasons. He aimed only at the first, which is a much weaker and easier claim to defend."},{"head":"Belief in God as basic","body":"We believe that other people have minds, or that the past happened, without proving it from more basic evidence. Plantinga argued that belief in God can be 'properly basic' in the same way: rational to hold without an argument, as long as it comes from a properly working mind. This view is called Reformed epistemology."},{"head":"Warrant and proper function","body":"Plantinga defined 'warrant', the thing that turns true belief into knowledge, as being produced by faculties working properly in the right environment according to a design aimed at truth. On this view, whether belief in God is warranted depends on whether God exists, so the question cannot be settled by epistemology alone."},{"head":"Evolution against naturalism","body":"Plantinga argued that if our minds were shaped only by blind evolution, aimed at survival rather than truth, we would have no good reason to trust them. That doubt would extend to the belief in naturalism itself, so naturalism undermines itself."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Plantinga's free will defence claims to explain why God actually allows the evil in the world.","truth":"He presented it as a defence, not a theodicy: it only aims to show that God and evil are logically consistent, without claiming to know God's real reasons."},"startHere":"Read Part I of God, Freedom, and Evil, a short book that sets out the free will defence step by step for non-specialists.","critics":"Mackie and others argued that an all-powerful God could have created free beings who always choose rightly, and later philosophers such as William Rowe shifted to an evidential argument: even if God and evil are consistent, the sheer amount of pointless suffering makes God unlikely.","legacy":"Plantinga is widely credited with reviving the philosophy of religion within analytic philosophy. Most philosophers now accept that his free will defence answered the logical form of the problem of evil, and Reformed epistemology remains a major position.","quote":null},"rp_wolff":{"life":["Robert Paul Wolff was born in New York City in 1933. He came of age in post-war America, when the power of governments to draft citizens, wage wars and enforce laws was largely taken for granted. During the 1960s, with the civil rights movement and protests against the Vietnam War, many people began asking when, if ever, citizens must obey the state. Wolff gave one of the sharpest philosophical answers.","He took all his degrees at Harvard, finishing his PhD in 1957, and then taught at Harvard, Chicago and Columbia. His first book, Kant's Theory of Mental Activity (1963), established him as a Kant scholar. In 1971 he moved to the University of Massachusetts Amherst, and in 1992 he transferred to its department of Afro-American Studies, where he taught until 2008. Besides In Defense of Anarchism (1970), he wrote critical studies of Rawls and Marx. He died in January 2025.","Wolff wrote clearly and provocatively and liked to pursue an argument to its conclusion even when it was unpopular. He cared about teaching and about practical causes: in 1990 he founded University Scholarships for South African Students, which helped disadvantaged black South Africans attend university. In retirement he wrote a widely read blog and posted long series of lectures online, explaining Kant, Marx and other thinkers to a general audience."],"timeline":[[1933,"Born in New York City"],[1957,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1963,"Publishes Kant's Theory of Mental Activity"],[1965,"Co-writes A Critique of Pure Tolerance with Herbert Marcuse and Barrington Moore Jr."],[1970,"Publishes In Defense of Anarchism"],[1971,"Moves to the University of Massachusetts Amherst"],[1977,"Publishes Understanding Rawls"],[2025,"Dies in Durham, North Carolina"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Autonomy comes first","body":"Following Kant, Wolff held that a mature moral agent takes full responsibility for their actions by deciding for themselves what is right. Doing something simply because someone told you to, without judging it yourself, gives up that responsibility."},{"head":"Authority versus autonomy","body":"Authority, in Wolff's sense, is the right to be obeyed just because of who is commanding. If you must do something because the state says so, not because you judge it right, you are no longer autonomous. Wolff concluded that the two cannot be reconciled."},{"head":"Philosophical anarchism","body":"So no state has legitimate authority over anyone. This does not mean you should break laws: often there are good reasons, such as safety, to do what the law says. But you follow the law because you judge it wise, not because the state commands it."},{"head":"Only unanimous democracy would do","body":"Wolff argued that the only state compatible with autonomy would be a direct democracy where every law is agreed by everyone. Majority rule fails, because the outvoted minority must obey laws they rejected, and unanimity is impossible in practice."},{"head":"Liberalism's limits","body":"In The Poverty of Liberalism and his book on Rawls, Wolff argued that liberal theories treat society as a collection of separate individuals and so miss the value of community and the realities of economic power. This led him toward Marx."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Wolff's anarchism is a call for chaos or for overthrowing governments by force.","truth":"His anarchism is philosophical: it denies that any state has a moral right to be obeyed, but allows that people often have good independent reasons to follow laws."},"startHere":"Read the first chapter of In Defense of Anarchism, a short and direct statement of the clash between authority and autonomy that can be read in an hour.","critics":"Many critics, including Jeffrey Reiman in his book-length reply In Defense of Political Philosophy (1972), argued that Wolff misdescribes autonomy: a rational person can autonomously decide to accept a legitimate authority's decisions, much as one sensibly defers to a doctor.","legacy":"In Defense of Anarchism set the terms for the modern debate about political obligation and legitimacy. Later philosophical anarchists such as A. John Simmons, and many defenders of the state, still begin by answering Wolff.","quote":null},"swinburne":{"life":["Richard Swinburne was born in 1934 in Smethwick, in the English Midlands. In the British philosophy of his youth, religious claims were widely dismissed as meaningless or unprovable, and many thought science had made belief in God unnecessary. Swinburne set out to show that the question could be treated like a scientific one, by weighing evidence and probabilities, and argued that the answer favours God.","He studied at Exeter College, Oxford, beginning in Classics and graduating in philosophy, politics and economics. He also studied the history and philosophy of science. He was a lecturer at Hull from 1963 to 1972 and professor at Keele from 1972 to 1984. From 1985 to 2002 he was Nolloth Professor of the Philosophy of the Christian Religion at Oxford. His trilogy The Coherence of Theism (1977), The Existence of God (1979) and Faith and Reason (1981) made his name, and he gave the Gifford Lectures in 1982–1984.","Swinburne works in a careful, systematic way, building long chains of argument and stating the probability he assigns at each step. After his trilogy he wrote a second series of books on Christian doctrines such as the Trinity, atonement and resurrection. Around 1996 he joined the Eastern Orthodox Church. He has also written short books for general readers, notably Is There a God? (1996)."],"timeline":[[1934,"Born in Smethwick, England"],[1963,"Becomes a lecturer in philosophy at Hull"],[1972,"Becomes professor of philosophy at Keele"],[1979,"Publishes The Existence of God"],[1985,"Becomes Nolloth Professor of the Philosophy of the Christian Religion at Oxford"],[1986,"Publishes The Evolution of the Soul, from his Gifford Lectures"],[1996,"Publishes Is There a God?"],[2013,"Publishes Mind, Brain, and Free Will"]],"ideas":[{"head":"A cumulative case","body":"Swinburne admits no single argument proves God exists. But like a detective adding up clues, he argues that the existence of the universe, its order, consciousness and religious experience together make God's existence more probable than not."},{"head":"Simplicity as a guide","body":"Scientists prefer simpler explanations when all else is equal. Swinburne argues that God, a single being with unlimited power, knowledge and freedom, is the simplest possible explanation of why there is an orderly universe at all."},{"head":"Probability reasoning","body":"He uses Bayes' theorem, a rule for updating how likely a hypothesis is in light of new evidence, to set out his case. Each piece of evidence, he argues, is more to be expected if God exists than if not."},{"head":"The principle of credulity","body":"If it seems to you that something is so, you should believe it unless you have special reason to doubt it. Swinburne applies this to religious experience: if someone seems to experience God, that is evidence God exists, unless there are grounds for distrust."},{"head":"The soul","body":"Swinburne defends substance dualism: each of us is a soul, a non-physical thing joined to a body. He argues that no amount of facts about brains settles which person you are, so persons must be more than their bodies."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Is There a God?, a short book written for general readers that summarises the argument of The Existence of God without the technical probability theory.","critics":"J. L. Mackie, in The Miracle of Theism (1982), argued that God is not a simple hypothesis at all and that natural explanations account for the evidence better. Others have questioned whether the probabilities Swinburne assigns can be given any objective basis.","legacy":"Swinburne showed that natural theology, arguing for God from evidence, could be done with the tools of modern philosophy of science. His work is a standard reference point for both defenders and critics of theism.","quote":null},"phillips_dz":{"life":["Dewi Zephaniah Phillips was born in 1934 in Morriston, near Swansea in Wales, and was a Welsh speaker all his life. In mid-twentieth-century Britain, philosophers often asked whether religious statements were true or false in the way scientific statements are. At Swansea a circle of thinkers influenced by Ludwig Wittgenstein, especially Rush Rhees, argued that this was the wrong question. Phillips became the best-known voice of that approach to religion.","He studied philosophy at Swansea and then at St Catherine's College, Oxford, where his research became his first book, The Concept of Prayer (1965). Between 1959 and 1961 he served as minister of a Congregational chapel in Swansea. After posts at Dundee and Bangor he returned to Swansea in 1965, becoming professor in 1971. From 1992 he also held the Danforth chair in philosophy of religion at Claremont Graduate University in California. He died in Swansea in 2006.","Phillips edited the journal Philosophical Investigations for many years and was a tireless organiser of conferences. He looked after the papers of Rush Rhees and Peter Winch. He wrote widely on literature, using novels and poems to bring out moral and religious ideas, and published books in Welsh, including a study of the poet R. S. Thomas. He was known as a forceful debater."],"timeline":[[1934,"Born in Morriston, Swansea"],[1965,"Publishes The Concept of Prayer and returns to teach at Swansea"],[1971,"Becomes professor of philosophy at Swansea"],[1976,"Publishes Religion Without Explanation"],[1992,"Becomes Danforth Professor at Claremont Graduate University"],[2004,"Publishes The Problem of Evil and the Problem of God"],[2006,"Dies in Swansea"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Grammar of religious belief","body":"Following Wittgenstein, Phillips argued that words get their meaning from how they are used in a way of life. To understand 'God' you must look at how believers pray, worship and live, not treat it like a word for an object in the universe."},{"head":"God is not a thing","body":"Asking whether God exists the way a distant planet exists is, for Phillips, a confusion. Belief in God is more like having a whole way of seeing and responding to life than holding a theory that might be confirmed by evidence."},{"head":"Against theodicy","body":"Philosophers try to justify evil by saying God allows suffering for a greater good. Phillips found this morally offensive: telling a grieving parent that their child's death serves a purpose treats suffering as a means and makes God a calculating manager."},{"head":"Prayer as expression","body":"In The Concept of Prayer he argued that prayers of petition are not attempts to make God change the world. They express a person's hopes and dependence, and their deepest form is something like 'Thy will be done'."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapters of The Problem of Evil and the Problem of God, his late book, which explains both his objections to theodicy and his view of how 'God' should be understood.","critics":"Kai Nielsen, who coined the label 'Wittgensteinian fideism' in 1967, argued that Phillips's approach shields religion from rational criticism. Realist philosophers such as John Hick objected that it empties belief in God of any claim about what really exists.","legacy":"Phillips is the main source for the Wittgensteinian approach to the philosophy of religion, which remains the leading alternative to evidence-based debates about God. His moral criticism of theodicy is widely discussed.","quote":null},"fried":{"life":["Charles Fried was born in Prague in 1935. His family fled Czechoslovakia in 1939, as Nazi Germany took control, and after a short time in England settled in New York in 1941. He grew up in an America that had fought fascism and was confident about law and rights, and he spent his career thinking about what law and morality require of individuals and governments.","He studied at Princeton, at Oxford and at Columbia Law School, and joined the Harvard Law School faculty in 1961, where he taught for more than sixty years. He wrote An Anatomy of Values (1970), Right and Wrong (1978) and Contract as Promise (1981). From 1985 to 1989 he was Solicitor General of the United States under President Reagan, arguing the government's cases before the Supreme Court. From 1995 to 1999 he was a justice of the Massachusetts Supreme Judicial Court. He died in January 2024.","Fried was unusual in working as a moral philosopher, a legal scholar and a public official. He wrote on medical ethics, contract, tort and constitutional law. Though known as a conservative, he often took independent positions, for example announcing in 2008 that he had voted for Barack Obama. He wrote several books with a strong personal voice, including Order and Law (1991), an account of his time as Solicitor General."],"timeline":[[1935,"Born in Prague"],[1941,"Family settles in New York after fleeing Nazi-occupied Europe"],[1961,"Joins the faculty of Harvard Law School"],[1978,"Publishes Right and Wrong"],[1981,"Publishes Contract as Promise"],[1985,"Becomes US Solicitor General (until 1989)"],[1995,"Appointed to the Massachusetts Supreme Judicial Court (until 1999)"],[2024,"Dies in Cambridge, Massachusetts"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Absolute wrongs","body":"Fried argued that some acts, such as lying, killing the innocent or using people as mere tools, are wrong in themselves, not just because of their results. You may not frame an innocent person even if it would calm a riot."},{"head":"The catastrophe threshold","body":"Yet Fried accepted that such rules may give way when the stakes become catastrophic, such as when a whole city would be destroyed. This position, sometimes called threshold deontology, tries to keep strict rules for ordinary life while not being fanatical."},{"head":"Contract as promise","body":"Why must people keep their contracts? Fried's answer is that a contract is a promise, and keeping promises is a moral duty: by promising you invite someone to trust you. Contract law, on this view, enforces a moral practice rather than just serving economic efficiency."},{"head":"Doing versus allowing","body":"Fried held that our strictest duties are not to do harm ourselves; duties to prevent harm by others are weaker. This explains, he argued, why we may refuse to kill one person to save several."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of Contract as Promise, a short and clear argument that ties a familiar moral idea, keeping one's word, to a whole area of law.","critics":"Critics of threshold views like Fried's, among them Larry Alexander, argue that any line at which rules give way is arbitrary. Legal scholars such as P. S. Atiyah argued that much of contract law rests on reliance and benefit, not on promise.","legacy":"Contract as Promise remains the best-known defence of the promise theory of contract and is read in law schools across the English-speaking world. Fried's work on absolute duties is a standard reference in debates about deontology.","quote":null},"hasker":{"life":["William Hasker was born in 1935. He belongs to a generation of Christian philosophers who, from the 1970s onward, helped revive the philosophy of religion in the English-speaking world. A long-running question in that field is how God's knowledge of the future fits with human freedom: if God already knows what you will choose tomorrow, can you really choose otherwise? Hasker became one of the leading defenders of a new answer.","He earned a PhD in theology and philosophy of religion at the University of Edinburgh and spent most of his career teaching philosophy at Huntington College (now Huntington University) in Indiana, where he is professor emeritus. His God, Time, and Knowledge (1989) argued against the traditional view of foreknowledge. In 1994 he co-wrote The Openness of God with Clark Pinnock and others. He also wrote The Emergent Self (1999) on the mind and The Triumph of God over Evil (2008).","Hasker writes in a careful analytic style and has engaged in long exchanges with critics in journals and books. He edited the journal Faith and Philosophy from 2000 to 2007 and was president of the Society for Philosophy of Religion in 2006. Besides the philosophy of religion, he has written an introductory textbook, Metaphysics: Constructing a World View (1983). His work often responds directly to named opponents."],"timeline":[[1935,"Born"],[1983,"Publishes Metaphysics: Constructing a World View"],[1989,"Publishes God, Time, and Knowledge"],[1994,"Co-writes The Openness of God"],[1999,"Publishes The Emergent Self"],[2000,"Becomes editor of Faith and Philosophy (until 2007)"],[2008,"Publishes The Triumph of God over Evil"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Foreknowledge threatens freedom","body":"If God knew a thousand years ago that you will eat cereal tomorrow, that past fact cannot now be changed, and God cannot be wrong. Hasker argued that this leaves you no real power to do otherwise, so complete foreknowledge rules out free choice."},{"head":"Open theism","body":"Hasker's answer is that the future free choices of creatures are not yet settled, so there is nothing there to know. God knows everything that can be known, including every possibility, but the future is partly open even for God."},{"head":"A God who takes risks","body":"On this view God created a world in which creatures can surprise him and respond freely to his love. God does not control every event but is wise and powerful enough to bring good out of whatever happens."},{"head":"Emergent dualism","body":"In The Emergent Self Hasker argued that the mind is produced by the brain, but once produced it is a real, unified thing of its own that is not just brain activity. This tries to combine respect for neuroscience with belief in a self that is more than matter."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Open theists like Hasker deny that God is all-knowing.","truth":"Hasker holds that God knows everything that can be known; he argues that future free choices are not yet there to be known, so not knowing them is no limit on God's knowledge."},"startHere":"Read Hasker's chapter, 'A Philosophical Perspective', in The Openness of God (1994), which sets out the argument from freedom to an open future in plain terms.","critics":"Defenders of traditional theism, such as Bruce Ware, argued that open theism conflicts with biblical prophecy and weakens God's control over history. Philosophers such as Linda Zagzebski have also offered ways to reconcile foreknowledge and freedom that Hasker rejects.","legacy":"Hasker gave open theism its most rigorous philosophical defence, making it a recognised position in academic debates about God and time. His emergent dualism is also a standard option in debates about the mind among philosophers of religion.","quote":null},"walzer":{"life":["Michael Walzer was born in New York City in 1935 and grew up partly in Johnstown, Pennsylvania. His political thinking was shaped by the civil rights movement and by the Vietnam War, which forced Americans to ask when a war is just and how it may be fought. He became one of the leading political thinkers of the left in the United States while also being a sharp critic of parts of it.","He studied at Brandeis University and earned his PhD at Harvard in 1961. His first book, The Revolution of the Saints (1965), was a history of English Puritan politics. He taught at Princeton and at Harvard, and in 1980 moved to the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, where he stayed until retiring. Just and Unjust Wars (1977) and Spheres of Justice (1983) are his most influential books. For decades he was co-editor of the democratic socialist magazine Dissent.","Walzer writes as a public intellectual as much as an academic philosopher. He builds his arguments from historical cases and from the moral language ordinary people use, rather than from abstract thought experiments. He has written about Jewish political thought, toleration, nationalism and humanitarian intervention, often in essays aimed at general readers. He also wrote widely read essays on politics in the Middle East and on the American left, and he continued to publish into his eighties."],"timeline":[[1935,"Born in New York City"],[1961,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1965,"Publishes The Revolution of the Saints"],[1977,"Publishes Just and Unjust Wars"],[1980,"Joins the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton"],[1983,"Publishes Spheres of Justice"],[1994,"Publishes Thick and Thin"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Just war, revived","body":"Against 'realists' who say morality has no place in war, Walzer argued that we judge wars morally all the time, calling some aggression and some massacres. He restated just war theory using historical cases: when going to war is justified, and how it may be fought."},{"head":"Protecting civilians","body":"A central rule of Walzer's theory is that soldiers may fight soldiers but must not deliberately target civilians. Even if your cause is just, bombing a city to break morale is a crime."},{"head":"Supreme emergency","body":"Walzer allowed one narrow exception: when a community faces imminent destruction by an enemy like Nazi Germany, the rules may be overridden. He insisted that even then those who break them carry moral guilt."},{"head":"Spheres of justice","body":"Different goods, such as health care, money, political office and love, have different meanings, and each should be shared by its own principles. Medical care should go by need, not by wealth; office should go by merit or election, not by purchase."},{"head":"Complex equality","body":"Injustice happens when one good dominates others, as when money buys votes, court rulings or school places. Walzer's 'complex equality' does not require everyone to have the same; it requires that success in one sphere not give power in all the rest."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Part One of Just and Unjust Wars, where Walzer argues against the 'realist' view that morality does not apply in war, using vivid historical examples.","critics":"Ronald Dworkin argued in a 1983 review of Spheres of Justice that tying justice to a society's shared meanings makes it relative: it cannot criticise a society whose shared meanings are themselves unjust. Jeff McMahan has challenged Walzer's view that soldiers on both sides of a war are morally equal.","legacy":"Just and Unjust Wars is still the standard text on the ethics of war, used in military academies as well as universities. Spheres of Justice made Walzer a leading voice of 'communitarian' political thought.","quote":null},"garner":{"life":["Richard Garner was an American philosopher born in 1936. In the second half of the twentieth century, philosophers increasingly asked whether moral claims can be objectively true. In 1977 J. L. Mackie argued that they cannot: all moral claims are false because they assume objective values that do not exist. Garner accepted this 'error theory' and asked the next question: if morality rests on an error, what should we do about it?","He studied at Ohio University, where he first became interested in Asian philosophy, then at Penn State, and earned his PhD at the University of Michigan, where he studied with Charles Stevenson, William Alston and William Frankena. He taught at Ohio State University from 1963 to 1995. With Bernard Rosen he wrote the textbook Moral Philosophy (1967). His main book, Beyond Morality, appeared in 1994. He died in December 2020.","Garner combined Western analytic philosophy with a long interest in Daoism and Buddhism; he taught himself to read classical Chinese. He wrote on the philosophy of language as well as ethics. Late in life he co-edited, with Richard Joyce, an anthology called The End of Morality, which gathered writers who take moral abolitionism seriously. He continued writing and debating these ideas after retiring, including as an adjunct professor at Rutgers from 2000 to 2005."],"timeline":[[1936,"Born"],[1963,"Joins the philosophy department at Ohio State University"],[1967,"Publishes Moral Philosophy, a textbook written with Bernard Rosen"],[1994,"Publishes Beyond Morality"],[1995,"Retires from Ohio State"],[2007,"Publishes the article 'Abolishing Morality'"],[2020,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Morality rests on an error","body":"When we say 'cruelty is wrong', we seem to state an objective fact that holds whatever anyone thinks. Garner agreed with Mackie that there are no such facts, so moral claims, taken literally, are all false."},{"head":"Abolitionism","body":"Some error theorists suggest we keep moral talk as a useful fiction. Garner disagreed: we should gradually stop using moral language altogether, the way people stopped talking about witches once they no longer believed in them."},{"head":"Morality makes conflict worse","body":"Garner argued that moral talk often inflames disputes. Each side claims objective rightness, demonises the other and refuses to compromise. Without it, people could state their preferences honestly and negotiate."},{"head":"Life without morality","body":"Giving up morality does not mean giving up kindness. Garner thought people could still act from compassion and good sense, and found models in Daoist and Buddhist texts that value spontaneity and attention over fixed rules."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Moral abolitionists like Garner want people to stop caring about others or to behave badly.","truth":"Garner argued that people would still be moved by sympathy and their own considered preferences, and that dropping moral language could make people less hostile, not more."},"startHere":"Read his article 'Abolishing Morality' (2007), a short and direct statement of why an error theorist should drop moral talk rather than keep it as a fiction.","critics":"Fictionalists such as Richard Joyce argued that moral talk is too useful to give up, since it helps people resist temptation and coordinate. Others doubt that dropping moral words would change behaviour, or think that the error theory itself is mistaken.","legacy":"Garner is the best-known defender of moral abolitionism, which has become a recognised option in debates about what follows from moral error theory.","quote":null},"hacking":{"life":["Ian Hacking was born in Vancouver, Canada, in 1936. In the mid-twentieth century, philosophy of science focused mainly on the logic of theories and on how evidence supports them. After Thomas Kuhn's 1962 book on scientific revolutions, many wondered whether science really tracks the truth at all. Hacking took a different route: he looked at what scientists actually do in laboratories, and at the history of how our ideas came about.","He studied at the University of British Columbia and at Trinity College, Cambridge, where he took his PhD in 1962. He taught at UBC, Cambridge and Stanford, and from 1983 at the University of Toronto. From 2000 to 2006 he held a chair in the philosophy and history of scientific concepts at the Collège de France in Paris. He won the Holberg Prize in 2009 and the Balzan Prize in 2014. He died in Toronto in 2023.","Hacking was known for the range of his curiosity. He wrote on probability and statistics, the philosophy of experiment, multiple personality disorder, a nineteenth-century epidemic of compulsive wandering and the philosophy of mathematics. He wrote in a clear, conversational style and drew on Michel Foucault's historical method while remaining an analytic philosopher. He was made a Companion of the Order of Canada in 2004."],"timeline":[[1936,"Born in Vancouver"],[1962,"Completes his PhD at Cambridge"],[1975,"Publishes The Emergence of Probability"],[1983,"Publishes Representing and Intervening; joins the University of Toronto"],[1990,"Publishes The Taming of Chance"],[1999,"Publishes The Social Construction of What?"],[2009,"Receives the Holberg Prize"],[2023,"Dies in Toronto"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Entity realism","body":"Theories change, so we may doubt what they say. But Hacking argued that when scientists use electrons as tools, aiming beams of them to study something else, they have strong grounds to believe electrons are real. What we can manipulate, we should believe in."},{"head":"Experiment has a life of its own","body":"Philosophers had treated experiments only as tests of theories. Hacking showed that experiments often come first, create new effects and lead to new instruments, so laboratory practice deserves philosophical attention in its own right."},{"head":"The history of probability","body":"In The Emergence of Probability he argued that our modern idea of probability, with both a statistical side and a side about degrees of belief, appeared only around 1660. The Taming of Chance traced how statistics came to shape how states understand their populations."},{"head":"Making up people","body":"New categories such as 'multiple personality' change the people they describe. Once people know about a label, they may come to think and act differently, which changes the category in turn. Hacking called this the 'looping effect'."},{"head":"Construction of what?","body":"When people say something is 'socially constructed', Hacking asked what exactly they mean. An idea, such as a classification, may be socially shaped while the thing it refers to is not, and confusing the two causes needless quarrels."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Hacking argued that scientific facts are socially constructed.","truth":"He was a realist about many scientific entities and wrote The Social Construction of What? to untangle such claims, distinguishing ideas and classifications, which can be shaped by society, from the things they classify."},"startHere":"Read the opening chapter of Representing and Intervening, a book based on introductory lectures, which explains the realism debate and why Hacking turns to experiment.","critics":"Critics of entity realism, such as Margaret Morrison and David Resnik, argued that you cannot use electrons as tools without also believing a good deal of theory about them, so Hacking's line between trusting entities and doubting theories is hard to hold.","legacy":"Hacking helped turn the philosophy of science toward experiment and practice and showed how history can do philosophical work. His ideas about looping effects are widely used in psychiatry, sociology and the study of human categories.","quote":null},"narveson":{"life":["Jan Narveson was born in 1936 in Erskine, Minnesota. He trained in the years when utilitarianism, the view that we should produce the greatest happiness, dominated discussions of ethics in English-speaking philosophy. He began as a utilitarian and asked new questions about future generations, then later moved to a quite different view, defending a libertarian society built on agreement between self-interested people.","He studied political science and philosophy at the University of Chicago, spent a year at Oxford and took his PhD at Harvard in 1961. He spent his career at the University of Waterloo in Ontario, where he is professor emeritus. His first book, Morality and Utility, and his article 'Utilitarianism and New Generations' both appeared in 1967. His main later work is The Libertarian Idea (1988). He was made an Officer of the Order of Canada in 2003.","Narveson writes in a direct, combative style and has published many debates with opponents, including a book with Marilyn Friedman on political correctness. He has been active outside the university, founding a chamber music society in Kitchener-Waterloo and helping to found the Institute for Liberal Studies, which promotes classical liberal ideas. He has also written popular textbooks and anthologies on moral problems for students."],"timeline":[[1936,"Born in Erskine, Minnesota"],[1961,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1967,"Publishes Morality and Utility and 'Utilitarianism and New Generations'"],[1988,"Publishes The Libertarian Idea"],[2003,"Made an Officer of the Order of Canada"],[2008,"Publishes You and the State"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Making people happy, not happy people","body":"Narveson argued that morality is about the welfare of people who exist or will exist. We have reason to improve their lives, but no duty to create extra people just because they would be happy. This is often called the person-affecting view of population ethics."},{"head":"Morality as agreement","body":"In his later work Narveson defended contractarianism: moral rules are those that rational, self-interested people would agree to for mutual benefit. Each of us gains from a world where people do not attack each other."},{"head":"Liberty as the core rule","body":"The rule such people would agree to, he argued, is mutual non-interference: do not use force or fraud against others. This leads to a libertarian society with strong property rights and a minimal role for the state."},{"head":"Against animal rights","body":"Since animals cannot make or keep agreements, Narveson argued, they are not parties to the moral contract and have no rights of their own. Any reasons to treat them well come from the interests of people who care about them."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Utilitarianism and New Generations' (1967), a short article that sets out his influential view that we should make people happy rather than make happy people.","critics":"Derek Parfit argued in Reasons and Persons (1984) that person-affecting views like Narveson's cannot explain why it would be wrong to choose policies that make future generations worse off. Critics of contractarianism point out that it seems to leave out animals, infants and others who cannot bargain.","legacy":"Narveson's slogan about making people happy rather than making happy people, from his 1973 paper 'Moral Problems of Population', framed much later work in population ethics. He is also a leading academic defender of libertarianism grounded in contract theory.","quote":null},"lehrer":{"life":["Keith Lehrer was born in 1936. He came into philosophy at a time when the theory of knowledge was changing quickly. In 1963 Edmund Gettier showed with short examples that a justified true belief can fail to be knowledge, and philosophers raced to find the missing ingredient. Lehrer was one of the most active contributors to that debate and went on to build his own theory of knowledge.","He studied at the University of Minnesota and took his PhD at Brown University, where Roderick Chisholm was one of his teachers. He taught at the University of Rochester and from 1973 at the University of Arizona, where he became Regents' Professor, and later also held a research post at the University of Miami. His books include Knowledge (1974), Thomas Reid (1989), Theory of Knowledge (1990) and Self-Trust (1997).","Lehrer has worked across a wide range: free will, the Scottish philosopher Thomas Reid, consensus in groups and, later, art and aesthetics. With the mathematician Carl Wagner he wrote Rational Consensus in Science and Society (1981), a formal model of how groups can reach agreement. He served as president of the Pacific Division of the American Philosophical Association. He also served for several years as an executive director of the APA."],"timeline":[[1936,"Born"],[1969,"Publishes 'Knowledge: Undefeated Justified True Belief' with Thomas Paxson"],[1973,"Joins the University of Arizona"],[1974,"Publishes Knowledge"],[1981,"Publishes Rational Consensus in Science and Society with Carl Wagner"],[1990,"Publishes Theory of Knowledge, introducing the Truetemp case"],[1997,"Publishes Self-Trust"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Undefeated justification","body":"Gettier cases show that you can be justified and right by luck. Lehrer and Paxson proposed that knowledge needs justification that no further truth would defeat. If there is a fact that would undermine your reason if you learned it, you do not know."},{"head":"Coherence theory","body":"A belief is justified, Lehrer argued, when it fits with the rest of what a person accepts and can meet the objections that system would raise. Justification is not built on a foundation of certain beliefs but comes from how beliefs hang together."},{"head":"The Truetemp case","body":"Imagine a man whose brain has a device implanted that gives him accurate beliefs about the temperature, though he has no idea it is there. His beliefs are reliable, but Lehrer argued he does not know the temperature, because he has no reason to trust them. This was aimed at reliabilism."},{"head":"Self-trust","body":"In later work Lehrer argued that all reasoning depends on trusting our own faculties, and that this trust is reasonable when we accept ourselves as trustworthy in what we accept. This ties knowledge to the person's own evaluation of their beliefs."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the early chapters of Theory of Knowledge (1990), an introductory book that explains the Gettier problem and the main rival theories before setting out his own.","critics":"Externalists such as Alvin Goldman argued that coherence theories demand too much reflection: children and animals seem to know things without checking their beliefs against a whole system. Others noted that a well-written novel can be perfectly coherent yet false.","legacy":"The Lehrer-Paxson defeasibility analysis is a standard response to the Gettier problem, and the Truetemp case is one of the most cited thought experiments in epistemology.","quote":null},"adams_rm":{"life":["Robert Merrihew Adams was born in Philadelphia in 1937. When he began his career, analytic philosophers mostly ignored religion and doubted that God could have anything to do with ethics. Adams became one of the leading figures who showed that theistic ideas could be defended with full philosophical rigour, while also doing major work on metaphysics, ethics and the history of philosophy.","He studied at Princeton, at Mansfield College, Oxford, and at Princeton Theological Seminary, and served as a Presbyterian pastor before taking his PhD at Cornell. He taught for many years at the University of California, Los Angeles, and from the early 1990s at Yale, where he chaired the philosophy department, until retiring in 2004. He then taught at Oxford and later held a research professorship at the University of North Carolina. He gave the Gifford Lectures in 1999 and died in 2024.","Adams wrote influential journal articles before he wrote books, including 'Must God Create the Best?' (1972) and 'Motive Utilitarianism' (1976). His study of Leibniz (1994) is a major work in the history of philosophy. His later books, Finite and Infinite Goods (1999) and A Theory of Virtue (2006), set out a complete ethical theory. He was a past president of the Society of Christian Philosophers."],"timeline":[[1937,"Born in Philadelphia"],[1972,"Publishes 'Must God Create the Best?'"],[1973,"Publishes 'A Modified Divine Command Theory of Ethical Wrongness'"],[1994,"Publishes Leibniz: Determinist, Theist, Idealist"],[1999,"Publishes Finite and Infinite Goods; gives the Gifford Lectures"],[2006,"Publishes A Theory of Virtue"],[2024,"Dies in Montgomery, New Jersey"]],"ideas":[{"head":"A modified divine command theory","body":"The old objection to grounding ethics in God's commands is that if God commanded cruelty, cruelty would become right. Adams replied that what makes acts wrong is being contrary to the commands of a loving God. A God who commanded cruelty for its own sake would not be the God whose commands bind us."},{"head":"Goodness as likeness to God","body":"In Finite and Infinite Goods Adams argued that God is the standard of all excellence, as a metre stick once set the standard for length. Finite things, like a kind act or a beautiful piece of music, are good insofar as they resemble God in some way."},{"head":"God need not create the best","body":"Must a perfect God create the best possible world? Adams argued not: God can create creatures out of grace, valuing them for their own sake, without wronging anyone by not creating better ones."},{"head":"Motive utilitarianism","body":"Adams pointed out that the best motives to have, like deep love for one's family, will sometimes lead to acts that are not the best possible. Judging motives and judging acts can come apart, which complicates simple utilitarianism."},{"head":"Virtue as being for the good","body":"In A Theory of Virtue he defined virtue as persisting excellence in being for what is good: loving, respecting and pursuing good things. This is meant to capture what we admire in good people, not just what is useful about them."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Divine command theories, including Adams's, imply that anything God commanded, even torturing children, would be right.","truth":"Adams's version ties moral obligation to the commands of a God who is loving and good by nature, so commands to be cruel for its own sake are ruled out."},"startHere":"Read his essay 'A Modified Divine Command Theory of Ethical Wrongness' (1973), reprinted in The Virtue of Faith, which is short and shows how he answers the classic objection to grounding ethics in God.","critics":"Critics such as Wes Morriston argued that Adams's view still faces a version of the Euthyphro dilemma: if God's commands bind only because God is good, goodness seems to be doing the work, and God's commands add nothing.","legacy":"Adams made divine command theory a serious option again in academic ethics. His work on Leibniz, on actuality and on 'thisness' in metaphysics is also widely cited.","quote":null},"pinnock":{"life":["Clark Pinnock was born in Toronto in 1937. He worked within evangelical Protestant Christianity in North America, a movement that stresses the authority of the Bible and personal conversion. In the second half of the twentieth century, evangelical theology was largely Calvinist in tone, emphasising God's total control over events. Pinnock gradually came to reject this picture and became the best-known theologian of what is called open theism.","He studied Ancient Near Eastern studies at the University of Toronto and took his PhD at the University of Manchester under the biblical scholar F. F. Bruce. He taught at New Orleans Baptist Theological Seminary, Trinity Evangelical Divinity School and Regent College, and from 1977 until his retirement in 2002 at McMaster Divinity College in Hamilton, Ontario. He died in 2010.","Pinnock's thinking changed openly over his career. He began as a defender of biblical inerrancy and Calvinist theology, moved toward Arminianism, which stresses human freedom, and then to open theism. He wrote with a pastoral tone for wide audiences. His views caused controversy: in 2003 the Evangelical Theological Society voted on whether to expel him, but the motion failed and he remained a member."],"timeline":[[1937,"Born in Toronto"],[1967,"Publishes A Defense of Biblical Infallibility"],[1977,"Joins McMaster Divinity College"],[1992,"Publishes A Wideness in God's Mercy"],[1994,"Co-writes The Openness of God"],[2001,"Publishes Most Moved Mover"],[2002,"Retires from McMaster"],[2010,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"God in relationship","body":"Pinnock held that the Bible shows God as personal and responsive: God grieves, changes plans and answers prayer. He argued that classical theology, influenced by Greek philosophy, had made God too distant and unchangeable."},{"head":"An open future","body":"Because God gave creatures real freedom, Pinnock argued, the future is not fixed in advance, and God knows it as a range of possibilities rather than a settled script. God's knowledge is complete, but there are not yet facts about future free choices to know."},{"head":"A God who takes risks","body":"Creating free beings is a risk: they may reject God. Pinnock saw this not as a weakness but as a sign of love, since love cannot be forced. God remains wise and powerful enough to work toward good ends whatever creatures do."},{"head":"A wide mercy","body":"In A Wideness in God's Mercy he argued that people who never heard of Christ can still be saved by responding to God in the light they have. This view is called inclusivism."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Pinnock's chapter 'Systematic Theology' in The Openness of God (1994), a short summary of the case for open theism written for non-specialists.","critics":"Evangelical critics such as Bruce Ware, in God's Lesser Glory (2000), argued that open theism contradicts biblical prophecy and diminishes God's sovereignty and reliability.","legacy":"Pinnock brought open theism into wide discussion among evangelical Christians and in academic theology, where it remains a much-debated alternative to classical views of God.","quote":null},"harman":{"life":["Gilbert Harman was born in East Orange, New Jersey, in 1938. He studied at Swarthmore College and then at Harvard under W. V. O. Quine, one of the most important American philosophers of the century, who urged that philosophy should be continuous with science. Harman carried that outlook into many fields, from the theory of reasoning and the philosophy of mind to ethics.","He spent his whole career at Princeton University, from 1963 until he retired in 2017, and co-directed its Cognitive Science Laboratory with the psychologist George Miller. His books include Thought (1973), The Nature of Morality (1977), Change in View (1986) and, with Judith Jarvis Thomson, Moral Relativism and Moral Objectivity (1996). Late in his career he worked with an engineer, Sanjeev Kulkarni, on statistical learning theory. He died in 2021.","Harman was known for short, clear papers that opened new debates, for example on 'inference to the best explanation' in 1965 and on character traits in 1999. He was an early champion of cognitive science and of using findings from psychology in philosophy, and taught many students who became leading philosophers. His work on reasoning later connected with machine learning, a field that studies how computers can learn from data."],"timeline":[[1938,"Born in East Orange, New Jersey"],[1963,"Joins the Princeton philosophy department"],[1965,"Publishes 'The Inference to the Best Explanation'"],[1975,"Publishes 'Moral Relativism Defended'"],[1977,"Publishes The Nature of Morality"],[1986,"Publishes Change in View"],[1996,"Publishes Moral Relativism and Moral Objectivity with Judith Jarvis Thomson"],[2021,"Dies in Princeton"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Morality and observation","body":"If you see children setting a cat on fire, you 'see' that it is wrong. But Harman asked whether the wrongness explains anything: your reaction is explained by your upbringing and psychology. Unlike physical facts, moral facts seem to play no part in explaining what we observe, which casts doubt on them."},{"head":"Moral relativism","body":"Harman argued that moral judgments only make sense relative to an implicit agreement among a group. Saying someone 'ought' not to harm others assumes they share our moral framework. A person entirely outside it, such as a hired killer with no such commitments, may simply have no reason to refrain."},{"head":"Inference to the best explanation","body":"Harman named and defended a common pattern of reasoning: we accept the hypothesis that best explains the evidence. A detective concludes the butler did it because that best explains the clues."},{"head":"Reasoning as change in view","body":"Logic tells us what follows from what, but not what to believe. Harman argued that reasoning is really about revising our beliefs, keeping them as stable as possible while adding or dropping beliefs when there is reason."},{"head":"Doubts about character","body":"Drawing on social psychology, Harman argued that how people act depends far more on their situation than on stable character traits. In experiments, small things, like being in a hurry, decide whether people help a stranger. This challenged virtue ethics."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of The Nature of Morality, 'Ethics and Observation', a short introduction to his argument that moral facts play no role in explanation.","critics":"Nicholas Sturgeon, in 'Moral Explanations' (1985), argued that moral facts do figure in good explanations, for example that Hitler's depravity helps explain what he did. On character, virtue ethicists replied that real virtue is rare, so the experiments do not refute it.","legacy":"Harman's 'explanatory challenge' to moral realism and his situationist critique of virtue ethics both started debates that continue today. 'Inference to the best explanation' is now standard philosophical vocabulary.","quote":null},"hirsch":{"life":["Eli Hirsch was born in 1938. He works in metaphysics, the part of philosophy that asks what exists and what things are. In the later twentieth century, analytic metaphysicians argued intensely about questions such as whether a table is a real object or just particles arranged table-wise, and whether any two objects together make up a third object. Hirsch became the best-known critic of the way these debates are framed.","He studied at Brooklyn College and took his MA and PhD at New York University. He spent his career at Brandeis University, where he became Charles Goldman Professor of Philosophy. His books include The Concept of Identity (1982), on what makes something the same thing over time, Dividing Reality (1993), on why we carve the world into the categories we do, and Quantifier Variance and Realism (2011), a collection of his papers on ontology.","Hirsch works through careful, patient argument and close attention to how ordinary language is used. Much of his influence has come through journal articles that started debates about 'metaontology', the study of what debates about existence are really about. He has also written on the problem of personal identity. His writing typically starts from simple cases, such as how many objects there are on a table, and asks what the dispute about them is really about."],"timeline":[[1938,"Born"],[1982,"Publishes The Concept of Identity"],[1993,"Publishes Dividing Reality"],[2005,"Publishes 'Physical-Object Ontology, Verbal Disputes, and Common Sense'"],[2011,"Publishes Quantifier Variance and Realism"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Quantifier variance","body":"Words like 'exists' and 'there is' can have several equally good meanings. One language might count any two objects as composing a third, another might not. Hirsch argued that neither is more correct; they simply describe the same world in different terms."},{"head":"Verbal disputes in ontology","body":"So when one philosopher says 'there are tables' and another says 'there are only particles arranged table-wise', they may not really disagree about the world. Each speaks truly in their own language, like two people who measure the same field in acres and hectares."},{"head":"Charity to ordinary speakers","body":"In interpreting a language, we should make its speakers' everyday claims come out true. Since ordinary English speakers say there are tables and chairs, Hirsch argued, the claim that there are no tables is simply false in English."},{"head":"Why these categories?","body":"In Dividing Reality Hirsch asked whether we could have divided the world differently, for example by having a word for a thing that is green before a certain time and blue after. He explored whether our ordinary categories are better than such strange alternatives or just familiar."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Physical-Object Ontology, Verbal Disputes, and Common Sense' (2005), reprinted in Quantifier Variance and Realism, which sets out his view that many debates about what exists are merely verbal.","critics":"Theodore Sider, in Writing the Book of the World (2011), argued that some meanings of 'exists' fit the structure of reality better than others, so debates about existence can be real, not merely verbal.","legacy":"Hirsch's quantifier variance is one of the central positions in metaontology, and his challenge to traditional metaphysics shaped a whole generation of work on whether ontological questions are deep or shallow.","quote":null},"kane":{"life":["Robert Kane was born in Boston in 1938. Debates about free will in his time were dominated by compatibilists, who argue that freedom fits with determinism, the idea that every event is fixed by earlier causes. Libertarians, who think freedom requires that some choices are not determined, were often thought to have no coherent account to offer. Kane spent his career trying to provide one that fits with modern science.","He studied at the College of the Holy Cross, spent a year at the University of Vienna and took his PhD at Yale in 1964. After teaching at Fordham and Haverford, he joined the University of Texas at Austin in 1970, where he spent the rest of his career and became Distinguished Teaching Professor. His books include Free Will and Values (1985), Through the Moral Maze (1994) and The Significance of Free Will (1996).","Kane is known as a teacher and writer of clear introductions. His A Contemporary Introduction to Free Will (2005) is widely used in courses, and he edited The Oxford Handbook of Free Will. He has also written on ethics and on how to live with moral disagreement in a pluralist society. He also taught law at the University of Texas School of Law, and in 1996 his book The Significance of Free Will won a university book award. He died in Guilford, Connecticut, in 2024."],"timeline":[[1938,"Born in Boston"],[1964,"Completes his PhD at Yale"],[1970,"Joins the University of Texas at Austin"],[1985,"Publishes Free Will and Values"],[1996,"Publishes The Significance of Free Will"],[2005,"Publishes A Contemporary Introduction to Free Will"],[2007,"Co-writes Four Views on Free Will"],[2024,"Dies in Guilford, Connecticut"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Ultimate responsibility","body":"To be truly responsible for what you do, Kane argued, you must be responsible for at least some of what made you the kind of person you are. If everything about your character traced back to things outside your control, you would not be the ultimate source of your actions."},{"head":"Self-forming actions","body":"We shape our characters through hard choices, such as whether to stop and help someone when late for an important meeting. In these moments, Kane argued, both options are genuinely open, and whichever you choose helps make you who you become."},{"head":"Effort against indeterminism","body":"In such a choice, Kane suggested, the brain is in an unsettled state, and there is a real chance either way. Because you are trying to make each option happen, whichever wins is your own achievement, not mere chance. To use an everyday comparison, a basketball player who sinks a hard shot still made it, even though missing was possible."},{"head":"Libertarianism without magic","body":"Kane's view does not appeal to a soul or to special agent powers. He tried to show that libertarian freedom could fit with a picture of humans as physical beings, as long as some brain events are not fully determined."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the chapters on his own view in A Contemporary Introduction to Free Will, where he explains self-forming actions in plain language alongside the rival positions.","critics":"The main objection is the 'luck problem', pressed by Alfred Mele and Peter van Inwagen among others: if two people identical up to the moment of choice could choose differently, the difference seems to be a matter of luck, not control.","legacy":"Kane is widely regarded as the leading defender of event-causal libertarianism, and his ideas of ultimate responsibility and self-forming actions are central to current free will debates.","quote":null},"fish":{"life":["Stanley Fish was born in Providence, Rhode Island, in 1938. He is a literary critic and legal scholar rather than a philosopher by training, but his arguments about interpretation became important in philosophy and law. In the 1960s and 1970s literary critics argued about whether the meaning of a text lies in the words themselves, the author's intention or the reader's response. Fish changed the terms of that debate.","He studied at the University of Pennsylvania and took his PhD at Yale in 1962. He taught at Berkeley and Johns Hopkins, and from 1986 to 1998 at Duke, where he was professor of English and of law. From 1999 to 2004 he was dean of liberal arts and sciences at the University of Illinois at Chicago, and later he taught law at Florida International University and elsewhere. His books include Surprised by Sin (1967), on Milton, and Is There a Text in This Class? (1980).","Fish became widely known outside universities as a provocative essayist and for many years wrote regular opinion columns for The New York Times. He enjoys defending surprising positions, such as that the humanities have no practical use and need none, and he has debated many critics in public. He has also written books for general readers, such as How to Write a Sentence (2011)."],"timeline":[[1938,"Born in Providence, Rhode Island"],[1962,"Completes his PhD at Yale"],[1967,"Publishes Surprised by Sin: The Reader in Paradise Lost"],[1980,"Publishes Is There a Text in This Class?"],[1989,"Publishes Doing What Comes Naturally"],[1994,"Publishes There's No Such Thing as Free Speech, and It's a Good Thing, Too"],[1999,"Becomes dean at the University of Illinois at Chicago"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The reader's experience","body":"In his early work on Milton, Fish argued that meaning is something that happens to readers as they move through a text, with its traps and surprises. Paradise Lost, he claimed, deliberately leads readers into error so they experience their own fallen state."},{"head":"Interpretive communities","body":"Meaning is neither simply in the text nor in the lone reader. It depends on the shared assumptions of an 'interpretive community', such as a group of lawyers or literary critics. Their background practices decide what counts as a sensible reading."},{"head":"Is there a text in this class?","body":"A student asked a teacher, 'Is there a text in this class?', meaning not 'which textbook?' but 'do you believe texts have fixed meanings?' Fish used this story to show that even simple sentences mean different things depending on the setting, yet hearers rarely have trouble understanding."},{"head":"No neutral free speech","body":"Every free speech principle draws a line somewhere: no one protects fraud or threats. Fish argued that where to draw the line is always a political judgment about which speech matters, so appeals to 'free speech' cannot settle disputes on their own."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Fish's slogan 'there's no such thing as free speech' means he favours censorship.","truth":"His claim is that every speech principle already excludes some speech based on values and politics, so we should argue openly about those values instead of pretending a neutral principle decides the case."},"startHere":"Read the title essay of Is There a Text in This Class?, a lively lecture that explains interpretive communities through a single classroom anecdote.","critics":"Ronald Dworkin, in a debate with Fish in the early 1980s, argued that interpretation can be constrained by principles of fit and justification, not only by community practice. Many critics also charge that Fish's view slides into relativism, though he denies it.","legacy":"The idea of interpretive communities became a standard concept in literary theory and legal theory. Fish's arguments continue to shape debates about free speech and about how judges read laws.","quote":null},"goldman":{"life":["Alvin Goldman was born in Brooklyn, New York, in 1938. When he began his career, the theory of knowledge had just been shaken by Edmund Gettier's 1963 paper showing that justified true belief is not always knowledge. Goldman responded by asking how beliefs are caused, an approach that led him to found 'reliabilism' and to bring psychology and cognitive science into epistemology.","He studied at Columbia and took his PhD at Princeton in 1965. He taught at the University of Michigan from 1963 to 1980, then at the University of Illinois at Chicago and for many years at the University of Arizona, before moving to Rutgers University, where he became Board of Governors Professor of Philosophy and Cognitive Science. His major books include A Theory of Human Action (1970), Epistemology and Cognition (1986), Knowledge in a Social World (1999) and Simulating Minds (2006). He died in August 2024.","Goldman worked closely with cognitive scientists and believed philosophy should draw on empirical findings. He also helped found 'social epistemology', the study of how institutions such as courts, science and the media help or hinder the spread of knowledge. Many of his short papers, such as 'What Is Justified Belief?', became standard readings. He also wrote on the philosophy of action and on how we understand other minds."],"timeline":[[1938,"Born in Brooklyn, New York"],[1965,"Completes his PhD at Princeton"],[1967,"Publishes 'A Causal Theory of Knowing'"],[1976,"Publishes 'Discrimination and Perceptual Knowledge', with the fake barn case"],[1979,"Publishes 'What Is Justified Belief?'"],[1986,"Publishes Epistemology and Cognition"],[1999,"Publishes Knowledge in a Social World"],[2024,"Dies"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The causal theory of knowing","body":"Goldman's first answer to Gettier was that knowledge requires the right causal link between a fact and your belief. You know there is a fire because the fire caused you to see smoke, not because of a lucky guess."},{"head":"Reliabilism","body":"A belief is justified if it comes from a reliable process, one that usually produces true beliefs, such as good eyesight in daylight. Beliefs from wishful thinking or guessing are unjustified, even if you happen to be right."},{"head":"The fake barn case","body":"Driving through a region full of fake barn facades, you look at the one real barn and believe 'that's a barn'. You are right, but Goldman argued you do not know, because you could not tell a real barn from the fakes around you. (He credited the example to Carl Ginet.)"},{"head":"Naturalised epistemology","body":"Which processes are reliable is an empirical question. So, Goldman argued, epistemology needs help from psychology and cognitive science, which study how memory, perception and reasoning actually work."},{"head":"Social epistemology","body":"Much of what we know comes from others: teachers, experts, news reports. Goldman studied which social practices, from courtroom rules to how we choose experts, tend to spread truth rather than error."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'What Is Justified Belief?' (1979), a short paper that sets out reliabilism clearly and is still the standard introduction to it.","critics":"Stewart Cohen and Keith Lehrer raised the 'new evil demon' problem: a person deceived by a demon whose beliefs are produced unreliably still seems justified. Earl Conee and Richard Feldman pressed the 'generality problem': any belief-forming process can be described at many levels, and reliability depends on which is chosen.","legacy":"Reliabilism became one of the main theories of justification, and Goldman's work did much to connect epistemology with cognitive science and to establish social epistemology as a field.","quote":null},"raz":{"life":["Joseph Raz was born in Haifa, in what was then British-ruled Palestine, in 1939. He grew up as the State of Israel was founded and studied law at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem. There he was encouraged to go to Oxford to work with H. L. A. Hart, the leading legal philosopher of the day, who had revived the question of what law is and how it relates to morality.","He completed his doctorate under Hart at Balliol College, Oxford, in 1967, and published it as The Concept of a Legal System (1970). After teaching at the Hebrew University, he returned to Oxford in 1972 as a fellow of Balliol and was professor of the philosophy of law from 1985 to 2006. He also taught at Columbia Law School and King's College London. His books include Practical Reason and Norms (1975), The Authority of Law (1979) and The Morality of Freedom (1986). He died in London in 2022.","Raz was known for dense, carefully argued writing that brought together legal philosophy, moral philosophy and the theory of practical reason. He supervised many students who became leading legal and political philosophers. In 2018 he received the Tang Prize in Rule of Law. His later work focused on the nature of reasons and value, and on how we can be responsible for what we do. He remained active as a writer until his death."],"timeline":[[1939,"Born in Haifa"],[1967,"Completes his DPhil at Oxford under H. L. A. Hart"],[1970,"Publishes The Concept of a Legal System"],[1975,"Publishes Practical Reason and Norms"],[1979,"Publishes The Authority of Law"],[1986,"Publishes The Morality of Freedom"],[2018,"Receives the Tang Prize in Rule of Law"],[2022,"Dies in London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The service conception of authority","body":"Authority is legitimate, Raz argued, when following it helps you act better on the reasons you already have than you would on your own. An air traffic controller has authority because pilots do better by following instructions than by judging for themselves."},{"head":"Exclusionary reasons","body":"A legitimate command does not just add one more reason to the scale. It also excludes acting on some of your own reasons. A soldier ordered to hold position should not reweigh the order against their own sense of tactics."},{"head":"The sources thesis","body":"Raz defended a strict form of legal positivism: what the law is can be identified from social facts, such as statutes and court decisions, without moral argument. Whether the law is good is a separate question."},{"head":"Autonomy and a good life","body":"In The Morality of Freedom he argued that a good life involves wholehearted engagement in worthwhile activities and relationships, chosen from a range of good options. Being the author of one's own life is part of what makes it go well."},{"head":"Perfectionist liberalism","body":"Because autonomy matters, governments should protect it, but they may also support valuable options, such as the arts, and discourage worthless ones. This sets Raz against liberals who say the state must be neutral about the good life."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the chapters on personal well-being and autonomy near the end of The Morality of Freedom, which set out his view of a good life in fairly accessible terms.","critics":"Ronald Dworkin argued that identifying the law always involves moral judgment, against Raz's positivism. Other critics, such as Stephen Darwall, argued that the service conception cannot explain why authorities can create real duties rather than just good advice.","legacy":"Raz is regarded as one of the leading legal philosophers after Hart. His account of authority and his perfectionist liberalism are standard positions in legal and political philosophy.","quote":null},"scanlon":{"life":["Thomas Michael Scanlon was born in 1940 and grew up in Indianapolis. He came into moral philosophy in the 1960s, when it was dominated by utilitarianism, the view that right acts produce the most overall wellbeing. John Rawls was developing a powerful alternative based on a social contract. Scanlon took Rawls's contractual idea and turned it from a theory of political justice into a general account of right and wrong.","He studied at Princeton, spent a year at Oxford on a Fulbright scholarship and took his PhD at Harvard in 1968. He taught at Princeton from 1966 to 1984 and then at Harvard until he retired in 2016 as Alford Professor of Natural Religion, Moral Philosophy, and Civil Polity. His papers 'A Theory of Freedom of Expression' (1972) and 'Contractualism and Utilitarianism' (1982) were followed by What We Owe to Each Other (1998), Moral Dimensions (2008) and Being Realistic about Reasons (2014).","Scanlon is known as a careful, patient writer who refines his views over decades. He received a MacArthur Fellowship in 1993. What We Owe to Each Other reached a large public audience when it was featured in the television comedy The Good Place. Before his major books he was also known for influential work on freedom of expression and on toleration, collected in The Difficulty of Tolerance (2003)."],"timeline":[[1940,"Born"],[1966,"Begins teaching at Princeton"],[1968,"Completes his PhD at Harvard"],[1982,"Publishes 'Contractualism and Utilitarianism'"],[1984,"Moves to Harvard"],[1998,"Publishes What We Owe to Each Other"],[2008,"Publishes Moral Dimensions"],[2014,"Publishes Being Realistic about Reasons"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Contractualism","body":"An act is wrong, Scanlon argued, if it would be disallowed by any set of principles for behaviour that no one could reasonably reject. Morality is about acting in ways we can justify to each person affected."},{"head":"Justifiability to each person","body":"We test a principle by looking at it from each person's point of view. If a rule would allow lying to you whenever it is convenient, you could reasonably reject it, because the burden on you is too great compared with the benefit to others."},{"head":"No adding up","body":"Unlike utilitarians, Scanlon compares people's complaints one by one rather than summing benefits. So we may not let one person suffer terribly just to give many people minor pleasures, such as letting a man be electrocuted so millions can keep watching a football match."},{"head":"Reasons come first","body":"Scanlon treats a reason as a basic notion: a consideration that counts in favour of something. Value and wellbeing are explained in terms of reasons, not the other way round, which he calls a 'buck-passing' account of value."},{"head":"Meaning and blame","body":"In Moral Dimensions he argued that a person's intentions often do not affect whether an act is permissible, but they affect its meaning. Blame, on his account, is a change in our relationship with someone whose attitudes have shown them to be untrustworthy."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read chapter 5 of What We Owe to Each Other, 'Wrongness and Reasons', where he sets out the contractualist test most directly.","critics":"Philip Pettit and others raised the 'redundancy objection': what makes a principle reasonable to reject seems to be that the act it allows is already wrong, so contractualism adds nothing. Others argue that refusing to add up numbers makes it hard to say why we should save more people rather than fewer.","legacy":"Scanlon's contractualism is now one of the main theories of right and wrong alongside consequentialism, Kantian ethics and virtue ethics. His ideas on reasons and value shaped much later work in metaethics.","quote":null},"finnis":{"life":["John Finnis was born in Adelaide, South Australia, in 1940. In mid-twentieth-century legal philosophy, the dominant view was legal positivism, which separates the question of what law is from the question of whether it is good. The older natural law tradition, which ties law to morality and goes back to Thomas Aquinas, was widely treated as outdated. Finnis became its leading modern defender in the English-speaking world.","He studied law at the University of Adelaide and went to University College, Oxford, as a Rhodes Scholar, completing a doctorate under H. L. A. Hart in 1965. At Hart's invitation he wrote Natural Law and Natural Rights (1980) for an Oxford series on legal theory. He was professor of law and legal philosophy at Oxford from 1989 to 2010 and also taught for many years at Notre Dame Law School. He has also practised as a barrister.","Finnis works closely with the Catholic tradition and with the philosopher Germain Grisez, with whom he developed what is often called the 'new natural law theory'. He has written extensively on Aquinas, on nuclear deterrence and on constitutional law. His positions on sexual ethics have been controversial, and many scholars, including colleagues at Oxford, have strongly disagreed with them. He supervised the doctoral thesis of the future US Supreme Court justice Neil Gorsuch."],"timeline":[[1940,"Born in Adelaide"],[1965,"Completes his DPhil at Oxford under H. L. A. Hart"],[1980,"Publishes Natural Law and Natural Rights"],[1983,"Publishes Fundamentals of Ethics"],[1987,"Co-writes Nuclear Deterrence, Morality and Realism"],[1989,"Becomes professor of law and legal philosophy at Oxford"],[1998,"Publishes Aquinas: Moral, Political, and Legal Theory"],[2011,"Publishes his Collected Essays in five volumes"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Basic goods","body":"Finnis lists a small number of basic goods that are good for any human being: life, knowledge, play, aesthetic experience, friendship, practical reasonableness and religion, later adding marriage. We see they are worth pursuing for their own sake, not because we happen to want them."},{"head":"Self-evident, not derived","body":"Finnis argues that these goods are grasped directly by practical reason, not deduced from facts about human nature. This is meant to avoid the charge, going back to Hume, that natural law illegitimately moves from 'is' to 'ought'."},{"head":"Practical reasonableness","body":"To live well we must pursue the basic goods sensibly: have a coherent plan of life, not favour some people arbitrarily, and never directly act against any basic good. One consequence is that intentionally killing the innocent is always wrong."},{"head":"Law and the common good","body":"Law, for Finnis, exists to coordinate people's actions for the common good. A law in the central sense serves that purpose; an unjust law is law only in a weaker, secondary sense. This lets him accept much of positivism while keeping a moral core."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read chapters 3 and 4 of Natural Law and Natural Rights, where he introduces knowledge as a basic good and then sets out the others.","critics":"Critics argue that the list of basic goods is not as self-evident as Finnis claims and that his conclusions on sexual ethics do not follow from it; others within natural law, such as Russell Hittinger, argued that cutting the goods off from human nature and God weakens the theory.","legacy":"Natural Law and Natural Rights brought natural law back into mainstream legal and moral philosophy. Finnis's 'new natural law theory' remains the most discussed modern version of the tradition.","quote":null},"sosa":{"life":["Ernest Sosa was born in Cárdenas, Cuba, in 1940. He came to the United States to study and entered epistemology just as Edmund Gettier's 1963 paper had shown that justified true belief is not always knowledge. Over the following decades the field split into internalists, who stress the believer's reasons, and externalists, who stress reliable processes. Sosa developed a view that tries to keep what is right in both.","He studied at the University of Miami and took his PhD at the University of Pittsburgh in 1964. He taught at Brown University from 1964 for over four decades and joined Rutgers University in 2007. He gave the John Locke Lectures at Oxford in 2005, published as A Virtue Epistemology (2007). His other books include Knowledge in Perspective (1991), Reflective Knowledge (2009), Knowing Full Well (2011) and Judgment and Agency (2015).","Sosa has been a central figure in the profession: he edited the journals Noûs and Philosophy and Phenomenological Research and served as president of the American Philosophical Association. He is known for patient, layered arguments that he revises in response to critics over many years, and for engaging with Descartes as a fellow epistemologist. He is a fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences."],"timeline":[[1940,"Born in Cárdenas, Cuba"],[1964,"Completes his PhD at Pittsburgh and begins teaching at Brown"],[1980,"Publishes 'The Raft and the Pyramid'"],[1991,"Publishes Knowledge in Perspective"],[2005,"Gives the John Locke Lectures at Oxford"],[2007,"Publishes A Virtue Epistemology; joins Rutgers"],[2015,"Publishes Judgment and Agency"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Knowledge as apt performance","body":"An archer's shot can be accurate (it hits), adroit (skilful) and apt (it hits because it was skilful). Sosa argued that knowledge is apt belief: a belief that is true because of the believer's intellectual skill or 'virtue'."},{"head":"Gettier luck explained","body":"If a gust of wind blows a badly aimed arrow onto the target, the hit is accurate but not apt. Gettier cases are like this: the belief is true, and the believer was competent, but the truth comes from luck rather than the competence."},{"head":"Animal and reflective knowledge","body":"Sosa distinguishes 'animal knowledge', apt belief from reliable faculties like vision, from 'reflective knowledge', where we also aptly judge that our first-order belief is apt. Children and animals have the first; careful thinkers can reach the second."},{"head":"The raft and the pyramid","body":"In a famous 1980 paper, Sosa compared foundationalism, where knowledge rests on a solid base like a pyramid, with coherentism, where beliefs support each other like planks on a raft. He argued that both pictures face problems and that virtue epistemology offers a better model."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read the first chapter of A Virtue Epistemology, where the archer analogy and the accurate, adroit, apt distinction are introduced clearly.","critics":"Jennifer Lackey argued that credit theories like Sosa's fail for testimony: when you learn a fact from a reliable informant, the credit for your true belief seems to belong mostly to them. Duncan Pritchard argued that fake barn cases show apt belief is not enough to rule out every kind of luck.","legacy":"Sosa is one of the founders of virtue epistemology, now a major approach in the theory of knowledge. His AAA (accurate, adroit, apt) structure is widely used beyond epistemology, for example in discussions of skill and action.","quote":null},"skinner":{"life":["Quentin Skinner was born in Oldham, near Manchester, in 1940. He came to Cambridge as a history student around 1960, when the history of political thought was often taught as a conversation among great books on timeless questions. Skinner, together with J. G. A. Pocock and John Dunn, changed this by insisting that texts be read in their historical context. This approach became known as the 'Cambridge School'.","He studied at Gonville and Caius College, Cambridge, graduating in 1962, and spent most of his career at Cambridge, becoming professor of political science in 1979 and Regius Professor of History from 1996 to 2008. He then moved to Queen Mary University of London. His essay 'Meaning and Understanding in the History of Ideas' (1969) made his name, followed by The Foundations of Modern Political Thought (1978), books on Machiavelli and Hobbes and Liberty before Liberalism (1998).","Skinner is known as an energetic lecturer and a clear writer whose short book on Machiavelli has been read by many students. He has received many honours, including the Wolfson History Prize in 1979 and the Balzan Prize in 2006. His work joins history and philosophy, and he has also written on Shakespeare's use of rhetoric. He has also been a pro-vice-chancellor at Cambridge."],"timeline":[[1940,"Born in Oldham, England"],[1969,"Publishes 'Meaning and Understanding in the History of Ideas'"],[1978,"Publishes The Foundations of Modern Political Thought"],[1981,"Publishes Machiavelli"],[1996,"Becomes Regius Professor of History at Cambridge; publishes Reason and Rhetoric in the Philosophy of Hobbes"],[1998,"Publishes Liberty before Liberalism"],[2008,"Publishes Hobbes and Republican Liberty; moves to Queen Mary University of London"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Reading texts in context","body":"To understand a political text, Skinner argued, we must know what its author was doing in writing it: whom they were answering, which debate they were joining. Reading Machiavelli as if he were writing for us today risks getting him badly wrong."},{"head":"Words as actions","body":"Drawing on J. L. Austin's idea that saying things is a kind of doing, Skinner treats texts as moves in arguments. Writing a defence of a king's power in 1640s England was an act of taking sides, not just an abstract statement."},{"head":"Freedom as non-domination","body":"Skinner recovered an older 'neo-Roman' idea of liberty. You are unfree if you live at the mercy of another's will, even if they never actually interfere. A slave with a kind master is still a slave, because the master could interfere at any time."},{"head":"Liberty before liberalism","body":"Skinner argued that this neo-Roman idea was common among seventeenth-century English writers, but it was later eclipsed by the liberal view, associated with Hobbes, that freedom is simply the absence of interference."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Liberty before Liberalism, a short book of lectures that shows both his historical method and his recovery of the neo-Roman idea of freedom.","critics":"Matthew Kramer and others argued that the neo-Roman idea of freedom collapses into the liberal one, since domination matters only because it reduces your actual options. Some philosophers also objected that strict contextualism makes past texts irrelevant to present-day questions.","legacy":"Skinner's contextual method transformed how the history of political thought is studied. His work on neo-Roman liberty, alongside Philip Pettit's, helped launch modern republicanism in political philosophy.","quote":null},"feldman":{"life":["Fred Feldman was born in 1941 and grew up in Maplewood, New Jersey. He entered philosophy when the old questions of ethics, such as what makes a life go well and why death is bad, were being taken up again with the tools of analytic philosophy. Feldman became known for defending a modernised hedonism and for clear, precise arguments about death.","He studied at Bard College and Harpur College (now Binghamton University) and took his PhD at Brown University, where he studied under Roderick Chisholm. He taught at the University of Massachusetts Amherst from 1969 until his retirement in 2013. His books include Introductory Ethics (1978), Doing the Best We Can (1986), Confrontations with the Reaper (1992), Utilitarianism, Hedonism, and Desert (1997), Pleasure and the Good Life (2004) and What Is This Thing Called Happiness? (2010).","Feldman is known for writing that is both rigorous and friendly, often stating views as numbered principles and testing them against examples. His textbooks have been widely used in teaching. He co-edited The Oxford Handbook of Philosophy of Death (2013), and many of his former students now work on wellbeing and death. His later work also took up questions of distributive justice and desert."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born"],[1969,"Joins the University of Massachusetts Amherst"],[1978,"Publishes Introductory Ethics"],[1986,"Publishes Doing the Best We Can"],[1992,"Publishes Confrontations with the Reaper"],[2004,"Publishes Pleasure and the Good Life"],[2010,"Publishes What Is This Thing Called Happiness?"],[2013,"Retires from UMass Amherst"]],"ideas":[{"head":"The deprivation account of death","body":"Epicurus argued death cannot harm you, since when death is, you are not. Feldman replied that death is bad for you because it deprives you of the good life you would otherwise have had. Death at 20 is worse than at 90 because it takes away more."},{"head":"When is death bad?","body":"Critics ask when death harms you, if you no longer exist afterwards. Feldman's answer was that the fact that your life is worse than it would have been is true at all times, so the harm is not located at one moment."},{"head":"Attitudinal hedonism","body":"Hedonism says pleasure is the only thing good in itself. Feldman's version treats pleasure as an attitude: taking pleasure in some state of affairs, such as enjoying that your friend succeeded. This avoids the objection that pleasure is just a buzz of sensation."},{"head":"Adjusting for desert","body":"Feldman modified hedonism so that pleasure counts for more when deserved and less when not. A sadist's pleasure in someone's suffering contributes less value than an innocent person's pleasure in a sunset."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read his essay 'Some Puzzles About the Evil of Death' (1991), a short and clear defence of the deprivation account that answers Epicurus directly.","critics":"Defenders of the Epicurean view, following Lucretius, ask why we are not troubled by the endless time before we were born, which deprives us of goods just as much as death does. Others, such as defenders of objective list theories, argue that hedonism leaves out goods like knowledge and friendship.","legacy":"Feldman's work made the deprivation account the standard view in the philosophy of death and kept hedonism a live option in theories of wellbeing.","quote":null},"van_fraassen":{"life":["Bas van Fraassen was born in 1941 in Goes, in the Netherlands, during the German occupation. In 1956 his family emigrated to Edmonton, Canada. When he entered philosophy, logical positivism had collapsed, and many philosophers concluded that science gives us the literal truth about unseen things like electrons and quarks. Van Fraassen became the most influential philosopher to argue for a more modest, empiricist view.","He studied at the University of Alberta and took his PhD at the University of Pittsburgh in 1966 under Adolf Grünbaum. He taught at Yale, the University of Toronto and the University of Southern California, and at Princeton from 1982 to 2008, then at San Francisco State University. The Scientific Image (1980) made his name. Later books include Laws and Symmetry (1989), Quantum Mechanics: An Empiricist View (1991), The Empirical Stance (2002) and Scientific Representation (2008).","Van Fraassen began with work in logic and the philosophy of space and time, and has written on probability, quantum mechanics and the philosophy of religion. He has edited journals of logic and received many honours, including the inaugural Hempel Award of the Philosophy of Science Association in 2012. In 2026 he received the Rolf Schock Prize in Logic and Philosophy."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born in Goes, the Netherlands"],[1956,"Family emigrates to Edmonton, Canada"],[1966,"Completes his PhD at Pittsburgh"],[1980,"Publishes The Scientific Image"],[1982,"Joins Princeton University"],[1989,"Publishes Laws and Symmetry"],[2002,"Publishes The Empirical Stance"],[2008,"Publishes Scientific Representation; moves to San Francisco State University"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Constructive empiricism","body":"Science aims at theories that are empirically adequate: they get everything we can observe right. To accept a theory, we need only believe that much, not that its claims about unobservable things like electrons are true."},{"head":"Acceptance is not belief","body":"Scientists can use a theory fully: rely on it, work within it, build experiments on it, without believing everything it says. Van Fraassen compared this to how people can work with a map without believing every detail of the mapmaker's notes."},{"head":"Against inference to the best explanation","body":"Realists say that the best explanation of a theory's success is that it is true. Van Fraassen replied that a theory's success can be explained in a Darwinian way: theories that fail are discarded, so the survivors fit the evidence, whether or not they are true."},{"head":"No laws of nature","body":"In Laws and Symmetry he argued that the idea of laws of nature, as something beyond the regularities we observe, is unneeded and confused. Science works better by focusing on symmetries and models."},{"head":"Empiricism as a stance","body":"In The Empirical Stance he argued that empiricism is not a theory that could be proved true but an attitude: a commitment to take experience seriously and resist speculative metaphysics."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Van Fraassen thinks electrons and atoms do not exist, or that science is untrustworthy.","truth":"He is agnostic about unobservable things rather than denying them, and he holds science in high regard; he only argues that accepting a theory need not mean believing all it says about the unseen."},"startHere":"Read chapter 2 of The Scientific Image, 'Arguments concerning Scientific Realism', which states constructive empiricism and answers the main realist arguments.","critics":"Critics such as Paul Churchland and Alan Musgrave argued that the line between observable and unobservable is arbitrary: we see things through glasses, microscopes and telescopes, so why trust some instruments and not others? Ian Hacking made a related point about microscopes.","legacy":"Constructive empiricism is the main alternative to scientific realism, and nearly every discussion of realism in the philosophy of science addresses van Fraassen.","quote":null},"edgington":{"life":["Dorothy Edgington was born in 1941. She studied philosophy at Oxford in the 1960s, when the logic of everyday words like 'if', and of vague words like 'tall' or 'heap', was becoming a central topic in philosophy of language. Classical logic handles such words awkwardly, and many paradoxes arise as a result. Edgington became one of the leading figures in applying probability to these puzzles.","She read philosophy, politics and economics at St Hilda's College, Oxford, and took a BPhil at Nuffield College. She taught at Birkbeck College, London, from 1968 to 1996, then was a fellow of University College, Oxford, returned to Birkbeck as professor and was Waynflete Professor of Metaphysical Philosophy at Oxford from 2003 to 2006. Her influential papers include 'Do Conditionals Have Truth-Conditions?' (1986), 'On Conditionals' (1995) and 'Vagueness by Degrees' (1997).","Edgington has worked mainly through journal articles rather than books, and her long survey 'On Conditionals' became a standard reference. She was president of the Mind Association in 2004–2005 and of the Aristotelian Society in 2007–2008, and is a Fellow of the British Academy. Birkbeck holds an annual lecture in her name. She is now professor emerita and a fellow of Magdalen College, Oxford."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born"],[1968,"Becomes a lecturer in philosophy at Birkbeck College"],[1985,"Publishes 'The Paradox of Knowability'"],[1986,"Publishes 'Do Conditionals Have Truth-Conditions?'"],[1995,"Publishes 'On Conditionals'"],[1997,"Publishes 'Vagueness by Degrees'"],[2003,"Becomes Waynflete Professor of Metaphysical Philosophy at Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Conditionals are not ordinary statements","body":"In logic textbooks, 'if A then B' is true whenever A is false or B is true. Edgington argued this gives absurd results: it would make 'if the moon is made of cheese, I am the Pope' true. She held that conditionals do not state facts that are simply true or false."},{"head":"If as supposition","body":"To judge 'if it rains, the match will be cancelled', you suppose it rains and ask how likely cancellation is. Edgington argued that believing a conditional is having a high conditional probability: a belief in B given A, not a belief in some fact."},{"head":"Vagueness by degrees","body":"Is a man with 10,000 hairs bald? Vague words have borderline cases. Edgington proposed that truth comes in degrees, and that these degrees obey the same rules as probabilities. 'He is bald' might be true to degree 0.7."},{"head":"Solving the heap paradox","body":"One grain of sand is not a heap, and adding one grain never turns a non-heap into a heap, yet many grains clearly are a heap. On Edgington's view, each step is almost but not completely true, and small losses of truth add up over many steps."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read her Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry 'Conditionals', a clear survey written by her that explains the problem with 'if' and her own suppositional view.","critics":"Defenders of truth-conditional theories, such as Frank Jackson and those following Robert Stalnaker, argue that conditionals must have truth values to be embedded in longer sentences and used in logic. Critics of degree theories of vagueness, such as Timothy Williamson, argue that degrees of truth just move the sharp boundaries elsewhere.","legacy":"Edgington's work made the suppositional, probability-based theory of conditionals a leading position, and her degree theory of vagueness is a standard option in debates about the heap paradox.","quote":null},"tooley":{"life":["Michael Tooley was born in Toronto in 1941. He came into philosophy in the 1960s, when analytic metaphysics was reviving after logical positivism, and philosophers began again to ask what laws of nature, causes and time really are. At the same time, debates about abortion were becoming central to applied ethics. Tooley made important and sometimes controversial contributions to both areas.","He studied at the University of Toronto and took his PhD at Princeton in 1968. He taught at Stanford and for many years at the Australian National University, and in 1992 joined the University of Colorado Boulder, where he is now emeritus. His paper 'Abortion and Infanticide' (1972) was followed by 'The Nature of Laws' (1977), Abortion and Infanticide (1983), Causation: A Realist Approach (1987) and Time, Tense, and Causation (1997).","Tooley writes long, closely argued books that try to settle questions systematically. He has also worked in the philosophy of religion, publishing Knowledge of God (2008), a debate with Alvin Plantinga, and The Problem of Evil (2019). He was elected a Corresponding Fellow of the Australian Academy of the Humanities in 1992. He co-edited a collection of readings on causation with Ernest Sosa (1993)."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born in Toronto"],[1968,"Completes his PhD at Princeton"],[1972,"Publishes 'Abortion and Infanticide'"],[1977,"Publishes 'The Nature of Laws'"],[1987,"Publishes Causation: A Realist Approach"],[1992,"Joins the University of Colorado Boulder"],[1997,"Publishes Time, Tense, and Causation"],[2008,"Publishes Knowledge of God, a debate with Alvin Plantinga"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Laws as relations between properties","body":"A law of nature, Tooley argued, is not just a regularity, like all copper conducting electricity so far. It is a real relation of necessitation between properties: being copper makes something conduct. This view is shared with Fred Dretske and David Armstrong."},{"head":"Causation is real","body":"Following David Hume, many philosophers say causation is nothing more than one kind of event regularly following another. Tooley argued that causation is a real relation between events that cannot be reduced to patterns, and he used thought experiments to show that two worlds could share all patterns but differ in their causes."},{"head":"A growing universe","body":"Tooley defended a dynamic view of time in which the past and present are real, but the future does not yet exist. As time passes, reality grows, as new events come into being. This is often called the growing block theory."},{"head":"Personhood and the right to life","body":"In his 1972 paper Tooley argued that only beings with a concept of themselves as continuing subjects of experience have a serious right to life. Since foetuses and newborns lack this, he concluded that both abortion and infanticide in early infancy can be permissible, a conclusion most people reject."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read 'Abortion and Infanticide' (1972), his most widely read paper, which states its argument clearly; readers should know that its conclusion about newborns is rejected by most people, including many philosophers.","critics":"Critics such as Don Marquis argued that what makes killing wrong is depriving a being of a valuable future, which foetuses and infants have. On laws, David Lewis and Bas van Fraassen asked how a relation between properties could force anything to happen.","legacy":"Tooley's account of laws, with Dretske's and Armstrong's, is one of the main non-Humean theories of laws of nature. His paper on abortion and infanticide remains one of the most discussed works in applied ethics.","quote":null},"slote":{"life":["Michael Slote was born in 1941. When he began working in ethics, the field was a contest between utilitarianism and Kantian theories, both focused on rules for right action. From the late 1950s some philosophers urged a return to the ancient focus on character and virtue. Slote became one of the most original contributors to this revival, and later developed an ethics based on empathy.","He took his PhD at Harvard and taught at Columbia, at Stony Brook and at Trinity College Dublin, where he held a professorship, and then at the University of Maryland, before moving to the University of Miami, where he became professor of ethics. His early books include Common-Sense Morality and Consequentialism (1985) and Beyond Optimizing (1989). His virtue ethics appeared in From Morality to Virtue (1992) and Morals from Motives (2001).","Slote has changed and developed his views over his career, moving from consequentialism to virtue ethics, and from virtue ethics to a 'sentimentalist' theory influenced by David Hume and by the ethics of care. In later books such as From Enlightenment to Receptivity (2013) he engaged with Chinese philosophy, including the ideas of yin and yang. He has written on the history of ethics as well as on current theory."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born"],[1985,"Publishes Common-Sense Morality and Consequentialism"],[1989,"Publishes Beyond Optimizing"],[1992,"Publishes From Morality to Virtue"],[2001,"Publishes Morals from Motives"],[2007,"Publishes The Ethics of Care and Empathy"],[2010,"Publishes Moral Sentimentalism"],[2013,"Publishes From Enlightenment to Receptivity"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Satisficing","body":"Must we always do the very best? Slote argued that it can be rational and moral to settle for what is good enough. A person who turns down a second slice of cake they would enjoy is not being irrational; this idea is called satisficing."},{"head":"Agent-based virtue ethics","body":"In Morals from Motives Slote argued that an act is right if it expresses good motives, such as benevolence or caring, and wrong if it expresses bad motives or a lack of good ones. The goodness of the act comes entirely from the agent's inner state."},{"head":"Empathy as the root","body":"Drawing on Hume and the ethics of care, Slote made empathy central. We feel more empathy for people near us and for dangers we can see, and he argued this explains common moral views, such as that we have stronger duties to a drowning child in front of us than to distant strangers."},{"head":"Morality from feeling","body":"Slote's sentimentalism holds that moral concepts like 'wrong' are rooted in feelings: to judge an act wrong is connected to feeling a chill of disapproval at its lack of empathy. Reason alone, on his view, cannot ground morality."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read The Ethics of Care and Empathy, a short book that sets out his empathy-based theory of right and wrong in plain terms.","critics":"Critics argue that agent-based ethics confuses a good person with a right act: someone with kind motives can still do the wrong thing out of ignorance. Others worry that grounding duty in empathy, which is biased toward the near and familiar, endorses those biases rather than correcting them.","legacy":"Slote helped shape modern virtue ethics and bring care ethics into mainstream moral theory, and he has encouraged dialogue between Western and Chinese ethical traditions.","quote":null},"ga_cohen":{"life":["Gerald Allan Cohen was born in Montreal in 1941 into a Jewish, communist and anti-religious working-class family, and attended a secular Yiddish school. He grew up in a political world of left-wing activism, but as he became a philosopher he wanted to test his socialist convictions by the highest standards of analytic argument rather than by party loyalty. Much of his later work asked whether socialism could be defended as a matter of justice.","He studied philosophy and politics at McGill University and took a BPhil at Oxford, where his teachers included Gilbert Ryle and Isaiah Berlin. He taught at University College London from 1963 to 1984 and was Chichele Professor of Social and Political Theory at All Souls College, Oxford, from 1985 to 2008. Karl Marx's Theory of History (1978) launched 'analytical Marxism'. Later he turned to justice, criticising Robert Nozick and then John Rawls. He died in Oxford in 2009.","Cohen was famous for his wit and for precise, demanding arguments. He was a member of the 'September Group' of analytical Marxists. He gave the Gifford Lectures in 1996, published as If You're an Egalitarian, How Come You're So Rich?, which mixed philosophy with memories of his Montreal childhood. His short final book, Why Not Socialism?, appeared in 2009. Several volumes of his essays and lectures were published after his death."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born in Montreal"],[1963,"Begins teaching at University College London"],[1978,"Publishes Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence"],[1985,"Becomes Chichele Professor at All Souls College, Oxford"],[1995,"Publishes Self-Ownership, Freedom, and Equality"],[1996,"Gives the Gifford Lectures in Edinburgh"],[2008,"Publishes Rescuing Justice and Equality"],[2009,"Publishes Why Not Socialism?; dies in Oxford"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Analytical Marxism","body":"Cohen restated Marx's theory of history with the clarity of analytic philosophy. He argued that the development of technology and productive power explains why societies have the economic structures they do, and defended this with careful logic rather than slogans."},{"head":"Against self-ownership","body":"Nozick argued that because we own ourselves, we may keep what we earn, and taxation for redistribution is wrong. Cohen took this argument very seriously and argued that self-ownership, even if granted, does not justify the large inequalities Nozick defended."},{"head":"Luck and justice","body":"Cohen held that inequalities are unjust when they come from luck, such as being born talented or rich, rather than from people's genuine choices. A person who works harder by choice may earn more; a person who is simply born with a gift has not earned its rewards."},{"head":"The personal is political","body":"Rawls allowed inequalities when giving talented people incentives would help the worst off. Cohen asked why the talented need incentives at all: if they truly believed in justice, they would work hard without extra pay. So justice applies to personal choices, not just to institutions."},{"head":"The camping trip","body":"In Why Not Socialism? Cohen imagined friends on a camping trip who share equipment and food and help each other, without anyone demanding payment. Most people find this attractive; Cohen argued the question is only whether we can find ways to extend such equality and community to a whole society."}],"misconception":null,"startHere":"Read Why Not Socialism?, a short book of under 100 pages that opens with the camping trip example and shows his style of argument at its most accessible.","critics":"Elizabeth Anderson, in 'What Is the Point of Equality?' (1999), argued that luck egalitarians like Cohen focus wrongly on compensating bad luck and should instead aim at relations of equality between citizens. Rawlsians argued that justice applies to the basic institutions of society, not to every personal choice.","legacy":"Cohen founded analytical Marxism and became one of the leading egalitarian political philosophers. His critiques of Nozick and Rawls remain central to debates about equality and justice.","quote":null},"rachels":{"life":["James Rachels was born in Columbus, Georgia, in 1941. He grew up in the American South during segregation, and came into philosophy in the 1960s, when moral philosophers were turning from abstract debates about the meaning of moral words to practical issues such as euthanasia, animals and the treatment of the dying. Rachels became one of the most influential writers in this new field of applied ethics.","He studied at Mercer University and took his PhD at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill in 1967. He taught at several universities, including the University of Miami, before moving in 1977 to the University of Alabama at Birmingham, where he chaired the philosophy department, served as a dean and later became University Professor. His article 'Active and Passive Euthanasia' (1975) was followed by The End of Life (1986), The Elements of Moral Philosophy (1986) and Created from Animals (1990). He died of cancer in 2003.","Rachels was known for clear, simple prose and sharp examples that make readers question familiar assumptions. His textbook The Elements of Moral Philosophy has been used by many thousands of students; later editions were revised by his son, the philosopher Stuart Rachels. He also wrote widely on animal rights and on whether there is a meaningful difference between humans and other animals. The Legacy of Socrates, a collection of his essays, appeared after his death."],"timeline":[[1941,"Born in Columbus, Georgia"],[1967,"Completes his PhD at the University of North Carolina"],[1975,"Publishes 'Active and Passive Euthanasia' in the New England Journal of Medicine"],[1977,"Joins the University of Alabama at Birmingham"],[1986,"Publishes The Elements of Moral Philosophy and The End of Life"],[1990,"Publishes Created from Animals: The Moral Implications of Darwinism"],[2003,"Dies in Birmingham, Alabama"]],"ideas":[{"head":"Killing and letting die","body":"Doctors' rules then allowed letting a patient die but forbade actively killing them. Rachels argued that the bare difference between killing and letting die makes no moral difference in itself, so the rule could not be justified on that basis alone."},{"head":"Smith and Jones","body":"Smith drowns his young cousin in the bath to gain an inheritance. Jones plans the same, but finds the boy has slipped and drowns, and simply watches him die. Rachels argued that Jones is no better than Smith, which shows that letting die is not in itself less bad than killing."},{"head":"Sometimes active euthanasia is kinder","body":"If a dying patient is in agony and the decision to let them die has already been made, Rachels argued, a quick lethal injection may be more humane than withdrawing treatment and waiting for days of suffering."},{"head":"Darwin and human dignity","body":"In Created from Animals Rachels argued that Darwin's theory undermines the idea that humans are radically special. Rather than treating all humans as sacred and all animals as unimportant, we should judge how to treat each being by its actual characteristics."},{"head":"Against cultural relativism","body":"In The Elements of Moral Philosophy Rachels argued that the fact that cultures disagree about morality does not show there is no truth about it. People once disagreed about whether the earth is flat, too."}],"misconception":{"myth":"Rachels argued that killing is always just as bad as letting die.","truth":"His claim was narrower: the difference between killing and letting die is not morally important in itself, though other factors, such as motive or consent, often differ between particular cases."},"startHere":"Read 'Active and Passive Euthanasia' (1975), only a few pages long, which contains the famous Smith and Jones example and shows his method of argument in miniature.","critics":"Philippa Foot and others defended the distinction between doing and allowing harm, arguing that negative duties not to harm are stricter than positive duties to help. Critics also argued that his Smith and Jones case is misleading, because the bad motive in both cases hides a real difference.","legacy":"Rachels's paper on euthanasia is among the most reprinted articles in applied ethics, and The Elements of Moral Philosophy introduced generations of students to moral philosophy.","quote":null}};
